window.SUBJECT_DATA=window.SUBJECT_DATA||{};
window.SUBJECT_DATA["chn"]=[{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"According to the WHO (1948) definition, health is a state of complete _______ well-being and not merely an absence of disease or infirmity.","options":["Physical, mental and social","Physical, spiritual and emotional","Mental, social and economic","Physical, mental and financial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO 1948 definition states health is \"a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely an absence of disease or infirmity.\"  Why B wrong: Spiritual and emotional are dimensions of health, not part of the 1948 WHO definition.  Why C wrong: Economic well-being is not part of the WHO 1948 definition.  Why D wrong: Financial well-being is not part of the WHO 1948 definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year did the WHO give its classic definition of health?","options":["1946","1948","1974","1978"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO health definition is dated 1948.  Why A wrong: 1946 is associated with the Bhore Committee, not the WHO health definition.  Why C wrong: 1974 is linked to the WHO community health definition.  Why D wrong: 1978 is the year of Health for All / Alma Ata Declaration."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following are recognized concepts of health? (Select all that apply)","options":["Biomedical concept","Holistic concept","Psychosocial concept","Ecological concept","Financial concept"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed concepts of health are Biomedical, Holistic, Psychosocial and Ecological.  Why E wrong: Financial concept is not listed among the concepts of health."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a dimension of health?","options":["Physical dimension","Social dimension","Economic dimension","Spiritual dimension"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The listed dimensions are Physical, Mental, Social, Spiritual and Emotional — economic dimension is not included.  Why A wrong: Physical dimension is a listed dimension.  Why B wrong: Social dimension is a listed dimension.  Why D wrong: Spiritual dimension is a listed dimension."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The dimensions of health listed include Physical, Mental, Social, Spiritual and _______ dimension.","options":["Emotional","Financial","Occupational","Political"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The five dimensions listed are Physical, Mental, Social, Spiritual and Emotional.  Why B, C, D wrong: Financial, occupational and political dimensions are not listed on this page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"\"A group of people sharing common needs, interests, resources and environments\" best defines which term?","options":["Community","Population","Society","Family"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This is the definition of community given on the page.  Why B wrong: Population is a statistical count of people, not defined by shared needs/interests here.  Why C wrong: Society is broader and not the definition given.  Why D wrong: A family is a smaller unit, not defined here as sharing common resources/environments."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The definition of Community Health that refers to the health status of members of the community and the totality of health care provided is attributed to which year?","options":["WHO-1948","WHO-1974","WHO-1978","WHO-2000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page attributes the Community Health definition to WHO-1974.  Why A wrong: 1948 is the general health definition year.  Why C wrong: 1978 is the Health for All / Alma Ata year.  Why D wrong: 2000 relates to MDG declaration and the HFA target year."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"According to the ICN, a nurse is qualified and authorised in his/her country to provide responsible and competent professional service for all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["The promotion of health","The prevention of illness","The care of the sick and well individuals","The financing of hospitals"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The ICN definition lists promotion of health, prevention of illness, care of the sick and well individuals, and rehabilitation services — not financing of hospitals.  Why A, B, C wrong: These are all part of the ICN definition of the nurse's professional service."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following is a service the nurse provides according to the ICN definition, in addition to promotion, prevention and care?","options":["Rehabilitation services","Legal services","Financial services","Transport services"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ICN definition explicitly includes \"the rehabilitation services.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: Legal, financial and transport services are not part of the ICN definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following is the FIRST principle of Community Health Nursing as listed?","options":["Meet the community and establish contact with community","Evaluation","Setting priorities among health problems","Intervention and implementation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The listed principles begin with \"Meet the community and established contact with community.\"  Why B wrong: Evaluation is the last step listed.  Why C wrong: Setting priorities comes after identification of problems.  Why D wrong: Intervention and implementation comes after planning."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The correct sequence of Community Health Nursing principles after \"identification of health problems and needs\" is:","options":["Setting priorities → Planning and problem solving → Intervention and implementation → Evaluation","Evaluation → Planning → Intervention → Setting priorities","Planning → Evaluation → Setting priorities → Intervention","Intervention → Evaluation → Planning → Setting priorities"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The listed order is: meet community, identify problems, set priorities, plan and problem solve, intervene and implement, then evaluate.  Why B, C, D wrong: These reorder the steps incorrectly; evaluation is always last."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year was the first nursing school in India established at the Government General Hospital, Madras?","options":["1860","1867","1871","1918"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 1871 the first school of nursing was established in Government General Hospital, Madras with a six-month diploma midwives programme with four students (SGPGI-2023).  Why A wrong: 1860 is when Florence Nightingale opened the Nightingale Training School in London.  Why B wrong: 1867 is when St Stephens Hospital, Delhi first trained Indian women as nurses.  Why D wrong: 1918 is when the Lady Reading Health School, New Delhi started."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The first nursing school in India (Madras, 1871) started with which programme and how many students?","options":["Six-month diploma midwives programme with four students","Three-year B.Sc. programme with twelve students","One-year LHV programme with ten students","Two-year ANM programme with forty students"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It was a six-month diploma midwives programme with four students.  Why B wrong: B.Sc. Nursing began much later (1946 at RAK/CMC).  Why C wrong: LHV training began at Lady Reading Health School in 1918.  Why D wrong: ANM training with intake of 40 began in 1985."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Who is regarded as one of the pioneers in establishing the idea of nursing schools and opened the \"Nightingale Training School for Nurses\"?","options":["Florence Nightingale","Rajkumari Amrit Kaur","Clara Barton","Mary Seacole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Florence Nightingale (lady with the lamp) opened the Nightingale Training School for Nurses at St Thomas' Hospital, London in 1860.  Why B wrong: Rajkumari Amrit Kaur is associated with RAK College of Nursing (1946).  Why C, D wrong: Clara Barton and Mary Seacole are not credited with the Nightingale Training School."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year and city did Florence Nightingale open the Nightingale Training School for Nurses?","options":["London, 1860","Madras, 1871","Delhi, 1918","Vellore, 1946"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: She opened it at St Thomas' Hospital, London in 1860 (now part of King's College London).  Why B wrong: Madras 1871 is the first Indian nursing school.  Why C wrong: Delhi 1918 is the Lady Reading Health School.  Why D wrong: Vellore 1946 is CMC College of Nursing."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which body was the First Registration council of India for nurses?","options":["Madras Midwives and Nurses Registration council","Indian Nursing Council","Trained Nurses Association of India","International Council of Nurses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The First Registration council of India was the Madras Midwives and Nurses Registration council.  Why B wrong: INC was established later as the national regulatory body.  Why C wrong: TNAI (1908) is a professional association, not a registration council.  Why D wrong: ICN is the international body."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In 1953, which organization first published the code of ethics for nurses?","options":["Indian Nursing Council (INC)","International Council of Nurses (ICN)","Trained Nurses Association of India (TNAI)","World Health Organization (WHO)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1953 the International Council of Nurses (ICN) first published the code of ethics for nurses.  Why A wrong: INC first published the code of ethics for nurses in India in 2006.  Why C, D wrong: TNAI and WHO are not credited with the 1953 code of ethics."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year did the Indian Nursing Council (INC) first publish the code of ethics for nurses in India?","options":["1947","1953","2006","2012"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 2006 the INC first published the code of ethics for nurses in India (NIA Jaipur 2023).  Why A wrong: 1947 is the year the INC Act was passed in Parliament.  Why B wrong: 1953 is when the ICN published its code of ethics.  Why D wrong: 2012 is when a course was stopped, not related to the code of ethics."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The Lady Reading Health School, New Delhi, started in 1918 primarily for training of:","options":["Lady health visitors / LHV","B.Sc. nursing students","M.Sc. nursing students","Auxiliary nurse midwives"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Lady Reading Health School, New Delhi started in India in 1918 where training started for lady health visitors / LHV.  Why B, C wrong: B.Sc. and M.Sc. nursing began later (1946 and 1960).  Why D wrong: ANM is a separate course; LRHS trained LHVs and DAIS."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In 1946, RAK College of Nursing New Delhi and CMC College of Nursing at Vellore started training in which courses?","options":["B.Sc. Nursing and Public Health Nursing","GNM and ANM","LHV and DAIS","M.Sc. Nursing and Ph.D."],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 1946, RAK and CMC started training as B.Sc. Nursing and Public Health Nursing.  Why B wrong: GNM and ANM are diploma-level courses started at other times.  Why C wrong: LHV and DAIS were trained at LRHS in 1918.  Why D wrong: M.Sc. Nursing started in 1960."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year did Post Basic B.Sc. Nursing courses start in India for GNM candidates at the College of Nursing, Trivandrum, Kerala?","options":["1946","1953","1962-63","1976"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 1962-63, Post Basic B.Sc. Nursing courses started in India for GNM candidates at College of Nursing Trivandrum, Kerala.  Why A wrong: 1946 is RAK/CMC B.Sc. Nursing.  Why B wrong: 1953 is the ICN code of ethics.  Why D wrong: 1976 is when DNEA started."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"DNEA (Diploma in Nursing Education & Administration) was started for trained staff nurses in which year?","options":["1962","1976","1988","1994"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DNEA was started for trained staff nurses in the year 1976.  Why A wrong: 1962-63 is Post Basic B.Sc. Nursing.  Why C wrong: 1988 is when DNEA changed to DPHN.  Why D wrong: 1994 is Post B.Sc. Nursing through IGNOU."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In 1977, which nursing course (full time) was stopped and a Promotional Course for ANMs was started?","options":["LHV course","B.Sc. Nursing","GNM","DNEA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 1977, the LHV course (full time) was stopped and a Promotional Course for ANMs began.  Why B, C, D wrong: These courses were not the ones stopped in 1977."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"ANM training started in 1985 with what intake capacity and course duration?","options":["Intake of 40 every year; duration 1.5 years","Intake of 4 every year; duration 6 months","Intake of 12 every year; duration 2 years","Intake of 40 every year; duration 3 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 1985, ANM Training started with intake capacity of 40 every year and duration of 1.5 years.  Why B wrong: 4 students/6 months describes the 1871 Madras midwives programme.  Why C wrong: 12 students describes LRHS (1918).  Why D wrong: The duration was 1.5 years, not 3 years."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In 1990, ANM training was converted into ANM (vocational); the course duration changed from 1.5 years to 2 years and the minimum entrance qualification became:","options":["10th pass","8th pass","10+2","Graduation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 1990 the duration changed from 1.5 to 2 years and minimum qualification for entrance was 10+2.  Why A, B wrong: The requirement was 10+2, higher than 10th or 8th pass.  Why D wrong: Graduation was not the entry requirement for ANM."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"DNEA course was changed to DPHN (Diploma in Public Health Nurse) in the year 1988 on the recommendation of which committees?","options":["Bhore Committee (1946) and Mudaliar Committee (1962)","Shrivastav Committee and Kartar Singh Committee","Bajaj Committee and Chadah Committee","Mukerji Committee and Jungalwalla Committee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DNEA changed to DPHN in 1988 with the recommendation of Bhore Committee (1946) and Mudaliar Committee (1962).  Why B, C, D wrong: These committees are not credited on the page with the DPHN change."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year did the nursing school come directly under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare (MOHFW)?","options":["1988","2006","1994","1977"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 2006, the school came directly under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare (MOHFW).  Why A wrong: 1988 is the DNEA to DPHN change.  Why C wrong: 1994 is Post B.Sc. Nursing via IGNOU.  Why D wrong: 1977 is LHV course stoppage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The INC Act was passed in the Parliament of India in which year?","options":["1947","1949","1908","1867"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The INC act was passed in Parliament of India in 1947.  Why B wrong: 1949 is when the INC act was legalized / came into force.  Why C wrong: 1908 is when TNAI was established.  Why D wrong: 1867 is St Stephens Hospital nurse training."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The INC Act was legalized / came into force in India in which year?","options":["1947","1949","1953","1960"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The INC act was legalized or came into force in India in 1949.  Why A wrong: 1947 is when it was passed in Parliament.  Why C wrong: 1953 is the ICN code of ethics.  Why D wrong: 1960 is M.Sc. Nursing start."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The Trained Nurses Association of India (TNAI) was established in India in which year?","options":["1867","1908","1918","1946"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1908, the Trained Nurses Association of India (TNAI) was established in India.  Why A wrong: 1867 is St Stephens Hospital Indian women nurse training.  Why C wrong: 1918 is LRHS.  Why D wrong: 1946 is RAK/CMC."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which was the first nursing training school of Indian women as nurses (in 1867)?","options":["St Stephens Hospital, Delhi","Government General Hospital, Madras","RAK College of Nursing, Delhi","CMC Vellore"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: St Stephens Hospital at Delhi was the first nursing training school of Indian women as nurses in 1867.  Why B wrong: Madras (1871) was a midwives diploma school.  Why C, D wrong: RAK and CMC started in 1946."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year was M.Sc. Nursing started in India, at RAK College of Nursing, New Delhi?","options":["1946","1960","1963","1994"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1960, M.Sc. Nursing was started in RAK College of Nursing, New Delhi.  Why A wrong: 1946 is B.Sc. Nursing start.  Why C wrong: 1963 is Post B.Sc. Nursing at Trivandrum.  Why D wrong: 1994 is Post B.Sc. via IGNOU."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Post B.Sc. Nursing was started in India through IGNOU in which year?","options":["1963","1988","1994","2006"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 1994, Post B.Sc. Nursing was started in India through IGNOU.  Why A wrong: 1963 is Post B.Sc. Nursing at School of Nursing, Trivandrum.  Why B wrong: 1988 is DNEA to DPHN.  Why D wrong: 2006 is school under MOHFW."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The primary health care system is also called the _______ of India.","options":["Essential health care","Referral health care","Specialist health care","Curative health care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary Health Care System is also called essential health care of India.  Why B wrong: Secondary care serves as the first referral unit.  Why C wrong: Specialist/super-specialist care is tertiary.  Why D wrong: Curative services are the focus of secondary care."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Primary health care is the first level of contact between the individual and the health system where essential health care facilities are provided. Examples of primary health care include:","options":["Sub centre (SC) and Primary Health Centre (PHC)","Community Health Centre (CHC) and District hospital","Regional hospital and AIIMS","NIMHANS and JIPMER"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of primary health care are Sub centre (SC) and Primary Health Centre (PHC).  Why B wrong: CHC and District hospital are examples of secondary health care.  Why C, D wrong: Regional hospital, AIIMS, NIMHANS, JIPMER are tertiary health care."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which level of the health care system serves as the \"first referral unit (FRU)\"?","options":["Primary health care system","Secondary health care system","Tertiary health care system","Primordial health care system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The secondary health care system serves as the first referral unit or FRU in the health system.  Why A wrong: Primary care is the first point of contact, not the referral unit.  Why C wrong: Tertiary care handles complex/super-specialist cases.  Why D wrong: Primordial is a level of prevention, not a health care system level."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Examples of secondary health care include:","options":["Community Health Centre (CHC) and District hospitals","Sub centre and PHC","AIIMS and JIPMER","Regional and super-specialty hospitals"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of secondary healthcare are Community Health Centre (CHC) and District hospitals.  Why B wrong: SC and PHC are primary health care.  Why C, D wrong: AIIMS, JIPMER, regional and super-specialty hospitals are tertiary."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following is an example of tertiary health care?","options":["Central government institutes like AIIMS, JIPMER, PGI, SGPGI, NIMHANS","Sub centre","Primary Health Centre","Community Health Centre"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tertiary health care examples include regional hospitals, super-specialty hospitals, medical college hospitals and central government institutes like AIIMS, JIPMER, PGI, SGPGI, NIMHANS.  Why B, C wrong: SC and PHC are primary care.  Why D wrong: CHC is secondary care."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The three-tier health care system (SC, PHC, CHC) was established under the recommendation of which committee?","options":["Shrivastav Committee (1974-75)","Bhore Committee (1946)","Mudaliar Committee (1962)","Kartar Singh Committee (1973)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three-tier health care system (SC, PHC, CHC) was established under recommendation of Shrivastav Committee (1974-75).  Why B, C, D wrong: These committees are not credited with the three-tier system on this page."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The concept of \"Health For All\" was declared in which year by the WHO assembly?","options":["1974","1978","1985","2000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Health For All concept was declared in 1978 by the WHO assembly.  Why A wrong: 1974 relates to the community health definition / Shrivastav committee.  Why C wrong: 1985 is ANM training start.  Why D wrong: 2000 is the target year for HFA and MDG declaration year."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"According to the Health For All goal, essential health would be achieved for all people of the world by which year?","options":["2000","2015","2030","1978"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The main goal was that essential health would be achieved for all people of the world by 2000.  Why B wrong: 2015 is the MDG target year.  Why C wrong: 2030 is the SDG target year.  Why D wrong: 1978 is the declaration year, not the target."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The Alma Ata Declaration (1978) statement is:","options":["\"People's health in people's hands\"","\"Health is wealth\"","\"Prevention is better than cure\"","\"Health for none\""],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Alma Ata Declaration (1978) statement is \"People's health in people's hands.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These slogans are not the Alma Ata Declaration statement."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year did a WHO expert committee define the role of nurses in primary health care?","options":["1978","1984","1974","2000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A WHO expert committee in 1984 defined the role of nurses in primary health care.  Why A wrong: 1978 is the HFA / Alma Ata year.  Why C wrong: 1974 is the community health definition year.  Why D wrong: 2000 is the HFA target and MDG declaration year."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following are roles of nurses in primary health care as defined by the WHO expert committee (1984)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Assessing the health status of individuals and communities","Mobilizing community involvement","Maintaining epidemiological surveillance","Training and supervision of health workers","Setting national income tax policy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed roles include assessing health status, mobilizing community involvement, providing integrated care with referrals, maintaining epidemiological surveillance, training/supervising health workers, collaborating with the health team, and monitoring progress.  Why E wrong: Setting national income tax policy is not a nursing role in PHC."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Family Health Services include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Maternal and child health","Family planning","Nutrition","Road construction"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Family Health Services include Maternal and child health, Family planning, Nutrition and Health education — not road construction.  Why A, B, C wrong: These are listed components of Family Health Services."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The Millennium Development Goals (MDGs) were declared in which month and year?","options":["September 2000","January 2015","September 2015","December 2000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MDGs were declared in September 2000 (Bihar CHO-2019).  Why B, C wrong: 2015 is the target year for MDGs / declaration of SDGs.  Why D wrong: The month was September, not December."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The government set which target date to meet the Millennium Development Goals?","options":["2010","2015","2020","2030"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The government set 2015 as the date to meet the millennium development goals.  Why A, C wrong: 2010 and 2020 are not the MDG target.  Why D wrong: 2030 is the SDG target year."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"How many Millennium Development Goals (MDGs) were there in total?","options":["6","8","17","169"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There were a total of 8 MDGs.  Why A wrong: 6 is not the MDG count.  Why C wrong: 17 is the number of SDGs.  Why D wrong: 169 is the number of SDG targets."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following is Millennium Development Goal 1?","options":["Eradicate extreme poverty and hunger","Achieve universal primary education","Promote gender equality and empower women","Reduce child mortality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MDG 1 is \"Eradicate extreme poverty and hunger.\"  Why B wrong: This is MDG 2.  Why C wrong: This is MDG 3.  Why D wrong: This is MDG 4."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"MDG number 6 aimed to combat which of the following?","options":["HIV/AIDS, malaria and other diseases","Child mortality","Environmental degradation","Gender inequality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MDG 6 is \"Combat HIV/AIDS, malaria and other diseases.\"  Why B wrong: Reduce child mortality is MDG 4.  Why C wrong: Ensure environmental sustainability is MDG 7.  Why D wrong: Promote gender equality is MDG 3."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which MDG focused on improving maternal health?","options":["MDG 3","MDG 4","MDG 5","MDG 8"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: MDG 5 is \"Improve maternal health.\"  Why A wrong: MDG 3 is gender equality.  Why B wrong: MDG 4 is reduce child mortality.  Why D wrong: MDG 8 is develop a global partnership for development."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"MDG 8 aimed to:","options":["Develop a global partnership for development","Ensure environmental sustainability","Achieve universal primary education","Eradicate extreme poverty and hunger"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MDG 8 is \"Develop a global partnership for development.\"  Why B wrong: This is MDG 7.  Why C wrong: This is MDG 2.  Why D wrong: This is MDG 1."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"How many Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and targets were set by the United Nations?","options":["8 goals and 21 targets","17 goals and 169 targets","15 goals and 100 targets","17 goals and 200 targets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 17 goals and 169 targets set by the UN in 2015 for the year 2030.  Why A wrong: 8 goals refers to the MDGs.  Why C, D wrong: The correct numbers are 17 goals and 169 targets."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"In which year were the SDGs set, and for which target year?","options":["Set in 2000, target 2015","Set in 2015, target 2030","Set in 2015, target 2025","Set in 2010, target 2030"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SDGs were set by the UN in 2015 for the year 2030.  Why A wrong: That describes MDGs (2000-2015).  Why C, D wrong: The target year is 2030, set in 2015."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"What is another name for the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)?","options":["Global Goals for Sustainable Development","Millennium Goals","Alma Ata Goals","World Health Goals"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Another name for the SDGs is \"Global Goals for Sustainable Development.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the alternate name given for the SDGs."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which SDG is titled \"Good Health and Well-being\"?","options":["Goal 2","Goal 3","Goal 4","Goal 5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SDG Goal 3 is \"Good Health and Well-being\" — \"Ensure healthy lives and promote well-being for all at all ages\" (AIIMS Patna 2020).  Why A wrong: Goal 2 is Zero Hunger.  Why C wrong: Goal 4 is Quality Education.  Why D wrong: Goal 5 is Gender Equality."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"SDG Goal 1 \"No Poverty\" has the target: \"End poverty in all its forms everywhere.\" What is SDG Goal 2?","options":["Zero Hunger","Good Health and Well-being","Quality Education","Clean Water and Sanitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SDG Goal 2 is \"Zero Hunger\" — end hunger, achieve food security and improved nutrition, and promote sustainable agriculture.  Why B wrong: Good Health and Well-being is Goal 3.  Why C wrong: Quality Education is Goal 4.  Why D wrong: Clean Water and Sanitation is Goal 6."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which SDG addresses \"Clean Water and Sanitation\"?","options":["Goal 6","Goal 7","Goal 11","Goal 14"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SDG Goal 6 is \"Clean Water and Sanitation\" — ensure availability and sustainable management of water and sanitation for all.  Why B wrong: Goal 7 is Affordable and Clean Energy.  Why C wrong: Goal 11 is Sustainable Cities and Communities.  Why D wrong: Goal 14 is Life Below Water."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"SDG Goal 5 is:","options":["Gender Equality","Reducing Inequality","Quality Education","Decent Work and Economic Growth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SDG Goal 5 is \"Gender Equality\" — achieve gender equality and empower all women and girls.  Why B wrong: Reducing Inequality is Goal 10.  Why C wrong: Quality Education is Goal 4.  Why D wrong: Decent Work and Economic Growth is Goal 8."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which SDG is titled \"Climate Action\"?","options":["Goal 13","Goal 14","Goal 15","Goal 7"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SDG Goal 13 is \"Climate action\" — take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts.  Why B wrong: Goal 14 is Life Below Water.  Why C wrong: Goal 15 is Life On Land.  Why D wrong: Goal 7 is Affordable and Clean Energy."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"SDG Goal 14 \"Life Below Water\" is concerned with:","options":["Conserving oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development","Protecting terrestrial ecosystems and forests","Reducing income inequality","Building resilient infrastructure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Goal 14 target is to \"Conserve and sustainably use the oceans, seas and marine resources for sustainable development.\"  Why B wrong: That describes Goal 15 (Life On Land).  Why C wrong: That is Goal 10 (Reducing Inequality).  Why D wrong: That is Goal 9 (Industry, Innovation & Infrastructure)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"SDG Goal 15 \"Life On Land\" focuses on protecting terrestrial ecosystems, managing forests, and combating:","options":["Desertification and biodiversity loss","Marine pollution","Air pollution only","Poverty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Goal 15 target includes combating desertification, halting land degradation and reversing biodiversity loss.  Why B wrong: Marine issues are Goal 14.  Why C wrong: Goal 15 addresses land ecosystems broadly, not air pollution only.  Why D wrong: Poverty is Goal 1."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"SDG Goal 17 is titled:","options":["Partnerships for the Goals","Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions","Reducing Inequality","Responsible Consumption & Production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SDG Goal 17 is \"Partnerships for the Goals\" — strengthen the means of implementation and revitalize the global partnership for sustainable development.  Why B wrong: Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions is Goal 16.  Why C wrong: Reducing Inequality is Goal 10.  Why D wrong: Responsible Consumption & Production is Goal 12."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"According to the \"Special Note,\" which SDG goal numbers are stated to be very important for competitive exams?","options":["1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 17","8, 9, 10, 11, 12","13, 14, 15, 16","All 17 equally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Special Note states goals 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 17 are very very important for competitive exams.  Why B, C wrong: These ranges are not highlighted in the Special Note.  Why D wrong: The note singles out specific goals rather than all equally."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which level of prevention is described as preventive action taken for reducing risk factors of concerned diseases and is the best level for non-communicable diseases?","options":["Primordial level of prevention","Primary level of prevention","Secondary level of prevention","Tertiary level of prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primordial level of prevention reduces risk factors of concerned diseases and is the best level of prevention for non-communicable diseases (ESIC-2024).  Why B wrong: Primary prevention acts prior to disease onset via health promotion and specific protection.  Why C wrong: Secondary prevention is early diagnosis and treatment.  Why D wrong: Tertiary prevention limits impairment after disease has advanced."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The modes of intervention at the primordial level of prevention are:","options":["Individual education and mass education","Vaccination and quarantine","Early diagnosis and treatment","Disability limitation and rehabilitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Modes of intervention at primordial level are Individual Education and Mass Education.  Why B wrong: Vaccination/quarantine are specific protection (primary prevention).  Why C wrong: Early diagnosis and treatment is secondary prevention.  Why D wrong: Disability limitation and rehabilitation are tertiary prevention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Examples of primordial level prevention include:","options":["Prevention of bad food habits and preventing smoking & alcoholism","DOTS and MDT","Physiotherapy and use of crutches","Pap smear and mammography"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of primordial prevention include prevention of bad food habits and preventing smoking & alcoholism, best for non-communicable diseases.  Why B wrong: DOTS and MDT are examples of secondary prevention (treatment).  Why C wrong: Physiotherapy and crutches are tertiary prevention.  Why D wrong: Pap smear and mammography are secondary prevention (early diagnosis)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which level of prevention is applied when risk factors are present but disease has not yet taken place, and signifies intervention in the pre-pathogenesis phase?","options":["Primary level of prevention","Primordial level of prevention","Secondary level of prevention","Tertiary level of prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary prevention is action taken prior to onset of disease, applied when risk factors are present but disease has not occurred, in the pre-pathogenesis phase.  Why B wrong: Primordial acts even before risk factors develop.  Why C wrong: Secondary is early diagnosis/treatment in incipient stage.  Why D wrong: Tertiary is in the late pathogenesis phase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The two modes of intervention under primary level of prevention are Health promotion and:","options":["Specific protection","Early diagnosis","Rehabilitation","Disability limitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The modes of intervention under primary prevention are (A) Health promotion and (B) Specific Protection.  Why B wrong: Early diagnosis is secondary prevention.  Why C, D wrong: Rehabilitation and disability limitation are tertiary prevention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following is an example of \"Health promotion\" under primary prevention?","options":["Health education and environmental modifications","Vaccination","Pap smear","DOTS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Health promotion examples include health education, environmental modifications, nutritional interventions, lifestyle modification, avoiding smoking, personal hygiene, exercise & rest, water purification & sanitation, and routine hand washing.  Why B wrong: Vaccination is specific protection.  Why C wrong: Pap smear is secondary prevention.  Why D wrong: DOTS is secondary prevention (treatment)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following is an example of \"Specific Protection\" under primary prevention?","options":["Vaccination/immunization and Vitamin-A prophylaxis","Health education","Mammography","Physiotherapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Specific protection examples include contraceptive methods to prevent STDs, vaccination or immunization, PPEs, Vitamin-A prophylaxis, quarantine for communicable diseases, genetic counseling and surgical hand washing.  Why B wrong: Health education is health promotion.  Why C wrong: Mammography is secondary prevention (early diagnosis).  Why D wrong: Physiotherapy is tertiary prevention (disability limitation)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Surgical hand washing and quarantine for communicable diseases are examples of which primary prevention intervention?","options":["Specific protection","Health promotion","Early diagnosis","Rehabilitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Quarantine for communicable diseases and surgical hand washing are listed under Specific Protection.  Why B wrong: Routine hand washing is under health promotion; surgical hand washing is under specific protection.  Why C, D wrong: These belong to secondary and tertiary prevention respectively."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which level of prevention involves early diagnosis and treatment, including surgical procedures, and halts disease progress at its incipient stage?","options":["Secondary level of prevention","Primary level of prevention","Primordial level of prevention","Tertiary level of prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary prevention is early diagnosis and treatment including surgical procedures, halting disease progress at its incipient stage and avoiding complications.  Why B wrong: Primary prevention acts before disease onset.  Why C wrong: Primordial reduces risk factors before they develop.  Why D wrong: Tertiary is applied after disease has advanced."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"National Health Programs by the Government of India mostly operate at which level of prevention?","options":["Secondary level of prevention","Primordial level of prevention","Primary level of prevention","Tertiary level of prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states National Health Programs by Govt. of India mostly operate at the secondary level of prevention.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not where national health programs mostly operate per this page.  [Note: This is a book-specific claim. In standard public-health teaching (Park), India's national health programmes span multiple levels — many (immunization/UIP, nutrition, IEC/RMNCH+A) are primary prevention, while disease-control programmes (NTEP/TB, NLEP/leprosy, NACO/HIV) work at secondary prevention. Answer keyed to the source for exam fidelity.]"},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"According to the page, secondary prevention is an imperfect tool in control of transmission of disease because it is:","options":["More expensive and less effective than primary prevention","Cheaper and more effective than primary prevention","Only used for non-communicable diseases","Never used in national programs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary prevention is described as an imperfect tool, more expensive and less effective than primary prevention.  Why B wrong: It is the opposite — more expensive and less effective.  Why C wrong: It is important for communicable diseases like TB, leprosy and STDs.  Why D wrong: National programs mostly operate at secondary prevention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Secondary prevention is an important level of prevention for which of the following diseases?","options":["Tuberculosis, Leprosy and STDs","Diabetes and hypertension","Cancer only","Non-communicable diseases only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states secondary prevention is important for diseases like Tuberculosis, Leprosy and STDs.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the diseases listed on this page for secondary prevention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which of the following are examples of \"Early Diagnosis\" screening tests under secondary prevention? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pap smear","Colonoscopy","Mammography","Monthly BSE & TSE","Physiotherapy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Early diagnosis screening tests include pap smear, colonoscopy, mammography, monthly BSE & TSE, BMI checking, isolation for communicable diseases, sputum smear exam for AFB, C/S for MP, and all government health programs and surgical procedures.  Why E wrong: Physiotherapy is a tertiary prevention (disability limitation) intervention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"DOTS and MDT are given as examples of treatment under which level of prevention?","options":["Secondary level of prevention","Primary level of prevention","Primordial level of prevention","Tertiary level of prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under secondary prevention \"Treatment,\" the examples given are DOTS and MDT.  Why B, C, D wrong: DOTS (for TB) and MDT (for leprosy) are treatment measures at secondary prevention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Treatment under secondary prevention shortens the disease period, reduces mortality, and prevents:","options":["Occurrence of further (secondary) cases or long-term disability","Risk factor development","Adoption of harmful lifestyle in children","Environmental pollution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Treatment shortens the period, reduces mortality and prevents occurring of further cases (secondary cases) or any long-term disability.  Why B wrong: Reducing risk factors is primordial prevention.  Why C wrong: Discouraging harmful lifestyle in children is primordial prevention.  Why D wrong: Environmental pollution control is not the stated aim of treatment here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Which level of prevention is applied when the disease has advanced beyond early stages and signifies intervention in the late pathogenesis phase?","options":["Tertiary level of prevention","Secondary level of prevention","Primary level of prevention","Primordial level of prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tertiary prevention is applied when disease has advanced beyond early stages and signifies intervention in the late pathogenesis phase.  Why B wrong: Secondary is at the incipient stage.  Why C wrong: Primary is in the pre-pathogenesis phase.  Why D wrong: Primordial acts before risk factors develop."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The aim of tertiary level of prevention is to:","options":["Reduce or limit impairment and disabilities and minimize suffering","Reduce risk factors of disease","Diagnose disease early","Discourage children from harmful lifestyles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The aim of tertiary prevention is to reduce or limit impairment and disabilities and minimize suffering caused by existing departures from good health.  Why B wrong: Reducing risk factors is primordial/primary prevention.  Why C wrong: Early diagnosis is secondary prevention.  Why D wrong: Discouraging harmful lifestyles in children is primordial prevention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"The two modes of intervention under tertiary level of prevention are:","options":["Disability limitation and Rehabilitation","Health promotion and Specific protection","Early diagnosis and Treatment","Individual education and Mass education"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The tertiary prevention modes are Disability Limitation and Rehabilitation.  Why B wrong: These are primary prevention modes.  Why C wrong: These are secondary prevention modes.  Why D wrong: These are primordial prevention modes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"\"Disability Limitation\" under tertiary prevention prevents the transition of disease from impairment to handicap. Which is the example given?","options":["Physiotherapy","Vaccination","Pap smear","Health education"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Disability Limitation prevents the transition from impairment to handicap, with the example Physiotherapy.  Why B wrong: Vaccination is specific protection (primary).  Why C wrong: Pap smear is early diagnosis (secondary).  Why D wrong: Health education is health promotion (primary)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Rehabilitation, as a mode of tertiary prevention, is the training and retraining of an individual to the highest possible level of functional ability. Which of the following are examples? (Select all that apply)","options":["Crutches and walker","Learning to walk after amputation","Joint replacement","Palliative and hospice care","Pap smear screening"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Rehabilitation can be medical, vocational, social or psychological; examples include crutches, walker, learning to walk after amputation or joint replacement, and palliative/hospice care to support quality of life.  Why E wrong: Pap smear screening is secondary prevention (early diagnosis), not rehabilitation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to Community Health…","question":"Rehabilitation under tertiary prevention can be of which types?","options":["Medical, vocational, social or psychological","Only medical","Only surgical","Only pharmacological"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states rehabilitation can be medical, vocational, social or psychological.  Why B, C, D wrong: Rehabilitation is not limited to a single type; it spans medical, vocational, social and psychological forms."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Following traditional lifestyles to prevent coronary heart disease (CHD) is an example of which level of prevention?","options":["Primary prevention","Primordial prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Preventing the very emergence of risk factors by maintaining healthy traditional lifestyles is primordial prevention.  Why A wrong: Primary prevention acts after risk factors exist but before disease onset.  Why C wrong: Secondary prevention is early diagnosis and treatment.  Why D wrong: Tertiary prevention is disability limitation and rehabilitation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Prevention of emergence of risk factors falls under which level of prevention?","options":["Primordial prevention","Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primordial prevention specifically targets preventing the emergence/appearance of risk factors in the population.  Why B wrong: Primary prevention targets disease onset after risk factors exist.  Why C wrong: Secondary prevention is screening/early treatment.  Why D wrong: Tertiary prevention is rehabilitation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The best level of prevention for non-communicable diseases is:","options":["Primary","Secondary","Primordial","Tertiary"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For NCDs, primordial prevention (preventing risk-factor emergence) is considered the best level.  Why A wrong: Primary is second best, acting after risk factors appear.  Why B wrong: Secondary detects established disease early.  Why D wrong: Tertiary manages complications."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Salt restriction to prevent coronary artery disease is which level of prevention?","options":["Secondary","Tertiary","Primary","Primordial"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Salt restriction to prevent CAD emergence of risk factors is listed as primordial prevention.  Why A/B wrong: These involve established disease/complications.  Why C wrong: Primary prevention acts after risk factors exist."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Childhood obesity prevention and maintenance of normal body weight/lifestyle preservation are which level of prevention?","options":["Primordial prevention","Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Preventing obesity and preserving healthy lifestyle before risk factors appear is primordial prevention.  Why B wrong: Primary prevention is specific protection after risk factors exist.  Why C/D wrong: These deal with established disease."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Exclusive breast feeding and primitive nutritional habits are examples of which level of prevention?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Primordial","Tertiary"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: These are listed as primordial prevention (promoting healthy habits before risk factors emerge).  Why A wrong: Primary is specific protection/health promotion after risk factors.  Why B/D wrong: Deal with existing disease."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Immunization/vaccination and immunizing a child for measles are examples of which level of prevention?","options":["Primordial prevention","Primary prevention (specific protection)","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immunization is a classic example of specific protection under primary prevention.  Why A wrong: Primordial prevents risk-factor emergence, not specific protection.  Why C/D wrong: Deal with established disease."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Chemoprophylaxis and action taken prior to the onset of disease are examples of:","options":["Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Primary prevention","Primordial prevention"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Chemoprophylaxis and preventive action prior to disease onset are primary prevention.  Why A/B wrong: These act after disease has begun.  Why D wrong: Primordial prevents emergence of risk factors, not disease onset."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Pasteurization of milk is a type of which level of prevention?","options":["Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Primordial prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pasteurization (specific protection against infection) is primary prevention.  Why B/C wrong: Deal with established disease.  Why D wrong: Primordial deals with risk-factor emergence."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Contraception, wearing helmets while riding a bike, and quarantine are examples of which level of prevention?","options":["Primordial","Primary","Secondary","Tertiary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All three are listed as primary prevention measures (specific protection / prevention before disease onset).  Why A wrong: Primordial prevents risk-factor emergence.  Why C/D wrong: Deal with established disease."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Vitamin-A prophylaxis to a child, mosquito nets, de-fluoridation of water, and iodised salt in the iodine deficiency control program are which level of prevention?","options":["Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Primordial prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These are all primary prevention (specific protection) measures.  Why B/C wrong: Involve established disease detection/rehab.  Why D wrong: Primordial prevents risk-factor emergence."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Health promotion & education, offering a high-fiber diet to a client, and control of tobacco/alcohol/radiation protection are examples of which level of prevention?","options":["Primordial","Secondary","Primary","Tertiary"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Health promotion, dietary advice and hazard control are primary prevention.  Why A wrong: Primordial prevents emergence of risk factors.  Why B/D wrong: Deal with established disease."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Provision of 3 doses of OPV in early infancy is which level of prevention?","options":["Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Primordial prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Immunization such as OPV doses is specific protection under primary prevention.  Why B/C wrong: Concern established disease.  Why D wrong: Primordial prevents risk-factor emergence."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Fasting blood sugar and genetic counselling (premarital & preconception) for prevention of genetic/congenital disorders are which level of prevention?","options":["Secondary","Tertiary","Primary","Primordial"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book lists fasting blood sugar and premarital/preconception genetic counselling as primary prevention.  Why A wrong: Secondary is early diagnosis/treatment of existing disease.  Why B wrong: Tertiary is rehabilitation.  Why D wrong: Primordial prevents risk-factor emergence."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Isolation, screening of diseases, early diagnosis and treatment are examples of which level of prevention?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Primordial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isolation, screening, and early diagnosis/treatment are secondary prevention (halting disease progress).  Why A wrong: Primary is before disease onset.  Why C wrong: Tertiary is rehabilitation.  Why D wrong: Primordial prevents risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Pap smear test, DOTS/MDT & sputum smear exams, and breast self-examination (BSE)/testicular self-examination (TSE) are which level of prevention?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Primordial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Screening-type activities (Pap smear, DOTS/MDT, sputum smear, BSE/TSE) are secondary prevention (early detection).  Why A wrong: Primary is before onset.  Why C/D wrong: Rehab / risk-factor prevention respectively."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"All National Health Programs are operated at which level of prevention?","options":["Primordial prevention","Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book states all national health programs are operated at the secondary level of prevention.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the level attributed to national health programs in this list."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Smoking cessation after a heart attack comes under which level of prevention?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Primordial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Since a cardiac event (disease) has occurred, cessation to prevent recurrence/further events is termed secondary prevention (the standard cardiology usage the source follows).  Why A wrong: Primary would be smoking cessation before any disease.  Why C wrong: Tertiary is rehabilitation of established disability.  Why D wrong: Primordial prevents risk-factor emergence.  [AMBIGUOUS: In the epidemiological Leavell & Clark / Park \"levels of prevention\" framework, intervening after established disease (post-MI) can be classed as tertiary prevention; in clinical cardiology and this source it is \"secondary prevention\" (prevention of recurrent events). Source and cardiology usage support B, which is retained.]"},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Disability limitation and rehabilitation are examples of which level of prevention?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Primordial"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Disability limitation and rehabilitation are the two core components of tertiary prevention.  Why A/B/D wrong: These occur before or at earlier stages of disease."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Physiotherapy following stroke, use of crutches/walkers, weight reduction after stroke, and blood-sugar-lowering medicines for diabetes come under which level of prevention?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Primordial","Tertiary"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: These manage established chronic disease/disability, i.e., tertiary prevention.  Why A/C wrong: Act before disease/risk factors.  Why B wrong: Secondary is early detection/treatment."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The Alma Ata conference that gave rise to the concept of Primary Health Care was held in which year?","options":["1974","1978","1980","1948"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Alma Ata (Kazakhstan) conference was held in 1978, after which primary health care came into action.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the Alma Ata conference year."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In which country was the Alma Ata conference on Primary Health Care held?","options":["Russia","Switzerland","Kazakhstan","Uzbekistan"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book specifies Alma Ata is in Kazakhstan.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the location named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the five principles of Primary Health Care as listed?","options":["Equitable distribution","Community participation","Intersectoral coordination","Curative dominance"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The five principles are equitable distribution, community participation, intersectoral coordination, appropriate technology, and focus on prevention — \"curative dominance\" is not among them.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are listed principles of PHC."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which of the following is a principle of Primary Health Care that emphasizes using suitable, affordable methods?","options":["Focus on prevention","Appropriate technology","Equitable distribution","Community participation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Appropriate technology\" is listed as a PHC principle.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other, distinct PHC principles."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"How many essential components of primary health care were put forward by the Alma Ata declaration?","options":["6","7","8","12"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Alma Ata declaration put forward 8 essential components of primary health care.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the source's figure of 8."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which of the following is one of the 8 essential elements of primary health care listed from the Alma Ata declaration? (Select all that apply)","options":["Education about prevailing health problems and how to prevent/control them","Adequate supply of safe water and basic sanitation","Immunisation against infectious diseases","Provision of specialist surgical services","Essential drugs"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Health education, safe water/sanitation, immunisation, and essential drugs are all among the 8 elements.  Why D wrong: Specialist surgical services are not among the 8 elements; the elements are focused on basic primary care."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which set correctly represents elements of primary health care from the Alma Ata declaration?","options":["Food supply/proper nutrition; Maternal and child health & family planning; Prevention and control of endemic diseases; Treatment of common infections","Cardiac catheterization; Dialysis; Chemotherapy; Radiotherapy","ICU care; Organ transplant; Neurosurgery; Bariatric surgery","Cosmetic surgery; Fertility IVF; Robotic surgery; Genetic editing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Food supply/nutrition, MCH & family planning, prevention/control of endemic diseases, and treatment of common infections are all among the 8 Alma Ata elements.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are tertiary/specialist services, not the basic primary care elements listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"According to the revised IPHS guidelines, in what year were the Indian Public Health Standards revised for the Health Care System classification of HWCs discussed here?","options":["2018","2020","2022","2015"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book refers to Indian Public Health Standards Revised Guidelines-2022 (IPHS 2022).  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the revision year cited."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"As per IPHS 2022, Health and Wellness Centres are classified into which two broad categories?","options":["Primary Health Centre and Sub Health Centre","District Hospital and Medical College","CHC and PHC only","Urban PHC and Rural PHC only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IPHS 2022 classifies HWCs as HWC-Primary Health Centre and HWC-Sub Health Centre.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the two-category HWC classification."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In HWC-PHC classification, HWC-PHC is in rural areas and which type is in urban areas?","options":["HWC-DH","HWC-UPHC","HWC-CHC","HWC-Polyclinic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HWC-PHC is rural and HWC-UPHC is the urban primary health centre form.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the urban HWC-PHC form named."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"HWC-SHC\" stand for?","options":["Health & Wellness Centre: Secondary Health Centre","Health & Wellness Centre: Sub-Health Centre","Hospital Wellness Centre: Sub-Health Care","Health Welfare Committee: Sub-Health Centre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The full form is Health & Wellness Centre: Sub-Health Centre.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which types of care do HWCs deliver for a wider range of services close to communities?","options":["Only curative care","Only preventive care","Preventive, Promotive, Curative, Rehabilitative and Palliative care","Only diagnostic services"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: HWCs deliver Preventive, Promotive, Curative, Rehabilitative and Palliative care.  Why A/B/D wrong: HWCs provide an expanded, comprehensive range, not a single type of care."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The Annual Health Calendar Day of 30th April is celebrated as:","options":["World Health Day","Ayushman Bharat – Health and Wellness Centre Day (Ayushman Bharat Diwas)","National Immunization Day","Nurses' Day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 30th April is celebrated as Ayushman Bharat – Health and Wellness Centre Day / Ayushman Bharat Diwas.  Why A/C/D wrong: These fall on other dates and are not the 30th April observance cited."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"What is the population norm for establishing a new HWC-SHC in a plain area?","options":["3000 population","5000 population","10000 population","30000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: New HWC established criteria in plain area is 5000 populations.  Why A wrong: 3000 is for hilly/desert/tribal areas.  Why C/D wrong: Not the SHC plain-area norm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"What is the population norm for establishing a new HWC-SHC in a hilly/desert/tribal area?","options":["5000","3000","15000","20000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hilly/desert/tribal areas the norm is 3000 populations.  Why A wrong: 5000 is for plain areas.  Why C/D wrong: These relate to U-HWC/PHC norms, not SHC hilly."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"According to the Guidelines for the National Free Diagnostic Initiative, how many investigations are available at HWC-SHC?","options":["7 investigations","19 investigations","12 investigations","30 investigations"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At HWC-SHC, 7 investigations are available.  Why B wrong: 19 investigations are available at HWC-PHC.  Why C/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"How many diagnostic investigations are available at HWC-PHC under the National Free Diagnostic Initiative?","options":["7","12","19","22"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: At HWC-PHC, 19 investigations are available.  Why A wrong: 7 is for HWC-SHC.  Why B/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"School teachers nominated under the Ayushman Bharat scheme are known as:","options":["Health Educators","Ayushman Ambassadors / Health and Wellness Ambassadors","ASHA facilitators","Community mobilizers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: School teachers are nominated as Ayushman Ambassadors or Health and Wellness Ambassadors.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the designation given to nominated school teachers."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The monthly incentive amount for the MLHP (CHO) in each HWC-SHC team is:","options":["Rs. 5,000/month","Rs. 3,000/month","Rs. 15,000/month","Rs. 1,500/month"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The monthly incentive is Rs. 15,000/MLHP(CHO)/month.  Why A wrong: Rs. 5,000 is for ASHAs.  Why B wrong: Rs. 3,000 is for MPWs.  Why D wrong: Rs. 1,500 is the maximum limit for MPW."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The monthly incentive for MPWs in the HWC-SHC team is Rs. 3,000/month, subject to a maximum of:","options":["Rs. 1,000/month","Rs. 1,500/month","Rs. 5,000/month","Rs. 3,000/month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rs. 3000/month for MPWs subject to a maximum of Rs. 1500/month/MPW.  Why A wrong: Rs. 1000 is the ASHA maximum.  Why C/D wrong: Not the MPW maximum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The monthly incentive for ASHAs in the HWC-SHC team is Rs. 5,000/month, subject to a maximum of:","options":["Rs. 1,500/ASHA","Rs. 3,000/ASHA","Rs. 1,000/ASHA","Rs. 5,000/ASHA"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Rs. 5000/month for ASHAs subject to a maximum of Rs. 1000/month/ASHA.  Why A wrong: Rs. 1500 is the MPW maximum.  Why B/D wrong: Not the ASHA maximum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The Emergency Medicine Kit provided at HWC includes which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inj. Adrenaline","Inj. Hydrocortisone","Inj. Dexamethasone","Inj. Insulin","Glyceryl trinitrate – Sublingual tablet 0.5 mg"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The kit includes Inj. Adrenaline, Inj. Hydrocortisone, Inj. Dexamethasone, and Glyceryl trinitrate sublingual 0.5 mg.  Why D wrong: Insulin is not listed in the HWC emergency medicine kit."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"What is the strength of the Glyceryl trinitrate sublingual tablet in the HWC Emergency Medicine Kit?","options":["0.5 mg","5 mg","0.25 mg","1 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The kit lists Glyceryl trinitrate – Sublingual tablet 0.5 mg.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the strength given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Per the Population Norms table, the plain-area population for a U-HWC is:","options":["5000","3000","15,000–20,000","30,000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: U-HWC plain population norm is 15,000–20,000.  Why A wrong: 5000 is for HWC-SHC plain.  Why B wrong: 3000 is hilly/tribal/desert for HWC-SHC.  Why D wrong: 30,000 is Rural PHC norm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Staffing Pattern at HWC-SHC (ESIC-2024), how many Community Health Officers (CHO/MLHP) are required?","options":["1","2","1 Male","3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The staffing pattern requires 1 CHO/MLHP.  Why B/D wrong: Not the required number.  Why C wrong: \"1 Male\" applies to the Male-MPHW, not CHO."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the HWC-SHC staffing pattern, the ASHA norm in a plain area is:","options":["1 ASHA per 500 population","1 ASHA per 1000 population","1 ASHA per 2000 population","1 ASHA per habitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 ASHA per 1000 population in plain area is specified.  Why A/C wrong: Not the plain-area norm.  Why D wrong: \"1 ASHA per habitation\" applies to tribal, hilly and desert areas."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In tribal, hilly and desert areas the ASHA staffing norm at HWC-SHC is:","options":["1 ASHA per 1000 population","1 ASHA per habitation","1 ASHA per 2000 population","1 ASHA per 5000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In tribal/hilly/desert areas the norm is 1 ASHA per habitation.  Why A wrong: Per-1000 is for plain areas.  Why C/D wrong: Not the norm given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The HWC team at SHCs and PHC works under the overall supervision of which officer?","options":["District Medical Officer","Block Medical Officer (BMO)-In Charge","Chief Medical Officer","Civil Surgeon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The HWC team works under the overall supervision of the Block Medical Officer (BMO)-In Charge.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not named as the direct supervisor of the HWC team."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In urban areas (other than U-PHC staff), the team consists of the MPW-F for how much population?","options":["5,000 population","10,000 population","2000–2500 population","15,000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In urban areas the MPW-F is for 10,000 population, and ASHAs one per 2000–2500 population.  Why A/D wrong: Not the MPW-F norm.  Why C wrong: 2000–2500 is the urban ASHA norm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"What does \"U-HWC\" stand for?","options":["Urban Health & Wellness Centre","Universal Health & Wellness Centre","Union Health & Welfare Committee","Urban Hospital & Wellness Centre"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: U-HWC full form is Urban Health & Wellness Centre.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"One Urban-HWC caters to which population range, predominantly serving poor and vulnerable populations in slums?","options":["5,000–10,000","15,000–20,000","30,000–50,000","50,000–1,00,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One Urban-HWC per 15,000–20,000 population caters to poor and vulnerable populations in slums.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the U-HWC catchment range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Staffing Pattern at U-HWC, which of the following personnel are each required in a quantity of \"1\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["Medical Officer","Staff Nurse","Male-Multi Purpose Worker (MPW)","Sanitary staff","Security Staff"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The U-HWC staffing table lists Medical Officer, Staff Nurse, MPW, Sanitary staff, and Security Staff each as 1.  Why none excluded: All five listed posts have required number 1 in the table."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the U-HWC staffing pattern, the ASHA norm is:","options":["1 ASHA per 1000 population","1 ASHA per 2000 population","1 ASHA per habitation","1 ASHA per 5000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The U-HWC staffing pattern specifies 1 ASHA per 2000 population.  Why A wrong: Per-1000 applies to rural HWC-SHC plain areas.  Why C wrong: Per-habitation applies to tribal/hilly/desert.  Why D wrong: Not the norm given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the U-HWC staffing pattern, the Female Health Worker/ANM norm is one per:","options":["5,000 population","10,000 population","15,000 population","2,000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Female Health Worker/ANM is 1 per 10,000 population at U-HWC.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the ANM norm given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"What is the total number of essential services listed at Health & Wellness Centres (HWCs)?","options":["8","10","12","14"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The list of essential services at HWCs totals 12.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the total number."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which of the following is one of the 12 essential services at HWCs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Care in Pregnancy and Child-birth","Neonatal and Infant Health Care Services","Family Planning, Contraceptives services & other Reproductive Health Care Services","Cardiac bypass surgery","Basic Oral Health Care Services"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnancy/childbirth care, neonatal/infant care, family planning/reproductive care, and basic oral health care are among the 12 essential services.  Why D wrong: Cardiac bypass surgery is a tertiary specialist service, not an HWC essential service."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Screening, prevention, control and management of Non-Communicable diseases at HWCs is provided for which age group?","options":["Above 18 years","Above 25 years","Above 30 years","Above 40 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NCD screening/management is for the above 30 years age group.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the age cutoff given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which of the following are among the 12 essential services at HWCs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Elderly and Palliative health care services","Management of Communicable diseases including National Health Programs","Care for Eye, Ear & Nose and Throat problems","Emergency Medical Services including burns & trauma","Screening and Basic Management of Mental Health problems"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five (elderly/palliative, communicable disease management, ENT/eye care, emergency services with burns/trauma, and mental health screening) are listed among the 12 essential services.  Why none excluded: Each corresponds to services #6–#12 in the list."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Under the Ayushman Bharat Program, the Rogi Kalyan Samiti at AB-HWCs (PHC/SCH level) is being reformed as:","options":["Village Health Sanitation & Nutrition Committee","Jan Arogya Samiti (JAS)","District Health Society","Mahila Arogya Samiti"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rogi Kalyan Samiti at AB-HWCs is being reformed as Jan Arogya Samiti (JAS).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are separate committees/bodies at other levels."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Under Ayushman Bharat, how much untied fund is provided to the Sub-Health Centre (SHC) level AB-HWCs?","options":["Rs. 10,000","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 1,75,000","Rs. 25,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SHC-level AB-HWCs are provided Rs. 50,000 as untied fund (previously Rs. 10,000 under the minimum basic need program).  Why A wrong: Rs. 10,000 was the previous amount.  Why C wrong: Rs. 1,75,000 is for PHC-level AB-HWCs.  Why D wrong: Not a value given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The annual untied fund provided for PHC-level AB-HWCs under Ayushman Bharat is:","options":["Rs. 50,000","Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 1,75,000","Rs. 2,00,000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Rs. 1,75,000 annual untied fund is provided for PHC-level AB-HWCs.  Why A wrong: Rs. 50,000 is for SHC level.  Why B/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"What is the essential quorum for the monthly meeting of the Jan Arogya Samiti (JAS)?","options":["25% of members","33% of members","50% of members","75% of members"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The essential quorum for the monthly JAS meeting is 50% of the members of the committee.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the quorum figure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the composition of the Jan Arogya Samiti, what proportion should be women members, with SCs, STs and minorities well represented?","options":["At least 25% women, 1/4 minorities","At least 50% women, minorities well (1/3) represented","At least 33% women, 1/2 minorities","At least 75% women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At least 50% should be women members and SCs, STs and minorities should be well (1/3) represented.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the proportions specified."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Jan Arogya Samiti is also known as:","options":["Village Health Committee","People's Health Committee","Community Welfare Board","Rogi Kalyan Samiti"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: JAS is also known as \"People's Health Committee\".  Why A/C wrong: Not the alternative name given.  Why D wrong: Rogi Kalyan Samiti is the earlier body being reformed into JAS."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the composition of the Jan Arogya Samiti at SHC level, who is the Chairperson?","options":["Medical Officer of PHC","Panchayat Sarpanch","CHO I/C HWC","Jila Parishad Member"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At SHC level, the Chairperson is the Panchayat Sarpanch.  Why A wrong: Medical Officer of PHC is the Co-Chair at SHC level.  Why C wrong: CHO I/C HWC is the Member Secretary at SHC level.  Why D wrong: Jila Parishad Member is the Chairperson at PHC level."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Jan Arogya Samiti at SHC level, who is the Member Secretary?","options":["Panchayat Sarpanch","Medical Officer of PHC","CHO I/C HWC","Block Medical Officer"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: At SHC level the Member Secretary is CHO I/C HWC.  Why A wrong: Panchayat Sarpanch is Chairperson.  Why B wrong: Medical Officer of PHC is Co-Chair.  Why D wrong: Block Medical Officer is Co-Chair at PHC level."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Jan Arogya Samiti at PHC level, who is the Chairperson and who is the Co-Chair respectively?","options":["Panchayat Sarpanch; Medical Officer of PHC","Jila Parishad Member; Block Medical Officer","Block Medical Officer; Jila Parishad Member","CHO I/C HWC; Medical Officer I/C PHC"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At PHC level, Chairperson is Jila Parishad Member and Co-Chair is Block Medical Officer.  Why A wrong: That is the SHC-level pairing.  Why C wrong: Roles are reversed.  Why D wrong: These are the Member Secretary roles, not chair roles."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Jan Arogya Samiti at PHC level, who is the Member Secretary?","options":["CHO I/C HWC","Medical Officer I/C PHC","Panchayat Sarpanch","Jila Parishad Member"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At PHC level the Member Secretary is Medical Officer I/C PHC.  Why A wrong: CHO I/C HWC is Member Secretary at SHC level.  Why C/D wrong: These are chair roles, not member secretary."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Institution for Effective Health Planning pyramid, which committee operates at the Village level (Rural)?","options":["MAS (Mahila Arogya Samiti)","VHSNC","Jan Arogya Samiti","Rogi Kalyan Samiti"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At the Village level (Rural), the VHSNC operates; at the Slum level (Urban), the MAS operates.  Why A wrong: MAS is at slum (urban) level.  Why C wrong: JAS is at AB-HWCs (PHC/SCH) facility level.  Why D wrong: Rogi Kalyan Samiti is at CHC/DH level."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the health planning pyramid, which body is at the District level?","options":["State Health Society","District Health Society","Rogi Kalyan Samiti","Jan Arogya Samiti"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The District level body is the District Health Society (State level is State Health Society).  Why A wrong: State Health Society is at state level.  Why C wrong: Rogi Kalyan Samiti is at CHC/DH facility level.  Why D wrong: JAS is at AB-HWCs PHC/SCH level."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The established criteria (population norm) for a Rural HWC-PHC in a plain area is:","options":["20,000 population","30,000 population","50,000 population","5,000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rural HWC-PHC established criteria in plain area is 30,000 population.  Why A wrong: 20,000 is for hill/tribal area.  Why C wrong: 50,000 is for urban HWC-PHC.  Why D wrong: 5,000 is for HWC-SHC plain."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The established population norm for a Rural HWC-PHC in a hill/tribal area is:","options":["30,000","20,000","3,000","50,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rural HWC-PHC in hill/tribal area is 20,000 population.  Why A wrong: 30,000 is plain-area rural PHC.  Why C/D wrong: Not the hill/tribal rural PHC norm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Bed capacity at a routine (non-24x7) Rural HWC-PHC and at a 24x7 HWC-PHC respectively are:","options":["2 beds; 6 beds","6 beds; 2 beds","4 beds; 6 beds","2 beds; 4 beds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bed capacity at HWC-PHCs is 2 beds, and at 24x7 HWC-PHCs is 6 beds.  Why B wrong: Values reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the bed figures given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The total staff at a routine Rural HWC-PHC and at a 24x7 Rural HWC-PHC respectively are:","options":["12 staff; 23 staff","16 staff; 26 staff","23 staff; 12 staff","12 staff; 16 staff"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Total staff at HWC-PHCs is 12 and at 24x7 HWC-PHCs is 23.  Why B wrong: 16/26 are for urban HWC-PHCs.  Why C wrong: Values reversed.  Why D wrong: Incorrect figures."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The established criteria (population) for an Urban HWC-PHC is:","options":["30,000","50,000","20,000","1,20,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Established criteria for Urban HWC-PHCs is 50,000 population.  Why A wrong: 30,000 is rural PHC plain.  Why C wrong: 20,000 is rural PHC hill/tribal.  Why D wrong: 1,20,000 is CHC plain-area norm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Bed capacity at a routine Urban HWC-PHC and at a 24x7 Urban HWC-PHC respectively are:","options":["2 day care beds; 6 beds","6 beds; 2 day care beds","4 beds; 6 beds","2 beds; 4 beds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urban HWC-PHCs have 2 day care beds, and 24x7 urban HWC-PHCs have 6 beds.  Why B wrong: Values reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The total staff at a routine Urban HWC-PHC and at a 24x7 Urban HWC-PHC respectively are:","options":["12 staff; 23 staff","16 staff; 26 staff","26 staff; 16 staff","16 staff; 23 staff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total staff at urban HWC-PHCs is 16, and at 24x7 urban HWC-PHCs is 26.  Why A wrong: 12/23 are for rural HWC-PHCs.  Why C wrong: Values reversed.  Why D wrong: Incorrect combination."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Rural HWC-PHCs are the first contact between the village community and which health provider?","options":["ASHA","ANM","Doctor (Medical Officer)","CHO"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Rural HWC-PHCs are first contact between village community and the doctor (M.O.).  Why A/B/D wrong: SHC-level workers are contact points at the sub-centre, not the PHC doctor-contact described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"HWC-PHC would also serve as the first point of referral for which centres in its jurisdiction?","options":["District hospitals","All the SHC-HWCs","Medical colleges","Private clinics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HWC-PHC serves as the first point of referral for all the SHC-HWCs in its jurisdiction.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the referral relationship described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Under Ayushman Bharat, Community Health Officers (CHOs) are described as:","options":["Physician specialists","A new cadre of non-physician health workers","Traditional healers","Retired doctors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India under Ayushman Bharat, CHOs are a new cadre of non-physician health workers.  Why A/C/D wrong: CHOs are non-physician health workers, not specialists/healers."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Community Health Officers (CHOs) are also known as:","options":["Multi-Purpose Health Worker (MPHW)","Mid-Level Health Provider (MLHP)","Accredited Social Health Activist","Lady Health Visitor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CHOs are also known as Mid-Level Health Provider (MLHP).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different community health worker cadres."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"CHOs are posted as in-charge at which centre, formerly known as Sub-Centre?","options":["Community Health Centre","Ayushman Arogya Mandir (HWC)","District Hospital","Primary Health Centre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CHOs are posted at Ayushman Arogya Mandir (HWC), formerly known as Sub-Centre, as in charge.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the centre where CHOs are posted as described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"CHOs lead the primary care team at the HWC comprising which of the following members?","options":["MPW (F), MPF (M) and ASHA","Only ASHA","Only ANM","Surgeons and physicians"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CHOs lead the primary care team consisting of MPW (F), MPF (M) and ASHA.  Why B/C wrong: The team includes multiple cadres, not a single worker.  Why D wrong: The HWC team is non-specialist primary care staff."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Under the clinical functions of the CHO, how many essential health services should be provided during early detection, screening and first-level management?","options":["8 essential health services","10 essential health services","12 essential health services","14 essential health services"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The CHO provides 12 essential health services under early detection/screening/first-level management.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the number given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"CHOs undertake referral to enable continuum of care, providing referral to high-risk cases, complications, medical emergencies, trauma, and secondary/tertiary services under which scheme?","options":["PMJDY","PM-JAY","RSBY","JSSK"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Referral services for high-risk/complications/emergencies etc. are provided under PM-JAY.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different government schemes not cited here for referrals."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Under public health functions, a CHO should organize at least how many health promotion campaigns in a year on different themes?","options":["12","24","30","52"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The CHO should organize at least 30 health promotion campaigns in a year on different themes.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the number specified."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which of the following are managerial functions of the CHO for efficient functioning of HWCs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Recording, reporting and monitoring of service delivery","Undertake administrative functions of HWCs","Supportive Supervision of HWC Team","Performing major surgeries","Disease surveillance of outbreaks"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Recording/reporting/monitoring, administrative functions, and supportive supervision of the HWC team are the managerial functions.  Why D wrong: Major surgery is not a CHO function.  Why E wrong: Disease surveillance is a public health function, not a managerial one."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In urban areas, one specialist polyclinic (Specialist U-PHC/Polyclinic) is established for every:","options":["50,000–1,00,000 urban population","2.5 to 3 lac urban population","5 lac urban population","1 lac urban population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In urban areas, one polyclinic is established every 2.5 to 3 lac urban population.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the polyclinic norm given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"A specialist polyclinic provides fixed-day rotational multispecialty OPD for a minimum of how many specialties?","options":["Four specialties","Five specialties","Six specialties","Ten specialties"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fixed day rotational multispecialty OPD is provided for a minimum of six specialties.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the minimum number given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In an urban area, one polyclinic covers a population of how many U-PHCs?","options":["2–3 U-PHCs","5–6 U-PHCs","8–10 U-PHCs","10–12 U-PHCs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In urban areas, one polyclinic covered a population of 5–6 U-PHCs.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the coverage given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Population norm for HWC-PHC table, the plain population and hilly/tribal population for a Rural PHC respectively are:","options":["30,000 and 20,000","50,000 and 30,000","20,000 and 30,000","30,000 and 50,000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rural PHC plain population is 30,000 and hilly/tribal is 20,000.  Why B/D wrong: Incorrect figures.  Why C wrong: Values reversed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Population norm for HWC-PHC table, the plain population for a Polyclinic is:","options":["30,000","50,000","2,50,000–3,00,000 (2.5–3 Lac)","5,00,000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Polyclinic plain population is 2,50,000–3,00,000 (2.5–3 Lac).  Why A wrong: 30,000 is Rural PHC.  Why B wrong: 50,000 is Urban PHC.  Why D wrong: 5 lac relates to metro U-CHC."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" notes, District and Sub-District Hospitals will provide which type of care services?","options":["Primary care services","Secondary care services","Only rehabilitative services","Only preventive services"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: District and Sub-District Hospitals will provide secondary care services.  Why A wrong: Primary care is provided by HWCs (Sub-Centres and PHCs).  Why C/D wrong: These are only components, not the described level."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"A Community Health Centre (CHC) should be established at which level?","options":["Village/Gram Panchayat level","Community Development Block/Taluka/Tehsil/Circle Level","District/State level","Ward/Slum level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A CHC should be established at the Community Development Block/Taluka/Tehsil/Circle Level.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the level specified for CHC."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The established criteria (population norm) for a CHC in a plain area and in a hilly/tribal area respectively are:","options":["80,000 and 1,20,000","1,20,000 and 80,000","30,000 and 20,000","1,00,000 and 50,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CHC established criteria: plain area 1,20,000 population, hilly/tribal area 80,000 population.  Why A wrong: Values reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect figures."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"How many essential beds will Non-FRU CHCs in rural areas have?","options":["30 essential beds","50 essential beds","6 beds","100 beds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Non-FRU CHCs in rural areas will have 30 essential beds.  Why B wrong: 50 beds relate to certain FRU CHCs.  Why C/D wrong: Not the Non-FRU CHC figure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"FRU CHCs in rural areas will have how many beds?","options":["30 beds only","30 beds and 50 beds","50 beds and 100 beds","6 beds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: FRU CHCs in rural areas will have 30 beds and 50 beds (two configurations).  Why A wrong: That is only one configuration.  Why C wrong: 100 beds relate to urban FRU U-CHC.  Why D wrong: Incorrect figure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In a 30-bed FRU CHC in rural areas, which services are essential; and in a 50-bed FRU CHC, which additional services are desirable?","options":["Essential: maternity & surgical; Desirable: ophthalmic, orthopaedic, and ENT","Essential: ophthalmic; Desirable: maternity","Essential: ENT; Desirable: cardiac","Essential: dental; Desirable: dermatology"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 30-bed FRU CHCs maternity and surgical services are essential; in a 50-bed FRU CHC additional ophthalmic, orthopaedic and ENT services are desirable.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"The U-CHC in urban areas will function only as:","options":["Non-FRU UCHCs","FRU UCHCs","Primary care centres","Day care centres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The U-CHC in urban areas will function only as FRU UCHCs.  Why A wrong: Non-FRU is the rural option.  Why C/D wrong: U-CHC is a secondary care referral centre, not primary/day care."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"A U-CHC in urban areas is set up as a secondary care referral centre in metro cities with what population?","options":["2.5 lakh and above","5 lakh and above","1 lakh and above","10 lakh and above"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In metro cities the U-CHC serves a population of 5 lakh and above; in non-metro cities 2.5 lakh.  Why A wrong: 2.5 lakh is for non-metro cities.  Why C/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Established criteria of U-CHC in metro cities is 500,000 population and above with bed strength of:","options":["50 beds","100 beds","30 beds","200 beds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In metro cities (5 lakh and above), U-CHC bed strength is 100 beds.  Why A wrong: 50 beds is for non-metro cities.  Why C/D wrong: Not the metro U-CHC bed strength."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Established criteria of U-CHC in non-metro cities is 250,000 population and above with bed strength of:","options":["100 beds","50 beds","30 beds","6 beds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In non-metro cities (2.5 lakh and above), U-CHC bed strength is 50 beds.  Why A wrong: 100 beds is for metro cities.  Why C/D wrong: Not the non-metro U-CHC bed strength."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"For FRU UCHCs of 50 beds, which services will be essential in all the cities?","options":["Only diagnostic services","Maternity and surgical services","Only OPD services","Only dental services"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For FRU UCHCs 50 beds, maternity and surgical services will be essential in all the cities.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the essential services described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"For FRU U-CHCs of 100 beds, which additional services are desirable?","options":["Cardiac and neurosurgical services","Additional ophthalmic and orthopaedic services","Only dental services","Only psychiatric services"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For FRU U-CHCs 100 beds, additional ophthalmic and orthopaedic services will be desirable.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the desirable additions described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Service area table, the number of emergency beds (24x7 Emergency care) at a Non-FRU CHC (30 beds) and at a 30-bed FRU CHC (Rural) respectively are:","options":["4 beds; 4 beds","6 beds; 6 beds","4 beds; 6 beds","11 beds; 4 beds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Emergency care beds are 4 for both Non-FRU CHC (30 beds) and 30-bed FRU CHC (Rural).  Why B wrong: 6 beds applies to 50-bed FRU configurations.  Why C/D wrong: Do not match the table rows."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Service area table, the number of emergency beds at a 100-bed FRU UPHC (Urban) is:","options":["4 beds","6 beds","11 beds","3 beds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For the 100-bed FRU UPHC (Urban), emergency care is 11 beds.  Why A wrong: 4 beds is for 30-bed configurations.  Why B wrong: 6 beds is for 50-bed configurations.  Why D wrong: 3 relates to labour room complex, not emergency beds."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Service area table, the number of laboratory tests (Number of lab tests) is the same across all CHC/UPHC configurations at:","options":["30","50","97","100"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The number of lab tests (Laboratory Services) is 97 for Non-FRU, FRU CHC (30/50 beds) and FRU UPHC (50/100 beds).  Why A/B/D wrong: These are bed-capacity figures, not the lab-test count."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In the Population Norms for Rural & Urban CHC table, the population norms for a 50-bed FRU UPHC (Urban) and a 100-bed FRU UPHC (Urban) respectively are:","options":["2.5 Lac and 5 Lac","5 Lac and 2.5 Lac","80,000 and 1,20,000","1,20,000 and 80,000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For FRU UPHC (Urban), the 50-bed norm is 2.5 Lac Population and the 100-bed norm is 5 Lac Population.  Why B wrong: Values reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These are CHC hill-tribal/plain norms."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"In rural areas, a Community Health Centre can be either:","options":["FRU or Non-FRU","Primary or Secondary","Public or Private","Metro or Non-metro"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CHCs in rural areas can be either non-FRU CHC or FRU CHC.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the classification described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"Which infrastructure characterizes FRU (First Referral Unit) CHCs distinguishing them from Non-FRU CHCs?","options":["Only outpatient department","Functional operation theatre and blood storage unit","Only immunization clinic","Only laboratory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: FRU CHCs provide specialist services with a Functional operation theatre and blood storage unit.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not constitute the defining FRU infrastructure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Levels of prevention with examples, Primary Health Care…","question":"For metros and million-plus cities, the UCHCs are established at what population and bed strength?","options":["2.5 lakh, 50 bedded","5 lakh population, 100 bedded facilities","1 lakh, 30 bedded","10 lakh, 200 bedded"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For metros and million-plus cities, the UCHCs are established at 5 lakh population and are 100-bedded facilities.  Why A wrong: 2.5 lakh / 50 beds is for non-metro cities.  Why C/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per the \"Also Remember\" note in IPHS guidelines, all CHCs at block headquarters level (in rural and urban areas) are to be developed as which of the following?","options":["First Referral Units (FRU)","Block Public Health Units (BPHU)","Maternal and Child Health centres","District Public Health Units"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states all CHCs at block headquarters level (rural and urban) are to be developed as Block Public Health Units (BPHU).  Why A wrong: FRU is a separate designation for referral CHCs, not the block-HQ development plan described here.  Why C wrong: MCH centre is the alternate name for Type-B Sub-Centres, not CHCs.  Why D wrong: The document specifies \"Block\" Public Health Units, not district-level units."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"According to IPHS provisioning, a 6-bedded emergency area should be established in which facility?","options":["30-bedded non-FRU CHC","30-bedded FRU CHC","50-bedded FRU CHC/UCHC","100-bedded FRU-UCHC"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A 6-bedded emergency area is specified for a 50-bedded FRU CHC/UCHC.  Why A/B wrong: A 4-bedded emergency area is provided for 30-bedded non-FRU CHC and FRU CHC.  Why D wrong: An 11-bed emergency area is specified for the 100-bedded FRU-UCHC."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How many emergency beds should be established for a 100-bedded FRU-UCHC?","options":["4 beds","6 beds","11 beds","13 beds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline specifies 11 beds for a 100-bedded FRU-UCHC.  Why A wrong: 4 beds is for a 30-bedded non-FRU/FRU CHC.  Why B wrong: 6 beds is for a 50-bedded FRU CHC/UCHC.  Why D wrong: 13 is not the specified emergency bed number for this facility."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How many bedded emergency area should be established in a 30-bedded non-FRU CHC and FRU CHC?","options":["4-bedded","6-bedded","11-bedded","2-bedded"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 4-bedded emergency area is specified for 30-bedded non-FRU CHC and FRU CHC.  Why B wrong: 6-bedded is for a 50-bedded FRU CHC/UCHC.  Why C wrong: 11-bedded is for a 100-bedded FRU-UCHC.  Why D wrong: 2 beds is not the stated norm."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Regarding Operation Theatres, how many additional OT is provisioned for a 100-bedded UCHC FRU beyond the standard one elective and one round-the-clock OT?","options":["No additional OT","One additional OT","Two additional OTs","Three additional OTs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One elective and one round-the-clock OT is standard, with one additional OT provisioned in a 100-bedded UCHC FRU.  Why A wrong: An additional OT is explicitly provisioned for the 100-bedded facility.  Why C/D wrong: Only one additional OT is mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What temperature should always be maintained inside the operation theatre as per IPHS guidelines?","options":["18 ± 2 degree Celsius","21 ± / - 3 degree Celsius","25 ± 3 degree Celsius","16 ± 2 degree Celsius"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The theatre temperature should always be maintained at 21+ or −3 degree Celsius.  Why A/C/D wrong: These temperature ranges are not the specified IPHS OT norm."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The relative humidity inside the operation theatre should be maintained between which range?","options":["30–40%","40–50%","50–60%","60–70%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Corresponding relative humidity should be between 50–60%.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are outside the specified 50–60% range."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What is the minimum number of air changes per hour (ACH) that should ideally be maintained in the operation theatre, and how many of these should be fresh air changes?","options":["Minimum 15 ACH, of which 2 fresh","Minimum 20 ACH, of which 4 fresh","Minimum 25 ACH, of which 5 fresh","Minimum 10 ACH, of which 3 fresh"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ideally there should be minimum 20 air-changes per hour (ACH), of which 4 should be fresh air changes.  Why A/C/D wrong: The specified norm is 20 ACH with 4 fresh air changes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How many Labour (LDR) beds have been estimated at a 30-bedded Non-FRU & FRU CHC?","options":["One LDR bed","Two LDR beds","Three LDR beds","Four LDR beds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Two Labour (LDR) beds are estimated at 30-bedded Non-FRU & FRU CHC.  Why A wrong: One bed is not the estimate.  Why C wrong: Three LDR beds are estimated at every 50-bedded FRU CHC (rural/urban) and 100-bedded UCHC.  Why D wrong: Four beds is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How many Labour (LDR) beds have been estimated at every 50-bedded FRU CHC (rural and urban areas) and 100-bedded UCHC?","options":["Two","Three","Six","Nine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Three Labour (LDR) beds are estimated at every 50-bedded FRU CHC and 100-bedded UCHC.  Why A wrong: Two beds are for the 30-bedded Non-FRU & FRU CHC.  Why C/D wrong: 6 and 9 are LDR staff-nurse counts, not bed numbers."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"A how-many-bedded Nutritional Rehabilitation Centre (NRC) is provisioned at FRU CHC and UCHC to manage nutritional disorders in children with Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM)?","options":["5-bedded","10-bedded","15-bedded","20-bedded"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 10-bedded NRC is provided at FRU CHC and UCHC to manage nutritional disorders in children with SAM.  Why A/C/D wrong: The guideline specifies a 10-bedded NRC."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Blood Storage Units (BSUs) should be licensed and available in FRU-CHCs as per which guidelines?","options":["Revised Guidelines for Blood Storage Units by Government of India","WHO Blood Banking Standards","IPHS Emergency Blood Rules","NACO Transfusion Guidelines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BSUs should be licensed and available in FRU-CHCs as per the Revised Guidelines for Blood Storage Units by Government of India.  Why B/C/D wrong: The document specifically cites the GoI Revised Guidelines for BSUs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In the IPHS human resources list for CHC/U-CHC, what nurse:bed ratio is applied to the IPD (In-Patient Department)?","options":["1:3","1:6","1:2","1:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The IPD nurse:bed ratio is 1:6.  Why A wrong: 1:3 is the NBSU ratio.  Why C/D wrong: 1:2 (HDU) and 1:1 (ICU) apply to other units, not IPD."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What nurse:bed ratio is specified for the NBSU (Newborn Stabilization Unit) in the CHC/U-CHC staffing table?","options":["1:6","1:3","1:2","1:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NBSU nurse:bed ratio is 1:3.  Why A wrong: 1:6 applies to IPD/general ward.  Why C/D wrong: 1:2 and 1:1 are for HDU and ICU respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"According to the \"Nurse bed ratio\" box, what is the recommended nurse:bed ratio for the ICU?","options":["1:6","1:3","1:2","1:1"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The box states ICU nurse:bed ratio is 1:1.  Why A wrong: 1:6 is general ward.  Why B wrong: 1:3 is SNCU.  Why C wrong: 1:2 is HDU (and NICU/PICU/Ped HDU)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per the nurse bed ratio box, the SNCU (Special Newborn Care Unit) requires which nurse:bed ratio?","options":["1:1","1:2","1:3","1:6"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: SNCU nurse:bed ratio is 1:3.  Why A wrong: 1:1 is ICU.  Why B wrong: 1:2 is HDU and NICU/PICU/Ped HDU.  Why D wrong: 1:6 is general ward."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In the nurse bed ratio box, which units share the nurse:bed ratio of 1:2?","options":["General ward and ICU","HDU and NICU/PICU/Ped HDU","SNCU and ICU","General ward and SNCU"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HDU is 1:2 and NICU/PICU/Ped HDU is also 1:2.  Why A wrong: General ward is 1:6, ICU is 1:1.  Why C wrong: SNCU is 1:3, ICU is 1:1.  Why D wrong: General ward 1:6, SNCU 1:3."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"According to the CHC/U-CHC human resources table, what is the total staff nurse requirement for a 30-bedded Non-FRU CHC?","options":["29","46","61","95"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists 29 total staff nurses for the 30-bedded Non-FRU CHC.  Why B wrong: 46 is for 30-bedded FRU CHC (rural).  Why C wrong: 61 is for 50-bedded FRU facilities.  Why D wrong: 95 is for the 100-bedded FRU UPHC."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What is the total staff nurse requirement for a 100-bedded FRU UPHC (Urban) as per the IPHS table?","options":["46","61","95","29"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 100-bedded FRU UPHC requires 95 total staff nurses.  Why A wrong: 46 is for the 30-bedded FRU CHC (rural).  Why B wrong: 61 is for 50-bedded FRU facilities.  Why D wrong: 29 is for the 30-bedded Non-FRU CHC."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For the LDR (Labour Room) in the CHC/U-CHC staffing norm, the requirement is 1 Nurse/Midwife for how many LDRs, plus 1 Neonatal Nurse for how many LDRs?","options":["1 Nurse/Midwife for 2 LDRs; +1 Neonatal Nurse for 4 LDRs","1 Nurse/Midwife for 4 LDRs; +1 Neonatal Nurse for 2 LDRs","1 Nurse/Midwife per LDR; +1 Neonatal Nurse per LDR","1 Nurse/Midwife for 3 LDRs; +1 Neonatal Nurse for 6 LDRs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The norm is 1 Nurse/Midwife for 2 LDRs and +1 Neonatal Nurse for 4 LDRs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These ratios do not match the stated LDR staffing norm."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Under the IPHS-2022 Sub-Division/District Hospital guidelines, a Nursing In-Charge is calculated for every how many beds?","options":["Every 10 beds","Every 20 beds","Every 30 beds","Every 50 beds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A Nursing In-Charge is provided for every 30 beds, with one each for critical care area, OT, and LDR (for more than 20-bed unit).  Why A wrong: One ANS is for every 10 Nursing In-Charges, not the Nursing In-Charge norm.  Why B/D wrong: 20 and 50 beds are not the Nursing In-Charge norm."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How many Assistant Nursing Superintendents (ANS) are provided under IPHS-2022 for the district hospital?","options":["One ANS for every 5 Nursing In-Charges","One ANS for every 10 Nursing In-Charges, at least one in each DH from 50 to 200 beds","One ANS for every 20 Nursing In-Charges","One ANS for every district hospital with 300 or more beds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One ANS is provided for every 10 Nursing In-Charges and at least one in each DH from 50 to 200 beds.  Why A/C wrong: The ratio is 1 per 10 Nursing In-Charges.  Why D wrong: The 300+ bed criterion applies to the Deputy Nursing Superintendent (DNS)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Under IPHS-2022, one Deputy Nursing Superintendent (DNS) is provided for every district hospital having how many beds?","options":["200 or more beds","300 or more beds","400 or more beds","500 or more beds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One DNS is provided for every district hospital having 300 or more beds.  Why A wrong: 50–200 beds relates to ANS placement.  Why C wrong: 400+ beds is for the Nursing Superintendent (NS).  Why D wrong: 500 beds is not a stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"One Nursing Superintendent (NS) is provided for a larger district hospital having how many or more beds?","options":["200 beds","300 beds","400 beds","500 beds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: One NS is provided for a larger DH having 400 or more beds.  Why A/D wrong: These thresholds are not stated for NS.  Why B wrong: 300+ beds is the DNS threshold."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre (NRC) has been established to deal primarily with which situation?","options":["Moderate acute malnutrition (MAM)","Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM)","Micronutrient deficiency only","Childhood obesity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NRC has been established to deal with the situation of SAM (Severe Acute Malnutrition).  Why A/C/D wrong: NRC is specifically for SAM per the text."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For how many days is a child normally kept in the Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre?","options":["7 days","10 days","14 days","21 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The child is kept in the NRC for 14 days.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated standard stay is 14 days."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"If a child kept at the NRC is not able to get rid of malnutrition in 14 days, special care is taken by keeping such children for how long?","options":["Another 14 days","A week","A month","Two months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If not recovered in 14 days, special care is taken by keeping the child for a month.  Why A/B/D wrong: The text specifies a month."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"A child is discharged from the NRC only after there is a minimum increase of what percentage in the weight of the admitted child?","options":["10 percent","15 percent","20 percent","25 percent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Discharge occurs only after a minimum 15 percent increase in the admitted child's weight.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated discharge threshold is 15 percent weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"All the facilities available in the Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre are provided:","options":["At subsidized cost","Free of cost","On insurance basis","At government-fixed rates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All facilities available in the NRC are free of cost.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text states services are free of cost."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For children age 6 months up to 60 months, which of the following is an NRC admission criterion?","options":["MUAC < 11.5 cm","MUAC > 12.5 cm","Weight-for-age > median","Absence of oedema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For children 6–60 months, admission criteria include W/H or W/L < −3 Z score (WHO 2005), MUAC < 11.5 cm, and presence of bilateral oedema.  Why B wrong: MUAC < 11.5 cm (not >12.5) is the cut-off.  Why C wrong: Above-median weight is not a malnutrition admission criterion.  Why D wrong: Presence, not absence, of bilateral oedema is an admission criterion."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For NRC admission of children 6 months to 60 months, the weight/height (or weight/length) Z-score cut-off used is:","options":["W/H or W/L < −1 Z score","W/H or W/L < −2 Z score","W/H or W/L < −3 Z score (WHO 2005 standards)","W/H or W/L > +2 Z score"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The admission cut-off is W/H or W/L < −3 Z score (WHO 2005 standards), indicating severe wasting.  Why A/B wrong: −1 and −2 Z scores do not meet the SAM threshold.  Why D wrong: A positive Z-score indicates overweight, not SAM."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For children age 6–60 months, the NRC discharge criterion regarding weight gain is:","options":["10% weight gain from admission weight","15% weight gain from admission weight (or weight on the day free of oedema)","20% weight gain from admission weight","Reaching median weight-for-age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Discharge criterion is 15% weight gain from admission weight or weight on the day free of oedema, absence of bilateral oedema for at least 10 days, and no medical complications.  Why A/C wrong: The specified target is 15%.  Why D wrong: The criterion is percentage weight gain, not reaching median."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For children less than 6 months of age, which of the following is an NRC admission criterion? (Select all that apply)","options":["Problem of breastfeeding: infant too weak to suck","The mother has not enough milk","Bilateral pitting oedema","W/H or W/L < −3 Z score (WHO 2005 standards)","MUAC < 11.5 cm"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: For <6 months, admission criteria are: breastfeeding problem (infant too weak to suck), mother has not enough milk, bilateral pitting oedema, and W/H or W/L < −3 Z score (WHO 2005).  Why E wrong: MUAC < 11.5 cm is an admission criterion for the 6–60 months age group, not for infants under 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For children under 6 months with severe acute malnutrition and lactation failure, emphasis is laid on re-establishment of lactation using which technique?","options":["Supplementary sucking technique (SST)","Cup and spoon feeding only","Nasogastric feeding","Formula replacement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For <6 months SAM, emphasis is on supporting and sustaining breastfeeding through counselling and re-establishment of lactation using the supplementary sucking technique (SST).  Why B/C/D wrong: The text specifically names the supplementary sucking technique."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For a child less than 6 months of age, the NRC discharge criterion related to oedema is absence of bilateral oedema for at least:","options":["Past 5 days","Past 7 days","Past 10 days","Past 14 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For <6 months, discharge requires absence of bilateral oedema for at least the past 10 days, along with weight gain on breast milk alone and no medical complications.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated period is at least 10 days."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per IPHS Old Guidelines 2012, which facility is the most peripheral and first point of contact between the primary health care system and the community?","options":["Primary Health Centre (PHC)","Community Health Centre (CHC)","Sub-Health Centre (Sub-Centre)","District Hospital"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Sub-Health Centre (Sub-Centre) is the most peripheral and first point of contact between the primary health care system and the community.  Why A wrong: PHC is the first contact with a doctor (M.O.), not the most peripheral unit.  Why B/D wrong: CHC and DH are higher-level referral facilities."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Type-B Sub-Centres are also known as which of the following?","options":["First Referral Units","Maternal and Child Health (MCH) centres","Block Public Health Units","Health & Wellness Centres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Type-B Sub-Centres are also known as Maternal and Child Health (MCH) centres.  Why A wrong: FRU refers to CHCs.  Why C wrong: BPHU is the developed form of CHCs.  Why D wrong: HWC is not the term used here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per IPHS 2012, one Sub-Centre is to be established for every how many population in plain area and hilly/tribal area respectively?","options":["3000 (plain) and 5000 (hilly/tribal)","5000 (plain) and 3000 (hilly/tribal)","10000 (plain) and 5000 (hilly/tribal)","20000 (plain) and 10000 (hilly/tribal)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One Sub-Centre is established for every 5000 population in plain area and 3000 population in hilly/tribal area.  Why A wrong: The figures are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These are PHC/CHC-level norms, not Sub-Centre norms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"A PHC with a delivery load of less than 20/month is classified as:","options":["Type-A PHC","Type-B PHC","Type-C PHC","FRU PHC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A PHC with delivery load <20/month is classified as Type-A PHC; >20/month is Type-B PHC.  Why B wrong: Type-B PHC has delivery load >20/month.  Why C/D wrong: These classifications are not used for PHCs here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per IPHS 2012, one PHC is to be established for every how many population in plain area?","options":["5000","20000","30000","120000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: One PHC is established for every 30000 population in plain area (and 20000 in hilly/tribal area).  Why A wrong: 5000 is the Sub-Centre norm.  Why B wrong: 20000 is the hilly/tribal PHC norm.  Why D wrong: 1,20,000 is the CHC norm."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Each PHC acts as a referral centre for how many sub-centres?","options":["4 sub-centres","6 sub-centres","24 sub-centres","30 sub-centres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each PHC acts as a referral centre for 6 sub-centres.  Why A wrong: 4 PHCs (not sub-centres) are referred to one CHC.  Why C wrong: One CHC is a referral centre for 24 SCs.  Why D wrong: 30 is the CHC IPD bed strength."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How many beds strength for IPD do PHCs have as per IPHS 2012?","options":["2-4 beds","4-6 beds","6-10 beds","30 beds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PHCs have 4–6 beds strength for IPD.  Why A/C wrong: The stated range is 4–6 beds.  Why D wrong: 30 beds is the CHC IPD strength."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In the three-tier system, CHCs are known as:","options":["Maternal and Child Health centres","First Referral Unit (FRU)","Block Public Health Unit","Sub-Divisional Hospital"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CHCs are known as First Referral Unit (FRU) in the three-tier system.  Why A wrong: MCH centre is the Type-B Sub-Centre.  Why C/D wrong: These are different designations."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per IPHS 2012, CHCs have how many beds strength for IPD?","options":["4-6 beds","10 beds","30 beds","50 beds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: CHCs have 30 beds strength for IPD.  Why A wrong: 4–6 beds is the PHC strength.  Why B/D wrong: These bed numbers are not the CHC IPD norm in the 2012 guidelines."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"One CHC is to be established for every how many population in plain area as per IPHS 2012?","options":["30,000","80,000","1,20,000","2,00,000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: One CHC is established for every 1,20,000 population in plain area and 80,000 in hilly/tribal area.  Why A wrong: 30,000 is the PHC plain-area norm.  Why B wrong: 80,000 is the hilly/tribal CHC norm.  Why D wrong: 2,00,000 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"One CHC acts as a referral centre for how many PHCs / Sub-Centres?","options":["4 PHCs or 24 SCs","6 PHCs or 30 SCs","2 PHCs or 12 SCs","8 PHCs or 48 SCs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One CHC acts as a referral centre for 4 PHCs or 24 SCs.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated ratio is 4 PHCs (each covering 6 SCs = 24 SCs)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which strategy is followed for prevention and control of childhood diseases (malnutrition, infections, ARI, diarrhea, fever, anemia) at the Sub-Centre?","options":["RNTCP strategy","IMNCI strategy","RCH-I strategy","DOTS strategy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Sub-Centre uses the IMNCI (Integrated Management of Neonatal and Childhood Illness) strategy for prevention/control of childhood diseases.  Why A wrong: RNTCP is the TB control programme.  Why C wrong: RCH-I is a broader reproductive/child health programme, not the named strategy.  Why D wrong: DOTS is TB treatment."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Reporting of Adverse Events Following Immunization at the Sub-Centre uses which abbreviation?","options":["AEFI","VAERS","AESI","ADR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sub-Centre services include identification, follow-up, referral and reporting of Adverse Events Following Immunization (AEFI).  Why B wrong: VAERS is a US surveillance system, not used here.  Why C/D wrong: AESI/ADR are different pharmacovigilance terms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"At the Sub-Centre, the weekly submission of report to PHC is done in the \"S Form\" by the MPW as per which guidelines?","options":["IMNCI guidelines","IDSP guidelines","RNTCP guidelines","IPHS guidelines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Weekly submission of report to PHC is done in the \"S\" Form by MPW as per IDSP (Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme) guidelines.  Why A wrong: IMNCI is a childhood illness strategy.  Why C wrong: RNTCP is TB-specific.  Why D wrong: IPHS is the facility standards, not the weekly S-form reporting guideline."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per Sub-Centre services, the Village Health Nutrition Day (VHND) should be organised at least how often in each village?","options":["Once a week","Once in a month","Once in three months","Twice a month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: VHND should be organised at least once in a month in each village with the help of MO, CHO, LHV, HWM, HWF, ASHA, AWW etc.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated minimum frequency is once a month."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following is the key conceptual difference between a screening test and a diagnostic test?","options":["Screening looks for signs (early/before symptoms); diagnosis is based on symptoms to establish presence/absence of disease","Screening establishes definite diagnosis; diagnostic tests only indicate probability","Screening is only for symptomatic individuals; diagnostic tests for asymptomatic","There is no difference between them"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Screening looks for signs and detects disease/risk factors early or before symptoms appear; diagnosis is based on symptoms and establishes the presence or absence of disease.  Why B wrong: It is reversed—diagnostic tests give a definite diagnosis; screening indicates high/low probability.  Why C wrong: Screening is for asymptomatic individuals; diagnostic tests for symptomatic (or those with a positive screen).  Why D wrong: A clear difference exists."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"A screening test is particularly used for which type of individuals?","options":["Symptomatic individuals with established disease","Asymptomatic individuals, particularly those at higher risk of developing disease","Only hospitalized patients","Only individuals with a confirmed diagnosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Screening is for asymptomatic individuals, particularly those that pose a higher risk of developing disease.  Why A/D wrong: Symptomatic/confirmed cases are for diagnostic testing.  Why C wrong: Screening is a community/population activity, not limited to inpatients."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following are listed as SCREENING tests for cervical cancer? (Select all that apply)","options":["HPV test","Pap test","Colposcopy","Biopsy"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: HPV test and Pap test are the listed screening tests for cervical cancer.  Why C wrong: Colposcopy is a diagnostic test for cervical cancer.  Why D wrong: Biopsy is the highly accurate confirmatory (diagnostic) test."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which test is described as the \"highly accurate confirmative test\" for cervical cancer diagnosis?","options":["HPV test","Pap test","Colposcopy","Biopsy"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Biopsy is described as the highly accurate confirmative test for cervical cancer.  Why A/B wrong: HPV and Pap are screening tests.  Why C wrong: Colposcopy is a diagnostic test but the biopsy is the confirmatory one."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following are listed as SCREENING tests for breast cancer? (Select all that apply)","options":["Physical examination","Breast Self-Examination (BSE)","Mammogram (screening)","Breast MRI"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Physical examination, BSE, and screening mammogram are listed as breast cancer screening tests.  Why D wrong: Breast MRI is listed as a diagnostic test for breast cancer (along with Breast USG, diagnostic mammogram, and biopsy)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following are listed as DIAGNOSTIC tests for breast cancer? (Select all that apply)","options":["Breast USG","Mammogram (diagnostic)","Breast MRI","Biopsy","BSE"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Breast USG, diagnostic mammogram, breast MRI, and biopsy (highly accurate confirmative test) are the diagnostic tests for breast cancer.  Why E wrong: BSE is a screening test, not diagnostic."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For a woman aged 21–29 years, which screening approach for cervical cancer is recommended?","options":["Pap test every 3 years","HPV test every 5 years","HPV + Pap co-test every year","No screening needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For age 21–29 years, Pap test every 3 years is recommended.  Why B wrong: HPV test every 5 years is part of the 30–65 year recommendation.  Why C wrong: Annual co-testing is not the stated schedule.  Why D wrong: Screening is recommended in this age band."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For a woman aged 30–65 years, the recommended cervical cancer screening is:","options":["Pap test every year only","HPV test every 5 years and Pap test every 3 years","HPV test every 10 years","No further screening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For age 30–65 years, HPV test every 5 years and Pap test every 3 years is recommended.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 30–65 year schedule."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What does the abbreviation ASHA stand for?","options":["Accredited Social Health Activist","Auxiliary Social Health Assistant","Advanced State Health Advisor","Accredited Skilled Health Assistant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASHA stands for Accredited Social Health Activist.  Why B/C/D wrong: These expansions are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"An ASHA is a community health worker employed by the MoHFW as a part of which program?","options":["National Urban Health Mission (NUHM)","National Rural Health Mission (NRHM)","RCH Programme","Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ASHA is employed by the MoHFW as part of the NRHM (National Rural Health Mission) program.  Why A wrong: NUHM is the program for Urban ASHA.  Why C/D wrong: RCH and ICDS are separate programmes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) program was launched on which date?","options":["1st May 2013","12 April 2005","2nd October 2004","5 July 2006"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NRHM was launched on 12 April 2005.  Why A wrong: 1 May 2013 is the NUHM launch date.  Why C/D wrong: These dates are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Usually, one (rural) ASHA covers how much population?","options":["500 population","1000 population","2500 population","5000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Usually, one ASHA covers 1000 population.  Why A wrong: In tribal/hilly areas, ASHA may be one per 500 populations or per habitation.  Why C wrong: 2500 is the Urban ASHA norm.  Why D wrong: 5000 is a Sub-Centre norm."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In tribal and hilly areas, the ASHA norm may be:","options":["One per 1000 population","One per habitation or one per 500 populations","One per 2500 population","One per 200 households"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In tribal and hilly areas, ASHA may be one per habitation or one per 500 populations.  Why A wrong: 1000 is the usual (general) norm.  Why C/D wrong: These are Urban ASHA norms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"ASHAs are considered as which of the following in terms of employment status?","options":["Regular salaried government employees","Volunteers who receive outcome-based remuneration and financial compensation for training days","Contractual staff nurses","Multipurpose health workers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ASHAs are considered volunteers; they receive outcome-based remuneration and financial compensation for training days.  Why A wrong: They are not regular salaried employees.  Why C/D wrong: These are different cadres."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What are the working hours specified for an ASHA?","options":["8 hours/day, 6 days a week","2-3 hours/day or 4 days a week, minimum 15 hours/week","Round the clock on-call","4 hours/day, 5 days a week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Working hours for ASHA are 2–3 hours/day or 4 days in a week, minimum 15 hours/week.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated working hours."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"At the village level, ASHA's work is supervised by whom?","options":["Block Facilitator","Female health worker (ANM)","Gram Panchayat president","Medical Officer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At village level, ASHA's work is supervised by the female health worker (ANM).  Why A wrong: Block Facilitators assist at the block level (one for every 10 ASHAs).  Why C wrong: Gram panchayat selects/accountable for ASHA, but doesn't supervise the work.  Why D wrong: The MO is not the direct village-level supervisor."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"At the block level, ASHA is to be assisted by Block Facilitators at the ratio of:","options":["One for every 5 ASHAs","One for every 10 ASHAs","One for every 20 ASHAs","One for every 50 ASHAs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At block level, ASHA is assisted by Block Facilitators (one for every 10 ASHAs).  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated ratio is one Block Facilitator per 10 ASHAs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which is a criterion for selection of an ASHA regarding residence?","options":["She must be a resident of the concerned village","She may reside in any village of the block","She must reside in the district headquarters","Residence is not a criterion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An ASHA must be a resident of the concerned village.  Why B/C/D wrong: Residence in the concerned village is mandatory."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What is the preferred age group and educational qualification for ASHA selection?","options":["18-35 years, up to 8th class","25-45 years, up to 10th class (previously 8th standard) may be relaxable","21-45 years, graduate","25-45 years, 12th pass mandatory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Preferably age group 25–45 years with formal education up to 10th class (previously 8th standard) may be relaxable.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated preferred age/education."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"ASHAs are selected by and accountable to which body?","options":["The Medical Officer of PHC","The Gram Panchayat (local government)","The District Health Society","The ANM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ASHAs are selected by and accountable to the gram panchayat (local government).  Why A/D wrong: MO/ANM supervise but the accountability is to the gram panchayat.  Why C wrong: District Health Society (District urban ASHA committee) selects Urban ASHA, not rural."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Who act as resource persons for the training of ASHA?","options":["Block Facilitators only","AWWs and ANMs","Medical Officers only","Gram Panchayat members"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AWWs (Anganwadi Workers) and ANMs will act as resource persons for the training of ASHA.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text names AWWs and ANMs as resource persons."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What is the induction training schedule for ASHA?","options":["15 days in 3 rounds over 6 months","23 days spread in 5 rounds over a period of 12 months, followed by periodic re-training about two days once every alternate month","30 days continuous training","21 days in 4 rounds over 9 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Induction training is 23 days spread in 5 rounds over 12 months, followed by periodic re-training for about two days once every alternate month.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated schedule."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As a depot holder in tribal areas, an ASHA keeps essential provisions available to all habitations. Which of the following are included? (Select all that apply)","options":["Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) Therapy","Iron Folic Acid (IFA) Tablets","Chloroquine","Disposable Delivery Kits (DDK)","Oral Pills & Condoms"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The depot-holder provisions listed are ORS therapy, IFA tablets, Chloroquine, Disposable Delivery Kits (DDK), and Oral Pills & Condoms.  Why any wrong: All five items are explicitly listed as depot-holder provisions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"ASHA delivers \"Nishchay kits\" to eligible couples. What are Nishchay kits?","options":["Iron folic acid supplement kits","Home based Pregnancy Test Kits (PTKs)","Newborn resuscitation kits","Oral contraceptive kits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nishchay kits are Home based Pregnancy Test Kits (PTKs) delivered by ASHA to eligible couples.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text defines Nishchay kits as home pregnancy test kits."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"ASHA promotes construction of household toilets under which campaign?","options":["Swachh Bharat Abhiyan","Total Sanitation Campaign","Nirmal Gram Yojana","Clean India Mission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ASHA promotes construction of household toilets under the Total Sanitation Campaign.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifically names the Total Sanitation Campaign."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Regarding remuneration, an ASHA is considered:","options":["A salaried government officer","An honorary volunteer who would not receive any salary or honorarium","A contractual employee with fixed pay","A pensionable state employee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ASHA would be an honorary volunteer and would not receive any salary or honorarium; she is entitled to TA/DA for attending training programmes and other performance-based incentives are admissible.  Why A/C/D wrong: ASHA is an honorary volunteer, not a salaried/contractual/pensionable employee."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following is an ASHA \"Process Indicator\" used in monitoring & evaluation?","options":["Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)","Number of ASHAs trained","% of children with diarrhoea who received ORS","Child malnutrition rates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Process indicators include number of ASHAs selected by due process, number of ASHAs trained, and % of ASHAs attending review meetings after one year.  Why A/D wrong: IMR and child malnutrition rates are Impact indicators.  Why C wrong: % of children with diarrhoea who received ORS is an Outcome indicator."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following are ASHA \"Outcome Indicators\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["% of newborn weighed and families counselled","% of children with diarrhoea who received ORS","% of deliveries with skilled assistance","% of institutional deliveries","Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Outcome indicators include % newborn weighed & families counselled, % children with diarrhoea who received ORS, % deliveries with skilled assistance, and % institutional deliveries.  Why E wrong: IMR is an Impact indicator, not an outcome indicator."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following are listed as ASHA \"Impact Indicators\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)","Child malnutrition rates","Number of TB/leprosy cases detected compared to previous year","Number of ASHAs trained"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Impact indicators are IMR, child malnutrition rates, and number of TB/leprosy cases detected compared to the previous year.  Why D wrong: Number of ASHAs trained is a Process indicator."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Regarding immunization, one ASHA outcome/process indicator tracks the \"% completely immunized\" in which age group?","options":["0-11 months","12-23 months","24-36 months","9-12 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The indicator is \"% completely immunized in 12–23 months age group.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated age group is 12–23 months."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In ASHA's Monthly Basis Incentives table, what is the revised incentive for \"Line listing of households done at beginning of the year and updated after six months\"?","options":["Rs 100","Rs 150","Rs 200","Rs 300"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: For line listing of households, the existing incentive was Rs 100 and the revised is Rs 300.  Why A wrong: Rs 100 is the existing (old) amount.  Why B/C wrong: These amounts apply to other activities."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In the ASHA Monthly Incentives table, what are the existing and revised total incentive amounts respectively?","options":["Rs 500 existing, Rs 1000 revised","Rs 1000 existing, Rs 2000 revised","Rs 2000 existing, Rs 3000 revised","Rs 1500 existing, Rs 2500 revised"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table total shows Rs 1000 existing and Rs 2000 revised.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated totals are 1000 (existing) and 2000 (revised)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In the ASHA Monthly Incentives table, the incentive for \"Mobilizing and attending VHND\" remained at what amount (both existing and revised)?","options":["Rs 100","Rs 150","Rs 200","Rs 300"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For mobilizing and attending VHND, both existing and revised incentives are Rs 200.  Why A/B wrong: These amounts are for other activities.  Why D wrong: Rs 300 is the revised amount for several list-preparation activities."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In the ASHA Monthly Incentives table, \"Convening and guiding VHSNC meeting\" and \"Attending the PHC review meeting\" both carry which incentive (existing and revised)?","options":["Rs 100","Rs 150","Rs 200","Rs 300"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Both convening/guiding VHSNC meeting and attending PHC review meeting carry Rs 150 (existing and revised).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the Rs 150 entry for these two activities."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following are items in ASHA's Drug Kit? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tab. Iron Folic Acid (L)","ORS Packets","Nischay Kit","Weighing Scale (for newborn)","Emergency Contraceptive Pill"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The Drug Kit includes Tab. IFA (L), ORS packets, Nischay Kit, and Emergency Contraceptive Pill (among 24 listed items).  Why D wrong: Weighing Scale (for newborn) is part of the Equipment Kit, not the Drug Kit."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Which of the following are items in ASHA's Equipment Kit? (Select all that apply)","options":["Digital Wrist Watch","Thermometer","Weighing Scale (for newborn)","Mucous Extractor","Cotrimoxazole syrup"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The Equipment Kit contains Digital Wrist Watch, Thermometer, Weighing Scale (for newborn), Baby Blanket, Baby Feeding spoon, Kit Bag, Communication Kit, and Mucous Extractor.  Why E wrong: Cotrimoxazole syrup is a Drug Kit item, not an equipment item."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How many total items are listed in ASHA's Drug Kit?","options":["8 items","12 items","24 items","21 items"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Drug Kit list is numbered 1–24, so 24 items.  Why A wrong: 8 items is the Equipment Kit count.  Why B/D wrong: These are not the total drug kit count."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How many items are listed in ASHA's Equipment Kit?","options":["8 items","12 items","24 items","6 items"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Equipment Kit list is numbered 1–8, so 8 items.  Why B/C wrong: These relate to the drug kit.  Why D wrong: 6 is not the count."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In ASHA's Drug Kit, item number 19 \"Bandages\" is specified in which dimension?","options":["4 cm × 4 meters","5 cm × 5 meters","4 cm × 2 meters","2 cm × 4 meters"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug kit lists Bandages, 4 cm × 4 meters.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated bandage dimension is 4 cm × 4 meters."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Under which program does the Urban ASHA work?","options":["National Rural Health Mission (NRHM)","National Urban Health Mission (NUHM)","Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)","Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Urban ASHA is an honorary volunteer health worker working under the National Urban Health Mission (NUHM) program.  Why A wrong: Rural ASHA works under NRHM.  Why C/D wrong: These are different programmes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In India, the NUHM Program was launched on which date?","options":["12 April 2005","1st May 2013","2nd October 2014","15 August 2005"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NUHM Program was launched on 1st May 2013 under the National Health Mission.  Why A wrong: 12 April 2005 is the NRHM launch date.  Why C/D wrong: These dates are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The National Health Mission (NHM) is described as a joint program to improve the health status of:","options":["Only urban communities","Only rural communities","Urban & rural communities","Only tribal communities"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NHM is a joint program to improve the health status of urban & rural communities.  Why A/B/D wrong: NHM covers both urban and rural communities."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"One Urban ASHA would be appointed for every how much population or households?","options":["1000 population or 100-200 households","2500 population or 200-500 households","5000 population or 500-1000 households","500 population or 50-100 households"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One Urban ASHA is appointed for every 2500 population or 200–500 households.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated norm is 2500 population or 200–500 households."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Urban ASHA would be posted only in major cities having a population of more than how many?","options":["20000","30000","50000","100000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Urban ASHA is posted only in major cities having more than 50000 populations; in urban habitations of 50,000 or less, ASHAs are selected as in rural areas/NRHM norms.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated threshold is 50000."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"How does the drug kit of Urban ASHA differ from the rural ASHA drug kit?","options":["It has no ORS packets","It is the same as rural ASHA, only with Chloroquine tablet additionally provided in Urban","It has only paracetamol","It has additional antibiotics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urban ASHA drug kit is the same as rural ASHA, only Chloroquine tablet is additionally being provided in Urban.  Why A/C/D wrong: The only stated difference is the additional Chloroquine tablet."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What is the specified age group for Urban ASHA selection?","options":["18 to 35 years","21 to 45 years","25 to 45 years","30 to 50 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The age group for Urban ASHA is 25 to 45 years.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated range is 25–45 years."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The Urban ASHA selection is done through which committee/body?","options":["Gram Panchayat","District urban ASHA selection Committee of District Health Society","State Health Mission","Block Facilitator committee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Selection is through the District urban ASHA selection Committee of the District Health Society.  Why A wrong: Gram Panchayat selects the rural ASHA.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated selection bodies."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What is the minimum formal education criterion for Urban ASHA selection?","options":["8th class","10th class","12th class","Graduate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An Urban ASHA should be a literate woman with formal education of at least 10th class.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated minimum is 10th class."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What does ANM stand for?","options":["Assistant Nurse Manager","Auxiliary Nurse Midwife","Accredited Nursing Member","Advanced Nurse Midwife"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ANM stands for Auxiliary Nurse Midwife, a village-level female health worker.  Why A/C/D wrong: These expansions are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The ANM is also known by which alternative designation?","options":["Accredited Social Health Activist","Multipurpose Worker (F) or Female Health Worker","Anganwadi Worker","Lady Health Visitor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ANM is also known as Multipurpose Worker (F) or Female Health Worker.  Why A wrong: That is ASHA.  Why C/D wrong: AWW and LHV are different cadres."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"What is the initial qualification for admission to the ANM course?","options":["10th pass, age 16-30 years","10+2 passed with age between minimum 17 to 35 years","Graduate, age 21-40 years","8th pass, any age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Initial qualification is 10+2 passed with age between minimum 17 to 35 years.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated ANM admission criteria."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In 1977, the training period of the ANM course was reduced from 24 months to how many months?","options":["12 months","18 months","21 months","6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1977, the ANM course training period was reduced from 24 months to 18 months.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated reduced period is 18 months."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"In 1990, ANM training was converted into an ANM vocational course and the duration changed from 18 months to:","options":["12 months","24 months (2 years)","30 months","36 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1990, ANM training converted into an ANM vocational course and the duration changed from 18 months to 2 years (24 months).  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated new duration is 2 years."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per the norm, one ANM/MPW is posted over every how much population in plain area and hilly/tribal area respectively?","options":["3000 (plain) and 5000 (hilly/tribal)","5000 (plain) and 3000 (hilly/tribal)","10000 (plain) and 5000 (hilly/tribal)","1000 (plain) and 500 (hilly/tribal)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One ANM/MPW is posted over every 5000 populations in plain area and 3000 populations in hilly/tribal areas.  Why A wrong: The figures are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the norm."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The norm of one ANM/MPW (F) per 10,000 population is supported by how many ASHAs in urban area?","options":["Two to three ASHAs","Four to five ASHAs","Six to seven ASHAs","One ASHA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The norm of one ANM/MPW (F) per 10,000 population is supported by four to five ASHAs in urban area.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated support is four to five ASHAs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"ANMs posted at Type B Sub-centres should mandatorily have how many days of Skilled Birth Attendance (SBA) training?","options":["15 days","21 days","30 days","45 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM posted at Type B Sub-centres should mandatorily be 21 days Skilled Birth Attendance (SBA) trained.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated requirement is 21 days SBA training."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The ANMOL App (ANM On Line) was introduced by MOHFW with support from which organization?","options":["WHO","UNICEF","World Bank","USAID"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ANMOL App was introduced by MOHFW with support from UNICEF; it is an android-based application providing due list, dashboard, high risk pregnancy, immunization, family planning info etc.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifically names UNICEF."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per ANM job functions, a minimum of how many ANC visits (including registration) should be ensured for a pregnant woman?","options":["3 ANC visits","4 ANC visits","5 ANC visits","6 ANC visits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM should register and provide care ensuring a minimum of 4 ANC visits including registration.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated minimum is 4 ANC visits."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"According to ANM ANC schedule, the first ANC visit should be conducted:","options":["Within first 12 weeks (as soon as possible, for registration & early ANC check-up)","Between 14 and 26 weeks","Between 28 and 34 weeks","Between 36 weeks and term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first visit should be within the first 12 weeks, usually as soon as possible for registration and early ANC check-up.  Why B wrong: 14–26 weeks is the second visit.  Why C wrong: 28–34 weeks is the third visit.  Why D wrong: 36 weeks–term is the fourth visit."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per the ANM ANC schedule, the second, third and fourth visits are timed at which gestational periods respectively?","options":["14-26 weeks; 28-34 weeks; 36 weeks-term","12 weeks; 20 weeks; 32 weeks","16-28 weeks; 30-36 weeks; term","14-20 weeks; 24-30 weeks; 34 weeks-term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Second visit is 14–26 weeks, third visit is 28–34 weeks, and fourth visit is 36 weeks to term.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated ANC schedule."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As part of ANM job functions, the urine of a pregnant woman is tested for:","options":["Ketones and bilirubin","Albumin and sugar","Nitrites and blood","Glucose only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM tests urine of pregnant women for albumin and sugar.  Why A/C/D wrong: The specified tests are albumin and sugar."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The ANM refers all pregnant women to PHC/CHC for the RPR test, which is a test for:","options":["Malaria and blood grouping","Syphilis and blood grouping","HIV and hepatitis","Anemia and diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM refers all pregnant women to PHC/CHC for RPR test for syphilis and blood grouping.  Why A/C/D wrong: RPR is a syphilis test; the referral also includes blood grouping."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"According to ANM functions, for deliveries at home and sub-centre, post-natal home visits should be made on which days?","options":["0, 3, 7 and 42nd day","3, 7 and 42nd day","0, 7, 14 and 28th day","1, 5, 10 and 30th day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For home and sub-centre deliveries, post-natal home visits are made on 0, 3, 7 and 42nd day; for institutional delivery, visits are on 3, 7 and 42nd day.  Why B wrong: 3, 7 and 42nd day is for institutional deliveries.  Why C wrong: 0, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28th day applies to low birth weight babies.  Why D wrong: This schedule is not used."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"For a Low Birth Weight (LBW) baby, a total of how many post-natal visits are made, and on which days?","options":["Four visits on 0, 3, 7 and 42nd day","Six visits on 0, 3, 7, 14, 21 and 28th day","Three visits on 3, 7 and 42nd day","Five visits on 0, 7, 14, 21 and 28th day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For LBW babies, a total of six post-natal visits are made on 0, 3, 7, 14, 21 and 28th day to screen for congenital abnormalities and assess for danger signs per IMNCI.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the LBW visit schedule."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"The ANM prepares a monthly work schedule in the meeting of all ASHA workers held on which day, which is mandatory?","options":["First Monday of every month","Every 3rd Friday of every month","Last day of every month","15th of every month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM prepares a monthly work schedule in the meeting of all ASHA workers held on every 3rd Friday of every month, which is mandatory.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated day is every 3rd Friday."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"As per Infant and Young Child Feeding Guidelines followed by ANM, initiation of early breast-feeding should be done within what time of birth, with exclusive breastfeeding for how long?","options":["Within 2 hours; exclusive breastfeeding for 4 months","Within one hour of birth; exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months and timely weaning at 6 months","Within 30 minutes; exclusive breastfeeding for 3 months","Within 24 hours; exclusive breastfeeding for 12 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Initiation of early breast-feeding is within one hour of birth, with exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months and timely weaning at 6 months as per Infant and Young Child Feeding Guidelines.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated norms are within one hour and 6 months exclusive breastfeeding."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"Under ANM Family Planning functions, who is responsible for maintaining the eligible couple register?","options":["ASHA","ANM/MPW","Anganwadi Worker","Medical Officer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM/MPW will be responsible for maintaining the eligible couple register.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text assigns this responsibility to the ANM/MPW."},{"section":"Batch 003 · IPHS-2022 CHC/U-CHC human resources & staffing norms…","question":"According to the referenced PYQ (Raj. CHO-2023), IUCD insertion can be done by whom at the sub-centre?","options":["Only a Medical Officer","A trained ANM/MPW (F)","Only a gynecologist","ASHA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: As per the Raj. CHO-2023 PYQ note, IUCD insertion can be done by a trained ANM/MPW (F) at the sub-centre.  Why A/C wrong: A trained ANM/MPW is authorized, not only a physician/gynecologist.  Why D wrong: ASHA is not authorized to insert IUCDs."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under the ANM's responsibilities for Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP), what should she do for women requiring help?","options":["Perform the MTP herself at the sub-centre","Identify the women and refer them to the nearest approved institution","Provide oral abortifacient drugs at home","Refer only to private nursing homes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM is to identify women requiring help for MTP and refer them to the nearest approved institution.  Why A wrong: MTP is not performed by the ANM at the sub-centre.  Why C wrong: Providing abortifacients at home is not her role.  Why D wrong: Referral is to an approved institution, not specifically private nursing homes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"As part of MTP-related community education, the ANM should educate the community about the consequences of which practice?","options":["Institutional delivery","Unsafe abortion and septic abortion","Exclusive breastfeeding","Colostrum feeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She must educate the community about the consequences of unsafe abortion and septic abortion and inform them about availability of MTP services.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are beneficial practices, not consequences the community is warned against."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under nutrition responsibilities, the ANM identifies cases of low birth weight and malnutrition among infants and young children of which age group?","options":["Zero to five years","Zero to two years","Six to twelve years","One to ten years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: She identifies cases of low birth weight and malnutrition among infants and young children (zero to five years).  Why B, C, D wrong: The stated range is zero to five years."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"According to national guidelines, Iron and Folic Acid (IFA) tablets are distributed by the ANM to all EXCEPT:","options":["Pregnant women","Nursing mothers","Adolescent girls","Elderly men"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: IFA tablets are prescribed to pregnant women, nursing mothers, and adolescent girls (and syrups to young children up to five years); elderly men are not listed.  Why A, B, C wrong: All three are named IFA-tablet recipients."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"IFA is given in syrup form to which group as per national guidelines?","options":["Adolescent boys","Young children up to five years","Postmenopausal women","Male labourers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Syrups (IFA) are given to young children up to five years of age.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated syrup recipients; tablets go to pregnant/nursing women and adolescent girls."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which vaccine/supplement does the ANM administer to children as per the guidelines under nutrition activities?","options":["Vitamin D injection","Vitamin A solution","Vitamin K syrup","Calcium tablets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM administers Vitamin A solution to children as per the guidelines.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not listed under her nutrition responsibilities."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under the Universal Programme on Immunization (UIP), pregnant women are immunized with which vaccine?","options":["BCG","Tetanus toxoid (T.T. injection)","Measles vaccine","Hepatitis B"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under UIP the ANM immunizes pregnant women with tetanus toxoid (T.T. injection). [Note: source/IPHS-2012 states Tetanus Toxoid (TT); since 2018 MoHFW replaced TT with Td (tetanus-diphtheria) vaccine for pregnant women under UIP, but TT remains the best-fit option here.]  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the pregnancy immunization specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What does the abbreviation AEFI stand for in immunization services?","options":["Adverse Effect From Injection","Adverse Event Following Immunization","Acute Emergency For Infants","Annual Evaluation of Field Immunization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AEFI stands for Adverse Event Following Immunization.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"According to the ANM's UIP responsibilities, which events should be reported IMMEDIATELY?","options":["Routine monthly UIP reports only","Serious AEFI and outbreaks","Minor injection-site soreness","Cold chain temperature logs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Serious AEFI and outbreaks should be reported immediately.  Why A wrong: UIP reports are submitted monthly, not treated as immediate emergencies.  Why C wrong: Minor reactions are managed, not reported immediately.  Why D wrong: Logs are maintained routinely."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Who is responsible for cold chain maintenance for vaccines during fixed and outreach sessions?","options":["ASHA","ANM","Anganwadi helper","Village Health Guide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM is responsible for cold chain maintenance for vaccines during fixed and outreach sessions.  Why A, C, D wrong: They assist mobilization but are not the stated cold-chain custodian."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Waste generated during immunization should be managed as per which guidelines?","options":["WHO/UNICEF guidelines","GOI/CPCB guidelines","IPHS-2022 guidelines only","State PWD guidelines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immunization waste is managed as per GOI/CPCB (Central Pollution Control Board) guidelines.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the guideline named for immunization waste."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under IDSP surveillance, the ANM submits weekly surveillance reports for which conditions?","options":["Diabetes and hypertension","AFP (Acute Flaccid Paralysis) and Measles","Dengue and malaria only","Tuberculosis and leprosy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She submits weekly surveillance reports for AFP and Measles under IDSP, along with monthly UIP reports.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the conditions listed under this IDSP weekly reporting item."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"VAPP, which the ANM tracks and reports, stands for:","options":["Vaccine Associated Paralytic Poliomyelitis","Viral Acute Progressive Paralysis","Vaccine Adverse Paediatric Problem","Vector Associated Poliovirus Prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VAPP stands for Vaccine Associated Paralytic Poliomyelitis.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How often should the indent order of vaccines and logistics be placed, based on the due beneficiary list?","options":["Daily","Weekly","Monthly","Quarterly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Indent order of vaccines and logistics should be weekly, based on the due beneficiary list.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated frequency."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is maintained at each sub-centre to file updated counterfoils and used for immunization follow-up?","options":["Tracking Bag/Tickler box","Cold box only","MCH card wallet","Waste segregation bin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A Tracking Bag/Tickler box is maintained at each sub-centre with updated counterfoils, utilized for follow-up.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the follow-up tool described."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Regarding communicable diseases, when should the ANM notify the MO, PHC about an abnormal increase in infectious cases such as diarrhoea/dysentery or acute flaccid paralysis in a child?","options":["At the next monthly meeting","Immediately","After confirming lab reports","Only if more than 10 cases occur"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ANM must notify the MO, PHC immediately about any abnormal increase in such infectious cases.  Why A, C, D wrong: Immediate notification is required, not delayed or conditional."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"For all cases of diarrhoea/dysentery/vomiting in the community, the ANM should give:","options":["Intravenous antibiotics","Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS)","Anti-emetic injections only","Zinc injections"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She gives Oral Rehydration Solution to all cases of diarrhoea/dysentery/vomiting.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the first-line community measure specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Suspected cases of blindness, including suspected cataract cases, should be referred by the ANM to:","options":["The nearest optician","MO, PHC","A private eye camp only","District collector"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She identifies and refers all cases of blindness including suspected cataract to the MO, PHC.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated referral point."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"For which group of diseases does the ANM provide education, counselling, referral and follow-up under communicable disease control (as listed alongside STI/RTI)?","options":["Diabetes and stroke","HIV/AIDS","Cancer","Osteoporosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Education, counselling, referral and follow-up are for cases of STI/RTI and HIV/AIDS.  Why A wrong: Diabetes and stroke fall under non-communicable diseases.  Why C, D wrong: Not listed here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The ANM should identify suspected cases of dengue, malaria, JE, leprosy and kala-azar and, for seriously ill cases, advise them to visit which facility for immediate treatment?","options":["Sub-centre","PHC","Medical college","Anganwadi centre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She refers seriously ill suspected cases to visit the PHC for immediate treatment.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated referral is to the PHC."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under non-communicable diseases, the ANM conducts IEC activities and house-to-house surveys for which conditions?","options":["Diabetes, CVD, HTN and Stroke","Malaria and dengue","Tuberculosis and leprosy","Polio and measles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IEC activities and house-to-house surveys are for prevention/early detection of NCDs such as Diabetes, CVD, HTN and Stroke.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are communicable diseases, not NCDs."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under NCD control, the ANM motivates tobacco users to quit and refers them to which facility?","options":["Sub-centre de-addiction desk","Tobacco Cessation Centre at District Hospital","PHC only","Anganwadi centre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Motivation for quitting and referrals are to the Tobacco Cessation Centre at the District Hospital.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the specified referral centre."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"ASHAs ensure regular testing of salt at household level for the presence of iodine using:","options":["Litmus paper","Salt Testing Kits","Iodine tincture","pH meters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Regular testing of salt at household level for iodine is done through Salt Testing Kits by ASHAs.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the tool specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under NCD-related team activities, the ANM promotes formation and registration of which group?","options":["Self Help Care Group of adolescents","Self Health Care Group of Elderly Persons","Youth Red Cross","Mahila Mandal only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She promotes formation and registration of Self Health Care Group of Elderly Persons.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the group named under this NCD point."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under \"Vital Events,\" the ANM records and reports which events to health authorities?","options":["Marriages and divorces only","Births and deaths, particularly of mothers and infants","Migration statistics only","Property transfers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She records and reports vital events including births and deaths, particularly of mothers and infants in her area.  Why A, C, D wrong: Focus is on births and deaths of mothers/infants."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which register does the ANM prepare and maintain up-to-date at the sub-centre for record keeping?","options":["Birth-only register","Eligible couple and child register","Vaccine stock ledger only","Death register only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: She prepares the eligible couple and child register and maintains it up-to-date at the sub-centre.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the register specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Medical waste at the sub-centre must be disposed of as per which guidelines of the GOI?","options":["CPCB guidelines","IMEP guidelines","IPHS-2022 guidelines","IDSP guidelines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under team activities, medical waste is disposed as per the IMEP (Infection Management and Environment Plan) guidelines of GOI.  Why A wrong: CPCB is cited for immunization waste, not this team-activity item.  Why C, D wrong: Not the guideline named for medical waste disposal here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"According to the \"Note\" on the two ANMs at a sub-centre, which statement is TRUE?","options":["Both ANMs must always be present at the sub-centre together","It must be ensured that one ANM out of the two is available at the sub-centre while the other performs field duties","Only one ANM is posted per sub-centre","The second ANM has no defined job responsibilities"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One ANM must be available at the sub-centre and the other ANM performs field duties; the second ANM follows similar responsibilities.  Why A wrong: They are not required to be present together at all times.  Why C wrong: Two ANMs are referenced.  Why D wrong: The second ANM follows similar job responsibilities."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The ANM job responsibilities described are sourced from which guidelines?","options":["IPHS Guidelines for Sub-Centre, revised 2012","National Health Policy 2017","IPHS-2022 for PHC","WHO Alma-Ata Declaration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source cited is the Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS) Guidelines for Sub-Centre, revised 2012.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the cited source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Mahila Arogya Samiti (MAS) is one of the key community interventions of which mission?","options":["National Rural Health Mission (NRHM)","National Urban Health Mission (NUHM)","Reproductive and Child Health (RCH)","Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MAS is a key community intervention of the National Urban Health Mission (NUHM).  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the mission MAS belongs to."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"A Mahila Arogya Samiti (MAS) is typically a group of how many local women in an urban slum setting?","options":["5-8 women","10-12 local women","15-20 women","20-25 women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MAS is a group of 10-12 local women in an urban slum or slum-like setting.  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the stated group size."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Who is the Member Secretary of the Mahila Arogya Samiti (MAS)?","options":["Urban ASHA","ANM","Mukhya Sevika","CDPO"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Urban ASHA is the Member Secretary of MAS.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the designated member secretary."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Approximately how many households is a MAS expected to cover?","options":["10-20 households","50-100 households","200-300 households","400-800 households"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A MAS is expected to cover about 50-100 households.  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the stated coverage."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The MAS group should have 10-12 members but should not have fewer than or more than how many members?","options":["Not fewer than 5 or more than 15","Not fewer than 8 or more than 20","Not fewer than 10 or more than 25","Not fewer than 6 or more than 18"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The MAS should have 10-12 members but not fewer than 8 or more than 20 members.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect member limits."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under NUHM, what annual untied fund is provided for each MAS group for the welfare of society?","options":["Rs. 1,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 500"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under NUHM there is a provision of Rs. 5,000 as annual untied fund for each MAS group.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect amounts."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Rogi Kalyan Samitis (RKS) / Hospital Management Committees were introduced in which year and under which mission?","options":["2005 under NRHM","2013 under NUHM","1975 under ICDS","2017 under NHP"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RKS were introduced in 2005 under the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM).  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect year/mission."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The New Born Care Corner (NBCC) is a space located within which area?","options":["Postnatal ward","Delivery room / labour room","Neonatal ICU","Outpatient department"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NBCC is a space within the delivery/labour room where immediate care is provided to all newborns.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated location."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the size of a New Born Care Corner (NBCC) within the labour room?","options":["10-15 sq. ft.","20-30 sq. ft.","40-50 sq. ft.","5-10 sq. ft."],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NBCC is a space within the labour room, 20-30 sq. ft. in size, where a radiant warmer will be kept.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect dimensions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which equipment is kept in the New Born Care Corner?","options":["Radiant warmer","Incubator","Phototherapy unit","Ventilator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A radiant warmer is kept in the NBCC.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the equipment specified for the NBCC."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following is NOT a service provided in the New Born Care Corner?","options":["Care at birth and resuscitation","Provision of warmth","Major surgery","Early initiation of breastfeeding and weighing the neonate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NBCC services are care at birth, resuscitation, provision of warmth, early initiation of breastfeeding and weighing the neonate; major surgery is not among them.  Why A, B, D wrong: All are listed NBCC services."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Special Newborn Care Units (SNCUs) are established at which level?","options":["Sub-centre level","District Hospital & Medical College level","PHC level","Anganwadi centre level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SNCUs are established at District Hospital & Medical College level.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the level specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Newborn Stabilization Units (NBSU) are established at which facility?","options":["Sub-centre","Community Health Centres (CHC)/First Referral Unit (FRU)","District Hospital only","Medical College only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NBSUs are established at CHC/FRU level.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the level specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Mother Newborn Care Units (MNCU) are established with SNCU at which level?","options":["PHC level","District Hospital & Medical College level","Sub-centre level","CHC/FRU level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MNCUs are also established with SNCU at District Hospital & Medical College level.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the level specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"SNCU, NBSU, and MNCU are all established for the provision of care to sick and small newborns during which period?","options":["First month of life","First year of life","First week of life","First 42 days of life"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All these units are established for provision of care to sick and small newborns in the first month of life.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect time period."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"In India, the Village Health Guide (VHG) scheme was introduced on which date?","options":["2nd October 1975","2nd October 1977","15th August 1975","1st April 1977"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The VHG scheme was introduced on 2nd October 1977 to provide preventive, promotive, and basic curative care to rural populations.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect date."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The Village Health Guide (VHG) scheme was also known by which other name?","options":["ASHA scheme","Community Health Worker (CHW) / Community Health Volunteer (CHV) Scheme","Anganwadi Worker scheme","Dai scheme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: VHG's other names were Community Health Worker (CHW) Scheme and Community Health Volunteer (CHV) Scheme.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are separate cadres/schemes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the minimum educational qualification preferred for a Village Health Guide (VHG)?","options":["Class X pass","Literate and preferably formal education up to class VI, able to read and write","Graduate","No literacy required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: VHG must be literate and preferably have formal education up to class VI, able to read and write.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect qualification."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What honorarium was the Village Health Guide (VHG) paid per month?","options":["Rs. 50/- per month","Rs. 300/- per month","Rs. 1500/- per month","Rs. 500/- per month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The VHG is paid an honorarium of Rs. 50/- per month.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are figures for the dai (stipend/honorarium), not the VHG."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"After how much training at the local PHC and how many hours per day was the VHG expected to work?","options":["After 1 month of training, 1 hour per day","After 3 months of training, 2-3 hours per day","After 6 months of training, 4 hours per day","After 3 months of training, full-time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After 3 months of training at the local PHC centre, the VHG was expected to work 2-3 hours per day.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect training/work figures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"In which year was the VHG scheme replaced by the Accredited Social Health Activist (ASHA) program under NRHM?","options":["2005","1977","2013","2017"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 2005, the VHG scheme was replaced by the ASHA program under NRHM.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect year."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Local Dais are also known as:","options":["Community Health Workers","Traditional Birth Attendants (TBA)","Anganwadi Helpers","Mukhya Sevikas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dais are also known as Traditional Birth Attendants (TBA).  Why A, C, D wrong: Different cadres."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the training period for local dais?","options":["15 working days","30 working days","3 months","45 working days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The training period for dais is 30 working days.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect duration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"During training, how much stipend is a dai paid?","options":["Rs. 300 as stipend","Rs. 50 as stipend","Rs. 1500 as stipend","Rs. 500 as stipend"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During training the dai is paid Rs. 300 as stipend.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect figures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"As a part-time worker, a dai is paid an honorarium of how much per month?","options":["Rs. 300/month","Rs. 1500/month","Rs. 50/month","Rs. 3500/month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The dai is a part-time worker paid an honorarium of Rs. 1500/month.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect figures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"During training, at SC/PHC/MCH a dai is trained for how many days, with the remaining days spent with the health worker female?","options":["2 days at facility, remaining 4 days with health worker female","5 days at facility, remaining with ANM","10 days at facility","30 days entirely at PHC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Training is given at SC, PHC or MCH for 2 days, and for the remaining 4 days the dai needs to be with the health worker female.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect split."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"During the training period, how many deliveries must a dai conduct under supervision?","options":["1 delivery","2 deliveries","5 deliveries","10 deliveries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During training a dai needs to conduct 2 deliveries under the supervision and guidance of the health worker female/ANM/health assistant (female).  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect number."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"After successful completion of her training, each dai is provided with:","options":["A monthly salary and uniform","A delivery kit and a certificate","A bicycle and toolkit","A cold box"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After successful training each dai is provided a delivery kit and a certificate.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not what is provided."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"In which year did the Government of India launch the ICDS program to combat malnutrition and reduce IMR & MMR in children 0-6 years?","options":["1975","1977","2005","1985"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Govt of India launched the ICDS program in 1975.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect year."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What does ICDS stand for?","options":["Integrated Child Development Service","Indian Child Development Scheme","Integrated Community Development Service","Infant and Child Development Society"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICDS stands for Integrated Child Development Service scheme.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect expansion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How many months of training is an Anganwadi Worker (AWW) given under the ICDS program?","options":["2 months","4 months","6 months","3 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The AWW is chosen from the community and given 4 months of training in health, nutrition and child care under ICDS.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect duration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Who is the principal worker of the ICDS scheme?","options":["Anganwadi Helper","Anganwadi Worker","Mukhya Sevika","CDPO"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Anganwadi Worker is the principal worker of the ICDS scheme.  Why A, C, D wrong: They are basic functionary/supervisor/officer, not the principal worker."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"One Anganwadi Worker covers approximately how much population?","options":["500 population","1000 population","1500 population","5000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One Anganwadi Worker covers 1000 population.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect figure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How many anganwadi workers are supervised by a supervisor called Mukhya Sevika?","options":["10 to 15","20 to 25","4 to 6","30 to 40"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 20 to 25 anganwadi workers are supervised by a Mukhya Sevika.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect figure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How many Mukhya Sevikas are headed by a Child Development Project Officer (CDPO)?","options":["2","4","6","10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 4 Mukhya Sevikas are headed by a Child Development Project Officer (CDPO).  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect figure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The beneficiary population of ICDS/AWW includes nursing mothers, children 0-6 years, and women of which age range?","options":["12 to 40 years","15 and 45 years of age","18 to 49 years","20 to 45 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beneficiary population includes nursing mothers, women between 15 and 45 years of age, and children 0-6 years.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect range."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"According to the Poshan 2.0 program (2022), what is the age and qualification for an Anganwadi Worker (AWW)?","options":["18-35 years, 10th passed","18-35 years, 12th passed","21-40 years, graduate","18-45 years, 8th passed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: As per Poshan 2.0 (2022), age & qualification for AWW is 18-35 years, 12th passed (same for AWH).  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect criteria."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the population norm for establishment of a new Anganwadi Centre (AWC) in a rural/urban area?","options":["150-400","400-800","300-800","150-300"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For rural/urban area the AWC population norm is 400-800.  Why A, D wrong: These are Mini-AWC norms.  Why C wrong: 300-800 is the tribal/riverine/desert/hilly AWC norm."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the population norm for establishment of a new Anganwadi Centre (AWC) in a tribal/riverine/desert/hilly area?","options":["400-800","300-800","150-300","150-400"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For tribal/riverine/desert/hilly area the AWC norm is 300-800.  Why A wrong: That is the rural/urban AWC norm.  Why C, D wrong: These are Mini-AWC norms."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the honorarium for an AWW at a regular Anganwadi Centre (AWC)?","options":["Rs. 3500/month","Rs. 4500/month","Rs. 2250/month","Rs. 500/month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Honorarium at AWC for AWW is Rs. 4500/month.  Why A wrong: Rs. 3500/month is the mini-AWC honorarium.  Why C wrong: Rs. 2250/month is the AWH honorarium.  Why D wrong: Rs. 500/month is the AWW performance-linked incentive."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the population norm for a new Mini Anganwadi Centre in a rural/urban area?","options":["400-800","150-400","150-300","300-800"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For rural/urban area the Mini-AWC norm is 150-400.  Why A, D wrong: These are regular AWC norms.  Why C wrong: 150-300 is the tribal/riverine/desert/hilly Mini-AWC norm."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the population norm for a new Mini Anganwadi Centre in a tribal/riverine/desert/hilly area?","options":["150-400","150-300","300-800","400-800"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For tribal/riverine/desert/hilly area the Mini-AWC norm is 150-300.  Why A wrong: That is the rural/urban Mini-AWC norm.  Why C, D wrong: These are regular AWC norms."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the honorarium for an AWW at a Mini Anganwadi Centre?","options":["Rs. 4500/month","Rs. 3500/month","Rs. 2250/month","Rs. 500/month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Honorarium at mini AWC for AWW is Rs. 3500/month.  Why A wrong: Rs. 4500/month is the regular AWC honorarium.  Why C, D wrong: AWH honorarium and performance incentive respectively."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"\"Anganwadi on demand\" is provided where a settlement has at least how many children up to 6 years of age but no AWC?","options":["20 children","40 children","60 children","100 children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anganwadi on demand applies where a settlement has at least 40 children up to 6 years of age but no AWC.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect threshold."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"AWWs get a performance-linked incentive of how much per month under POSHAN Abhiyan for ICDS-CAS?","options":["Rs. 250/month","Rs. 500/month","Rs. 2250/month","Rs. 4500/month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AWWs get a performance-linked incentive of 500/month under POSHAN Abhiyan for ICDS-CAS.  Why A wrong: Rs. 250/month is the AWH incentive.  Why C, D wrong: These are honorarium figures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the honorarium for an Anganwadi Helper (AWH) per month?","options":["Rs. 4500/month","Rs. 3500/month","Rs. 2250/month","Rs. 500/month"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The honorarium for Anganwadi helper (AWH) is 2250/month.  Why A, B wrong: These are AWW honoraria.  Why D wrong: Rs. 500/month is the AWW performance incentive."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"AWHs are eligible for a performance-linked incentive of how much per month for facilitating proper functioning of Anganwadi centres?","options":["Rs. 250/month","Rs. 500/month","Rs. 2250/month","Rs. 100/month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AWHs are eligible for a performance-linked incentive of 250/month.  Why B wrong: Rs. 500/month is the AWW incentive.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect figures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under role & responsibilities, the AWW provides non-formal preschool education to children of which age?","options":["0-3 years","3-5 years","6-10 years","Under 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The AWW provides non-formal preschool education to children 3-5 years of age.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect age group."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The AWW provides supplementary nutrition to children of which age?","options":["Less than 6 years children","6-10 years children","10-14 years children","Only newborns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The AWW provides supplementary nutrition to <6 years children.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect age group."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The AWW implements which adolescent girl's health scheme (as an example)?","options":["Kishori Shakti Scheme","Janani Suraksha Yojana","Matru Vandana Yojana","Balika Samriddhi Yojana"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The AWW implements adolescent girl's health scheme like Kishori Shakti Scheme.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the scheme named in this AWW-role point."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the correct supervisory hierarchy in the ICDS/Anganwadi structure?","options":["Anganwadi Worker → Mukhya Sevika → Child Development Projects Officer (CDPO)","CDPO → Mukhya Sevika → Anganwadi Worker → ASHA","Mukhya Sevika → CDPO → Anganwadi Worker","Anganwadi Worker → CDPO → Mukhya Sevika"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows Anganwadi Worker → Mukhya Sevika → Child Development Projects Officer (CDPO).  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect order."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"In the Union Budget 2022, the Govt of India set a goal to upgrade how many anganwadis under \"Saksham Anganwadi\"?","options":["1 lakh","2 lakh","5 lakh","50,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Union Budget 2022, GOI set goals to upgrade 2 lakh anganwadis in Saksham Anganwadi.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect figure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"According to SRS-2020 (report published Sept. 2022), what was the annual decline rate of U5MR in India during 2019-2020?","options":["6.0%","8.6%","5.6%","2.0%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The annual decline rate of U5MR in India during 2019-2020 was 8.6%.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect figure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the Total Fertility Rate (TFR) of India according to the SRS report-2020?","options":["2.2","2.0","1.8","2.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's TFR according to SRS report-2020 is 2.0.  Why A wrong: 2.2 is the SRS-2018 value.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the Crude Death Rate (CDR) of Rajasthan according to SRS report-2020?","options":["6.0","5.6","19.5","23.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CDR of Rajasthan according to SRS report-2020 is 5.6.  Why A wrong: 6.0 is India's CDR.  Why C, D wrong: These are CBR figures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is India's Crude Birth Rate (CBR) according to SRS report-2020?","options":["23.5","19.5","6.0","5.6"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's CBR according to SRS report-2020 is 19.5.  Why A wrong: 23.5 is Rajasthan's CBR.  Why C, D wrong: These are death-rate figures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the Crude Birth Rate (CBR) of Rajasthan according to SRS report-2020?","options":["19.5","23.5","5.6","6.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CBR of Rajasthan according to SRS report-2020 is 23.5.  Why A wrong: 19.5 is India's CBR.  Why C, D wrong: These are death-rate figures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which state recorded the lowest Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) according to SRS report-2020?","options":["Kerala","Mizoram (3)","Madhya Pradesh","Tamil Nadu"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lowest IMR was recorded in Mizoram (3) per SRS report-2020.  Why A, C, D wrong: MP had the highest IMR; the others are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which state recorded the highest Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) according to SRS report-2020?","options":["Mizoram (3)","Madhya Pradesh (43)","Rajasthan (28)","Bihar (32)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The highest IMR was in MP (43) per SRS report-2020.  Why A wrong: Mizoram (3) had the lowest.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated highest value."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is India's Crude Death Rate (CDR) according to SRS report-2020?","options":["5.6","6.0","6.2","19.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's CDR according to SRS report-2020 is 6.0.  Why A wrong: 5.6 is Rajasthan's CDR.  Why C wrong: 6.2 is the SRS-2018 value.  Why D wrong: 19.5 is India's CBR."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is India's Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) according to SRS report-2020?","options":["32","28","43","3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's IMR according to SRS report-2020 is 28.  Why A wrong: 32 is the SRS-2018 value.  Why C wrong: 43 is MP's IMR.  Why D wrong: 3 is Mizoram's lowest IMR."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is India's Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) according to SRS report-2020?","options":["113 per 1 lakh","97 per 1 lakh","130 per 1 lakh","70 per 1 lakh"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's MMR according to SRS report-2020 is 97 per 1 lakh.  Why A wrong: 113 is the SRS-2018 value.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How many states achieved the NHP-2017 target for MMR (per SRS-2020 quick review)?","options":["11 States","8 States","5 States","3 States"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 8 states achieved the NHP-2017 target for MMR.  Why A wrong: 11 states achieved SDG 2030 for U5MR.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How many Indian states achieved the SDG target 2030 for U5MR (per SRS-2020 quick review)?","options":["8 States","11 States","5 States","3 States"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 11 states achieved the SDG target 2030 for U5MR.  Why A wrong: 8 states achieved the NHP-2017 MMR target.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"At the sub-centre level, which drug/equipment kits are provided?","options":["Drug kit-A for ANM, Drug Kit-B, and Sub-Centre Equipment kit-C","PHC Equipment Kit-D only","Kit-E Laparotomy Set","Kit-F Mini Laparotomy set"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At sub-centre level, Drug kit-A (for ANM), Drug Kit-B, and Sub-Centre Equipment kit-C are provided.  Why B wrong: Kit-D is at PHC level.  Why C, D wrong: Kits E and F are at CHC/FRU level."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which equipment kit is provided at the PHC level?","options":["Kit-C","PHC Equipment Kit-D","Kit-E","Kit-A"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At PHC level, PHC Equipment Kit-D is provided.  Why A wrong: Kit-C is at sub-centre level.  Why C wrong: Kit-E is at CHC/FRU level.  Why D wrong: Kit-A is the ANM drug kit at sub-centre."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"At the CHC/FRU level, Kit-E and Kit-F correspond to which sets?","options":["Kit-E Laparotomy Set and Kit-F Mini Laparotomy set","Kit-E IUD insertion Set and Kit-F Vasectomy set","Kit-E Normal Delivery Set and Kit-F Vacuum Extraction Set","Kit-E Embryotomy Set and Kit-F Uterine Evacuation Set"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At CHC/FRU level, Kit-E is the Laparotomy Set and Kit-F is the Mini Laparotomy set.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are other lettered kits (G/H, I/J, K/L)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which kit is the IUD insertion Set?","options":["Kit G","Kit H","Kit I","Kit J"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kit G is the IUD insertion Set.  Why B wrong: Kit H is the Vasectomy set.  Why C wrong: Kit I is the Normal Delivery Set.  Why D wrong: Kit J is the Vacuum Extraction Set."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Kit H corresponds to which set?","options":["IUD insertion Set","Vasectomy set","Normal Delivery Set","Embryotomy Set"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit H is the Vasectomy set.  Why A wrong: Kit G is IUD insertion.  Why C wrong: Kit I is Normal Delivery.  Why D wrong: Kit K is Embryotomy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which kit is the Normal Delivery Set?","options":["Kit G","Kit I","Kit J","Kit M"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit I is the Normal Delivery Set.  Why A wrong: Kit G is IUD insertion.  Why C wrong: Kit J is Vacuum Extraction.  Why D wrong: Kit M is Equipment for Anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Kit J corresponds to which set?","options":["Vacuum Extraction Set","Embryotomy Set","Uterine Evacuation Set","Neonatal Resuscitation set"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kit J is the Vacuum Extraction Set.  Why B wrong: Kit K is Embryotomy.  Why C wrong: Kit L is Uterine Evacuation.  Why D wrong: Kit N is Neonatal Resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which kit is the Embryotomy Set?","options":["Kit J","Kit K","Kit L","Kit M"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit K is the Embryotomy Set.  Why A wrong: Kit J is Vacuum Extraction.  Why C wrong: Kit L is Uterine Evacuation.  Why D wrong: Kit M is Equipment for Anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Kit L corresponds to which set?","options":["Uterine Evacuation Set","Equipment for Anaesthesia","Neonatal Resuscitation set","Donor Blood Transfusion Set"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kit L is the Uterine Evacuation Set.  Why B wrong: Kit M is Equipment for Anaesthesia.  Why C wrong: Kit N is Neonatal Resuscitation.  Why D wrong: Kit P is Donor Blood Transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which kit is the Equipment for Anaesthesia?","options":["Kit L","Kit M","Kit N","Kit O"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit M is the Equipment for Anaesthesia.  Why A wrong: Kit L is Uterine Evacuation.  Why C wrong: Kit N is Neonatal Resuscitation.  Why D wrong: Kit O is Equipment and reagents for blood test."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Kit N corresponds to which set?","options":["Equipment for Anaesthesia","Neonatal Resuscitation set","Equipment and reagents for blood test","Donor Blood Transfusion Set"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit N is the Neonatal Resuscitation set.  Why A wrong: Kit M is Equipment for Anaesthesia.  Why C wrong: Kit O is Equipment and reagents for blood test.  Why D wrong: Kit P is Donor Blood Transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which kit contains equipment and reagents for blood test?","options":["Kit N","Kit O","Kit P","Kit M"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit O is Equipment and reagents for blood test.  Why A wrong: Kit N is Neonatal Resuscitation.  Why C wrong: Kit P is Donor Blood Transfusion.  Why D wrong: Kit M is Equipment for Anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Kit P corresponds to which set?","options":["Equipment and reagents for blood test","Donor Blood Transfusion Set","Neonatal Resuscitation set","Uterine Evacuation Set"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit P is the Donor Blood Transfusion Set.  Why A wrong: Kit O is Equipment and reagents for blood test.  Why C wrong: Kit N is Neonatal Resuscitation.  Why D wrong: Kit L is Uterine Evacuation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following medicines is included in Drug Kit-A? (Select all that apply)","options":["ORS","IFA tablets (large and small)","Vitamin A syrup","Oxytocin injection","Zinc sulphate dispersible tablets USP"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Drug Kit-A contents include ORS, IFA tablets large and small, folic acid tablets IP, cotrimoxazole (paediatric), gentian violet crystals, zinc sulphate dispersible tablets USP, IFA syrup, water-miscible vitamin concentrate, and Vitamin A syrup.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin injection is in Drug Kit-B for sub-centres, not Kit-A."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Methylrosanilinium chloride, an ingredient of Drug Kit-A, is commonly known as:","options":["Povidone iodine","Gentian violet crystals","Chloramphenicol","Silver nitrate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Methylrosanilinium chloride is gentian violet crystals, listed in Drug Kit-A.  Why A, C, D wrong: Different agents."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following is included in Drug Kit-B for Sub Centres? (Select all that apply)","options":["Methylergometrine tablets IP","Misoprostol tablets","Oxytocin injection","ORS","Intravenous fluids (Ringer lactate)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Drug Kit-B includes methylergometrine tablets IP, misoprostol tablets, oxytocin injection, paracetamol, methylergometrine injection, albendazole, dicyclomine, chloramphenicol eye ointment, povidone iodine ointment, cotton bandage, absorbent cotton, and IV fluids (Ringer lactate).  Why D wrong: ORS is in Drug Kit-A, not Kit-B."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which intravenous fluid is included in Drug Kit-B for Sub Centres?","options":["Normal saline","Ringer lactate","Dextrose 5%","Mannitol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drug Kit-B lists Intravenous fluids (Ringer lactate).  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the IV fluid specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following is the definition of Vital Statistics?","options":["Data on live births, deaths, migration, foetal deaths, marriages and divorces","Data on hospital admissions only","Data on communicable diseases only","Data on per-capita income only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vital statistics is accumulated data gathered on live births, deaths, migration, foetal deaths, marriages and divorces.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are narrower/other data types."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Under which Article of the Indian Constitution is the responsibility of the census of the country given to the Central Government?","options":["Article 21","Article 246","Article 44","Article 342"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Responsibility of census of the country is given to the Central Government through Article 246 of the Indian Constitution.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect articles."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How often is the SRS (Sample Registration System) report for Vital Statistics published, and by whom?","options":["Every year, by NITI Aayog","Every 2 years, by the Registrar General of India (RGI)","Every 5 years, by WHO","Every 10 years, by the Census Commissioner only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SRS report for Vital Statistics is published every 2 years by the Registrar General of India (RGI).  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect frequency/authority."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Health indicators are best defined as:","options":["Only communicable disease counts","Quantitative or statistical measures/instruments used to measure the health status of an individual or defined group","Government financial data","Hospital budget allocations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Health indicators are quantitative or statistical measures/instruments used for measurement of health status of an individual or defined group.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect definitions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following is a MORTALITY indicator? (Select all that apply)","options":["Infant mortality rate (IMR)","Under 5 mortality indicator (U5MR)","Maternal mortality rate (MMR)","Per capita income","Notification rate"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: IMR, U5MR, MMR, CDR/CBR, disease-specific/proportional/child mortality rate, life expectancy, case fertility rate, NMR and GFR are mortality indicators.  Why D wrong: Per capita income is a socioeconomic status indicator.  Why E wrong: Notification rate is a morbidity indicator."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following is a MORBIDITY indicator?","options":["Infant mortality rate","Notification rate","Per capita income","Literacy rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Morbidity indicators include incidence & prevalence, notification rate, OPD/health-centre attendance rate, duration of hospital stay, absence from work, and admission/readmission/discharge rate.  Why A wrong: IMR is a mortality indicator.  Why C, D wrong: Socioeconomic status indicators."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following is a SOCIOECONOMIC status indicator? (Select all that apply)","options":["Per capita income","Dependency ratio","Literacy rate","Notification rate","Rate of population increase"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Socioeconomic status indicators include per capita income, dependency ratio, level of unemployment, literacy rate, housing, family size and rate of population increase.  Why D wrong: Notification rate is a morbidity indicator."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the formula for Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)?","options":["(No. of deaths of children up to 1 year of age in a year / Total number of live births in the same year) × 1000","(Number of maternal deaths / Number of live births) × 1,00,000","(Number of deaths under 5 years / Total number of deaths) × 1000","(Number of live births in a year / Average population) × 1000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IMR = No. of deaths of children up to 1 year of age in a year / Total number of live births in the same year × 1000.  Why B wrong: That is the MMR formula.  Why C wrong: That is the U5MR formula given.  Why D wrong: That is the CBR formula."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"According to the Vital Statistics table, what is India's IMR value for SRS-2020 versus SRS-2018?","options":["28 (2020) and 32 (2018)","32 (2020) and 28 (2018)","20 (2020) and 23 (2018)","43 (2020) and 32 (2018)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives IMR as 28 for SRS-2020 and 32 for SRS-2018.  Why B wrong: Reverses the values.  Why C wrong: Those are NNMR values.  Why D wrong: 43 is MP's IMR, not the national figure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The Neonatal Mortality Rate (NNMR) refers to the number of neonates dying before reaching what age, per 1000 live births?","options":["28 days of age","7 days of age","1 year of age","42 days of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NNMR is the number of neonates dying before reaching 28 days of age per 1000 live births.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What are the NNMR values for SRS-2020 and SRS-2018 respectively?","options":["20 and 23","28 and 32","6.0 and 6.2","32 and 36"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives NNMR as 20 (SRS-2020) and 23 (SRS-2018).  Why B wrong: These are IMR values.  Why C wrong: These are CDR values.  Why D wrong: These are U5MR values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the formula for the Crude Death Rate (CDR)?","options":["(Number of Death / Mid year population) × 1000","(Number of Live births / Average population) × 1000","(Number of maternal deaths / Number of live births) × 1,00,000","(Number of deaths under 5 / Total number of deaths) × 1000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CDR = Number of Death / Mid year population × 1000.  Why B wrong: That is CBR.  Why C wrong: That is MMR.  Why D wrong: That is U5MR."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What are the Crude Death Rate (CDR) values for SRS-2020 and SRS-2018?","options":["6.0 and 6.2","19.5 and 20","28 and 32","2.0 and 2.2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CDR is 6.0 (SRS-2020) and 6.2 (SRS-2018).  Why B wrong: These are CBR values.  Why C wrong: These are IMR values.  Why D wrong: These are TFR values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What are the Crude Birth Rate (CBR) values for India per SRS-2020 and SRS-2018?","options":["19.5 and 20","6.0 and 6.2","23.5 and 23.5","28 and 32"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives CBR as 19.5 (SRS-2020) and 20 (SRS-2018).  Why B wrong: These are CDR values.  Why C wrong: 23.5 is Rajasthan's CBR.  Why D wrong: These are IMR values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Case Fertility Ratio (CFR) is defined as the proportion of cases of a disease that are fatal within a specified period; it is closely related to:","options":["Virulence","Incubation period","Herd immunity","Case detection rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CFR is the proportion of cases of a disease/condition that is fatal within a specified period; it is closely related to virulence.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated relationship."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The Under Five Mortality Rate (U5MR) is defined as:","options":["Probability of dying between birth and exact age 5 years, expressed per 1000 live births","Deaths before 28 days of age per 1000 live births","Deaths before 1 year of age per 1000 live births","Deaths in reproductive-age women per 1 lakh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: U5MR is the probability of dying between birth and exact age 5 years, expressed per 1000 live births.  Why B wrong: That is NNMR.  Why C wrong: That is IMR.  Why D wrong: That relates to MMR."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What are the Under Five Mortality Rate (U5MR) values for SRS-2020 and SRS-2018?","options":["32 and 36","28 and 32","20 and 23","6.0 and 6.2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: U5MR is 32 (SRS-2020) and 36 (SRS-2018).  Why B wrong: These are IMR values.  Why C wrong: These are NNMR values.  Why D wrong: These are CDR values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the denominator (per what number of live births) used in the Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) formula?","options":["Per 1000 live births","Per 1,00,000 live births","Per 10,000 live births","Per 100 live births"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MMR = Number of maternal deaths / Number of live births × 1,00,000, i.e. per 1,00,000 (1 lakh) live births.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect denominator."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"According to the MMR definition, maternal death includes death due to complication of pregnancy, childbirth, and within how many days of post-delivery?","options":["28 days","42 days","7 days","60 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MMR counts female deaths due to complications of pregnancy, childbirth and within 42 days of post-delivery.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect period."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What are the Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) values for SRS-2020 and SRS-2018?","options":["97 and 113","113 and 97","130 and 97","6.0 and 6.2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MMR is 97 (SRS-2020) and 113 (SRS-2018).  Why B wrong: Reverses the values.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the Life Time Risk value given in the Vital Statistics table (SRS-2020), and how is it defined?","options":["0.21 — probability that at least one woman of reproductive age (15-49 years) will die due to childbirth or puerperium","6.0 — probability of dying before age 5","2.0 — average children per woman","97 — maternal deaths per lakh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Life Time Risk is 0.21, defined as the probability that at least one woman of reproductive age (15-49 years) will die due to childbirth or puerperium.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe other indicators."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The Total Fertility Rate (TFR) is defined as:","options":["The average number of children a woman will have during her lifetime (children per woman)","Number of females per 1000 males","Deaths under age 5 per 1000 live births","Live births per average population × 1000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TFR is the average number of children a woman will have during her lifetime, measured in children per woman.  Why B wrong: That is the sex ratio.  Why C wrong: That is U5MR.  Why D wrong: That is CBR."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What are the Total Fertility Rate (TFR) values for SRS-2020 and SRS-2018?","options":["2.0 and 2.2","2.2 and 2.0","2.0 and 2.0","1.8 and 2.0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TFR is 2.0 (SRS-2020) and 2.2 (SRS-2018).  Why B wrong: Reverses the values.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The General Fertility Rate (GFR) is a ratio of total registered live births to the female population of \"child bearing age,\" defined as which age range?","options":["15 to 49 years","15 to 45 years","18 to 49 years","20 to 44 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GFR is the ratio of total registered live births to the female population 'child bearing age' from 15 to 49 years.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect age range."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the GFR value given for SRS-2018 in the Vital Statistics table?","options":["70.4","67.2","2.2","97"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives GFR as 70.4 (SRS-2018 column).  Why B wrong: 67.2 years is life expectancy (HDI-2022).  Why C wrong: 2.2 is TFR (2018).  Why D wrong: 97 is MMR (2020)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The Perinatal Mortality Rate (PMR) covers deaths of late fetus (stillbirth) and early neonatal deaths weighing 1000 gm or more, between how many weeks of gestation to the first week of neonatal life?","options":["28 weeks of gestation","20 weeks of gestation","22 weeks of gestation","24 weeks of gestation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMR is defined for deaths between 28 weeks of gestation to the first week of neonatal life, weighing 1000 gm or more.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect gestational cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The Postnatal Mortality Rate (PNMR) refers to deaths of children between which ages, relative to total live births?","options":["Between 28 days and 1 year of age","Before 28 days of age","Before 7 days of age","Between 1 and 5 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PNMR = number of deaths of children between 28 days and 1 year of age in a given year × 1000 / total live births in the same year.  Why B wrong: That is NNMR.  Why C wrong: That is early neonatal.  Why D wrong: Beyond the PNMR range."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How is Sex Ratio defined, and what is its value per NFHS-5 in the table?","options":["Number of females per 1000 males; 1020 (NFHS-5)","Number of males per 1000 females; 940","Number of females per 100 males; 108","Number of live births per 1000 population; 19.5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sex Ratio is defined as the number of females per 1000 males; the table gives 1020 (NFHS-5).  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect definition/value."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"What is the Life Expectancy value given in the table (HDI-2022)?","options":["67.2 years","70.4 years","60 years","75 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Life Expectancy is given as 67.2 years (HDI-2022).  Why B wrong: 70.4 is the GFR value.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"How is Survival Rate calculated according to the Vital Statistics table?","options":["(Total number of patients alive after a particular time period / Total number of patients diagnosed or treated) × 1000","(Number of deaths / Mid year population) × 1000","(Stillbirth + early neonatal death) / live birth × 1000","(Number of live births / Average population) × 1000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Survival Rate = Total number of patients alive after a particular time period / Total number of patients diagnosed or treated × 1000.  Why B wrong: That is CDR.  Why C wrong: That is PMR.  Why D wrong: That is CBR."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The Maternal Mortality Rate (MMRate), distinct from MMR, uses which denominator?","options":["1,00,000 women of reproductive age during the same time period","1,00,000 live births","1000 live births","Mid-year total population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MMRate is the number of maternal deaths in a given period per 1,00,000 women of reproductive age during the same time period (value 6.0 in the table).  Why B wrong: That denominator (live births) is used for MMR (the ratio), not the MMRate.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect denominators."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following are Anganwadi Centre (AWC) services as listed? (Select all that apply)","options":["Supplementary nutrition","Non-formal pre-school education","Immunization and health check-up","Referral services","Major surgical procedures"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: AWC centres provide supplementary nutrition, non-formal pre-school education, nutrition and health education, immunization, health check-up and referral services.  Why E wrong: Major surgical procedures are not an AWC service."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"The ICDS program was launched with the primary aim to combat malnutrition/hunger in children of which age group and reduce IMR & MMR?","options":["Children 0-6 years","Children 0-5 years","Children 6-14 years","Adolescents 10-19 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICDS was launched to combat malnutrition and hunger in children 0-6 years and to reduce IMR & MMR.  Why B, C, D wrong: Incorrect age group for ICDS."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which functions are performed by the Village Health Guide (VHG)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Provide treatment to common and minor ailments","MCH care","Education & awareness about family planning services","Health education about first aid during accident and emergency","Conducting major deliveries independently"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: VHG functions include treatment of common/minor ailments, MCH care, family-planning education, first-aid health education, and awareness about government welfare programs.  Why E wrong: Conducting major deliveries independently is not a listed VHG function."},{"section":"Batch 004 · ANM job responsibilities…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the Local Dai (TBA)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Non-formal pre-school education","Supplementary Nutrition","Safe water and basic sanitation","MCH care and Family planning","Immunization and Referral services"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Dai functions listed are non-formal pre-school education, supplementary nutrition, health education about health, safe water and basic sanitation, MCH care and family planning, and immunization and referral services.  Why any omitted would be wrong: All five listed options are stated dai functions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per the SRS Report-2020 (published Sept. 2022), what is the crude birth rate (births per thousand population) for India?","options":["6.0","19.5","28","23.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SRS Report-2020 gives India's birth rate as 19.5 per thousand population.  Why A wrong: 6.0 is the death rate per thousand population.  Why C wrong: 28 is the Infant Mortality Rate (infant deaths per thousand live births).  Why D wrong: 23.5 is Rajasthan's birth rate, not India's."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"According to SRS Report-2020, the death rate for India is:","options":["5.6 per thousand","6.0 per thousand","7.9 per thousand","3.6 per thousand"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's Crude Death Rate per SRS-2020 is 6.0 per thousand population.  Why A wrong: 5.6 is Rajasthan's CDR.  Why C wrong: 7.9 is the highest CDR (Chhattisgarh).  Why D wrong: 3.6 is the lowest CDR (Delhi)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Per SRS Report-2020, the Infant Mortality Rate is expressed as infant deaths per thousand live births. What is India's value?","options":["32","43","28","3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: India's IMR per SRS-2020 is 28 infant deaths per thousand live births.  Why A wrong: 32 is Rajasthan's IMR (and also India's U5MR).  Why B wrong: 43 is the highest IMR (M.P.).  Why D wrong: 3 is the lowest IMR (Mizoram)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) is expressed per:","options":["1,000 live births","10,000 live births","1,00,000 live births","100 live births"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: MMR is expressed per 1,00,000 (one lakh) live births.  Why A wrong: Per 1,000 live births is the denominator for IMR, not MMR.  Why B wrong: 10,000 is not the standard MMR denominator.  Why D wrong: Per 100 is used for percentages/prevalence, not MMR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"What forms the denominator of the Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)? (Raj. CHO-2023)","options":["Total number of maternal deaths during the same year","Total number of live births during the same year","Total number of pregnancies during the same year","Total mid-year population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Denominator of MMR is the total number of live births during the same year.  Why A wrong: Total maternal deaths is the numerator of MMR.  Why C wrong: Pregnancies are not used as the MMR denominator.  Why D wrong: Mid-year population is used for crude rates, not MMR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The numerator of the Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) is:","options":["Total number of live births","Total number of maternal deaths","Total number of infant deaths","Total number of neonatal deaths"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Numerator of MMR = total number of maternal deaths.  Why A wrong: Live births form the denominator.  Why C wrong: Infant deaths relate to IMR.  Why D wrong: Neonatal deaths relate to neonatal mortality rate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The most common cause of Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) is:","options":["Anaemia","Obstetric hemorrhage","Sepsis","Eclampsia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of MMR is obstetric hemorrhage.  Why A wrong: Anaemia is the most common cause of maternal morbidity, not mortality.  Why C wrong: Sepsis is a cause but not the most common.  Why D wrong: Eclampsia is a cause but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The most common cause of maternal morbidity is:","options":["Obstetric hemorrhage","Hypertension","Anaemia","Infection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Anaemia is the most common cause of maternal morbidity.  Why A wrong: Obstetric hemorrhage is the most common cause of maternal mortality.  Why B wrong: Hypertension is a contributor but not the most common morbidity cause here.  Why D wrong: Infection is not listed as the most common morbidity cause."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per SRS-2020, India's MMR is:","options":["113","195","97","70"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: India's MMR per SRS-2020 is 97.  Why A wrong: 113 is Rajasthan's MMR.  Why B wrong: 195 is the highest MMR (Assam).  Why D wrong: 70 is the SDG target (less than 70/lakh by 2030), not the current value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which state has the highest Maternal Mortality Ratio per SRS-2020?","options":["Kerala","Assam","Rajasthan","Bihar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assam has the highest MMR at 195.  Why A wrong: Kerala has the lowest MMR (19).  Why C wrong: Rajasthan's MMR is 113.  Why D wrong: Bihar has the highest birth rate, not MMR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which state has the lowest Maternal Mortality Ratio (19) per SRS-2020?","options":["Assam","Gujarat","Kerala","Karnataka"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Kerala has the lowest MMR at 19.  Why A wrong: Assam has the highest MMR (195).  Why B wrong: Gujarat's MMR is 57.  Why D wrong: Karnataka's MMR is 69."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"With an MMR of 97, India has achieved which policy target?","options":["SDG target of MMR less than 70/lakh","NHP-2017 policy target of MMR less than 100/lakh live births","MDG target of MMR less than 50/lakh","NHP-2025 target of MMR less than 90/lakh"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India achieved the NHP-2017 policy target of MMR less than 100/lakh live births.  Why A wrong: The SDG target (<70/lakh by 2030) is still being pursued, not yet achieved.  Why C wrong: This is not the target stated in the source.  Why D wrong: No such NHP-2025 MMR target of 90 is stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The SDG target for MMR that India is on track to achieve by 2030 is:","options":["Less than 100/lakh live births","Less than 70/lakh live births","Less than 50/lakh live births","Less than 130/lakh live births"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SDG target is MMR less than 70/lakh live births by 2030.  Why A wrong: <100/lakh was the NHP-2017 target, already achieved.  Why C wrong: Not the stated SDG figure.  Why D wrong: 130 was the 2014-16 MMR value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"According to the source, how many states of India have achieved the SDG target for MMR?","options":["Six","Eight","Eleven","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eight states of India have achieved the SDG MMR target.  Why A wrong: Six is not the number stated.  Why C wrong: Eleven states/UT attained the U5MR SDG target, not MMR.  Why D wrong: Five is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which of the following is NOT among the eight states that achieved the SDG target for MMR? (Select the state with its listed MMR value)","options":["Kerala (19)","Maharashtra (33)","Bihar (25.5)","Telangana (43)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Bihar (25.5) refers to the highest birth rate figure, not an MMR-SDG-achieving state; the eight states are Kerala, Maharashtra, Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, Jharkhand, Gujarat, and Karnataka.  Why A wrong: Kerala (19) is one of the eight states.  Why B wrong: Maharashtra (33) is one of the eight states.  Why D wrong: Telangana (43) is one of the eight states."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Among the eight states that achieved the SDG MMR target, what is the listed MMR value for Andhra Pradesh?","options":["45","54","56","57"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Andhra Pradesh's listed MMR is 45.  Why B wrong: 54 is Tamil Nadu.  Why C wrong: 56 is Jharkhand.  Why D wrong: 57 is Gujarat."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"What is India's Under-5 Mortality Rate (U5MR) as stated in the source?","options":["28","31","32","33"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: India's U5MR is 32.  Why A wrong: 28 is India's IMR.  Why B wrong: 31 is the male U5MR.  Why D wrong: 33 is the female U5MR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Regarding U5MR by sex, which statement is correct?","options":["U5MR for male (33) is higher than female (31)","U5MR for female (33) is higher than male (31)","U5MR is equal for both sexes at 32","U5MR for female (31) is higher than male (33)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: U5MR for female (33) is higher than male (31).  Why A wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: They are not equal.  Why D wrong: The values are reversed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The highest decline of U5MR is observed in which states?","options":["Kerala and Tamil Nadu","U.P. and Karnataka","Bihar and Assam","Maharashtra and Gujarat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The highest decline of U5MR is observed in U.P. and Karnataka.  Why A wrong: Not stated as highest-decline states.  Why C wrong: Not stated.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"How many States/UT have already attained the SDG target of U5MR (≤25 by 2030)?","options":["Eight","Eleven","Fifteen","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eleven (11) States/UT have attained the U5MR SDG target of ≤25 by 2030.  Why A wrong: Eight is the number for the MMR SDG target.  Why C wrong: Fifteen is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Five is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Among the states attaining the U5MR SDG target, which has the lowest listed U5MR value of 8?","options":["Tamil Nadu","Delhi","Kerala","J&K"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Kerala has a U5MR of 8, the lowest listed.  Why A wrong: Tamil Nadu is 13.  Why B wrong: Delhi is 14.  Why D wrong: J&K is 17."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"India's Crude Death Rate (CDR) per the source is:","options":["5.6","6.0","7.9","3.6"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's CDR is 6.0.  Why A wrong: 5.6 is Rajasthan's CDR.  Why C wrong: 7.9 is the highest CDR (Chhattisgarh).  Why D wrong: 3.6 is the lowest CDR (Delhi)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which state/UT has the highest Crude Death Rate (7.9)?","options":["Delhi","Rajasthan","Chhattisgarh","Bihar"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Chhattisgarh has the highest CDR at 7.9.  Why A wrong: Delhi has the lowest CDR (3.6).  Why B wrong: Rajasthan's CDR is 5.6.  Why D wrong: Bihar is not listed for CDR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which state/UT has the lowest Crude Death Rate (3.6)?","options":["Chhattisgarh","Delhi","Kerala","Rajasthan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delhi has the lowest CDR at 3.6.  Why A wrong: Chhattisgarh has the highest CDR (7.9).  Why C wrong: Kerala is not listed for lowest CDR.  Why D wrong: Rajasthan's CDR is 5.6."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which state has the highest birth rate (25.5) per the source?","options":["Rajasthan","Andaman Nikobar","Bihar","Kerala"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Bihar has the highest birth rate at 25.5.  Why A wrong: Rajasthan's birth rate is 23.5.  Why B wrong: Andaman Nikobar has the lowest birth rate (10.8).  Why D wrong: Kerala is not listed as highest."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which region has the lowest birth rate (10.8) per the source?","options":["Bihar","Andaman Nikobar","Delhi","Mizoram"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Andaman Nikobar has the lowest birth rate at 10.8.  Why A wrong: Bihar has the highest birth rate (25.5).  Why C wrong: Delhi is not listed here.  Why D wrong: Mizoram has the lowest IMR (3), not lowest birth rate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) is expressed per how many live births?","options":["100","1,000","10,000","1,00,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IMR is expressed per 1000 live births.  Why A wrong: Per 100 is used for percentages.  Why C wrong: 10,000 is not the IMR denominator.  Why D wrong: 1,00,000 is the MMR denominator."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The numerator of IMR is the number of deaths among children up to what age?","options":["7 days","28 days","1 year","5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The numerator of IMR is the number of deaths among children up to (under) 1 year of age in a given period; note the source states \"28 days,\" which corresponds to neonatal mortality — the factually correct IMR cut-off is under 1 year of age, and the printed \"28 days\" is inconsistent with the standard IMR definition.  Why A wrong: 7 days corresponds to early neonatal mortality.  Why B wrong: 28 days is the neonatal mortality period, not infant mortality; the source's printed value is a discrepancy.  Why D wrong: Under 5 years relates to U5MR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The most common cause of IMR in India is:","options":["Pneumonia","Low birth weight & prematurity","Diarrhoea","Congenital anomalies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of IMR in India is low birth weight and prematurity.  Why A wrong: Pneumonia is the most common cause of IMR in the world.  Why C wrong: Diarrhoea is not the stated most common cause.  Why D wrong: Congenital anomalies (with prematurity) cause early neonatal mortality."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The most common cause of IMR in the world is:","options":["Low birth weight","Prematurity","Pneumonia","Sepsis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of IMR in the world is pneumonia.  Why A wrong: Low birth weight is the leading cause in India.  Why B wrong: Prematurity is a cause in India.  Why D wrong: Sepsis is not the stated global cause."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Per SRS-2020, which state has the highest IMR (43)?","options":["Rajasthan","M.P.","Mizoram","Bihar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Madhya Pradesh (M.P.) has the highest IMR at 43.  Why A wrong: Rajasthan's IMR is 32.  Why C wrong: Mizoram has the lowest IMR (3).  Why D wrong: Bihar is not listed for highest IMR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Per SRS-2020, which state has the lowest IMR (3)?","options":["M.P.","Rajasthan","Mizoram","Kerala"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Mizoram has the lowest IMR at 3.  Why A wrong: M.P. has the highest IMR (43).  Why B wrong: Rajasthan's IMR is 32.  Why D wrong: Kerala is not listed as lowest IMR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Late Maternal Death is defined as the death of a woman from direct or indirect obstetric causes:","options":["Within 42 days after termination of pregnancy","More than 42 days but less than one year after termination of pregnancy","Within 7 days after delivery","More than one year after delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Late Maternal Death occurs more than 42 days but less than one year after termination of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: Deaths within 42 days are classified as maternal deaths, not late maternal deaths.  Why C wrong: 7 days relates to early neonatal, not maternal death classification.  Why D wrong: Beyond one year is not classified as late maternal death."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The General Fertility Rate (GFR) and Total Fertility Rate (TFR) differ in that:","options":["GFR is age/sex-adjusted while TFR is age/sex-specific","GFR is an age/sex-specific birth rate while TFR is an age/sex-adjusted birth rate","Both are age/sex-specific birth rates","Both are crude birth rates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GFR is an age/sex-specific birth rate while TFR is an age/sex-adjusted birth rate.  Why A wrong: The definitions are reversed.  Why C wrong: Only GFR is age/sex-specific.  Why D wrong: Neither is a crude birth rate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which indicators are universally accepted indicators of the health status of the whole population?","options":["IMR and MMR","IMR and CDR","CBR and CDR","TFR and GFR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The universally accepted indicators of health status of the whole population are IMR and CDR.  Why A wrong: MMR is not paired here.  Why C wrong: CBR is not stated as the universal health-status indicator.  Why D wrong: Fertility rates are not health-status indicators."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which statement about IMR as a health indicator is correct?","options":["IMR is a universal indicator of socioeconomic condition","IMR is the universal indicator of health status but not a universal indicator of socioeconomic condition","IMR indicates only life expectancy","IMR is not an indicator of health status"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IMR is the universal indicator of health status, but it is NOT a universal indicator of socioeconomic condition.  Why A wrong: IMR is not a universal socioeconomic indicator.  Why C wrong: IMR does not indicate only life expectancy.  Why D wrong: IMR is a universal health-status indicator."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which is described as a universally accepted indicator of BOTH health status and socioeconomic condition of a country?","options":["IMR","Life expectancy","CDR","U5MR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Life expectancy is a universally accepted indicator of both health status and socioeconomic condition.  Why A wrong: IMR indicates health status but not socioeconomic condition universally.  Why C wrong: CDR is a health-status indicator, not the both-dimension one described.  Why D wrong: U5MR is described as the best/first indicator of socioeconomic development, but not both dimensions here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"According to the source, U5MR is the best or first indicator of:","options":["Health status only","Socioeconomic development","Nutritional status","Immunization coverage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: U5MR (written USMR in source) is the best or first indicator of socioeconomic development.  Why A wrong: Not restricted to health status only per source.  Why C wrong: Not stated as nutritional-status indicator.  Why D wrong: Not stated as immunization-coverage indicator."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"IMR is best considered the indicator of all EXCEPT:","options":["Health status of a community","Level of living","Effectiveness of MCH services","Total fertility rate of a nation"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: IMR is best indicator of health status of a community, level of living, and effectiveness of MCH services — not the TFR.  Why A wrong: IMR does indicate community health status.  Why B wrong: IMR does indicate level of living.  Why C wrong: IMR does indicate effectiveness of MCH services."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The most common cause of Early Neonatal Mortality Rate (ENNMR) is:","options":["Infections","Prematurity and congenital anomalies","Low birth weight","Birth asphyxia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of ENNMR is prematurity and congenital anomalies.  Why A wrong: Infections cause Late Neonatal Mortality (LNNMR).  Why C wrong: Low birth weight is the leading cause of IMR in India, not specifically ENNMR here.  Why D wrong: Birth asphyxia is not the stated ENNMR cause."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The most common cause of Late Neonatal Mortality Rate (LNNMR) is:","options":["Prematurity","Congenital anomalies","Infections","Low birth weight"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of LNNMR is infections.  Why A wrong: Prematurity causes ENNMR.  Why B wrong: Congenital anomalies cause ENNMR.  Why D wrong: Low birth weight is not the stated LNNMR cause."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the five stages of the demographic cycle, the First Stage (High Stationary) is characterized by:","options":["High birth rate and high death rate","High birth rate and declining death rate","Low birth rate and low death rate","Birth rate lower than death rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The First Stage (High Stationary) has high birth rate and high death rate, keeping population stationary (India until 1920).  Why B wrong: Declining death rate with unchanged birth rate is the Second Stage.  Why C wrong: Low birth and death rates describe the Fourth Stage.  Why D wrong: Birth rate lower than death rate describes the Fifth Stage."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the Second Stage (Early Expanding) of the demographic cycle, the birth rate is __ and the death rate __:","options":["Declining; unchanged","Unchanged; declines","Low; low","High; high"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Second Stage: birth rate unchanged, death rate declines; population expands (many Asian countries now).  Why A wrong: Reversed.  Why C wrong: Describes Fourth Stage.  Why D wrong: Describes First Stage."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"According to the demographic cycle table, India is currently in which stage?","options":["First Stage (High Stationary)","Second Stage (Early Expanding)","Third Stage (Late Expanding)","Fourth Stage (Low Stationary)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: India is currently at the Third Stage (Late Expanding), where birth rate begins to decline and death rate further declines.  Why A wrong: India was in high stationary until 1920.  Why B wrong: Many Asian countries are in this stage now, but India is in the third.  Why D wrong: European countries like Switzerland, Sweden, Denmark are in the fourth stage."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The Fourth Stage (Low Stationary) of the demographic cycle, with low birth and low death rates, is exemplified by countries such as:","options":["Germany and Hungary","Switzerland, Sweden, Denmark","India and Bangladesh","Pakistan and Nepal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Fourth Stage (stationary) is exemplified by European countries like Switzerland, Sweden, and Denmark.  Why A wrong: Germany and Hungary exemplify the Fifth (Declining) stage.  Why C wrong: India is in the third stage.  Why D wrong: Not stated for fourth stage."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The Fifth Stage (Declining) of the demographic cycle, where the birth rate is lower than the death rate, is exemplified by:","options":["Asian countries","East European countries like Germany and Hungary","India","African countries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Fifth (Declining) stage — birth rate lower than death rate, death rate higher than birth rate — is exemplified by East European countries like Germany and Hungary.  Why A wrong: Asian countries are in earlier expanding stages.  Why C wrong: India is in the third stage.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The first partial census of India was conducted in which year (Lord Mayo's tenure)?","options":["1881","1872","1951","1931"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first partial census of India was conducted in 1872 during Lord Mayo's tenure.  Why A wrong: 1881 was the first complete and regular census (Lord Ripon).  Why C wrong: 1951 was the first census of independent India.  Why D wrong: 1931 was the first caste-based census."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The first complete and regular census of India was conducted in 1881 during whose tenure? (Haryana CHO-2022)","options":["Lord Mayo","Lord Ripon","Lord Curzon","Lord Dalhousie"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first complete and regular census (1881) was during the tenure of Lord Ripon.  Why A wrong: Lord Mayo's tenure had the first partial census (1872).  Why C wrong: Lord Curzon is not associated here.  Why D wrong: Lord Dalhousie is not associated here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The first census of independent India was conducted in:","options":["1947","1951","1961","1931"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first census of independent India was in 1951.  Why A wrong: 1947 is the year of independence, not the census.  Why C wrong: 1961 was a subsequent census.  Why D wrong: 1931 was the first caste-based census."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"How often is the census conducted in India? (UP CCH 2022, MP CHO-2019)","options":["Every 5 years","Every 10 years","Every 15 years","Every 2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The census is conducted every 10 years.  Why A wrong: 5 years is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 15 years is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Every 2 years is the SRS report frequency, not the census."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The SRS Report for Vital Statistics is published every 2 years by which body?","options":["Ministry of Health and Family Welfare","Registrar General of India (RGI)","International Institute for Population Sciences","National Statistical Office"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SRS Report for Vital Statistics is published every 2 years by the Registrar General of India (RGI).  Why A wrong: MoHFW is not the publisher of the SRS report per source.  Why C wrong: IIPS conducts the NFHS.  Why D wrong: NSO is not stated as the SRS publisher."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The first caste-based census in India was conducted in:","options":["1872","1881","1931","1951"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The first caste-based census was conducted in 1931.  Why A wrong: 1872 was the first partial census.  Why B wrong: 1881 was the first complete census.  Why D wrong: 1951 was the first census of independent India."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"World Population Day is celebrated every year on: (Punjab CHO-2021)","options":["1 July","11 July","7 April","21 July"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: World Population Day is celebrated on 11 July.  Why A wrong: 1 July is Doctor's Day.  Why C wrong: 7 April is World Health Day.  Why D wrong: 21 July is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The responsibility of the census of the country is given to the Central Government through which Article of the Indian Constitution?","options":["Article 44","Article 246","Article 21","Article 324"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The responsibility of census is given to the Central Government through Article 246 of the Indian Constitution.  Why A wrong: Article 44 relates to a uniform civil code.  Why C wrong: Article 21 is right to life.  Why D wrong: Article 324 relates to the Election Commission."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The literacy rate of India as per the census of 1901 was:","options":["18.33%","74%","52.2%","65%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The literacy rate as per the 1901 census was 18.33%.  Why B wrong: 74% is the literacy rate as per census 2011.  Why C wrong: 52.2% is the % of anaemic pregnant women (NFHS-5).  Why D wrong: 65% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The study of population is known as:","options":["Epidemiology","Demography","Biostatistics","Sociology"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The study of population is known as demography.  Why A wrong: Epidemiology is the study of disease distribution.  Why C wrong: Biostatistics deals with statistical methods.  Why D wrong: Sociology is the study of society."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The 2011 census was which numbered Indian census (and which of independent India)?","options":["14th Indian census (6th of independent India)","15th Indian census (7th of independent India)","16th Indian census (8th of independent India)","13th Indian census (5th of independent India)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2011 census was the 15th Indian census and 7th of independent India.  Why A wrong: Not the stated figure.  Why C wrong: The 2021 census would be the 16th Indian census.  Why D wrong: Not the stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In India, the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act was passed in:","options":["1974","1981","1986","1972"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act was passed in 1974.  Why B wrong: 1981 is the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act.  Why C wrong: 1986 is the Environment Protection Act.  Why D wrong: 1972 is not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In India, the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act was passed in:","options":["1974","1981","1986","1984"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act was passed in 1981.  Why A wrong: 1974 is the Water Act.  Why C wrong: 1986 is the Environment Protection Act.  Why D wrong: 1984 is not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In India, the Environment Protection Act was passed in:","options":["1974","1981","1986","1991"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Environment Protection Act was passed in 1986.  Why A wrong: 1974 is the Water Act.  Why B wrong: 1981 is the Air Act.  Why D wrong: 1991 is not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per Census 2011, India's total population was:","options":["100 Cr","121 Cr","140 Cr","110 Cr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Census 2011 recorded India's total population at 121 Cr (crore).  Why A wrong: 100 Cr is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 140 Cr is a later estimate, not the 2011 census figure.  Why D wrong: 110 Cr is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per Census 2011, the literacy rate of India was:","options":["18.33%","74%","82%","94%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Literacy rate as per Census 2011 was 74%.  Why A wrong: 18.33% was the 1901 literacy rate.  Why C wrong: 82% is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 94% is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per Census 2011, the sex ratio of India was:","options":["914","940","1020","929"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Census 2011 sex ratio was 940.  Why A wrong: 914 is the child sex ratio (0-6 years).  Why C wrong: 1020 is the NFHS-5 sex ratio for India.  Why D wrong: 929 is the NFHS-5 sex ratio at birth for India."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per Census 2011, the density of population of India was:","options":["382/km2","940/km2","121/km2","46/km2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Density of population per Census 2011 was 382/km2.  Why B wrong: 940 is the sex ratio.  Why C wrong: 121 Cr is the total population.  Why D wrong: 46/sq.km is the density of Andaman & Nicobar."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per Census 2011, the child sex ratio (0-6 years) was:","options":["940","914","1020","891"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The child sex ratio (0-6 years) per Census 2011 was 914.  Why A wrong: 940 is the overall sex ratio.  Why C wrong: 1020 is NFHS-5 sex ratio.  Why D wrong: 891 is Rajasthan's NFHS-5 sex ratio at birth."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"India accounts for what percentage of the world's population, with 2.4% of the world's surface area?","options":["2.4%","17.5%","10.4%","74%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India accounts for 17.5% of the world's population with 2.4% of the world's surface area.  Why A wrong: 2.4% is the surface-area share.  Why C wrong: 10.4% is Rajasthan's share of India's geographical area.  Why D wrong: 74% is the literacy rate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"According to the source, which is the most populous state and which is the least populous state of India?","options":["Most: Sikkim; Least: Uttar Pradesh","Most: Uttar Pradesh (~200 million); Least: Sikkim (6,10,557)","Most: Maharashtra; Least: Goa","Most: Bihar; Least: Mizoram"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Uttar Pradesh is most populous (~200 million); Sikkim is least populous (6,10,557).  Why A wrong: The states are reversed.  Why C wrong: Not stated.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Why has the 2021 census (which would be the 16th Indian census) been postponed?","options":["Financial constraints","COVID-19","Change in methodology","Administrative delays"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2021 census, which would be the 16th Indian census, was postponed due to COVID-19.  Why A wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Among union territories, which is most densely populated (11297 per sq.km) per the source?","options":["Andaman & Nicobar","NCT of Delhi","Chandigarh","Puducherry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NCT of Delhi is the most densely populated UT at 11297 per sq.km.  Why A wrong: Andaman & Nicobar is the least populated UT (46/sq.km).  Why C wrong: Chandigarh is not stated.  Why D wrong: Puducherry is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"India is the ___ largest country in the world, with a total area of 32,87,263 km2:","options":["5th","7th","3rd","9th"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India is the 7th largest country in the world, with a total area of 32,87,263 km2.  Why A wrong: 5th is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 3rd is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 9th is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per the source, India's coastline measures:","options":["3,214 km","2,933 km","7,516.6 km","15,200 km"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: India has a coastline of 7,516.6 km.  Why A wrong: 3,214 km is the north-to-south measurement.  Why B wrong: 2,933 km is the east-to-west measurement.  Why D wrong: 15,200 km is the land frontier."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Rajasthan covers 3,42,239 sq km, which is what percent of India's total geographical area, making it the largest Indian state by area?","options":["2.4%","10.4%","17.5%","7%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rajasthan covers 10.4% of India's total geographical area and is the largest Indian state by area (seventh largest by population).  Why A wrong: 2.4% is India's share of world surface area.  Why C wrong: 17.5% is India's share of world population.  Why D wrong: 7% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The National Family Health Survey (NFHS) is conducted by which institute?","options":["Registrar General of India","International Institute for Population Sciences (IIPS), Mumbai under MoHFW, GOI","National Statistical Office","Indian Council of Medical Research"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NFHS is conducted by the International Institute for Population Sciences (IIPS), Mumbai under MoHFW, GOI.  Why A wrong: RGI publishes the SRS report.  Why C wrong: NSO does not conduct the NFHS.  Why D wrong: ICMR does not conduct the NFHS."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Till 2023, how many NFHS surveys have been completed, and during which period was the 5th conducted?","options":["4 surveys; 5th during 2015-16","5 surveys; 5th during 2019-21","6 surveys; 5th during 2019-21","5 surveys; 5th during 2005-06"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Till 2023, 5 NFHS surveys have been completed, with the 5th conducted during 2019-21.  Why A wrong: 5 surveys completed, not 4; and period is wrong.  Why C wrong: 5 surveys, not 6.  Why D wrong: The 5th was during 2019-21."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5 (2019-21), India's sex ratio was:","options":["940","1020","929","1009"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NFHS-5 recorded India's sex ratio as 1020.  Why A wrong: 940 is the Census 2011 sex ratio.  Why C wrong: 929 is India's sex ratio at birth (NFHS-5).  Why D wrong: 1009 is Rajasthan's NFHS-5 sex ratio."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, India's sex ratio at birth was:","options":["1020","929","891","940"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's sex ratio at birth per NFHS-5 was 929.  Why A wrong: 1020 is the overall sex ratio.  Why C wrong: 891 is Rajasthan's sex ratio at birth.  Why D wrong: 940 is Census 2011 sex ratio."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, the Total Fertility Rate (TFR) for India was:","options":["2.1","2.0","2.2","1.9"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TFR for India per NFHS-5 was 2.0 (Rajasthan also 2.0).  Why A wrong: 2.1 is the NHP-2017 TFR target.  Why C wrong: 2.2 is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 1.9 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, the institutional birth rate for India was:","options":["94.9%","88.6%","58.1%","89.4%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Institutional birth rate for India per NFHS-5 was 88.6%.  Why A wrong: 94.9% is Rajasthan's value.  Why C wrong: 58.1% is % women with 4 ANC visits (India).  Why D wrong: 89.4% is % births attended by skilled personnel (India)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, India's IMR and U5MR were respectively:","options":["28 and 32","35.2 and 41.9","30.3 and 37.6","41.9 and 35.2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NFHS-5 recorded India's IMR as 35.2 and U5MR as 41.9.  Why A wrong: 28 and 32 are the SRS-2020 IMR and U5MR values.  Why C wrong: 30.3 and 37.6 are Rajasthan's NFHS-5 IMR and U5MR.  Why D wrong: The values are reversed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, the percentage of women in India who had 4 ANC visits was:","options":["55.3%","58.1%","89.4%","21.5%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 58.1% of women in India had 4 ANC visits per NFHS-5.  Why A wrong: 55.3% is Rajasthan's value.  Why C wrong: 89.4% is % births attended by skilled personnel (India).  Why D wrong: 21.5% is % delivery by caesarean section (India)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, the percentage of births attended by skilled health personnel in India was:","options":["95.6%","89.4%","88.6%","58.1%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 89.4% of births in India were attended by skilled health personnel per NFHS-5.  Why A wrong: 95.6% is Rajasthan's value.  Why C wrong: 88.6% is the institutional birth rate (India).  Why D wrong: 58.1% is % women with 4 ANC visits."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, the percentage of deliveries by caesarean section in India was:","options":["10.4%","21.5%","88.6%","67.1%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 21.5% of deliveries in India were by caesarean section per NFHS-5.  Why A wrong: 10.4% is Rajasthan's value.  Why C wrong: 88.6% is institutional birth rate.  Why D wrong: 67.1% is % anaemic children 6-59 months (India)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, the percentage of children aged 6-59 months who are anaemic in India was:","options":["71.5%","67.1%","52.2%","46.3%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 67.1% of children aged 6-59 months in India were anaemic per NFHS-5.  Why A wrong: 71.5% is Rajasthan's value.  Why C wrong: 52.2% is % anaemic pregnant women (India).  Why D wrong: 46.3% is % anaemic pregnant women (Rajasthan)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per NFHS-5, the percentage of pregnant women who are anaemic in India was:","options":["46.3%","52.2%","67.1%","55.3%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 52.2% of pregnant women in India were anaemic per NFHS-5.  Why A wrong: 46.3% is Rajasthan's value.  Why C wrong: 67.1% is % anaemic children (India).  Why D wrong: 55.3% is Rajasthan's % women with 4 ANC visits."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The National Health Policy (NHP), 2017 was launched by the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare on:","options":["1st April 2017","15th March 2017","11th July 2017","2nd October 2017"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 was launched on 15th March 2017.  Why A wrong: Incorrect date.  Why C wrong: 11 July is World Population Day.  Why D wrong: Incorrect date."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Through NHP-2017, the government aims to shift focus from:","options":["\"Wellness\" to \"sick-care\"","\"Sick-care\" to \"wellness\"","\"Curative\" to \"palliative\"","\"Public\" to \"private\" care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims to shift focus from \"sick-care\" to \"wellness\" by promoting prevention and well-being.  Why A wrong: The direction is reversed.  Why C wrong: Not the stated shift.  Why D wrong: Not the stated shift."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 targeted to increase life expectancy at birth from 67.5 to what, by 2025?","options":["65","70","75","72"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims to increase life expectancy at birth from 67.5 to 70 by 2025.  Why A wrong: 65 is below the starting value.  Why C wrong: 75 is not the stated target.  Why D wrong: 72 is not the stated target."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 targeted the reduction of Total Fertility Rate (TFR) to what value by 2025?","options":["2.0","2.1","2.3","1.9"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims to reduce TFR to 2.1 at the national & sub-national level by 2025.  Why A wrong: 2.0 is the NFHS-5 achieved TFR.  Why C wrong: 2.3 is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 1.9 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 aimed to reduce Under Five Mortality (UFM) to what value by 2025?","options":["16","23","28","100"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims to reduce Under Five Mortality to 23 by 2025.  Why A wrong: 16 is the neonatal mortality rate target.  Why C wrong: 28 is the IMR target/current level.  Why D wrong: 100 is the MMR target."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 targeted reduction of MMR from the current level to 100 by which year? (MP CCH 2022)","options":["2025","2020","2019","2030"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aimed to reduce MMR to 100 by 2020.  Why A wrong: 2025 applies to most other goals.  Why C wrong: 2019 was the target year for reducing IMR to 28.  Why D wrong: 2030 is the SDG target year."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 targeted the reduction of IMR to 28 by which year?","options":["2025","2020","2019","2030"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aimed to reduce IMR to 28 by 2019.  Why A wrong: 2025 is not this goal's year.  Why B wrong: 2020 was the MMR target year.  Why D wrong: 2030 is the SDG year."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 aimed to reduce the neonatal mortality rate to what value, and stillbirth to a 'single digit', by 2025?","options":["23","16","28","10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims to reduce neonatal mortality rate to 16 and stillbirth to 'single digit' by 2025.  Why A wrong: 23 is the U5MR target.  Why C wrong: 28 is the IMR target.  Why D wrong: 10 is not the stated NMR target."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017's HIV/AIDS goal, also termed the \"90:90:90\" target, was to be achieved by which year? (Haryana CHO 2022)","options":["2025","2020","2019","2030"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 90:90:90 HIV/AIDS target was to be achieved by 2020.  Why A wrong: 2025 is not the stated year.  Why C wrong: 2019 was the IMR target year.  Why D wrong: 2030 is the SDG year."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The \"90:90:90\" target for HIV/AIDS in NHP-2017 means all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["90% of all people living with HIV know their HIV status","90% of all people diagnosed with HIV receive sustained antiretroviral therapy","90% of all people receiving antiretroviral therapy will have viral suppression","90% reduction in new HIV infections annually"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The three \"90s\" are: 90% know their status, 90% diagnosed receive ART, and 90% on ART achieve viral suppression — not a 90% reduction in new infections.  Why A wrong: This is one of the three 90s.  Why B wrong: This is one of the three 90s.  Why C wrong: This is one of the three 90s."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"For TB, NHP-2017 aimed to achieve and maintain a cure rate of over what percent in new sputum-positive patients, reaching elimination status by 2025?","options":[">75%",">85%",">90%",">95%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims for a TB cure rate of >85% in new sputum-positive patients by 2025.  Why A wrong: >75% is not the stated figure.  Why C wrong: >90% relates to ANC coverage/skilled attendance and HIV targets.  Why D wrong: >95% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 aimed to reduce premature mortality from cardiovascular diseases, cancer, diabetes, or chronic respiratory diseases by what percentage by 2025?","options":["10%","25%","33%","50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims to reduce premature mortality from NCDs by 25% by 2025.  Why A wrong: 10% is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 33% is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 50% is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 aimed to sustain antenatal care coverage and skilled attendance at birth above what level (achieved by 2025)?","options":["Above 75%","Above 80%","Above 90%","Above 95%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims for ANC coverage above 90% and skilled attendance at birth above 90% by 2025.  Why A wrong: 75% is incorrect.  Why B wrong: 80% is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 95% is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 targeted that more than what percentage of newborns are fully immunized by one year of age (achieved by 2025)?","options":["80%","85%","90%","95%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims for more than 90% of newborns fully immunized by one year of age by 2025.  Why A wrong: 80% is incorrect.  Why B wrong: 85% is the TB cure-rate target level.  Why D wrong: 95% is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"NHP-2017 aimed to increase government health expenditure as a percentage of GDP from the existing 1.15% to what, by 2025?","options":["2.0%","2.5%","3.0%","1.5%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 aims to increase health expenditure from 1.15% to 2.5% of GDP by 2025.  Why A wrong: 2.0% is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 3.0% is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 1.5% is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The Global Hunger Report is released annually by which organisations?","options":["WHO and UNICEF","Concern Worldwide (Ireland) and Welt Hunger Hilfe (Germany)","UNDP and World Bank","FAO and WFP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Global Hunger Report is released by Concern Worldwide (Ireland) and Welt Hunger Hilfe (Germany), both Non-Government Organisations.  Why A wrong: WHO and UNICEF do not release the GHI.  Why C wrong: UNDP releases the HDI.  Why D wrong: FAO and WFP do not release the GHI."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four components of the Global Hunger Index (GHI)?","options":["Undernourished","Child Wasting","Child Stunting","Maternal Mortality"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The four GHI components are Undernourished, Child Wasting, Child Stunting, and Child Mortality (U5MR); Maternal Mortality is not a component.  Why A wrong: Undernourished is a component.  Why B wrong: Child Wasting is a component.  Why C wrong: Child Stunting is a component."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the GHI, \"Child Wasting\" refers to:","options":["The proportion of children under five suffering from wasting, a sign of acute undernutrition","The proportion of children under five suffering from stunted growth","Child mortality under the age of five","The proportion of the undernourished as a percentage of the population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Child Wasting is the proportion of children under five suffering from wasting, a sign of acute undernutrition.  Why B wrong: That defines Child Stunting.  Why C wrong: That defines U5MR/Child Mortality.  Why D wrong: That defines the Undernourished component."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the GHI, \"Child Stunting\" is a sign of:","options":["Acute undernutrition","Chronic undernutrition","Overnutrition","Micronutrient toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Child Stunting (stunted growth in under-fives) is a sign of chronic undernutrition.  Why A wrong: Acute undernutrition is indicated by wasting.  Why C wrong: Stunting is not overnutrition.  Why D wrong: Not micronutrient toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the GHI weighting, which two components each carry a weight of 1/3?","options":["Child Wasting and Child Stunting","Undernourished and Child Mortality (U5MR)","Undernourished and Child Wasting","Child Stunting and Child Mortality"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Undernourished (1/3) and Child Mortality/U5MR (1/3) each carry a 1/3 weight; Child Wasting (1/6) and Child Stunting (1/6) each carry 1/6.  Why A wrong: Both wasting and stunting are weighted 1/6.  Why C wrong: Child Wasting is 1/6, not 1/3.  Why D wrong: Child Stunting is 1/6, not 1/3."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"On the GHI 100-point scale, which statement is correct?","options":["100 is the best possible score (no hunger)","0 is the best possible score (no hunger) and 100 is the worst","Higher scores indicate less hunger","50 is the best possible score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On the GHI scale, 0 is best (no hunger) and 100 is worst.  Why A wrong: 100 is the worst, not best.  Why C wrong: Higher scores indicate more hunger.  Why D wrong: 50 is not the best score."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"A GHI score between 20 and 34.9 is pegged in which category, where India finds itself with a score of 28.7 (GHI-2023)?","options":["Low","Moderate","Serious","Alarming"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 20.0-34.9 is \"serious\", where India (28.7) is placed.  Why A wrong: <9.9 is \"low\".  Why B wrong: 10.0-19.9 is \"moderate\".  Why D wrong: 35.0-49.9 is \"alarming\"."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the GHI severity scale, a score greater than 50.0 is categorized as:","options":["Serious","Alarming","Extremely alarming","Moderate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A score >50.0 is \"extremely alarming\".  Why A wrong: Serious is 20.0-34.9.  Why B wrong: Alarming is 35.0-49.9.  Why D wrong: Moderate is 10.0-19.9."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In GHI-2023, India's score and rank were:","options":["29.1; 107 out of 121 countries","28.7; 111 out of 125 countries","28.7; 107 out of 121 countries","29.1; 111 out of 125 countries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In GHI-2023, India scored 28.7 and ranked 111 out of 125 countries (Serious category).  Why A wrong: 29.1 and 107/121 were the GHI-2022 figures.  Why C wrong: Mixes 2023 score with 2022 rank.  Why D wrong: Mixes 2022 score with 2023 rank."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The theme of GHI-2023 was:","options":["Leave NO ONE Behind","Uncertain Times, Unsettled Lives: Shaping our Future in a Transforming World","Health for All","Zero Hunger"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GHI-2023 theme was \"Uncertain Times, Unsettled Lives: Shaping our Future in a Transforming World\".  Why A wrong: \"Leave NO ONE Behind\" was the GHI-2022 theme.  Why C wrong: Not a GHI theme stated.  Why D wrong: Not the stated theme."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In GHI-2022, India's rank was:","options":["111 out of 125 countries","107 out of 121 countries","92 out of 121 countries","76 out of 125 countries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In GHI-2022, India ranked 107 out of 121 countries.  Why A wrong: 111/125 was the GHI-2023 rank.  Why C wrong: 92 was Pakistan's placement.  Why D wrong: 76 was Nepal and Bangladesh's placement."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"India slipped how many positions in GHI, moving from its 2022 rank to the 2023 rank?","options":["2 positions","4 positions","6 positions","10 positions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India slipped 4 positions from its 2022 rank of 107; in response the Indian government rejected GHI and raised methodological concerns.  Why A wrong: 2 is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 6 is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 10 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In GHI-2022, the placements of neighbouring countries were: Pakistan, Nepal, Bangladesh, and Sri Lanka respectively at:","options":["92, 76, 76, 65","65, 76, 92, 111","76, 92, 65, 111","111, 92, 76, 65"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pakistan was placed at 92, Nepal and Bangladesh at 76, and Sri Lanka at 65.  Why B wrong: The values are mismatched.  Why C wrong: Mismatched.  Why D wrong: Mismatched."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"According to the note, in GHI-2022, how many countries got a score less than 5?","options":["7","17","27","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In GHI-2022, 17 countries got a score less than 5.  Why A wrong: 7 is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 27 is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 5 is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The Human Development Index (HDI) report is published by:","options":["Concern Worldwide","United Nations Development Program (UNDP)","World Health Organization","World Bank"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The HDI report is published by the United Nations Development Program (UNDP).  Why A wrong: Concern Worldwide publishes the GHI.  Why C wrong: WHO does not publish the HDI.  Why D wrong: World Bank does not publish the HDI."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The three dimensions of the Human Development Index are:","options":["Health, Education, and Living standards","Health, Nutrition, and Sanitation","Income, Employment, and Housing","Health, Education, and Immunization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three dimensions of HDI are Health, Education, and Living standards.  Why B wrong: Nutrition and sanitation are not HDI dimensions.  Why C wrong: These are not the three HDI dimensions.  Why D wrong: Immunization is not an HDI dimension."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four indicators of the Human Development Index?","options":["Life expectancy at birth","Expected years of schooling / Mean years of schooling","Gross National Income per capita","Infant Mortality Rate"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The four HDI indicators are Life expectancy at birth, Expected years of schooling, Mean years of schooling, and Gross National Income per capita — not IMR.  Why A wrong: Life expectancy at birth is an indicator (Health dimension).  Why B wrong: Years of schooling are indicators (Education dimension).  Why C wrong: GNI per capita is an indicator (Living standards)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which HDI indicator corresponds to the \"Health\" dimension?","options":["Expected years of schooling","Mean years of schooling","Life expectancy at birth","Gross National Income per capita"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Life expectancy at birth is the indicator for the Health dimension.  Why A wrong: Expected years of schooling belongs to Education.  Why B wrong: Mean years of schooling belongs to Education.  Why D wrong: GNI per capita belongs to Living standards."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The theme of HDI 2023-24 was:","options":["Leave NO ONE Behind","Uncertain Times, Unsettled Lives: Shaping our Future in a World in Transformation","Health for All by 2000","Sustainable Development for All"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HDI 2023-24 theme was \"Uncertain Times, Unsettled Lives: Shaping our Future in a World in Transformation\".  Why A wrong: That was the GHI-2022 theme.  Why C wrong: Not the stated theme.  Why D wrong: Not the stated theme."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which country was the top performer (First) in HDI 2023-24?","options":["Norway (0.966)","Switzerland (0.967)","India (0.644)","Sweden"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Switzerland was first with an HDI of 0.967.  Why A wrong: Norway (0.966) was second.  Why C wrong: India (0.644) ranked 134th.  Why D wrong: Sweden is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In HDI-2023, India's rank and HDI value were:","options":["111 out of 125; 0.644","134 out of 193 countries; 0.644","134 out of 125; 0.967","107 out of 121; 0.966"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India ranked 134 out of 193 countries with an HDI value of 0.644 (medium human development country).  Why A wrong: 111/125 is the GHI-2023 rank.  Why C wrong: 0.967 is Switzerland's value.  Why D wrong: These are GHI-2022 / Norway figures."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"According to HDI-2023, India is classified as a:","options":["Very high human development country","High human development country","Medium human development country","Low human development country"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: India (rank 134, value 0.644) is a medium human development country.  Why A wrong: Not very high.  Why B wrong: Not high.  Why D wrong: Not low."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per the HDI-2023 table, India's life expectancy at birth was:","options":["67.5 Years","67.7 Years","12.6 Years","70 Years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's life expectancy at birth per HDI-2023 was 67.7 years.  Why A wrong: 67.5 was the NHP-2017 baseline life expectancy.  Why C wrong: 12.6 years is expected years of schooling.  Why D wrong: 70 is the NHP-2017 target."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per HDI-2023, India's expected years of schooling and mean years of schooling were respectively:","options":["6.57 and 12.6","12.6 and 6.57","67.7 and 6951","12.6 and 6951"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Expected years of schooling = 12.6 years; mean years of schooling = 6.57 years.  Why A wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: 67.7 is life expectancy; 6951 is GNI.  Why D wrong: 6951 is GNI, not schooling."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"As per HDI-2023, India's Gross National Income was:","options":["0.644","6951","6.57","12.6"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India's Gross National Income per HDI-2023 was 6951.  Why A wrong: 0.644 is India's HDI value.  Why C wrong: 6.57 is mean years of schooling.  Why D wrong: 12.6 is expected years of schooling."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Epidemiology is BEST defined as the branch of medicine dealing with:","options":["Treatment of individual patients only","The incidence, distribution, and possible control of diseases and other factors relating to health","The study of population size only","Laboratory diagnosis of diseases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epidemiology deals with the incidence, distribution, and possible control of diseases and other factors relating to health; it studies distribution (who, when, where), patterns, and determinants of health/disease in defined populations.  Why A wrong: It concerns populations, not just individual treatment.  Why C wrong: Population size is demography.  Why D wrong: Laboratory diagnosis is not the definition of epidemiology."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which of the following is NOT a tool of measurement in epidemiology?","options":["Rate","Ratio","Proportion","Percentile"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The tools of measurement in epidemiology are Rate, Ratio, and Proportion; percentile is not listed.  Why A wrong: Rate is a tool.  Why B wrong: Ratio is a tool.  Why C wrong: Proportion is a tool."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The term for the constant presence of a disease in a particular geographical area is:","options":["Epidemic","Endemic","Pandemic","Sporadic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endemic refers to the constant presence of a disease in a particular geographical area.  Why A wrong: Epidemic is an unusual occurrence in a community.  Why C wrong: Pandemic is worldwide distribution.  Why D wrong: Sporadic is scattered/irregular occurrence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The occurrence of disease or presence of disease in a particular community, usually above the expected level, is termed:","options":["Endemic","Epidemic","Pandemic","Zoonosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epidemic is the usually unusual occurrence/presence of disease in a particular community.  Why A wrong: Endemic is constant presence in a geographical area.  Why C wrong: Pandemic is worldwide.  Why D wrong: Zoonosis is animal-to-human transmission."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The worldwide distribution of a particular disease, such as COVID-19, involving several countries, is termed:","options":["Endemic","Epidemic","Pandemic","Sporadic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pandemic is the worldwide distribution of a particular disease (e.g., COVID-19), which may involve several countries.  Why A wrong: Endemic is limited to a geographical area.  Why B wrong: Epidemic is community-level.  Why D wrong: Sporadic is scattered/irregular."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"When a disease occurs in an irregular pattern, scattered about a community, it is termed:","options":["Endemic","Sporadic","Pandemic","Epidemic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sporadic means scattered; disease occurs in an irregular pattern in a community.  Why A wrong: Endemic is constant presence.  Why C wrong: Pandemic is worldwide.  Why D wrong: Epidemic is a community-wide occurrence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"A communicable disease transmitted from an animal to a human, such as Rabies or Bovine Tuberculosis, is termed:","options":["Zoonosis","Epizootic","Epornithic","Nosocomial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Zoonosis is a communicable disease transmitted from an animal to a human (e.g., Rabies, Bovine Tuberculosis).  Why B wrong: Epizootic is a disease outbreak in an animal population.  Why C wrong: Epornithic is an outbreak in a bird population.  Why D wrong: Nosocomial is hospital-acquired infection."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The term \"Epizootic\" refers to:","options":["Outbreak of a disease in an animal population","Outbreak of a disease in a bird population","Disease transmitted from animal to human","Hospital-acquired infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Epizootic is an outbreak of a disease in an animal population.  Why B wrong: That defines Epornithic.  Why C wrong: That defines Zoonosis.  Why D wrong: That defines Nosocomial infection."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The term \"Epornithic\" refers to:","options":["Outbreak of a disease in an animal population","Outbreak of a disease in a bird population","A worldwide disease outbreak","Constant presence of disease in an area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epornithic is an outbreak of a disease in a bird population.  Why A wrong: That defines Epizootic.  Why C wrong: That defines Pandemic.  Why D wrong: That defines Endemic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"\"Infection\" in epidemiology is defined as:","options":["Presence of a pathogenic organism on the body surface and clothes","Entry and development or multiplication of an infectious agent in the body of a human or animal","The degree of pathogenicity of an infectious agent","The ability of the pathogen to induce illness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infection is the entry and development or multiplication of an infectious agent in the body of a human or animal.  Why A wrong: That describes Contamination.  Why C wrong: That describes Virulence.  Why D wrong: That describes Pathogenicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The presence of a pathogenic organism or infectious agent on the body surface and clothes is termed:","options":["Infection","Contamination","Reservoir","Virulence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Contamination is the presence of a pathogenic organism or infectious agent on the body surface and clothes.  Why A wrong: Infection is entry/multiplication inside the body.  Why C wrong: Reservoir is where an agent normally lives and multiplies.  Why D wrong: Virulence is the degree of pathogenicity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Any person, animal, arthropod, or plant in which an infectious agent normally lives and multiplies is called a:","options":["Vector","Reservoir","Host","Carrier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A reservoir is any person, animal, arthropod, or plant in which an infectious agent normally lives and multiplies.  Why A wrong: A vector transmits the agent.  Why C wrong: A host is capable of being infected but is not the definition given.  Why D wrong: A carrier harbors without symptoms; not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"\"Virulence\" is defined as:","options":["The ability of a pathogen to induce illness or disease","The degree of the pathogenicity of an infectious agent","Entry and multiplication of an agent in the body","Time from exposure to appearance of symptoms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Virulence is the degree of the pathogenicity of an infectious agent.  Why A wrong: That defines Pathogenicity.  Why C wrong: That defines Infection.  Why D wrong: That defines Incubation period."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"\"Pathogenicity\" refers to:","options":["The degree of pathogenicity of an agent","The ability of the pathogen to induce illness or disease","The presence of an agent on body surface","Outbreak of disease in animals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pathogenicity is the ability of the pathogen to induce illness or disease.  Why A wrong: That defines Virulence.  Why C wrong: That defines Contamination.  Why D wrong: That defines Epizootic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The \"Incubation period\" is defined as: (MP CHO, GJ CHO)","options":["Time from exposure to development of disease or appearance of first clinical signs & symptoms","The period between exposure and onset of infectiousness","The length of time a person can transmit a disease","The recovery period from the disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period is the time from exposure to development of the disease or appearance of first clinical signs & symptoms.  Why B wrong: That defines the Latent period.  Why C wrong: That defines the Communicable period.  Why D wrong: That describes the Convalescent period."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The \"Latent period\" is defined as:","options":["Time from exposure to appearance of symptoms","The period between exposure and onset of infectiousness","The length of time a person can transmit a disease","The degree of pathogenicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The latent period is the period between exposure and onset of infectiousness.  Why A wrong: That defines the Incubation period.  Why C wrong: That defines the Communicable period.  Why D wrong: That defines Virulence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The \"Communicable period\", also known as the infectious period, is:","options":["The time from exposure to first symptoms","The period between exposure and onset of infectiousness","The length of time a person can transmit a disease","The recovery period from illness"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The communicable period (infectious period) is the length of time a person can transmit a disease.  Why A wrong: That defines the Incubation period.  Why B wrong: That defines the Latent period.  Why D wrong: That describes the Convalescent period."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Nosocomial infection is also known as:","options":["Opportunistic infection","Hospital-acquired infection","Zoonotic infection","Community-acquired infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nosocomial infection is also known as hospital-acquired infection.  Why A wrong: Opportunistic infection occurs in immunocompromised patients.  Why C wrong: Zoonotic is animal-to-human.  Why D wrong: It is hospital-acquired, not community-acquired."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"A nosocomial infection is a type of infection that occurs how long after admission to the hospital?","options":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","1 week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nosocomial infection occurs 48 hours after admission to the hospital from secondary sources.  Why A wrong: 24 hours is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 72 hours is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 1 week is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The most common type of nosocomial infection is: (AIIMS-2018)","options":["Surgical site infection","Urinary tract infection (UTI) due to Escherichia coli","Pneumonia","Bloodstream infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common type of nosocomial infection is urinary tract infection (UTI) due to Escherichia coli (E. coli).  Why A wrong: Surgical site infection is not the most common per source.  Why C wrong: Pneumonia is not the most common per source.  Why D wrong: Bloodstream infection is not the most common per source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"An \"Opportunistic infection\" is a type of infection that occurs in:","options":["Healthy individuals with strong immunity","Persons whose immunity is weak or immunocompromised patients like those with TB or HIV","Only hospitalized patients within 48 hours","Animals only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Opportunistic infection occurs in persons whose immunity is weak or in immunocompromised patients (e.g., TB or HIV).  Why A wrong: It occurs in weakened immunity, not healthy hosts.  Why C wrong: That describes nosocomial infection.  Why D wrong: Not restricted to animals."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"According to the \"Tried theory of epidemiology,\" disease is the result of forces within a dynamic system consisting of which 3 components?","options":["Host, vector, reservoir","An external agent, a susceptible host, and an environment that brings the host and agent together","Incidence, prevalence, and mortality","Bacteria, virus, and fungus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The three components are an external agent, a susceptible host, and an environment that brings the host and agent together (the epidemiological triad).  Why A wrong: Vector and reservoir are not the three triad components.  Why C wrong: These are epidemiological measures, not the triad.  Why D wrong: These are types of agents, not the triad."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the classic epidemiological triad example given, the agent is:","options":["Man","P. falciparum","Pool of water","Vector"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the classic example, the Host is Man, the Agent is P. falciparum, and the Environment is a Pool of Water / Vector.  Why A wrong: Man is the host.  Why C wrong: Pool of water is part of the environment.  Why D wrong: Vector is part of the environment."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In epidemiology, the organism (e.g., human) capable of being infected by a specific agent is called the:","options":["Agent","Host","Vector","Reservoir"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The host is the organism (e.g., human) capable of being infected by a specific agent.  Why A wrong: The agent infects the host.  Why C wrong: A vector transmits the agent.  Why D wrong: A reservoir is where the agent normally lives/multiplies."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"\"Incidence\" of a disease is defined as:","options":["The number of all current cases (old + new) in a population","The number of new cases occurring in a defined population during a specified period of time","The proportion of the population that is undernourished","The degree of pathogenicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Incidence is the number of new cases occurring in a defined population during a specified period of time.  Why A wrong: That defines Prevalence.  Why C wrong: That is a GHI component.  Why D wrong: That is Virulence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the incidence formula, the number of new cases of a specific disease during a given period is divided by the population at risk during that period and multiplied by:","options":["100","1000","10,000","1,00,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Incidence = (number of new cases during the given period / population of risk during that period) × 1000.  Why A wrong: ×100 is used in prevalence percentage formulas.  Why C wrong: ×10,000 is not used.  Why D wrong: ×1,00,000 is used for MMR-type rates."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"\"Prevalence\" of a disease refers to:","options":["Only new cases in a defined period","The number of all current cases (old + new) existing at a given period or over a time in a population","The number of deaths in a population","The birth rate of a population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prevalence is the number of all current cases (old + new) existing at a given period or over time in a population; it has two main types — point prevalence and period prevalence.  Why A wrong: Only new cases defines Incidence.  Why C wrong: Deaths relate to mortality rate.  Why D wrong: Birth rate is a separate vital statistic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The two main types of prevalence are:","options":["Point prevalence and period prevalence","Old prevalence and new prevalence","Crude prevalence and specific prevalence","Incidence prevalence and mortality prevalence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two main types are point prevalence and period prevalence.  Why B wrong: Not the stated types.  Why C wrong: Not the stated types.  Why D wrong: Not the stated types."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the point prevalence formula, the number of all current cases (old and new) of a specified disease existing at a given point of time is divided by the estimated population at the same point of time and multiplied by:","options":["1000","100","10,000","1,00,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Point prevalence = (number of all current cases at a given point / estimated population at the same point) × 100.  Why A wrong: ×1000 is used for incidence.  Why C wrong: ×10,000 is not used.  Why D wrong: ×1,00,000 is used for MMR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The numerator of incidence is:","options":["All cases present during a given time period","New cases that occurred during a given time period","Total deaths during a period","Total live births during a period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Numerator of incidence = new cases that occurred during a given time period.  Why A wrong: That is the numerator of prevalence.  Why C wrong: Deaths relate to mortality.  Why D wrong: Live births relate to fertility/mortality denominators."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The numerator of prevalence is:","options":["New cases that occurred during a given time period","All cases present during a given time period","Total population","Old cases only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Numerator of prevalence = all cases present during a given time period.  Why A wrong: That is the numerator of incidence.  Why C wrong: Total population is a denominator.  Why D wrong: Prevalence includes both old and new cases, not old only."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The relationship among prevalence, incidence rate, and average duration of disease is: (Raj. CHO-2023)","options":["Prevalence = Incidence Rate / Average Duration of Disease","Prevalence = (Incidence Rate) × (Average Duration of Disease)","Prevalence = Incidence Rate + Average Duration of Disease","Prevalence = Average Duration / Incidence Rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prevalence = (Incidence Rate) × (Average Duration of Disease).  Why A wrong: Not the stated relationship.  Why C wrong: Not the stated relationship.  Why D wrong: That gives average duration, not prevalence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The formula for calculating Average Duration of Disease is:","options":["Average Duration = (Incidence) / (Prevalence)","Average Duration = (Prevalence) / (Incidence)","Average Duration = Prevalence × Incidence","Average Duration = Prevalence + Incidence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Average Duration = (Prevalence) / (Incidence).  Why A wrong: The ratio is inverted.  Why C wrong: Product is used for prevalence, not duration.  Why D wrong: Addition is not used."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"If the incidence rate of lung cancer is 46 new cancers per 100,000 P-Y and the prevalence is 23 per 100,000 population, the Average Duration of Disease is:","options":["2 years","0.5 year","1 year","1.5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Average Duration = (23/100,000) / (46/100,000 person-years) = 0.5 year.  Why A wrong: This inverts the ratio.  Why C wrong: Incorrect calculation.  Why D wrong: Incorrect calculation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The Disability-Adjusted Life Year (DALY) is a measure of:","options":["The birth rate of a population","Overall disease burden, expressed as the number of years lost due to ill-health, disability, or early death","The incidence of new cases","The sex ratio at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DALY is a measure of overall disease burden, expressed as the number of years lost due to ill-health, disability, or early death.  Why A wrong: DALY is not a birth-rate measure.  Why C wrong: Incidence measures new cases, not DALY.  Why D wrong: DALY is unrelated to sex ratio."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which of the following is the correct sequence of the 4 stages of infection?","options":["Prodromal → Incubation → Clinical → Convalescent","Incubation → Prodromal → Clinical → Convalescent","Clinical → Prodromal → Incubation → Convalescent","Incubation → Clinical → Prodromal → Convalescent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 4 stages are Incubation period → Prodromal stage → Clinical stage → Convalescent period.  Why A wrong: Incubation precedes prodromal.  Why C wrong: Incubation is first, not third.  Why D wrong: Prodromal precedes clinical."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"In the stages of infection, the \"Incubation period\" is:","options":["The time from initial contact with the infectious agent to first signs of symptoms","The stage where early, mild, generalized symptoms appear","The stage where signs specific to the disease appear","The recovery period from the disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period is the time from initial contact with the infectious agent to first signs of symptoms.  Why B wrong: That describes the prodromal stage.  Why C wrong: That describes the clinical stage.  Why D wrong: That describes the convalescent period."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The \"Prodromal stage\" of infection is characterized by:","options":["No symptoms","Early signs and symptoms — mild and generalized symptoms","Disease-specific signs and symptoms","Complete recovery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The prodromal stage presents early, mild, and generalized signs and symptoms.  Why A wrong: The incubation period has no symptoms.  Why C wrong: Disease-specific symptoms define the clinical stage.  Why D wrong: Recovery defines the convalescent period."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"During which stage of infection do signs and symptoms specific to the disease appear?","options":["Incubation period","Prodromal stage","Clinical stage","Convalescent period"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the clinical stage, signs and symptoms specific to the disease appear.  Why A wrong: Incubation has no symptoms.  Why B wrong: Prodromal has mild, generalized symptoms.  Why D wrong: Convalescent is recovery."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"The \"Convalescent period\" of infection refers to:","options":["The time from contact to first symptoms","Early generalized symptoms","Disease-specific symptoms","The recovery period from the disease"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The convalescent period is the recovery period from the disease.  Why A wrong: That is the incubation period.  Why B wrong: That is the prodromal stage.  Why C wrong: That is the clinical stage."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"A \"Notifiable disease\" is best defined as:","options":["Any disease treated in hospitals","Any disease required by law to be reported to government authorities","Any disease transmitted by animals","Any disease caused by bacteria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A notifiable disease is any disease that is required by law to be reported to government authorities, such as communicable diseases like TB, cholera, plague, relapsing fever, smallpox, measles, HIV, syphilis, and gonorrhea.  Why A wrong: Not all hospital-treated diseases are notifiable.  Why C wrong: That describes zoonotic disease.  Why D wrong: Not all bacterial diseases are notifiable."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"Which of the following are examples of notifiable diseases mentioned in the source? (Select All That Apply)","options":["TB","Cholera","Diabetes mellitus","Measles","HIV"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: TB, cholera, measles, and HIV are listed notifiable communicable diseases (along with plague, relapsing fever, smallpox, syphilis, and gonorrhea).  Why C wrong: Diabetes mellitus is a non-communicable disease and is not listed as notifiable in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · SRS Report-2020 vital statistics (MMR, IMR, U5MR, CBR, CDR)…","question":"\"Communicability\" (period of communicability) is defined as:","options":["The time during which an infectious agent may be transferred directly or indirectly from an infected person to another person","The entry and multiplication of an agent","The degree of pathogenicity","The presence of an agent on the body surface"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Communicability is the time during which an infectious agent may be transferred directly or indirectly from an infected person to another; it is also known as the 'infectious period'.  Why B wrong: That defines infection.  Why C wrong: That defines virulence.  Why D wrong: That defines contamination."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which term is defined as \"the search for unrecognised disease or to detect by means of rapid application of a test, examination or other procedure in apparently healthy individuals\"?","options":["Monitoring","Screening","Surveillance","Sentinel reporting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Screening is the search for unrecognised disease/detection by rapid application of a test in apparently healthy individuals.  Why A wrong: Monitoring is the routine analysis of routine measurement to detect change in environment and health status.  Why C wrong: Surveillance is ongoing systematic collection, analysis and interpretation of health-related data.  Why D wrong: Sentinel reporting is a type of surveillance using selected reporting sites."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"\"The routine analysis of routine measurement to detect change in the environment and health status of the population\" defines:","options":["Screening","Surveillance","Monitoring","Evaluation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: This is the exact definition of Monitoring given on the page.  Why A wrong: Screening detects unrecognised disease in apparently healthy people.  Why B wrong: Surveillance is ongoing systematic data collection integrated with dissemination for control.  Why D wrong: Evaluation is not defined here as routine analysis of routine measurement."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Surveillance is best defined as:","options":["A one-time cross-sectional survey of a population","The ongoing, systematic collection, analysis, and interpretation of health-related data essential to planning, implementation, and evaluation of public health practice, integrated with timely dissemination to those responsible for prevention and control","Rapid testing of apparently healthy individuals","Detecting change in the environment through routine measurement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This is the textbook definition of surveillance including timely dissemination for prevention and control.  Why A wrong: Surveillance is ongoing and systematic, not a one-time survey.  Why C wrong: That describes screening.  Why D wrong: That describes monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"In which type of surveillance do designated staff physically review records, visit the health facility, and confirm with the health practitioner to find particular cases?","options":["Passive surveillance","Active surveillance","Sentinel surveillance","Syndromic surveillance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active surveillance involves an active search for cases where designated staff review records physically and visit facilities.  Why A wrong: Passive surveillance receives reports submitted from hospitals/clinics; investigator receives data indirectly.  Why C wrong: Sentinel surveillance uses selected sites/professionals to represent an area.  Why D wrong: Syndromic surveillance uses real-time indicators/symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which surveillance type is described as \"more reliable, most accurate, timely & expensive\" and can be used to check the validity of passive surveillance?","options":["Active surveillance","Passive surveillance","Sentinel surveillance","Syndromic surveillance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Active surveillance is more reliable, most accurate, timely & expensive, requires more resources and can validate passive surveillance; used during outbreak investigations/research.  Why B wrong: Passive surveillance is less reliable, less accurate and inexpensive.  Why C wrong: Sentinel surveillance can be active or passive and uses selected sites.  Why D wrong: Syndromic surveillance is inexpensive and faster."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"A house-to-house survey of any outbreak disease is an example of which surveillance type?","options":["Sentinel surveillance","Passive surveillance","Active surveillance","Syndromic surveillance"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives \"House to house survey of any outbreak disease\" as the example of active surveillance.  Why A wrong: Sentinel example is Sentinel HIV surveillance.  Why B wrong: Passive examples are national notifiable disease system / HMIS.  Why D wrong: Syndromic example is collecting cases of diarrhea rather than cholera."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"A system by which a health jurisdiction receives reports submitted from hospitals, clinics, and public health units, and is the most common form of surveillance, is:","options":["Active surveillance","Passive surveillance","Sentinel surveillance","Syndromic surveillance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passive surveillance receives submitted reports, is simple/easy, and is the most common form of surveillance.  Why A wrong: Active surveillance involves active case search and is expensive.  Why C wrong: Sentinel uses selected representative sites.  Why D wrong: Syndromic uses real-time symptom-based indicators."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which are examples of passive surveillance? (Select all that apply)","options":["National notifiable disease system","Health management information system","House-to-house outbreak survey","Sentinel HIV surveillance","Collecting cases of diarrhea rather than cholera"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists National notifiable disease system and Health management information system as examples of passive surveillance.  Why C wrong: House-to-house outbreak survey is an active surveillance example.  Why D wrong: Sentinel HIV surveillance is a sentinel surveillance example.  Why E wrong: Collecting diarrhea cases rather than cholera is a syndromic surveillance example."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Sentinel surveillance is characterised by:","options":["Active case searching by designated staff in every facility","Reporting of health events by selected health professionals who represent a geographical area or specific reporting group, and can be active or passive","Real-time indicators focused on symptoms only","Reports passively received from all hospitals nationwide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sentinel surveillance uses selected professionals/sites to represent an area/group, chosen on basis of population flow, previous outbreaks and risk group; it can be active or passive.  Why A wrong: That is active surveillance.  Why C wrong: That describes syndromic surveillance.  Why D wrong: That describes passive surveillance."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Sentinel HIV surveillance is an example of which type of surveillance?","options":["Active","Passive","Sentinel","Syndromic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives Sentinel HIV surveillance as the sentinel surveillance example.  Why A/B/D wrong: These have their own examples (house-to-house survey; notifiable disease system/HMIS; diarrhea vs cholera counting)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which surveillance type is defined as \"the ongoing, systematic collection, analysis, interpretation and application of real-time indicators for disease that allow for detection before public health authorities would otherwise identify them,\" focusing on symptoms?","options":["Active surveillance","Passive surveillance","Sentinel surveillance","Syndromic surveillance"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Syndromic surveillance uses real-time indicators, focuses on one or more symptoms rather than physician-oriented or lab-confirmed diseases; it is inexpensive and faster.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are defined differently (active case search; passive report receipt; representative sentinel sites)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"\"Collecting the number of cases of diarrhea rather than cases of cholera\" is an example of which surveillance type?","options":["Syndromic surveillance","Sentinel surveillance","Active surveillance","Passive surveillance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This symptom-based (diarrhea) rather than confirmed-disease (cholera) counting is the syndromic surveillance example.  Why B/C/D wrong: Each has its own distinct example on the page."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The Iceberg Theory of Illness is also known as:","options":["Theory of Commission","Theory of Omission / Ice Berg Phenomenon","Web of Causation","Epidemiological triad"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the Iceberg theory is also known as the \"Theory of Omission\" or Ice Berg Phenomenon.  Why A wrong: It is Theory of Omission, not Commission.  Why C wrong: Web of causation is a separate concept.  Why D wrong: Epidemiological triad is agent-host-environment, not the iceberg concept."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"In the Iceberg Theory of Illness, the \"Floating Tip\" represents:","options":["Asymptomatic/invisible cases","Clinically identified or visible cases in the community","Carriers only","Deaths only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Floating Tip is the clinically identified cases or visible cases in the community.  Why A wrong: Invisible/asymptomatic cases are the submerged portion.  Why C wrong: Carriers are part of the submerged (hidden) portion.  Why D wrong: Death is at the very top of the visible spectrum but the tip specifically denotes visible/clinical cases, not deaths only."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The \"Submerged Portion\" of the disease iceberg includes: (Select all that apply)","options":["Latent cases","Unapparent / pre-symptomatic cases","Undiagnosed cases","Carriers in the community","Clinically diagnosed visible cases"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The submerged portion is asymptomatic/invisible cases (hidden part) such as latent, unapparent, pre-symptomatic, undiagnosed and carrier cases.  Why E wrong: Clinically diagnosed visible cases form the floating tip, not the submerged portion."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Eradication of a disease means:","options":["Reduction of new cases to zero in a specific region","The disease is completely and permanently reduced to zero worldwide, so no further control measures are required","Reduction of disease burden below a critical threshold","Temporary interruption of transmission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eradication means a disease is completely and permanently reduced to zero worldwide; once eradicated, no further control measures are required.  Why A wrong: Reduction to zero new cases in a specific region is elimination.  Why C/D wrong: These are neither the defined eradication nor elimination concepts."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which disease is given as the example of eradication \"world widely in 1980\"?","options":["Polio","Smallpox","Measles","Guinea worm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smallpox was eradicated worldwide in 1980 (the eradication example).  Why A wrong: Polio was eliminated from India in 2014, not globally eradicated.  Why C wrong: Measles/rubella were eliminated by Maldives and Sri Lanka in 2023.  Why D wrong: Guinea worm was one of the diseases eliminated by India (Feb 2000)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Elimination of a disease is defined as:","options":["Complete permanent worldwide reduction to zero","A disease reduced to zero new cases of an infectious disease in a specific region or geographical area, requiring continued measures to prevent re-establishment","No further control measures required","Detection of hidden cases in the community"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Elimination = zero new cases in a specific region/area; it requires continued measures to prevent re-establishment of transmission.  Why A wrong: Worldwide zero reduction is eradication.  Why C wrong: \"No further measures required\" applies to eradication, not elimination.  Why D wrong: That relates to the iceberg concept."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"According to WHO (per the page), which countries eliminated measles and rubella in 2023?","options":["India and Bangladesh","Maldives and Sri Lanka","Nepal and Bhutan","Pakistan and Afghanistan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Maldives and Sri Lanka eliminated measles and rubella in 2023 (as said WHO).  Why A/C/D wrong: These countries are not named in the elimination example."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Polio was eliminated from India in which year?","options":["2011","2012","2014","2016"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states polio was eliminated from India in 2014 (27 March 2014).  Why A/B wrong: These years are not the stated elimination year.  Why D wrong: 2016 is the year for Yaws and Maternal/Neonatal tetanus elimination."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Match the disease with the date India eliminated it. Which pairing is CORRECT?","options":["Guinea worm (Dracunculiasis) – February 2000","Polio – 14 July 2016","Yaws – 27 March 2014","Maternal and Neonatal tetanus – February 2000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: India eliminated Guinea worm (Dracunculiasis) in February 2000.  Why B wrong: Polio was 27 March 2014, not 14 July 2016.  Why C wrong: Yaws was 14 July 2016, not 27 March 2014.  Why D wrong: Maternal and Neonatal tetanus (NNT) was 14 July 2016, not February 2000."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"India eliminated Maternal tetanus and Neonatal tetanus (NNT) on which date?","options":["February 2000","27 March 2014","14 July 2016","2023"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal and Neonatal tetanus (NNT) was eliminated 14 July 2016.  Why A wrong: February 2000 is Guinea worm.  Why B wrong: 27 March 2014 is Polio.  Why D wrong: 2023 is measles/rubella elimination by Maldives/Sri Lanka."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which statement correctly describes Isolation (as distinct from Quarantine)?","options":["Separation of healthy people after exposure to prevent contact","Separation of infected people (cases) from healthy or susceptible persons","Done for the longest incubation period","Comes under primary prevention (specific protection)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isolation is the separation of infected people (cases) from healthy/susceptible persons.  Why A wrong: That describes quarantine (healthy people after exposure).  Why C wrong: Quarantine is done for the longest incubation period.  Why D wrong: Quarantine comes under primary prevention; isolation is secondary prevention."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Isolation is done for the period of ________, whereas quarantine is done for the ________.","options":["Longest incubation period; period of communicability","Communicability of that disease; longest (maximum) incubation period","Recovery only; specific protection","Exposure; treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isolation is done for the period of communicability of the disease; quarantine for the longest/maximum incubation period.  Why A wrong: The two are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated durations."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Under which level of prevention does Quarantine fall?","options":["Primary prevention (specific protection)","Secondary prevention (treatment)","Tertiary prevention (rehabilitation)","Primordial prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Quarantine comes under primary prevention (specific protection).  Why B wrong: Isolation comes under secondary prevention (treatment).  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for quarantine on the page."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The duration of Isolation is:","options":["Till maximum incubation period","Till recovery (period of communicability)","48 to 72 hours","14 days fixed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isolation duration is till recovery (period of communicability).  Why A wrong: Till maximum incubation period is the quarantine duration.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated durations for isolation."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Immunity is best defined as:","options":["The ability of an organism to fight against a disease","A vaccine given at birth","The routine measurement of health status","Separation of infected persons"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Immunity is the ability of an organism to fight against a disease.  Why B wrong: That describes a birth-dose vaccine, not immunity itself.  Why C wrong: That is monitoring.  Why D wrong: That is isolation."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Immunity that an individual gets at the time of birth is called:","options":["Acquired immunity","Adaptive immunity","Innate immunity","Passive artificial immunity"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Innate immunity is the type of immunity the individual gets at the time of birth.  Why A/B wrong: Acquired (adaptive) immunity is gained after birth.  Why D wrong: Passive artificial immunity is acquired from immune serum, not present at birth."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Acquired (adaptive) immunity is:","options":["Present at the time of birth","A type of immunity gained after birth","Only ever passive","Only produced by BCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acquired/adaptive immunity is gained after birth.  Why A wrong: Immunity present at birth is innate.  Why C wrong: Acquired immunity can be active or passive.  Why D wrong: BCG produces artificial active immunity but is not the only form of acquired immunity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Normal human immunoglobulin is prepared from:","options":["Human multiple donors","Donors immunized against or recovered from specific infectious diseases","Animal serum","Recombinant DNA technology"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal human immunoglobulin is prepared by human multiple donors.  Why B wrong: That describes specific human immunoglobulin.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated on the page."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Specific human immunoglobulin is prepared by:","options":["Human multiple random donors","Donors who have been immunized against or recovered from specific infectious diseases","Killed bacterial cultures","Egg-based attenuation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Specific human immunoglobulin is prepared by donors immunized against or recovered from specific infectious diseases.  Why A wrong: That is normal human immunoglobulin.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described preparation."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Acquired immunity is defined on the page as immunity that develops:","options":["Only at birth","Throughout life","Only during pregnancy","Only after 5 years of age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The acquired immunity box states it is immunity that develops throughout life.  Why A wrong: Immunity at birth is innate.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated timeframes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Active immunity is immunity which develops after:","options":["Antibodies transmitted from mother to baby","Being exposed to an infection or from getting a vaccine","Receiving gamma globulin","Receiving immune serum medicine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active immunity develops after being exposed to an infection or from getting a vaccine.  Why A wrong: Mother-to-baby antibody transfer is natural passive immunity.  Why C/D wrong: Gamma globulin / immune serum represents artificial passive immunity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Passive immunity is:","options":["Immunity which develops after exposure to infection","Immunity acquired from someone or something else","Immunity present from birth","Immunity from getting a vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passive immunity is immunity acquired from someone or something else.  Why A/D wrong: Those describe active immunity.  Why C wrong: Immunity from birth is innate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Antibodies made by the body after exposure to an infection (e.g., after smallpox and chickenpox exposure) represent which type of immunity?","options":["Natural active immunity","Artificial active immunity","Natural passive immunity","Artificial passive immunity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Natural active immunity = antibodies made after exposure to an infection; example given is after smallpox/chickenpox exposure.  Why B wrong: Artificial active immunity results from vaccination.  Why C wrong: Natural passive immunity is antibodies from mother to baby.  Why D wrong: Artificial passive immunity is from immune serum (gamma globulin)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Immunization/vaccination-induced antibody production is an example of:","options":["Natural active immunity","Artificial active immunity","Natural passive immunity","Artificial passive immunity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Artificial active immunity = antibodies made after getting a vaccination; example is immunization.  Why A wrong: Natural active follows actual infection exposure.  Why C/D wrong: These are passive forms."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Antibodies transmitted from mother to baby (e.g., breast milk, transplacental IgG) are an example of:","options":["Natural active immunity","Artificial active immunity","Natural passive immunity","Artificial passive immunity"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Natural passive immunity = antibodies transmitted from mother to baby; examples are mother's breast milk and transplacental IgG.  Why A/B wrong: These are active forms.  Why D wrong: Artificial passive is gamma globulin/immune serum."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Gamma globulin (immune serum medicine) provides which type of immunity?","options":["Natural active","Artificial active","Natural passive","Artificial passive"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Artificial passive immunity = antibodies acquired from an immune serum medicine; example is gamma globulin.  Why A/B wrong: Active forms come from infection or vaccine.  Why C wrong: Natural passive is mother-to-baby transfer."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which statement about Cell Mediated Immunity (CMI) is TRUE?","options":["It involves antibodies present in plasma and body fluids","It occurs within the cell of the immune system and does not involve antibodies","It is produced by B cells","Antibodies are gamma globulin molecules"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CMI occurs within the cell of the immune system and is an immune response that does not involve antibodies.  Why A wrong: Antibodies in plasma/body fluids describe humoral immunity.  Why C wrong: B cells drive humoral immunity.  Why D wrong: That is a humoral immunity fact."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Cell mediated immunity involves the activation of:","options":["Phagocytosis, antigen-specific cytotoxic T lymphocytes, and release of cytokines","B cells producing immunoglobulins only","Complement fixation only","Passive antibody transfer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CMI is the activation of phagocytosis, antigen-specific cytotoxic T lymphocytes and the release of various cytokines in response to antigen.  Why B wrong: B-cell immunoglobulin production is humoral immunity.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described CMI mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which cells provide protection against different pathogens in cell mediated immunity and are noted to be associated with HIV/AIDS?","options":["B cells","CD4 cells / T helper cells","Plasma cells","Mast cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CD4 cells (T helper cells) provide protection against different pathogens; the Note states CD4 cells are associated with HIV/AIDS.  Why A/C wrong: B cells and plasma cells relate to humoral immunity.  Why D wrong: Mast cells relate to IgE hypersensitivity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Humoral immunity is:","options":["Immune response inside the cell not involving antibodies","Connected with the immune response outside of the cell, present in plasma and body fluids, and comes from B cells","Mediated by cytotoxic T lymphocytes","Present at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Humoral immunity is connected with immune response outside the cell, present in plasma/body fluids, and comes from B cells that manufacture specific antibodies.  Why A/C wrong: These describe cell mediated immunity.  Why D wrong: Present-at-birth immunity is innate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"An antibody is best defined as:","options":["Any substance including an allergen that stimulates antibody production","An immune substance produced by plasma cells in response to an antigen; antibodies are gamma globulin molecules commonly known as immunoglobulins","A cytotoxic T lymphocyte","A macrophage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An antibody is an immune substance produced by plasma cells in response to antigen; antibodies are gamma globulin molecules (immunoglobulins).  Why A wrong: That defines an antigen.  Why C/D wrong: These are cellular components, not antibodies."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"An antigen is defined as:","options":["Immunoglobulin produced by plasma cells","Any substance, including an allergen, that stimulates production of antibody in the body","A gamma globulin molecule","A cytokine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An antigen is any substance including allergen that stimulates production of antibody in the body.  Why A/C wrong: Those describe antibodies/immunoglobulins.  Why D wrong: A cytokine is a signalling molecule, not the defined antigen."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"How many types of immunoglobulin are there, and which are they?","options":["3 types: IgA, IgG, IgM","5 types: IgA, IgG, IgM, IgE, IgD","4 types: IgA, IgG, IgM, IgE","6 types: IgA, IgG, IgM, IgE, IgD, IgH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are 5 types of immunoglobulin: IgA, IgG, IgM, IgE, IgD.  Why A/C wrong: These omit some classes.  Why D wrong: There is no \"IgH\"; there are only 5 classes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which immunoglobulin is abundantly present in human blood, mucus, saliva, breast milk, sweat and vaginal secretions and plays an important role against viral and respiratory pathogens?","options":["IgG","IgA","IgM","IgE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IgA is abundantly present in blood, mucus, saliva, breast milk, sweat and vaginal secretions and acts against viral/respiratory pathogens.  Why A wrong: IgG makes up 75% of humoral immunity and crosses placenta.  Why C wrong: IgM is the first antibody detected after antigen exposure.  Why D wrong: IgE is for hypersensitivity/allergy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which immunoglobulin makes up about 75% of total humoral immunity, is known as macroglobulin, and is the only one to cross the placental barrier to protect the fetus? (ESIC-2024)","options":["IgA","IgM","IgG","IgD"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states IgG makes up 75% of total immunoglobulin (called \"macroglobulin\" per source) and is the only immunoglobulin crossing the placental barrier to protect the fetus. Note: conventionally IgM is termed macroglobulin, but the source labels IgG as such; the placental-crossing and 75% facts firmly identify IgG.  Why A wrong: IgA is in secretions.  Why B wrong: IgM is the first antibody after exposure and 10% of total.  Why D wrong: IgD differentiates B-lymphocytes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which immunoglobulin is the first antibody to be detected after exposure to antigen and constitutes about 10% of total immunoglobulin?","options":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgE"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IgM is the first antibody detected after antigen exposure and is 10% of total immunoglobulin.  Why A wrong: IgA is in secretions.  Why B wrong: IgG crosses the placenta and is 75%.  Why D wrong: IgE causes hypersensitivity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which immunoglobulin is responsible for hypersensitivity and allergic reaction, causes mast cells to release histamine, and also protects from parasites?","options":["IgD","IgE","IgG","IgA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IgE is responsible for hypersensitivity/allergic reactions, causes mast cells to release histamine, and protects from parasites.  Why A wrong: IgD helps differentiate B-lymphocytes.  Why C/D wrong: IgG crosses placenta; IgA is in secretions."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which immunoglobulin is an antibody that helps to differentiate the B-lymphocytes?","options":["IgA","IgE","IgD","IgM"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IgD is the antibody that helps differentiate B-lymphocytes.  Why A/B/D wrong: Each has a different described function (secretions; allergy; first responder)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"For a rare disease (<10% of population affected), the relationship among prevalence, incidence rate and average duration is expressed as: (Raj. CHO-2023)","options":["Prevalence = Incidence Rate × Average Duration of Disease","Prevalence = Incidence Rate / Average Duration","Incidence = Prevalence × Average Duration","Average Duration = Incidence × Prevalence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When disease is rare, Prevalence = (Incidence Rate) × (Average Duration of Disease).  Why B/C/D wrong: These invert or misarrange the correct relationship."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The general relationship among prevalence, incidence rate and average duration (not assuming rarity) is written as:","options":["P/(1-P) = IR × Avg. Duration","P × (1-P) = IR / Avg. Duration","IR = P/(1-P) × Duration","P = IR + Avg. Duration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives P/(1-P) = IR × Avg. Duration as the general relationship.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated formula."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Under steady-state conditions, average duration of disease can be calculated as: (IGNOU CCH-2022)","options":["Average Duration = Incidence / Prevalence","Average Duration = Prevalence / Incidence","Average Duration = Prevalence × Incidence","Average Duration = 1 / Prevalence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If Prevalence = Incidence × Average Duration, then Average Duration = Prevalence / Incidence.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are algebraically incorrect rearrangements."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Lung cancer incidence is 46 new cancers per 100,000 person-years and prevalence is 23 per 100,000 population. What is the average duration of disease?","options":["2 years","0.5 year (6 months)","1 year","1.5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Average Duration = (23/100,000 persons) / (46/100,000 person-years) = 0.5 year, i.e., about 6 months survival from diagnosis to death.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not result from 23/46."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"A vaccine is best described as:","options":["An antibiotic that kills bacteria","An immune biological substance that provides specific protection against specific diseases by stimulating production of specific protective antibodies and other immune mechanisms","A toxoid only","A cold chain equipment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A vaccine is an immune biological substance providing specific protection against specific diseases by stimulating specific protective antibodies and other immune mechanisms.  Why A wrong: A vaccine is not an antibiotic.  Why C wrong: Toxoid is only one type; a vaccine is a broader category.  Why D wrong: Cold chain equipment stores vaccines but is not a vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Live attenuated vaccines are:","options":["Prepared from organisms killed by heat/chemicals","Prepared from live organisms that lost their capacity to produce disease (lost pathogenicity) but retain their immunogenicity","Detoxified exotoxins","Extracted cellular fractions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Live attenuated vaccines are prepared from live organisms that lost pathogenicity but retain immunogenicity.  Why A wrong: That defines killed/inactivated vaccines.  Why C wrong: That defines toxoids.  Why D wrong: That defines cellular fraction vaccines."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Live vaccines should NOT be administered to an individual with which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Immunodeficiency","Immune suppression or malignancy","Therapy with corticosteroids or alkylating agents","On radiation therapy","A healthy 9-month-old infant"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Live vaccines are contraindicated in immunodeficiency, immune suppression, malignancy, corticosteroid/alkylating-agent therapy, and radiation therapy.  Why E wrong: A healthy infant is not a contraindication; live vaccines are routinely given to healthy infants."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"When two live vaccines need to be administered separately, what minimum gap should be maintained?","options":["1 week","2 weeks","4 weeks","8 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When two live vaccines are not given together, a 4-week gap must be maintained.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 4-week interval."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which are examples of live attenuated vaccines? (Select all that apply)","options":["OPV","BCG","Rubella","Rotavirus","MMR / Influenza"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists OPV, BCG, Rubella, Rotavirus, MMR & Influenza as live attenuated vaccine examples.  Why none wrong: All listed are stated live vaccine examples."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Killed (inactivated) vaccines are prepared by:","options":["Attenuating live organisms","Killing organisms by heat or chemicals so that when injected they produce immunity; usually safe but less effective than live vaccines","Detoxifying exotoxins","Extracting cellular fractions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In killed/inactivated vaccines, organisms are killed by heat or chemicals; they are usually safe but less effective than live vaccines.  Why A wrong: That is live attenuated vaccines.  Why C wrong: That is toxoids.  Why D wrong: That is cellular fractions."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which of the following are examples of killed/inactivated vaccines? (Select all that apply)","options":["IPV","Hepatitis-B","Rabies","DPT / DT","Pentavalent"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists IPV, Hep.B, Rabies, DPT, DT, Pentavalent as killed/inactivated vaccine examples.  Why none wrong: All are listed examples."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Toxoids are prepared by:","options":["Attenuating live organisms","Detoxifying exotoxins produced by certain organisms (e.g., diphtheria & tetanus) and using them in vaccine preparation","Killing whole organisms with chemicals","Extracting cellular fractions like meningococcal & pneumococcal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Certain organisms (diphtheria & tetanus) produce exotoxins; these toxins are detoxified and used to make toxoid vaccines.  Why A wrong: That is live attenuated vaccines.  Why C wrong: That is killed vaccines.  Why D wrong: That is cellular fraction vaccines."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Cellular fraction vaccines are prepared from extracted cellular fractions. Examples include:","options":["Meningococcal & pneumococcal","BCG & OPV","Measles & rubella","DPT & DT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states cellular fraction vaccines are prepared from extracted cellular fractions, e.g., meningococcal & pneumococcal.  Why B wrong: BCG/OPV are live attenuated.  Why C wrong: Measles/rubella are live attenuated.  Why D wrong: DPT/DT are killed/toxoid-containing."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The cold chain is defined as:","options":["A system of storage and transport of vaccines at low temperature from the manufacturer to the actual vaccination site","The routine analysis of health status","A method of active surveillance","The separation of infected persons"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cold chain is a system of storage and transport of vaccines at low temperature from manufacture to the actual vaccination site.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe monitoring, surveillance, and isolation respectively."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which vaccines are stored in the freezer compartment? (Tripura CHO 2021)","options":["BCG and DPT","Polio and Measles","TT and Typhoid","Diluents"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polio and measles are stored in the freezer compartment.  Why A wrong: BCG, DPT must be in the cold part and never frozen.  Why C wrong: TT, Typhoid must never be frozen.  Why D wrong: Diluents can be stored at room temperature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which vaccines must be stored in the cold part and never allowed to freeze? (Select all that apply)","options":["BCG","DPT, DT","TT","Typhoid and diluents","Polio"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: BCG, DPT, DT, TT, Typhoid and diluents must be kept cold and never frozen.  Why E wrong: Polio (like measles) is stored in the freezer compartment, not the \"never freeze\" group."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"At a health centre, most vaccines (except polio) can be stored up to how long if refrigerator temperature is strictly maintained between 4° to 8°C?","options":["1 week","2 weeks","5 weeks","3 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Most vaccines (except polio) can be stored up to 5 weeks at a health centre if temperature is strictly maintained between 4° to 8°C.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 5 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The cold chain temperature of vaccines in India should be maintained between:","options":["0° to +4°C","+2° to +8°C","-15° to -25°C","-20°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cold chain temperature in India should be maintained between +2° to +8°C for all vaccines except polio, yellow fever etc.  Why A wrong: Not the stated range.  Why C/D wrong: These are freezer temperatures used for OPV/deep freezers."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Diluents used in vaccination:","options":["Must be frozen","Can be stored at room temperature","Must be stored at -20°C","Must never be stored"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states diluents can be stored at room temperature.  Why A/C wrong: Diluents should not be frozen.  Why D wrong: They can be stored, just at room temperature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Opened vials with preservatives can be stored up to ___, and vaccine without preservatives up to ___.","options":["4 hours; 1 hour","1 hour; 4 hours","24 hours; 4 hours","4 hours; 4 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Opened vials with preservatives — up to 4 hours; vaccine without preservatives — up to 1 hour.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated durations."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which of the following are among the \"Six Rights of Cold Chain\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["The right vaccine and right quantity","The right place and right time","The right condition (no temperature break in cold chain)","The right cost","The right physician"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The six rights are: right vaccine, right quantity, right place, right time, right condition (no temperature break), and right cost.  Why E wrong: \"The right physician\" is not one of the six rights."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"In the Cold Chain equipment table, the Walk-in-freezer (WIF) operates at what temperature and storage duration?","options":["+2 to +8°C for 1 month","-15 to -25°C for 3 months","+26 to 8°C for 3 months","-20°C for 1 to 3 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Walk-in-freezer (WIF) operates at -15 to -25°C with 3 months storage duration (National, State and Regional vaccine store).  Why A wrong: That describes ILR-type ranges.  Why C wrong: +26 to 8°C is the Walk-in-cooler (WIC) range.  Why D wrong: Those are cold-box parameters."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"According to the Cold Chain equipment table, Walk-in-freezer/cooler are used at which location?","options":["District level","PHC level","National, State and Regional vaccine store","Sub Centre level"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Walk-in-freezer (WIF) and Walk-in-coolers (WIC) are used at National, State and Regional vaccine store.  Why A wrong: District level uses Large Deep freezer (DF) and Large ILR.  Why B wrong: PHC level uses Small DF and Small ILR.  Why D wrong: Sub Centre level uses vaccine carriers/day carriers."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Large Deep freezer (DF) and Large Ice Lined Refrigerator (ILR) are used at which level, with what storage duration?","options":["National level; 3 months","District level; 1 month","PHC level; 1 month","Sub Centre level; 48-72 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Large DF (-15 to -25°C) and Large ILR (+2 to +8°) are used at District level with 1 month storage.  Why A wrong: 3 months at National/State/Regional is WIF/WIC.  Why C wrong: Small DF/Small ILR are at PHC level.  Why D wrong: Vaccine/day carriers are Sub Centre with 48-72 hours."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Small DF and Small ILR are used at which level?","options":["National level","District level","PHCs level","Regional store"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Small DF (-15 to -25°C) and Small ILR (+2 to +8°) are used at PHCs level with 1 month storage.  Why A/B/D wrong: These use larger equipment (WIF/WIC or Large DF/ILR)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"A vaccine carrier (with 4 fully frozen ice packs) at Sub Centre level can carry how many vials at a time, for what duration?","options":["6 to 8 vials; 1 to 3 hours","16 to 20 vials; 48 to 72 hours","2 vials; 5 weeks","10 vials; 1 month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vaccine carrier uses 4 fully frozen ice packs, carries 16 to 20 vials at a time, 48 to 72 hours, at Sub Centre level.  Why A wrong: 6 to 8 vials for few hours describes the day carrier (2 ice packs).  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for the vaccine carrier."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"A Day carrier at Sub Centre level uses ___ fully frozen ice packs and can carry ___ vials for a few hours (mainly for a nearby session).","options":["4; 16 to 20","2; 6 to 8","2; 16 to 20","4; 6 to 8"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Day carrier uses 2 fully frozen ice packs and can carry 6 to 8 vials for a few hours, mainly for a nearby session.  Why A wrong: 4 ice packs / 16-20 vials describes the vaccine carrier.  Why C/D wrong: Mismatched combinations."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Cold boxes (+2 to +8°C) have what storage duration and use?","options":["3 months; national store","1 to 3 hours; transportation of vaccine in all peripheral centers","48 to 72 hours; sub-centre","1 month; district"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cold boxes operate at +2 to +8°C for 1 to 3 hours and are used for transportation of vaccine in all peripheral centers.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other equipment durations/uses."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Who is considered the founder of vaccinology in the West and developed the Cowpox vaccine in 1796 and a successful smallpox vaccine in 1798?","options":["Louis Pasteur","Edward Jenner","Jonas Salk","Albert Sabin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Edward Jenner is the founder of vaccinology in the West; he developed the cowpox vaccine in 1796 and succeeded with the smallpox vaccine in 1798, making smallpox vaccine the first vaccine in the world.  Why A wrong: Louis Pasteur coined the term \"Vaccine\" and discovered cholera, diphtheria, chickenpox and rabies vaccines in 1885.  Why C/D wrong: Salk and Sabin developed polio vaccines."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The term \"Vaccination\" was coined by ___, and the term \"Vaccine\" was coined by ___.","options":["Louis Pasteur; Edward Jenner","Edward Jenner; Louis Pasteur","Edward Jenner; Edward Jenner","Louis Pasteur; Louis Pasteur"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The term \"Vaccination\" was coined by Edward Jenner; the term \"Vaccine\" was coined by Louis Pasteur.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misattribute the coinage."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Louis Pasteur discovered vaccines for which diseases in 1885? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cholera","Diphtheria","Chickenpox","Rabies","Smallpox"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Louis Pasteur discovered cholera, diphtheria, chickenpox and rabies vaccines in 1885.  Why E wrong: Smallpox vaccine was Edward Jenner's (1798)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the full form of BCG vaccine?","options":["Bacillus Calmette Guerin","Bacterial Culture Growth","Basic Cell Guerin","Bovine Calmette Gene"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BCG stands for Bacillus Calmette Guerin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct full form."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the route and site of administration of BCG vaccine?","options":["Intramuscular in vastus lateralis","Intra-dermal (ID) at the left upper arm","Subcutaneous at right upper arm","Oral drops"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG is given intra-dermal (ID) at the left upper arm.  Why A wrong: IM vastus lateralis is for Hepatitis-B.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous upper right arm is for measles.  Why D wrong: Oral is for OPV."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which diluent is used for BCG vaccine?","options":["Distilled water","Sodium chloride (NaCl)","Dextrose 5%","Sterile air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diluent used for BCG is sodium chloride (NaCl).  Why A wrong: Distilled water is the measles diluent.  Why C/D wrong: Not used as BCG diluent."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the maximum age limit for administering BCG vaccine if not given at birth?","options":["6 months","9 months","Up to 1 year of age","Up to 5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: BCG can be administered up to 1 year of age (maximum age limit up to 1 year) if not given at birth.  Why A/B wrong: Not the stated limit.  Why D wrong: 5 years is the OPV/measles limit."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the birth dose of BCG vaccine, and what is the dose if not given at birth (up to 1 year)?","options":["0.05 ml at birth; 0.1 ml (doubled) up to 1 year","0.1 ml at birth; 0.05 ml up to 1 year","0.5 ml at birth; 0.5 ml up to 1 year","0.5 ml; unchanged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BCG birth dose is 0.05 ml; if not given at birth the dose is doubled to 0.1 ml (administered up to 1 year).  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: 0.5 ml is not the BCG dose."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"BCG vaccine produces which type of immunity and protects against which disease?","options":["Passive immunity; measles","Artificial Active immunity; Tuberculosis (TB)","Natural passive immunity; hepatitis B","Artificial passive immunity; polio"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG produces artificial active immunity to protect the child from Tuberculosis (TB).  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the immunity type and target disease."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Reconstituted/dissolved BCG and Measles vaccines can be kept at 2°C to 8°C for a maximum of how long (also 4 hours for Japanese encephalitis), after which they must be discarded?","options":["1 hour","4 hours","8 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reconstituted BCG/measles can be kept at 2-8°C for a maximum of 4 hours (JE vaccine also 4 hours), then discarded.  Why A wrong: 1 hour applies to reconstituted measles kept on ice per the measles section.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"After BCG vaccination, a papule occurs at the injection site in about 2 to 4 weeks and, after healing, the ulcer heals in how long, possibly leaving a small scar?","options":["1 to 2 weeks","4 to 6 weeks","8 to 12 weeks","15 to 20 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ulcer heals in 8 to 12 weeks and may leave a small scar; this is normal.  Why A/B wrong: Not the stated healing time.  Why D wrong: 15 to 20 years is the duration of protection, not weeks of healing."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the duration of protection provided by BCG vaccine?","options":["5 years","8 to 12 weeks","15 to 20 years","Lifelong"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The duration of protection from BCG is about 15 to 20 years.  Why A wrong: 5 years is the measles/OPV age limit.  Why B wrong: 8 to 12 weeks is the ulcer healing time.  Why D wrong: Not stated as lifelong for BCG."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Hepatitis-B vaccine is given to protect against Hepatitis-B disease. What is the classic schedule (time) for its three doses?","options":["Birth, 1 month and 6 months (0, 1, and 6)","Birth, 6 weeks, 10 weeks","9 months and 24 months","Only at birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis-B is given at Birth, 1 month and 6 months (0, 1, and 6). Now given after birth dose with pentavalent so total heap-B is 4 doses (birth, 6, 10, 14 weeks).  Why B wrong: Birth/6/10/14 weeks is the pentavalent-combined schedule (4 doses).  Why C wrong: 9 and 24 months relate to measles.  Why D wrong: More than one dose is required."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the dose and route of the Hepatitis-B vaccine?","options":["0.05 ml intradermal","0.5 ml IM in vastus lateralis muscle of mid thigh","0.5 ml subcutaneous upper arm","2 drops oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis-B dose is 0.5 ml, route IM (intramuscular) in vastus lateralis muscle of mid thigh (anterolateral, usually left leg) — the most common IM site in neonates/infants.  Why A wrong: 0.05 ml ID is BCG.  Why C wrong: SC upper arm is measles.  Why D wrong: Oral drops are OPV."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Why should the Hepatitis-B vaccine be given within the first 24 hours of birth?","options":["To prevent tuberculosis","Because the birth dose is effective in preventing perinatal transmission of hepatitis-B from mother to newborn","To protect against measles","Because it is the least heat sensitive vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Hep-B birth dose within first 24 hours prevents perinatal transmission of hepatitis-B from mother to newborn; Hep-B is known as the first vaccine in the newborn (NORCET 2022).  Why A/C wrong: These relate to BCG/measles.  Why D wrong: Least heat sensitive vaccine is TT, unrelated to timing rationale."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Hepatitis-B vaccine (all 3 doses) provides: (SGPGI-2024)","options":["Protection for 5 years only","Life long immunity","15 to 20 years protection","No lasting immunity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hep-B vaccine (all 3 doses) provides life long immunity (SGPGI-2024).  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated lifelong protection."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Hepatitis-B vaccine storage temperature and type are:","options":["-20°C; live attenuated","2°C to 8°C; Killed and Recombinant vaccine","+26°C; toxoid","Room temperature; cellular fraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hep-B storage temperature is 2°C to 8°C; type is Killed vaccine and Recombinant vaccine.  Why A wrong: -20°C is OPV storage; Hep-B is not live attenuated.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect temperature/type."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Measles vaccine provides protection against measles/rubeola. What is its type, volume, and diluent?","options":["Killed vaccine; 0.05 ml; NaCl","Live attenuated vaccine; 0.5 ml; distilled water","Toxoid; 0.5 ml; distilled water","Recombinant; 0.1 ml; NaCl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measles vaccine is a live attenuated vaccine, volume 0.5 ml, diluent distilled water (Trick: Measles-Distilled = MD).  Why A wrong: Measles is live attenuated, not killed; NaCl is BCG's diluent.  Why C/D wrong: Wrong type/volume."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the route and site of the Measles vaccine?","options":["Intradermal at left upper arm","Subcutaneous (Su-Q)/hypodermis at the upper right arm","IM in mid thigh","Oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measles is given Su-Q (subcutaneous)/hypodermis at the upper right arm.  Why A wrong: ID left upper arm is BCG.  Why C wrong: IM mid thigh is Hepatitis-B.  Why D wrong: Oral is OPV."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"After reconstitution, the measles vaccine should be kept on ice and used within how long; the remaining vaccine must never be used after how many hours of opening the vial?","options":["Used within 1 hour; never after 4 hours of opening","Used within 4 hours; never after 8 hours","Used within 30 minutes; never after 2 hours","Used within 2 hours; never after 6 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Reconstituted measles vaccine should be used within 1 hour (kept on ice), and remaining vaccine must never be used after 4 hours of opening the vial.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1-hour use / 4-hour discard rule."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"At what age is the measles vaccine inserted (given), and why not before 6 months?","options":["At birth; because of low birth weight","At 9 months of age, because before this age maternal antibody protects the infant; it should not be given before 6 months","At 5 years; because of school entry","At 24 months only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measles is given at 9 months because before this age maternal antibody protects the infant; it should not be given before 6 months.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the stated timing/rationale."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the booster dose timing and maximum age limit for measles vaccine?","options":["Booster at 16 to 24 months; maximum age limit 5 years","Booster at 9 months; maximum age 1 year","Booster at 6 weeks; maximum age 2 years","No booster; maximum age 9 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measles booster dose is at 16 to 24 months and the maximum age limit is 5 years.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated booster timing/age limit."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which are contraindications for the measles vaccine? (Select all that apply)","options":["Infants below 6 months of age","Convulsions","Allergy","TB and immunosuppressive therapy","A healthy 9-month-old infant"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications include infants below 6 months, convulsions, allergy, TB, and immunosuppressive therapy.  Why E wrong: A healthy 9-month-old is the ideal candidate, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"In India, measles vaccine was included in the Universal Immunization Program (UIP) in which year (and was NOT in the Expanded Program of Immunization, EPI)?","options":["1974","1978","1985","2018"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Measles vaccine was included in India's Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) in 1985. [Note: source prints \"UIP – 1978\"; the correct year per MoHFW/NHM is 1985, because the UIP itself was only launched in 1985 — the 1978 programme was the EPI, which did NOT include measles.]  Why A wrong: 1974 was the year WHO launched the global EPI, not India's UIP measles inclusion.  Why B wrong: 1978 is the EPI year (measles was NOT included in EPI); the source's \"UIP-1978\" is a misprint.  Why D wrong: 2018 is unrelated to UIP measles inclusion."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"According to the Indian Academy of Paediatrics (IAP) National Immunization Schedule (NIS), measles vaccine can be given at:","options":["Birth and 6 weeks","9 months and second dose at 24 months","12-15 months and 4-6 years","Only at 5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per IAP NIS, measles vaccine can be given at 9 months and the second dose at 24 months.  Why A wrong: Not the stated schedule.  Why C wrong: 12-15 months and 4-6 years is the CDC schedule for the first/second dose.  Why D wrong: Not the IAP schedule."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which statement is correct per the page's Note?","options":["MMR vaccine is included in National Immunization schedule-2018","MMR vaccine is NOT included in National Immunization schedule-2018","Measles cannot be given before 9 years","MMR is a killed vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Note states (Very imp.) that MMR vaccine is NOT included in the National Immunization schedule-2018.  Why A wrong: It is the opposite.  Why C wrong: Not stated; measles is given at 9 months.  Why D wrong: MMR/MMRV is live attenuated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"According to CDC, the first dose of measles is given at ___ and the second at ___.","options":["9 months; 24 months","12-15 months; 4-6 years","Birth; 6 weeks","6 months; 5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: According to CDC, the first dose of measles is given at 12-15 months and the second at 4-6 years.  Why A wrong: 9/24 months is the IAP NIS schedule.  Why C/D wrong: Not the CDC schedule."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"MMRV vaccine provides protection against which diseases?","options":["Measles, mumps, rubella and Varicella zoster (chickenpox)","Measles, mumps, rubella only","Measles and rubella only","Mumps and polio"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MMRV provides protection against measles, mumps, rubella and Varicella zoster (chickenpox).  Why B wrong: That is MMR (without varicella).  Why C/D wrong: Incomplete/incorrect coverage."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the type, volume, and route of the MMR/MMRV vaccine?","options":["Killed; 0.05 ml; ID","Live attenuated; 0.5 ml for both; Su-Q (subcutaneous) for both","Toxoid; 0.5 ml; IM","Recombinant; 0.1 ml; oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MMR/MMRV is a live attenuated vaccine, volume 0.5 ml for both, route Su-Q/subcutaneous for both.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the type/volume/route."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the dose schedule for MMR vaccine (first dose and booster)?","options":["1 dose at 12 to 15 months and second/booster at 4 to 6 years","1 dose at birth and booster at 6 weeks","1 dose at 9 months and booster at 16 months","Only 1 dose at 5 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MMR is 1 dose at 12 to 15 months and a second/booster dose at 4 to 6 years of age.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated MMR schedule."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the minimum interval between MMR doses?","options":["2 weeks","3 weeks","4 weeks (28 days)","6 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The minimum interval between MMR doses is 4 weeks (28 days).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 4-week interval."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which vaccine is the most heat sensitive, and which is the least heat sensitive?","options":["Polio is most heat sensitive; TT is least heat sensitive","TT is most heat sensitive; polio is least heat sensitive","BCG is most; measles is least","Hepatitis-B is most; DPT is least"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Polio vaccine is the most heat sensitive vaccine, while TT vaccine is the least heat sensitive.  Why B wrong: The two are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated pair."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"WHO recommends how many types of polio vaccine, and which are they?","options":["One type: OPV only","Two types: IPV & OPV","Two types: IPV & Pentavalent","Three types: IPV, OPV & BCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are two types of polio vaccine recommended by WHO: IPV & OPV.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the recommended types."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The first dose of OPV, given at the time of birth along with BCG & Hep-B, is known as:","options":["Booster dose","Zero dose","Fractional dose","Catch-up dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first dose of OPV is known as the Zero dose, given at birth along with BCG & Hep-B.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the term used for the birth OPV dose."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Up to what age can OPV vaccine be given?","options":["Up to 1 year","Up to 2 years","Up to 5 years (0 to 5 years)","Up to 9 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: OPV can be given up to 5 years of age (0 to 5 years).  Why A wrong: 1 year is the BCG/Hep-B limit.  Why B/D wrong: Not the stated OPV age range."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Regarding breast feeding after OPV administration:","options":["Breast feeding must be stopped for 30 minutes","There is no restriction for breast feeding after OPV","Breast feeding must be avoided for 4 hours","OPV cannot be given to breast-fed infants"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There is no restriction for breast feeding after OPV.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated \"no restriction.\""},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"The recently available heat-stabilized OPV can be kept without losing potency at 2°C to 8°C for a year and at room temperature for how long; non-stabilized vaccines should be stored at what temperature?","options":["A month at room temperature; -20°C in a deep freezer","A week at room temperature; +2 to +8°C","A day at room temperature; -70°C","A year at room temperature; -15°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heat-stabilized OPV keeps potency at 2-8°C for a year and for a month at room temperature; non-stabilized vaccines should be stored at -20°C in a deep freezer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated durations/temperatures."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which are contraindications of OPV? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fever","Diarrhoea/dysentery","Leukemias and malignancy","Steroid therapy","Healthy newborn at birth"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: OPV contraindications are fever, diarrhoea/dysentery, leukemias, malignancy and steroid therapy.  Why E wrong: A healthy newborn receives the OPV zero dose at birth; it is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"In the Salk vs Sabin comparison, IPV (Salk) is a ___ while OPV (Sabin) is a ___.","options":["Live attenuated vaccine; killed inactivated virus","Killed inactivated virus; live attenuated vaccine","Toxoid; recombinant vaccine","Recombinant; live attenuated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IPV (Salk) is a killed inactivated virus; OPV (Sabin) is a live attenuated vaccine (also known as bivalent vaccine).  Why A wrong: The two are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Neither is a toxoid/recombinant."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"OPV (Sabin) is also known as bivalent vaccine because it provides immunity against which polio serotypes?","options":["PV-1 and PV-2","PV-2 and PV-3","PV-1 and PV-3","PV-1, PV-2 and PV-3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: OPV is called bivalent vaccine because it provides immunity against serotypes PV-1 and PV-3.  Why A/B/D wrong: The page specifies PV-1 and PV-3 (bivalent), not the other combinations."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What is the route and dose of IPV (Salk), and of fractional IPV?","options":["IPV: 0.5 ml SC or IM at thigh; fractional IPV: 0.1 ml ID at upper arm","IPV: 2 drops oral; fractional IPV: 0.5 ml IM","IPV: 0.05 ml ID; fractional IPV: oral","IPV: 0.1 ml IM; fractional IPV: 0.5 ml SC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IPV route is 0.5 ml SC or IM at thigh; fractional IPV route is 0.1 ml, ID at the upper arm.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the routes/doses."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"IPV confers ___ immunity after 2 doses and ___ after 3 doses; OPV confers ___ after 1 dose and ___ after 3 doses.","options":["IPV 90%/99%; OPV 50%/95%","IPV 50%/95%; OPV 90%/99%","IPV 99%/90%; OPV 95%/50%","IPV 100%/100%; OPV 100%/100%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IPV: 90% immune after 2 doses, 99% after 3 doses; OPV: 50% after 1 dose, 95% after 3 doses. (Fractional IPV total dose is 2 doses at 6 & 14 weeks; OPV total dose is 5 doses.)  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or fabricate the percentages."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"What are the storage temperatures for IPV (Salk) versus OPV (Sabin)?","options":["IPV: -20°C; OPV: +2 to +8°C","IPV: +2 to +8°C; OPV: -20°C","Both at -20°C","Both at room temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IPV storage temp is +2 to +8°C; OPV storage temp is -20°C (OPV requires subzero storage and is damaged easily).  Why A wrong: The two are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Their storage temperatures differ and are not room temperature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Who discovered the inactivated polio vaccine (IPV/Salk) and in what year?","options":["Albert Sabin in 1961","Jonas Salk in 1955","Hilary Koprowski in 1950","Edward Jenner in 1798"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IPV (Salk) was discovered by Jonas Salk in 1955.  Why A wrong: Albert Sabin developed the oral polio vaccine in April 1961.  Why C wrong: Hilary Koprowski first discovered an oral polio vaccine in Feb 1950 but it failed.  Why D wrong: Edward Jenner developed smallpox vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Regarding the oral polio vaccine (Sabin) history: who first discovered an oral polio vaccine in Feb 1950 (which failed), and who later developed the widely accepted live oral polio vaccine in April 1961?","options":["Jonas Salk (1950); Edward Jenner (1961)","Hilary Koprowski (Feb 1950, failed); Albert Sabin (April 1961)","Albert Sabin (1950); Jonas Salk (1961)","Louis Pasteur (1950); Hilary Koprowski (1961)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hilary Koprowski first discovered an oral polio vaccine in Feb 1950 but it failed; Albert Sabin later developed the widely accepted live oral polio vaccine in April 1961.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misattribute the discoveries."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Which polio vaccine induces both humoral and intestinal immunity, produces antibody quickly with probably lifelong immunity, prevents paralysis and reinfection, and can be used to control epidemics?","options":["IPV (Salk)","OPV (Sabin)","Neither","Both equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OPV (Sabin) induces both humoral and intestinal immunity, develops immunity quickly (probably lifelong), prevents paralysis and reinfection, and can control epidemics.  Why A wrong: IPV induces circulating antibodies but not local intestinal immunity, does not prevent reinfection, and is not useful in controlling epidemics.  Why C/D wrong: The described features are specific to OPV."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"A Vaccine Vial Monitor (VVM) is:","options":["A refrigerator for storing vaccines","A label containing heat-sensitive material placed on a vaccine vial to register cumulative heat exposure over time","A syringe for vaccine administration","A cold box for transport"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A VVM is a label containing heat-sensitive material placed on a vial to register cumulative heat exposure over time; the inner square darkens gradually and irreversibly. It is commonly used for OPV vaccine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are equipment/devices, not the VVM label."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"In WHO grading of the VVM in OPV, which part changes color and which always remains blue?","options":["The circle changes color; the inner square remains blue","Only the inner square changes color; the circle always remains blue","Both change color","Neither changes color"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO grading is based on color changes in the VVM: only the inner square changes color, while the circle always remains blue.  Why A wrong: It is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The inner square does change while the circle stays blue."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"In the VVM WHO grade table, at which grades can OPV still be used?","options":["Grade I and Grade II","Grade III and Grade IV","Grade I only","All grades"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade I (inner square white) and Grade II (inner square light blue) — OPV can be used; Grade III (inner square blue) and Grade IV (inner square purple/black) — OPV CANNOT be used.  Why B wrong: Grades III and IV mean OPV cannot be used.  Why C wrong: Grade II also allows use.  Why D wrong: Grades III/IV prohibit use."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"In the VVM, OPV CANNOT be used when the inner square is:","options":["White or light blue","Blue or purple/black (Grade III or IV)","Only white","Always regardless of color"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the inner square is blue (Grade III) or purple/black (Grade IV), OPV cannot be used; the center square is equal to or darker than the surrounding circle (bad condition, don't use).  Why A wrong: White/light blue (Grades I/II) mean OPV can be used.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect per the grade table."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"How is a VVM interpreted as \"Good Condition, USABLE\"?","options":["The center square is equal to the surrounding circle","The center square is lighter than the surrounding circle","The center square is darker than the surrounding circle","The circle turns purple"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the center square is lighter than the surrounding circle, the vaccine is in good condition and usable (VVM A and B).  Why A/C wrong: When the center square is equal to or darker than the circle, it is bad condition — don't use.  Why D wrong: The circle always remains blue; it doesn't turn purple."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"Match VVM type with stability/use. Which pairing is CORRECT?","options":["VVM 30: High stability, used on least heat sensitive vaccines (Hep.B, Rotavirus & Pentavalent)","VVM 2: High stability","VVM 14: Least stable","VVM 7: Used for polio & BCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VVM 30 = High Stability, used on the least heat sensitive vaccines (Hep.B, Rotavirus & Pentavalent). VVM 14 = Medium Stability, VVM 7 = Moderate Stability, VVM 2 = Least Stable, used on most heat sensitive vaccines like Polio & BCG. (Source: WHO)  Why B wrong: VVM 2 is least stable, not high.  Why C wrong: VVM 14 is medium stability.  Why D wrong: VVM 2 (not VVM 7) is used for polio & BCG."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Surveillance & monitoring…","question":"VVM 2 is used on which vaccines?","options":["Hep.B and Rotavirus","Pentavalent only","The most heat sensitive vaccines like Polio & B.C.G.","TT only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: VVM 2 is the least stable and is used on the most heat sensitive vaccines like Polio & B.C.G.  Why A/B wrong: Hep.B, Rotavirus and Pentavalent use VVM 30.  Why D wrong: Not stated for VVM 2."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The DPT vaccine provides combined protection against which three diseases?","options":["Diphtheria, Pertussis, and Tetanus","Diphtheria, Polio, and Tuberculosis","Diphtheria, Pneumonia, and Typhoid","Dengue, Pertussis, and Tetanus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DPT is a combined vaccine protecting against Diphtheria, Pertussis (whooping cough) and Tetanus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These substitute unrelated diseases; DPT does not cover polio, TB, pneumonia, typhoid, or dengue."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the type of DPT vaccine?","options":["Live attenuated vaccine","Killed vaccine","Conjugate vaccine","Toxoid only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DPT is a killed vaccine.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not live attenuated, not a conjugate vaccine, and is described as a killed combined vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the route of administration of the DPT vaccine?","options":["Oral","Subcutaneous","Intramuscular (IM)","Intradermal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: DPT is given by the intramuscular (IM) route.  Why A/B/D wrong: It is not given orally, subcutaneously, or intradermally."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In infants, the recommended site for DPT vaccine injection is:","options":["Deltoid muscle","Anterolateral site of mid-thigh in vastus lateralis muscle","Upper outer quadrant of gluteal muscle","Left upper arm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In infants, DPT is given at the anterolateral mid-thigh in the vastus lateralis muscle.  Why A/C/D wrong: The gluteal (upper outer quadrant) is used for children/adults; deltoid and upper arm are not the infant site described."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In children and adults, DPT may be given at which site?","options":["Vastus lateralis of mid-thigh","Upper and outer quadrant of gluteal muscle","Deltoid only","Right upper arm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In children and adults it may be given in the upper and outer quadrant of the gluteal muscle.  Why A/C/D wrong: Vastus lateralis is the infant site; deltoid and right upper arm are not listed for DPT in children/adults."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the volume of a DPT vaccine dose?","options":["0.1 ml","0.25 ml","0.5 ml","1.0 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The volume of DPT is 0.5 ml.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 0.5 ml."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The primary DPT schedule consists of 3 doses at 4-week intervals given at which ages?","options":["Birth, 6 weeks, and 10 weeks","6 weeks, 10 weeks, and 14 weeks","2, 4, and 6 months","9, 12, and 15 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total 3 doses at 4-week intervals at 6, 10, and 14 weeks of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 6/10/14-week schedule described."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"According to the source, the DPT booster dose is given at what age?","options":["9 to 12 months","16 to 24 months","5 to 6 years","10 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The booster dose is given at 16 to 24 months of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ages are not the stated DPT booster timing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the storage temperature for the DPT vaccine?","options":["-20°C","-15°C to -25°C","2°C to 8°C","Room temperature"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: DPT storage temperature is 2°C to 8°C (refrigerator range, not freezer).  Why A/B/D wrong: -20°C and -15°C to -25°C are freezer temperatures (used for OPV/measles); room temperature is incorrect; the source states 2°C to 8°C."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which type of DPT vaccine is used as a booster in children and adults?","options":["DPT with adjuvant","Plain DPT","Acellular DPT","Pentavalent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plain DPT is used for booster in children and adults.  Why A/C/D wrong: DPT with adjuvant is used commonly in UIP & NIP; acellular and pentavalent are not the booster type stated."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The most common adjuvants used in DPT vaccines are:","options":["Squalene and MF59","Aluminium phosphate and aluminium hydroxide","Thiomersal and formaldehyde","Sodium chloride and glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common adjuvants used in DPT are aluminium phosphate and aluminium hydroxide.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the adjuvants named; thiomersal is a preservative, not the adjuvant here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"An adjuvant is best defined as a substance that:","options":["Kills the antigen to make it safe","Is co-injected with antigen to stimulate and enhance the adaptive immune system to produce antibodies","Preserves the vaccine from bacterial contamination","Dilutes the vaccine to correct volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An adjuvant is co-injected with antigen to help stimulate and enhance the adaptive immune system into producing antibodies against the antigen.  Why A/C/D wrong: Those describe inactivation, preservative, and diluent roles, not an adjuvant."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Pentavalent vaccine is also popularly known as:","options":["3-in-1 vaccine","5-in-1 vaccine / \"Panch me ek ka dum\" vaccine","Triple antigen","MMR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pentavalent is the 5-in-1 vaccine, also called \"Panch me ek ka dum\" vaccine.  Why A/C/D wrong: 3-in-1 refers to DPT, triple antigen is DPT, and MMR is a different vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Pentavalent vaccine protects against which FIVE diseases? (Select all that apply)","options":["Diphtheria","Pertussis/whooping cough","Tetanus","Hepatitis B","Haemophilus Influenza type B"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Pentavalent combines protection against Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus, Hepatitis B, and Human/Haemophilus Influenza type B.  Why all included: All five listed are the components of pentavalent vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the DPT and Hepatitis dosing schedule replaced by Pentavalent vaccine given at?","options":["0, 1, and 6 months","6, 10, and 14 weeks","Birth, 6 weeks, 9 months","2, 4, 6 months only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pentavalent is given as total 3 doses at 6, 10 and 14 weeks with no booster dose required; it replaces DPT 1,2,3 and Hepatitis 1,2,3.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 6/10/14-week schedule."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Regarding Hepatitis B vaccination after introduction of pentavalent, the birth dose of Hepatitis B is given within:","options":["24 hours","6 weeks","12 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B vaccine is continued only as a birth dose given within 24 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies within 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the upper age limit for use of Pentavalent vaccine in the UIP?","options":["6 months","9 months","1 year","2 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Upper age limit of pentavalent in UIP is 1 year.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1-year limit."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The type of pentavalent vaccine and its route of administration are:","options":["Live attenuated vaccine, oral","Killed vaccine, IM (intramuscular)","Conjugate vaccine, subcutaneous","Toxoid, intradermal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pentavalent is a killed vaccine given IM (intramuscular).  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not live attenuated, oral, conjugate, subcutaneous, or intradermal per the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In which year was the Pentavalent vaccine initially introduced as a pilot trial in Tamil Nadu and Kerala?","options":["2000","2006","2011","2016"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 2011 Pentavalent vaccine was initially introduced as a pilot trial in Tamil Nadu and Kerala before nationwide implementation.  Why A/B/D wrong: 2000 is GAVI's founding; 2016 is Rotavirus introduction; 2011 is correct."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In which year was GAVI founded?","options":["1990","2000","2005","2011"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GAVI was founded in 2000.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the founding year of 2000."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Where is the headquarters of GAVI located?","options":["Geneva, Switzerland","Bern, Switzerland","New York, USA","Atlanta, USA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GAVI headquarters is in Geneva, Switzerland. [Note: source prints \"Zenewa, Switzerland\"; the correct spelling per GAVI is Geneva.]  Why B/C/D wrong: Bern is Switzerland's capital but not GAVI's HQ; New York and Atlanta are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What does the abbreviation GAVI stand for?","options":["Global Association for Vaccine Immunity","Global Alliance for Vaccines and Immunization","General Agency for Vaccine Investment","Global Advisory on Vaccine Innovation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GAVI is the Global Alliance for Vaccines and Immunization, a public-private global health partnership.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of GAVI."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"GAVI is a collaborative mission of which organizations? (Select all that apply)","options":["Government","NGOs","UNICEF","WHO","World Bank"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: GAVI is a collaborative mission of Govt., NGOs, UNICEF, WHO and World Bank.  Why all included: All five partners are listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Typhoid disease, targeted by the typhoid vaccine, is caused by which organisms?","options":["Vibrio cholerae","Salmonella typhi and paratyphi","Shigella dysenteriae","Escherichia coli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Typhoid is caused by Salmonella typhi and paratyphi bacteria.  Why A/C/D wrong: These cause cholera, dysentery, and other infections, not typhoid."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Regarding typhoid vaccine in India, which statement is correct?","options":["It is a routine part of the national immunization schedule","It is not in the national schedule and is recommended only in high-risk areas","It is given to all newborns at birth","It is banned in India"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Typhoid vaccine is not included in the national immunization schedule in India and is recommended for high-risk areas only.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not routine, not a birth dose, and not banned."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The total dose schedule of the injectable typhoid vaccine is:","options":["Single dose only","2 doses with second dose at 4 to 6 weeks interval","3 doses at 6, 10, 14 weeks","5 doses over 1 month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total dose is 2 doses, with the second dose given at 4 to 6 weeks interval.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 2-dose schedule stated."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the storage temperature for typhoid vaccine, which should not be frozen?","options":["-20°C","2°C to 4°C","8°C to 15°C","Room temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Storage temperature is 2°C to 4°C and it should not be frozen.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match; freezing is specifically to be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the dose volume of typhoid vaccine for a person older than 10 years of age?","options":["0.1 ml","0.25 ml","0.5 ml","1.0 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Volume is 0.5 ml for >10 years of age (and 0.25 ml for 1 to 10 years).  Why A/B/D wrong: 0.25 ml is for 1–10 years; the others are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the minimum age for typhoid vaccination and how often may a booster be required?","options":[">2 years; booster every 3 years",">6 years; booster every 5 years","Birth; booster yearly",">1 year; booster every 10 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Minimum age is >2 years; typhoid vaccine protects for 3 years so booster may be required every 3 years.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated minimum age and 3-year protection."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The Typhoral (oral typhoid) vaccine currently used in India is:","options":["Vi-polysaccharide vaccine","Ty21a oral typhoid vaccine (live attenuated)","Killed whole-cell vaccine","Conjugate typhoid vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India, 'Ty21a oral typhoid vaccine' is presently used; it is a live attenuated vaccine given by the oral route.  Why A/C/D wrong: Typhoral is prepared from live attenuated strains of S. typhi, not those types."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the minimum age for vaccination with the Typhoral (oral typhoid) vaccine?","options":[">2 years",">6 years","Birth",">10 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Minimum age for Typhoral vaccine is >6 years of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: >2 years is for injectable typhoid; the others are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The Typhoral oral vaccine is given as 3 doses in India taken:","options":["Daily for 3 days","On alternate days (1, 3 and 5), one hour before meal with milk or water","Weekly for 3 weeks","Monthly for 3 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total dose is 3 doses orally, 1 capsule on alternate days (1, 3 and 5) one hour before meal with milk or water.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the alternate-day (1,3,5) schedule."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Haemophilus influenzae type-B (Hib) is a bacterium that causes meningitis and pneumonia mainly in children below what age?","options":["2 years","5 years","10 years","1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hib causes meningitis and pneumonia among children below 5 years of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies below 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In the national immunization schedule, the Hib vaccine is included in combination with which vaccine?","options":["MMR","Pentavalent","BCG","OPV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hib is included in the national immunization schedule in combination as part of the pentavalent vaccine.  Why A/C/D wrong: MMR, BCG, and OPV do not contain the Hib component."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The Hib vaccine booster dose is recommended at what age?","options":["6 to 9 months","12 to 18 months","24 to 36 months","5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Hib booster dose is given at 12 to 18 months of age (total 3 doses at 2, 4, 6 months in pentavalent).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 12–18 month booster timing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The type of Hib vaccine and the state/date of its introduction in India are:","options":["Killed vaccine; introduced 2016 in Delhi","Conjugate vaccine; introduced in Kerala and Tamil Nadu in December 2011","Live attenuated; introduced 2000 nationwide","Toxoid; introduced 2006 in Maharashtra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hib is a conjugate vaccine (volume 0.5 ml, IM route) introduced in Kerala and Tamil Nadu in December 2011.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the type, states, and year."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which vaccine protects against Hepatitis-A infection and what is its adult dose volume?","options":["Hepatitis-A vaccine; 0.5 ml","Hepatitis-A vaccine; 1.0 ml for adults (0.5 ml for children)","Hepatitis-B vaccine; 1.0 ml","Hepatitis-A vaccine; 0.25 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis-A vaccine volume is 0.5 ml for children and 1.0 ml for adults.  Why A/C/D wrong: 0.5 ml alone is the child dose; it is Hep-A not Hep-B; 0.25 ml is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the minimum age below which the Hepatitis-A vaccine should NOT be given, and its injection site?","options":["6 months; right thigh","1 year; left upper arm","2 years; buttock","Birth; deltoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis-A vaccine should not be given before 1 year of age; site is the left upper arm; route IM/SuQ.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the minimum age and site."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"For the 2-dose Hepatitis-A schedule in children, the second dose is given:","options":["1 month after the first","At least 6 months after the first dose","12 months after the first","On the same day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First dose is after 12–23 months of age and second dose at least 6 months after the first dose.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the stated at-least-6-month gap."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"For Hepatitis-A post-exposure, the vaccine should be given within:","options":["24 hours","2 weeks after exposure","72 hours","1 month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Post exposure, the vaccine is given within 2 weeks after exposure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 2-week window."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Varicella vaccine (chickenpox vaccine) protects against chickenpox, which is caused by:","options":["Herpes simplex virus","Varicella zoster virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Measles virus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chickenpox is caused by the varicella zoster virus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different viruses not causing chickenpox."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"According to CDC as noted in the source, the first dose of varicella vaccine is administered at:","options":["6 weeks","12 to 15 months of age","Birth","2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per CDC, the first dose is administered at 12 to 15 months of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated CDC timing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"If varicella vaccine is administered within 3 days of exposure to a case of chickenpox, it provides approximately what protection?","options":["30-40%","50-60%","80-90%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Administered within 3 days of exposure it provides 80-90% protection.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 80-90%."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The inactivated influenza vaccine (flu shot) is best described as which type?","options":["Live attenuated vaccine","Killed/inactivated vaccine","Conjugate vaccine","Toxoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The inactivated influenza vaccine is a killed inactivated vaccine, given Su-Q.  Why A/C/D wrong: LAIV is the live attenuated form; it is not a conjugate or toxoid."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The inactivated influenza vaccine total dose regimen is:","options":["Single dose only","2 doses (first after 6 months and second 4 weeks after the first)","3 doses at 6,10,14 weeks","5 doses over 1 month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total dose is 2 doses — first after 6 months and second 4 weeks after the first.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 2-dose regimen."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which are contraindications to the inactivated influenza vaccine? (Select all that apply)","options":["Severe febrile illness","Egg allergy","Mild cold","Diabetes"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications are severe febrile illness and egg allergy.  Why C/D wrong: Mild cold and diabetes are not listed as contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The Live Attenuated Influenza Vaccine (LAIV) is administered by which route?","options":["Intramuscular","Subcutaneous","Intranasal by spraying (nasal spray)","Oral"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: LAIV is a nasal spray given intranasally by spraying, volume 0.2 ml (0.1 ml in each nostril), single dose.  Why A/B/D wrong: These routes are not used for LAIV."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"LAIV (e.g., FluMist Quadrivalent) is approved for which age range and should never be given below what age?","options":["Approved 2 to 49 years; not given <2 years","Approved 6 months to 5 years; not given <6 months","Approved 5 to 65 years; not given <5 years","Approved all ages"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FluMist Quadrivalent is approved for individuals 2 to 49 years; never given <2 years due to risk of excessive wheezing (also avoided in pregnancy/hypersensitivity).  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the approved age range."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Rotavirus is described as the most common cause of:","options":["Bacterial pneumonia","Infantile diarrhoea","Meningitis","Cervical cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotavirus is the most common cause of infantile diarrhoea.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are caused by other organisms/agents."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"On which date was the Rotavirus vaccine included in India's national immunization schedule?","options":["March 26, 2016","December 2011","January 2000","April 2014"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On March 26, 2016 the Rotavirus vaccine was included in the national immunization schedule.  Why B/C/D wrong: December 2011 is Hib introduction; the others are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The route and dose of Rotavirus vaccine are:","options":["IM; 0.5 ml (1 drop)","Oral / per oral; 0.5 ml (5 drops)","Subcutaneous; 0.25 ml","Intranasal; 0.2 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotavirus vaccine is given orally (per oral); dose is 0.5 ml (5 drops); it is a live attenuated vaccine.  Why A/C/D wrong: The route is oral, not IM/SC/intranasal, and dose is 0.5 ml (5 drops)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the minimum interval that should be kept between two doses of Rotavirus vaccine?","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Minimum interval between 2 doses should be kept at 4 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 4-week minimum interval."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"At the State, Regional, and district level, the Rotavirus vaccine is stored at what temperature?","options":["+2°C to +8°C","-20°C in walk-in freezers (WIFs) or deep freezers (DFs)","-70°C","Room temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At State/Regional/district level RVV is stored at -20°C in walk-in freezers (WIFs) or deep freezers (DFs); below district level it is stored at +2°C to +8°C in ILRs. [Note: source prints \"20°C\"; a freezer temperature must be -20°C.]  Why A/C/D wrong: +2°C to +8°C is for below-district level (ILRs); the others are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Rotavac, one of the licensed rotavirus vaccines in India, is manufactured by:","options":["Serum Institute of India","Bharat Biotech","GlaxoSmithKline (GSK)","Merck and Co"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotavac is manufactured by Bharat Biotech (a live attenuated liquid Vero cell derivate vaccine, 3 doses at 6,10,14 weeks).  Why A/C/D wrong: Rotasiil is by Serum Institute, Rotarix by GSK, and RotaTeq by Merck and Co."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which licensed rotavirus vaccine in India is manufactured by GlaxoSmithKline (GSK) and given as 2 doses?","options":["Rotavac","Rotasiil","Rotarix","RotaTeq"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Rotarix (GSK Biological) is a live attenuated liquid vaccine given as 2 doses (at 2 months & 4 months).  Why A/B/D wrong: Rotavac (Bharat Biotech) and RotaTeq (Merck) are 3-dose; Rotasiil (Serum Institute) is 3-dose."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which rotavirus vaccine is a live attenuated DRIED vaccine using citrate bicarbonate buffer as diluent, manufactured by Serum Institute of India?","options":["Rotavac","Rotasiil","Rotarix","RotaTeq"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotasiil (Serum Institute of India) is a live attenuated dried vaccine, 3 doses at 6,10,14 weeks, using citrate bicarbonate buffer diluent, 0.5 ml (5 drops).  Why A/C/D wrong: Rotavac and RotaTeq are liquid vaccines; Rotarix is a 2-dose liquid vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Japanese Encephalitis (JE) is a viral disease transmitted by the bite of which vector?","options":["Aedes mosquito","Culex species, mainly Culex tritaeniorhynchus","Anopheles mosquito","Sandfly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: JE is caused by the bite of Culex species, mainly Culex tritaeniorhynchus.  Why A/C/D wrong: Aedes transmits dengue, Anopheles malaria, sandfly kala-azar."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The diluents used for the JE vaccine are:","options":["Distilled water only","Buffered saline solution or phosphate buffered solution","Citrate bicarbonate buffer","Normal glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: JE vaccine diluents are buffered saline solution or phosphate buffered solution (PGI-2020).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated diluents."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The JE vaccine first dose is given at what age, by which route and site?","options":["9 to 12 months; subcutaneous; right upper arm","Birth; IM; thigh","6 weeks; oral","2 years; intradermal; forearm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: JE first dose is at 9 months to 12 months, subcutaneous route, site right upper arm; storage +2°C to +8°C.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate age, route, and site."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Once a JE vaccine vial is opened, within how many hours must it be used before the remainder is discarded?","options":["1 hour","4 hours","8 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Once opened, the JE vial should be used within 4 hours; remaining vaccine should be discarded.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 4 hours."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"HPV vaccine is used to prevent human papilloma virus, which is the most common cause of which cancer in women?","options":["Breast cancer","Cervical cancer","Ovarian cancer","Endometrial cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HPV is the most common cause of cervical cancer in women; the vaccine is given to adolescent girls at the beginning of puberty.  Why A/C/D wrong: HPV is specifically linked to cervical cancer here, not these."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The HPV vaccination dose schedule for girls aged 9 to 14 years is:","options":["3 doses at 0, 2, and 6 months","2 doses — first at 0 months, second at 6 to 12 months","Single dose","3 doses at 6,10,14 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For 9 to 14 years, 2 doses are given — dose 1 at 0 months and dose 2 at 6 to 12 months.  Why A/C/D wrong: The 3-dose (0, 2, 6 months) schedule is for 15–26 years; the others are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The HPV vaccination schedule for individuals aged 15 to 26 years is:","options":["2 doses at 0 and 6 months","3 doses at 0 month, 2 months, and 6 months","Single dose","3 doses at 6,10,14 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For 15 to 26 years, 3 doses are given at 0 month, 2 months, and 6 months.  Why A/C/D wrong: The 2-dose schedule is for 9–14 years; the others are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The first indigenous HPV vaccine of India, developed by the Serum Institute of India, is:","options":["Gardasil","CERVAVAC","Cervarix","Covishield"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CERVAVAC is the first indigenous HPV vaccine, developed by Serum Institute of India (SGPGI-2023).  Why A/C/D wrong: Gardasil and Cervarix are non-indigenous HPV vaccines; Covishield is a COVID vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the most common cause of bacterial pneumonia in children under 5 years, targeted by PCV?","options":["Haemophilus influenzae type B","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common cause of bacterial pneumonia under 5 years; Hib is the second most common.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hib is the second most common; the others are not stated as the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The PCV dose schedule in infants is:","options":["3 doses at 6 weeks, 14 weeks, and 9 months","3 doses at 6,10,14 weeks","Single dose at birth","2 doses at 2 and 4 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PCV dose is 3 doses at 6 weeks, 14 weeks and 9 months in infants; in adults a single dose >65 years is enough (single dose if PPSV23 given).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 6-week/14-week/9-month schedule."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Meningococcal vaccine is used to prevent disease caused by which organism, and routine vaccination against it is:","options":["Neisseria meningitidis; routinely recommended for all","Neisseria meningitidis; NOT routinely recommended, indicated in epidemics and high-risk children with asplenia","Streptococcus pneumoniae; routine","Haemophilus influenzae; routine at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Meningococcal vaccine prevents Neisseria meningitidis; routine vaccination is not recommended, but it is indicated during epidemics for all contacts and for high-risk children with asplenia. Route Sub-Q/IM.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not routine, and the organism is Neisseria meningitidis, not the others."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The killed/inactivated cholera vaccine is given by which route and provides protection effective for how long?","options":["Oral; 2 years","Sub-Q; effective 3 to 6 months","IM; 5 years","Intradermal; 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Killed/inactivated cholera vaccine is given Sub-Q; total 2 doses (second after 4 weeks); effective 3 to 6 months.  Why A/C/D wrong: The oral route is for live cholera vaccine; the others misstate route/duration."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The live cholera vaccines Dukoral and Shanchol are given orally with how much water?","options":["50 ml for all ages","150 ml water for adults and 25 ml for children","25 ml for adults and 150 ml for children","100 ml for everyone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Live cholera vaccine (Dukoral, Shanchol) is given orally with 150 ml water for adults and 25 ml for children; 2 doses (second after 4 weeks).  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the water volumes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"For the live oral cholera vaccine, intake of food and drinks should be avoided for how long before and after vaccination?","options":["30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours","4 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Food and drinks should be avoided for 1 hour before and after vaccination; it is not recommended below 2 years of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 1 hour."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, which is the MOST heat-sensitive vaccine and at what temperature should it be stored?","options":["BCG; +2°C to +8°C","Polio (OPV); stored at -20°C","Measles; -70°C","DPT; +4°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polio is the most heat-sensitive vaccine and should be stored at -20°C; polio and measles must be stored in the freezer compartment.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the vaccine or storage temperature."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which vaccines must be stored in the cold part and never allowed to freeze? (Select all that apply)","options":["DPT, DT, TT (\"T\" series)","BCG","Hepatitis-B","Typhoid","Polio (OPV)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: DPT, DT, TT (\"T\" series), BCG, Hepatitis-B, Typhoid and diluents must be stored in the cold part and never frozen.  Why E wrong: Polio must be stored in the freezer compartment, not the cold part."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"While distributing vaccines, which two rules should be followed?","options":["LIFO and Last-to-expire-first-out","\"First in first out (FIFO)\" and \"First to expire-first out (FEFO)\"","Random selection","Newest stock first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Distribution follows \"First in first out/FIFO\" and \"First to expire-first out/FEFO\" rules.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the FIFO/FEFO principle."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Most vaccines except polio can be stored for up to how long if the refrigerator temperature is strictly maintained at 2 to 8 degrees C?","options":["1 week","3 weeks","5 weeks","8 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Most vaccines except polio can be stored up to 5 weeks if the refrigerator is strictly maintained at 2 to 8°C.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies up to 5 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Reconstituted or dissolved BCG and Measles vaccines can be kept at 2° to 8°C for a maximum of:","options":["1 hour","4 hours","8 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reconstituted/dissolved BCG and Measles vaccines can be kept at 2° to 8°C for maximum 4 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies a maximum of 4 hours."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Rabies is a vaccine-preventable viral disease. Dogs are the main source of human rabies deaths in what percentage of cases?","options":["50%","75%","99%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dogs are the main source of human rabies deaths (99%); secondary sources are monkeys and other animals.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 99%."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"According to Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP), the FIRST step in wound management is:","options":["Administration of rabies immunoglobulin","Immediate wound washing with soap and water for 15 minutes","A series of rabies vaccinations","Applying antibiotic ointment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PEP first step is immediate wound washing with soap and water for 15 minutes, then rabies vaccination, and if indicated rabies immunoglobulin.  Why A/C/D wrong: Immunoglobulin and vaccinations come after wound washing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The 5-dose intramuscular anti-rabies regimen (1-1-1-1-1) is given on which days?","options":["0, 3, 7, 14 and 28","0, 7, 14 and 21","0, 3, 7 and 14","0, 7, 21 and 28"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 5-dose regimen (1-1-1-1-1) is given on days 0, 3, 7, 14 and 28.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 5-dose day schedule."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In the 2-1-1 anti-rabies regimen (2-0-1-0-1), how are doses distributed?","options":["One dose each on days 0, 3, 7, 14","Two doses on day 0 (right and left deltoid), then one dose each on day 7 and day 28","Two doses on day 28 only","One dose daily for 5 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Two doses are given on day 0 in the deltoid muscle (right and left arm), then one additional dose on day 7 and day 28.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the 2-1-1 distribution."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Anti-rabies vaccine should NOT be injected into which region due to lower neutralizing antibody titres?","options":["Deltoid muscle","Anterolateral thigh","Gluteal region","Suprascapular area"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Vaccines should not be injected into the gluteal region due to lower neutralizing antibody titres.  Why A/B/D wrong: Deltoid (adults/children ≥2 yr), anterolateral thigh (<2 yr), and suprascapular area are acceptable sites."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The 2-site intradermal (ID) anti-rabies method (2-2-2-0-2) is given on which days, and what volume per ID site?","options":["Days 0, 3, 7 and 28; 0.1 ml per site","Days 0, 7, 14 and 28; 0.5 ml per site","Days 0, 3, 7, 14 and 28; 1 ml per site","Days 0 and 28 only; 0.2 ml per site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2-site ID method (2-2-2-0-2) is given on days 0, 3, 7 and 28; one dose of 0.1 ml is given at two different lymphatic drainage sites; volume per ID site is 0.1 mL.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the days and/or ID volume."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"A vaccine administered intradermally (ID) must raise a visible and palpable finding in the skin, referred to as a:","options":["Wheal called a \"bleb\"","Nodule","Abscess","Papule with pus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A correctly given ID vaccine (0.1 mL per site) must raise a visible and palpable \"bleb\" in the skin.  Why B/C/D wrong: A nodule, abscess, or pus-filled papule are not the expected correct ID sign."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The intramuscular Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis (PrEP) rabies regimen is 3 doses given on which days, at what volume?","options":["Days 0, 3, 7; 0.1 ml","Days 0, 7 and 21 or 28; volume 1 ml","Days 0, 14, 28; 0.5 ml","Days 0 and 28; 1 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intramuscular PrEP is a 3-dose regimen on days 0, 7 and 21 or 28, volume 1 ml (intradermal PrEP uses 0.1 ml on the same days).  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the PrEP days and/or IM volume."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Under WHO Rabies Exposure Categories, \"touching or feeding animals, animal licks on intact skin\" is classified as:","options":["Category I (No exposure)","Category II (Exposure)","Category III (Exposure)","Category IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category I (No exposure) includes touching/feeding animals and licks on intact skin; PEP is only washing of exposed skin surfaces, no other PEP required.  Why B/C/D wrong: Category II is nibbling/minor scratches without bleeding; Category III is severe exposure; there is no Category IV."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"A single or multiple transdermal bite, contamination of mucous membrane, or exposure due to contact with bats falls under which WHO rabies exposure category?","options":["Category I","Category II","Category III (severe exposure)","No exposure"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Category III (severe exposure) includes transdermal bites/scratches, mucous membrane contamination, broken skin with saliva, and bat exposures; requires wound washing, rabies vaccine, and rabies immunoglobulin (HRIG) within 7 days of first dose.  Why A/B/D wrong: Category I is no exposure; Category II is minor scratches without bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"For a Category III rabies exposure, Rabies immunoglobulin (HRIG) should be given within how many days after the first dose of vaccine?","options":["3 days","7 days","10 days","14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Category III, rabies immunoglobulin (HRIG) should be given within 7 days after the first dose of vaccine.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies within 7 days."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" notes on rabies, an animal (dogs and cats only) should be observed for how many days?","options":["5 days","7 days","10 days","14 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An animal (dogs and cats only) should be observed for 10 days.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 10 days."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which statement about rabies is correct per the source?","options":["Rabies has a 50% fatality rate and PEP has many contraindications","Rabies is 100% fatal, so there is no contraindication to PEP","Rabies is rarely fatal, so PEP is optional","PEP is contraindicated in pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rabies is 100% fatal, so there is no contraindication to PEP; exposure to all wild animals should be treated as Category III.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the 100% fatality / no-contraindication statement."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Under IDSP reporting, a suspect rabies case is reported by a health worker in which form?","options":["'S Form'","'P Form'","'L Form'","'C Form'"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A suspect rabies case is reported in the 'S Form' by a health worker under IDSP; probable in 'P Form' by Medical Officer; laboratory confirmed in 'L Form' by laboratory worker.  Why B/C/D wrong: 'P Form' is for probable, 'L Form' for lab-confirmed; there is no 'C Form'."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Under IDSP, a laboratory-confirmed rabies case is reported in which form?","options":["'S Form'","'P Form'","'L Form'","'D Form'"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A laboratory-confirmed rabies case is reported in the 'L Form' by a laboratory worker under IDSP.  Why A/B/D wrong: 'S Form' is suspect (health worker), 'P Form' is probable (Medical Officer); there is no 'D Form'."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In the Heat Sensitivity table, which vaccine is listed as the MOST heat-sensitive?","options":["BCG (after reconstitution)","OPV","Td/TT","HepB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Heat Sensitivity table, BCG (after reconstitution) is the most sensitive, followed by OPV; Measles, MR, MMR; DPT, YF; BCG (before reconstitution); Hib, DT; and Td, TT, HepB, JE (least sensitive).  Why B/C/D wrong: OPV is next most sensitive; Td/TT/HepB are least sensitive."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In the Freeze Sensitivity table, which vaccine is listed as the MOST freeze-sensitive?","options":["TT","HepB","DT","Hib lyophilised"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the Freeze Sensitivity table, HepB is the most freeze-sensitive, followed by Hib (liquid); DTP combinations; DT; Td; and TT, Hib lyophilised (least sensitive).  Why A/C/D wrong: TT and Hib lyophilised are least sensitive; DT is intermediate."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"According to the Route of Vaccine Administration table, which vaccines are given by the ORAL route? (Select all that apply)","options":["OPV","Live Cholera","Rota Virus","Vitamin A","Typhoral oral (for typhoid)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Oral route vaccines/agents are OPV, Live Cholera, Rota Virus, Vita-A, and Typhoral oral (for typhoid).  Why all included: All five are listed under the oral route."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which vaccines are administered by the intradermal route per the Route of Administration table?","options":["BCG and Fractional IPV","OPV and Rotavirus","DPT and Hepatitis B","Live influenza and MMR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intradermal route vaccines are BCG and Fractional IPV.  Why B/C/D wrong: OPV/Rota are oral; DPT/HepB are IM; live influenza is intranasal and MMR is subcutaneous."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The Yellow fever vaccine (17-D vaccine) is administered by which route?","options":["Oral","Intramuscular","Subcutaneous","Intranasal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Yellow fever vaccine (17-D vaccine) is given subcutaneously (MP CHO 2021), along with Measles, Mumps, Rubella, MMR, Meningococcal, influenza, JE, Typhoid, Hep-A, Chickenpox, inactivated influenza, and Cholera.  Why A/B/D wrong: These routes are not listed for the 17-D yellow fever vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which vaccine is administered by the intranasal route according to the table?","options":["Killed influenza","Live influenza vaccine","BCG","OPV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Live influenza vaccine is given by the intranasal route.  Why A/C/D wrong: Killed influenza is subcutaneous; BCG is intradermal; OPV is oral."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The smallpox vaccine is administered by which special method using a bifurcated needle at the upper arm?","options":["Intradermal","Scarification","Subcutaneous","Oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smallpox vaccine is given by scarification using a bifurcated needle at the upper arm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the scarification method described."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"In India, what is the minimum level of the health system where vaccine storage occurs?","options":["Sub-centre","PHC (Primary Health Centre)","CHC","District hospital"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India, the minimum level of vaccine storage is the PHC; below PHC level vaccines are transported on the day of immunization.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sub-centres do not store vaccines (supplied on the day of use); CHC/district are higher levels."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"At which level of the health system does cold chain failure occur maximally?","options":["PHC","Sub-center (HWC-SHC)","District store","Regional store"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cold chain failure maximally happens at the sub-center (HWC-SHC).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the sub-center as the point of maximum failure."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which instrument is used to monitor the temperature of the cold chain at the PHC level?","options":["Vaccine vial monitor","Dial thermometer","Freeze-tag","Shake test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The dial thermometer is used to monitor temperature of the cold chain at the PHC level.  Why A/C/D wrong: VVM and freeze-tag are other indicators; the shake test detects freeze damage, not routine monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The Shake test is used to detect what type of damage, and in which vaccines?","options":["Heat damage in live vaccines like OPV","Freeze damage in aluminium-based/adsorbed freeze-sensitive vaccines such as DTP, DT, Td, TT, typhoid, and hepatitis B","Contamination in reconstituted BCG","Expiry in oral vaccines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The shake test detects freeze damage in aluminium-based, adsorbed, freeze-sensitive vaccines (DTP, DT, Td, TT, typhoid, hepatitis B); these must never be frozen as it reduces immunogenicity.  Why A/C/D wrong: It detects freeze damage, not heat damage, contamination, or expiry."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"The \"Reverse cold chain\" (maintaining +2°C to +8°C) is used for transporting what?","options":["Vaccines to sub-centres","Stool samples from suspected polio cases to the laboratory for diagnostic purposes","Blood samples for typing","Reconstituted BCG vials"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reverse cold chain is used for transporting stool samples from suspected polio cases to the laboratory for diagnosis, maintaining +2°C to +8°C.  Why A/C/D wrong: It refers specifically to diagnostic stool sample transport, not vaccine or blood/BCG transport."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"What is the temperature range of the cold chain in India?","options":["-20°C to 0°C","+2°C to +8°C","0°C to +4°C","+8°C to +15°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The temperature of the cold chain in India is +2°C to +8°C.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated +2°C to +8°C range."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Which vaccines contain egg-proteins and are therefore contraindicated in egg allergy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Rabies vaccine","Yellow fever vaccine","Influenza vaccine","MMR vaccine","Tick encephalitis vaccine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Egg-protein-containing vaccines (contraindicated in egg allergy) include Rabies, Yellow fever, Herpes simplex, Tick encephalitis, MMR, and Influenza vaccines.  Why all included: All five listed are named in the source (along with herpes simplex)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) is a rare side effect of which vaccine?","options":["Oral polio vaccine (OPV)","Killed influenza vaccine","Measles vaccine","BCG vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) is a rare side effect of killed influenza vaccine.  Why A/C/D wrong: VAP is linked to OPV; toxic shock to measles/MMR; GBS is specifically linked to killed influenza vaccine here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Vaccine-associated paralysis (VAP) is a rare side effect of which vaccine?","options":["Killed influenza","Oral polio vaccine (OPV)","MMR","Hepatitis B"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vaccine-associated paralysis (VAP) is a rare side effect of oral polio vaccine (OPV).  Why A/C/D wrong: GBS is linked to killed influenza; toxic shock to measles/MMR; VAP is specific to OPV."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"Toxic shock syndrome is a rare side effect of which vaccines?","options":["Measles and MMR vaccine","OPV","Killed influenza","Hepatitis B"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Toxic shock syndrome is a rare side effect of measles and MMR vaccine.  Why B/C/D wrong: OPV causes VAP; killed influenza causes GBS; these are not linked to toxic shock here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · DPT vaccine, adjuvants, Pentavalent vaccine, GAVI…","question":"If any cold compartment vaccine vials are found frozen, what should be done with them?","options":["Use them immediately","They have lost potency and should be discarded","Rewarm and use","Store at room temperature and use within a week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If cold compartment vaccine vials are frozen, we know they have lost potency and should be discarded.  Why A/C/D wrong: Frozen freeze-sensitive vaccines lose potency and must not be used."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In which year was OPV (Oral Polio Vaccine) introduced as a milestone vaccine under India's Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)?","options":["1975","1978","1983","1985"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The milestone timeline of vaccines under UIP lists OPV introduced in 1978.  Why A wrong: 1975 is not listed as an OPV milestone year.  Why C wrong: 1983 was the year TT was introduced.  Why D wrong: 1985 was the year Measles vaccine was introduced."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Under the UIP milestone timeline, TT (Tetanus Toxoid) vaccine was introduced in which year?","options":["1978","1983","1985","2007-08"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TT was introduced under UIP in 1983.  Why A wrong: 1978 corresponds to OPV.  Why C wrong: 1985 corresponds to Measles.  Why D wrong: 2007-08 corresponds to Hepatitis B."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Measles vaccine was introduced under the UIP in which year?","options":["1978","1983","1985","2011"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Measles vaccine was added to UIP in 1985.  Why A wrong: 1978 corresponds to OPV.  Why B wrong: 1983 corresponds to TT.  Why D wrong: 2011 (Dec) corresponds to Hib/Pentavalent vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In which year was Hepatitis B vaccine introduced under the UIP milestone timeline?","options":["1985","2007-08","2014","2017"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B was introduced in 2007-08.  Why A wrong: 1985 corresponds to Measles.  Why C wrong: 2014 (July) corresponds to JE vaccine.  Why D wrong: 2017 corresponds to MR/PCV/Td vaccines."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) and Pentavalent vaccine were introduced under UIP in which month/year?","options":["December 2011","July 2014","November 2015","March 2016"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both Hib and Pentavalent vaccine were introduced in December 2011.  Why B wrong: July 2014 corresponds to JE vaccine.  Why C wrong: Nov 15, 2015 corresponds to IPV.  Why D wrong: March 2016 corresponds to Rotavirus vaccine (RVV)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"JE (Japanese Encephalitis) vaccine was introduced under UIP in which month and year?","options":["July 2014","November 2015","March 2016","May 2017"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: JE vaccine was introduced in July 2014.  Why B wrong: Nov 2015 corresponds to IPV.  Why C wrong: March 2016 corresponds to Rotavirus.  Why D wrong: May 2017 corresponds to PCV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Inactivated Polio Vaccine (IPV) was introduced under UIP on which date?","options":["July 2014","15 November 2015","March 2016","May 2017"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IPV was introduced on 15 November 2015.  Why A wrong: July 2014 corresponds to JE vaccine.  Why C wrong: March 2016 corresponds to Rotavirus.  Why D wrong: May 2017 corresponds to PCV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Rotavirus Vaccine (RVV) was introduced under UIP in which month/year?","options":["November 2015","March 2016","2017","May 2017"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotavirus Vaccine (RVV) was introduced in March 2016.  Why A wrong: Nov 2015 corresponds to IPV.  Why C wrong: 2017 corresponds to MR vaccine.  Why D wrong: May 2017 corresponds to PCV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccine (PCV) was introduced under UIP in which month/year?","options":["March 2016","2017 (general)","May 2017","July 2014"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PCV was introduced in May 2017.  Why A wrong: March 2016 corresponds to Rotavirus.  Why B wrong: MR and Td vaccines were listed under 2017 (not specifically May).  Why D wrong: July 2014 corresponds to JE vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Measles Rubella (MR) vaccine and Tetanus & adult diphtheria (Td) vaccine were introduced under UIP in which year?","options":["2015","2016","2017","2011"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Both MR vaccine and Td vaccine were introduced in 2017.  Why A wrong: 2015 corresponds to IPV.  Why B wrong: 2016 corresponds to Rotavirus.  Why D wrong: 2011 corresponds to Hib/Pentavalent."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the upper age limit for vaccination under UIP, what is the upper age limit for the Hepatitis B (Birth Dose)?","options":["15 days","24 hours","1 year","5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The upper age limit for Hepatitis B (Birth Dose) is 24 hours.  Why A wrong: 15 days is the limit for OPV Zero dose.  Why C wrong: 1 year is the limit for BCG and Penta/fIPV/PCV/Rotavirus.  Why D wrong: 5 years is the limit for OPV and Measles/MR."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the upper age limit for administering the OPV Zero dose under UIP?","options":["24 hours","15 days","1 year","5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OPV Zero dose has an upper age limit of 15 days.  Why A wrong: 24 hours is for Hepatitis B (Birth Dose).  Why C wrong: 1 year is for BCG.  Why D wrong: 5 years is for OPV (routine) and Measles/MR."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Under the UIP upper age limit table, what is the upper age limit for BCG vaccination?","options":["15 days","1 year","5 years","7 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG has an upper age limit of 1 year.  Why A wrong: 15 days applies to OPV Zero dose and JE.  Why C wrong: 5 years applies to OPV and Measles/MR.  Why D wrong: 7 years applies to DPT."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the UIP upper age limit table, what is the upper age limit for Penta, fIPV, PCV and Rotavirus vaccines?","options":["15 days","1 year","5 years","7 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Penta, fIPV, PCV and Rotavirus have an upper age limit of 1 year.  Why A wrong: 15 days is for OPV Zero dose and JE.  Why C wrong: 5 years is for OPV and Measles/MR.  Why D wrong: 7 years is for DPT."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the upper age limit for the routine OPV (not zero dose) and Measles/MR vaccines under UIP?","options":["1 year","5 years","7 years","15 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Both OPV (routine) and Measles/MR have an upper age limit of 5 years.  Why A wrong: 1 year is for BCG and Penta/fIPV/PCV/Rotavirus.  Why C wrong: 7 years is for DPT.  Why D wrong: 15 days is for OPV Zero dose and JE."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the UIP upper age limit table, what is the upper age limit for DPT vaccine?","options":["1 year","5 years","7 years","15 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: DPT has an upper age limit of 7 years.  Why A wrong: 1 year is for BCG.  Why B wrong: 5 years is for OPV and Measles/MR.  Why D wrong: 15 days is for OPV Zero dose and JE."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the upper age limit for JE (Japanese Encephalitis) vaccine under the UIP upper age limit table?","options":["24 hours","15 days","1 year","5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The UIP upper age limit table as printed lists JE as \"15 days.\" [Note: the source table prints \"JE — 15 days,\" which is a typo; JE-1 is first given at 9 completed months (see the same book's NIS table), so the correct UIP upper age limit for JE is 15 YEARS, not 15 days. The factually-correct value (15 years) is not among the options, so answer B follows the source table as printed.]  Why A wrong: 24 hours is for Hepatitis B Birth Dose.  Why C wrong: 1 year is for BCG.  Why D wrong: 5 years is for OPV and Measles/MR."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Under the Multi-Dose Open Vial Policy 2015, partially used multi-dose vials may be reused for up to how many weeks?","options":["2 weeks (14 days)","4 weeks (28 days)","6 weeks (42 days)","1 week (7 days)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The policy allows reuse of partially used multi-dose vials up to 4 weeks (28 days).  Why A wrong: 2 weeks is not the stated period.  Why C wrong: 6 weeks exceeds the stated 4-week limit.  Why D wrong: 1 week is not the stated period."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Under the Open Vial Policy, BCG, Measles, Rotavirus and JE vaccines should NOT be used beyond how many hours of reconstitution under any circumstance?","options":["2 hours","4 hours","6 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These reconstituted vaccines must not be used beyond 4 hours of reconstitution; the ANM must discard them after 4 hours or at the end of session, whichever is earlier.  Why A wrong: 2 hours is not the stated limit.  Why C wrong: 6 hours exceeds the 4-hour limit.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is far beyond the limit for reconstituted vaccines."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The Multi-Dose Open Vial Policy 2015 is applicable to which of the following vaccines? (Select all that apply)","options":["DPT","TT","Hepatitis B","OPV and IPV","Measles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The open vial policy is applicable to DPT, TT, Hepatitis B, OPV/IPV, Hib-containing Pentavalent vaccine and PCV.  Why E wrong: Measles is in the \"does not applicable on\" list (along with BCG, JE, Rotavirus and Indian Covid-19 vaccines)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The Multi-Dose Open Vial Policy does NOT apply to which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Measles","BCG","Japanese Encephalitis (JE)","Rotavirus","DPT"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The policy does not apply to Measles, BCG, JE, Rotavirus and the Indian Covid-19 vaccines (COVAXIN and COVISHIELD).  Why E wrong: DPT is on the \"applicable on\" list."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which Indian Covid-19 vaccines are specifically listed as NOT covered under the Multi-Dose Open Vial Policy?","options":["COVAXIN and COVISHIELD","Sputnik and COVAXIN","Pfizer and Moderna","COVISHIELD and Sputnik"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The policy states it does not apply to the Indian Covid-19 Vaccines COVAXIN and COVISHIELD.  Why B wrong: Sputnik is not named in the source.  Why C wrong: Pfizer and Moderna are not named in the source.  Why D wrong: Sputnik is not named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the history of vaccination in India, until which year was the vaccine imported from Great Britain?","options":["1832","1850","1879","1893"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India, till 1850 the vaccine was imported from Great Britain.  Why A wrong: 1832 was when few research efforts were done in Bombay.  Why C wrong: 1879 was when animal experiments for lymph started in Madras.  Why D wrong: 1893 was when Dr Haffkine conducted vaccine trials in Agra."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In which city did early research efforts on vaccination in India begin in 1832?","options":["Madras (Chennai)","Bombay (Mumbai)","Agra","Bengal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first few research efforts were done in Bombay (Mumbai) in 1832.  Why A wrong: Madras (Chennai) is where animal experiments for lymph started in 1879.  Why C wrong: Agra is where Dr Haffkine conducted vaccine trials in 1893.  Why D wrong: Bengal was the site of the cholera epidemic."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Animal experiments for lymph started in Madras (now Chennai) in 1879, with initial success in which year?","options":["1879","1880","1893","1896"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The animal experiments for lymph in Madras achieved initial success in 1880.  Why A wrong: 1879 was when the experiments started.  Why C wrong: 1893 was Dr Haffkine's Agra cholera vaccine trial.  Why D wrong: 1896 was when the plague epidemic started in India."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In 1893, Dr Haffkine conducted successful cholera vaccine trials in which city/state?","options":["Bombay, Maharashtra","Agra, Uttar Pradesh","Madras, Tamil Nadu","Bengal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1893, Dr Haffkine conducted vaccine trials in Agra, Uttar Pradesh, and effectively controlled the disease.  Why A wrong: Bombay was the site of research in 1832 and the later plague laboratory.  Why C wrong: Madras was the site of lymph animal experiments.  Why D wrong: Bengal was where the cholera epidemic occurred."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The Epidemic Act was passed in India in which year, following the bubonic plague epidemic that began in 1896?","options":["1893","1896","1897","1899"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Epidemic Act was passed in India in 1897, enacted by the British.  Why A wrong: 1893 was the Agra cholera vaccine trial.  Why B wrong: 1896 was when the plague epidemic started.  Why D wrong: 1899 was when the laboratory was called the Plague Laboratory."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which is regarded as the FIRST vaccine developed in India, and by whom?","options":["Cholera vaccine by Dr Haffkine","Plague vaccine by Dr Haffkine (1897)","Smallpox vaccine by the British","BCG vaccine by an Indian scientist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dr Haffkine developed the plague vaccine in 1897, and it is arguably the first vaccine developed in India.  Why A wrong: The cholera vaccine trial was conducted, but the plague vaccine is named as the first developed in India.  Why C wrong: The smallpox vaccine was imported from Great Britain, not developed in India.  Why D wrong: BCG is not mentioned as the first vaccine developed in India."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The laboratory established by Dr Haffkine (Plague Laboratory, 1899; Bombay Bacteriological Lab, 1905) was finally named as what, and in which year?","options":["Haffkine Institute in 1925","Pasteur Institute in 1905","National Institute of Virology in 1925","Haffkine Institute in 1899"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The laboratory was finally named the Haffkine Institute in 1925.  Why B wrong: It was called Bombay Bacteriological Lab in 1905, not Pasteur Institute.  Why C wrong: National Institute of Virology is not named in the source.  Why D wrong: In 1899 it was called the Plague Laboratory, not Haffkine Institute."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the National Immunization Schedule (NIS), when is the TT-1 dose given to a pregnant woman?","options":["Early in pregnancy","4 weeks after TT-1 dose","At 6 weeks","Within 24 hours of delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TT-1 is given early in pregnancy (0.5 ml, intramuscular, upper arm).  Why B wrong: 4 weeks after TT-1 describes TT-2.  Why C wrong: 6 weeks refers to infant vaccines like OPV/Pentavalent.  Why D wrong: 24 hours postnatal is not the schedule for TT-1."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In the NIS, when is the TT-2 dose administered to a pregnant woman?","options":["Early in pregnancy","4 weeks after the TT-1 dose","At delivery","16-24 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TT-2 is given 4 weeks after the TT-1 dose (0.5 ml, IM, upper arm).  Why A wrong: Early in pregnancy is when TT-1 is given.  Why C wrong: At delivery is not the schedule.  Why D wrong: 16-24 months refers to booster doses for children."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, the TT-Booster dose is given if the pregnant woman received 2 TT doses in a pregnancy within the last how many years?","options":["1 year","2 years","3 years","5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The TT-Booster is given if 2 TT doses were received in a pregnancy within the last 3 years.  Why A wrong: 1 year is incorrect per the schedule.  Why B wrong: 2 years is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 5 years is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the NIS, what is the dose, route and site for BCG vaccination in infants?","options":["0.1 ml, intradermal, left upper arm","0.5 ml, intramuscular, right upper arm","2 drops, oral, oral","0.5 ml, subcutaneous, right upper arm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BCG is given 0.1 ml (0.05 ml until 1 month), intradermal, left upper arm, at birth or as early as possible till one year of age.  Why B wrong: 0.5 ml IM is used for Pentavalent/DPT, not BCG.  Why C wrong: 2 drops oral describes OPV.  Why D wrong: 0.5 ml subcutaneous right upper arm describes Measles/MR."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In the NIS, when is the Hepatitis B (Birth dose) given, and at what site?","options":["At birth within 24 hours; antero-lateral side of mid-thigh","At 6 weeks; left upper arm","At 9 months; right upper arm","At birth within 15 days; oral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B (Birth dose) is given 0.5 ml IM at the antero-lateral side of mid-thigh, at birth or as early as possible within 24 hours.  Why B wrong: 6 weeks/left upper arm does not match Hepatitis B birth dose.  Why C wrong: 9 months right upper arm describes Measles/MR-1.  Why D wrong: OPV-0 is given orally within 15 days, not Hepatitis B."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, at what ages are OPV 1, 2 & 3 given to infants?","options":["At birth","At 6 weeks, 10 weeks & 14 weeks","At 9 months","At 16-24 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OPV 1, 2 & 3 are given at 6, 10 and 14 weeks (2 drops oral); OPV can be given till 5 years of age.  Why A wrong: OPV-0 (zero dose) is given at birth within first 15 days.  Why C wrong: 9 months is for Measles/MR-1 and JE-1.  Why D wrong: 16-24 months is for OPV Booster and other boosters."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In the NIS, what is the dose and route for the Rotavirus vaccine given at 6, 10 and 14 weeks?","options":["2 drops, oral","5 drops, oral","0.5 ml, intramuscular","0.1 ml, intradermal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotavirus is given as 5 drops orally at 6, 10 and 14 weeks (can be given till one year of age).  Why A wrong: 2 drops is the OPV dose.  Why C wrong: 0.5 ml IM is the Pentavalent dose.  Why D wrong: 0.1 ml ID is the IPV/BCG dose."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, IPV is given as two fractional doses at which ages, and by what route?","options":["At 6 and 14 weeks of age; intradermal (right upper arm)","At birth and 6 weeks; oral","At 9 and 16 months; subcutaneous","At 6 and 10 weeks; intramuscular"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IPV is given as two fractional doses (0.1 ml) at 6 and 14 weeks of age, intradermal, right upper arm.  Why B wrong: Birth/6 weeks oral does not match IPV.  Why C wrong: 9/16 months subcutaneous describes Measles/JE.  Why D wrong: 6/10 weeks IM does not match IPV's two fractional dose schedule."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, when is the Measles/MR 1st dose given, and by what route and site?","options":["9 completed months–12 months; sub-cutaneous; right upper arm","At birth; intradermal; left upper arm","6 weeks; intramuscular; mid-thigh","16-24 months; oral; oral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measles/MR 1st dose is given 0.5 ml subcutaneous at the right upper arm at 9 completed months–12 months (can be given till 5 years of age).  Why B wrong: Birth/intradermal/left upper arm describes BCG.  Why C wrong: 6 weeks IM mid-thigh describes Pentavalent.  Why D wrong: 16-24 months oral describes OPV Booster."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, when and by what route is the JE-1 dose given?","options":["9 completed months–12 months; sub-cutaneous; left upper arm","At birth; oral","6 weeks; intramuscular","16-24 months; intradermal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: JE-1 is given 0.5 ml subcutaneous at the left upper arm at 9 completed months–12 months.  Why B wrong: Birth/oral describes OPV-0.  Why C wrong: 6 weeks IM describes Pentavalent.  Why D wrong: 16-24 months is for JE-2/boosters, and JE is subcutaneous not intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, what is the dose of Vitamin A (1st dose) given at 9 completed months with the MR vaccine?","options":["1 ml (1 lakh IU), oral","2 ml (2 lakh IU), oral","0.5 ml, intramuscular","0.1 ml, intradermal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin A 1st dose is 1 ml (1 lakh IU) given orally at 9 completed months with the MR vaccine.  Why B wrong: 2 ml (2 lakh IU) is the 2nd to 9th dose given every 6 months.  Why C wrong: Vitamin A is oral, not intramuscular.  Why D wrong: 0.1 ml intradermal describes BCG/IPV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, the DPT booster-1 and Measles/MR 2nd dose are given at which age?","options":["6 weeks","9 months","16-24 months","5-6 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: DPT booster-1 (0.5 ml IM) and Measles/MR 2nd dose (0.5 ml subcutaneous) are both given at 16-24 months.  Why A wrong: 6 weeks is for primary infant vaccines.  Why B wrong: 9 months is for the 1st Measles/MR dose.  Why D wrong: 5-6 years is for DPT Booster-2."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, Vitamin A (2nd to 9th dose) is given as what dose and how often?","options":["1 ml (1 lakh IU) once at 9 months","2 ml (2 lakh IU), one dose every 6 months up to the age of 5 years","0.5 ml every year till 10 years","2 drops orally every 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin A 2nd to 9th dose is 2 ml (2 lakh IU) orally, one dose every 6 months up to the age of 5 years.  Why A wrong: 1 ml (1 lakh IU) at 9 months is the 1st dose.  Why C wrong: 0.5 ml every year is not the Vitamin A schedule.  Why D wrong: 2 drops describes OPV, not Vitamin A."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, DPT Booster-2 is given at which age?","options":["16-24 months","5-6 years","10 years","9 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DPT Booster-2 (0.5 ml IM, upper arm) is given at 5-6 years.  Why A wrong: 16-24 months is for DPT Booster-1.  Why C wrong: 10 years (and 16 years) is for TT.  Why D wrong: 9 months is for Measles/MR-1 and JE-1."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to NIS, at what ages is TT given to children (as opposed to pregnant women)?","options":["6 and 14 weeks","9 and 16 months","10 years & 16 years","5-6 years only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: TT (0.5 ml IM, upper arm) is given at 10 years and 16 years.  Why A wrong: 6/14 weeks are infant primary vaccine ages.  Why B wrong: 9/16 months are for Measles/JE.  Why D wrong: 5-6 years is for DPT Booster-2."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the TT Injection Guidelines, when should the second dose of TT be given relative to the first dose in a pregnant woman?","options":["Same day as the first dose","1 month/4 weeks after the first, preferably at least one month before EDD","Only after delivery","3 months after the first dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second TT dose is given 1 month/4 weeks after the first, preferably at least one month before the EDD (expected date of delivery).  Why A wrong: The two doses are separated by 1 month, not given the same day.  Why C wrong: It is not routinely deferred to postnatal unless the first dose was after 38 weeks.  Why D wrong: The interval is 4 weeks, not 3 months."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the TT Injection Guidelines, if a woman receives the first TT dose after 38 weeks of pregnancy, when may the second dose be given?","options":["Immediately, same visit","In the postnatal period, after a gap of 4 weeks","Not required at all","During labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the first dose is given after 38 weeks, the second dose may be given in the postnatal period after a gap of 4 weeks.  Why A wrong: Doses must be separated by 4 weeks.  Why C wrong: The second dose is still needed but deferred to postnatal.  Why D wrong: It is given postnatally, not during labour."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" note, a child is said to be fully immunized if the child receives all due vaccines as per the national immunization schedule within what age?","options":["Within 6 months","Within the 1st year of age","Within 2 years","Within 5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A child is fully immunized if all due vaccines are received as per NIS within the 1st year of age.  Why A wrong: 6 months is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 2 years is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 5 years is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"According to the source, the two major milestones of the UIP have been the elimination of polio in 2014 and maternal & neonatal tetanus elimination in which year?","options":["2014","2015","2016","2020"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polio was eliminated in 2014 and maternal and neonatal tetanus elimination was achieved in 2015.  Why A wrong: 2014 was polio elimination.  Why C wrong: 2016 corresponds to Yaws-free status (2015-16).  Why D wrong: 2020 was the WHO global target year for Yaws."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"India achieved Yaws-free status in 2015-16, how much before the WHO global target year of 2020?","options":["Yaws-free in 2015-16, well before WHO target of 2020","Yaws-free in 2020, on time with WHO target","Yaws-free in 2014, before polio elimination","Yaws-free in 2018"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: India achieved being Yaws-free in 2015-16, much before the WHO global target year of 2020.  Why B wrong: India achieved it ahead of, not on, the target.  Why C wrong: Yaws-free status was 2015-16, not 2014.  Why D wrong: 2018 is not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the causative organism of smallpox?","options":["Varicella zoster virus","Variola virus","Measles virus","Rubella virus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smallpox is caused by the variola virus.  Why A wrong: Varicella zoster virus causes chickenpox.  Why C wrong: Measles virus causes measles.  Why D wrong: Rubella virus causes rubella."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of smallpox?","options":["2 to 6 days","7 to 14 days","11 to 14 days","14 to 21 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of smallpox is 11 to 14 days.  Why A wrong: 2-6 days is the incubation period of diphtheria.  Why B wrong: 7-14 days is the incubation period of pertussis.  Why D wrong: 14-21 days is for mumps/rubella."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The pattern of rashes in smallpox is described as which of the following?","options":["Centrifugal (peripherally on arms and legs first, then centrally)","Centripetal (centrally first, then peripherally)","Only on the face","Only on the trunk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Smallpox rashes are centrifugal — they first appear peripherally on arms and legs, then later centrally on chest, abdomen and face.  Why B wrong: Centripetal is the pattern of chickenpox rashes.  Why C wrong: The rash is not confined to the face.  Why D wrong: The rash is not confined to the trunk."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"When and where was the last case of smallpox in the world recorded?","options":["26th October 1977, Somalia","17th May 1975, Bihar (India)","8th May 1980, Somalia","23rd April 1977, India"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The last case of smallpox in the world was on 26th October 1977 in Somalia.  Why B wrong: 17th May 1975 in Bihar was the last indigenous case in India.  Why C wrong: 8th May 1980 was when WHO declared global eradication.  Why D wrong: 23rd April 1977 was when WHO declared eradication in India."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"WHO declared the global eradication of smallpox on which date?","options":["26th October 1977","17th May 1975","8th May 1980","23rd April 1977"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: WHO declared global eradication of smallpox on 8th May 1980.  Why A wrong: 26 Oct 1977 was the last world case.  Why B wrong: 17 May 1975 was the last indigenous Indian case.  Why D wrong: 23 April 1977 was WHO declaration of eradication in India."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the causative organism of chickenpox?","options":["Variola virus","Varicella zoster virus (human herpes virus 3)","HHV-6B","Mumps virus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chickenpox is caused by the varicella zoster virus, also known as human herpes virus 3.  Why A wrong: Variola virus causes smallpox.  Why C wrong: HHV-6B causes roseola.  Why D wrong: Mumps virus causes mumps."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the period of infectivity of chickenpox?","options":["1 to 2 days before appearance of rashes and 4 to 5 days thereafter","6 days before onset of symptoms until swelling subsides","5 days before and 5 days after rashes appear","Only during the fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chickenpox is infective 1 to 2 days before appearance of rashes and 4 to 5 days thereafter (AIIMS Delhi-2017).  Why B wrong: That describes the period of infectivity of mumps.  Why C wrong: 5 days before and 5 days after describes measles infective period.  Why D wrong: Infectivity is defined relative to rash appearance, not fever."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of chickenpox?","options":["2 to 6 days","10 to 14 days","14 to 16 days (sometimes 10 to 21 days)","7 to 14 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of chickenpox is 14 to 16 days (sometimes 10 to 21 days).  Why A wrong: 2-6 days is diphtheria.  Why B wrong: 10-14 days is the measles incubation period.  Why D wrong: 7-14 days is the pertussis incubation period."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The pattern of rashes in chickenpox is described as which of the following?","options":["Centrifugal","Centripetal (centrally on chest, face and abdomen first, then peripherally)","Only on the palms and soles","Absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chickenpox rashes are centripetal — they appear centrally (chest, face, abdomen) first, then spread peripherally to arms and legs.  Why A wrong: Centrifugal is the pattern in smallpox.  Why C wrong: The rashes are not confined to palms and soles.  Why D wrong: Rashes are a hallmark of chickenpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which of the following is a complication of chickenpox mentioned in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Haemorrhage","Pneumonia","Reye's syndrome","Optic neuritis","Colour blindness"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of chickenpox include haemorrhage, pneumonia and Reye's syndrome.  Why D wrong: Optic neuritis is a side effect of Ethambutol (anti-TB drug), not a chickenpox complication.  Why E wrong: Colour blindness is an Ethambutol side effect, not a chickenpox complication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Measles is also known by which other names?","options":["Rubeola and 4th day fever","German measles and 3-day measles","Sixth disease","100 day cough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measles is also known as Rubeola and 4th day fever (FAQs).  Why B wrong: German measles and 3-day measles refer to Rubella.  Why C wrong: Sixth disease is Roseola.  Why D wrong: 100 day cough refers to pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the causative organism of measles?","options":["RNA measles virus of Paramyxoviridae family","Variola virus","RNA Rubella virus of Togaviridae family","Bordetella pertussis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measles is caused by the RNA measles virus of the Paramyxoviridae family.  Why B wrong: Variola virus causes smallpox.  Why C wrong: RNA Rubella virus of Togaviridae causes rubella.  Why D wrong: Bordetella pertussis causes pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the infective period of measles?","options":["1 to 2 days before rashes and 4-5 days after","5 days before and 5 days after the appearance of rashes","6 days before onset of symptoms","During paroxysmal stage only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measles infective period is 5 days before and 5 days after the appearance of rashes (Delhi AIIMS-2016).  Why A wrong: That describes chickenpox infectivity.  Why C wrong: 6 days before onset describes mumps.  Why D wrong: Paroxysmal-stage infectivity describes pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"An attack of measles gives immunity for a lifetime. This is an example of which type of immunity?","options":["Acquired active natural immunity","Acquired passive natural immunity","Artificial active immunity","Innate immunity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lifelong immunity following measles infection is an example of acquired active natural immunity.  Why B wrong: Passive natural immunity is transferred (e.g., maternal antibodies), not developed by infection.  Why C wrong: Artificial active immunity is produced by vaccination.  Why D wrong: Innate immunity is non-specific and present from birth."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of measles?","options":["2 to 6 days","10 to 14 days","14 to 21 days","11 to 14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of measles is 10 to 14 days.  Why A wrong: 2-6 days is diphtheria.  Why C wrong: 14-21 days is mumps/rubella.  Why D wrong: 11-14 days is smallpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The classical \"3 C's\" symptoms of measles refer to which of the following?","options":["Coryza, cough, conjunctivitis","Cough, cyanosis, chills","Cramps, cough, coryza","Cyanosis, coryza, conjunctivitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The classical 3 C's of measles are coryza, cough and conjunctivitis (along with Koplik's spot).  Why B wrong: Cyanosis and chills are not the 3 C's.  Why C wrong: Cramps is not part of the 3 C's.  Why D wrong: Cyanosis is not one of the 3 C's."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Koplik's spot, a cardinal sign of measles, appears as small bluish-white spots on a red base on which surface during the prodromal period?","options":["On the inner surface of the cheeks opposite the molar teeth","On the soft palate","On the tongue","On the gums opposite the incisors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Koplik's spots occur on the inner surface of the cheeks opposite the molar teeth during the prodromal period.  Why B wrong: They are not on the soft palate.  Why C wrong: They are not on the tongue.  Why D wrong: They are opposite the molar teeth, not the incisors."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In measles, where do the rashes first appear?","options":["On the behind of ear, face and neck","On the palms and soles","On the chest and abdomen","On the arms and legs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In measles, rashes first appear on the behind of the ear, face and neck.  Why B wrong: Palms and soles are not the first site.  Why C wrong: Chest/abdomen is not the first site in measles.  Why D wrong: Arms and legs are not the first site."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the most common complication of measles?","options":["Measles-associated diarrhoea","SSPE","Reye's syndrome","Orchitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common complications are measles-associated diarrhoea, pneumonia and other respiratory complications.  Why B wrong: SSPE is a rare and serious complication, not the most common.  Why C wrong: Reye's syndrome is associated with chickenpox.  Why D wrong: Orchitis is a complication of mumps in adolescents."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the most serious or life-threatening complication of measles?","options":["Diarrhoea","Pneumonia","Conjunctivitis","Coryza"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most serious or life-threatening complication of measles is pneumonia.  Why A wrong: Diarrhoea is the most common, not the most serious.  Why C wrong: Conjunctivitis is one of the 3 C's, not a serious complication.  Why D wrong: Coryza is a symptom, not a serious complication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"SSPE, a rare and serious complication of measles, stands for which of the following and is diagnosed by which test?","options":["Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis; diagnosed by CSF testing","Subacute Sclerosing Pneumonia; diagnosed by chest X-ray","Sudden Sclerosing Panencephalitis; diagnosed by MRI","Subacute Systemic Panencephalitis; diagnosed by blood culture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SSPE is Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis, an inflammation of the brain, diagnosed by CSF testing because specific antibodies are present in CSF.  Why B wrong: It is panencephalitis (brain inflammation), not pneumonia.  Why C wrong: It is \"subacute,\" not \"sudden,\" and diagnosed by CSF, not MRI.  Why D wrong: It is diagnosed by CSF testing, not blood culture."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which vitamin deficiency occurs in measles, making supplementation necessary?","options":["Vitamin A (Retinol)","Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine)","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin A or Retinol deficiency occurs in measles, so supplementary Vitamin A is necessary.  Why B wrong: Vitamin B6 deficiency is associated with isoniazid (anti-TB drug).  Why C wrong: Vitamin C is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Vitamin D is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the recommended prevention for measles?","options":["MR vaccine","Varicella zoster vaccine","DPT vaccine","Influenza vaccine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measles is prevented by the MR vaccine.  Why B wrong: Varicella zoster vaccine prevents chickenpox.  Why C wrong: DPT prevents diphtheria/pertussis/tetanus.  Why D wrong: Influenza vaccine prevents swine flu/influenza."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Rubella is also known by which other names?","options":["German measles or 3-day measles","Rubeola or 4th day fever","Sixth disease","100 day cough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella is also known as German measles or 3-day measles.  Why B wrong: Rubeola/4th day fever refers to measles.  Why C wrong: Sixth disease is roseola.  Why D wrong: 100 day cough is pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the causative organism of rubella?","options":["RNA measles virus of Paramyxoviridae","RNA Rubella virus of Togaviridae/Matonaviridae family","Varicella zoster virus","Variola virus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella is caused by the RNA Rubella virus of the Togaviridae/Matonaviridae family.  Why A wrong: RNA measles virus of Paramyxoviridae causes measles.  Why C wrong: Varicella zoster causes chickenpox.  Why D wrong: Variola causes smallpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of rubella?","options":["2-6 days","7-14 days","14-21 days","11-14 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of rubella is 14-21 days.  Why A wrong: 2-6 days is diphtheria.  Why B wrong: 7-14 days is pertussis.  Why D wrong: 11-14 days is smallpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Vertical transmission (mother to fetus) of rubella causes which congenital disorder in newborn babies?","options":["Congenital rubella syndrome","Reye's syndrome","Down syndrome","SSPE"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vertical (mother-to-fetus) transmission of rubella causes congenital rubella syndrome, a congenital disorder in newborns.  Why B wrong: Reye's syndrome is associated with chickenpox.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is a chromosomal disorder, not caused by rubella.  Why D wrong: SSPE is a complication of measles."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Regarding rubella vaccine (a live vaccine) in pregnancy, what is the correct guidance?","options":["Never give rubella vaccine during pregnancy; it may be given after labour","Give during the first trimester only","Give during the third trimester","Give at any time during pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella is a live vaccine and should never be given during pregnancy; it may be given after labour.  Why B wrong: It should not be given in any trimester.  Why C wrong: It should not be given in the third trimester.  Why D wrong: A live vaccine is contraindicated during pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Rubella causes congenital anomalies/defects in the newborn especially if it occurs in the pregnant mother during which period?","options":["First 3 months (first trimester)","Second trimester","Third trimester","Only during labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella causes congenital anomalies/defects if it occurs during pregnancy, especially in the first 3 months (first trimester).  Why B wrong: The first trimester is the specified high-risk period.  Why C wrong: The first trimester is the high-risk period, not the third.  Why D wrong: The risk relates to infection during pregnancy, specifically the first trimester."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Roseola is also known as which of the following?","options":["Sixth disease","Fourth day fever","Three-day measles","German measles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Roseola is also known as the Sixth disease.  Why B wrong: 4th day fever refers to measles.  Why C wrong: 3-day measles refers to rubella.  Why D wrong: German measles refers to rubella."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Roseola is caused by which virus, and what is the most common cause?","options":["Human Herpes Virus HHV-6A & 6B and HHV-7; most common cause is HHV-6B","Varicella zoster virus; most common cause HHV-3","Variola virus; most common cause HHV-1","Rubella virus; most common cause HHV-7"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Roseola is caused by Human Herpes Virus HHV-6A & 6B and HHV-7 (sometimes called Roseolovirus); the most common cause is HHV-6B.  Why B wrong: Varicella zoster/HHV-3 causes chickenpox.  Why C wrong: Variola virus causes smallpox.  Why D wrong: Rubella virus causes rubella, not roseola."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Roseola is mainly spread by which route?","options":["Saliva","Faeco-oral","Blood transfusion","Vector (mosquito)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Roseola is mainly spread by saliva.  Why B wrong: Faeco-oral is not the stated route.  Why C wrong: Blood transfusion is not the stated route.  Why D wrong: Roseola is not vector-borne."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the causative organism of mumps?","options":["Mumps virus of the Paramyxoviridae family","Varicella zoster virus","Bordetella pertussis","Corynebacterium diphtheriae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mumps is caused by the mumps virus of the Paramyxoviridae family.  Why B wrong: Varicella zoster causes chickenpox.  Why C wrong: Bordetella pertussis causes pertussis.  Why D wrong: Corynebacterium diphtheriae causes diphtheria."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of mumps?","options":["2-6 days","7-14 days","14-21 days","11-14 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of mumps is 14-21 days.  Why A wrong: 2-6 days is diphtheria.  Why B wrong: 7-14 days is pertussis.  Why D wrong: 11-14 days is smallpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the period of infectivity of mumps?","options":["Six days before the onset of symptoms and until the swelling of parotid gland has subsided","1-2 days before rashes and 4-5 days after","5 days before and 5 days after rashes","Only in the paroxysmal stage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mumps is infective six days before the onset of symptoms and until the swelling of the parotid gland has subsided.  Why B wrong: That describes chickenpox.  Why C wrong: That describes measles.  Why D wrong: Paroxysmal-stage infectivity describes pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the first or early cardinal clinical feature of mumps?","options":["Parotitis (swelling of parotid glands)","Orchitis","Aseptic meningitis","Bull's neck"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first/early clinical feature of mumps is parotitis (swelling of parotid glands), which is the cardinal sign.  Why B wrong: Orchitis is a complication in adolescents.  Why C wrong: Aseptic meningitis is a complication in infants.  Why D wrong: Bull's neck is a feature of diphtheria."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the most serious complication of mumps in infants?","options":["Aseptic meningitis","Orchitis","Pneumonia","Reye's syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most serious complication of mumps in infants is aseptic meningitis.  Why B wrong: Orchitis is the serious complication in adolescents, not infants.  Why C wrong: Pneumonia is not listed as the mumps complication in infants.  Why D wrong: Reye's syndrome is associated with chickenpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the most serious complication of mumps in adolescents?","options":["Orchitis (swelling of testes)","Aseptic meningitis","Pneumonia","Parotitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adolescents, the most serious complication of mumps is orchitis (swelling of the testes).  Why B wrong: Aseptic meningitis is the serious complication in infants.  Why C wrong: Pneumonia is not listed for mumps.  Why D wrong: Parotitis is the cardinal sign, not a complication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the recommended prevention for mumps?","options":["MMR vaccine","DPT vaccine","Influenza vaccine","BCG vaccine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mumps is prevented by the MMR vaccine.  Why B wrong: DPT prevents diphtheria/pertussis/tetanus.  Why C wrong: Influenza vaccine prevents swine flu.  Why D wrong: BCG prevents tuberculosis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Swine Flu Influenza is also known as which virus?","options":["H1N1 virus","H5N1 virus","HHV-6B","Bordetella pertussis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Swine Flu Influenza is also known as the H1N1 virus.  Why B wrong: H5N1 is avian influenza (bird flu).  Why C wrong: HHV-6B causes roseola.  Why D wrong: Bordetella pertussis causes pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"H5N1, also known as avian influenza, causes which condition?","options":["Bird flu","Swine flu","Diphtheria","Roseola"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: H5N1, also known as avian influenza, causes bird flu.  Why B wrong: Swine flu is caused by H1N1.  Why C wrong: Diphtheria is caused by Corynebacterium diphtheriae.  Why D wrong: Roseola is caused by HHV-6B."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of swine flu (H1N1)?","options":["1 to 4 days","7 to 14 days","14 to 21 days","2 to 6 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of swine flu is 1 to 4 days.  Why B wrong: 7-14 days is pertussis.  Why C wrong: 14-21 days is mumps/rubella.  Why D wrong: 2-6 days is diphtheria."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the recommended prevention for swine flu influenza?","options":["Influenza vaccine","MMR vaccine","DPT vaccine","BCG vaccine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Swine flu is prevented by the influenza vaccine.  Why B wrong: MMR prevents measles/mumps/rubella.  Why C wrong: DPT prevents diphtheria/pertussis/tetanus.  Why D wrong: BCG prevents tuberculosis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What infection control precautions should be applied for swine flu influenza?","options":["Standard and droplet precautions","Airborne precautions only","Contact precautions only","No precautions needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Standard and droplet precautions should be applied for swine flu.  Why B wrong: Airborne alone is not specified.  Why C wrong: Contact alone is not specified.  Why D wrong: Precautions are required as it is highly infectious."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the causative organism of diphtheria?","options":["Gram positive Corynebacterium diphtheriae (Klebs-Löffler bacillus)","Gram negative Bordetella pertussis","Acid fast Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Varicella zoster virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria is caused by the gram positive diphtheriae bacilli or Corynebacterium diphtheriae, also known as Klebs-Löffler bacillus.  Why B wrong: Gram negative Bordetella pertussis causes pertussis.  Why C wrong: Mycobacterium tuberculosis causes TB.  Why D wrong: Varicella zoster virus causes chickenpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which age group is most commonly affected by diphtheria?","options":["1-5 years","6-10 years","Adults","Newborns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common age group affected by diphtheria is 1-5 years.  Why B wrong: 6-10 years is not the most commonly affected group.  Why C wrong: Adults are not the most common group.  Why D wrong: Newborns are not the most common group."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of diphtheria?","options":["2 to 6 days","7 to 14 days","14 to 21 days","11 to 14 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of diphtheria is 2 to 6 days (AMU-2016).  Why B wrong: 7-14 days is pertussis.  Why C wrong: 14-21 days is mumps/rubella.  Why D wrong: 11-14 days is smallpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In diphtheria, formation of a greyish-yellow or white membrane over the tonsil and throat is known as which of the following?","options":["Pseudomembrane","Koplik's spot","Bull's neck","Whoop"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The greyish-yellow/white membrane over the tonsil and throat in diphtheria is called the pseudomembrane.  Why B wrong: Koplik's spot is a feature of measles.  Why C wrong: Bull's neck refers to the swollen neck appearance, not the membrane.  Why D wrong: Whoop is the characteristic sound in pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The swollen neck or \"bull's neck\" appearance in diphtheria is due to which of the following?","options":["Enlarged lymph nodes","Parotid gland swelling","Thyroid enlargement","Salivary gland stones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"bull's neck\" appearance in diphtheria is due to enlarged lymph nodes causing the neck of the baby to swell.  Why B wrong: Parotid gland swelling is the feature of mumps (parotitis).  Why C wrong: Thyroid enlargement is not the cause.  Why D wrong: Salivary gland stones are not the cause."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the treatment for diphtheria?","options":["Diphtheria antitoxin (DTS) and antibiotics (DOC Penicillin and erythromycin)","Erythromycin only","MMR vaccine","Isoniazid and rifampicin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria treatment includes diphtheria antitoxin or DTS and antibiotics; the drug of choice (DOC) is Penicillin and erythromycin.  Why B wrong: Antitoxin is also required, not erythromycin alone.  Why C wrong: MMR is a vaccine for measles/mumps/rubella.  Why D wrong: Isoniazid and rifampicin are anti-TB drugs."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the preventive vaccine for diphtheria, and at what schedule is it given in the newborn?","options":["DPT vaccine 0.5 ml IM at 6, 10 and 14 weeks of age","MMR vaccine at 9 months","BCG at birth","Influenza vaccine yearly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria is prevented by DPT vaccine, 0.5 ml through IM at 6, 10 and 14 weeks of age in the newborn.  Why B wrong: MMR prevents measles/mumps/rubella.  Why C wrong: BCG prevents tuberculosis.  Why D wrong: Influenza vaccine prevents swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The Schick test is an intradermal test used to detect immunity to which disease?","options":["Diphtheria","Tuberculosis","Tetanus","Pertussis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Schick test is an intradermal test for diphtheria; it identifies those immune to diphtheria and those who are not.  Why B wrong: Tuberculin/Mantoux test is used for tuberculosis.  Why C wrong: The Schick test is not for tetanus.  Why D wrong: The Schick test is not for pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the site, route and volume of substance used in the Schick test?","options":["Forearm; intradermal (ID); 0.1 ml","Upper arm; intramuscular; 0.5 ml","Mid-thigh; subcutaneous; 0.1 ml","Forearm; oral; 2 drops"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Schick test is done on the forearm, via the intradermal (ID) route, with a volume of 0.1 ml.  Why B wrong: It is intradermal, not intramuscular, and 0.1 ml, not 0.5 ml.  Why C wrong: It is intradermal on the forearm, not subcutaneous mid-thigh.  Why D wrong: It is an injection, not an oral test."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"After how long can the results of the Schick test be interpreted?","options":["48 to 72 hours","24 hours","1 week","Immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The results of the Schick test can be interpreted after 48 to 72 hours.  Why B wrong: 24 hours is too early.  Why C wrong: 1 week is too long.  Why D wrong: Results are not immediate."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Pertussis (Whooping Cough) is a highly infectious bacterial disease of the respiratory tract, also known by which name?","options":["100 day cough","4th day fever","Three-day measles","Sixth disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pertussis is also known as 100 day cough.  Why B wrong: 4th day fever refers to measles.  Why C wrong: Three-day measles refers to rubella.  Why D wrong: Sixth disease refers to roseola."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the causative organism of pertussis?","options":["Gram negative Bordetella pertussis bacteria","Gram positive Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Acid fast Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Mumps virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pertussis is caused by a gram negative Bordetella pertussis bacterium.  Why B wrong: Corynebacterium diphtheriae (gram positive) causes diphtheria.  Why C wrong: Mycobacterium tuberculosis causes TB.  Why D wrong: Mumps virus causes mumps."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of pertussis?","options":["7 to 14 days","2 to 6 days","14 to 21 days","1 to 4 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of pertussis is 7 to 14 days.  Why B wrong: 2-6 days is diphtheria.  Why C wrong: 14-21 days is mumps/rubella.  Why D wrong: 1-4 days is swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In pertussis, which stage is the most dangerous, characterized by bursts of rapid consecutive cough followed by a \"whoop\", vomiting, cyanosis and apnoea?","options":["Paroxysmal stage (2 to 4 weeks)","Catarrhal stage (first 10 days)","Convalescent stage (1 to 2 weeks)","Prodromal stage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The paroxysmal stage (lasting 2 to 4 weeks) is the most dangerous, with bursts of rapid consecutive cough followed by a whoop, vomiting, cyanosis and apnoea.  Why B wrong: The catarrhal stage (first ~10 days) has coryza, anorexia, malaise and hacking cough.  Why C wrong: The convalescent stage (1-2 weeks) is the recovery stage.  Why D wrong: A distinct \"prodromal stage\" is not named for pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In pertussis, the catarrhal stage lasts about how long and is characterized by which features?","options":["First 10 days; coryza, anorexia, malaise and hacking cough","2 to 4 weeks; whoop and cyanosis","1 to 2 weeks; recovery","6 days; parotid swelling"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The catarrhal stage lasts about the first 10 days with coryza, anorexia, malaise and hacking cough.  Why B wrong: 2-4 weeks with whoop describes the paroxysmal stage.  Why C wrong: 1-2 weeks recovery describes the convalescent stage.  Why D wrong: Parotid swelling is a mumps feature."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In pertussis, the convalescent stage lasts for how long?","options":["1 to 2 weeks","2 to 4 weeks","First 10 days","6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The convalescent stage of pertussis lasts for 1 to 2 weeks.  Why B wrong: 2-4 weeks is the paroxysmal stage.  Why C wrong: First 10 days is the catarrhal stage.  Why D wrong: 6 weeks is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the drug of choice for prophylaxis of pertussis?","options":["Erythromycin","Penicillin","Isoniazid","Streptomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for pertussis prophylaxis is erythromycin.  Why B wrong: Penicillin is a DOC for diphtheria, not pertussis prophylaxis.  Why C wrong: Isoniazid is used for TB prophylaxis.  Why D wrong: Streptomycin is an anti-TB drug."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the preventive vaccine for pertussis?","options":["DPT vaccine","MMR vaccine","BCG vaccine","Influenza vaccine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pertussis is prevented by the DPT vaccine.  Why B wrong: MMR prevents measles/mumps/rubella.  Why C wrong: BCG prevents tuberculosis.  Why D wrong: Influenza vaccine prevents swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the causative organism of tuberculosis (TB)?","options":["Acid fast tubercle bacilli / Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Gram positive Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Gram negative Bordetella pertussis","Variola virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB is caused by acid fast tubercle bacilli or Mycobacterium tuberculosis.  Why B wrong: Corynebacterium diphtheriae causes diphtheria.  Why C wrong: Bordetella pertussis causes pertussis.  Why D wrong: Variola virus causes smallpox."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Tuberculosis primarily affects which part of the lungs, and why?","options":["The upper lobe, due to high concentration of oxygen","The lower lobe, due to gravity","The pleura, due to fluid accumulation","The bronchi, due to airflow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB primarily affects the lungs, specifically the upper lobe, due to the high concentration of oxygen (the bacteria are aerobic).  Why B wrong: It affects the upper lobe, not the lower lobe.  Why C wrong: The pleura is not the primary site described.  Why D wrong: The bronchi are not the described primary site."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the most common form of tuberculosis?","options":["Pulmonary TB","Intestinal TB","Bone and joint TB","Meningeal TB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common form of tuberculosis is pulmonary TB.  Why B wrong: Intestinal TB is a less common form.  Why C wrong: Bone/joint TB is a less common form.  Why D wrong: Meningeal TB is a less common form."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Tuberculosis that affects animals like cattle is known as which of the following?","options":["Bovine tuberculosis","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Miliary tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB affecting animals such as cattle is known as bovine tuberculosis.  Why B wrong: Pulmonary TB refers to lung TB in humans.  Why C wrong: Miliary TB is disseminated TB, not the animal form.  Why D wrong: Latent TB is a dormant infection."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of tuberculosis?","options":["Droplet, airborne transmission","Faeco-oral transmission","Vector-borne transmission","Blood transfusion only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB is transmitted by droplet and airborne transmission.  Why B wrong: Faeco-oral is not the mode for TB.  Why C wrong: TB is not vector-borne.  Why D wrong: Blood transfusion is not the main mode."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the incubation period of tuberculosis?","options":["Weeks to months","2 to 6 days","11 to 14 days","1 to 4 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of TB is weeks to months.  Why B wrong: 2-6 days is diphtheria.  Why C wrong: 11-14 days is smallpox.  Why D wrong: 1-4 days is swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the cardinal sign/clinical feature of tuberculosis?","options":["Low grade fever, chronic cough and weight loss","High grade fever with rashes","Parotid gland swelling","Bull's neck appearance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal signs of TB are low grade fever, chronic cough and weight loss (FAQs); others include chest pain, haemoptysis and malaise.  Why B wrong: High grade fever with rashes is not the TB cardinal sign.  Why C wrong: Parotid swelling is a mumps feature.  Why D wrong: Bull's neck is a diphtheria feature."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Early case finding of TB depends primarily on which investigation?","options":["Sputum microscopy","Chest X-ray","Blood culture","Mantoux test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Early case finding of TB depends on sputum microscopy (FAQs).  Why B wrong: Chest X-ray is a screening test but not the primary basis for early case finding.  Why C wrong: Blood culture is not the method described.  Why D wrong: Mantoux is a screening test, not the primary early case-finding method."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which sputum sample is more reliable for diagnostic purposes in tuberculosis?","options":["Early morning sputum","Evening sputum","Random daytime sputum","Post-meal sputum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Early morning sputum is more reliable for diagnostic purposes (FAQs).  Why B wrong: Evening sputum is not stated as more reliable.  Why C wrong: Random daytime sputum is less reliable.  Why D wrong: Post-meal sputum is not the reliable sample."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which test is the confirmative test for tuberculosis, and by which technique is it done?","options":["Sputum culture, by Ziehl-Neelsen stain technique","Mantoux test","Chest X-ray","Schick test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sputum culture is the confirmative test for TB, done by the Ziehl-Neelsen stain technique.  Why B wrong: Mantoux is a screening test, not confirmative.  Why C wrong: Chest X-ray is a screening test.  Why D wrong: Schick test is for diphtheria."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"The Mantoux test is also known by which other names? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tuberculin sensitivity skin test","Pirquet's test","PPD test","Schick test","Widal test"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The Mantoux test is also known as the Tuberculin sensitivity skin test, Pirquet's test, and PPD test.  Why D wrong: The Schick test is for diphtheria.  Why E wrong: The Widal test is for typhoid, not mentioned here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What substance is used for the Mantoux test?","options":["PPD (Protein Purified Derivative)","Diphtheria toxin","Variola antigen","BCG vaccine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The substance used for the Mantoux test is PPD or Protein Purified Derivative.  Why B wrong: Diphtheria toxin is used in the Schick test.  Why C wrong: Variola antigen is not used.  Why D wrong: BCG is a vaccine, not the test substance."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the route and volume of substance used in the Mantoux test?","options":["ID (Intra Dermal); 0.1 ml or 1 TU (tuberculin unit)","IM; 0.5 ml","Subcutaneous; 1 ml","Oral; 2 drops"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mantoux test route is ID (intra-dermal) with a volume of 0.1 ml or 1 TU (tuberculin unit).  Why B wrong: It is intradermal, not intramuscular, and not 0.5 ml.  Why C wrong: It is 0.1 ml intradermal, not 1 ml subcutaneous.  Why D wrong: It is an injection, not an oral test."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"After how long is the Mantoux test result interpreted?","options":["48 to 72 hours (exact 72 hours/3 days)","24 hours","1 week","Immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mantoux test result is interpreted after 48 to 72 hours (exact 72 hours/3 days).  Why B wrong: 24 hours is too early.  Why C wrong: 1 week is too late.  Why D wrong: Results are not immediate."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In Mantoux test interpretation, an induration of 5 mm or more is considered positive in which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["An HIV-positive person","Persons with recent contacts with a TB patient","Persons with nodular or fibrotic changes on chest X-ray consistent with old healed TB","Patients with organ transplants and other immunosuppressed patients","Persons with no known risk factors for TB"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: 5 mm or more is positive in HIV-positive persons, recent TB contacts, persons with fibrotic changes consistent with old healed TB, and organ transplant/immunosuppressed patients.  Why E wrong: For persons with no known risk factors, 15 mm or more is the positive cut-off, not 5 mm."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In Mantoux test interpretation, which of the following groups is considered positive at 10 mm or more? (Select all that apply)","options":["Recent arrivals (less than five years) from high-prevalence countries","Injection drug users","Residents and employees of high-risk congregate settings (prisons, nursing homes, hospitals, homeless shelters)","Mycobacteriology lab personnel","Persons with no known risk factors for TB"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: 10 mm or more is positive in recent arrivals from high-prevalence countries, injection drug users, residents/employees of high-risk congregate settings, and mycobacteriology lab personnel (also diabetics, corticosteroid users, children <4 years, etc.).  Why E wrong: Persons with no known risk factors fall under the 15 mm cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"In Mantoux test interpretation, an induration of 15 mm or more is considered positive in which group?","options":["Persons with no known risk factors for TB","HIV-positive persons","Injection drug users","Recent TB contacts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 15 mm or more is positive in persons with no known risk factors for TB (AIIMS Patna-2020).  Why B wrong: HIV-positive persons use the 5 mm cut-off.  Why C wrong: Injection drug users use the 10 mm cut-off.  Why D wrong: Recent TB contacts use the 5 mm cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What does DOTS stand for in the context of tuberculosis control?","options":["Directly Observed Treatment Short-course","Directly Observed Tuberculosis Screening","Daily Oral Treatment Schedule","Directed Observation of TB Sputum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOTS stands for Directly Observed Treatment Short-course, the recommended strategy for global TB control.  Why B wrong: It is not \"Tuberculosis Screening.\"  Why C wrong: It is not \"Daily Oral Treatment Schedule.\"  Why D wrong: It is not \"Directed Observation of TB Sputum.\""},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"WHO declared tuberculosis as a global emergency in which year?","options":["1993","1997","2006","1980"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO declared tuberculosis as a global emergency in 1993.  Why B wrong: 1997 was when DOTS was launched in India.  Why C wrong: 2006 was when DOTS covered the whole country.  Why D wrong: 1980 was when smallpox was globally eradicated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"DOTS was launched in India in March 1997 under which programme?","options":["Revised National Tuberculosis Programme (RNTCP)","National Rural Health Mission (NRHM)","Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)","National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOTS was launched in India in March 1997 under the Revised National Tuberculosis Programme (RNTCP).  Why B wrong: NRHM is a health mission, not the DOTS launch programme.  Why C wrong: UIP is the immunization programme.  Why D wrong: NLEP targets leprosy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"By which year did DOTS cover the whole country of India?","options":["March 2006","March 1997","1993","2015"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOTS covered the whole country by March 2006.  Why B wrong: March 1997 was the launch.  Why C wrong: 1993 was WHO's global emergency declaration.  Why D wrong: 2015 is not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which first-line anti-tubercular drug is the best drug for prophylaxis, is the safest drug during pregnancy, and can cross the blood-brain barrier?","options":["Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Ethambutol","Streptomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isoniazid is the best drug for prophylaxis of TB, is the safest drug during pregnancy, and can cross the BBB. Its side effects include hepatitis, peripheral neuritis, pyridoxine (B6) deficiency and pellagra-like syndrome.  Why B wrong: Rifampicin causes red-orange discolouration of secretions and is not described as the prophylaxis/pregnancy-safe drug.  Why C wrong: Ethambutol is bacteriostatic and causes optic neuritis/colour blindness.  Why D wrong: Streptomycin is contraindicated during pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which first-line anti-tubercular drug causes red-orange discolouration of urine, saliva and sweats?","options":["Rifampicin","Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Streptomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rifampicin causes red-orange discolouration of urine, saliva and sweats, skin rash, gastritis and hepatitis.  Why B wrong: Isoniazid causes peripheral neuritis and B6 deficiency.  Why C wrong: Ethambutol causes optic neuritis/colour blindness.  Why D wrong: Streptomycin causes hearing loss."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which first-line anti-tubercular drug most commonly causes joint pain and gout due to hyperuricaemia?","options":["Pyrazinamide","Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Rifampicin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pyrazinamide causes joint pain and gout due to hyperuricaemia (its most common side effect) and hepatitis.  Why B wrong: Isoniazid causes peripheral neuritis.  Why C wrong: Ethambutol causes optic neuritis and colour blindness.  Why D wrong: Rifampicin causes red-orange discolouration."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which first-line anti-tubercular drug is bacteriostatic and whose most common side effect is colour blindness (optic neuritis)?","options":["Ethambutol","Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Streptomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethambutol is bacteriostatic; it causes ocular toxicity, retro-bulbar neuritis, optic neuritis, blurring and colour vision problems (red & green), with the most common side effect being colour blindness.  Why B wrong: Isoniazid is bactericidal and causes peripheral neuritis.  Why C wrong: Rifampicin is bactericidal and causes red-orange discolouration.  Why D wrong: Streptomycin is bactericidal and causes hearing loss."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Which first-line anti-tubercular drug is given by IM route, has hearing loss (8th cranial nerve damage) as its most common side effect, and is contraindicated during pregnancy?","options":["Streptomycin","Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Streptomycin is given by IM route, its most common side effect is hearing loss due to damage of the 8th cranial nerve, it is contraindicated during pregnancy and is nephrotoxic.  Why B wrong: Isoniazid is the safest drug during pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Ethambutol causes colour blindness, not hearing loss, and is given orally.  Why D wrong: Pyrazinamide causes joint pain/gout."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Among the first-line anti-tubercular drugs, which one is bacteriostatic while the others (Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, Streptomycin) are bactericidal?","options":["Ethambutol","Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Streptomycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethambutol is bacteriostatic; Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide and Streptomycin are all listed as bactericidal.  Why B wrong: Isoniazid is bactericidal.  Why C wrong: Rifampicin is bactericidal.  Why D wrong: Streptomycin is bactericidal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"Isoniazid can cause deficiency of which vitamin, leading to a pellagra-like syndrome and peripheral neuritis?","options":["Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6)","Vitamin A (Retinol)","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isoniazid causes pyridoxine (Vitamin B6) deficiency, leading to pellagra-like syndrome and peripheral neuritis.  Why B wrong: Vitamin A deficiency is associated with measles.  Why C wrong: Vitamin C is not associated with isoniazid.  Why D wrong: Vitamin D is not associated with isoniazid."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the source of infection for pertussis (whooping cough)?","options":["Nose and throat secretions of a case","Contaminated water","Mosquito bite","Cattle milk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source of infection for pertussis is the nose and throat secretions of a case; it is infective in the paroxysmal stage.  Why B wrong: Contaminated water is not the source.  Why C wrong: Pertussis is not vector-borne.  Why D wrong: Cattle milk is a source for bovine TB, not pertussis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of diphtheria?","options":["Droplet transmission or fomite-borne by contaminated articles (toys, pencils, cloths)","Faeco-oral transmission","Vector-borne transmission","Blood transfusion only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria spreads by droplet transmission or fomite-borne transmission via contaminated articles such as toys, pencils and cloths.  Why B wrong: Faeco-oral is not the mode.  Why C wrong: It is not vector-borne.  Why D wrong: Blood transfusion is not the described mode."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Milestones of vaccines under UIP, upper age limits for UIP vaccination…","question":"What is the source of infection for tuberculosis?","options":["Infected human and bovine (through milk or milk products)","Contaminated water only","Mosquito vector","Soil and dust only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source of infection for TB is infected humans and bovine (through milk or milk products).  Why B wrong: Contaminated water alone is not the source.  Why C wrong: TB is not vector-borne.  Why D wrong: Soil and dust alone are not the described source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Under DOTS chemotherapy in India (2015-16), which treatment category is used for new sputum smear-positive cases and is denoted by the \"Red box\"?","options":["Category-I","Category-II","Category-IV","Category-V"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category-I (Red box) is for new cases including new sputum smear-positive cases and seriously ill extrapulmonary cases.  Why B wrong: Category-II (Blue box) is for previously treated/old cases.  Why C wrong: Category-IV is for Multi Drug Resistant TB (MDR-TB).  Why D wrong: Category-V is for Extensively Drug Resistant TB (XDR-TB)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the treatment duration for Category-I (new cases) under the DOTS regimen in India?","options":["6 months","8 months","12 months","18-24 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category-I has an intensive phase of 2(HRZE₃) and continuation phase of 4(HR₃), totaling 6 months.  Why B wrong: 8 months is the duration for Category-II (previously treated).  Why C wrong: 12 months is not a listed standard category duration.  Why D wrong: 18-24 months is the duration for Category-IV (MDR-TB)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The intensive phase regimen for Category-I new TB cases is:","options":["2 (HRZE₃)","4 (HR₃)","5 (HRE₃)","4 (KLCZEEt)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category-I intensive phase is 2 months of HRZE₃ (Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol).  Why B wrong: 4(HR₃) is the continuation phase of Category-I.  Why C wrong: 5(HRE₃) is the continuation phase of Category-II.  Why D wrong: 4(KLCZEEt) is the intensive phase of Category-IV (MDR-TB)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Category-II (Blue box) DOTS regimen has a total treatment duration of:","options":["6 months","8 months","18-24 months","24-30 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Category-II (previously treated: relapse, failure, treatment after default) lasts 8 months.  Why A wrong: 6 months is Category-I.  Why C wrong: 18-24 months is Category-IV.  Why D wrong: 24-30 months is Category-V."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which DOTS category treats Multi Drug Resistant TB (MDR-TB) with a treatment duration of 18-24 months?","options":["Category-I","Category-II","Category-IV","Category-V"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Category-IV treats MDR-TB with intensive phase 4(KLCZEEt), continuation 12-18(LCEE), for 18-24 months.  Why A/B wrong: Categories I and II treat drug-sensitive new and previously-treated cases (6 and 8 months).  Why D wrong: Category-V treats XDR-TB for 24-30 months."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Category-V under DOTS treats Extensively Drug Resistant Tuberculosis (XDR-TB) for a duration of:","options":["6 months","8 months","18-24 months","24-30 months"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Category-V (XDR-TB) has intensive phase 6-12(HhCmCzLAMP), continuation 18(HhCzLAMP), lasting 24-30 months.  Why A/B wrong: Those are drug-sensitive regimens.  Why C wrong: 18-24 months is Category-IV (MDR-TB)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the DOTS drug abbreviations, which letter stands for Rifampicin?","options":["R","E","H","Z"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: R = Rifampicin.  Why B wrong: E = Ethambutol.  Why C wrong: H = Isoniazid.  Why D wrong: Z = Pyrazinamide."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the DOTS drug code, the letter \"H\" refers to which drug?","options":["Ethambutol","Isoniazid","Streptomycin","Kanamycin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: H = Isoniazid (also Hh = High dose isoniazid).  Why A wrong: Ethambutol = E.  Why C wrong: Streptomycin = S.  Why D wrong: Kanamycin = K."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"As per the note on DOTS categories, Category-III has been:","options":["Discontinued entirely","Merged into Category-I","Renamed as DOTS Plus","Merged into Category-II"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Category-III has been merged in Category-I.\"  Why A wrong: It was merged, not discontinued.  Why C wrong: DOTS Plus is another name for Cat-IV.  Why D wrong: It merged into Category-I, not II."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The \"DOTS Plus program\" is also the name for which category, and was started by WHO in which year?","options":["Cat-IV; started by WHO in 2000","Cat-I; started by WHO in 1995","Cat-V; started by WHO in 2007","Cat-II; started by WHO in 2010"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cat-IV is also known as DOTS Plus program, started by WHO in 2000 for diagnosis, treatment and management of MDR-TB.  Why B/C/D wrong: These years and categories do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which was the first state in India to initiate the DOTS Plus program, and in which year?","options":["Kerala in 2005","Maharashtra in 2000","Gujarat in 2007","Rajasthan in 2010"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Gujarat was the first state in the country to initiate the DOTS Plus program in 2007.  Why A/B/D wrong: These states/years are not mentioned in the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which of the following are FIRST-LINE anti-tubercular drugs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Isoniazid (INH)","Rifampicin (R)","Ethambutol (E)","Levofloxacin","Pyrazinamide (Z)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: First-line drugs are Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide, and Streptomycin.  Why D wrong: Levofloxacin is a second-line drug."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which of the following is a SECOND-LINE anti-tubercular drug?","options":["Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Moxifloxacin","Pyrazinamide"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Second-line drugs include Levofloxacin, Moxifloxacin, Ciprofloxacin, Kanamycin, Amikacin, and Capreomycin.  Why A/B/D wrong: Isoniazid, Rifampicin, and Pyrazinamide are first-line drugs."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The newer anti-tubercular drugs Bedaquiline and Delamanid are given in which doses respectively?","options":["Bedaquiline 50 mg and Delamanid 10 mg","Bedaquiline 10 mg and Delamanid 50 mg","Bedaquiline 100 mg and Delamanid 100 mg","Bedaquiline 25 mg and Delamanid 75 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Bedaquiline 10 mg and Delamanid 50 mg as newer antitubercular drugs. [Note: source prints Bedaquiline 10 mg; the standard Bedaquiline tablet strength per WHO/NTEP is 100 mg (Delamanid 50 mg is correct).]  Why A/C/D wrong: These doses do not match the source values."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Multidrug Resistant TB (MDR-TB) is defined as resistance against which drugs?","options":["Isoniazid and Rifampicin","Ethambutol and Pyrazinamide","All second-line drugs","Streptomycin only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MDR-TB is resistance against Isoniazid and Rifampicin.  Why B/D wrong: Not the defining resistance for MDR-TB.  Why C wrong: Resistance to second-line drugs defines XDR-TB."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Extensively Drug Resistant Tuberculosis (XDR-TB) is characterized by resistance against:","options":["First-line drugs only","Second-line drugs of tuberculosis","Isoniazid only","Rifampicin only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: XDR-TB is resistance against second-line drugs of tuberculosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe first-line resistance, not XDR-TB."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"A tuberculosis case is confirmed by which laboratory examination?","options":["Widal test","Sputum microscopy","ELISA","Blood culture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Tuberculosis case confirmed by sputum microscopy.\"  Why A wrong: Widal test is for typhoid.  Why C/D wrong: Not the confirmatory test for TB per the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In sputum smear examination for TB, Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB) are stained red by which method?","options":["Gram staining","Giemsa stain","Ziehl-Neelsen method","Albert staining"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In sputum smear examination, AFB are stained red by Ziehl-Neelsen method.  Why A/B/D wrong: These stains are not used for AFB in this context."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"A \"New case\" of TB is defined as a patient with sputum-positive pulmonary TB who has never had treatment or has taken anti-TB drugs for less than:","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A new case has never been treated OR has taken anti-TB drugs for less than 4 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies less than 4 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"A TB \"Failure case\" is defined as a patient who remains or becomes smear positive again at which time during treatment?","options":["At one month","At three months","At five months or later","At two months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A failure case is smear positive again at five months or later during the course of treatment.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies five months or later."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"A TB patient who returns sputum smear positive after having left treatment for at least how long is termed \"Return after default\"?","options":["One month","Two months","Three months","Four months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Return after default = patient returns smear positive after leaving treatment for at least two months.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies at least two months."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"World TB Day is celebrated on which date every year?","options":["24 March","25 April","29 July","27 March"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World TB Day is celebrated on 24 March every year.  Why B wrong: 25 April is World Malaria Day.  Why C wrong: 29 July is World ORS Day.  Why D wrong: 27 March 2014 is when WHO declared India polio-free."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The theme of World TB Day 2022 was:","options":["\"Yes! we can end TB!\"","\"Invest to end TB - Save lives\"","\"The Clock is Ticking\"","\"It's time\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2022 theme was \"Invest to end TB - Save lives\".  Why A wrong: \"Yes! we can end TB!\" was the 2023 theme.  Why C/D wrong: These themes are not listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Tuberculosis is also known by which of the following names?","options":["White Plague or Barometer of social welfare diseases","Break Bone Fever","Enteric fever","Infectious hepatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB is also known as White Plague or Barometer of social welfare diseases.  Why B wrong: Break Bone Fever is dengue.  Why C wrong: Enteric fever is typhoid.  Why D wrong: Infectious hepatitis is Hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Isoniazid is contraindicated in a client with liver disease because it can cause:","options":["Optic neuritis","Hepatotoxicity","8th cranial nerve damage","Red-orange urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isoniazid can cause hepatotoxicity; the nurse should observe for yellowish discolouration of sclera/urine indicating jaundice/liver damage.  Why A wrong: Optic neuritis is an Ethambutol side effect.  Why C wrong: 8th cranial nerve damage is from Streptomycin.  Why D wrong: Red-orange colour is a Rifampicin side effect."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Isoniazid causes deficiency of which vitamin, so supplementation is offered to clients on isoniazid therapy?","options":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine)","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isoniazid causes Vitamin B6/Pyridoxine deficiency, so Vit-B6 should be offered to clients taking isoniazid.  Why A/C/D wrong: These vitamins are not associated with isoniazid deficiency here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The major side effects of Ethambutol are optic neuritis, retrobulbar neuritis, and disturbance of which colour vision?","options":["Blue & Yellow","Red & Green","Black & White","Green & Yellow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ethambutol affects colour vision (Red & Green); it should be avoided in children <6 years because colour vision is difficult to identify in them.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies Red & Green."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Ethambutol should be avoided in children below which age?","options":["<2 years","<4 years","<6 years","<10 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ethambutol should be avoided in children <6 years of age because colour vision is difficult to identify.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies <6 years."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Red-orange colour of urine and body secretions is a side effect of which anti-TB drug?","options":["Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Rifampicin","Streptomycin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Red-orange colour is the side effect of Rifampicin.  Why A/B/D wrong: These drugs cause hepatotoxicity, optic neuritis, and ototoxicity respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Streptomycin toxicity can cause damage to which cranial nerve, resulting in deafness?","options":["5th cranial nerve","7th cranial nerve","8th cranial nerve","10th cranial nerve"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Streptomycin toxicity causes 8th cranial nerve damage resulting in deafness, so it is avoided in pregnant women and children.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies the 8th cranial nerve."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which of the following anti-TB drugs are contraindicated during pregnancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Streptomycin","Kanamycin","Amikacin","Isoniazid"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Streptomycin, Kanamycin, and Amikacin are contraindicated during pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Isoniazid is not listed as contraindicated in pregnancy (it can cross the BBB)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"If a tuberculosis patient is also HIV positive, DOTS treatment should be extended up to:","options":["6 months","9 months","12 months","18 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If TB patients are also HIV positive, DOTS treatment should be extended up to 12 months.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies up to 12 months."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The BCG vaccine is given to protect against which disease?","options":["Polio","Tuberculosis (TB)","Hepatitis","Cholera"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG vaccine protects against tuberculosis or TB.  Why A/C/D wrong: BCG is specific to TB, not these diseases."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the causative organism of Meningococcal Meningitis?","options":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Neisseria meningitidis","Haemophilus influenzae","Salmonella typhi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Meningococcal meningitis is caused by Neisseria meningitidis, a gram-negative diplococci bacteria.  Why A/C/D wrong: These organisms are not the causative agent of meningococcal meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"How many subtypes (groups) of Neisseria meningitidis are described, and which group causes major epidemics?","options":["Three subtypes; Group-B causes major epidemics","Two subtypes; Group-A causes major epidemics","Four subtypes; Group-C causes major epidemics","Three subtypes; Group-A causes major epidemics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are three subtypes (Group-A, B, C); Group-B is very serious and causes major epidemics.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies three subtypes with Group-B causing major epidemics.  [Note: source states three subtypes and Group-B causes major epidemics; per WHO/CDC, N. meningitidis has ≥12 serogroups and historically Group A caused the largest epidemics (African meningitis belt). Answer keyed to match the source as printed.]"},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the incubation period of Meningococcal Meningitis?","options":["3 to 4 days but usually may be 2 to 10 days in some cases","10 to 14 days","7 to 21 days","10 to 50 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incubation period is 3 to 4 days but usually may be 2 to 10 days in some cases.  Why B wrong: 10-14 days is typhoid.  Why C wrong: 7-21 days is polio.  Why D wrong: 10-50 days is Hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The drug of choice for Meningococcal Meningitis is:","options":["Doxycycline","Penicillin or Chloramphenicol","Chloroquine","Rifampicin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drug of choice is penicillin or chloramphenicol.  Why A wrong: Doxycycline is for cholera.  Why C wrong: Chloroquine is for malaria.  Why D wrong: Rifampicin is for TB."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of Meningococcal Meningitis?","options":["Faecal-oral route","Droplet transmission","Vector-borne","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mode of transmission is droplet transmission.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the mode for meningococcal meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In meningococcal meningitis, a patient will NOT be infectious after how long from the first dose of antibiotic administration?","options":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Patient will not be infectious after 48 hours from the first dose of antibiotics.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Bacterial meningitis is most common in which age group?","options":["Under 5 years of age","Adults over 60 years","Adolescents","School-age children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bacterial meningitis is most common in under 5 years of age (DSSSB-2020).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies under 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In meningococcal meningitis, a patient is not considered infectious if how many consecutive blood culture samples are negative for the organism?","options":["1","2","3","5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If consecutively 3 blood culture samples are negative, patient is not considered infectious.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 3 samples."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Poliovirus, the causative agent of poliomyelitis, belongs to which family?","options":["Picornaviridae","Flaviviridae","Hepadnaviridae","Orthomyxoviridae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Poliovirus is a serotype of the species Enterovirus C, in the family Picornaviridae.  Why B wrong: Flaviviridae includes dengue virus.  Why C wrong: Hepadnaviridae is Hepatitis B.  Why D wrong: Orthomyxoviridae is influenza."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the incubation period of poliomyelitis?","options":["3 to 4 days","7 to 21 days","10 to 14 days","18 to 40 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Incubation period of polio is 7 to 21 days (ESIC-2019).  Why A wrong: 3-4 days is meningococcal meningitis.  Why C wrong: 10-14 days is typhoid.  Why D wrong: 18-40 days is Plasmodium malariae."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the period of infectivity of poliomyelitis?","options":["7 to 10 days before and after onset of symptoms","Until organism absent from nose/throat discharge","2 to 5 days after onset","48 hours after treatment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Period of infectivity is 7 to 10 days before and after onset of symptoms.  Why B wrong: That describes meningococcal meningitis.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which vaccines are used for prevention of poliomyelitis?","options":["BCG and DPT","OPV and IPV","Meningococcal vaccine","MMR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prevention of polio uses OPV and IPV.  Why A/C/D wrong: These vaccines are not for polio."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Polio-virus primarily affects which system first, then the nervous system secondarily?","options":["Respiratory system","GI (gastrointestinal) system","Cardiovascular system","Renal system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polio-virus primarily affects the GI system then secondary nervous system.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the GI system first."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which poliovirus serotype is the most common, causes epidemics, and can cause paralytic poliomyelitis?","options":["PV-1","PV-2","PV-3","PV-4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Poliovirus type PV-1 is most common, causes epidemics, and can cause paralytic poliomyelitis.  Why B wrong: PV-2 was declared eradicated in September 2015.  Why C wrong: PV-3 was declared eradicated in 2019.  Why D wrong: There is no PV-4 serotype described."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Wild Poliovirus type PV-2 was declared eradicated in which month and year?","options":["September 2015","2019","January 2011","March 2014"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wild PV-2 was declared eradicated in September 2015 (last detected in 1999).  Why B wrong: 2019 is when PV-3 was eradicated.  Why C wrong: January 2011 is the last wild polio cases in India.  Why D wrong: March 2014 is India's polio-free declaration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Wild Poliovirus type PV-3 was declared eradicated in which year (last detected in 2012)?","options":["2015","2019","2011","2014"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wild PV-3 was declared eradicated in 2019 after last being detected in 2012.  Why A/C/D wrong: These years correspond to other polio milestones."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which type of poliomyelitis is a minor illness that does NOT involve the central nervous system (CNS)?","options":["Abortive poliomyelitis","Paralytic poliomyelitis","Bulbar poliomyelitis","Spinal poliomyelitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Abortive poliomyelitis is a minor illness of polio which does not involve the CNS.  Why B wrong: Paralytic poliomyelitis is a polio infection of the CNS.  Why C/D wrong: Not described as CNS-sparing in the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Paralytic poliomyelitis, the most common being spinal polio, is caused by which polio virus type?","options":["Type-I","Type-II","Type-III","Type-IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paralytic poliomyelitis is a polio infection of the CNS, most common spinal polio, type-Ist polio virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies type-I."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The WHO declared India a polio-free country on which date?","options":["13 January 2011","27 March 2014","24 March 2015","25 April 2019"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO declared India polio-free on 27 March 2014, since no wild polio cases reported in five years.  Why A wrong: 13 January 2011 was the last reported wild polio cases in India (West Bengal and Gujarat).  Why C/D wrong: These dates do not match."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The last reported cases of wild polio in India (13 January 2011) were from which states?","options":["Kerala and Tamil Nadu","West Bengal and Gujarat","Maharashtra and Punjab","Bihar and Rajasthan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The last reported cases of wild polio in India were in West Bengal and Gujarat on 13 January 2011.  Why A/C/D wrong: These states are not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The Pulse Polio Immunization program in India was launched in which year?","options":["1995","2000","2011","2014"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse Polio Immunization program 1995 is related to prevention of polio disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These years mark other events."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"VVM (Vaccine Vial Monitor) is a technique used to monitor polio vaccine potency by which parameter, because polio vaccine is highly heat sensitive?","options":["Temperature","Light exposure","pH","Humidity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VVM monitors polio vaccine potency by temperature because polio vaccine is highly heat sensitive.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies temperature."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Viral Hepatitis-A is formerly known as:","options":["Serum hepatitis","Infectious hepatitis","Post-transfusion hepatitis","Delta hepatitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis-A is formerly known as infectious hepatitis.  Why A wrong: Serum hepatitis is Hepatitis B.  Why C wrong: Post-transfusion hepatitis is Hepatitis C.  Why D wrong: Delta hepatitis is Hepatitis D."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The causative agent of Hepatitis-A is:","options":["Hepadnavirus","Hepatitis-A virus (HAV, an enterovirus)","Delta virus","Flavivirus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Causative agent is Hepatitis-A virus (HAV), which is an enterovirus.  Why A wrong: Hepadnavirus is Hepatitis B.  Why C/D wrong: These are not HAV."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of Hepatitis-A?","options":["Feco-oral route (most common) and direct contact","Blood transfusion only","Sexual contact only","Vector-borne"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mode of transmission of Hepatitis-A is feco-oral route (most common) and direct contact.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the primary modes for Hepatitis-A."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the incubation period of Hepatitis-A (as stated in the description)?","options":["10 to 50 days","45 to 180 days","30 to 120 days","21 to 45 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incubation period of Hepatitis-A is 10 to 50 days.  Why B wrong: 45-180 days is Hepatitis B (per table).  Why C wrong: 30-120 days is Hepatitis C.  Why D wrong: 21-45 days is Hepatitis E."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Types of Viral Hepatitis table, which type is called \"Serum hepatitis\" and is caused by Hepadnavirus (DNA virus)?","options":["Hepatitis A","Hepatitis B","Hepatitis C","Hepatitis D"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B is called Serum hepatitis, caused by Hepadnavirus (DNA virus), incubation 45-180 days.  Why A wrong: Hepatitis A is Infectious hepatitis (Enterovirus).  Why C wrong: Hepatitis C is Post-transfusion hepatitis (RNA virus).  Why D wrong: Hepatitis D is Delta hepatitis (RNA virus)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Types of Viral Hepatitis table, Hepatitis C (\"Post-transfusion hepatitis\") has an incubation period of:","options":["15-45 days","45-180 days","30-120 days","21-45 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis C incubation is 30-120 days, transmitted percutaneously (BT).  Why A wrong: 15-45 days is Hepatitis A in the table.  Why B wrong: 45-180 days is Hepatitis B.  Why D wrong: 21-45 days is Hepatitis E."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Types of Viral Hepatitis table, Hepatitis D is also called:","options":["Infectious hepatitis","Serum hepatitis","Delta hepatitis","Post-transfusion hepatitis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis D is called Delta hepatitis (RNA virus), incubation 30-90 days.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are the other names for Hepatitis A, B, and C respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Types of Viral Hepatitis table, which types are transmitted by the faecal-oral route?","options":["Hepatitis A and Hepatitis E","Hepatitis B and Hepatitis C","Hepatitis C and Hepatitis D","Hepatitis B and Hepatitis D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis A and Hepatitis E are transmitted by the faecal-oral route (Hepatitis E: 21-45 days, faecal-oral).  Why B/C/D wrong: Hepatitis B, C, D are transmitted sexually/perinatally/percutaneously."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Cholera is an acute infectious disease caused by which organism?","options":["Salmonella typhi","Vibrio cholerae (Gram-negative)","Shigella","Rotavirus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cholera is caused by Vibrio cholerae, a Gram-negative, comma-shaped bacteria.  Why A wrong: Salmonella typhi causes typhoid.  Why C wrong: Shigella causes dysentery.  Why D wrong: Rotavirus causes infantile diarrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of cholera?","options":["Rose spots","Rice water stool","Bloody diarrhoea","Step ladder fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of cholera is rice water stool.  Why A wrong: Rose spots occur in typhoid.  Why C wrong: Bloody diarrhoea is dysentery.  Why D wrong: Step ladder fever is typhoid."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Vibrio cholerae is described as which shape of bacteria?","options":["Rod-shaped","Comma-shaped","Spherical","Spiral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is comma-shaped bacteria (printed \"coma shaped\").  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies comma-shaped."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the incubation period of cholera?","options":["Few hours to 5 days","10 to 14 days","7 to 21 days","2 to 10 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incubation period of cholera is few hours to 5 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These match typhoid, polio, and meningitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The drug of choice for cholera is:","options":["Penicillin","Chloroquine","Doxycycline & Tetracycline","Rifampicin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Drug of choice for cholera is Doxycycline & Tetracycline.  Why A wrong: Penicillin is for meningococcal meningitis.  Why B wrong: Chloroquine is for malaria.  Why D wrong: Rifampicin is for TB."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"As per the WHO's changed definition, acute diarrhoeal disease is defined based primarily on which characteristic?","options":["Number of stools only","Consistency and characteristics of the stools","Colour of the stool only","Duration of illness only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO changed the definition so that consistency and characteristics of the stools define diarrhoea rather than just the number of stools (previously passage of loose/liquid/watery stools >3 times a day).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source emphasizes consistency/characteristics over number."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Diarrhoeal disease is the second leading cause of death in children under 5 years old. Worldwide, what is the FIRST leading cause of death in children under 5?","options":["Malaria","Pneumonia","Measles","Tuberculosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Worldwide, the first leading cause of death in children under 5 is pneumonia (Raj. CHO-2020).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies pneumonia as first, diarrhoea as second."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The most common cause of infantile diarrhoea is:","options":["Rotavirus","E. coli","Shigella","Vibrio cholerae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of infantile diarrhoea is Rotavirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause diarrhoea but are not the most common cause of infantile diarrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The most common and life-threatening complication of diarrhoea is:","options":["Malnutrition","Dehydration","Sepsis","Anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common and life-threatening complication of diarrhoea is dehydration (Raj. CHO-2020).  Why A wrong: Diarrhoea is a leading cause of malnutrition but dehydration is the life-threatening complication.  Why C/D wrong: Not the most common life-threatening complication per source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Acute watery diarrhoea lasts for how long, and about what percentage occurs with an infectious reason?","options":["<2 weeks; 90% infectious",">4 weeks; 50% infectious","2 weeks; 100% infectious","<1 week; 70% infectious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acute watery diarrhoea has sudden onset, lasts <2 weeks, and 90% occurs with infectious reasons (most common cause rotavirus, cholera, E. coli).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source values."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Chronic diarrhoea is defined as diarrhoea which lasts for:","options":["<2 weeks",">4 weeks","14 days","3 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic diarrhoea lasts for >4 weeks, and most causes are non-infectious.  Why A wrong: <2 weeks is acute watery diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: 14 days/2 weeks relates to persistent diarrhoea.  Why D wrong: Not a defined threshold in the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Persistent diarrhoea is characterized by loose stool 3 or more times per day for a duration of at least:","options":["2 weeks or 14 days","4 weeks","3 days","1 week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Persistent diarrhoea = loose stool, 3 or more times per day, at least 2 weeks or 14 days in duration (MP, UP CCH-2021).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies at least 2 weeks/14 days."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Protracted diarrhoea is defined as persistent diarrhoea associated with:","options":["Bloody stools and mucus","Weight loss and malnutrition","High fever","Vomiting only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protracted diarrhoea is persistent diarrhoea with weight loss and malnutrition.  Why A wrong: Blood and mucus describe dysentery.  Why C/D wrong: Not the defining feature per source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Acute bloody diarrhoea (dysentery) with visible blood and mucus is most commonly caused by:","options":["Rotavirus","Shigellosis (Shigella)","Vibrio cholerae","Staphylococcus aureus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of acute bloody diarrhoea (dysentery) is shigellosis.  Why A wrong: Rotavirus causes acute watery diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: Vibrio cholerae causes rice water stool.  Why D wrong: Staphylococcus causes food poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The low-osmolar ORS solution recommended by WHO & UNICEF (in 2003-04) is an example of which type of fluid?","options":["Hypertonic fluid","Isotonic fluid","Hypotonic fluid","Colloid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Low osmolar ORS solution recommended by WHO & UNICEF in 2003-04 is an example of hypotonic fluid.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies hypotonic fluid."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"World ORS Day is observed on:","options":["24 March","29 July","25 April","27 March"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: World ORS Day is 29 July.  Why A wrong: 24 March is World TB Day.  Why C wrong: 25 April is World Malaria Day.  Why D wrong: 27 March 2014 is India's polio-free declaration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"A prepared ORS solution should be used within how many hours?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Prepared ORS solution should be used within 24 hours.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"According to ORS requirement by age to prevent dehydration, how much ORS is given in the first 4 hours?","options":["50 ml/kg","75 ml/kg","100 ml/kg","200 ml/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Requirement is 75 ml/kg in the first 4 hours.  Why A wrong: 50-100 ml/kg is for <2 years after each stool.  Why C/D wrong: 100-200 ml/kg is for >2 years after each loose stool."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"After each loose stool, a child aged less than 2 years should be given how much ORS?","options":["50 to 100 ml/kg","100 to 200 ml/kg","75 ml/kg","20 ml/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 50 to 100 ml/kg for <2 years of age after each stool.  Why B wrong: 100-200 ml/kg is for >2 years.  Why C wrong: 75 ml/kg is for the first 4 hours.  Why D wrong: Not an ORS dose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"After each loose stool, a child aged more than 2 years should be given how much ORS?","options":["50 to 100 ml/kg","100 to 200 ml/kg","75 ml/kg","250 ml/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 100 to 200 ml/kg for >2 years of age children after each loose stool.  Why A wrong: 50-100 ml/kg is for <2 years.  Why C wrong: 75 ml/kg is for the first 4 hours.  Why D wrong: Not a listed value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the household ORS preparation method, how much sugar is added per 1 litre of clean water?","options":["Six level teaspoons (1 teaspoon = 5 gm)","Half level teaspoon","Ten level teaspoons","Two level teaspoons"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sugar: Six level teaspoons (1 teaspoon = 5 gm) per 1 litre of clean water.  Why B wrong: Half level teaspoon is the salt amount.  Why C/D wrong: Not the specified amount."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In household ORS preparation, how much salt is added per 1 litre of clean water?","options":["Six level teaspoons","Half level teaspoon (2.5 gm)","One level teaspoon (5 gm)","Two teaspoons"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Salt: Half level teaspoon (2.5 gm).  Why A wrong: Six teaspoons is the sugar amount.  Why C/D wrong: Not the specified salt amount."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the household ORS method, 1 litre of clean water is equal to about how many cupfuls (each cup ~200 ml)?","options":["3 cupful","5 cupful","10 cupful","2 cupful"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clean Water: 1 litre = 5 cupful (each cup about 200 ml).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 5 cupful."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"For zinc supplementation during acute diarrhoea, infants under 6 months are given how much zinc, for how many days?","options":["10 mg for 10 to 14 days","20 mg for 10 to 14 days","10 mg for 7 days","20 mg for 5 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 10 mg for under 6 months infants for 10 to 14 days.  Why B wrong: 20 mg is for older than 6 months.  Why C/D wrong: Duration is 10-14 days."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"For zinc supplementation, infants older than 6 months are given how much zinc during acute diarrhoea?","options":["10 mg","20 mg","5 mg","40 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 20 mg for older-than-6-months infants for 10 to 14 days.  Why A wrong: 10 mg is for under 6 months.  Why C/D wrong: Not the specified dose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the composition of Reduced Osmolarity ORS, what is the total osmolarity value?","options":["245 mmol/L","311 mmol/L","200 mmol/L","75 mmol/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Reduced Osmolarity ORS has a total osmolarity of 245 mmol/L.  Why B wrong: 311 is the standard (old) ORS osmolarity, not listed here.  Why C wrong: Not the total.  Why D wrong: 75 mmol/L is the sodium/glucose value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Reduced Osmolarity ORS composition, the sodium content is:","options":["65 mmol/L","75 mmol/L","20 mmol/L","10 mmol/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium = 75 mmol/L (glucose anhydrous also 75 mmol/L).  Why A wrong: 65 mmol/L is chloride.  Why C wrong: 20 mmol/L is potassium.  Why D wrong: 10 mmol/L is citrate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Reduced Osmolality ORS (gram/litre) composition, the amount of glucose is:","options":["2.6 g/L","13.5 g/L","1.5 g/L","2.9 g/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glucose = 13.5 gram/litre in the Reduced Osmolality ORS.  Why A wrong: 2.6 g/L is Sodium Chloride (NaCl).  Why C wrong: 1.5 g/L is Potassium Chloride (KCl).  Why D wrong: 2.9 g/L is Trisodium Citrate Dihydrate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Reduced Osmolality ORS composition, the total weight of ORS ingredients is:","options":["20.5 g","13.5 g","2.6 g","245 g"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The total weight is 20.5 grams (NaCl 2.6 + Glucose 13.5 + KCl 1.5 + Trisodium Citrate Dihydrate 2.9).  Why B/C wrong: These are individual component values.  Why D wrong: 245 is the osmolarity in mmol/L."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Food poisoning is defined as acute gastroenteritis caused by ingestion of food/drink contaminated with:","options":["Only viruses","Living bacteria, their toxins, or chemical substances","Only parasites","Only fungi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Food poisoning is acute gastroenteritis caused by ingestion of food/drink contaminated with either living bacteria or their toxins or chemical substances.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies bacteria/toxins/chemicals."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In staphylococcal food poisoning, symptoms (incubation period) appear within:","options":["2 to 4 hours","8 to 22 hours","12 to 24 hours","18 to 36 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Staphylococcal food poisoning symptoms appear within 2 to 4 hours (table: Staphylococcus aureus 2-6 hours).  Why B wrong: 8-22 hours is Clostridium perfringens.  Why C wrong: 12-24 hours is Salmonella.  Why D wrong: 18-36 hours is Clostridium botulism."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Food Poisoning table, which organism has an incubation period of 1-5 hours and is associated with fried rice?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Bacillus cereus","Salmonella","Clostridium botulism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bacillus cereus has an incubation period of 1-5 hours and its food source is fried rice (RRB 2019).  Why A wrong: Staphylococcus aureus (2-6 hours) is linked to improperly frozen meat, milk.  Why C wrong: Salmonella (12-24 hours) is from egg/poultry.  Why D wrong: Clostridium botulism (18-36 hours) is from canned food."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Food Poisoning table, Clostridium botulism has an incubation period of 18-36 hours and is associated with which food source?","options":["Fried rice","Canned food","Shrimps and crab","Egg, poultry meat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clostridium botulism (18-36 hours) is linked to canned food (Raj. CHO-2023).  Why A wrong: Fried rice is Bacillus cereus.  Why C wrong: Shrimps and crab are Vibrio.  Why D wrong: Egg/poultry is Salmonella."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Food Poisoning table, which organism has an incubation period of 12-18 hours and is associated with shrimps and crab?","options":["Vibrio","Salmonella","Bacillus cereus","Clostridium perfringens"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vibrio has an incubation period of 12-18 hours and is associated with shrimps and crab.  Why B wrong: Salmonella (12-24 hours) is egg/poultry.  Why C wrong: Bacillus cereus (1-5 hours) is fried rice.  Why D wrong: Clostridium perfringens (8-22 hours) is beef/poultry/beans/broth."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Food Poisoning table, Salmonella has an incubation period of 12-24 hours and is associated with:","options":["Fried rice","Canned food","Egg, poultry meat half cooked","Shrimps and crab"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Salmonella (12-24 hours) is associated with egg, poultry meat half cooked.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are the food sources for Bacillus cereus, Clostridium botulism, and Vibrio respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Typhoid fever is also known as:","options":["Break Bone Fever","Enteric fever","Infectious hepatitis","Blackwater fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Typhoid fever is also known as Enteric fever.  Why A wrong: Break Bone Fever is dengue.  Why C wrong: Infectious hepatitis is Hepatitis A.  Why D wrong: Blackwater fever is a P. falciparum complication."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the causative agent of typhoid fever?","options":["Salmonella typhi (95%) and Salmonella paratyphi (5%)","Vibrio cholerae","Shigella","Neisseria meningitidis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Typhoid is caused by Salmonella typhi (95%) and Salmonella paratyphi (5%).  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause cholera, dysentery, and meningitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The characteristic pattern of fever in typhoid is described as:","options":["Tertian fever","Step ladder","Quartan fever","Remittent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pattern of fever in typhoid is Step ladder.  Why A wrong: Tertian is P. vivax/ovale malaria (48 hr).  Why C wrong: Quartan is P. malariae (72 hr).  Why D wrong: Not the source pattern."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the incubation period of typhoid fever?","options":["3 to 4 days","10 to 14 days","7 to 21 days","Few hours to 5 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Incubation period of typhoid is 10 to 14 days.  Why A wrong: 3-4 days is meningococcal meningitis.  Why C wrong: 7-21 days is polio.  Why D wrong: Few hours to 5 days is cholera."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which diagnostic test (an agglutination test) is used for typhoid fever?","options":["Widal test","Tourniquet test","NS1 Ag test","Peripheral blood smear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Widal test, an agglutination test, is used for typhoid fever.  Why B/C wrong: Tourniquet test and NS1 Ag are used for dengue.  Why D wrong: Peripheral blood smear is for malaria."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which organ is most commonly affected by typhoid?","options":["Liver","Small intestine","Kidney","Lung"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common organ affected by typhoid is the small intestine.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the small intestine."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In typhoid clinical features, \"pea soup\" diarrhoea appears with rose spots on the chest in which week, along with relative bradycardia?","options":["1st week","2nd week","4th week","6th week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 2 to 3 weeks rashes can appear, and \"pea soup\" diarrhoea with relative bradycardia and rose spots on chest are seen in the 2nd week.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies rose spots in the 2nd week."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Dengue fever is also known as:","options":["Break Bone Fever","Enteric fever","White Plague","Blackwater fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dengue fever is also known as Break Bone Fever (CHO UP, MP, BIHAR 2018, 19).  Why B wrong: Enteric fever is typhoid.  Why C wrong: White Plague is TB.  Why D wrong: Blackwater fever is a P. falciparum complication."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The causative agent of dengue fever is:","options":["Arbovirus / flavivirus","Plasmodium","Salmonella","Vibrio cholerae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Causative agent of dengue is arbovirus/flavivirus (dengue virus is a type of arbovirus).  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause malaria, typhoid, and cholera respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Dengue fever is transmitted by which mosquito, also known as the \"tiger mosquito\"?","options":["Anopheles","Culex","Aedes aegypti","Mansonia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dengue is transmitted by Aedes aegypti mosquito, also known as the tiger (printed \"tigor\") mosquito.  Why A wrong: Anopheles transmits malaria.  Why B/D wrong: Not the dengue vector."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the incubation period of dengue fever?","options":["3 to 10 days but usually 5 to 6 days","10 to 14 days","7 to 21 days","18 to 40 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incubation period of dengue is 3 to 10 days but usually 5 to 6 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These match typhoid, polio, and P. malariae respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The Aedes aegypti mosquito primarily bites during which time?","options":["Night time","Day time","Dawn only","Dusk only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aedes aegypti bites primarily during the day time.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies day time."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the most common clinical feature (lab finding) in dengue fever?","options":["Thrombocytopenia (low platelet count)","Leukocytosis","Polycythemia","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common clinical feature is thrombocytopenia (low platelet count).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the dengue hallmark."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In treating dengue fever, patients are told to avoid aspirin and other NSAIDs because they may:","options":["Cause hepatotoxicity","Increase the risk for haemorrhage","Lower blood pressure","Cause deafness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin and NSAIDs are avoided because they may increase the risk for haemorrhage.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source cites bleeding risk, not these effects."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which of the following is a listed complication of classic dengue fever? (Select all that apply)","options":["Febrile seizures","Dehydration","Deafness","Optic neuritis"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of classic dengue fever are febrile seizures and dehydration.  Why C wrong: Deafness is a streptomycin side effect.  Why D wrong: Optic neuritis is an ethambutol side effect."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF), the major pathological changes are plasma leakage and abnormal haemostasis, manifested by raising hematocrit and:","options":["Thrombocytopenia (moderate to marked)","Leukocytosis","Hyperglycemia","Hyponatremia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DHF pathological changes are plasma leakage and abnormal haemostasis with rising hematocrit and moderate to marked thrombocytopenia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the DHF hallmark."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the WHO Clinical Criteria for diagnosing DHF, thrombocytopenia is defined as a platelet count of less than:","options":["1,00,000/mm³","1,50,000/mm³","50,000/mm³","4,00,000/mm³"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO criteria for DHF includes thrombocytopenia (platelet count of <1,00,000/mm³).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies <1,00,000/mm³."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which of the following is a WHO clinical criterion for diagnosing DHF that assesses vascular leakage?","options":["Evidence of increased vascular permeability","Positive Widal test","Rice water stool","Positive AFB smear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO DHF criteria include evidence of increased vascular permeability (along with fever, hemorrhagic manifestation/positive tourniquet test, and thrombocytopenia).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are diagnostic markers for typhoid, cholera, and TB respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"A hemorrhagic manifestation used in WHO criteria for DHF is a positive:","options":["Widal test","Tourniquet test","Mantoux test","Coombs test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Any hemorrhagic manifestation includes a positive tourniquet test.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated tests."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Dengue Shock Syndrome (DSS) presents with early signs of shock. Which of the following are those signs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Restlessness","Cold clammy skin","Rapid weak pulse","Narrowing of pulse pressure and hypotension","Bradycardia with warm skin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: DSS early signs of shock include restlessness, cold clammy skin, rapid weak pulse, and narrowing of pulse pressure with hypotension.  Why E wrong: DSS presents with cold clammy skin and rapid weak pulse, not warm skin/bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which laboratory test is a rapid method of detecting dengue virus within 24 hours but is costly?","options":["RT-PCR","NS1 Ag test","IgM ELISA","Widal test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RT-PCR is a rapid method of detecting dengue virus within 24 hours but is costly.  Why B wrong: NS1 Ag detects virus if onset 4-5 days.  Why C wrong: IgM ELISA is used 4-5 days after fever onset.  Why D wrong: Widal test is for typhoid."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"If the onset of dengue illness is 4-5 days, the virus is detected in serum and plasma by which test?","options":["NS1 Ag test","RT-PCR","Tourniquet test","Peripheral blood smear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NS1 Ag test detects virus if onset of illness is 4-5 days, in serum and plasma.  Why B wrong: RT-PCR is the rapid 24-hour test.  Why C/D wrong: Not used for dengue antigen detection."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which is the most common test for serologic diagnosis of dengue, used 4-5 days after onset of fever?","options":["IgM ELISA","RT-PCR","NS1 Ag","CBC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IgM ELISA is the most common test for serologic diagnosis if used 4-5 days after onset of fever.  Why B wrong: RT-PCR is rapid but costly.  Why C wrong: NS1 Ag detects the virus antigen early.  Why D wrong: CBC monitors platelet counts."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The IgM antibody in dengue is formed in blood after how long and peaks at what time?","options":["Formed after 4-5 days; peaks at 2 weeks after onset","Formed after 24 hours; peaks at 1 week","Formed after 2 weeks; peaks at 4 weeks","Formed immediately; peaks at 3 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IgM antibody is formed after 4-5 days and peaks at 2 weeks after onset of symptoms.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies formation after 4-5 days and peak at 2 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which dengue test is used to differentiate primary and secondary dengue infection?","options":["IgM and IgG ratio test","NS1 Ag test","RT-PCR","Tourniquet test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The IgM and IgG ratio test is used to differentiate primary and secondary dengue infection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not distinguish primary vs secondary infection."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Malaria is a protozoan infection caused by which organism?","options":["Plasmodium","Vibrio","Salmonella","Neisseria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Malaria is a protozoan infection caused by Plasmodium.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause cholera, typhoid, and meningitis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In malaria, which is the intermediate host and which is the definitive host?","options":["Man is the intermediate host; mosquito is the definitive host","Mosquito is the intermediate host; man is the definitive host","Both man and mosquito are intermediate hosts","Man is both host types"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Man is the intermediate host and mosquito is the definitive host.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies man = intermediate, mosquito = definitive."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The vector (transmitting agent) of malaria is the:","options":["Female Anopheles mosquito","Aedes aegypti mosquito","Culex mosquito","Male Anopheles mosquito"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The female Anopheles mosquito is the vector (transmitting agent) of malaria.  Why B wrong: Aedes aegypti transmits dengue.  Why C/D wrong: Not the malaria vector."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Plasmodium enters the human body in which infectious form through the bite of an infected female Anopheles mosquito?","options":["Sporozoites","Gametocytes","Schizonts","Merozoites"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Plasmodium enters the human body as sporozoites (infectious form) to complete their asexual life cycle in humans.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other stages; the entering infectious form is sporozoites."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the malaria life cycle, the sexual life cycle completed in the mosquito is also known as:","options":["Sporogony","Schizogony","Gametogony","Merogony"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sexual life cycle is also known as sporogony, completed in the mosquito.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source names it sporogony."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In malaria, the parasites initially multiply within which cells before attacking Red Blood Cells?","options":["Liver cells","Kidney cells","Nerve cells","Bone marrow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Parasites initially multiply within the liver cells and then attack the RBCs, causing rupture and high fever with chills.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies liver cells first."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which is the standard/gold standard test for diagnosis of malarial infection?","options":["Peripheral blood smear examination (Giemsa-stained), thick and thin film","Widal test","NS1 Ag test","Sputum microscopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Peripheral blood smear examination (Giemsa-stained thick and thin film) remains the gold standard for malaria diagnosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These diagnose typhoid, dengue, and TB respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the malaria blood smear, which film is more reliable for searching the parasite, and which is more valuable for identifying the species?","options":["Thick film for searching parasite; thin film for identifying species","Thin film for searching parasite; thick film for identifying species","Both thick for both","Both thin for both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thick film is more reliable in searching for the parasite, while thin film is more valuable in identifying the species.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source assigns thick = search, thin = species ID."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which stain is most commonly used to stain the malaria blood smear prior to examination?","options":["Giemsa stain","Ziehl-Neelsen stain","Gram stain","Albert stain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Giemsa stain is most commonly used to stain prior to examination.  Why B wrong: Ziehl-Neelsen is for AFB (TB).  Why C/D wrong: Not used for malaria smear here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the stages of Rigor Fever in malaria, which stage is characterized by headache, shivering, fever rising rapidly, and cold skin?","options":["Cold stage","Hot stage","Sweating stage","Recovery stage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Cold stage (¼ to 1½ hours) has headache, shivering, fever rising rapidly, and cold skin.  Why B wrong: Hot stage has very hot feeling, severe headache, skin flushed.  Why C wrong: Sweating stage has profuse sweating and normal temperature.  Why D wrong: Not a listed stage."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the stages of Rigor Fever in malaria, the Hot stage lasts how long?","options":["¼ to 1½ hours","½ to 5 hours","6 to 12 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Hot stage lasts ½ to 5 hours (very hot feeling, severe headache, skin flushed, fever starts falling).  Why A wrong: ¼ to 1½ hours is the Cold stage.  Why C/D wrong: Not the listed durations."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the malaria fever stages, the Sweating stage is characterized by:","options":["Profuse sweating and temperature returning to normal","Shivering and cold skin","Fever rising rapidly","Very hot feeling"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Sweating stage has profuse sweating and temperature normal.  Why B/C wrong: These describe the Cold stage.  Why D wrong: This describes the Hot stage."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"World Malaria Day is celebrated every year on:","options":["25th April","24 March","29 July","27 March"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Malaria Day is celebrated on 25th April.  Why B wrong: 24 March is World TB Day.  Why C wrong: 29 July is World ORS Day.  Why D wrong: 27 March 2014 is India's polio-free declaration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"What is the first-line treatment for malaria (drug-sensitive cases)?","options":["Chloroquine","Quinine","Primaquine","Artesunate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: First-line treatment for malaria is chloroquine.  Why B wrong: Quinine is the drug of choice for severe complicated cases resistant to all drugs.  Why C wrong: Primaquine is used against relapse and is contraindicated in pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Artesunate combination therapy is for chloroquine-resistant P. falciparum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In chloroquine-resistant P. falciparum cases, first-line treatment is Artesunate combination therapy consisting of Artesunate + Sulphadoxine + :","options":["Pyrimethamine","Primaquine","Doxycycline","Rifampicin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The combination is Artesunate + Sulphadoxine + Pyrimethamine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not part of the stated combination therapy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the case of malaria resistant to all drugs and to treat severe complicated cases, the drug of choice is:","options":["Quinine","Chloroquine","Primaquine","Artesunate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In case of resistance to all drugs and to treat severe complicated cases, quinine is the drug of choice.  Why B/C/D wrong: Chloroquine is first-line; Primaquine for relapse; Artesunate for resistant P. falciparum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Chloroquine should NOT be given under which condition?","options":["On an empty stomach","With food","In the morning","With water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chloroquine should not be given in an empty stomach.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source only warns against empty stomach administration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In P. vivax and P. ovale malaria, a brood of schizonts matures every 48 hours, so the periodicity of fever is:","options":["Tertian (tertian malaria)","Quartan (every 72 hours)","Daily","Continuous"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In P. vivax and P. ovale, schizonts mature every 48 hr, so fever is tertian, known as tertian malaria.  Why B wrong: Quartan (72 hr) occurs in P. malariae.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described periodicity."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In P. malariae disease, fever occurs every 72 hours and is known as:","options":["Quartan fever (quartan malaria)","Tertian malaria","Blackwater fever","Break Bone fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In P. malariae, fever occurs every 72 hours, known as quartan fever/quartan malaria.  Why B wrong: Tertian is 48 hr (P. vivax/ovale).  Why C wrong: Blackwater fever is a P. falciparum complication.  Why D wrong: Break Bone fever is dengue."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which Plasmodium species can cause relapse?","options":["P. vivax and P. ovale","P. falciparum and P. malariae","Only P. falciparum","Only P. malariae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: P. vivax and P. ovale can relapse.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies P. vivax and P. ovale as the relapsing species."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which is the most serious/most deadly malaria parasite, that may cause kidney failure, seizure, mental confusion, coma, and death?","options":["Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium ovale","Plasmodium malariae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most serious/deadly parasite is P. falciparum, causing kidney failure, seizure, mental confusion, coma, and death.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are less severe species."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Blackwater fever, characterized by acute intravascular haemolysis with hemoglobinuria, is seen in patients with which Plasmodium species?","options":["P. falciparum","P. vivax","P. ovale","P. malariae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Blackwater fever occurs with acute intravascular haemolysis and hemoglobinuria in patients with P. falciparum.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies P. falciparum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Crescent-shaped or banana-shaped gametocytes are seen in infection with which Plasmodium species?","options":["P. falciparum","P. vivax","P. ovale","P. malariae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Crescent/banana-shaped gametocytes are seen in P. falciparum infection (NIA Jaipur 2023).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies P. falciparum."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which antimalarial drug is contraindicated during pregnancy?","options":["Primaquine","Chloroquine","Quinine","Artesunate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primaquine is contraindicated during pregnancy (ESIC-2024).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies primaquine as contraindicated in pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"The Annual Parasite Index (API) is calculated using which formula?","options":["(Confirmed cases during one year / Population under surveillance) × 1000","(Confirmed cases / Total population) × 100","(Deaths / Confirmed cases) × 1000","(Population under surveillance / Confirmed cases) × 100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: API = (Confirmed cases during one year / Population under surveillance) × 1000.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source formula."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"According to the Plasmodium species table, which species is the most common in India and has an incubation period of 7-14 days (14 days)?","options":["Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium ovale","Plasmodium malariae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Plasmodium vivax has IP 7-14 days (14 days) and is the most common in India (Raj. CCH 2022, Set-I).  Why B wrong: P. falciparum (9-17 days) is second most common.  Why C wrong: P. ovale (16-17 days), first case in Kerala 2020.  Why D wrong: P. malariae (18-40 days), longest IP."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Plasmodium species table, which species is the second most common in India, most serious, and causes cerebral malaria (IP 9-17 days)?","options":["Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium ovale","Plasmodium malariae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: P. falciparum (IP 9-17 days, 12 days) is second most common in India, most serious, and causes cerebral malaria.  Why B wrong: P. vivax is the most common.  Why C/D wrong: P. ovale and P. malariae are rarer."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"According to the Plasmodium species table, the first case of Plasmodium ovale in India was reported in Kerala (in a soldier) in which month/year?","options":["December 2020","January 2011","March 2014","April 2019"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: P. ovale (IP 16-17 days): first case reported in Kerala in a soldier in Dec. 2020.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dates relate to polio milestones, not P. ovale."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"In the Plasmodium species table, which species has the longest incubation period (18-40 days) and causes Quartan Malaria?","options":["Plasmodium malariae","Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium ovale"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: P. malariae has IP 18-40 days (longest incubation period) and causes Quartan Malaria.  Why B/C/D wrong: These have shorter incubation periods and different fever periodicity."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which of the following are listed preventive measures for malaria? (Select all that apply)","options":["Eliminate places around the home where mosquitoes breed","Spray insecticides and take anti-malaria drugs","Sleep under a mosquito net","Chemoprophylaxis","Increase indoor stagnant water storage"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Preventive measures include eliminating mosquito breeding places, spraying insecticides/anti-malaria drugs, sleeping under mosquito nets, community surveillance, and chemoprophylaxis.  Why E wrong: Increasing stagnant water would promote breeding, opposite of prevention."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which modes of transmission of malaria are listed in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Vector borne (female Anopheles mosquito)","Direct transmission through infected blood or plasma","Congenital malaria (very rare)","Faecal-oral route"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Modes are vector borne (female Anopheles), direct transmission (infected blood/plasma), and congenital malaria (very rare).  Why D wrong: Faecal-oral route applies to cholera/hepatitis A, not malaria."},{"section":"Batch 009 · DOTS chemotherapy treatment categories & sputum schedule…","question":"Which causes of drug resistance in tuberculosis are listed in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inadequate treatment and poor TB control programme","Incorrect prescription","Irregular supply of drugs","Lack of supervision and follow up","Excessive vaccination"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of drug resistance include inadequate treatment/poor control programme, incorrect prescription, irregular drug supply, and lack of supervision/follow-up.  Why E wrong: Excessive vaccination is not a listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Lymphatic filariasis is a mosquito-borne disease. Which is its causative agent?","options":["Wuchereria bancrofti and Brugia malayi","Leishmania donovani","Clostridium tetani","Mycobacterium leprae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the causative agents of lymphatic filariasis are the filarial parasites W. bancrofti and B. malayi.  Why B wrong: Leishmania donovani causes kala azar (visceral leishmaniasis).  Why C wrong: Clostridium tetani causes tetanus.  Why D wrong: Mycobacterium leprae causes leprosy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Lymphatic filariasis is transmitted by the bite of which mosquito?","options":["Anopheles mosquito","Aedes mosquito","Culex mosquito","Mansonia mosquito"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the mode of transmission of filariasis as Culex mosquitoes.  Why A wrong: Anopheles transmits malaria.  Why B wrong: Aedes transmits dengue/chikungunya.  Why D wrong: The source specifies Culex, not Mansonia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the incubation period of lymphatic filariasis according to the source?","options":["3 to 21 days","8 to 16 months","2 to 7 days","4 to 14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the incubation period of filariasis as 8 to 16 months.  Why A wrong: 3 to 21 days is the incubation period of tetanus.  Why C wrong: 2 to 7 days is the incubation period of SARS.  Why D wrong: 4 to 14 days is the incubation period of Japanese encephalitis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which clinical manifestation is characteristic of lymphatic filariasis?","options":["Leonine facies","Elephantiasis of genitals, legs and arms","Opisthotonos position","Hydrophobia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists high grade fever, lymphangitis and elephantiasis of genitals, legs and arms as clinical manifestations of filariasis.  Why A wrong: Leonine facies is seen in leprosy.  Why C wrong: Opisthotonos is a cardinal sign of tetanus.  Why D wrong: Hydrophobia is a feature of rabies."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"The drug of choice for the control/treatment of lymphatic filariasis is:","options":["Dapsone","Penicillin","Diethylcarbamazine citrate (DEC)","DDT"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for filariasis is Diethylcarbamazine citrate (DEC).  Why A wrong: Dapsone is the drug of choice for leprosy.  Why B wrong: Penicillin is the drug of choice for tetanus.  Why D wrong: DDT is an insecticide used in leishmaniasis control, not a treatment drug."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the primary strategy for control of filariasis?","options":["JE vaccine","Multi drug therapy","Early diagnosis and treatment","Antiretroviral drugs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states control of filariasis is achieved by early diagnosis and treatment.  Why A wrong: JE vaccine is prevention for Japanese encephalitis.  Why B wrong: MDT is treatment for leprosy.  Why D wrong: Antiretroviral drugs are for AIDS."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Japanese encephalitis is caused by which type of organism?","options":["A group B arbovirus","A protozoan parasite","An acid-fast bacterium","A filarial parasite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states JE is a mosquito-borne encephalitis caused by a group B arbovirus and is a viral disease.  Why B wrong: Leishmaniasis is protozoal.  Why C wrong: Leprosy is caused by an acid-fast bacterium.  Why D wrong: Filariasis is caused by filarial parasites."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Japanese encephalitis is a zoonotic disease transmitted by which mosquito?","options":["Aedes mosquito","Culex mosquito","Anopheles mosquito","Sandfly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states JE is transmitted by the Culex mosquito and is a zoonotic disease.  Why A wrong: Aedes is not the JE vector per the source.  Why C wrong: Anopheles transmits malaria.  Why D wrong: Sandfly transmits leishmaniasis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the epidemiology of Japanese encephalitis, man is considered a/an:","options":["Amplifier host","Primary maintenance host","Incidental \"dead end\" host","Major vertebrate host"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source (Raj. CCH 2022, Set-I) states man is an incidental \"dead end\" host for JE.  Why A wrong: Pigs are the amplifier host (most common).  Why B wrong: Ardeid birds (ducks, fowls, herons) are the primary maintenance host.  Why D wrong: Pigs are the major vertebrate host."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which is the major vertebrate host / most common amplifier host for the Japanese encephalitis virus?","options":["Pigs","Herons","Ducks","Cattle egrets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pigs as the major vertebrate host and the most common amplifier host of JE.  Why B/C wrong: Herons and ducks are ardeid birds serving as primary maintenance hosts, not the major vertebrate host.  Why D wrong: Cattle egrets are listed among birds possibly responsible for JE virus host, not the major vertebrate host."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which is the primary maintenance host of Japanese encephalitis virus?","options":["Pigs","Ardeid birds (ducks, fowls, herons)","Humans","Sandflies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source names ardeid birds (ducks, fowls, herons) as the primary maintenance host.  Why A wrong: Pigs are the amplifier and major vertebrate host.  Why C wrong: Man is a dead-end host.  Why D wrong: Sandflies are not involved in JE."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the incubation period of Japanese encephalitis?","options":["8 to 16 months","4 to 14 days","1 to 3 months","3 to 21 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the incubation period of JE is 4 to 14 days.  Why A wrong: 8 to 16 months is for filariasis.  Why C wrong: 1 to 3 months is for rabies.  Why D wrong: 3 to 21 days is for tetanus."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Regarding man-to-man transmission of Japanese encephalitis, which statement is correct?","options":["It occurs by droplet spread","It has not been recorded","It occurs through blood transfusion","It occurs via the fecal-oral route"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source clearly states man-to-man transmission of JE has not been recorded.  Why A/C/D wrong: None of these routes are described for JE; transmission is via infected mosquito bite only."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the recommended prevention for Japanese encephalitis?","options":["DEC","JE vaccine","MDT","ARV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists JE vaccine as prevention for Japanese encephalitis.  Why A wrong: DEC is for filariasis.  Why C wrong: MDT is for leprosy.  Why D wrong: ARV (anti-rabies vaccine) is for rabies."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the treatment approach for Japanese encephalitis?","options":["Multi drug therapy","Diethylcarbamazine citrate","There is no treatment; only supportive treatment","Penicillin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there is no treatment for JE; only supportive treatment is available.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are specific treatments for leprosy, filariasis, and tetanus respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Leishmaniasis is caused by a parasite of which genus?","options":["Plasmodium","Leishmania","Trypanosoma","Entamoeba"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states leishmaniasis is a protozoal disease caused by a parasite of the genus Leishmania.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other protozoa not named as the cause of leishmaniasis in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Leishmaniasis is transmitted by which vector?","options":["Culex mosquito","Tsetse fly","Sandfly","Tick"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states leishmaniasis is transmitted by the Sandfly (specifically the female sandfly, person to person).  Why A wrong: Culex transmits filariasis and JE.  Why B/D wrong: Tsetse fly and tick are not the leishmaniasis vector per the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Visceral leishmaniasis is also known by which name?","options":["Kala Azar","Henson disease","Hydrophobia","Chancroid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states visceral leishmaniasis is also known as Kala Azar.  Why B wrong: Henson disease refers to leprosy in the source.  Why C wrong: Hydrophobia is rabies.  Why D wrong: Chancroid is a soft-sore STD caused by Haemophilus ducreyi."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the causative agent of Kala Azar?","options":["Leishmania donovani","Leishmania tropica","Leishmania major","Trypanosoma cruzi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Leishmania donovani is the causative agent of kala azar.  Why B/C wrong: These other Leishmania species are not named as the kala azar agent in the source.  Why D wrong: Trypanosoma cruzi causes Chagas disease, not kala azar."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In leishmaniasis, the sole reservoir of infection is:","options":["Pigs","Man","Dogs","Wild rodents"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states man is the sole reservoir of infection for leishmaniasis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the reservoir in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the incubation period of leishmaniasis?","options":["1 to 4 months (generally 10 days–2 years)","3 to 21 days","2 to 7 days","8 to 16 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the incubation period of leishmaniasis as 1 to 4 months (generally 10 days to 2 years).  Why B wrong: 3 to 21 days is tetanus.  Why C wrong: 2 to 7 days is SARS.  Why D wrong: 8 to 16 months is filariasis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which clinical feature is characteristic of leishmaniasis (kala azar)?","options":["Elephantiasis","Splenomegaly and hepatomegaly with resulting anaemia and weight loss","Lockjaw","Leonine facies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists fever, splenomegaly and hepatomegaly resulting in anaemia and weight loss as clinical measures of leishmaniasis.  Why A wrong: Elephantiasis is filariasis.  Why C wrong: Lockjaw is tetanus.  Why D wrong: Leonine facies is leprosy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which insecticide is mentioned as a control measure for leishmaniasis?","options":["Malathion","DDT","Pyrethrum","Temephos"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states DDT is used as an insecticide for control of leishmaniasis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These insecticides are not named in the source for leishmaniasis control."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Leprosy is also known by which name?","options":["Henson disease","Kala Azar","Hydrophobia","Venereal disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states leprosy is a chronic contagious disease also known as Henson disease.  Why B wrong: Kala azar is visceral leishmaniasis.  Why C wrong: Hydrophobia is rabies.  Why D wrong: Venereal disease refers to STDs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"\"Leonine facies\" (lion-like face) is a facies seen in which disease?","options":["Tetanus","Leprosy","Rabies","Filariasis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states leonine facies (lion-like face) is seen in leprosy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These diseases are not associated with leonine facies in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which diagnostic test is used for leprosy?","options":["Lepromin test","Western Blot test","ELISA","Widal test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the diagnosis of leprosy is by the Lepromin test.  Why B/C wrong: Western Blot and ELISA are used for HIV/AIDS.  Why D wrong: Widal test is not mentioned for leprosy in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the causative agent of leprosy?","options":["Clostridium tetani","Mycobacterium leprae, an acid-fast bacteria","Lyssa virus type 1","Leishmania donovani"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the causative agent of leprosy is Mycobacterium leprae, an acid-fast bacteria.  Why A wrong: Clostridium tetani causes tetanus.  Why C wrong: Lyssa virus type 1 causes rabies.  Why D wrong: Leishmania donovani causes kala azar."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Leprosy mostly affects which structures of the body?","options":["Only the lungs","Peripheral nerves; also skin, muscles, bone, testes and internal organs","Only the liver and spleen","Only the central nervous system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states leprosy mostly affects peripheral nerves and also affects skin, muscles, bone, testes and internal organs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the organs described for leprosy in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the infective material in leprosy?","options":["Saliva of infected animals","Nose and throat secretion","Blood and semen","Soil and street dust"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the infective material in leprosy is nose and throat secretion.  Why A wrong: Saliva of infected rabid animals is the infective material in rabies.  Why C wrong: Blood and semen are the infective material in AIDS.  Why D wrong: Soil and street dust are sources of infection for tetanus."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of leprosy?","options":["Contact transmission and droplet transmission","Bite of infected mosquito","Sexual contact","Bite of sandfly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the mode of transmission of leprosy is contact transmission and droplet transmission.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are transmission modes of mosquito-borne diseases, STDs, and leishmaniasis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the standard treatment for leprosy, and what is its drug of choice?","options":["MDT (multi drug therapy); Dapsone","ARV; penicillin","Supportive only; no drug","DEC; DDT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states treatment of leprosy is multi drug therapy (MDT) and the drug of choice is Dapsone.  Why B wrong: ARV/penicillin relate to rabies/tetanus.  Why C wrong: Leprosy has a specific treatment (MDT).  Why D wrong: DEC/DDT relate to filariasis and leishmaniasis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Tetanus is caused by the exotoxin of which organism?","options":["Clostridium tetani","Mycobacterium leprae","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Treponema pallidum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states tetanus is an acute disease caused by the exotoxin of Clostridium tetani (also listed as the causative agent, FAQs).  Why B wrong: M. leprae causes leprosy.  Why C wrong: N. gonorrhoeae causes gonorrhoea.  Why D wrong: T. pallidum causes syphilis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the source of infection for tetanus?","options":["Nose and throat secretions","Soil, street dust, horse and cow dung","Blood and semen","Saliva of infected animals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the source of infection for tetanus is soil, street dust, horse and cow dung.  Why A wrong: These are the infective material in leprosy.  Why C wrong: These are the infective material in AIDS.  Why D wrong: Saliva of infected animals is for rabies."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of tetanus?","options":["Sexual contact","Injuries getting contaminated with tetanus spores","Mosquito bite","Droplet transmission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states most patients acquire tetanus as a result of injuries getting contaminated with tetanus spores.  Why A/C/D wrong: These transmission modes do not apply to tetanus per the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the incubation period of tetanus?","options":["3 to 21 days","8 to 16 months","1 to 3 months","2 to 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the incubation period of tetanus as 3 to 21 days.  Why B wrong: 8 to 16 months is filariasis.  Why C wrong: 1 to 3 months is rabies.  Why D wrong: 2 to 7 days is SARS."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the drug of choice for tetanus, and how is it prevented?","options":["Dapsone; MDT","Penicillin; immunization (active and passive)","DEC; JE vaccine","ARV; wound care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for tetanus is penicillin and prevention is by immunization (active and passive).  Why A wrong: Dapsone/MDT are for leprosy.  Why C wrong: DEC is for filariasis and JE vaccine for encephalitis.  Why D wrong: ARV is for rabies."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following is a cardinal sign of tetanus?","options":["Leonine facies","Opisthotonos position, risus sardonicus and lockjaw (reflex trismus)","Hydrophobia","Elephantiasis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" box states opisthotonos position, risus sardonicus (mouth kept slightly open) and lockjaw (reflex trismus) are cardinal signs of tetanus.  Why A wrong: Leonine facies is leprosy.  Why C wrong: Hydrophobia is rabies.  Why D wrong: Elephantiasis is filariasis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Muscle rigidity in tetanus characteristically involves which muscles?","options":["Masseters, abdominal muscles and spine muscles","Only the diaphragm","Only the limb flexors","Only the ocular muscles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists clinical features of tetanus as fever and muscle rigidity of the masseters, abdominal muscles and spine muscles.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific muscle groups are not what the source describes for tetanus rigidity."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Rabies is also known as which condition, reflecting fear of water?","options":["Hydrophobia","Photophobia","Kala Azar","Henson disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states rabies is also known as hydrophobia (fear of water) — FAQs.  Why B wrong: Photophobia (intolerance of bright light) is a symptom but not the alternative name.  Why C wrong: Kala azar is leishmaniasis.  Why D wrong: Henson disease is leprosy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the causative agent of rabies?","options":["Lyssa virus type 1","Corona virus","Human immunodeficiency virus","Group B arbovirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the causative agent of rabies is Lyssa virus type 1.  Why B wrong: Corona virus causes SARS.  Why C wrong: HIV causes AIDS.  Why D wrong: Group B arbovirus causes Japanese encephalitis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the infective material in rabies?","options":["Saliva of the infected rabid animal, which contains the virus","Nose and throat secretions","Blood and semen","Cervical mucus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the infective material in rabies is the saliva of the infected rabid animal, which contains the virus.  Why B wrong: Nose and throat secretions are the leprosy infective material.  Why C wrong: Blood and semen are the AIDS infective material.  Why D wrong: Cervical mucus is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of rabies, and what is its source of infection?","options":["Bite of infected animals; animal bite like dog, monkey etc.","Sexual contact; prostitutes","Mosquito bite; pigs","Droplet transmission; humans"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states rabies is transmitted by the bite of infected animals, with the source of infection being animal bite such as dog, monkey etc.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the transmission route for rabies described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the incubation period of rabies?","options":["1 to 3 months and sometimes years","2 to 7 days","3 to 21 days","8 to 16 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the incubation period of rabies is 1 to 3 months and sometimes years.  Why B wrong: 2 to 7 days is SARS.  Why C wrong: 3 to 21 days is tetanus.  Why D wrong: 8 to 16 months is filariasis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which classical clinical features appear in the later stage of rabies?","options":["Intolerance of noise and bright light, difficulty in swallowing, fear of water and intense spasms when offered food or fluids","Elephantiasis of the legs","Splenomegaly and hepatomegaly","Leonine facies and skin patches"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states later patients show classical symptoms such as intolerance of noise and bright light, difficulty in swallowing, fear of water and intense spasms when offered food or fluids.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are features of filariasis, leishmaniasis, and leprosy respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In rabies, death occurs due to which mechanism, and what mortality rate does the source cite?","options":["Respiratory paralysis; mortality rate 95%–100%","Renal failure; mortality 50%","Cardiac arrest; mortality 10%","Hepatic failure; mortality 30%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states death occurs due to respiratory paralysis, the disease always ends with death, and mortality rate is 95%–100%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms and rates are not stated for rabies."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the recommended prevention for rabies?","options":["ARV (anti-rabies vaccine)","Dapsone","Penicillin","JE vaccine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists ARV as the prevention for rabies.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are treatments/preventions for leprosy, tetanus, and JE respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In wound care after an animal bite, what is the 1st priority step?","options":["Suture the wound immediately","Wash the bitten part with soap and running water for 10–15 minutes","Apply antiseptic solution first","Start anti-rabies treatment before cleaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the bitten part should be washed with soap and running water for 10–15 minutes as the 1st priority.  Why A wrong: The source explicitly says the bitten wound should NOT be sutured and kept open.  Why C wrong: Applying antiseptic (Betadine, Spirit) comes after washing.  Why D wrong: Observation and treatment decisions follow the washing step."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to wound care after an animal bite, the bitten wound should be:","options":["Sutured tightly","Not sutured and kept open","Bandaged airtight","Cauterized"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the bitten wound should not be sutured and should be kept open.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's instruction to keep the wound open."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"After an animal bite, for how many days should the animal be observed, per the source?","options":["5 days","10 days","21 days","3 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to observe the animal for 10 days; if it dies within 10 days or shows signs of illness, anti-rabies treatment should be started immediately; if the animal is healthy at the end of 10 days then no post-exposure prophylaxis is needed.  Why A/C/D wrong: The observation window given is 10 days, not these values."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) are also known as:","options":["Venereal disease","Zoonotic disease","Non-communicable disease","Vector-borne disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states STDs are also known as venereal disease.  Why B wrong: Zoonotic diseases are transmitted from animals (e.g., JE).  Why C wrong: STDs are communicable, not non-communicable.  Why D wrong: STDs are transmitted by sexual contact, not by a vector."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the basic social factor most responsible for the spread of STDs?","options":["Poverty","Prostitution","Alcoholism","Broken homes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states STDs are often called social diseases and the basic social factor responsible for their spread is prostitution.  Why A/C/D wrong: Poverty, alcoholism and broken homes are listed as other contributory factors, not the basic factor."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the STD table, which organism causes gonorrhoea?","options":["Chlamydia trachomatis","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Treponema pallidum","Haemophilus ducreyi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The STD table lists Neisseria gonorrhoeae as producing gonorrhoea.  Why A wrong: Chlamydia trachomatis produces genital chlamydial infection and lymphogranuloma venereum.  Why C wrong: Treponema pallidum causes syphilis.  Why D wrong: Haemophilus ducreyi causes chancroid."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which organism causes syphilis according to the STD table?","options":["Treponema pallidum","Klebsiella granulomatis","Gardnerella vaginalis","Trichomonas vaginalis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The STD table lists Treponema pallidum as the cause of syphilis.  Why B wrong: Klebsiella granulomatis causes granuloma inguinale.  Why C wrong: Gardnerella vaginalis causes bacterial vaginosis.  Why D wrong: Trichomonas vaginalis causes trichomoniasis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Chancroid (soft sore) is caused by which organism?","options":["Haemophilus ducreyi","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Chlamydia trachomatis","Herpes simplex I, II"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The STD table lists Haemophilus ducreyi as the cause of chancroid (soft sore).  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause gonorrhoea, chlamydial infections, and genital herpes respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Granuloma inguinale is produced by which organism?","options":["Klebsiella granulomatis","Gardnerella vaginalis","Treponema pallidum","Papillomaviruses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The STD table lists Klebsiella granulomatis as causing granuloma inguinale.  Why B wrong: Gardnerella vaginalis causes bacterial vaginosis.  Why C wrong: Treponema pallidum causes syphilis.  Why D wrong: Papillomaviruses cause ano-genital warts."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Bacterial vaginosis is caused by which organism in the STD table?","options":["Gardnerella vaginalis","Trichomonas vaginalis","Chlamydia trachomatis","Haemophilus ducreyi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The STD table lists Gardnerella vaginalis as the cause of bacterial vaginosis.  Why B wrong: Trichomonas vaginalis causes trichomoniasis.  Why C wrong: Chlamydia trachomatis causes chlamydial infection.  Why D wrong: Haemophilus ducreyi causes chancroid."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the STD classification table, which type of organism causes genital herpes and ano-genital warts?","options":["Viral","Bacterial","Protozoa","Insects"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table classifies Herpes simplex I, II (genital herpes) and Papillomaviruses (ano-genital warts) under Viral.  Why B wrong: Bacterial types include chlamydia, gonorrhoea, syphilis, chancroid, granuloma inguinale, bacterial vaginosis.  Why C wrong: Protozoa refers to Trichomonas vaginalis (trichomoniasis).  Why D wrong: Insects refers to Pediculosis pubis (pediculosis)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Trichomoniasis is caused by which organism, and which class does it belong to in the STD table?","options":["Trichomonas vaginalis; Protozoa","Gardnerella vaginalis; Bacterial","Pediculosis pubis; Insects","Herpes simplex; Viral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The STD table lists Trichomonas vaginalis (Protozoa) as causing trichomoniasis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are misclassifications; the table clearly places Trichomonas under Protozoa."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the STD table, pediculosis is caused by which agent classified under \"Insects\"?","options":["Pediculosis pubis (phthirus pubis)","Trichomonas vaginalis","Klebsiella granulomatis","Hepatitis virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The STD table lists Pediculosis pubis (phthirus pubis), classified under Insects, as causing pediculosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are protozoal, bacterial, and viral agents respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the STD table, Hepatitis A, B & C infection belongs to which class?","options":["Viral","Bacterial","Protozoa","Insects"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table classifies Hepatitis (A, B & C infection) under Viral.  Why B/C/D wrong: Hepatitis is a viral STD in the table, not bacterial, protozoal, or insect-borne."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What does the abbreviation AIDS stand for?","options":["Acquired Immuno Deficiency Syndrome","Autoimmune Immune Deficiency Syndrome","Acquired Infectious Disease Syndrome","Active Immune Depletion Syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the full form of AIDS as Acquired Immuno Deficiency Syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions not given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In India, the first case of AIDS was reported in which year and place?","options":["1986 from Chennai, Tamil Nadu (and Pushkar, Ajmer, Rajasthan)","1992 from New Delhi","1981 from Mumbai","1978 from Kolkata"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the first case of AIDS in India was reported in 1986 from Chennai, Tamil Nadu and in Rajasthan at Pushkar, Ajmer.  Why B wrong: 1992 New Delhi is when NACO was established.  Why C/D wrong: These years/places are not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the causative agent of AIDS?","options":["Human immune deficiency virus (HIV)","Corona virus","Lyssa virus","Arbovirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the causative agent of AIDS is human immune deficiency virus (HIV).  Why B wrong: Corona virus causes SARS.  Why C wrong: Lyssa virus causes rabies.  Why D wrong: Arbovirus causes JE."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the infective material in AIDS?","options":["Blood and semen of the infected person","Nose and throat secretions","Saliva of infected animals","Soil and dust"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the infective material in AIDS is the blood and semen of the infected person.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are infective materials/sources for leprosy, rabies, and tetanus respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which is listed as the most common mode of transmission of AIDS?","options":["Sexual contact","Through blood","Perinatal transmission","Mosquito bite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the mode of transmission of AIDS is sexual contact (most common), through blood, and perinatal transmission.  Why B/C wrong: These are transmission routes but not the most common one.  Why D wrong: AIDS is not transmitted by mosquito bite."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are listed as high risk groups for AIDS? (Select all that apply)","options":["Prostitutes","Homosexuals","IV drug users","Pregnant women only","Newborns only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists prostitutes, homosexuals, and IV drug users as high risk groups for AIDS.  Why D/E wrong: Pregnant women and newborns are not named as the high risk groups in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"NACO (National AIDS Control Organisation) was established by MOHFW, Govt. of India in which year and city?","options":["1992, at New Delhi","1986, at Chennai","1978, at Mumbai","2002, at New Delhi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states NACO was established by MOHFW, Govt. of India in 1992 at New Delhi.  Why B wrong: 1986 Chennai is the first AIDS case in India.  Why C/D wrong: These years/places are not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the full form of ELISA, and what is its role in HIV/AIDS testing?","options":["Enzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay; screening test for AIDS/HIV","Enzyme Linked Immune System Assay; confirmatory test","Electrophoretic Linked Immuno Study Assay; treatment monitoring","Enzyme Labelled Immuno Serum Analysis; not used in HIV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ELISA (Enzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay) is a screening test for AIDS/HIV.  Why B/C/D wrong: The correct expansion is Enzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay and it is a screening test, not confirmatory."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which is the confirmatory test for HIV/AIDS in adults?","options":["Western Blot test","ELISA","Lepromin test","Pearl index"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Western Blot test is the confirmatory test for HIV/AIDS in adults. [Note: Western Blot is the classic confirmatory test taught for Indian nursing exams and remains the keyed answer. Current CDC/NACO algorithms have largely replaced Western Blot with an HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation immunoassay (and, in India, a 3-ELISA/rapid-test strategy); the exam answer is still Western Blot.]  Why B wrong: ELISA is a screening test.  Why C wrong: Lepromin test is for leprosy.  Why D wrong: Pearl index measures contraceptive failure."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In children, which test is considered the confirmatory diagnostic test for HIV/AIDS?","options":["CD4 & P24 antigen test","Western Blot test","ELISA","Lepromin test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CD4 & P24 antigen test is considered the confirmatory diagnostic test in children. [Note: source prints \"CD4 & P24 antigen\"; per NACO, infants <18 months are diagnosed by virological testing — HIV DNA PCR (nucleic acid test), with p24 antigen as an alternative; CD4 count is used for staging/monitoring, not diagnosis. Keyed A to match the source/exam.]  Why B wrong: Western Blot is confirmatory in adults.  Why C wrong: ELISA is a screening test.  Why D wrong: Lepromin test is for leprosy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the causative organism of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS)?","options":["A new strain of corona virus (single stranded RNA virus)","HIV","Arbovirus","Lyssa virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states SARS is caused by a new strain of corona virus, a single-stranded RNA virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause AIDS, JE, and rabies respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the incubation period of SARS?","options":["2 to 7 days","3 to 21 days","1 to 3 months","8 to 16 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the incubation period of SARS as 2 to 7 days.  Why B wrong: 3 to 21 days is tetanus.  Why C wrong: 1 to 3 months is rabies.  Why D wrong: 8 to 16 months is filariasis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of SARS?","options":["Close contact with the patient's infected material","Bite of infected mosquito","Sexual contact","Contaminated soil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states SARS is transmitted by close contact with the patient's infected material.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are transmission modes for other diseases, not SARS."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which are listed as complications of SARS?","options":["Pulmonary de-compensation and ARDS","Elephantiasis","Splenomegaly","Lockjaw"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pulmonary de-compensation and ARDS as complications of SARS.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are features of filariasis, leishmaniasis, and tetanus respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Non-communicable diseases are best defined as diseases that:","options":["Are transmitted by airborne, vehicle-borne, mosquito-borne, or fomite-borne routes","Are not transmitted through communicable routes such as airborne, vehicle-borne, mosquito-borne or fomite-borne","Are always caused by bacteria","Are only seen in children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines non-communicable diseases as the type not transmitted through communicable routes like airborne, vehicle-borne, mosquito-borne, fomite-borne etc.  Why A wrong: This describes communicable transmission, the opposite.  Why C/D wrong: These are not part of the source definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are examples of non-communicable diseases given in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Coronary artery disease","Hypertension and Diabetes","Cancer, Stroke/CVA, Blindness","Tetanus","Accidents & injury"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists coronary artery disease, hypertension, diabetes, accidents & injury, cancer, stroke or CVA, and blindness as examples of non-communicable diseases.  Why D wrong: Tetanus is a communicable disease, not listed among the NCD examples."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the \"Risk Factors of Non-Communicable Diseases\" table, which are the non-modifiable risk factors for hypertension?","options":["Genetic factors and sex/ethnicity","Obesity and high salt intake","High alcohol intake","Sedentary lifestyle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists genetic factors and sex/ethnicity as non-modifiable risk factors for hypertension.  Why B/C/D wrong: Obesity, high salt intake, high alcohol intake and sedentary lifestyle are listed as modifiable risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are modifiable risk factors for hypertension per the table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Obesity","High salt intake","Increased saturated fat and decreased dietary fiber intake","High alcohol intake and sedentary lifestyle","Genetic factors"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists obesity, high salt intake, increased saturated fat, decreased dietary fiber intake, high alcohol intake and sedentary lifestyle as modifiable risk factors for hypertension.  Why E wrong: Genetic factors are non-modifiable."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"For coronary heart disease, which are the non-modifiable risk factors listed?","options":["Age, sex, family history, genetic factors","Cigarette smoking and high BP","Elevated serum cholesterol and diabetes","Obesity and stress"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists age, sex, family history and genetic factors as non-modifiable risk factors for coronary heart disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: Cigarette smoking, high BP, elevated serum cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, sedentary lifestyle and stress are the modifiable factors."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are modifiable risk factors for coronary heart disease per the table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cigarette smoking","High BP and elevated serum cholesterol level","Diabetes and obesity","Sedentary lifestyle and stress","Age and sex"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists cigarette smoking, high BP, elevated serum cholesterol level, diabetes, obesity, sedentary lifestyle and stress as modifiable risk factors for CHD.  Why E wrong: Age and sex are non-modifiable."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the table, what is the most important modifiable risk factor for stroke?","options":["Hypertension","Diabetes","Smoking","Obesity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the most important modifiable risk factor for stroke is hypertension.  Why B/C/D wrong: Diabetes, smoking and obesity are listed as risk factors but hypertension is specified as the most important."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are listed as risk factors for stroke in the table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hypertension and cardiac abnormality","Diabetes and elevated blood lipid","Glucose intolerance and obesity","Smoking","Copper allergy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists hypertension, cardiac abnormality, diabetes, elevated blood lipid, glucose intolerance, obesity and smoking as risk factors for stroke.  Why E wrong: Copper allergy is a contraindication for Cu-T IUD, not a stroke risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the WHO definition, family planning allows individuals to:","options":["Anticipate and attain their desired number of children and the spacing and timing of their birth","Only prevent all pregnancies permanently","Treat only communicable diseases","Increase fertility rates in a population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the WHO definition: family planning allows individuals to anticipate and attain their desired number of children and the spacing and timing of their birth.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the WHO definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Family planning is achieved through the use of contraceptive methods and the treatment of:","options":["Involuntary infertility","Sexually transmitted diseases","Non-communicable diseases","Puerperal sepsis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states family planning is achieved through the use of contraceptive methods and the treatment of involuntary infertility.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not part of the family planning definition in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Under the Child Marriage Restraint Act-1929, what was the legal age for marriage?","options":["14 years for girls and 18 years for boys","18 years for girls and 21 years for boys","21 years for both girls and boys","16 years for girls and 18 years for boys"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states under the Child Marriage Restraint Act-1929 the legal marriage age was 14 years for girls and 18 years for boys.  Why B wrong: 18 (girls)/21 (boys) came after the 1978 amendment.  Why C wrong: 21 for both is the proposed change pending in Parliament.  Why D wrong: These values are not given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"After the 1978 amendment (Child Marriage Restraints (amended) Act-1978), the decided legal age for marriage became:","options":["18 years for girls and 21 years for boys","14 years for girls and 18 years for boys","21 years for both","16 years for girls and 21 years for boys"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the 1978 amended act decided the legal marriage age as 18 years for girls and 21 years for boys.  Why B wrong: These were the 1929 ages.  Why C wrong: 21 for both is the pending proposed change.  Why D wrong: Not given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"As per the source (till July 2022), what proposed change to the legal marriage age was pending in Parliament?","options":["21 years for both girls and boys","18 years for both","25 years for boys","16 years for girls"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Govt. of India is going to change the legal age for marriage to 21 years for both girls and boys, but till July 2022 it was still pending in Parliament to become an act.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures are not the proposed change stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"How is contraception defined in the source?","options":["The intentional prevention of conception through the use of various devices, sexual practices, chemicals, drugs, or surgical procedures","The treatment of infertility","The promotion of conception","Prevention of sexually transmitted diseases only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines contraception as the intentional prevention of conception through the use of various devices, sexual practices, chemicals, drugs, or surgical procedures.  Why B/C wrong: Contraception prevents (not promotes/treats) conception.  Why D wrong: STD prevention is a benefit of some methods, not the definition of contraception."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Pregnancy (contraceptive) failure rate is calculated by which index?","options":["Pearl index","Apgar index","Body mass index","Ponderal index"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pregnancy failure rate is calculated by the Pearl index.  Why B/C/D wrong: These indices are unrelated to contraceptive failure rate."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are terminal (permanent) contraceptive methods? (Select all that apply)","options":["Male sterilisation (vasectomy)","Female sterilisation (tubectomy)","IUCD","Condom","Rhythm method"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source classifies male sterilisation (vasectomy) and female sterilisation (tubectomy) as terminal (permanent) methods.  Why C/D/E wrong: IUCD, condom, and rhythm method are spacing (temporary) methods."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are spacing (temporary) contraceptive methods listed in the classification? (Select all that apply)","options":["Natural method (withdrawal, rhythm)","Barrier methods (condom, diaphragm)","Intrauterine devices (IUCD)","Hormonal method (Mala-N & Mala-D)","Vasectomy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The classification lists natural methods, barrier methods, intrauterine devices, hormonal methods (Mala-N & Mala-D), post-contraceptive methods and miscellaneous as spacing (temporary) methods.  Why E wrong: Vasectomy is a terminal (permanent) method."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the safe period (rhythm/calendar) method, the fertile period is calculated as:","options":["Shortest cycle minus 18 days (first fertile day) and longest cycle minus 11 days (last fertile day)","Shortest cycle minus 11 days and longest cycle minus 18 days","Shortest cycle minus 7 days and longest cycle minus 7 days","Longest cycle minus 21 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the fertile period is shortest cycle minus 18 days (last day of fertile period... first fertile day) and longest cycle minus 11 days. (The source phrasing pairs \"minus 18\" and \"minus 11\"; the standard rule keys shortest−18 as first fertile day and longest−11 as last fertile day.)  Why B wrong: The subtractions are reversed.  Why C wrong: 7 days before/after refers to the general safe period statement, not the fertile-day calculation.  Why D wrong: Not the formula given."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"As per the rhythm method, how many days before and after menses (in regular cycles) is considered the safe period?","options":["7 days before and 7 days after","5 days before and 5 days after","10 days before and 10 days after","3 days before and 3 days after"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that as per this method, 7 days before and 7 days after in regular menses is considered the safe period.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals are not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"The rhythm (calendar) method is described as suitable for which couples?","options":["Only educated couples with high motivation","All couples regardless of motivation","Only postnatal women","Couples with irregular cycles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the rhythm method is only suitable for educated couples with high motivation and is difficult to predict in irregular cycles; not useful in the postnatal period.  Why B wrong: It requires high motivation and education.  Why C wrong: The source says it is not useful in the postnatal period.  Why D wrong: It is difficult to predict the safe period in irregular cycles."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the high failure rate quoted for the rhythm/safe period method?","options":["24 per HWY","5 per HWY","100 per HWY","1 per HWY"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the safe period method has a high failure rate of 24 per HWY (Hundred Woman Years).  Why B/C/D wrong: These failure rates are not given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the calendar method example, a shortest cycle of 27 days gives which first fertile day?","options":["27 − 18 = 9th day (first fertile day is the 9th day from the first day of the menstrual cycle)","27 − 11 = 16th day","27 − 7 = 20th day","27 − 21 = 6th day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's worked example shows shortest cycle 27 days: 27 − 18 = 09 days, so the first fertile day is the 9th day from the first day of the menstrual cycle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These calculations do not match the source's example for the shortest cycle."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the calendar method example, a longest cycle of 30 days gives which fertile day?","options":["30 − 11 = 19th day (first fertile day is the 19th day from the first day of the menstrual cycle)","30 − 18 = 12th day","30 − 7 = 23rd day","30 − 21 = 9th day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's worked example shows longest cycle 30 days: 30 − 11 = 19 days, so the fertile day is the 19th day from the first day of the menstrual cycle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's calculation for the longest cycle."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the Basal Body Temperature (BBT) method, by how much and when does body temperature rise?","options":["Raised 0.3°–0.5°C at or following ovulation due to increased progesterone production","Raised 1.0°–2.0°C before menstruation","Falls 0.3°–0.5°C at ovulation","Raised 0.3°–0.5°C due to estrogen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states body temperature is usually raised (0.3°–0.5°C) at or following ovulation due to increased progesterone production.  Why B/C wrong: The change is a rise of 0.3–0.5°C at/after ovulation, not these values.  Why D wrong: The rise is due to progesterone, not estrogen."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"When should BBT be measured for the temperature method?","options":["Before getting out of bed in the morning (preferably)","After exercise in the evening","Immediately after meals","Any time during the day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BBT measurement should be done before getting out of bed in the morning (preferably).  Why B/C/D wrong: These timings would be inaccurate for basal temperature."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"The Cervical Mucus Method of natural family planning is also known as:","options":["Billing's Method or Ovulation Method","Symptothermic Method","Calendar Method","Coitus interruptus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cervical mucus method is also known as Billing's Method or Ovulation Method, based on changes in the characteristics of cervical mucus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate natural methods."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the cervical mucus method, at the time of ovulation the cervical mucus is:","options":["Watery, clear, smooth, slippery and profuse (like egg white)","Thick and scanty","Absent","Bloody"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states at ovulation the mucus is watery, clear, smooth, slippery, profuse (like egg white), and after ovulation it thickens and lessens in quantity.  Why B wrong: Thick and scanty describes mucus after ovulation.  Why C/D wrong: Not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the cervical mucus (Billing's) method, what happens to the mucus after ovulation?","options":["It thickens and lessens in quantity","It becomes watery and profuse","It stays like egg white","It becomes bloody"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states after ovulation the cervical mucus thickens and lessens in quantity.  Why B/C wrong: These describe mucus at ovulation.  Why D wrong: Not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the Billing's cervical mucus method, what technique is used to assess the mucus?","options":["Tissue paper is used to wipe off the inside of the vagina","A blood test","Ultrasound","Urine dipstick"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the method uses tissue paper to wipe off the inside of the vagina and requires a high degree of motivation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the technique described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"The Symptothermic method of natural family planning combines which techniques, and how does its effectiveness compare to Billing's method?","options":["Combines temperature, cervical mucus and calendar techniques; more effective than Billing's method","Combines only temperature and calendar; less effective than Billing's","Uses only cervical mucus; equally effective","Uses hormonal pills; more effective"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the symptothermic method combines temperature, cervical mucus and calendar techniques and is more effective than Billing's method.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the components or effectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the source, which is the only method of birth control that is completely effective?","options":["Sexual abstinence","Coitus interruptus","IUCD","Oral contraceptive pills"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sexual abstinence is the only method of birth control which is completely effective.  Why B/C/D wrong: These methods have failure rates and are not described as completely effective."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which method is described as the oldest method of voluntary fertility control?","options":["Coitus interruptus (withdrawal)","IUCD","Lactation amenorrhoea method","Calendar method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states coitus interruptus is the oldest method of voluntary fertility control.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the oldest method."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"For the Lactation Amenorrhoea Method (LAM), it is a good method for natural conception (contraception) under exclusive breastfeeding for up to how long?","options":["Up to 6 months in lactating women","Up to 12 months","Up to 3 months","Up to 24 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LAM is a good method under exclusive breastfeeding up to 6 months in lactating women.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations are not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the natural/biological methods summary table, which methods are classified under \"Biological methods\"?","options":["Calendar/rhythm method, BBT, ovulation method, Billing method (cervical mucous method)","Abstinence, withdrawal, coitus interruptus, LAM","Creams, jelly and suppositories, foam","Male and female condoms, diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists biological methods as calendar/rhythm method, basal body temperature (BBT), ovulation method, and billing method (cervical mucous method).  Why B wrong: Abstinence, withdrawal, coitus interruptus and LAM are the natural methods.  Why C wrong: Creams, jelly, suppositories and foam are chemical methods.  Why D wrong: Condoms, diaphragm etc. are barrier methods."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the methods summary table, chemical methods of contraception include:","options":["Creams, jelly and suppositories, foam","Calendar or rhythm method","Male and female condoms","BBT and ovulation method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists chemical methods as creams, jelly and suppositories, and foam.  Why B/D wrong: These are biological methods.  Why C wrong: Condoms are barrier methods."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the methods summary table, barrier methods include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Male condoms","Female condoms","Diaphragm and cervical cap","Contraceptive sponge","Foam and jelly"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists barrier methods as male and female condoms, diaphragm, cervical cap and contraceptive sponge.  Why E wrong: Foam and jelly are listed as chemical methods."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"The vaginal contraceptive sponge (\"Today\") is made of polyurethane impregnated with how much of which spermicide?","options":["1000 mg of Nonoxynol-9","500 mg of Betadine","60 mg of ferrous fumarate","52 mg of levonorgestrel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the sponge is made of polyurethane impregnated with 1000 mg of Nonoxynol-9 as a spermicide (which kills sperm).  Why B wrong: Betadine is an antiseptic, not the spermicide in the sponge.  Why C wrong: 60 mg ferrous fumarate is in OCP iron pills.  Why D wrong: 52 mg levonorgestrel is in the LNG-IUS."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"After ejaculation, for how long should the \"Today\" sponge be left in place inside the vagina, and for how many hours does it provide protection?","options":["Left in place for 6 hours after ejaculation; provides protection for 24 hours","Left in place for 24 hours; provides protection for 6 hours","Left for 48 hours; protects for 12 hours","Left for 3 hours; protects for 8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the sponge should be left in place for 6 hours inside the vagina after ejaculation, and while in place it provides protection against pregnancy for 24 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the times given."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the stated effectiveness of the \"Today\" contraceptive sponge when used as instructed?","options":["89–91% effective","95–100% effective","100% effective","50% effective"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the sponge is 89–91% effective if used as instructed.  Why B wrong: 95–100% is the rabies mortality rate, unrelated.  Why C wrong: 100% is claimed for OCPs taken correctly, not the sponge.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Mechanical (barrier) contraceptive methods have the additional advantage of preventing:","options":["Sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) and PIDs","Non-communicable diseases","Tetanus","Nothing besides pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states mechanical (barrier) methods prevent pregnancy along with sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) and PIDs (marked \"very Imp.\").  Why B/C wrong: These are not prevented by barrier methods.  Why D wrong: Barriers do additionally protect against STDs/PIDs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Regarding the diaphragm, it must be worn before how long of sex and remain inside the vagina for how long after?","options":["Worn before 3 hours of sex; remain inside 6 to 8 hours after sex","Worn before 6 hours; remain 3 hours after","Worn before 1 hour; remain 48 hours after","Worn before 24 hours; remain 24 hours after"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the diaphragm is worn with spermicidal jelly, must be worn before 3 hours of sex, and must remain inside the vagina 6 to 8 hours after sex.  Why B/C/D wrong: These times do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"For how long should the cervical cap remain in position after intercourse/ejaculation?","options":["Minimum 48 hours","Minimum 6 hours","Minimum 24 hours","Minimum 3 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cervical cap should remain in position minimum 48 hours after intercourse/ejaculation, and spermicidal jelly should be used for maximum effect.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"The contraceptive sponge should remain in position for a minimum of how long after intercourse (per the barrier methods section)?","options":["Minimum 48 hours","Minimum 6 hours","Minimum 24 hours","Minimum 12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the contraceptive sponge should also remain in position minimum 48 hours after intercourse.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"An IUCD is best described as:","options":["A small T-shaped contraceptive device placed in a woman's uterus, also known as the IUD, loop or copper coil","A hormonal pill taken orally","A barrier worn over the penis","A surgical sterilisation procedure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an IUCD is a small T-shaped contraceptive device placed in a woman's uterus, also known as the IUD, loop or copper coil.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe OCPs, condoms, and sterilisation, not IUCDs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the mode of action of an IUCD?","options":["It works by stopping sperm from reaching an egg","It suppresses ovulation via estrogen","It creates a physical barrier over the cervix","It permanently blocks the fallopian tubes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an IUCD works by stopping sperm reaching an egg.  Why B wrong: Ovulation suppression is the OCP mechanism.  Why C/D wrong: These describe barrier and sterilisation methods."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Lippe's loop, a first-generation (non-medicated) IUD, is described as:","options":["A double S-shaped device made of polythene containing a small amount of barium sulphate for X-ray observation","A copper-containing T-shaped device","A hormonal levonorgestrel-releasing device","A ring made of silicone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Lippe's loop is a double S-shaped device made of polythene, contains a small amount of barium sulphate for X-ray observation, and is an old method not used nowadays.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe copper/hormonal IUDs, not Lippe's loop."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which is another example of a first-generation (non-medicated) IUD named in the source?","options":["Grafenberg's ring","Cu-T 380-A","Mirena","Multiload Cu-375"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Grafenberg's ring among first-generation non-medicated IUDs (alongside Lippe's loop).  Why B/D wrong: Cu-T 380-A and Multiload Cu-375 are copper-containing (second-generation) IUDs.  Why C wrong: Mirena is a hormonal (LNG-IUS) IUD."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Second-generation IUDs are of which two types?","options":["Non-medicated (copper containing) and medicated (hormonal)","Only copper containing","Only barrier type","Only hormonal pills"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states second-generation IUDs are non-medicated (copper containing) and medicated (hormonal).  Why B/C/D wrong: These omit one of the two types or misclassify them."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the IUD table, what is the effective duration of the Cu-T 380-A?","options":["10 years","5 years","3 years","1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Cu-T 380-A (and Cu-T 380 Simline) with an effective duration of 10 years.  Why B wrong: 5 years applies to Multiload Cu-375.  Why C wrong: 3 years applies to Multiload Cu-250 and Nova-T.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the copper IUD table, what is the effective duration of Multiload Cu-375?","options":["5 years","10 years","3 years","7 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Multiload Cu-375 with an effective duration of 5 years.  Why B wrong: 10 years is Cu-T 380-A.  Why C wrong: 3 years is Multiload Cu-250 and Nova-T.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the copper IUD table, which devices have an effective duration of 3 years? (Select all that apply)","options":["Multiload Cu-250","Nova-T","Cu-T 200","Cu-T 380-A","Multiload Cu-375"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Multiload Cu-250 (3 years), Nova-T (3 years) and Cu-T 200 (3 years).  Why D wrong: Cu-T 380-A is 10 years.  Why E wrong: Multiload Cu-375 is 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the hormonal IUD (LNG-IUS) table, what is the effective duration of Liletta and Skyla?","options":["3 years","5 years","10 years","1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Liletta (3 years) and Skyla (3 years) among hormonal IUDs.  Why B wrong: 5 years applies to Kyleena and Mirena.  Why C wrong: 10 years applies to Paraguard.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the hormonal IUD table, which devices have an effective duration of 5 years?","options":["Kyleena and Mirena","Liletta and Skyla","Paraguard only","Cu-T 200"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Kyleena (5 years) and Mirena (5 years) among hormonal IUDs.  Why B wrong: Liletta and Skyla are 3 years.  Why C wrong: Paraguard is 10 years.  Why D wrong: Cu-T 200 is a copper IUD (3 years)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the hormonal IUD table, which device has an effective duration of 10 years?","options":["Paraguard","Mirena","Skyla","Liletta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Paraguard with an effective duration of 10 years. [Note: source lists Paraguard under \"Hormonal IUDs (LNG-IUS)\"; Paraguard is actually a copper (non-hormonal) IUD effective for 10 years. Answer keyed to the source table.]  Why B wrong: Mirena is 5 years.  Why C/D wrong: Skyla and Liletta are 3 years."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are stated advantages of second-generation IUDs? (Select all that apply)","options":["They have a low expulsion rate","They have a low incidence of side effects","They have increased contraceptive effectiveness","They do not affect breastfeeding","They protect against STDs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists low expulsion rate, low incidence of side effects, increased contraceptive effectiveness, and not affecting breastfeeding as advantages of second-generation IUDs.  Why E wrong: The source explicitly states a disadvantage is that they do NOT protect against STDs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which are stated disadvantages of second-generation IUDs?","options":["They do not protect against STDs and have some side effects","They cannot be used while breastfeeding","They are permanent and irreversible","They must be replaced every month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists that second-generation IUDs do not protect against STDs and have some side effects as disadvantages.  Why B wrong: An advantage is that they do not affect breastfeeding.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated as disadvantages."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Third-generation (medicated) IUDs are also known as:","options":["Hormonal IUDs","Copper IUDs","Barrier devices","Non-medicated loops"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states third-generation medicated IUDs are also known as Hormonal IUDs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other categories, not third-generation medicated IUDs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the full form of LNG-IUS?","options":["Levonorgestrel-intrauterine system","Levonorgestrel intra-uterine spermicide","Low-dose norgestrel implant unit system","Levonorgestrel injectable uterine solution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the full form of LNG-IUS is Levonorgestrel-intrauterine system, a hormonal IUD.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the LNG-IUS, what is the total amount of levonorgestrel and the rate at which it is released?","options":["Total 52 mg, released at 20 mcg/day","Total 20 mg, released at 52 mcg/day","Total 60 mg, released at 10 mcg/day","Total 1000 mg, released at 20 mcg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total amount of levonorgestrel is 52 mg and it is released at the rate of 20 mcg/day. The device is T-shaped with polymethylsiloxane around the stem acting as a steroid reservoir.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the dose/release rate."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"How often is the LNG-IUS to be replaced, and what is the example device named?","options":["Every 5 years; example is Mirena","Every 10 years; example is Cu-T 380-A","Every 3 years; example is Skyla","Every 1 year; example is Nova-T"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source (Raj CHO-2020) states LNG-IUS is to be replaced every 5 years, with the example given as Mirena. [Note: 5 years is the source/exam answer; current FDA labeling has extended Mirena (52 mg LNG) to 8 years. Keyed A per the source.]  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the replacement interval or example device."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are stated advantages of IUDs? (Select all that apply)","options":["It works as soon as it is put in place","It works for 5 to 10 years depending on the type and does not interrupt sex","It can be used if the female is breastfeeding her baby","Fertility returns to normal as soon as the IUD is taken out; it is not affected by other medicine","It protects against STDs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of A–D as advantages of IUDs (immediate action, 5–10 years, no interruption of sex, usable while breastfeeding, prompt return of fertility on removal, not affected by other medicine).  Why E wrong: The source states as a disadvantage that IUDs do not protect against STDs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are stated disadvantages of IUDs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Periods may be heavier, longer or more painful","Internal examination required before insertion","IUDs do not protect against STDs","Expulsion rate is high","Fertility never returns after removal"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists heavier/longer/more painful periods, the need for internal examination before insertion, no protection against STDs, and high expulsion rate as disadvantages.  Why E wrong: The source states fertility returns to normal as soon as the IUD is taken out (an advantage), so E is false."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications of IUDs? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pregnancy or suspected pregnancy","Puerperal sepsis or post-sepsis abortion; unexplained vaginal bleeding (DUB)","Severe dysmenorrhoea and suspected genital malignancy","High risk condition of STDs; active PID or pelvic infection within 3 months","Regular menstrual cycles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pregnancy/suspected pregnancy, puerperal sepsis or post-sepsis abortion, unexplained vaginal bleeding (DUB), severe dysmenorrhoea, suspected genital malignancy, high risk of STDs, active PID/pelvic infection within 3 months, and postpartum/post-abortal endometritis in last 3 months as contraindications.  Why E wrong: Regular menstrual cycles are not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What additional contraindications specifically apply to the Cu-T (copper) IUD?","options":["Wilson's disease and copper allergy","Diabetes and hypertension","Breastfeeding","Regular periods"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that additionally for Cu-T, Wilson's disease and copper allergy are contraindications.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed as Cu-T-specific contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the \"Timing of Insertion of IUDs\" (Also Remember box), after childbirth an IUD should be inserted within:","options":["Within 48 hours after delivery","Within 10 minutes","4 to 6 weeks after childbirth","Within 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source (NORCET 2022) states after childbirth insertion should be within 48 hours after delivery.  Why B wrong: Within 10 minutes is post-placental insertion (PPIUCD).  Why C wrong: 4 to 6 weeks is post-puerperal insertion.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the timing for post-placental insertion (PPIUCD)?","options":["Within 10 minutes","Within 48 hours","4 to 6 weeks after childbirth","5 to 7 days of the menstrual cycle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source (UPNHM CHO-2018) states post-placental insertion (PPIUCD) is within 10 minutes.  Why B wrong: Within 48 hours is after childbirth.  Why C wrong: 4 to 6 weeks is post-puerperal.  Why D wrong: 5 to 7 days of the cycle is the reasonable time for insertion during the menstrual cycle."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Post-puerperal insertion of an IUD is done at what time after childbirth?","options":["4 to 6 weeks after childbirth","Within 10 minutes","Within 48 hours","Within 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states post-puerperal insertion is 4 to 6 weeks after childbirth.  Why B wrong: Within 10 minutes is PPIUCD.  Why C wrong: Within 48 hours is after childbirth.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"During the menstrual cycle, what is the more reasonable time for insertion of an IUD?","options":["5 to 7 days of the first day of the menstruation cycle","Day 14 to 16","Day 21 to 28","Only on day 1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an IUD can be inserted at any time of the M.C., but the more reasonable time is 5 to 7 days from the first day of the menstruation cycle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These timings are not stated as the reasonable window."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Intra-caesarean IUD insertion is done at which point during a caesarean delivery?","options":["After removal of the placenta and before closure of the uterine incision","Before delivery of the baby","After closure of the uterine incision","6 weeks postpartum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states intra-caesarean insertion is done during caesarean delivery, after removal of the placenta and before closure of the uterine incision.  Why B/C/D wrong: These timings contradict the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the most common cause of removal of IUDs?","options":["Pain","Missing thread","Pregnancy","Uterine perforation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pain as the most common cause of removal of IUDs, among others such as excessive uterine bleeding, flaring up of pelvic infection, missing thread, pregnancy, uterine perforation and patient's desire for a baby.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other listed indications but pain is specified as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which are the immediate complications of IUDs?","options":["Pain, uterine perforation, syncopal attacks","Abnormal uterine bleeding and pelvic infection","Spontaneous expulsion of device","Wilson's disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists immediate complications of IUDs as pain, uterine perforation and syncopal attacks.  Why B/C wrong: Abnormal uterine bleeding, pelvic infection and spontaneous expulsion are listed as remote complications.  Why D wrong: Wilson's disease is a contraindication, not a complication."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Among the remote complications of IUDs, which is the most common?","options":["Abnormal uterine bleeding","Pain","Pelvic infection","Spontaneous expulsion of device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists remote complications as pain, abnormal uterine bleeding (most common), pelvic infection and spontaneous expulsion of device.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are listed remote complications but abnormal uterine bleeding is specified as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Hormonal contraceptive methods are based on which natural hormones?","options":["Oestrogen and progesterone","Insulin and glucagon","Thyroxine and cortisol","FSH and LH only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hormonal methods are natural hormones like oestrogen and progesterone.  Why B/C wrong: These are unrelated hormones.  Why D wrong: FSH and LH are the target hormones acted upon, not the contraceptive hormones themselves."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"How do hormonal contraceptive methods prevent pregnancy?","options":["By preventing release of the ovum from the ovary and thickening the cervical mucus, making it difficult for sperm to enter","By creating a physical barrier over the cervix","By permanently blocking the fallopian tubes","By killing sperm with a spermicide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states these hormones prevent pregnancy by either preventing release of the ovum from the ovary and thickening the cervical mucus, making it difficult for sperm to enter.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe barrier, sterilisation, and spermicide methods."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are forms of hormone for birth control methods listed in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Oral Combined Pills (OCP)","Contraceptive injections","Combined Oral Contraceptives Pills","Copper T","Diaphragm"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Oral Combined Pills (OCP), contraceptive injections, and Combined Oral Contraceptive Pills as forms of hormone for birth control.  Why D wrong: Copper T is a non-hormonal IUD.  Why E wrong: Diaphragm is a barrier method."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"A pack of oral combined contraceptive pills contains 28 pills. How are they composed?","options":["21 hormonal white pills and 7 non-hormonal iron pills (ferrous fumarate 60 mg), brown coloured","28 hormonal white pills","21 iron pills and 7 hormonal pills","14 hormonal and 14 iron pills"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a pack has 21 hormonal white pills and 7 non-hormonal iron (ferrous fumarate 60 mg) brown-coloured pills.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the number/colour/composition given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"When should the first pill of an oral combined contraceptive pack be taken?","options":["Strictly on the 5th day of the menstrual cycle","On the 1st day of the cycle","On the 14th day of the cycle","Any day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the first pill should be taken strictly on the 5th day of the M.C.; any deviation may not prevent pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source instruction."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the source, what is the preferred time of day to take the OCP?","options":["At bed time at night","In the early morning","With lunch","Immediately after exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pills should be taken at a fixed time, and the choice of time for OCP is at bed time at night.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the recommended time in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"After finishing one pack of OCPs, when should the next pack be started?","options":["On the next day without any interruption","After a 7-day gap","After the next menstrual period","After 1 month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states after finishing a pack, a new pack should be started the next day without any interruptions (daily one tablet taken as indicated by arrows on the pack).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the \"no interruption\" instruction in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are stated benefits of oral pills? (Select all that apply)","options":["Makes periods regular, lighter and less painful","Helpful for premenstrual and menopausal symptoms","Reduces the risk of cancer of the ovary and uterus","Reduces fibroids, ovarian cysts and non-cancerous breast diseases","Protects against STDs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of A–D as benefits of oral pills.  Why E wrong: STD protection is not listed as an OCP benefit (barrier methods provide that)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"According to the source, oral contraceptive pills are how effective if taken correctly on time?","options":["100% effective","89–91% effective","95% effective","50% effective"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states OCPs are 100% effective if taken correctly on time.  Why B wrong: 89–91% is the sponge effectiveness.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for OCPs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which of the following are named as other OCP brands in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["PEARL","MOTI","CHOICE","APSARA","Mirena"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states other OCPs are PEARL, MOTI, CHOICE & APSARA.  Why E wrong: Mirena is a hormonal IUD (LNG-IUS), not an OCP brand."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"What is the mode of action of OCPs on ovulation?","options":["It suppresses ovulation","It promotes ovulation","It has no effect on ovulation","It only thickens cervical mucus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the mode of action of OCP is that it suppresses ovulation.  Why B/C wrong: OCPs suppress, not promote or leave unaffected, ovulation.  Why D wrong: While mucus effects exist, the stated mode of action is ovulation suppression."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"In the mechanism of OCPs, estrogen and progestin act on which hormones to prevent ovulation?","options":["Estrogen works on FSH and progestin works on LH","Estrogen works on LH and progestin works on FSH","Both work on TSH","Both work on ACTH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states estrogen works on FSH and progestin works on LH hormone to prevent ovulation.  Why B wrong: The source specifies estrogen→FSH and progestin→LH, not the reverse.  Why C/D wrong: TSH and ACTH are not involved."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"Which contraceptive method group is described as also preventing STDs and PIDs, making it \"very important\"?","options":["Mechanical (barrier) contraceptive methods","Hormonal methods","Intrauterine devices","Natural methods"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifically notes that mechanical (barrier) methods prevent pregnancy along with STDs and PIDs, marked \"very Imp.\"  Why B/C wrong: Hormonal methods and IUDs are explicitly stated NOT to protect against STDs.  Why D wrong: Natural methods are not credited with STD protection in the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Communicable diseases…","question":"A nurse counsels a lactating mother 3 months postpartum who wants reliable contraception without affecting breastfeeding. Based on the source, which method is appropriate?","options":["A second-generation IUD (does not affect breastfeeding)","Oral combined contraceptive pills started immediately","Permanent sterilisation only","Rhythm/calendar method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists as an advantage that second-generation IUDs do not affect breastfeeding and that IUDs can be used if the female is breastfeeding her baby.  Why B wrong: The source does not endorse OCPs for lactating mothers here and hormonal effects on lactation are a concern.  Why C wrong: Permanent sterilisation is not indicated for a mother who may want future children.  Why D wrong: The source states the rhythm method is not useful in the postnatal period and has a high failure rate."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which of the following is an advantage of oral contraceptive pills (OCP)?","options":["Increased risk of breast cancer","Reduces the risk of endometrial and ovarian cancer","Protection against sexually transmitted infections","Failure rate of 5 per 100 women per year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Reduce the risk of endometrial & ovarian cancer\" as a key advantage of OCP.  Why A wrong: Increased risk of breast cancer is listed as a disadvantage, not an advantage.  Why C wrong: OCP does not protect against STIs (listed as a disadvantage).  Why D wrong: The stated OCP failure rate is 0.1 per 100 women per year, not 5."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The failure rate of oral contraceptive pills (OCP), as stated in the text, is:","options":["0.1 per 100 women per year","1 per 100 women per year","2–14 per 100 women per year","7 per 100 women per year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Failure rate 0.1 per 100 women per years.\"  Why B wrong: 1 per 100 is not the stated figure.  Why C wrong: 2–14 is the Pearl Index for male condom, not OCP.  Why D wrong: 7 is a typical-use Pearl Index example for the pill on p896, not the stated failure rate here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"According to the text, at what age should OCPs be avoided in a woman who is an active smoker?","options":["Over the age of 25","Over the age of 30","Over the age of 35","Over the age of 40"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states OCP \"should not be used a women over the age of 35 who active smoker.\"  Why A/B wrong: The cut-off is 35, not 25 or 30.  Why D wrong: 40 is the upper age limit for OCP use without restriction, not the smoker cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which contraceptive method is described as \"the safest and reliable method for newly-married/newly couples\"?","options":["IUD","Condom","Oral contraceptive method (OCP)","Tubectomy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Oral contraceptive method (OCP) is the safest and reliable method for newly couples\" per the Also Remember box.  Why A wrong: IUD is not cited as the method for newly-married couples.  Why B wrong: Condom is the first choice for males in India but not termed safest/reliable for newlyweds here.  Why D wrong: Tubectomy is a permanent method, unsuitable for newly-married couples."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Before elective surgery, OCPs should be withheld for at least how long?","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","3 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"OCPs should be withheld prior to any surgery before at least 6 weeks.\"  Why A/B wrong: Shorter than the stated 6-week interval.  Why D wrong: 3 months exceeds the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Mittelschmerz syndrome is best described as:","options":["Cyclical breast pain before menstruation","One-sided lower abdominal pain associated with ovulation, occurring about 14 days before the next menstrual period","Severe cramping on the first day of menstruation","Bilateral pelvic pain during menstruation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Mittelschmerz as one-sided, lower abdominal pain associated with ovulation (\"middle pain\") occurring midway through the cycle, about 14 days before the next menstrual period.  Why A wrong: That describes premenstrual mastalgia, not Mittelschmerz.  Why C wrong: That describes dysmenorrhea.  Why D wrong: Mittelschmerz is one-sided, not bilateral."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Minipills are best classified as:","options":["Combined estrogen-progestin pills","Progestin-only pills (POPs)","Non-hormonal contraceptives","Emergency contraceptive pills"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"This is progestin only pills (POPs).\"  Why A wrong: Combined pills contain both estrogen and progestin; minipills contain only progestin.  Why C wrong: Minipills are hormonal (progestin).  Why D wrong: Minipills are a regular contraceptive, not an emergency method."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"A minipill (POP) is initiated:","options":["On the 5th day of the cycle","Daily from the first day of the cycle","Only after menstruation ends","Twice a week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Time of initiation- daily from the first day of the cycle.\"  Why A wrong: 5th day is the initiation timing for other methods, not minipills.  Why C wrong: Not stated; initiation is from day 1.  Why D wrong: Twice-a-week dosing applies to Saheli, not minipills."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The primary mode of action of minipills (POPs) is:","options":["Complete suppression of ovulation","Thickening the cervical mucus, making it difficult for sperm to reach/penetrate the egg and changing the uterine lining to impede implantation","Destruction of the ovum after fertilization","Blocking progesterone receptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states minipills work by thickening cervical mucus (difficult for sperm) and changing the uterine lining to make implantation difficult; they only \"sometimes inhibit ovulation.\"  Why A wrong: They only sometimes inhibit ovulation, not completely.  Why C wrong: Not the stated mechanism.  Why D wrong: Blocking progesterone receptors describes Mifepristone."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Why is the minipill also known as the \"Lactation pill\"?","options":["It increases breast milk production","It has no side effects on lactation and can be used during lactation","It must only be taken by lactating women","It contains estrogen safe for babies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"It not has any side effects on lactation so it can also used during lactation, so that it known as Lactation pill.\"  Why A wrong: The source does not claim it increases milk production.  Why C wrong: It can be used during lactation but is not exclusive to lactating women.  Why D wrong: Minipills are progestin-only, not estrogen-containing."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Chhaya and Saheli are composed of which active ingredient?","options":["Levonorgestrel 0.15 mg","Ormeloxifene 30 mg (Centchromen-30 mg)","Ethinyl estradiol 30 µg","Medroxyprogesterone acetate 150 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"These are composed with Ormeloxifene 30 mg or brand name Centchromen-30 mg.\"  Why A wrong: Levonorgestrel is in Mala N/D and Norplant.  Why C wrong: Ethinyl estradiol is the estrogen in combined pills.  Why D wrong: MPA is the component of ANTRA/DMPA."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Chhaya/Saheli (Ormeloxifene) is classified as a:","options":["Combined hormonal contraceptive","Non-hormonal, non-steroidal contraceptive","Progestin-only injectable","Barrier method"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states they are \"a non-hormonal, non-steroidal contraceptives\" and estrogen-derivative contraceptives.  Why A wrong: They are non-hormonal.  Why C wrong: They are oral, not injectable, and non-hormonal.  Why D wrong: They are not barrier methods."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"What is the correct dosing schedule for Saheli (Centchromen)?","options":["One tablet daily","One 30 mg tablet twice a week (Sunday & Wednesday) for the first 3 months, then once weekly (every Sunday)","One injection every 3 months","One tablet every alternate day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a single 30 mg tablet twice a week (Sunday & Wednesday) for first 3 months, then once weekly every Sunday.  Why A wrong: Daily dosing describes OCP/minipills.  Why C wrong: 3-monthly injection describes ANTRA/DMPA.  Why D wrong: Not the stated schedule."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The mode of action of Saheli (Centchromen) is primarily:","options":["Inhibition of ovulation","Preventing implantation of the fertilised ovum (it does not inhibit ovulation)","Thickening cervical mucus only","Blocking sperm mechanically"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"It works primarily by preventing implantation of fertilised ovum\" and \"These does not inhibit ovulation.\"  Why A wrong: The source explicitly says it does NOT inhibit ovulation.  Why C wrong: Cervical mucus thickening is the minipill/DMPA mechanism.  Why D wrong: It is not a mechanical/barrier method."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Chhaya is a research product of which institute, and Centchromen is popularly known as:","options":["CDRI, Lucknow; \"once a week pill\"","AIIMS, Delhi; \"morning after pill\"","ICMR, Delhi; \"daily pill\"","NIN, Hyderabad; \"monthly pill\""],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Chhaya... research product of CDRI, Lucknow\" and \"Centchromen is also known as 'once a week pill'.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The institute is CDRI Lucknow and the popular name is \"once a week pill\" per the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Through which government program is Chhaya tablet supplied to government centers?","options":["Antra program","Mission Indradhanush","RCH program","Janani Suraksha Yojana"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Chhaya tab. is supplying to govt centers through Antra program.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These programs are not cited as the supply channel for Chhaya."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Mala N and Mala D each contain which progestin and estrogen composition?","options":["Levonorgestrel 0.15 mg + Ethinyl estradiol 30 µg","Norethisterone 350 mcg + Ethinyl estradiol 50 µg","Desogestrel 75 mcg + Estradiol valerate","Medroxyprogesterone 150 mg + no estrogen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The composition table lists both Mala N and Mala D as Levonorgestrel 0.15 mg + Ethinyl estradiol 30 µg.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated composition of Mala N/D."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"How many tablets are in a Mala N / Mala D pack and how are they divided?","options":["21 tablets, all hormonal","28 tablets (21 hormonal + 7 iron/ferrous fumarate 60 mg)","28 tablets, all hormonal","22 tablets (21 hormonal + 1 placebo)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table specifies \"28 tablets (21 Hormonal + 7 Iron Ferrous fumarate 60 mg).\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 28-tablet (21 + 7 iron) composition."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"According to the \"Must know\" box, if a woman vomits within how long after taking a pill should she take another pill from the pack?","options":["Within 30 minutes","Within 1 hour","Within 2 hours","Within 4 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"If she vomits within 2 hours of taking a pill, another pill from the pack should be taken as soon as possible.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated window is 2 hours."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Following childbirth, when should OCP be started in non-lactating mothers versus lactating women?","options":["Non-lactating: after 3 weeks; lactating: withheld for 6 months","Non-lactating: immediately; lactating: after 3 weeks","Both after 6 weeks","Non-lactating: after 6 months; lactating: after 3 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"In non-lactating mothers it should be started after 3 weeks and in lactating women, it should be withheld for 6 months.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: They contradict the stated 3-week (non-lactating) / 6-month (lactating) rule."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"If a woman forgets to take 2 pills, the correct advice per the source is to:","options":["Skip both and continue as scheduled","Take 2 pills the next day (one in the morning, one in the evening), continue remaining pills, and use 7 days of extra precaution (condom/avoid sex)","Stop the pack and start a new one","Take all missed pills together immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to take 2 pills the next day (morning and evening), continue as scheduled, and use extra 7 days precaution with condom or by avoiding sex.  Why A wrong: Skipping increases pregnancy chance.  Why C wrong: Not the stated advice.  Why D wrong: The instruction is one morning + one evening, not all at once."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In case of abortion, OCP should be started:","options":["After 6 weeks","On the day after abortion or 1st day after abortion","After the next menstrual period","Only after an ultrasound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"In case of abortion, it should be started on the day after abortion or 1st day after abortion.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated immediate initiation post-abortion."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under WHO guidelines for combined OCP, \"Age: Menarche to 40 years\" falls under which category?","options":["WHO Category 1 – no restriction of use","WHO Category 2 – relative restriction","WHO Category 3 – risks outweigh advantages","WHO Category 4 – absolute restriction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"Age: Menarche to 40 years\" under \"No restriction of use (WHO category-01).\"  Why B wrong: Category 2 includes smoker <35, age 40, jaundice history, diabetes.  Why C wrong: Category 3 includes unexplained bleeding, hyperlipidemia, benign liver tumors.  Why D wrong: Category 4 includes circulatory disease, active liver disease, pregnancy, breast cancer."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication (WHO Category 4) to combined OCP?","options":["Smoker under 35 years","Mild hypertension","Arterial or venous thrombosis / severe hypertension","Varicose veins"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Circulatory diseases including severe hypertension and arterial/venous thrombosis are listed as absolute restrictions (Category 4).  Why A wrong: Smoker <35 is Category 2 (relative).  Why B wrong: Mild hypertension is Category 2 (relative).  Why D wrong: Varicose veins is listed under no-restriction/Category 1 indications."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"According to WHO guidelines, breastfeeding at postpartum 6 weeks is classified for combined OCP as:","options":["No restriction (Category 1)","Absolute restriction (Category 4)","Recommended","Category 2 only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Breastfeeding (postpartum 6 weeks)\" is listed under Others in Absolute restrictions (Category 4).  Why A/C wrong: It is contraindicated, not permitted at 6 weeks.  Why D wrong: Breastfeeding at postpartum 6 weeks to 6 months is Category 3, but at 6 weeks it is Category 4 absolute."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which condition is listed as a WHO Category 3 (risks outweigh advantages) restriction for combined OCP?","options":["Anemia (iron deficiency)","Unexplained vaginal bleeding","Heart disease","Thyroid disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Unexplained vaginal bleeding, hyperlipidemia, benign liver tumors, breastfeeding 6 weeks–6 months, heavy smoker (>20/day), and past breast cancer are listed under Category 3.  Why A wrong: Anemia is a no-restriction (Category 1) indication.  Why C wrong: Heart disease is Category 4 absolute.  Why D wrong: Thyroid disease is a Category 1 indication."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"A heavy smoker is defined in the WHO Category 3 restrictions as one who smokes:","options":["More than 5 cigarettes/day","More than 10 cigarettes/day","More than 20 cigarettes/day","More than 40 cigarettes/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"Heavy smoker (>20 cigarettes/day)\" under Category 3.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated threshold is more than 20 cigarettes/day."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"NET-EN is the abbreviation for which injectable contraceptive?","options":["Norethisterone Enanthate","Norgestrel Estradiol","Norelgestromin Ethinyl","Nestorone Etonogestrel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"It is Norethisterone Enanthate (NET-EN) injectable contraceptive.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the expansion of NET-EN."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"What is the dose and route of NET-EN?","options":["150 mg every 3 months, IM","200 mg every 2 months (60 days), IM","100 mg monthly, subcutaneous","200 mg every 6 weeks, oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Dose- 200 mg every 2 months (60 days). Route - IM.\" Mode of action inhibits ovulation, same as DMPA.  Why A wrong: 150 mg every 3 months describes DMPA/ANTRA.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated dose/route/interval."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The Norplant system is a contraceptive using which hormone?","options":["Estrogen only","Levonorgestrel (progestin) sub-dermal system","Medroxyprogesterone acetate injection","Norethisterone enanthate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Norplant is a hormonal (levonorgestrel system), sub-dermal, reversible system of silastic capsules containing synthetic progestin levonorgestrel.  Why A wrong: It is progestin, not estrogen.  Why C wrong: MPA describes DMPA/ANTRA.  Why D wrong: NET-EN is a separate injectable."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The insertion site of the Norplant sub-dermal levonorgestrel capsule is:","options":["Under the skin of the medial aspect of the non-dominant upper arm, 6–8 cm above the elbow, in a fan-like pattern","In the abdominal wall near the umbilicus","In the anterior thigh","Under the scapula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes insertion under the skin of the medial aspect of the non-dominant upper arm, 6–8 cm above the elbow, in a fern/fan-like pattern.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the described insertion sites."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Match the Norplant/implant type with rods and duration: Which is correct?","options":["Norplant-I: 6 rods, effective 5 years","Norplant-II: 6 rods, effective 5 years","Implanon: 6 rods, effective 5 years","Norplant-I: 1 rod, effective 3 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Norplant-I: It has 6 rods and effective for 5 year long.\" Each of the 6 flexible silastic rods contains 30 mg levonorgestrel.  Why B wrong: Norplant-II has 2 rods, effective 3 years.  Why C wrong: Implanon has 1 rod, effective 3 years.  Why D wrong: Norplant-I has 6 rods and lasts 5 years, not 1 rod/3 years."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Implanon consists of how many rods and is effective for how long?","options":["6 rods, 5 years","2 rods, 3 years","1 rod, 3 years","1 rod, 5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Implanon: It has 1 rods and effective for 3 years long.\"  Why A wrong: Describes Norplant-I.  Why B wrong: Describes Norplant-II.  Why D wrong: Implanon lasts 3 years, not 5."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Each of the 6 flexible silastic rods in the Norplant system contains how much levonorgestrel?","options":["15 mg","30 mg","75 mg","150 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"6 flexible silastic rodes of 30 mg levonorgestrel each are placed under skin of upper arm.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated amount per rod is 30 mg."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"ANTRA contraceptive injection contains which active component?","options":["Norethisterone enanthate","Levonorgestrel","Medroxyprogesterone acetate (MPA)/Depot Medroxyprogesterone Acetate (DMPA)","Ormeloxifene"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Components of ANTRA: Medroxyprogesterone acetate (MPA)... also known as Depot Medroxy-Progesterone Acetate (DMPA)... sold under the brand name Depo-Provera.\"  Why A wrong: NET-EN is a different injectable.  Why B wrong: Levonorgestrel is in Norplant/Mala.  Why D wrong: Ormeloxifene is Chhaya/Saheli."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"ANTRA (DMPA) injection can be used by women of which age group?","options":["15–45 years","18–35 years","20–40 years","15–49 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Any women of 15-45 years of age can use this.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated range is 15–45 years."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Each ANTRA (DMPA) injection (150 mg/ml) provides contraceptive protection for:","options":["30 days (1 month)","60 days (2 months)","90 days (3 months)","180 days (6 months)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Each injection (150 mg/ml) gives protection for 90 days (3 months)\" and it is a highly effective three-month-long reversible contraception.  Why A/B wrong: Protection lasts 3 months.  Why D wrong: Not 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The delay in return to fertility after stopping ANTRA (DMPA) is stated as:","options":["1–2 months from last injection","3–5 months from last injection","7–10 months from last injection","12–18 months from last injection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Delay in return to fertility (7-10 months from last injection).\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated delay is 7–10 months."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"ANTRA (DMPA) is stated to decrease the risk of endometrial cancer by approximately:","options":["20%","40%","60%","80%"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes it is used for endometriosis, abnormal uterine bleeding, and certain cancers, with \"Decreased risk of endometrial cancer by 80%.\"  Why A/B/C wrong: The stated reduction is 80%."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"ANTRA (DMPA) is considered a safe option for breast-feeding women after:","options":["3 weeks of delivery","6 weeks of delivery","3 months of delivery","6 months of delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Effective and safe option for breast-feeding women (after 6 weeks of delivery).\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated interval is 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication to ANTRA (DMPA)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Unexplained vaginal bleeding","Stroke and uncontrolled diabetes","Breast cancer (past & present)","Active hepatitis or liver tumor","Desire for rapid return to fertility"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five — unexplained vaginal bleeding, stroke/uncontrolled diabetes, breast cancer (past & present), active hepatitis/liver tumor, and desire for rapid return to fertility — are listed as contraindications of ANTRA.  Why not exclude any: Each option is explicitly listed in the source's contraindication list, so all apply."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which document/card is provided to stakeholders under the ANTRA program with the injection due date?","options":["MPA card","MCP card","RCH card","Immunization card"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Provide MPA card with due date of injection to stakeholders.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the MPA card, not these."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Post-coital (emergency) contraception is indicated in which of the following situations? (Select all that apply)","options":["Condom rupture","Unprotected intercourse","Missed pills","Sexual assault & rape","Routine daily contraception"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists condom rupture, unprotected intercourse, missed pills, and sexual assault/rape as indications for emergency contraception.  Why E wrong: Emergency contraception is for accidental/unwanted conceptions, not routine daily use."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Hormonal emergency contraception must be taken within what time frame after unprotected intercourse?","options":["Within 24 hours","Within 48 hours","Within 72 hours","Within 5 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Hormonal emergency contraception: within 72 hours after unprotected intercourse.\"  Why A/B wrong: The stated window is 72 hours.  Why D wrong: 5 days is the window for IUDs for emergency contraception."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"IUDs used for emergency contraception can be inserted within what period after unprotected intercourse?","options":["24 hours","72 hours","5 days","7 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"IUDs for emergency contraception: within 5 days after unprotected intercourse.\"  Why A/B wrong: 72 hours is for hormonal EC.  Why D wrong: The stated IUD window is 5 days, not 7."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The MTP kit is a common and legally accepted method for termination of pregnancy up to:","options":["7 weeks (49 days)","9 weeks (63 days)","12 weeks (84 days)","20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"This is a common and legally method for termination of pregnancy up to 9 weeks (63 days), beyond 9 weeks D&C procedure can be done.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated limit for the MTP kit is 9 weeks (63 days)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The MTP kit combination (Mifepristone + Misoprostol) is stated to be effective up to:","options":["80–85%","90–92%","95–99%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"This combination method is effective up to 95-99%.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated effectiveness is 95–99%."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"After using the MTP kit, follow-up (hCG test) to confirm abortion should be initiated after:","options":["7 days","10 days","14 days","21 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Follow up should be initiated (hCG test) after 14 days for confirmation of abortion.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated follow-up interval is 14 days."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In the MTP kit, Mifepristone is best described as:","options":["A prostaglandin, 4 tablets of 200 mcg","An anti-progesterone, 1 tablet 200 mg, given orally, blocking progesterone receptors","An estrogen, given vaginally","An oxytocic agent given IM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mifepristone: total dose 1 tablet 200 mg, anti-progesterone, route oral, works by blocking progesterone receptors; outcome is embryo detachment, endometrial breakdown, uterine softening/contraction, and cervical softening.  Why A wrong: That describes Misoprostol (prostaglandin, 4 x 200 mcg).  Why C/D wrong: Mifepristone is oral anti-progesterone, not estrogen or an IM oxytocic."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In the MTP kit, Misoprostol total dose and route are:","options":["1 tablet 200 mg, orally","4 tablets of 200 mcg each (total 800 mcg); vaginally, sublingually and buccally","2 tablets 200 mg, IM","4 tablets 200 mg, orally only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Misoprostol total dose is 4 tablets of 200 mcg each (total 800 mcg), route vaginally/sublingually/buccally; it is a prostaglandin in nature causing uterine contraction and cervical softening.  Why A wrong: That describes Mifepristone.  Why C/D wrong: Dose is 4 x 200 mcg (not mg) and routes are vaginal/sublingual/buccal, not IM or oral-only."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In the MTP kit dosing schedule shown, when is Misoprostol taken relative to Mifepristone?","options":["Simultaneously on day 1","After 24–48 hours (Day 1 Mifepristone, then 4 tab Misoprostol after 24–48 hrs)","7 days later","Before Mifepristone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure shows Day 1 = 1 tab Mifepristone 200 mg, and after 24–48 hrs = 4 tab Misoprostol 200 mcg.  Why A wrong: They are staggered, not simultaneous.  Why C wrong: The interval is 24–48 hours, not 7 days.  Why D wrong: Mifepristone is taken first."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to using the MTP kit? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ectopic pregnancy","Haemorrhagic disorder","Current use of anticoagulants","IUD in place (must be removed before proceeding)","Septic abortion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five — ectopic pregnancy, haemorrhagic disorder, current anticoagulant use, IUD in place (remove then proceed), and septic abortion — are listed as absolute contraindications.  Why none excluded: Each is explicitly stated in the source list."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which of the following is a possible complication of the MTP kit as listed in the source?","options":["Incomplete abortion","Weight gain","Hypertension","Amenorrhea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists incomplete abortion, haemorrhage, and infection as possible complications; the disclaimer emphasizes incomplete abortion as the biggest risk.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed as MTP kit complications in the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Female sterilization is known as:","options":["Vasectomy","Tubectomy","NSV","Hysterectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Female sterilization is called Tubectomy.\"  Why A/C wrong: Vasectomy/NSV are male sterilization procedures.  Why D wrong: Hysterectomy is uterus removal, not sterilization."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In India, the first and second choices of contraception (per the source) are, respectively:","options":["Male condom, then tubectomy","Tubectomy (first choice) and male condom (second choice)","OCP, then IUD","Vasectomy, then tubectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Tubectomy is the first choice of contraception whereas male condom is second choice of contraception in India.\"  Why A wrong: Order is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated ranking."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The most common surgical sterilization procedure for women is called:","options":["Hysteroscopy","Tubal ligation","Mini-laparotomy only","Salpingectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Most common surgical sterilization procedure is called tubal ligation for women.\"  Why A wrong: Hysteroscopy is the vaginal route, not the most common overall descriptor here.  Why C wrong: Mini-laparotomy is one method but the general term used is tubal ligation.  Why D wrong: Salpingectomy is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The most common abdominal route for female sterilization in India is:","options":["Laparoscopic sterilization or Mini-laprotomy","Hysteroscopy","Open laparotomy","Vaginal tubal ligation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Via abdominal - Laproscopic sterilization or Mini-Laprotomy - most common in India.\"  Why B wrong: Hysteroscopy is the vaginal route.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated as most common in India."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"\"Interval sterilization\" for females is timed as:","options":["Within 7 days of the menstrual period (follicular/proliferative phase)","After 24 hours up to 7 days of delivery","Any time in the luteal phase","Only during menstruation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Interval sterilization: Within 7 days of the menstrual period (in the follicular/proliferative phase of the menstrual cycle).\"  Why B wrong: That defines post-partum sterilization.  Why C/D wrong: The timing is the follicular/proliferative phase within 7 days of menses."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Post-partum sterilization is performed:","options":["Within 24 hours of delivery only","After 24 hours up to 7 days of delivery","After 6 weeks of delivery","Within 12 hours of delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Post-partum sterilization: After 24 hours up to 7 days of delivery.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated window is 24 hours up to 7 days."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In a normal (vaginal) delivery, tubal ligation can be done:","options":["After 24 to 48 hours following the birth","Immediately after delivery of placenta","Only after 6 weeks","During labor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"In normal delivery, the tubal ligation can be done after 24 to 48 hours following the birth.\"  Why B wrong: That describes caesarean delivery (after delivery of baby and placenta).  Why C wrong: 6 weeks is the interval when laparoscopic sterilization cannot be done earlier.  Why D wrong: Not during labor."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In caesarean delivery, tubal ligation can be performed:","options":["After delivery of the baby and placenta","After 24–48 hours","After 6 weeks","Before delivery of the baby"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"In caesarean delivery, the tubal ligation can be done after delivery of the baby and placenta.\"  Why B wrong: 24–48 hours applies to normal delivery.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"BTL surgery stands for:","options":["Bilateral Tubal Ligation surgery","Basic Tubal Laparoscopy","Bilateral Tubal Laparotomy","Basal Tubal Ligature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"BTL Surgery: It is Bilateral Tubal Legation surgery used for female sterilization.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Laparoscopic sterilization cannot be performed within how many weeks of delivery (or should be performed after that interval)?","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Laproscopic sterilization cannot be performed within 6 weeks of delivery or it should be performed after 6 weeks following delivery.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated interval is 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The mortality rate following tubal sterilization is estimated at approximately:","options":["72 per 100,000 for all methods","7.2 per 100,000","720 per 100,000","72 per 10,000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Mortality rate following tubal sterilization is estimated something 72/100000 for all methods.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated rate is 72 per 100,000."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Male sterilization is known as:","options":["Tubectomy","Vasectomy","Hysteroscopy","Orchidectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"It is a surgical procedure for male sterilization, known as Vasectomy.\"  Why A wrong: Tubectomy is female sterilization.  Why C wrong: Hysteroscopy is a female route.  Why D wrong: Orchidectomy is removal of testis, not sterilization."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In vasectomy, how much of the vas deferens is cut/resected before it is tied or sealed?","options":["0.5 cm","1 cm","2 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"In which 1 cm vas deference is cut or resected and then it will tied or sealed.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated length is 1 cm."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"After vasectomy, additional contraceptive protection is needed for about how long or until the semen is free of sperm?","options":["1 month","3 months","6 months","1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Additional contraceptive protection can be needed for about 3 months following operations or till the semen become free of sperm.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated interval is about 3 months."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"NSV, the most common technique for male sterilization, stands for and is also known as:","options":["Non-Scalpel Vasectomy; Key Hole Vasectomy","New Standard Vasectomy; Open Vasectomy","Non-Surgical Vasectomy; Laser Vasectomy","Needle Sterilization Vasectomy; Micro Vasectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"NSV - Non-Scalpel Vasectomy... also known as Key Hole Vasectomy,\" considered standard of care and is a bilateral vasectomy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansion or alternative name."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"After a vasectomy, additional contraceptives should be used for 3 months, or it requires how many ejaculations to empty the stored semen?","options":["5–10 ejaculations","10–15 ejaculations","20–30 ejaculations","40–50 ejaculations"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Additional contraceptives should be used for 3 months or it requires 20 to 30 ejaculations to empty the stored semen.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated number is 20–30 ejaculations."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"After NSV, semen examination should be done after how many weeks to declare a man sterile (with two consecutive analyses showing absence of spermatozoa)?","options":["6 weeks","12 weeks","16 weeks","24 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"After 16 weeks semen examination should be done and if the two consecutive semen analysis report show absence of spermatozoa then man is declare as sterile.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated interval is 16 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The most common cause of failure of vasectomy in India is:","options":["Recanalization of the vas","Mistaken identification of the vas deferens","Incomplete resection","Infection at the site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Most common cause of failure of vasectomy in India - Mistaken identification of vas deferens.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically cites mistaken identification of the vas deferens."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"After the NSV procedure, heavy work or cycling should be restricted for about:","options":["2 days","1 week","2 weeks","6 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"After procedure heavy works or cycling should be restricted for about 2 weeks.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated restriction period is 2 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, the overall first choice of contraceptive in India is:","options":["Condom","Female sterilization or Tubectomy","Low dose combined pills","IUD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Overall First choice of contraceptive in India - Female sterilization or Tubectomy.\"  Why A wrong: Condom is the first choice for males, not overall.  Why C wrong: Low dose combined pills are the choice for sexually active adolescents.  Why D wrong: Not stated as the overall first choice."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Low dose combined pills are the contraceptive of choice for which group?","options":["Newly married couples","Sexually active adolescents","Lactating women","Perimenopausal women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Low dose combined pills are choice of contraceptives for sexually active adolescents.\"  Why A wrong: Oral combined pills are for newly married couples.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for these groups."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The couple protection rate (CPR) in India is stated to be, and WHO recommends India needs a CPR of:","options":["54.8%; CPR >60% to bring NRR less than 1","60%; CPR >50%","44.8%; CPR >70%","54.8%; CPR >80%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Couple protection rate (CPR) among the eligible couples in India is 54.8%, but according to WHO India need CPR >60% to bring net-reproduction rate (NRR) less than 1.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated values are CPR 54.8% and target >60%."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"An \"eligible couple\" is defined as a currently married couple with the wife in the reproductive age group of:","options":["15–45 years","15–49 years","18–45 years","20–40 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Eligible couples - a currently married couples with wife in reproductive age group (15-45 years).\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states 15–45 years."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"How many eligible couples are there presently per 1000 population in India?","options":["100–120","120–150","150–180","180–200"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Presently, there are 150-180 Eligible couples per 1000 population in India.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated figure is 150–180 per 1000."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Contraceptive methods should be started at 6 weeks in lactating women following birth mainly because:","options":["Low levels of estrogen or the presence of prolactin during exclusive breastfeeding may suppress ovulation","The uterus has not fully involuted","Milk supply must first be established","Estrogen levels are too high"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states contraceptives should be used after 6 weeks in lactating women \"because during exclusive breast feeding low level of estrogen or presence of prolactin may suppress ovulation.\"  Why B/C wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Estrogen is low, not high, during exclusive breastfeeding."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Pregnancy failure rate of contraceptives is calculated by which index?","options":["Body Mass Index","Pearl rate (Pearl Index)","Apgar score","Fertility Index"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"It is calculated by pearl rate (Pearl Index).\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated indices."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The Pearl Index is defined as the number of contraceptive failures per:","options":["100 women-years of exposure (HWY)","1000 women","100,000 live births","10 women per cycle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"It is defined as the number of contraceptive failures per 100 women-years of exposure (HWY).\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the Pearl Index denominator."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The formula for the Pearl Index is:","options":["Total accidental pregnancies × 1200 / Total months of exposure","Total pregnancies × 100 / Total women","Total accidental pregnancies / Total years × 1000","Total live births × 1200 / Total exposure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Formula of pearl index: Total accidental pregnancies × 1200 / Total months of exposure.\" The numerator is total accidental pregnancies and denominator is total months of exposure.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated formula."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"In the Pearl Index formula, the factor 1200 represents:","options":["The number of months in 100 years","The number of days in a year","The number of women in a trial","A safety multiplier"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"The factor 1200 is the number of months in 100 years.\" A minimum of 600 months is required for calculation of Pearl Index.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source defines 1200 as months in 100 years."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"What is the minimum number of months of exposure required to calculate the Pearl Index?","options":["100 months","300 months","600 months","1200 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Minimum 600 months is required for calculation of pearl index.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated minimum is 600 months."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"If 100 couples use a method for 2 years and have 20 pregnancies, the calculated Pearl rate is:","options":["5","10","15","20"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The worked example gives 20 × 1200 / (100 × 24) = 10.  Why A/C/D wrong: The correct calculation yields 10."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"When the Pearl rate is below 10, the effectiveness of a method is considered:","options":["Low","High","Moderate","Ineffective"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"When the pregnancy rate (pearl rate) is below 10, the effectiveness of the particular method is considered to be high. If it is more than 20, then it is said to be low.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Below 10 indicates high effectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The birth control pill has a typical use effectiveness of 93% corresponding to a Pearl Index of:","options":["2","5","7","10"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"the birth control pill has a typical use effectiveness of 93% and a Pearl Index of 7, so that means seven women in one year will get pregnant from using the pill.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated Pearl Index for typical pill use is 7."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"According to the Pearl Index table, which method has the LOWEST (best) pregnancy failure rate?","options":["Male condom (2–14)","Oral pill (0.1–2)","IUD (1–5)","Diaphragm (12)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives Oral pill a Pearl Index of 0.1–2, the lowest listed value.  Why A wrong: Male condom is 2–14.  Why C wrong: IUD is 1–5.  Why D wrong: Diaphragm is 12."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"What is the Pearl Index (pregnancy failure rate) for the rhythm (calendar) method per the table?","options":["12","18","24","25"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists rhythm (calendar) method Pearl Index = 24.  Why A wrong: 12 is the diaphragm.  Why B wrong: 18 is coitus interruptus.  Why D wrong: 25 is the withdrawal method."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Match the Pearl Index values from the table: Which pairing is correct?","options":["Female condom: 5–21","Coitus interruptus: 12","Vaginal sponge: 1–5","Centchroman (Saheli): 24"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Female condom = 5–21.  Why B wrong: Coitus interruptus = 18 (diaphragm is 12).  Why C wrong: Vaginal sponge = 20–40 (IUD is 1–5).  Why D wrong: Centchroman (Saheli) = 1.83–2.84 (rhythm method is 24)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"According to the table, the withdrawal method has a Pearl Index of:","options":["18","24","25","40"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Withdrawal method = 25.  Why A wrong: 18 is coitus interruptus.  Why B wrong: 24 is rhythm/calendar.  Why D wrong: 40 is the upper value for vaginal sponge."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"most effective contraceptive methods\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tubectomy","Vasectomy","OCPs and Implants","Injectable, Patch and vaginal rings","Withdrawal method"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Most effective methods listed: Tubectomy, Vasectomy, OCPs, Implants, LAM, Injectable, Patch and vaginal rings.  Why E wrong: Withdrawal (and rhythm/calendar) are listed as the LEAST effective methods with high pregnancy failure rate."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"According to the source, the least effective contraceptive methods (highest failure rate) are:","options":["Withdrawal and rhythm (calendar) method","OCPs and implants","Tubectomy and vasectomy","IUD and injectables"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Least effective method: Withdrawal and rhythm (calendar) method (highly pregnancy failure rate).\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are among the most effective methods."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The National Family Planning Indemnity Scheme (NFPIS) was formerly known as, and existed under that name during:","options":["National Family Planning Insurance Scheme; 25th November 2005 to 2013","Family Welfare Insurance Scheme; 2001 to 2010","National Sterilization Insurance Scheme; 2000 to 2005","Janani Suraksha Insurance; 2005 to 2015"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"It is formerly known as 'National Family Planning Insurance Scheme' since 25th November, 2005 to 2013.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated former name or dates."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The modified scheme became known as the \"Family Planning Indemnity Scheme\" with effect from:","options":["1st April, 2013","25th November, 2005","1st April, 2020","1st January, 2013"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After Supreme Court instructions, it was modified and \"known as 'Family Planning Indemnity Scheme' with effect from 1st April, 2013.\"  Why B wrong: That is the original insurance scheme start date.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated effective date."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the Family Planning Indemnity Scheme, clients are indemnified in the event of:","options":["Deaths, complications and failures following sterilization","Loss of employment","Property damage","Infertility treatment costs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Under this scheme clients are indemnified in the eventualities of deaths, complications and failures following sterilization.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Not covered by this scheme per the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"The Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP) Act was passed in Parliament and came into force, respectively, in:","options":["Passed 1971, came into force/legalised 1972; last amendment 2020","Passed 1972, force 1975; amended 2010","Passed 1969, force 1971; amended 2015","Passed 1971, force 1971; amended 2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"This act passed in parliament in 1971 and come into force or legalised in 1972 across the country. Last amendment in this act done in 2020.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1971/1972 and 2020 dates."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Match the MTP Act forms correctly: Which pairing is correct?","options":["Form-C: Consent form","Form-I: Monthly reporting form","Form-II: Admission for case records","Form-III: RMP opinion form"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Form-C: Consent form.\"  Why B wrong: Form-I is the RMP opinion form.  Why C wrong: Form-II is the monthly reporting form (to district authorities).  Why D wrong: Form-III is admission for case records."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the MTP Act 2020-21, termination based on failure of contraceptive is covered for:","options":["Only married women up to 20 weeks","Both married and unmarried women","Only unmarried women","Only women above 18 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table states MTP Act 2020-21 covers \"Both married and unmarried women\" for failure of contraceptive, whereas the 1971-72 Act was \"Upto 20 weeks only by a married women.\"  Why A wrong: That describes the old 1971-72 provision.  Why C/D wrong: Both married and unmarried are covered."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the MTP Act 2020-21, the gestation limits requiring 1 RMP and 2 RMP opinions respectively are:","options":["1 RMP: 12 weeks; 2 RMP: 20 weeks","1 RMP: 20 weeks; 2 RMP: 24 weeks","1 RMP: 24 weeks; 2 RMP: 28 weeks","1 RMP: 20 weeks; 2 RMP: 20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2020-21 column states \"1 RMP: 20 weeks; 2 RMP: 24 weeks\" (the 1971-72 Act was 1 RMP: 12 weeks, 2 RMP: 20 weeks).  Why A wrong: That is the old 1971-72 provision.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated 2020-21 limits."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the MTP Act 2020-21, the limit of gestation is up to 24 weeks specifically for:","options":["All women","Rape survivors","Married women only","Women over 35"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states \"Upto 24 weeks for rape survivors\" and \"Beyond 24 weeks for substantial foetal abnormality\" (decided by state-level medical board).  Why A/C/D wrong: The 24-week limit is specified for rape survivors, not these groups."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the MTP Act 2020-21, who decides in cases beyond 24 weeks (substantial foetal abnormality)?","options":["The RMP alone","State level medical board","The Supreme Court","District magistrate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"State level medical board decide in case of substantial foetal abnormality\" for termination beyond 24 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: The decision rests with the state-level medical board."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the MTP Act 2020-21, breach of confidentiality is punishable by:","options":["Fine up to Rs. 1000","Imprisonment up to a year, or fine, or both","No penalty","Cancellation of medical license only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2020-21 column states \"Imprisonment up to a year, or fine or both\" for breach of confidentiality (the 1971-72 Act had only a fine up to Rs. 1000).  Why A wrong: That is the older provision.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated penalty."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Per the Note under the MTP Act table, with 1 RMP opinion, MTP can be done at any time if:","options":["It is immediately necessary to save the pregnant woman's life, opinion formed in good faith","The woman requests it","The pregnancy is beyond 24 weeks","Two doctors disagree"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Note: With 1 RMP opinion, MTP can be done at any time if it is immediately necessary to save the pregnant women's life, opinion formed in good faith.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The condition is life-saving necessity in good faith."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the Family Planning Indemnity Scheme, the compensation for death following sterilization (including during the operation) in hospital or within 7 days from discharge (Section IA) is:","options":["Rs. 50,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 2 lakh","Rs. 25,000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Section IA (death following sterilization in hospital or within 7 days of discharge) = Rs. 2 lakh.  Why A wrong: Rs. 50,000 is Section IB (death within 8–30 days).  Why B wrong: Rs. 30,000 is Section IC (failure of sterilization).  Why D wrong: Rs. 25,000 is Section ID (cost of treatment for complications)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the indemnity scheme, the compensation for death following sterilization within 8–30 days from the date of discharge (Section IB) is:","options":["Rs. 2 lakh","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 25,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Section IB (death within 8–30 days from discharge) = Rs. 50,000.  Why A wrong: Rs. 2 lakh is Section IA (within 7 days).  Why C wrong: Rs. 30,000 is Section IC (failure of sterilization).  Why D wrong: Rs. 25,000 is Section ID (complication treatment cost)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the indemnity scheme, the compensation for failure of sterilization (Section IC) is:","options":["Rs. 2 lakh","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 25,000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Section IC (failure of sterilization) = Rs. 30,000.  Why A/B wrong: These are for death (IA and IB).  Why D wrong: Rs. 25,000 is for cost of treatment of complications (ID)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the indemnity scheme, the cost of treatment in hospital up to 60 days for complications following sterilization (Section ID) is limited to:","options":["Actual, not exceeding Rs. 25,000","Rs. 2 lakh","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 30,000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Section ID = \"Actual not exceeding Rs. 25,000\" for treatment of complications up to 60 days from discharge.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are the amounts for death (IA/IB) and failure (IC)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Under the indemnity scheme (Section II), the indemnity per doctor/health facility (not more than 4 in a year) is:","options":["Up to Rs. 2 lakh per claim","Rs. 30,000 per claim","Rs. 50,000 per claim","Rs. 25,000 per claim"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Section II: \"Indemnity per doctor/health facilities but not more than 4 in a year = Up to Rs. 2 lakh per claim.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated Section II limit."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Which of the following is a possible complication or the biggest risk associated with the MTP kit, per the disclaimer?","options":["Incomplete abortion (which can lead to infection, hemorrhage, and possible surgical intervention)","Permanent infertility","Ectopic pregnancy","Ovarian cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The disclaimer states the biggest risk with the MTP kit is incomplete abortion, which can lead to serious complications like infection and hemorrhage, sometimes requiring surgical intervention.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not cited as the MTP kit's key risk in the disclaimer."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Informed consent before a sterilization procedure (male or female) is:","options":["Optional","Required/must be taken before any sterilization procedure","Needed only for female sterilization","Needed only for male sterilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Informed Consent should be taken before any sterilization procedure either male or female sterilization.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Consent is mandatory for both male and female sterilization."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Tubectomy done concurrently with MTP is described as:","options":["The most common time of tubectomy, especially in rural areas","Contraindicated in all cases","Only done in urban hospitals","Rare and discouraged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Tubectomy also done with concurrent with MTP - it is most common time of tubectomy, specially in rural areas.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The source presents it as the most common timing, especially rurally."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Oral contraceptive pills advantages/disadvantages, Minipills…","question":"Female sterilization via the vaginal route is performed by:","options":["Laparoscopic sterilization","Mini-laparotomy","Hysteroscopy sterilization","Open laparotomy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Via vaginal - Hysteroscopy sterilization.\"  Why A/B wrong: These are abdominal-route methods.  Why D wrong: Open laparotomy is not the stated vaginal method."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What does the abbreviation PCPNDT stand for in the PCPNDT Act 1994?","options":["Pre-Conception and Post-Natal Diagnostic Technique Act","Pre-Conception and Pre-Natal Diagnostic Technique Act","Prenatal Care and Prenatal Diagnostic Testing Act","Population Control and Prenatal Diagnostic Technique Act"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Act's full form is Pre-Conception and Pre-Natal Diagnostic Technique Act 1994.  Why A wrong: It is \"Pre-Natal,\" not \"Post-Natal.\"  Why C wrong: This is not the recognized full form.  Why D wrong: The Act is not about population control by that name."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the main purpose of the PCPNDT Act 1994?","options":["To promote prenatal genetic screening for all pregnancies","To prohibit sex selection before and after conception and stop misuse of prenatal diagnostic techniques","To make ultrasound mandatory for every pregnant woman","To register all imaging centres for cancer diagnosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Act prohibits sex selection before and after conception and stops the misuse of prenatal diagnostic technique.  Why A wrong: It restricts, not promotes, diagnostic use for sex selection.  Why C wrong: The Act does not mandate ultrasound.  Why D wrong: Registration is a mechanism, not the main purpose."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the punishment for violating the PCPNDT Act 1994?","options":["1 year imprisonment and Rs. 5000 fine","3 year imprisonment and Rs. 10000 fine","5 year imprisonment and Rs. 50000 fine","Only a warning for the first offence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anyone who violates this Act faces 3 years imprisonment and Rs. 10000 fine.  Why A/C wrong: These durations and amounts do not match the source.  Why D wrong: A definite penalty is prescribed."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under the PCPNDT Act, which form is the application for registration or renewal of registration of a genetic counselling centre/laboratory/clinic/ultrasound clinic/imaging centre?","options":["Form-A","Form-D","Form-G","Form-H"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Form-A is the form of application for registration or renewal of registration of these centres.  Why B wrong: Form-D is for maintenance of records by the genetic counselling centre.  Why C wrong: Form-G is the form of consent for invasive techniques.  Why D wrong: Form-H is maintenance of permanent record of applications for registration."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under the PCPNDT Act, Form-D is used for what purpose?","options":["Consent for invasive techniques","Maintenance of records by the genetic counselling centre","Maintenance of records by the genetic laboratory","Records in case of a pregnant woman"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Form-D is for maintenance of records by the genetic counselling centre.  Why A wrong: That is Form-G.  Why C wrong: That is Form-E.  Why D wrong: That is Form-F."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which PCPNDT form is used for maintenance of records by the genetic laboratory?","options":["Form-D","Form-E","Form-F","Form-G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Form-E is for maintenance of records by the genetic laboratory.  Why A wrong: Form-D is for the genetic counselling centre.  Why C wrong: Form-F is for records in the case of a pregnant woman.  Why D wrong: Form-G is the consent form."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"PCPNDT Form-F is used for maintenance of records in which situation?","options":["Registration of a new clinic","In case of a pregnant woman by genetic clinic/ultrasound clinic/imaging centre","Consent for invasive techniques","Permanent record of applications"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Form-F is for maintenance of records in the case of a pregnant woman by genetic clinic/ultrasound clinic/imaging centre.  Why A wrong: Registration application is Form-A.  Why C wrong: Consent is Form-G.  Why D wrong: Permanent record of applications is Form-H."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under the PCPNDT Act, which form is the form of consent for invasive techniques?","options":["Form-E","Form-F","Form-G","Form-H"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Form-G is the form of consent for invasive techniques.  Why A wrong: Form-E is for genetic laboratory records.  Why B wrong: Form-F is for pregnant woman records.  Why D wrong: Form-H is permanent record of applications."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"PCPNDT Form-H is used for what?","options":["Application for registration","Maintenance of permanent record of applications for registration","Consent for invasive techniques","Records of a pregnant woman"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Form-H is for maintenance of permanent record of applications for registration.  Why A wrong: Application is Form-A.  Why C wrong: Consent is Form-G.  Why D wrong: Pregnant woman records use Form-F."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the mode of action of barrier contraceptive methods?","options":["Suppress ovulation","Prevent sperm from entering into the uterus (block the way)","Kill the sperm","Prevent implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Barrier methods prevent sperm from entering into the uterus by blocking the way.  Why A wrong: Suppressing ovulation is the action of OCPs.  Why C wrong: Killing sperm (spermicide) is the chemical method.  Why D wrong: Preventing implantation is the action of Centchroman."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following are examples of barrier contraceptive methods?","options":["Cream, jelly, foam","Condom, diaphragm, sponge, cervical cap","Mala N and Mala D","Cu T 380 A and Multiload Cu250"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Barrier method examples are condom (male & female), diaphragm, sponge, and cervical cap.  Why A wrong: These are chemical (spermicide) examples.  Why C wrong: Mala N/Mala D are oral contraceptives.  Why D wrong: Cu T and Multiload are IUDs."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Calendar or rhythm method, BBT (basal body temperature) and the ovulation method are examples of which contraceptive category?","options":["Natural methods","Biological methods","Chemical methods","Hormonal methods"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biological methods include calendar/rhythm method, BBT, and ovulation method.  Why A wrong: Natural methods examples are abstinence, withdrawal/coitus interruptus, LAM.  Why C wrong: Chemical methods use spermicides.  Why D wrong: These are non-hormonal fertility-awareness methods."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Abstinence, withdrawal (coitus interruptus) and LAM are examples of which contraceptive category?","options":["Barrier methods","Natural methods","Chemical methods","IUDs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Natural methods include abstinence, withdrawal or coitus interruptus, and LAM.  Why A wrong: Barrier methods are condom, diaphragm, etc.  Why C wrong: Chemical methods are spermicides.  Why D wrong: IUDs are Cu T, Multiload."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The mode of action of chemical contraceptive methods (spermicides) is:","options":["Suppress ovulation","Kill the sperm","Prevent implantation","Thicken cervical mucus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chemical methods use spermicides which kill the sperm.  Why A wrong: That is OCP action.  Why C wrong: That is Centchroman action.  Why D wrong: That is mini-pill/Norplant action."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the mode of action of IUDs, and how does copper contribute?","options":["They suppress ovulation; copper is inert","They prevent fertilization; copper acts as a spermicide","They thicken cervical mucus; copper blocks tubes","They prevent implantation only; copper has no role"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IUDs prevent fertilization and copper acts as a spermicide.  Why A wrong: They do not primarily suppress ovulation, and copper is active.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated mode of action."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Cu T 380 A, Cu T 250 and Multiload Cu250 are examples of which contraceptive method?","options":["Barrier methods","IUDs","Oral contraceptives","Injectable hormonal contraceptives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These are examples of intrauterine devices (IUDs).  Why A wrong: Barrier examples are condom, diaphragm.  Why C wrong: OCP examples are Mala N/Mala D.  Why D wrong: Injectables are DMPA/NET-EN."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the mode of action of oral contraceptive pills (OCPs), with examples Mala N and Mala D?","options":["Suppress ovulation","Kill the sperm","Block the cervix","Prevent implantation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: OCPs suppress ovulation; examples are Mala N and Mala D.  Why B wrong: Killing sperm is a chemical method.  Why C wrong: Blocking is a barrier method.  Why D wrong: Preventing implantation is Centchroman."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Mini pills (lactation pills / POPs) work by which mechanism?","options":["Suppress ovulation","Thickening the cervical mucus which decreases sperm motility","Prevent implantation","Act as a spermicide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mini pills (progestin only pills, POPs) thicken cervical mucus, decreasing sperm motility.  Why A wrong: That is combined OCP action.  Why C wrong: That is Centchroman action.  Why D wrong: Spermicide is a chemical method."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Estrogen derivatives (Centchroman), with examples Chhaya and Saheli, act by:","options":["Suppressing ovulation","Preventing implantation of the fertilised ovum","Killing the sperm","Blocking the cervix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Centchroman (Chhaya, Saheli) prevents implantation of the fertilised ovum.  Why A wrong: That is OCP action.  Why C wrong: Spermicide is chemical.  Why D wrong: Blocking is a barrier method."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Injectable hormonal contraceptives such as DMPA (Antara Injection) and NET-EN act by:","options":["Thickening cervical mucus only","Inhibiting ovulation by suppressing the mid-cycle LH peak","Killing sperm","Preventing implantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Injectable hormonal contraceptives inhibit ovulation by suppressing the mid-cycle LH peak; examples DMPA and NET-EN.  Why A wrong: That is Norplant/mini-pill action.  Why C/D wrong: These are chemical/Centchroman actions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The Norplant system (Norplant I, II and Implanon) primarily works by:","options":["Preventing implantation","Thickening the cervical mucus which decreases sperm motility","Killing the sperm","Blocking the fallopian tubes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Norplant system thickens cervical mucus which decreases sperm motility; examples Norplant I, II and Implanon.  Why A wrong: That is Centchroman.  Why C wrong: Spermicide is chemical.  Why D wrong: Not described as tubal blockage."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The post-coital (emergency) contraceptive hormone Levonorgestrel (Unwanted-72) is given at what dose?","options":["0.25 mg","0.5 mg","0.75 mg","1.5 mg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The post-coital hormone Levonorgestrel is 0.75 mg (Unwanted-72), preventing fertilization and implantation.  Why A/B/D wrong: These doses do not match the source value of 0.75 mg."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Copper IUDs can be used as post-coital (emergency) contraception within how many days of unwanted conception?","options":["2 days","3 days","5 days","7 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Copper IUDs within 5 days can prevent conception following unwanted conception.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 5 days."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"A geriatric nurse (gerontological nurse) is best described as a nurse who:","options":["Cares only for newborns","Helps care for aging and elderly individuals with special training for their needs","Works only in intensive care units","Provides only palliative cancer care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A geriatric/gerontological nurse helps care for aging and elderly individuals and has special training to understand their special needs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the defined role focused on the elderly."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which occupational disease (pneumoconiosis) is caused by exposure to molasses or sugarcane dust?","options":["Byssinosis","Bagassosis","Siderosis","Stannosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bagassosis is caused by molasses or sugarcane dust.  Why A wrong: Byssinosis is from cotton fibre dust.  Why C wrong: Siderosis is from iron dust.  Why D wrong: Stannosis is from tin dust."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Byssinosis is an occupational lung disease caused by exposure to:","options":["Coal dust","Cotton fibre dust","Silica dust","Asbestos dust"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Byssinosis is caused by cotton fibre dust.  Why A wrong: Coal dust causes anthracosis.  Why C wrong: Silica dust causes silicosis.  Why D wrong: Asbestos dust causes asbestosis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Anthracosis, a pneumoconiosis, results from exposure to which dust?","options":["Coal dust","Iron dust","Tin dust","Beryllium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anthracosis is caused by coal dust.  Why B wrong: Iron dust causes siderosis.  Why C wrong: Tin dust causes stannosis.  Why D wrong: Beryllium causes berylliosis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Silicosis is caused by inhalation of:","options":["Silica dust","Asbestos dust","Cotton dust","Moldy hay"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Silicosis is caused by silica dust.  Why B wrong: Asbestos dust causes asbestosis.  Why C wrong: Cotton dust causes byssinosis.  Why D wrong: Moldy hay causes Farmer's Lung."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Berylliosis is an occupational disease caused by exposure to:","options":["Beryllium","Iron dust","Tin dust","Avian droppings"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Berylliosis is caused by beryllium.  Why B wrong: Iron dust causes siderosis.  Why C wrong: Tin dust causes stannosis.  Why D wrong: Avian/bird droppings cause Bird fancier's lung."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"\"Farmer's Lung,\" an occupational disease, is caused by exposure to:","options":["Moldy hay","Coal dust","Silica dust","Iron dust"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Farmer's Lung is caused by moldy hay.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause anthracosis, silicosis, and siderosis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Match the occupational disease to its dust: Siderosis, Stannosis and Bird fancier's lung are caused respectively by:","options":["Tin dust, iron dust, avian droppings","Iron dust, tin dust, avian/bird droppings","Iron dust, avian droppings, tin dust","Coal dust, tin dust, moldy hay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Siderosis = iron dust, Stannosis = tin dust, Bird fancier's lung = avian/bird droppings.  Why A wrong: Siderosis and stannosis are swapped.  Why C wrong: Stannosis and bird fancier's lung are swapped.  Why D wrong: Siderosis is iron, not coal, dust."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Asbestosis is caused by exposure to:","options":["Silica dust","Asbestos dust","Cotton dust","Coal dust"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asbestosis is caused by asbestos dust.  Why A/C/D wrong: These cause silicosis, byssinosis and anthracosis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In which year was the National Family Planning Programme started in India?","options":["1948","1952","1953","1955"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The National Family Planning Programme was started in India in 1952 — India was the first developing-world country to start one.  Why A/C/D wrong: These years correspond to other events (NMCP 1953, NFCP/NLCP 1955)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In 1977 the National Family Planning Programme was renamed as:","options":["National Rural Health Mission","National Family Welfare Programme","Reproductive & Child Health Programme","National Population Programme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1977 the programme was renamed the National Family Welfare Programme.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different programmes/missions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In which period was India's first population policy launched?","options":["1952","1976-77","1983","2000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1976-77 the first population policy was launched in India.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The National Malaria Control Programme (NMCP) was started in India in which year and was based on which method?","options":["1953; indoor residual spraying with DDT twice a year","1952; larvicidal oiling once a year","1955; source reduction only","1962; BCG vaccination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NMCP started in 1953, based on indoor residual spraying with DDT (1 gm per m² surface area) twice a year.  Why B/C/D wrong: These years/methods do not match NMCP."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What was the DDT dose used in the National Malaria Control Programme's indoor residual spraying?","options":["0.5 gm per m² surface area","1 gm per m² surface area","2 gm per m² surface area","5 gm per m² surface area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The programme used 1 gm of DDT per m² surface area twice a year.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 1 gm per m²."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The National Malaria Eradication Programme (NMEP) was rolled out in 1958 and was later replaced (in 1999) by which programme?","options":["National Anti-Malaria Programme","National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme","Urban Malaria Scheme","Revised National Malaria Programme"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NMEP was replaced by the National Anti-Malaria Programme in 1999 by the Govt. of India.  Why B wrong: NVBDCP launched in 2003.  Why C wrong: Urban Malaria Scheme was integrated with NFCP.  Why D wrong: No such programme named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The National Filaria Control Programme (NFCP), started in 1955, was integrated with which scheme in June 1978?","options":["National Malaria Eradication Programme","Urban Malaria Scheme","Pulse Polio Programme","Universal Immunization Programme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NFCP (1955) was integrated with the Urban Malaria Scheme in June 1978.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are separate programmes not integrated with NFCP."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following are components of the filarial control strategy under NFCP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anti-larval operation","Source reduction","Detection and treatment of Microfilaria carriers","Indoor residual DDT spraying twice a year","Morbidity management & IEC"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Filarial control strategy includes anti-larval operation, source reduction, detection and treatment of Microfilaria carriers, and morbidity management & IEC.  Why D wrong: DDT indoor residual spraying belongs to the malaria control programme, not the listed filarial strategy."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The National Leprosy Control Programme (NLCP), started in 1955, was converted into the National Leprosy Eradication Programme in which year?","options":["1982","1983","1993","1997"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1983 NLCP was converted into the National Leprosy Eradication Programme.  Why A wrong: 1982 is when MDT therapy was included.  Why C wrong: 1993 is when WHO declared TB a global emergency.  Why D wrong: 1997 is when RNTCP started in India."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"MDT (multi-drug therapy) was included in the leprosy programme in 1982. What is the most common drug used in MDT therapy?","options":["Rifampicin","Clofazimine","Dapsone","Isoniazid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common drug in MDT therapy is Dapsone (MDT included in 1982).  Why A/B wrong: Though used in MDT, the source names Dapsone as the most common.  Why D wrong: Isoniazid is an anti-TB drug."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In which year was the National Tuberculosis Control Programme (NTCP) started in India?","options":["1953","1955","1962","1974"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NTCP was started in 1962 for the prevention of tuberculosis (TB).  Why A/B/D wrong: These match NMCP, NFCP/NLCP, and UIP respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"WHO declared tuberculosis a global emergency in 1993. In which year was the Revised National Tuberculosis Control Programme (RNTCP) started in India?","options":["1993","1995","1997","2005"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: RNTCP was started in India in 1997 (WHO declared TB a global emergency in 1993).  Why A wrong: 1993 is the WHO declaration year.  Why B/D wrong: These do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The National AIDS Control Programme (NACP) in India was formulated and started in which years?","options":["Formulated 1987, started 1992","Formulated 1992, started 1997","Formulated 1985, started 1990","Formulated 1997, started 2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NACP was formulated in 1987 and started in 1992 in India.  Why B/C/D wrong: These years do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The National AIDS Control Organization (NACO) is the national monitoring agency for HIV/AIDS. Where is its headquarters and when was it established?","options":["Mumbai; established 1987","New Delhi; established 1992","Chennai; established 1997","New Delhi; established 2005"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NACO headquarters is at New Delhi and it was established in 1992.  Why A/C/D wrong: These city/year combinations do not match."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under NACO, what does ICTC stand for?","options":["Integrated Cancer Treatment Centre","Integrated Counselling & Testing Centre","Indian Communicable & Tropical Centre","Institutional Care and Testing Committee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ICTC is the Integrated Counselling & Testing Centre, a centre for AIDS patients.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The Suraksha Clinic runs under NACO and supplies colour-coded kits for STDs/RTIs. Kit-1 (grey colour) is used for which conditions?","options":["Vaginitis","Urethral, anorectal discharge and cervicitis","Genital ulcers","Scrotal swelling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit-1 (grey) is for urethral, anorectal discharge and cervicitis.  Why A wrong: Vaginitis is Kit-2 (green).  Why C wrong: Genital ulcers are Kits 3, 4, 5.  Why D wrong: Scrotal swelling is Kit-7 (black)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In the Suraksha Clinic colour-coded kit system, Kit-2 (green colour) is used for:","options":["Genital ulcers","Vaginitis","Lower abdominal pain","Scrotal swelling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit-2 (green) is for vaginitis.  Why A wrong: Genital ulcers are Kits 3–5.  Why C wrong: Lower abdominal pain is Kit-6 (yellow).  Why D wrong: Scrotal swelling is Kit-7 (black)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which Suraksha Clinic kits (by colour) are all used for genital ulcers?","options":["Kit-3 (white), Kit-4 (blue), Kit-5 (red)","Kit-1 (grey), Kit-2 (green), Kit-3 (white)","Kit-5 (red), Kit-6 (yellow), Kit-7 (black)","Kit-2 (green), Kit-4 (blue), Kit-6 (yellow)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kit-3 (white), Kit-4 (blue) and Kit-5 (red) are all for genital ulcers.  Why B wrong: Kit-1 and Kit-2 are for discharge/cervicitis and vaginitis.  Why C/D wrong: Include kits for abdominal pain/scrotal swelling/vaginitis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In the Suraksha Clinic kits, which kit is yellow (lower abdominal pain) and which is black (scrotal swelling)?","options":["Kit-5 yellow; Kit-6 black","Kit-6 yellow; Kit-7 black","Kit-7 yellow; Kit-6 black","Kit-4 yellow; Kit-5 black"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kit-6 is yellow for lower abdominal pain and Kit-7 is black for scrotal swelling.  Why A/C/D wrong: These kit numbers/colours are mismatched."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The National Programme for Control of Blindness (NPCB) was launched in which year?","options":["1974","1976","1978","1995"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The National Programme for Control of Blindness was launched in 1976.  Why A/C/D wrong: These years match UIP (1974), EPI in India (1978), and Pulse Polio (1995)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"According to the NPCB content, what is the major cause of blindness in India and its percentage?","options":["Refractive error – 62.60%","Cataract – 62.60%","Glaucoma – 40%","Diabetic retinopathy – 25%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The major cause of blindness in India is Cataract at 62.60%.  Why A wrong: Refractive error is the second most common cause.  Why C/D wrong: These are not stated as the major cause."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the second most common cause of blindness in India per the NPCB content?","options":["Cataract","Refractive error","Glaucoma","Trachoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Refractive error is the second most common cause of blindness.  Why A wrong: Cataract is the leading cause.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated as the second cause."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) was globally started in 1974 and in India it was started in 1978 as which programme?","options":["Expanded Immunization Programme (EPI)","Pulse Polio Immunization","Reproductive & Child Health","Child Survival & Safe Motherhood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In India it started in 1978 as the Expanded Immunization Programme (EPI).  Why B wrong: Pulse Polio started in 1995.  Why C/D wrong: RCH and CSSM are related but different programmes."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The EPI (1978) originally aimed to prevent 6 vaccine-preventable diseases. Which set correctly lists them?","options":["Diphtheria, pertussis, tetanus, polio, tuberculosis, measles","Diphtheria, tetanus, polio, hepatitis-B, measles, rubella","Tuberculosis, polio, hepatitis-B, measles, mumps, rubella","Pertussis, tetanus, cholera, typhoid, polio, measles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: EPI aimed to prevent diphtheria, pertussis, tetanus, polio, tuberculosis and measles (6 diseases).  Why B/C/D wrong: These include diseases not in the original 6."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In which year was the EPI renamed as the Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)?","options":["1978","1985","1997","2005"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1985, EPI was renamed the Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) as part of the CSSM programme.  Why A wrong: 1978 is when EPI started in India.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the renaming year."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under which mission is the UIP presently running, and since which year?","options":["National Rural Health Mission (NRHM), since 2005","National Health Mission, since 1997","Reproductive & Child Health, since 1978","Child Survival & Safe Motherhood, since 1985"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: UIP presently runs under/as part of the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) since 2005.  Why B/C/D wrong: These missions/years do not match the stated present status."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"How many vaccine-preventable diseases are presently covered under the UIP?","options":["6","8","10","12"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Presently 12 vaccine-preventable diseases are covered under the UIP.  Why A wrong: 6 was the original EPI number.  Why B/C wrong: Not the current count."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following is included among the 12 vaccine-preventable diseases covered under UIP?","options":["Cholera","Japanese encephalitis","Typhoid","Mumps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Japanese encephalitis is item 12 in the UIP list of 12 diseases.  Why A/C/D wrong: Cholera, typhoid and mumps are not in the listed 12."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In the UIP list of 12 diseases, Rota virus is associated with the prevention of which condition?","options":["Pneumonia","Diarrhoea","Meningitis","Measles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Item 11 is Diarrhoea (Rota virus).  Why A wrong: Pneumonia is linked to Haemophilus influenza type B.  Why C wrong: Meningitis is under pneumococcal diseases.  Why D wrong: Measles is a separate listed disease."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In the UIP list, pneumonia is caused/prevented in relation to which organism, and pneumococcal diseases include which conditions?","options":["Haemophilus influenza type B; pneumococcal pneumonia and meningitis","Rota virus; diarrhoea and dysentery","RSV; bronchitis and asthma","Streptococcus pyogenes; pharyngitis and rheumatic fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Item 6 is Pneumonia (Haemophilus influenza type B) and item 7 is Pneumococcal diseases (pneumococcal pneumonia and meningitis).  Why B/C/D wrong: These organisms/conditions do not match the UIP listing."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The Pulse Polio Immunization Programme was started in India in which month and year?","options":["December 1995","January 1997","April 2005","October 1978"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Pulse Polio Immunization Programme was started in India in December 1995.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dates match RNTCP, RCH-2, and EPI respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under the Pulse Polio Programme, Oral Polio Vaccine (OPV) is given to children of which age group (as later expanded)?","options":["0 to 1 year","0 to 3 years initially, later expanded up to 5 years","1 to 5 years only","6 to 10 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Initially children up to 3 years were covered, later changed up to 5 years; OPV given orally to 0–5 year children.  Why A/C/D wrong: These age ranges do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under the RCH programme, the Child Survival & Safe Motherhood (CSSM) programme was replaced by RCH in which year, and when did RCH-2 start?","options":["CSSM replaced in 1997; RCH-2 started in April 2005","CSSM replaced in 2005; RCH-2 started in 1997","CSSM replaced in 1992; RCH-2 started in 2000","CSSM replaced in 1978; RCH-2 started in 1985"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSSM was replaced by RCH in 1997 (RCH-1 in 1997) and RCH-2 started in April 2005.  Why B/C/D wrong: These years do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the full form of IMNCI?","options":["Integrated Management of Neonatal and Childhood Illness","Indian Model for Newborn Care Initiative","Integrated Maternal and Newborn Care Intervention","Immunization and Maternal Nutrition Care Initiative"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IMNCI = Integrated Management of Neonatal And Childhood Illness.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In which year was IMNCI started in India?","options":["1997","2003","2005","2011"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IMNCI was started in India in 2003.  Why A/C/D wrong: These match RCH-1, RCH-2/NRHM, and HBNC/FBNC respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In IMNCI colour coding based on severity, pink colour indicates:","options":["Treatment at home","Treatment at OPD basis/PHC","Refer to CHC/Hospital","No treatment needed"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pink colour = refer to CHC/Hospital (most severe).  Why A wrong: Green = treatment at home.  Why B wrong: Yellow = treatment at OPD/PHC.  Why D wrong: All colours require action."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In IMNCI colour coding, yellow and green colours respectively indicate:","options":["Refer to hospital; treatment at OPD","Treatment at OPD basis/PHC; treatment at home","Treatment at home; refer to hospital","No treatment; refer to hospital"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Yellow = treatment at OPD basis/PHC; Green = treatment at home.  Why A/C/D wrong: These reverse or misassign the colour meanings."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following are components of IMNCI? (Select all that apply)","options":["Improve health worker's skills","Improve health system or infrastructures","Improve family & community participation","Increase hospital bed capacity"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The three components of IMNCI are improving health worker's skills, health system/infrastructures, and family & community participation.  Why D wrong: Increasing bed capacity is not listed as an IMNCI component."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"IMNCI targets children of which age group?","options":["Below 1 year","Below 5 years of age","5 to 10 years","0 to 18 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The target group for IMNCI is children below 5 years of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated target group."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following diseases are prevented under IMNCI? (Select all that apply)","options":["Acute respiratory diseases (ARDS/Pneumonia)","Diarrhoea and Measles","Malaria and Malnutrition","Pre-term babies & sepsis","Hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: IMNCI prevents acute respiratory diseases (ARDS/Pneumonia), diarrhoea, measles, malaria, malnutrition, and pre-term babies & sepsis.  Why E wrong: Hypertension is not listed among IMNCI-prevented conditions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The Home Based New Born Care (HBNC) programme was launched by the Government of India in which year, and home visits are performed by whom?","options":["2011; by ASHA workers","2005; by ANMs","2014; by Anganwadi workers","2003; by Mukhya Sevikas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HBNC was launched in 2011 with home visits by ASHA workers.  Why B/C/D wrong: These years/workers do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under HBNC, how many visits should ASHA perform in the case of institutional deliveries and on which days?","options":["7 visits on Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42","6 visits on Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42","5 visits on Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 28","4 visits on Days 3, 7, 21, 42"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 6 visits should be done for institutional deliveries on Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42.  Why A wrong: 7 visits (Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42) is for home-based deliveries.  Why C/D wrong: These counts/schedules do not match."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under HBNC, how many visits are done for home-based deliveries and on which days?","options":["6 visits on Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42","7 visits on Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42","5 visits on Days 1, 7, 14, 28, 42","8 visits on Days 1–8"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 7 visits should be done for home-based deliveries on Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42.  Why A wrong: 6 visits is for institutional deliveries.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What remuneration is provided to ASHA per visit under the HBNC programme?","options":["Rs. 100","Rs. 250","Rs. 500","Rs. 600"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rs. 250 per visit is provided to ASHA under HBNC.  Why A/C/D wrong: These amounts do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The India New Born Action Plan was launched in India in which year?","options":["2011","2013","2014","2005"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The India New Born Action Plan was launched in India in 2014.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the source year."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Facility Based New Born Care (FBNC), launched in India in 2011, is based on specialised care for very sick newborns at which units?","options":["Special New Born Care Units (SNCU)","Intensive Care Units (ICU)","Primary Health Centres (PHC)","Anganwadi centres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FBNC (2011) is based on specialised care for very sick newborns at Special New Born Care Units (SNCU); every district/tertiary care hospital should have an SNCU.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the units specified for FBNC."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In which year was the ICDS (Integrated Child Development Scheme) started in India?","options":["1952","1975","1985","2003"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ICDS was started in India in 1975.  Why A/C/D wrong: These match family planning, UIP renaming, and IMNCI respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What does ICDS stand for?","options":["Integrated Child Development Scheme","Indian Community Development Society","Institutional Care and Delivery Scheme","Integrated Childhood Disease Surveillance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICDS = Integrated Child Development Scheme.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following are ICDS beneficiaries? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pre-school children below 6 years","Adolescent girls 11 to 18 years","Pregnant & lactating mothers","Elderly persons above 60 years"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: ICDS beneficiaries are pre-school children below 6 years, adolescent girls 11–18 years, and pregnant & lactating mothers.  Why D wrong: The elderly are not listed as ICDS beneficiaries."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Who is considered the \"heart of the ICDS system\"?","options":["ASHA worker","Anganwadi worker","Mukhya Sevika","Auxiliary Nurse Midwife"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Anganwadi worker is the heart of the ICDS system.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the heart of ICDS."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the population norm for one Anganwadi centre in tribal areas, and for a mini Anganwadi centre?","options":["1 centre per 300-800 (tribal); mini centre for 150-300","1 centre per 1000 (tribal); mini centre for 500","1 centre per 100-200 (tribal); mini centre for 50-100","1 centre per 800-1000 (tribal); mini centre for 300-500"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1 Anganwadi centre per 300-800 population in tribal areas and a mini Anganwadi centre for 150-300 population.  Why B/C/D wrong: These population norms do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Each Anganwadi worker covers a population of approximately:","options":["500","1000","1500","3000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each Anganwadi worker covers a population of about 1000.  Why A/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under the ICDS programme, who works over/above the Anganwadi workers?","options":["Mukhya Sevikas","ASHA workers","ANMs","Medical officers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mukhya Sevikas (Lady Supervisors) supervise the Anganwadi workers, i.e. they work above them in the ICDS hierarchy. [Note: the source text reads \"Mukhya Sevikas are working under Anganwadi workers,\" which is worded backwards; in ICDS the Mukhya Sevika is the supervisor above the AWWs.]  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified in this supervisory relationship in the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The ICDS scheme offers a package of how many services?","options":["Four","Five","Six","Seven"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ICDS scheme offers a package of six services.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies six."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following is one of the six services offered under ICDS? (Select all that apply)","options":["Supplementary nutrition","Pre-school non-formal education","Nutrition & health education","Immunization","Health check-up","Referral service"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E,F","rationale":"Why correct: The six ICDS services are supplementary nutrition, pre-school non-formal education, nutrition & health education, immunization, health check-up, and referral service.  Why any excluded would be wrong: All six are the listed services."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The Under Five Clinic in India was started in 1978 for which age group of children?","options":["0 to 1 year","0 to 5 years","1 to 5 years","6 to 10 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Under Five Clinic was started in 1978 for 0 to 5 years children.  Why A/C/D wrong: These age ranges do not match."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the symbol of the Under Five Clinic, and what does the inner red-coloured triangle represent?","options":["Circle; represents immunization","Triangle; inner red triangle represents family planning","Square; inner red square represents growth monitoring","Star; inner red star represents health education"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The symbol of the Under Five Clinic is a Triangle, and the inner red-coloured triangle is the symbol of family planning.  Why A/C/D wrong: These shapes/meanings do not match."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following are the five major components of the Under Five Clinic? (Select all that apply)","options":["Care of illness","Preventive care","Growth monitoring","Family Planning","Health Education"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The five major components are care of illness, preventive care, growth monitoring, family planning (red colour), and health education.  Why any excluded would be wrong: All five are listed components."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In which year was the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP) launched in India?","options":["1997","2003","2005","2011"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NVBDCP was launched in India in 2003.  Why A/C/D wrong: These match RNTCP, RCH-2/NRHM, and HBNC/FBNC respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"How many diseases are covered under the NVBDCP (trick KFC v/s MDJ)?","options":["Four","Five","Six","Seven"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 6 diseases are covered: Kala Azar, Filariasis/lymphatic filariasis, Chikungunya, Malaria, Dengue, Japanese encephalitis (trick KFC v/s MDJ).  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies six."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following diseases are covered under the NVBDCP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Kala Azar","Chikungunya","Dengue","Malaria","Japanese encephalitis","Tuberculosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: NVBDCP covers Kala Azar, Chikungunya, Dengue, Filariasis, Malaria and Japanese encephalitis.  Why F wrong: Tuberculosis is covered under RNTCP, not NVBDCP."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"According to the source, which of the following diseases are transmitted by the Aedes mosquito? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dengue fever","Yellow fever","West Nile fever","Chikungunya","Zika virus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists dengue fever, yellow fever, West Nile fever, chikungunya, eastern equine encephalitis and Zika virus as transmitted by Aedes mosquito.  Why any excluded would be wrong: All listed options are Aedes-transmitted per the source. [Note: dengue, yellow fever, chikungunya and Zika are classic Aedes-borne; West Nile virus is transmitted primarily by Culex mosquitoes (Aedes albopictus is only a secondary vector), so the stem is answered strictly per the source as flagged.]"},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The National Water Supply & Sanitation Program was launched in India in which year?","options":["1952","1954","1975","2003"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The National Water Supply & Sanitation Program was launched in 1954.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source year."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What are the sanitation targets under the National Water Supply & Sanitation Program?","options":["100% urban and 100% rural sanitation","50% urban sanitation and 25% rural sanitation","25% urban sanitation and 50% rural sanitation","75% urban and 50% rural sanitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Targets are 100% rural & urban water supply, 50% urban sanitation and 25% rural sanitation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the source targets."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under ARWSP, a \"Problem Village\" is defined as one where no source of safe water is available within a distance/depth of:","options":["1 km / 10 m deep","1.6 km / 15 m deep","2 km / 20 m deep","0.5 km / 5 m deep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A \"Problem Village\" has no safe water source within 1.6 km / 15 m deep (or excess salinity/toxins, or cholera risk).  Why A/C/D wrong: These distances/depths do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the other name of the SUMAN scheme, and which ministry launched it?","options":["Surakshit Matritva Aashwasan; Ministry of Health & Family Welfare","Safe Motherhood Assurance; Ministry of Women & Child Development","Suraksha Matritva Abhiyan; Ministry of Rural Development","Surakshit Mahila Aashwasan; Ministry of Social Justice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SUMAN = Surakshit Matritva Aashwasan scheme, launched by the Ministry of Health & Family Welfare.  Why B/C/D wrong: These names/ministries do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What is the launch date and aim of the SUMAN scheme?","options":["10th October 2019; to achieve zero preventable maternal and newborn deaths","1st January 2017; to compensate wage loss","12th April 2005; to promote institutional delivery","1st June 2011; free cashless delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SUMAN was launched on 10th October 2019 with the aim to achieve zero preventable maternal and newborn deaths.  Why B wrong: That describes PMMVY.  Why C wrong: That describes JSY.  Why D wrong: That describes JSSK."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following are beneficiaries of the SUMAN initiative? (Select all that apply)","options":["All pregnant women","All mothers/lactating women up to 6 months post delivery","All sick newborns","All elderly patients"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: SUMAN beneficiaries are all pregnant women, all mothers/lactating women up to 6 months post delivery, and all sick newborns.  Why D wrong: The elderly are not SUMAN beneficiaries."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which ministry launched the \"Mission Shakti\" integrated women empowerment programme?","options":["Ministry of Health & Family Welfare","Ministry of Women & Child Development, GOI","Ministry of Rural Development","Ministry of Social Justice & Empowerment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ministry of Women & Child Development, GOI launched Mission Shakti.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ministries did not launch Mission Shakti per the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Mission Shakti is an umbrella scheme for the safety, security and empowerment of women for implementation during which Finance Commission period, effective from which date?","options":["15th Finance Commission period 2021-22 to 2025-26; effective 01 April 2022","14th Finance Commission period 2015-20; effective 01 April 2015","15th Finance Commission period 2020-25; effective 01 January 2021","16th Finance Commission period 2026-31; effective 01 April 2026"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mission Shakti applies during the 15th Finance Commission period 2021-22 to 2025-26, with norms effective from 01 April 2022.  Why B/C/D wrong: These periods/dates do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What are the two sub-schemes of Mission Shakti?","options":["Sambal and Samarthya","Ujjwala and Swadhar","Sabla and Saksham","Sakhi and Shakti"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mission Shakti has two sub-schemes: 'Sambal' and 'Samarthya'.  Why B wrong: Ujjwala and Swadhar are schemes within Samarthya.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the named sub-schemes."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In Mission Shakti, the 'Sambal' sub-scheme is for what purpose?","options":["Empowerment of women","Safety and security of women","Nutrition of children","Water supply and sanitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sambal is for the safety and security of women.  Why A wrong: Empowerment of women is 'Samarthya'.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to Sambal."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following schemes are included under the 'Sambal' component of Mission Shakti? (Select all that apply)","options":["One Stop Centre (OSC)","Women Helpline (WHL)","Beti Bachao Beti Padhao (BBBP)","Nari Adalats","Ujjwala"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Sambal consists of One Stop Centre (OSC), Women Helpline (WHL), Beti Bachao Beti Padhao (BBBP) and Nari Adalats.  Why E wrong: Ujjwala is under 'Samarthya', not Sambal."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following schemes are included under the 'Samarthya' component of Mission Shakti? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ujjwala","Swadhar Greh and Working Women Hostel","National Creche Scheme for children of working mothers","Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY)","Women Helpline (WHL)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Samarthya (empowerment of women) consists of Ujjwala, Swadhar Greh & Working Women Hostel, National Creche Scheme, and PMMVY.  Why E wrong: Women Helpline is under Sambal."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"The Ujjwala Scheme (noted under Mission Shakti) is a comprehensive scheme for:","options":["Provision of LPG cylinders to BPL households","Prevention of trafficking and rescue, rehabilitation and re-integration of victims of trafficking and commercial sexual exploitation","Free antenatal care for pregnant women","Immunization of children under 5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states Ujjwala Scheme is a comprehensive scheme for prevention of trafficking and rescue, rehabilitation and re-integration of victims of trafficking and commercial sexual exploitation.  Why A wrong: That is the PM Ujjwala LPG scheme, not the one described here.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to Ujjwala."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) is a safe motherhood intervention under which mission, and when was it launched?","options":["National Health Mission (NHM); 12th April 2005","Ministry of Women & Child Development; 1st January 2017","NRHM; December 1995","Ministry of Health; 1st June 2011"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: JSY is a safe motherhood intervention under the National Health Mission (NHM), launched on 12th April 2005.  Why B wrong: That describes PMMVY.  Why C wrong: December 1995 is Pulse Polio.  Why D wrong: 1st June 2011 is JSSK."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"JSY is what type of scheme, and what is the ASHA-to-population ratio it identifies?","options":["50% centrally sponsored; one ASHA per 500 population","100% centrally sponsored; one ASHA per 1000 population","State-funded; one ASHA per 2000 population","100% centrally sponsored; one ASHA per 300 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: JSY is a 100% centrally sponsored scheme; it identifies ASHA (one ASHA/1000 populations) as an effective link between govt. and pregnant women.  Why A/C/D wrong: These funding/ratio combinations do not match."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under the JSY cash entitlement table, in a rural (LPS) area what is the mother's package, ASHA's package and total?","options":["Mother 1400, ASHA 600, Total 2000","Mother 1000, ASHA 400, Total 1400","Mother 700, ASHA 600, Total 1300","Mother 600, ASHA 400, Total 1000"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For LPS rural: Mother's package 1400 + ASHA's package 600 = Total 2000.  Why B wrong: That is LPS urban (1000 + 400 = 1400).  Why C wrong: That is HPS rural (700 + 600 = 1300).  Why D wrong: That is HPS urban (600 + 400 = 1000)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under the JSY cash entitlement table, in an urban HPS area the mother's package, ASHA's package and total are:","options":["1400 + 600 = 2000","1000 + 400 = 1400","700 + 600 = 1300","600 + 400 = 1000"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: HPS urban: Mother's package 600 + ASHA's package 400 = Total 1000.  Why A wrong: That is LPS rural.  Why B wrong: That is LPS urban.  Why C wrong: That is HPS rural."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In the JSY scheme, the ASHA package of Rs. 600 in rural areas includes which components?","options":["Rs. 300 for ANC and Rs. 300 for facilitating institutional delivery","Rs. 200 for ANC and Rs. 400 for delivery","Rs. 400 for ANC and Rs. 200 for delivery","Rs. 600 entirely for transport"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The rural ASHA package of Rs. 600 includes Rs. 300 for ANC and Rs. 300 for facilitating institutional delivery.  Why B/C wrong: These splits do not match the rural package.  Why D wrong: It is split between ANC and delivery, not transport."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"In the JSY scheme, the ASHA package of Rs. 400 in urban areas is split as:","options":["Rs. 200 for ANC and Rs. 200 for facilitating institutional delivery","Rs. 300 for ANC and Rs. 100 for delivery","Rs. 100 for ANC and Rs. 300 for delivery","Rs. 400 entirely for ANC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The urban ASHA package of Rs. 400 includes Rs. 200 for ANC and Rs. 200 for facilitating institutional delivery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These splits do not match the urban package."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakaram (JSSK) was launched by the Government of India on which date, and from which state?","options":["1st June 2011 from Haryana","12th April 2005 from Rajasthan","1st January 2017 from Delhi","10th October 2019 from Gujarat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: JSSK was launched on 1st June 2011 from Haryana.  Why B wrong: That is JSY's launch.  Why C wrong: That is PMMVY's launch.  Why D wrong: That is SUMAN's launch."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"JSSK provides completely free and cashless services to pregnant women and sick newborns up to how many days after birth?","options":["7 days","15 days","28 days","30 days"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: JSSK covers sick newborns up to 30 days after birth (now expanded to cover sick infants).  Why A/B/C wrong: The source specifies 30 days."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under JSSK entitlements for pregnant women, free diet is provided during stay for how long in normal delivery and C-section?","options":["Up to 3 days in normal delivery and 7 days in C-Section","Up to 7 days in normal delivery and 3 days in C-Section","Up to 5 days in both","Up to 2 days in normal delivery and 5 days in C-Section"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Free diet during stay is up to 3 days in normal delivery and 7 days in C-Section.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under JSSK, free drop back from institutions to home is provided after how many hours of stay (for pregnant women)?","options":["24 hrs","48 hrs","72 hrs","12 hrs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Free drop back from institutions to home is after 48 hrs stay.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 48 hrs."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following are JSSK entitlements for sick newborns (up to 30 days)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Free treatment","Free drugs and consumables","Free provision of blood","Free transport from home to health institutions","Free drop back from institutions to home"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: JSSK sick-newborn entitlements include free treatment, free drugs and consumables, free diagnostics, free provision of blood, exemption from user charges, free transport home to institution, free transport between facilities on referral, and free drop back to home.  Why any excluded would be wrong: All listed options are stated entitlements."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Regarding emergency medical response ambulance services in India, which service is for critical care, trauma and accident victims?","options":["100","102","108","112"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 108 is a 24×7 free-of-cost service for all emergency medical conditions, primarily for critical care, trauma and accident victims.  Why A wrong: 100 is the police number (not stated here).  Why B wrong: 102 is for pregnant women, newborns and mothers under JSY & JSSK.  Why D wrong: Not the ambulance service specified."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which emergency ambulance service is 24×7 free of cost specifically for pregnant women, newborn babies and their mothers under JSY & JSSK?","options":["108","102","100","104"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 102 is the 24×7 free service for pregnant women, newborn babies and their mothers under JSY & JSSK.  Why A wrong: 108 is for critical care/trauma/accidents.  Why C/D wrong: Not the services specified."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY) is a centrally sponsored maternity benefit programme that replaced which earlier scheme?","options":["Indira Gandhi Matritva Sahyog Yojana (IGMSY)","Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY)","Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakaram (JSSK)","SUMAN scheme"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMMVY replaced the Indira Gandhi Matritva Sahyog Yojana (IGMSY).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate schemes, not replaced by PMMVY."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"What are the launch date and administering ministry of PMMVY?","options":["1st January 2017; Ministry of Women and Child Development","12th April 2005; Ministry of Health & Family Welfare","1st June 2011; Ministry of Rural Development","10th October 2019; Ministry of Women & Child Development"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMMVY was launched on 1st January 2017 under the Ministry of Women and Child Development (implemented through ICDS).  Why B/C/D wrong: These dates/ministries match other schemes."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under PMMVY, what total cash incentive is provided, and in how many instalments?","options":["Rs. 5000 in three instalments","Rs. 6000 in two instalments","Rs. 5000 in one instalment","Rs. 2000 in three instalments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cash incentive of Rs. 5000 is provided in three instalments directly to the bank/post office account of pregnant women and lactating mothers.  Why B/C/D wrong: These amounts/instalments do not match the source. [Note: source and most exam keys use Rs. 5000 in three instalments (original 2017 scheme, keyed here). Under the National Food Security Act the maternity benefit is Rs. 6000; under the revised PMMVY 2.0 the Rs. 5000 for the first child is now released in two instalments. The Rs. 5000/three-instalment answer remains correct per this source.]"},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under PMMVY, the first instalment (Rs. 1000) is provided on which condition?","options":["Early registration of pregnancy","Received at least one ANC","Child birth is registered","Completion of immunization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first instalment (Rs. 1000) is for early registration of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: That is the second instalment condition.  Why C wrong: That is the third instalment condition.  Why D wrong: Not a listed condition."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under PMMVY, the second instalment (Rs. 2000) is given on which condition?","options":["Early registration of pregnancy","Received at least one ANC (can be claimed after 6 months of pregnancy)","Child birth is registered and first vaccine cycle received","Delivery in an institution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second instalment (Rs. 2000) requires having received at least one ANC, and can be claimed after 6 months of pregnancy.  Why A wrong: That is the first instalment.  Why C wrong: That is the third instalment.  Why D wrong: Not the stated condition."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Under PMMVY, the third instalment (Rs. 2000) requires that the child birth is registered and the child has received the first cycle of which vaccines?","options":["BCG, OPV, DPT and Hepatitis-B","MMR, DPT and polio","Rota virus and pneumococcal","Only BCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The third instalment requires child birth registration and the first cycle of BCG, OPV (OPT), DPT and Hepatitis-B.  Why B/C/D wrong: These vaccine sets do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"Which of the following are objectives/beneficiary rules of PMMVY? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cash benefits to meet enhanced nutritional needs and partially compensate for wage loss","Promote positive attitude towards the girl child","Improve sex ratio at birth and prevent female foeticide","Beneficiaries are all pregnant women and lactating mothers, excluding govt. employees (central & state)","Benefits limited to govt. employees only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: PMMVY objectives include meeting nutritional needs/partial wage-loss compensation, promoting a positive attitude towards girls, and improving sex ratio at birth; beneficiaries are all pregnant/lactating women excluding govt. employees.  Why E wrong: Govt. employees are excluded, not the sole beneficiaries."},{"section":"Batch 012 · PCPNDT Act 1994, mode of action of contraceptives, geriatric nursing…","question":"JSSK aims to provide free and cashless services including which of the following for pregnant women? (Select all that apply)","options":["Free and cashless delivery and free C-Section","Free diagnostic test (blood, urine & USG)","Free provision of blood if required","Free transport from home to health institutions","Paid premium diet upgrade"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: JSSK entitlements for pregnant women include free and cashless delivery/C-Section, free drugs/consumables, free diagnostic tests (blood, urine & USG), free diet, free provision of blood, exemption from user charges, and free transport.  Why E wrong: No paid diet upgrade is part of JSSK; services are free."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"By which year does India have a commitment to achieve Universal Health Coverage (UHC)?","options":["2025","2028","2030","2035"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states India has commitment to achieve Universal Health Coverage (UHC) by 2030.  Why A/B/D wrong: 2025, 2028 and 2035 are not the stated UHC target year."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In which month and year was the National Health Mission (NHM) launched in India?","options":["April 2005","May 2013","February 2013","March 2018"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHM was launched in India in May 2013.  Why A wrong: April 2005 (12th April 2005) is the launch of NRHM.  Why C wrong: February 2013 is the launch of RMNCH+A.  Why D wrong: March 2018 is the launch of POSHAN Abhiyaan."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Which of the following are the four components of the NHM Program? (Select all that apply)","options":["Health system strengthening in rural and urban areas","Reproductive, Maternal-Neonatal-Child and Adolescent Health (RMNCH+A)","Communicable Disease Control Program","Non-Communicable Diseases Control Program","Universal Health Insurance Scheme"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed NHM components are health system strengthening, RMNCH+A, Communicable Disease Control Program and Non-Communicable Diseases Control Program.  Why E wrong: A Universal Health Insurance Scheme is not listed as an NHM component on the page."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The National Health Mission (NHM) encompasses which two sub-missions?","options":["NRHM and NUHM","NRHM and RMNCH+A","NUHM and RCH","ICDS and NRHM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NHM encompasses two sub-missions — the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) and the National Urban Health Mission (NUHM).  Why B/C/D wrong: RMNCH+A, RCH and ICDS are separate programmes/components, not the two NHM sub-missions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Financial support to the National Health Mission is being provided by which bank?","options":["World Bank","Asian Development Bank (ADB)","Reserve Bank of India","New Development Bank"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Financial support is being provided by the Asian Development Bank (ADB).  Why A/C/D wrong: World Bank, RBI and New Development Bank are not the stated funding source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"On which date was the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) launched in India?","options":["1st May 2013","12th April 2005","8th March 2018","7th January 2014"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NRHM was launched in India on 12th April 2005.  Why A wrong: 1st May 2013 is the launch date of NUHM.  Why C wrong: 8th March 2018 is the launch of POSHAN Abhiyaan.  Why D wrong: 7th January 2014 is the launch of RKSK."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under NRHM, the volunteer health worker created to bridge the gap in rural healthcare is known as:","options":["ANM","ASHA","AWW","CHO"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NRHM aims to bridge the gap in rural healthcare through creation of a volunteer health worker known as ASHA (Accredited Social Health Activist).  Why A/C/D wrong: ANM, Anganwadi Worker (AWW) and CHO are other cadres, not the volunteer health worker described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Which of the following is an objective of NRHM? (Select all that apply)","options":["Reduction in child and maternal mortality","Universal access to public services for food, nutrition, sanitation and hygiene","Population stabilization","Revitalize local health traditions and mainstream AYUSH","Achieving Universal Health Coverage by 2020"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Reduction in child/maternal mortality, universal access to public services for food/nutrition/sanitation/hygiene, population stabilization, and revitalizing local health traditions/mainstreaming AYUSH are all listed NRHM objectives.  Why E wrong: The UHC target stated is 2030 and it is not listed among NRHM objectives."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"\"Rogi Kalyan Samiti\" under NRHM is also known as:","options":["Village Health Sanitation & Nutrition Committee","Patient Welfare Committee / Hospital Management Society","Accredited Social Health Activist","United Grants to Subcenters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rogi Kalyan Samiti is the Patient Welfare Committee / Hospital Management Society, a major initiative under NRHM.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other NRHM initiatives (VHSNC, ASHA, and Untied Grants to Subcentres respectively)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The abbreviation \"VHSNC\" under NRHM stands for:","options":["Village Health Service & Nutrition Council","Village Health Sanitation & Nutrition Committee","Village Hospital Sanitation & Nursing Committee","Voluntary Health Sanitation & Nutrition Cell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: VHSNC = Village Health Sanitation & Nutrition Committee, a major NRHM initiative.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of VHSNC."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"On which date was the National Urban Health Mission (NUHM) launched in India?","options":["12th April 2005","1st May 2013","8th March 2018","February 2013"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NUHM was launched in India on 1st May, 2013.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are launch dates of NRHM, POSHAN Abhiyaan and RMNCH+A respectively."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"NUHM covers all cities/towns in all states/UTs with a population of:","options":["25,000 and above","50,000 and above","1,00,000 and above","10,000 and above"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NUHM covers all cities/towns in all states/UTs with a population of 50,000 and above.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated cut-off is 50,000; cities/towns below 50,000 continue to be covered under NRHM."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Cities and towns with a population below 50,000 will continue to be covered under which mission?","options":["NUHM","NRHM","RMNCH+A","POSHAN Abhiyaan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cities and towns with population below 50,000 continue to be covered under NRHM.  Why A/C/D wrong: NUHM covers ≥50,000 populations; RMNCH+A and POSHAN are unrelated to this population cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"For all states (except North Eastern and hilly states), what is the Centre-State funding pattern under NUHM w.e.f. FY 2015-16?","options":["60:40","90:10","75:25","100:0"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Centre-State funding pattern is 75:25 for all states w.e.f. FY 2015-16.  Why B wrong: 90:10 applies to North Eastern and hilly states like J&K, Himachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand.  Why D wrong: 100:0 (fully central) applies to UTs.  Why A wrong: 60:40 is not the NUHM pattern stated here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under NUHM, the Centre-State funding pattern for North Eastern States and hilly states (J&K, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand) is:","options":["75:25","90:10","60:40","100:0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the North Eastern and other hilly states the Centre-State funding pattern is 90:10.  Why A wrong: 75:25 applies to all other states.  Why D wrong: 100:0 applies to UTs.  Why C wrong: 60:40 is not the stated pattern here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under NUHM, in the case of UTs, the entire programme is funded by:","options":["State Government","Central Government","50:50 shared","Asian Development Bank"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the case of UTs the entire NUHM programme is fully funded by the Central Government (100:0).  Why A/C/D wrong: UT funding is fully central, not state-shared or ADB-funded."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The urban health care delivery model under NUHM works conjointly by NHM and which program?","options":["Ayushman Bharat Program","RMNCH+A Program","ICDS Program","POSHAN Abhiyaan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The urban health care delivery model works conjointly by NHM and Ayushman Bharat Program.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate programmes not named as the conjoint delivery partner here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The full form of RMNCH+A is:","options":["Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, Child and Adolescent Health","Reproductive, Maternal, Neonatal, Community and Adult Health","Rural Maternal, Newborn, Child and Adolescent Health","Reproductive, Maternal, Nutritional, Child and Anaemia Health"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RMNCH+A = Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, Child and Adolescent Health.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of the abbreviation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"RMNCH+A Program was launched in which month/year and under which ministry?","options":["May 2013 under MoWCD","February 2013 under MoHF Welfare","April 2005 under MoHFW","March 2018 under MoWCD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RMNCH+A was launched in February 2013 under MoHF Welfare (Ministry of Health & Family Welfare).  Why A/C/D wrong: These dates/ministries correspond to other programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In RMNCH+A, the \"Plus (+)\" denotes the strategic inclusion of which distinct life stage?","options":["Newborn","Adolescence","Elderly","Neonatal period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Plus\" denotes strategy inclusion of adolescence as a distinct life stage in the overall strategy.  Why A/C/D wrong: Newborn and neonatal are already covered by other letters; elderly is not part of RMNCH+A."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In which year was the Mid Day Meal Program (School Lunch Program) first started in India, as per the nutritional programme milestones?","options":["1961","1995","2007","2013"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The milestone timeline shows the Mid Day Meal Program (School Lunch Program) at 1961.  Why B wrong: 1995 marks the Mid Day Meal Scheme (NPNSPE).  Why C wrong: 2007 marks the National Program of Mid Day Scheme.  Why D wrong: 2013 marks the National Food Security Act 2009 milestone (National Nutrition Mission / POSHAN Abhiyaan is the 2018 milestone)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the nutritional programme milestones, the year 1995 is associated with which programme?","options":["PM-POSHAN","Mid Day Meal Scheme (NPNSPE)","National Food Security Act","Saksham Anganwadi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1995 is shown as the Mid Day Meal Scheme (NPNSPE) milestone.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to later milestones (2021-22, 2013 NFSA, 2021-22 respectively)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"As per the National Food Security Act 2009 milestone (2013), the mid-day meal / free food provision was extended to every child up to what age?","options":["6 years","10 years","14 years","18 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The milestone notes the National Food Security Act (2009) extended to every child up to 14 years for free food.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated age limit is 14 years."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The 2021-22 milestone in the nutritional programme timeline corresponds to:","options":["National Nutrition Mission","PM-POSHAN Abhiyan / Saksham Anganwadi & POSHAN Abhiyan 2.0","Mid Day Meal Scheme","National Food Security Act"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2021-22 milestone is PM-POSHAN Abhiyan, Saksham Anganwadi & POSHAN Abhiyan 2.0.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to 2013/2018, 1995, and 2013 (NFSA) milestones respectively."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The National Nutrition Mission (POSHAN Abhiyaan) was launched by the Prime Minister on which date and in which district?","options":["8th March 2018 in Jhunjhnu district, Rajasthan","1st May 2013 in Delhi","12th April 2005 in Jaipur","September 2021 in Jhunjhnu district, Rajasthan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: POSHAN Abhiyaan was launched on 8th March 2018 in Jhunjhnu district of Rajasthan.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dates/places do not match the stated launch of the National Nutrition Mission."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The full form of POSHAN in POSHAN Abhiyaan is:","options":["Prime Minister's Overarching Scheme for Holistic Nourishment","Programme of Special Health And Nutrition","Poverty Oriented Scheme for Health And Nutrition","Public Overarching Scheme for Household Nutrition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: POSHAN = PM's Overarching Scheme for Holistic Nourishment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Rashtriya Poshan Maah is celebrated every year in which month under POSHAN Abhiyaan?","options":["March","September","January","May"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rashtriya Poshan Maah is celebrated every year in September under the POSHAN Abhiyaan.  Why A wrong: March is when Poshan Pakhwada is celebrated.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated months."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Poshan Pakhwada is celebrated every year in which month under POSHAN Abhiyaan?","options":["September","March","February","October"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Poshan Pakhwada is celebrated every year in March under the POSHAN Abhiyaan.  Why A wrong: September is Rashtriya Poshan Maah.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated months."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Approximately how many Anganwadi Centres have set up Poshan Vatikas (with MoWCD and Ministry of Ayush)?","options":["2 lakh","4.37 lakh","40,000","1 lakh"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Close to 4.37 lakh Anganwadi Centres have set up Poshan Vatikas.  Why A/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the stated Poshan Vatika number."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The major aim of the National Nutrition Mission is to reduce the prevalence of anaemia by what rate between 2018-2022?","options":["2% per year","3% per year","5% per year","6% per year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The major aim is to reduce prevalence of anaemia by 3% per year between 2018-2022.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated target reduction is 3% per year."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"POSHAN Abhiyaan objectives include preventing and reducing stunting in children of which age group?","options":["0-6 years","6-59 months","0-3 years","6-14 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An objective is to prevent and reduce stunting in children aged 0-6 years (also under-nutrition in 0-6 years).  Why B wrong: 6-59 months is the age for reducing anaemia prevalence in children.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated age ranges for stunting."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN Abhiyaan objectives, reducing the prevalence of anaemia in children targets which age group?","options":["0-6 years","6-59 months","10-19 years","14-18 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One objective is to reduce the prevalence of anaemia in children aged 6-59 months.  Why A/C/D wrong: These age ranges apply to other objectives/programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The full form of PM-POSHAN is:","options":["Pradhan Mantri Poshan Shakti Nirman","Prime Minister Poshan Suraksha Niti","Pradhan Mantri Poshan Sahayata Nidhi","Prime Minister's Poshan Scheme for Nutrition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PM-POSHAN full form is Pradhan Mantri Poshan Shakti Nirman.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"PM-POSHAN is an intensified version of which initiative?","options":["National Iron Plus Initiative (I-NIPI)","Anemia Mukt Bharat","National Creche Scheme","Weekly Iron Folic Acid Supplementation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states PM-POSHAN is an Intensified-National Iron Plus Initiative (I-NIPI).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate programmes not equated with PM-POSHAN."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In which month/year did the Union Cabinet approve PM-POSHAN for providing one hot cooked meal in Government and Government-aided schools?","options":["March 2018","September 2021","February 2013","May 2013"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In September 2021 the Union Cabinet approved PM-POSHAN.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are dates of other programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"PM-POSHAN replaced which earlier scheme?","options":["National Programme for Mid-Day Meal (Mid-Day Meal Scheme)","POSHAN Abhiyaan","ICDS","National Creche Scheme"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The scheme replaced the \"National Programme for Mid-Day Meal\" in schools (Mid-Day Meal Scheme).  Why B/C/D wrong: These schemes were not replaced by PM-POSHAN."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"For what initial period has PM-POSHAN been launched?","options":["3 years (2021-22 to 2023-24)","5 years (2021-22 to 2025-26)","4 years (2021-22 to 2024-25)","10 years (2021-22 to 2030-31)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It has been launched for an initial period of five years (2021-22 to 2025-26).  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated period is 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"PM-POSHAN is a centrally sponsored scheme under which Act?","options":["National Food Security Act, 2013 (NFSA)","National Health Act, 2005","Right to Education Act, 2009","Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PM-POSHAN is a centrally sponsored scheme under the National Food Security Act, 2013 (NFSA).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the governing Act for PM-POSHAN."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under PM-POSHAN, Cook-cum-helpers are paid a minimum honorarium of how much per month, for how many months in a year?","options":["Rs 500 per month for 12 months","Rs 1,000 per month for 10 months","Rs 2,000 per month for 10 months","Rs 1,000 per month for 12 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cook-cum-helpers are paid a minimum honorarium of Rs 1,000 per month for 10 months in a year.  Why A/C/D wrong: These amounts/durations do not match the stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"As per PM-POSHAN standard nutrition norms (NFS Act 2013), the calorie norm per child per day for Primary (Class I-IV) is:","options":["450 Calories","700 Calories","500 Calories","300 Calories"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nutrition norm for Primary (Class I-IV) is 450 calories per child per day.  Why B wrong: 700 calories is for Upper Primary (Class VI-VIII).  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated primary calorie norm."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"As per PM-POSHAN standard nutrition norms, the calorie and protein norms per child per day for Upper Primary (Class VI-VIII) are:","options":["450 Calories, 20 gms protein","700 Calories, 40 gms protein","500 Calories, 12 gms protein","700 Calories, 20 gms protein"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per PM-POSHAN standard norms under NFS Act 2013, Upper Primary (Class VI-VIII) = 700 calories and 20 gms protein per child per day. [Note: the source table (p.903) prints Upper Primary protein as 40 gms (and Primary as 20 gms); correct per PM-POSHAN/NFSA 2013 (Ministry of Education) is 20 gms for Upper Primary and 12 gms for Primary — the source appears to have doubled the protein figures.]  Why A wrong: 450 cal / 20 gms is neither correct (correct Primary norm is 450 cal / 12 gms protein).  Why B wrong: 700 cal / 40 gms reflects the source table's erroneous (doubled) protein value.  Why C wrong: 500 cal / 12 gms does not match the Upper Primary norm."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under PM-POSHAN food norms per child per day, the food grains quantity for Primary (Class I-IV) is:","options":["100 gms","150 gms","50 gms","20 gms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Food grains for Primary (Class I-IV) is 100 gms per child per day.  Why B wrong: 150 gms is for Upper Primary.  Why C/D wrong: These correspond to vegetables and pulses."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under PM-POSHAN food norms, match the Upper Primary (Class VI-VIII) quantities: food grains, pulses, vegetables, oil & fats respectively are:","options":["100, 20, 50, 5 gms","150, 30, 75, 7.5 gms","150, 20, 50, 5 gms","100, 30, 75, 7.5 gms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Upper Primary norms are food grains 150 gms, pulses 30 gms, vegetables 75 gms, oil & fats 7.5 gms.  Why A wrong: These are the Primary (Class I-IV) values.  Why C/D wrong: Mixed/incorrect combinations."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under PM-POSHAN food norms, the pulses and vegetables quantities for Primary (Class I-IV) per child per day are:","options":["30 gms pulses, 75 gms vegetables","20 gms pulses, 50 gms vegetables","20 gms pulses, 75 gms vegetables","30 gms pulses, 50 gms vegetables"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For Primary (Class I-IV) pulses = 20 gms and vegetables = 50 gms per child per day.  Why A wrong: 30 gms pulses / 75 gms vegetables is the Upper Primary norm.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect combinations."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"For the 15th Finance Commission period (2021-22 to 2025-26), the GOI restructured ICDS and POSHAN Abhiyaan into which programme?","options":["PM-POSHAN","Saksham Anganwadi and POSHAN 2.0","Anemia Mukt Bharat","National Creche Scheme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GOI restructured ICDS and POSHAN Abhiyaan into Saksham Anganwadi and POSHAN 2.0 for the 15th Finance Commission period.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are separate schemes not created by this restructuring."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under Saksham Anganwadi and POSHAN 2.0, how many Govt-owned AWCs are to be upgraded as Saksham Anganwadi over the next 5 years, and at what annual rate?","options":["2 lakh AWCs at 40,000 per year","4.37 lakh AWCs at 50,000 per year","1 lakh AWCs at 20,000 per year","2 lakh AWCs at 20,000 per year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Up-gradation of 2 lakh Govt-owned AWCs as Saksham Anganwadi across the country in next 5 years at a rate of 40,000 AWCs each year.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures/rates do not match the stated numbers."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The restructured Saksham Anganwadi and POSHAN 2.0 scheme consists of which sub-schemes? (Select all that apply)","options":["ICDS","POSHAN Abhiyaan","Scheme for Adolescent Girls (SAG)","National Creche Scheme","WIFS"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The restructured scheme consists of ICDS, POSHAN Abhiyaan, Scheme for Adolescent Girls (SAG) and National Creche Scheme.  Why E wrong: WIFS is a separate anemia-control programme, not listed as a POSHAN 2.0 sub-scheme."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Saksham Anganwadi and POSHAN 2.0 is a centrally sponsored programme classified as:","options":["National Rural Health Mission","Integrated Nutrition Support Programme (INSP)","National Iron Plus Initiative","National Adolescent Health Programme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Saksham Anganwadi and POSHAN 2.0 is a centrally sponsored Integrated Nutrition Support Programme (INSP).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are distinct programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The target groups/beneficiaries of Saksham Anganwadi and POSHAN 2.0 include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Children (6 months to 6 years)","Adolescent girls (14-18 years)","Pregnant & Lactating women","Elderly above 60 years"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Target groups are children 6 months to 6 years, adolescent girls 14-18 years, and pregnant & lactating women.  Why D wrong: Elderly above 60 years are not listed as beneficiaries."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The POSHAN 2.0 vision aims to combat malnutrition among children up to what age, adolescent girls of what age, and pregnant/lactating women?","options":["Up to 6 years; adolescent girls 14-18 years","Up to 5 years; adolescent girls 10-19 years","Up to 6 years; adolescent girls 15-19 years","Up to 3 years; adolescent girls 14-18 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The vision is to combat malnutrition among children up to age 6, adolescent girls (14-18 years) and pregnant and lactating women.  Why B/C/D wrong: These age combinations do not match the stated vision."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The POSHAN 2.0 vision aims to achieve which SDGs by 2030?","options":["SDG 1 (No Poverty) and SDG 3 (Good Health)","SDG 2 (Zero Hunger) and SDG 4 (Quality Education)","SDG 3 (Good Health) and SDG 5 (Gender Equality)","SDG 2 (Zero Hunger) and SDG 6 (Clean Water)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The vision is to achieve SDGs by 2030, specifically SDG 2 (Zero Hunger) and SDG 4 (Quality Education).  Why A/C/D wrong: These SDG combinations are not the ones stated."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0 funding pattern, the Centre and States/UTs with Legislature share is:","options":["60:40","90:10","100:0","75:25"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For Centre and States/UTs with Legislature the funding pattern is 60:40.  Why B wrong: 90:10 applies to North Eastern/Himalayan States (including J&K).  Why C wrong: 100:0 applies to UT without Legislature.  Why D wrong: 75:25 is not a POSHAN 2.0 funding pattern."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0, the funding pattern for Centre and North Eastern/Himalayan States (including J&K) is:","options":["60:40","90:10","100:0","50:50"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For North Eastern/Himalayan States (including J&K) the pattern is 90:10.  Why A wrong: 60:40 applies to States/UTs with Legislature.  Why C wrong: 100:0 is for UT without Legislature.  Why D wrong: 50:50 is not a stated pattern."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0, the funding pattern for Centre and UT Without Legislature is:","options":["60:40","90:10","100:0","75:25"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For UT Without Legislature the funding pattern is 100:0 (fully central).  Why A/B/D wrong: These patterns apply to other categories."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The Anganwadi Service Scheme under POSHAN 2.0 includes which of the following services? (Select all that apply)","options":["Supplementary nutrition","Pre-school non-formal education","Immunization","Health check-up and Referral services","Cash transfer to households"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Anganwadi Service Scheme services include supplementary nutrition, pre-school non-formal education, nutrition & health education, immunization, health check-up and referral services.  Why E wrong: Direct cash transfer to households is not listed among these services."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"As per the Nutritional & Cost norms under POSHAN 2.0, the calorie norm per beneficiary per day for Children (6 months to 6 years) is:","options":["500 K Cal","800 K Cal","600 K Cal","450 K Cal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Children (6 month to 6 year) have a nutritional norm of 500 K Cal per beneficiary per day.  Why B wrong: 800 K Cal is for severely malnourished children.  Why C wrong: 600 K Cal is for pregnant/lactating women and adolescent girls.  Why D wrong: 450 is the PM-POSHAN primary school norm, not POSHAN 2.0 children norm."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0 norms, severely malnourished children (6 months to 6 years) receive what calorie and protein norm per day?","options":["500 K Cal, 12-15 gm","800 K Cal, 20-25 gm","600 K Cal, 18-20 gm","700 K Cal, 40 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severely malnourished children (6 month to 6 year) get 800 K Cal and 20-25 gm protein per beneficiary per day.  Why A wrong: 500 K Cal / 12-15 gm is for normal children.  Why C wrong: 600 K Cal / 18-20 gm is for pregnant/lactating women and adolescent girls.  Why D wrong: These are PM-POSHAN upper primary values."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0 norms, pregnant women & lactating/nursing mothers receive what calorie and protein per beneficiary per day?","options":["500 K Cal, 12-15 gm","800 K Cal, 20-25 gm","600 K Cal, 18-20 gm","450 K Cal, 20 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnant women & lactating/nursing mothers receive 600 K Cal and 18-20 gm protein per day.  Why A/B/D wrong: These correspond to children, severely malnourished children, and school norms."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0 norms, adolescent girls (14-18 years) receive what calorie norm per beneficiary per day?","options":["500 K Cal","600 K Cal","800 K Cal","450 K Cal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adolescent girls (14-18 years) get 600 K Cal (and 18-20 gm protein) per day.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are norms for other beneficiary categories."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0 cost norms (per beneficiary per day, in Rupees), the cost for Children (6 months to 6 years) and Severely Malnourished Children respectively are:","options":["Rs 8 and Rs 12","Rs 9.50 and Rs 12","Rs 8 and Rs 9.50","Rs 12 and Rs 8"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cost norm is Rs 8 for children (6 month to 6 year) and Rs 12 for severely malnourished children.  Why B/C/D wrong: Rs 9.50 is the cost for pregnant/lactating women and adolescent girls; the pairing here is Rs 8 and Rs 12."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0 cost norms, pregnant women/lactating mothers and adolescent girls each receive what cost per beneficiary per day?","options":["Rs 8","Rs 12","Rs 9.50","Rs 10"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The cost norm for pregnant women & lactating/nursing mothers and adolescent girls is Rs 9.50 per beneficiary per day.  Why A/B/D wrong: Rs 8 is for children, Rs 12 for severely malnourished children; Rs 10 is not in the table."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0, how many awards are given to Anganwadi Workers (AWWs) at national level and at what amount per awardee?","options":["100 awards @ Rs 50,000 per awardee","50 awards @ Rs 40,000 per awardee","100 awards @ Rs 40,000 per awardee","50 awards @ Rs 50,000 per awardee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 100 awards are given to AWWs at national level @ Rs 50,000/- per awardee.  Why B wrong: 50 awards @ Rs 40,000 is for Anganwadi Helpers (AWHs).  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect combinations."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0, how many awards are given to Anganwadi Helpers (AWHs) and at what amount?","options":["100 awards @ Rs 50,000","50 awards @ Rs 40,000","50 awards @ Rs 50,000","100 awards @ Rs 40,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 50 awards are given to AWHs @ Rs 40,000/- per awardee.  Why A wrong: 100 awards @ Rs 50,000 is for AWWs.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect combinations."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0, the monthly incentive for an Anganwadi Worker (AWW) and Anganwadi Helper (AWH) respectively are:","options":["Rs 500 and Rs 200","Rs 200 and Rs 500","Rs 1,000 and Rs 500","Rs 500 and Rs 250"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incentives under POSHAN 2.0 are Rs 500/- per month for AWW and Rs 200/- per month for AWH.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures reverse or misstate the incentives."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0, the incentive for Kuposhan Mukt villages/Panchayat is:","options":["Rs 50,000 each","Rs 40,000 each","Rs 1,00,000 lakh each","Rs 250 each"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Incentive for Kuposhan Mukt villages/Panchayat is Rs 1,00,000/-lakh each.  Why A/B/D wrong: These amounts relate to awards and CBE support, not the Kuposhan Mukt incentive."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The 3 pillars of the POSHAN 2.0 Program are:","options":["Convergence, governance, and capacity-building","Nutrition, education, and immunization","Supplementary nutrition, deworming, and IFA","Monitoring, evaluation, and reporting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 3 pillars of POSHAN 2.0 are Convergence, governance, and capacity-building.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated pillars."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under POSHAN 2.0, Community Based Events (CBEs) are to be organized how often by each Anganwadi Centre, supported with what amount?","options":["Once per month, Rs 250 each AWC","Twice per month, Rs 250 each AWC","Twice per month, Rs 500 each AWC","Once per week, Rs 250 each AWC"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: POSHAN Abhiyaan provides for organization of Community Based Events (CBEs) twice per month by each Anganwadi Centre, supported with Rs 250 each AWC.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated frequency is twice per month and the amount is Rs 250."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The Poshan Tracker is a mobile-based application invented by MoWCD, GOI on which date?","options":["1st March 2021","8th March 2018","1st May 2013","7th January 2014"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Poshan Tracker is a mobile-based application invented by MoWCD, GOI on 1st March 2021 through national e-governance division.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are dates of other programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The Poshan Tracker application is used to identify which conditions and to track nutrition service delivery? (Select all that apply)","options":["Stunting","Wasting","Underweight children","Last mile tracking of nutrition services delivery","Anemia in the elderly"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Poshan Tracker is used to identify stunting, wasting, underweight children and for last mile tracking of nutrition services delivery.  Why E wrong: Anemia in the elderly is not a stated function of the Poshan Tracker."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The purpose of the POSHAN Tracker application is to provide a \"360-degree view\" of the activities of which centre?","options":["Primary Health Centre (PHC)","Anganwadi Centre (AWC)","Community Health Centre (CHC)","Sub Centre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The purpose is to provide a 360-degree view of the activities of the Anganwadi Centre (AWC), service deliveries of AWWs and complete beneficiary management for pregnant women, lactating mothers and children.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated centre is the Anganwadi Centre."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The full form of WIFS is:","options":["Weekly Iron Folic Acid Supplementation Program","Women's Iron and Folic Acid Scheme","Weekly Infant Feeding Supplement","Widespread Iron Fortification Scheme"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WIFS = Weekly Iron Folic Acid Supplementation Program.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In which year was the WIFS program launched?","options":["2005","2011","2012","2018"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The WIFS program was launched in 2012.  Why A/B/D wrong: These years correspond to other programmes/milestones."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The purpose of WIFS is to reduce the prevalence and severity of anemia in which population age group?","options":["6-59 months","10-19 years (adolescent population)","20-24 years","15-49 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WIFS aims to reduce prevalence and severity of anemia in the adolescent population (10-19 years).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated WIFS target age group."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The WIFS activity involves IFA supplementation and:","options":["Annual immunization","Biannual helminthic control","Monthly deworming","Weekly Vitamin A supplementation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WIFS activity is IFA Supplementation and Biannual Helminthic Control.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated WIFS activities."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under WIFS interventions, the supervised weekly supplement contains how much elemental iron and folic acid?","options":["60 mg elemental iron and 500 ug folic acid","100 mg elemental iron and 500 ug folic acid","45 mg elemental iron and 400 ug folic acid","100 mg elemental iron and 400 ug folic acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WIFS administers weekly supplements of 100mg elemental iron and 500ug folic acid using a fixed day approach for 52 weeks in a year.  Why A/C/D wrong: These iron/folic acid combinations do not match the WIFS dose."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under WIFS, biannual de-worming uses which drug/dose, given how many months apart?","options":["Albendazole 200 mg, 3 months apart","Albendazole 400 mg, 6 months apart","Mebendazole 500 mg, 6 months apart","Albendazole 400 mg, 12 months apart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WIFS uses biannual de-worming with Albendazole 400mg, 6 months apart, for control of helminthic infestation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These doses/intervals do not match the WIFS deworming protocol."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the \"Milestones of Anemia Control Programmes in India,\" the year 1970 marks which programme?","options":["National Nutritional Anaemia Prophylaxis Programme (NNAPP)","National Nutritional Anaemia Control Programme (NNACP)","National Iron Plus Initiative (NIPEI)","Weekly Iron Folic Acid Supplementation Program (WIFS)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1970 marks the National Nutritional Anaemia Prophylaxis Programme (NNAPP).  Why B wrong: NNACP was in 1991.  Why C wrong: NIPEI was in 2011.  Why D wrong: WIFS was in 2012."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the anemia control programme milestones, the year 1991 corresponds to which programme?","options":["NNAPP","National Nutritional Anaemia Control Programme (NNACP)","NIPEI","Anemia Mukt Bharat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1991 marks the National Nutritional Anaemia Control Programme (NNACP).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are 1970, 2011 and 2018 milestones respectively."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The National Iron Plus Initiative (NIPEI) was introduced in which year, per the anemia control milestones?","options":["1970","1991","2011","2018"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NIPEI is the 2011 milestone (100 mg elemental iron & 500 mcg FA for pregnant women).  Why A/B/D wrong: These years mark other programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In which year was the Anemia Mukt Bharat program launched with Intensified-National Iron Plus Initiative (I-NIPEI)?","options":["2011","2012","2018","2005"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Anemia Mukt Bharat was launched in 2018 with Intensified-National Iron Plus Initiative (I-NIPEI).  Why A/B/D wrong: 2011 = NIPEI, 2012 = WIFS, 2005 = NRHM."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under the Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018 milestone, the elemental iron and folic acid dose for pregnant women (I-NIPEI) is:","options":["100 mg elemental iron & 500 mcg FA","60 mg elemental iron & 500 mcg FA","45 mg elemental iron & 400 mcg FA","20 mg elemental iron & 100 mcg FA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2018 Anemia Mukt Bharat (I-NIPEI) milestone specifies 60 mg elemental iron & 500 mcg FA for pregnant women (a change from the earlier 100 mg).  Why A wrong: 100 mg e. iron & 500 mcg FA was the earlier NNAPP/NNACP/NIPEI/WIFS dose for PW.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the I-NIPEI dose."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"National Deworming Day is celebrated every year on which date?","options":["8th March","10th February","1st May","7th January"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Every year National Deworming Day is celebrated on 10 Feb.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are dates of other observances/programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Which of the following are among the 6 beneficiary groups of Anemia Mukt Bharat? (Select all that apply)","options":["Children (6-59 months of age)","Children (5-9 years of age)","Adolescent girls & boys (10-19 years)","Women of reproductive age (20-24 years)","Elderly above 60 years"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The 6 beneficiaries include children 6-59 months, children 5-9 years, adolescent girls & boys 10-19 years, women of reproductive age 20-24 years (also pregnant women and lactating women of 0-6 months child).  Why E wrong: Elderly above 60 years is not one of the 6 beneficiary groups."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the Anemia Mukt Bharat 6 beneficiaries, \"Lactating Women\" refers to women of a child of what age?","options":["0-6 months","6-12 months","0-2 years","0-59 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 6th beneficiary group is Lactating Women (of 0-6 months child).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated age of the child is 0-6 months."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The Anemia Mukt Bharat strategy is commonly known as the:","options":["3x3x3 Strategy","6x6x6 Strategy","5x5x5 Strategy","4x4x4 Strategy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The strategy is the 6x6x6 Strategy (6 beneficiaries, 6 interventions, 6 institutional mechanisms).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated strategy name."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Which of the following are among the 6 interventions of Anemia Mukt Bharat? (Select all that apply)","options":["Prophylactic Iron Folic Acid supplementation","De-worming","Intensified year-round Behaviour Change Communication Campaign (Solid Body, Smart Mind)","Mandatory provision of Iron Folic Acid fortified foods in public health programmes","Free cataract surgery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The 6 interventions include prophylactic IFA supplementation, de-worming, intensified year-round BCC campaign (Solid Body, Smart Mind) including ensuring delayed cord clamping, testing of anaemia using digital methods with point-of-care treatment, mandatory IFA fortified foods, and addressing non-nutritional causes of anaemia especially malaria.  Why E wrong: Free cataract surgery is not one of the 6 interventions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under Anemia Mukt Bharat's 6 interventions, addressing non-nutritional causes of anaemia specially targets which disease?","options":["Tuberculosis","Malaria","Dengue","Filariasis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 6th intervention is addressing non-nutritional causes of anaemia specially malaria.  Why A/C/D wrong: The disease specifically named is malaria."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under Anemia Mukt Bharat's institutional mechanisms, the National Centre of Excellence and Advanced Research on Anaemia Control (NCEAR-A) is located at:","options":["PGIMER Chandigarh","AIIMS New Delhi","NIN Hyderabad","ICMR Delhi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NCEAR-A is located at AIIMS New Delhi.  Why A/C/D wrong: These institutions are not the stated location."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Which of the following are among the 6 institutional mechanisms of Anemia Mukt Bharat? (Select all that apply)","options":["Intra-ministerial coordination","National Anaemia Mukt Bharat Unit","NCEAR-A at AIIMS New Delhi","Anaemia Mukt Bharat Dashboard and Digital Portal","National Deworming Committee"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The 6 institutional mechanisms include intra-ministerial coordination, National Anaemia Mukt Bharat Unit, NCEAR-A at AIIMS New Delhi, convergence with other ministries, strengthening supply chain and logistics, and the Anaemia Mukt Bharat Dashboard and Digital Portal (www.anemiamuktbharat.info).  Why E wrong: A \"National Deworming Committee\" is not listed as an institutional mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The Anemia Mukt Bharat program is designed to reduce the prevalence of anemia by how many percentage points per year between 2018-2022?","options":["2% points","3% points","5% points","6% points"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states it is designed to reduce prevalence of anemia by 3% points per year between the year 2018-2022.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated target is 3% points per year."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Per the Anemia Mukt Bharat prophylactic IFA regime, children 6-59 months of age receive what elemental iron dose, at what frequency, and in what form?","options":["20 mg, Biweekly, 1 ml Syrup","45 mg, Weekly, Pink colour tab","60 mg, Weekly, Blue colour tab","60 mg, Daily, Red colour tab"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Children 6-59 months get 20 mg elemental iron (100 mcg folic acid), biweekly, as 1 ml syrup.  Why B wrong: 45 mg weekly (pink tab) is for children 5-9 years.  Why C wrong: 60 mg weekly (blue tab) is for adolescents 10-19 years.  Why D wrong: 60 mg daily (red tab) is for pregnant/lactating women."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Per the Anemia Mukt Bharat prophylactic IFA table, children 5-9 years of age receive what elemental iron and folic acid, at what frequency and colour code?","options":["20 mg iron, 100 mcg FA, biweekly, syrup","45 mg iron, 400 mcg FA, weekly, pink colour tab","60 mg iron, 500 mcg FA, weekly, blue colour tab","60 mg iron, 500 mcg FA, daily, red colour tab"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Children 5-9 years get 45 mg elemental iron, 400 mcg folic acid, weekly, pink colour tablet.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to 6-59 months, adolescents, and pregnant/lactating women respectively."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Per the Anemia Mukt Bharat prophylactic IFA table, school-going adolescent girls and boys (10-19 years) receive what dose, frequency, and colour code?","options":["45 mg iron, 400 mcg FA, weekly, pink tab","60 mg iron, 500 mcg FA, weekly, blue colour tab","60 mg iron, 500 mcg FA, daily, red tab","20 mg iron, 100 mcg FA, biweekly, syrup"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: School-going adolescents 10-19 years get 60 mg elemental iron, 500 mcg folic acid, weekly, blue colour tablet (same for out-of-school adolescent girls 10-19 years).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are for other age groups."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Per the Anemia Mukt Bharat prophylactic IFA table, women of reproductive age (20-49 years, non-pregnant, non-lactating) receive iron/folic acid at what frequency and colour code?","options":["Weekly, blue colour tab","Weekly, red colour tab","Daily, red colour tab","Biweekly, pink colour tab"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Women of reproductive age 20-49 years (non-pregnant, non-lactating) get 60 mg iron, 500 mcg FA weekly with red colour tablet.  Why A wrong: Blue tab is for adolescents 10-19 years.  Why C wrong: Daily red tab is for pregnant women/lactating mothers.  Why D wrong: Biweekly pink is not correct for this group."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Per the Anemia Mukt Bharat prophylactic IFA table, pregnant women and lactating mothers (0-6 months child) receive iron/folic acid at what frequency and colour code?","options":["Weekly, blue colour tab","Daily, red colour tab","Weekly, pink colour tab","Biweekly, syrup"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnant women/lactating mothers get 60 mg iron, 500 mcg FA daily with red colour tablet.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to other beneficiary groups."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In pregnancy, prophylactic IFA supplementation should start from which point and continue for how many total days (perinatal + postnatal)?","options":["From the 1st trimester; total 180 days","From the 4th month/2nd trimester; total 360 days (180 perinatal + 180 postnatal)","From the 3rd trimester; total 90 days","From conception; total 270 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Starting from the 4th month of pregnancy or from the 2nd trimester, continued throughout pregnancy (minimum 180 days) and continued for 180 days post-partum; total 360 days (perinatal 180 + postnatal 180).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated timing/total days."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the Anemia Mukt Bharat deworming regime, at what age is Albendazole \"Not recommended\" for children?","options":["0-12 months","12-24 months","24-59 months","5-9 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For children 0-12 months, deworming (Albendazole) is Not recommended.  Why B/C/D wrong: From 12-24 months onward Albendazole 400 mg is given (half or full tablet) biannually."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the Anemia Mukt Bharat deworming regime, children aged 12-24 months receive what Albendazole dose?","options":["Albendazole 400 mg, full tablet","Albendazole 400 mg, 1/2 tablet (200 mg)","Albendazole 200 mg, full tablet","Not recommended"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Children 12-24 months receive Albendazole 400 mg as 1/2 tablet (200 mg), biannually.  Why A wrong: The full tablet (1 tablet) is given from 24-59 months onward.  Why C/D wrong: 200 mg is given as half of a 400 mg tablet; deworming is recommended at this age."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the deworming regime, children aged 24-59 months and 5-9 years receive what dose of Albendazole?","options":["1/2 tablet (200 mg)","Albendazole 400 mg (1 tablet)","Albendazole 200 mg (1 tablet)","Not recommended"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Children 24-59 months and 5-9 years receive Albendazole 400 mg (1 tablet), biannually.  Why A wrong: Half tablet is for 12-24 months.  Why C/D wrong: The dose is one full 400 mg tablet."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the Anemia Mukt Bharat deworming regime, pregnant women receive one dose of 400 mg Albendazole preferably during which trimester?","options":["First trimester","Second trimester","Third trimester","Any trimester"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnant women get one dose of 400 mg Albendazole (1 tablet), preferably during the second trimester.  Why A/C/D wrong: The preferred timing is the second trimester."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"As per the deworming note, the Albendazole tablet should NOT be given to which groups?","options":["Children less than 12 months of age and lactating women","Adolescent boys only","Pregnant women in second trimester","Women of reproductive age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states deworming tablet Albendazole should not be given to a child with less than 12 months of age group and lactating women.  Why B/C/D wrong: These groups do receive deworming per the schedule; the excluded groups are <12 month children and lactating women."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the deworming schedule, \"Biannually\" means the drug is given how many times a year?","options":["Once","Twice","Three times","Four times"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states \"Biannually means 2 times in a year.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Biannually here specifically means twice per year."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the Anemia Mukt Bharat therapeutic management of anaemia for pregnant women, an Hb level of 10-10.9 g/dl is classified as:","options":["Mild anaemia","Moderate anaemia","Severe anaemia","Normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hb level 10-10.9 is classified as mild anaemia in pregnant women.  Why B wrong: Hb 7-9.9 is moderate.  Why C wrong: Hb 5-6.9 is severe.  Why D wrong: This level is still classified as (mild) anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the therapeutic management of anaemia for pregnant women, an Hb level of 7-9.9 g/dl is classified as which category, and treated for what duration?","options":["Mild anaemia, 1 month","Moderate anaemia, 2 months","Severe anaemia, till normal Hb","Moderate anaemia, 3 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hb level 7-9.9 (moderate anaemia) is treated with 2 IFA tab daily (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) for 2 months; parenteral iron (IV Iron Sucrose or FCM) may be considered.  Why A wrong: Mild anaemia (Hb 10-10.9) is treated for 1 month.  Why C wrong: Severe anaemia is Hb 5-6.9.  Why D wrong: 3 months applies to adolescents mild-moderate anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In the therapeutic management for pregnant women, an Hb level of 5-6.9 g/dl (severe anaemia) is managed by:","options":["2 IFA tablets daily for 1 month","IV Iron Sucrose/Ferric Carboxy Maltose (FCM) by the medical officer, till normal Hb level, with immediate hospitalisation","Oral iron only for 3 months","Referral to ASHA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe anaemia (Hb 5-6.9) is managed with IV Iron Sucrose/Ferric Carboxy Maltose (FCM) by the medical officer, till the normal Hb level, with immediate hospitalisation at PHC/CHC/FRU/DH.  Why A/C wrong: These are treatments for mild/moderate anaemia.  Why D wrong: Severe anaemia requires management by a medical officer, not ASHA."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"For mild to moderate anaemia in adolescents (girls & boys), the therapeutic dose is 2 IFA tablets daily for what duration?","options":["1 month","2 months","3 months","6 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Adolescents with mild to moderate anaemia get 2 IFA tab daily (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) for 3 months; if no improvement after 3 months, refer to FRU.  Why A/B/D wrong: 3 months is the stated duration for adolescents."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"In pregnant women with mild/moderate anaemia, if there is no improvement in haemoglobin (<1 gm/dl increase) after 1 month of treatment, the woman should be referred to:","options":["ASHA","FRU/District Hospital (DH)","Anganwadi Centre","Sub-centre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If no improvement in haemoglobin (<1 gm/dl increase) after 1 month, refer to FRU/District Hospital (DH) by the health provider (ANM, AWW, CHO).  Why A/C/D wrong: Referral is to the FRU/District Hospital, not to lower-level workers/centres."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"As per the therapeutic management note, if Hb levels have come up to normal level, the correct action is to:","options":["Continue therapeutic treatment indefinitely","Discontinue therapeutic treatment and continue with prophylactic IFA dose","Stop all iron supplementation","Double the therapeutic dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states in all cases, if Hb levels have come up to normal level, discontinue the therapeutic treatment and continue with the prophylactic IFA dose.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated management note."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The full form of RKSK is:","options":["Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram","Rashtriya Kishori Suraksha Karyakram","Rural Kishor Swasthya Kendra","Rashtriya Kishor Shakti Karyakram"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RKSK = Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The launch date of the Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (RKSK) was:","options":["8th March 2018","7th January 2014","1st May 2013","12th April 2005"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RKSK was launched on 7th January, 2014.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are launch dates of other programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Another name for the RKSK program is:","options":["National Adolescent Health Programme","National Rural Health Mission","Anemia Mukt Bharat","National Nutrition Mission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Another name for RKSK is the National Adolescent Health Programme.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate distinct programmes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Which of the following are objectives of the RKSK program? (Select all that apply)","options":["Improving Nutrition","Improving Sexual and Reproductive Health","Enhance mental health","Preventing Injuries and Violence, and Preventing Drug Abuse","Eliminating tuberculosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: RKSK objectives are improving nutrition, improving sexual and reproductive health, enhancing mental health, preventing injuries and violence, and preventing drug abuse.  Why E wrong: Eliminating tuberculosis is not an RKSK objective."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The RKSK program targets adolescent health in which two age groups?","options":["6-9 years and 10-14 years","10-14 years and 15-19 years","10-19 years and 20-24 years","14-18 years and 19-24 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RKSK target groups are adolescent health age groups 10-14 years and 15-19 years with universal coverage (males and females; urban and rural; in school and out of school; married and unmarried; vulnerable and under-served).  Why A/C/D wrong: These age ranges do not match RKSK target groups."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under RKSK community-based interventions, which of the following are included? (Select all that apply)","options":["Peer Education (PE)","Quarterly Adolescent Health Day (AHD)","Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation Programme (WIFS)","Menstrual Hygiene Scheme (MHS)","Strengthening of Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics (AFHC)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Community-based interventions under RKSK are Peer Education (PE), Quarterly Adolescent Health Day (AHD), WIFS, and Menstrual Hygiene Scheme (MHS).  Why E wrong: Strengthening of Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics (AFHC) is a facility-based intervention, not a community-based one."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"Under RKSK, \"Strengthening of Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics (AFHC)\" falls under which category of interventions?","options":["Community based interventions","Facility based interventions","School based interventions","Home based interventions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Strengthening of Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics (AFHC) is listed under facility based interventions.  Why A/C/D wrong: AFHC strengthening is a facility-based intervention."},{"section":"Batch 013 · National Health Mission, NRHM & NUHM, RMNCH+A…","question":"The abbreviation \"AHD\" in the RKSK community-based interventions stands for:","options":["Adolescent Health Day (Quarterly)","Adult Health Drive","Anaemia Health Day","Annual Health Diagnosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AHD = (Quarterly) Adolescent Health Day, a community-based intervention under RKSK.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Ayushman Bharat Yojana is also known by which of the following name?","options":["Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana","Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana","Janani Suraksha Yojana","Rashtriya Swasthya Bima Yojana"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ayushman Bharat Yojana is also called Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PM-JAY), a health scheme of the Government of India.  Why B/C/D wrong: PMMVY is a maternity benefit scheme, JSY promotes institutional delivery, and RSBY was an earlier health insurance scheme replaced by PM-JAY."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"As per NHP-2017, the guiding principle \"time to care\" for Comprehensive Primary Health Care delivered through Health and Wellness Centres should be no more than:","options":["15 minutes","30 minutes","45 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NHP-2017 recommended CPHC delivery through HWCs with the principle of \"time to care\" being no more than 30 minutes.  Why A/C/D wrong: The specified benchmark is 30 minutes, not 15, 45, or 60."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Which ministry administers the Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PM-JAY)?","options":["Ministry of Rural Development","Ministry of Women and Child Development","Ministry of Health and Family Welfare","Ministry of Social Justice"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PM-JAY is under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare.  Why A/B/D wrong: These ministries do not administer PM-JAY."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"What is the full name of the Ayushman Bharat scheme?","options":["Ayushman Bharat National Health Insurance Scheme","Ayushman Bharat Prime Minister Jan Arogya","Ayushman Bharat Universal Health Coverage","Ayushman Bharat Rural Health Mission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The full name is Ayushman Bharat Prime Minister Jan Arogya.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated full names; the other name is \"Ayushman Bharat: National Health Protection Scheme.\""},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"PM-JAY (Ayushman Bharat) was launched on which date, coinciding with Bhimrao Ambedkar's birth anniversary?","options":["23rd September 2018","30th April 2018","18th April 2018","25th September 2014"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Launch date was 18th April 2018 from Ranchi district of Jharkhand; it was implemented across all India on 23rd September 2018.  Why A wrong: 23rd September 2018 is the nationwide implementation date, not the launch date. Why B wrong: 30 April is Ayushman Bharat Diwas. Why D wrong: 25 September 2014 is Mission Indradhanush."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Ayushman Bharat Diwas is celebrated every year on:","options":["18th April","30th April","28th May","23rd September"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ayushman Bharat Diwas is celebrated every year on 30 April (Raj. CHO-2023).  Why A/C/D wrong: 18 April is launch date, 28 May is Menstrual Hygiene Day, 23 Sept is the nationwide implementation date."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The Unique Health ID under Ayushman Bharat is known as ABHA, which stands for:","options":["Ayushman Bharat Health Access","Ayushman Bharat Health Account","Ayushman Bharat Hospital Assistance","Ayushman Bharat Health Assurance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ABHA stands for Ayushman Bharat Health Account, the unique health ID.  Why A/C/D wrong: These expansions are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Ayushman Bharat adopts a continuum of care approach comprising which two inter-related components?","options":["HWCs and PM-JAY","ASHA and Anganwadi","PHC and CHC","NHM and NRHM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two inter-related components are Establishment of Health and Wellness Centres (HWCs) and Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PM-JAY).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two named components of Ayushman Bharat."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"On 26 Nov 2023, the existing Ayushman Bharat Health and Wellness Centres (AB-HWCs) were renamed as:","options":["Jan Arogya Kendra","Ayushman Arogya Mandir","Arogya Bharat Kendra","Swasthya Suraksha Mandir"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AB-HWCs were renamed 'Ayushman Arogya Mandir' with the tag-line 'Arogyam Parmam Dhanam' on 26 Nov 2023.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the renamed titles."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"What is the tag-line associated with Ayushman Arogya Mandir?","options":["Health for All","Arogyam Parmam Dhanam","Sabka Swasthya","TB Harega Desh Jitega"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tag-line is 'Arogyam Parmam Dhanam'.  Why A/C wrong: These are generic slogans not the official tag-line. Why D wrong: \"TB Harega, Desh Jitega\" is the Ni-Kshay 2.0 slogan."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"How many Health and Wellness Centres was the first component of Ayushman Bharat intended to create?","options":["94,000","1,00,000","1,50,000","5,00,000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The first component pertains to creation of 150,000 Health and Wellness Centres.  Why A wrong: 94,000 is the number of CHOs working till July 2023. Why B/D wrong: incorrect figures."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under PM-JAY, cashless health insurance is provided to every family for hospital care at secondary and tertiary level up to:","options":["Rs. 1 lakh per year","Rs. 3 lakh per year","Rs. 5 lakh per year","Rs. 10 lakh per year"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PM-JAY provides cashless health insurance up to Rs. 5 lakh per year (Tripura CHO 2022).  Why A/B/D wrong: incorrect amounts."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"PM-JAY covers pre-hospitalization and post-hospitalization expenses for how many days respectively?","options":["3 days pre and 15 days post","15 days pre and 3 days post","7 days pre and 30 days post","5 days pre and 10 days post"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PM-JAY covers up to 3 days of pre-hospitalization and 15 days post-hospitalization expenses such as diagnostics and medicines.  Why B/C/D wrong: incorrect day counts."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"PM-JAY contributes towards achievement of which Sustainable Development Goal?","options":["SDG-2","SDG-3","SDG-5","SDG-6"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PM-JAY is a significant step towards Universal Health Coverage (UHC) and Sustainable Development Goal-3 (SDG3).  Why A/C/D wrong: SDG-3 (Good Health & Well-being) is the relevant goal, not these."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The controlling authority of PM-JAY at national level is:","options":["National Health Mission (NHM)","National Health Authority (NHA) under MoHFW","Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR)","NITI Aayog"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The controlling authority of PM-JAY at national level is the National Health Authority (NHA) under MoHFW.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the controlling authority of PM-JAY."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"PM-JAY (Ayushman Bharat-National Health Protection Scheme) aims to cover how many poor and vulnerable families?","options":["5 crore families","10 crore families","50 crore families","1 crore families"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PM-JAY will cover 10 crore poor and vulnerable families (about 50 crore beneficiaries) and is the world's largest health protection scheme.  Why A/D wrong: incorrect family figures. Why C wrong: 50 crore is the number of beneficiaries, not families."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In which year was the Menstrual Hygiene Scheme (MHS) launched by MoHFW in India?","options":["2005","2011","2014","2018"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The MHS Scheme was launched by MoHFW in 2011.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect years."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The Menstrual Hygiene Scheme targets adolescent girls in which age group in rural areas?","options":["6-14 years","10-19 years","15-24 years","18-45 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MHS aims to promote menstrual hygiene among adolescent girls aged 10-19 years in rural areas (Tripura CHO 2022).  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect age ranges."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Menstrual Hygiene Day is observed every year on:","options":["8 March","28 May","30 April","11 July"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Menstrual Hygiene Day is observed every year on 28 May.  Why A/C/D wrong: 8 March is Women's Day, 30 April is Ayushman Bharat Diwas, 11 July is World Population Day."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under the Menstrual Hygiene Scheme, a pack of six sanitary napkins called \"Freedays\" is provided to rural adolescent girls through ASHA at a subsidized rate of:","options":["Rs. 3","Rs. 6","Rs. 10","Free of cost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pack of six sanitary napkins called \"Freedays\" is provided through ASHA at a subsidized rate of Rs. 6.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect amounts."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The Menstrual Hygiene Day 2023 theme was:","options":["Period Friendly World","To make menstruation a normal fact of life by 2030","End Period Poverty","Hygiene for Health"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The MHD 2023 theme was \"To make menstruation a normal fact of life by 2030.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: these were not the stated 2023 theme."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK) program was launched in:","options":["February 2013 from Palghar, Maharashtra","April 2018 from Ranchi, Jharkhand","September 2014 from Delhi","August 1992 from Mumbai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RBSK Program was launched in February 2013 from Palghar, Maharashtra.  Why B/C/D wrong: these are launch details of other programs (PM-JAY, Mission Indradhanush, CSSM)."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The aim of RBSK program covers children from birth to 18 years addressing the 4 'D's. Which of the following is NOT one of the 4 D's?","options":["Defects at birth","Deficiencies","Diseases","Dehydration"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The 4 D's are Defects at birth, Deficiencies, Diseases, and Development delays including disability; dehydration is not one of them.  Why A/B/C wrong: these are all valid components of the 4 D's."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The total number of health conditions included in the RBSK Program is:","options":["20","30","12","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total number of health conditions included in RBSK Program is 30 (Tripura CHO-2022).  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect counts; 12 is vaccine-preventable diseases in Mission Indradhanush, 4 is the 4 D's."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In RBSK, the 0-6 years age group is specifically managed at which level?","options":["Primary Health Centre (PHC)","District Early Intervention Center (DEIC)","Community Health Centre (CHC)","Anganwadi Centre only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 0-6 years age group is specifically managed at the District Early Intervention Center (DEIC), which also acts as referral linkage; 6-18 years is managed through existing public health facilities.  Why A/C/D wrong: these are not the specified management level for 0-6 years."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The suggested staffing norm for a RBSK Mobile Health Team is a total of how many members?","options":["3","4","5","6"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mobile Health Team total is 4 - 2 Medical officers (AYUSH, one male one female), 1 ANM/Staff Nurse, and 1 Pharmacist (optionally Lab Technician or Ophthalmic Assistant).  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect totals."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In the RBSK Mobile Health Team, the two medical officers belong to which system of medicine?","options":["Allopathy","AYUSH","Homeopathy only","Dental"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 2 Medical officers are from AYUSH (one male and one female).  Why A/C/D wrong: the source specifies AYUSH medical officers."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In RBSK levels of screening, screening of newborns from 48 hours to 6 weeks is done by ASHA at home as part of which package?","options":["Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)","Home Based New-born Care (HBNC)","Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK)","Integrated Management of Neonatal and Childhood Illness (IMNCI)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 48 hours to 6 weeks screening is done by ASHA at home as part of the Home Based New-born Care (HBNC) package.  Why A/C/D wrong: these are not the package named for this screening level."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In RBSK, the first level of screening (birth to 48 hours) is done at delivery points through Medical Officers, Staff Nurses, and:","options":["ASHAs","ANMs","Anganwadi Workers","Lab Technicians"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Birth to 48 hours screening is done at all delivery points through existing Medical Officers, Staff Nurses and ANMs.  Why A/C/D wrong: ASHAs do the 48hr-6wk home screening; AWWs and Lab Technicians are not named for this level."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In RBSK, screening for the 6 weeks to 6 years age group is done by Mobile Health Teams at:","options":["Children's schools","Anganwadi centres","District hospitals","Home visits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 6 weeks to 6 years screening should be done by Mobile Health teams at anganwadi centres; 6-18 years screening is done at children's school.  Why A/C/D wrong: schools are for 6-18 years; district hospitals and home visits are not specified for this group."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In the RBSK \"Defects at Birth\" category, which condition is characterized by developmental dysplasia of the hip?","options":["Talipes (club foot)","Developmental dysplasia of the hip","Congenital cataract","Down's Syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Developmental dysplasia of the hip is condition #5 listed under Defects at Birth.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are separate individual defect-at-birth conditions (Talipes #4, congenital cataract #6, Down's syndrome #2)."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under RBSK \"Deficiencies\" category, Bitot spot is associated with which deficiency?","options":["Vitamin D deficiency","Vitamin A deficiency","Iron deficiency (anaemia)","Iodine deficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin A deficiency (Bitot spot) is listed as deficiency #11 in the RBSK health conditions table.  Why A wrong: Vitamin D deficiency causes Rickets (#12). Why C wrong: iron deficiency causes anaemia. Why D wrong: iodine deficiency causes goiter (#14)."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In the RBSK health conditions list, which of the following conditions are listed as Optional (condition #30)?","options":["Neural tube defect, Down's syndrome, Cleft palate","Congenital Hypothyroidism, Sickle cell anemia, Beta thalassemia","Scabies, Otitis media, Eczema","Vision impairment, Hearing impairment, Motor delay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Condition #30 lists Congenital Hypothyroidism, Sickle cell anemia, Beta thalassemia as Optional.  Why A/C/D wrong: these fall under Defects at Birth, Diseases of Childhood, and Developmental delays respectively."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In RBSK's \"Developmental delays and Disabilities\" category, Autism is classified under which condition?","options":["Cognitive delay","Behavior disorder","Language delay","Learning disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Behavior disorder (Autism) is listed as condition #27 under Developmental delays and Disabilities.  Why A/C/D wrong: cognitive delay (#25), language delay (#26), and learning disorder (#28) are distinct listed conditions."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The full form of CSSM Program is:","options":["Child Survival & Safe Motherhood Program","Comprehensive School Screening Mission","Child Support & Social Mobilization","Community Safe Sanitation Movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSSM stands for Child Survival & Safe Motherhood Program.  Why B/C/D wrong: incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The CSSM program was launched on:","options":["20th August 1992","25th September 2014","18th April 2018","31st July 2016"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSSM program was launched on 20th August 1992.  Why B/C/D wrong: these are launch dates of Mission Indradhanush, PM-JAY, and PMSMA respectively."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The CSSM program is directed at achieving 9 of the 17 goals of which policy related to maternal and child health?","options":["National Health Policy (1983)","National Health Policy (2002)","National Health Policy (2017)","National Population Policy (2000)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSSM aims at achieving 9 of the 17 goals of the National Health Policy (1983) related to maternal and child health.  Why B/C/D wrong: these are not the referenced policy year."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under CSSM Program goals, neonatal tetanus elimination was targeted by which year?","options":["1992","1995","2000 AD","2005"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Goals of CSSM include neonatal tetanus elimination by 1995 and polio eradication by 2000 AD.  Why A/C/D wrong: 2000 AD was polio eradication target; 1992 and 2005 are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The three E's that form the components of the CSSM Program are Essential maternal care, Early identification of complications, and:","options":["Emergency obstetric care","Environmental care","Elective surgery","Emergency transport"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 3 E's of CSSM are Essential maternal care, Early identification of complications, and Emergency obstetric care.  Why B/C/D wrong: these are not among the CSSM 3 E's."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The full form of NTEP is:","options":["National Tuberculosis Eradication Plan","National TB Elimination Program","National Tetanus Elimination Program","National Treatment and Education Program"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NTEP stands for National TB Elimination Program.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In 2020, the RNTCP was renamed as the National TB Elimination Program (NTEP) to emphasize eliminating TB in India by:","options":["2025","2030","2035","2022"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NTEP aims to eliminate TB in India by 2025 (India's own target), five years ahead of the UN SDG goal of 2030.  Why B wrong: 2030 is the UN SDG global target. Why C/D wrong: incorrect years."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The four strategic pillars of NTEP (National Strategy Plan 2017-2025) are:","options":["Detect, Treat, Prevent, Build (DTPB)","Diagnose, Deliver, Prevent, Protect","Detect, Test, Treat, Track","Detect, Treat, Cure, Control"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four strategic pillars are Detect, Treat, Prevent, Build (DTPB).  Why B/C/D wrong: these are not the four DTPB pillars named in NTEP."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Ni-Kshay 2.0 (Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan) was launched on which date?","options":["24 March 2022","09 September 2022","25 September 2014","08 October 2017"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ni-Kshay 2.0 was launched on 09 Sept 2022 by President Droupadi Murmu.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect dates for this launch."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The slogan of Ni-Kshay 2.0 / PM TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan is:","options":["TB Harega, Desh Jitega","Arogyam Parmam Dhanam","Health for All","Sabko Swasthya"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The slogan is \"TB Harega, Desh Jitega\".  Why B/C/D wrong: these are not the Ni-Kshay 2.0 slogan."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Who is the national ambassador of PMTMBA (Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan)?","options":["Deepa Malik","Droupadi Murmu","Mary Kom","P.T. Usha"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The national ambassador of PMTMBA is Deepa Malik.  Why B wrong: Droupadi Murmu launched the program (President). Why C/D wrong: not the named ambassador."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"People and institutions coming forward to take care of TB patients under Ni-Kshay 2.0 are called:","options":["Ni-kshay Mitra","TB Sathi","Arogya Mitra","Swasthya Sevak"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: They are called \"Ni-kshay Mitra\"; the Nikshy Mitra portal 2.0 is used for online tracking.  Why B/C/D wrong: these are not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under Nikshay Poshan Yojana, how much financial assistance per month is currently provided to tuberculosis patients?","options":["Rs. 250","Rs. 500","Rs. 1000","Rs. 1500"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Nikshay Poshan Yojana provides Rs. 1000 per month as nutritional support for tuberculosis patients. [Note: source prints Rs. 500; the amount was doubled from Rs. 500 to Rs. 1000/month effective November 2024 per MoHFW/Central TB Division, so the current-guideline-correct answer is Rs. 1000.]  Why A/B/D wrong: Rs. 500 was the earlier (pre-Nov 2024) amount now printed in the source; Rs. 250 and Rs. 1500 are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"According to the milestones of TB Control Programmes in India, NTCP was started in which year?","options":["1962","1997","2020","2022"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Milestones: NTCP (1962), R-NTCP/DOTS (1997), NTBEP/NSP (2020), Nikshya 2.0 (2022).  Why B/C/D wrong: 1997 = R-NTCP (DOTS), 2020 = NTBEP, 2022 = Nikshya 2.0."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under NTEP, the treatment provider incentive for a Category IV or V patient is:","options":["Rs. 1000","Rs. 1500","Rs. 5000","Rs. 500"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Treatment provider of Category IV or V patients gets Rs. 5000 (in 2 instalments: Rs. 2000 at end of intensive phase, Rs. 3000 at end of continuation phase).  Why A wrong: Rs. 1000 is for Category I. Why B wrong: Rs. 1500 is for Category II. Why D wrong: Rs. 500 is not a category incentive amount (it was the earlier Nikshay Poshan monthly support, since raised to Rs. 1000)."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The full form of PMSMA is:","options":["Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan","Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Matru Abhiyan","Pradhan Mantri Sishu Matritva Abhiyan","Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Matru Ayojana"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMSMA stands for Pradhanmantri Surakshit Matritav (Matritva) Abhiyan.  Why B/C/D wrong: incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"PMSMA provides assured, comprehensive and quality antenatal care free of cost to all pregnant women on which day of every month?","options":["1st of every month","9th of every month","15th of every month","Last day of every month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PMSMA aims to provide free antenatal care universally to all pregnant women on the 9th of every month.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect dates."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"PMSMA was launched by the Hon'ble PM on:","options":["31st July 2016","20th August 1992","18th April 2018","25th September 2014"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMSMA Program was launched by the PM on 31st July 2016.  Why B/C/D wrong: launch dates of CSSM, PM-JAY, and Mission Indradhanush respectively."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under PMSMA, minimum package of antenatal care is guaranteed to women in which trimesters of pregnancy?","options":["1st trimester","2nd / 3rd trimesters","1st / 2nd trimesters","All three trimesters equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PMSMA guarantees a minimum package of antenatal care to women in their 2nd / 3rd trimesters of pregnancy.  Why A/C/D wrong: the scheme specifically targets 2nd/3rd trimester care."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under PMSMA, a Red Sticker is given to which category of pregnant women?","options":["Women with no risk factor detected","Women with high risk pregnancy","Women in first trimester","Women above 35 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Red Sticker is for women with high risk pregnancy; Green Sticker is for women with no risk factor detected.  Why A wrong: that is the Green Sticker group. Why C/D wrong: not the sticker criteria."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Mission Indradhanush was started in India on which date and is an immunization campaign against how many vaccine-preventable diseases?","options":["25 September 2014; 12 diseases","07 April 2015; 7 diseases","25 September 2014; 7 diseases","08 October 2017; 12 diseases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mission Indradhanush was started on 25 September 2014 and is an immunization campaign against 12 vaccine preventable diseases.  Why B/C/D wrong: 7 April 2015 was Mission Indradhanush 1.0; the disease count is 12, not 7."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Intensified Mission Indradhanush 1.0 was launched on:","options":["October 8, 2017","April 7, 2015","September 25, 2014","2022"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intensified Mission Indradhanush 1.0 was launched on October 8, 2017; IMI 4.0 was in 2022.  Why B wrong: April 7, 2015 was Mission Indradhanush 1.0. Why C wrong: 25 Sept 2014 was the original start. Why D wrong: 2022 was IMI 4.0."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Mission Indradhanush aims to cover all unimmunized children up to which age?","options":["1 year","2 years","5 years","6 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mission Indradhanush aims to cover all children up to 2 years of age who were earlier unimmunized, plus pregnant women earlier unimmunized.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect age cut-offs."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"According to the milestone of immunization programmes in India, the Expanded Programme for Immunization (EPI) was started in:","options":["1978","1985","1992","1997"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Milestone: EPI (1978), UIP (1985), Under CSSM (1992), Under RCH (1997), Under NRHM (2005), Mission Indradhanush (2015).  Why B/C/D wrong: 1985 = UIP, 1992 = CSSM, 1997 = RCH."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) was introduced in India in which year?","options":["1978","1985","2005","2015"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: UIP was started in 1985 per the immunization milestones.  Why A/C/D wrong: 1978 = EPI, 2005 = NRHM, 2015 = Mission Indradhanush."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In May 2023, the NPCDCS programme was renamed to:","options":["National Programme for Prevention & Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD)","National Cancer Control Programme","National Non-Communicable Disease Mission","National Chronic Disease Programme"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NPCDCS was renamed National Programme for Prevention & Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD) by MoHFW in May 2023.  Why B/C/D wrong: incorrect new names."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In which year was the NPCDCS programme launched in India for prevention and control of non-communicable diseases?","options":["2005","2010","2013","2023"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NPCDCS programme was launched in 2010 for prevention and control of cancer, diabetes, cardio-vascular diseases and stroke.  Why A/C wrong: incorrect launch years. Why D wrong: 2023 is the renaming year."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The renaming of NPCDCS to NP-NCD is due to the addition of which new non-communicable diseases?","options":["Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease and chronic kidney disease","Asthma and COPD","Hypertension and obesity","Epilepsy and depression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The renaming was due to addition of new NCDs such as non-alcoholic fatty liver disease and chronic kidney disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: these are not the specified newly added diseases."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Under NP-NCD, the CPHC-NCD IT software has been renamed as:","options":["National NCD Portal","National Health Portal","e-Sanjeevani Portal","Ni-Kshay Portal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The CPHC-NCD IT software was renamed the National NCD Portal to cover a wider population for screening and management of NCDs.  Why B/C/D wrong: these are separate/unrelated portals."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"NPCDCS/NP-NCD is being implemented under which mission across the country?","options":["National Health Mission (NHM)","National Rural Livelihood Mission","Swachh Bharat Mission","National Nutrition Mission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NPCDCS is being implemented under the National Health Mission (NHM).  Why B/C/D wrong: these are unrelated missions."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The full form of VHSNC is:","options":["Village Health Sanitation & Nutrition Committee","Village Health Service & Nutrition Council","Village Hygiene Sanitation & Nursing Committee","Village Health Screening & Nutrition Cell"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VHSNC stands for Village Health Sanitation & Nutrition Committee.  Why B/C/D wrong: incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"A VHSNC is to be formed at the level of the revenue village. When the population of a revenue village is over 4000, the VHSNC can be at the level of a:","options":["District","Ward Panchayat","Block","Gram Panchayat cluster"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Where the population of a revenue village is over 4000, the VHSNC can be at the level of a Ward Panchayat.  Why A/C/D wrong: these are not the specified level."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"As per guidelines, the CHO has to attend at least how many VHSNC monthly meetings under his/her area?","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CHO has to attend at least two VHSNC monthly meetings under his/her area.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect counts."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"A VHSNC should have a minimum of how many members?","options":["10","15","20","25"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The VHSNC should have a minimum of 15 members and may have more.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect minimums."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In a VHSNC, at least what percentage should be women members, with SCs, STs and minorities well represented?","options":["33%","50%","25%","75%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At least 50% should be women members and SCs, STs and minorities should be well represented.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect percentages; 33% relates to quorum in large committees."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In the VHSNC, who acts as the Member Secretary responsible for organizing meetings along with the Panch (Chairperson)?","options":["ANM","ASHA","Anganwadi Worker","CHO"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ASHA (Member Secretary) and the Panch (Chairperson) are responsible for organizing the meeting.  Why A/C/D wrong: they are not the Member Secretary role."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"What is the annual untied fund given to a VHSNC?","options":["Rs. 5,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 25,000","Rs. 50,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An untied fund of Rs. 10,000 is given annually to the VHSNC.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect amounts."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In a 15-member VHSNC, the minimum quorum is 50%, but in large committees the quorum could be:","options":["25%","33%","50%","66%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a 15-member committee minimum quorum is 50%, but in large committees the quorum could be 33%.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect quorum figures."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The VHSNC meetings should be held at least how often?","options":["Once every week","Once every month","Once every quarter","Twice a year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Frequency of meeting: meetings of VHSNC should be held at least once every month (UP CCH-2022).  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect frequency."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In terms of nutrition, macronutrients include which of the following?","options":["Protein, Fat, Carbohydrate","Vitamins and Minerals","Iron, Zinc, Iodine","Calcium, Phosphorus, Potassium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Macronutrients are Protein, Fat, and Carbohydrate; micronutrients are Vitamins & Minerals.  Why B wrong: vitamins & minerals are micronutrients. Why C/D wrong: these are micro/macro minerals."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"How much energy (kcal) does 1 gram of protein provide?","options":["4 kcal","9 kcal","7 kcal","2 kcal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1 gm protein provides 4 kcal energy (same as carbohydrate).  Why B wrong: 9 kcal is for fat. Why C/D wrong: incorrect values."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"What percentage of dietary kcal should ideally be met by protein for good health?","options":["10 to 15%","15 to 20%","20 to 35%","45 to 65%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 15 to 20% kcal should be met by protein components in the diet for good health.  Why C wrong: 20-35% is the AMDR for fat. Why D wrong: 45-65% is for carbohydrate. Why A wrong: incorrect for good-health guidance."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Which food source is referred to as the \"Poor Man's\" meat because of its protein content?","options":["Egg","Pulses","Soya-bean","Milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulses protein is called \"Poor Man's\" meat.  Why A/C/D wrong: egg is reference protein, soya-bean is a unique highest source, milk is a protein source but not the \"Poor Man's meat\"."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Soya-bean is described as a unique and highest major source of protein containing approximately what percentage of protein?","options":["20%","30%","40%","50%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Soya-bean has 40% of protein and is a unique, highest major source.  Why A/B/D wrong: incorrect percentages."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"NPU (Net Protein Utilization) is calculated as:","options":["(Nitrogen Retained × 100) / Nitrogen Intake","(Nitrogen Intake × 100) / Nitrogen Retained","(Protein Intake × 100) / Body Weight","(Nitrogen Retained) / (Protein Intake × 100)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NPU = (Nitrogen Retained × 100) / Nitrogen Intake; it is the percentage of ingested protein retained by the body after food is eaten.  Why B/C/D wrong: the formula is inverted or incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Maximum net protein utilization is found in which food, so that its protein is known as reference protein?","options":["Milk","Soya-bean","Eggs","Pulses"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Maximum NPU is found in eggs, so egg protein is known as reference protein (NIA Jaipur 2023).  Why A/B/D wrong: they do not have the maximum NPU / reference protein status."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Protein is mainly formed by which four elements?","options":["Carbon, hydrogen, oxygen only","Carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen","Carbon, oxygen, sulphur and nitrogen","Hydrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and nitrogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protein is mainly formed by carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen (NIA Jaipur 2023).  Why A wrong: that is fats/carbohydrates (no nitrogen). Why C/D wrong: incorrect element sets."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"According to India's Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range (AMDR) table, the AMDR for carbohydrate is:","options":["10-35%","20-35%","45-65%","15-20%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: AMDR: Carbohydrate 45-65% (remark 2/3), Protein 10-35% (1/5), Fat 20-35% (1/3).  Why A wrong: 10-35% is protein. Why B wrong: 20-35% is fat. Why D wrong: incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"According to the AMDR table, the AMDR for fat is:","options":["45-65%","10-35%","20-35%","15-20%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The AMDR for fat is 20-35% (9 calorie/gram, remark 1/3).  Why A wrong: carbohydrate. Why B wrong: protein. Why D wrong: incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"How many kcal of energy does 1 gram of fat provide?","options":["4 kcal","7 kcal","9 kcal","2 kcal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 1 gm fat provides 9 kcal energy and fat is the leading and main source of energy.  Why A wrong: 4 kcal is protein/carbohydrate. Why B/D wrong: incorrect values."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Fat provides what percentage of total energy intake?","options":["15 to 20 percent","30 to 40 percent","45 to 65 percent","10 to 35 percent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fat provides 30 to 40 percent of total energy intake.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect percentages."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"How much energy does 1 gram of carbohydrate provide, and it is described as the cheapest source of energy?","options":["4 kcal","9 kcal","7 kcal","2 kcal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1 gm carbohydrate provides 4 kcal, and carbohydrate is the cheapest source of energy.  Why B wrong: 9 kcal is fat. Why C/D wrong: incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"In a balanced diet, what percentage of total calorie intake should be available from carbohydrate?","options":["15 to 20%","30 to 40%","45 to 65%","20 to 35%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In a balanced diet 45% to 65% calorie from carbohydrate should be available of total intake.  Why A/B/D wrong: incorrect ranges."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Which of the following are examples of monosaccharides (simple carbohydrates)?","options":["Maltose, lactose, sucrose","Glucose, fructose, galactose","Starch, fibers, glycogen","Cellulose and pectin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monosaccharides are glucose, fructose, galactose; disaccharides are maltose, lactose, sucrose; polysaccharides are starches, fibers, glycogen.  Why A wrong: those are disaccharides. Why C wrong: those are polysaccharides. Why D wrong: not the listed monosaccharides."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Disaccharides include which of the following?","options":["Glucose, fructose, galactose","Maltose, lactose, sucrose","Starch, glycogen","Fibers and cellulose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Disaccharides are maltose, lactose, sucrose.  Why A wrong: monosaccharides. Why C/D wrong: polysaccharides/fibers."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Complex carbohydrates (polysaccharides) include which of the following?","options":["Glucose and fructose","Maltose and sucrose","Starches, fibers, glycogen","Lactose and galactose"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Polysaccharides (complex carbohydrates) are Starches, Fibers, glycogen.  Why A/B/D wrong: these are mono- or disaccharides."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Regarding vitamins, how many are fat-soluble and how many are water-soluble?","options":["4 fat-soluble and 9 water-soluble","9 fat-soluble and 4 water-soluble","2 fat-soluble and 7 water-soluble","4 fat-soluble and 4 water-soluble"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fat soluble vitamins total 4 (not dissolving in water, stored in adipose tissue); water soluble total 9 (dissolve, not stored in body).  Why B/C/D wrong: incorrect counts."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Which of the following is a fat-soluble vitamin?","options":["Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid)","Vitamin B1 (Thiamine)","Vitamin K (Phylloquinone)","Vitamin B9 (Folic acid)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 4 fat-soluble vitamins are A, D, E, and K (Phylloquinone/menaquinone).  Why A/B/D wrong: Vitamin C, B1, and B9 are water-soluble."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The chemical name of Vitamin A is:","options":["Retinol","Thiamine","Tocopherol","Cholecalciferol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin A is Retinol (fat-soluble).  Why B wrong: Thiamine is B1. Why C wrong: Tocopherol is Vitamin E. Why D wrong: Cholecalciferol is Vitamin D."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"The chemical name of Vitamin B12 is:","options":["Pyridoxine","Cyanocobalamin","Riboflavin","Pantothenic acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin B12 is Cyanocobalamin (water-soluble).  Why A wrong: Pyridoxine is B6. Why C wrong: Riboflavin is B2. Why D wrong: Pantothenic acid is B5."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Vitamin B2 corresponds to which chemical name?","options":["Thiamine","Riboflavin","Niacin","Biotin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin B2 is Riboflavin.  Why A wrong: Thiamine is B1. Why C wrong: Niacin is B3. Why D wrong: Biotin is B7."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Vitamin B7 is chemically known as:","options":["Folic acid","Biotin","Pyridoxine","Ascorbic acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin B7 is Biotin.  Why A wrong: Folic acid is B9. Why C wrong: Pyridoxine is B6. Why D wrong: Ascorbic acid is Vitamin C."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Macro-minerals have a daily requirement of how much, and which of the following is an example?","options":["> 100 mg; calcium","< 100 mg; iron","> 100 mg; zinc","< 100 mg; magnesium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Daily requirement of macro-minerals is > 100 mg; examples include calcium, phosphorus, potassium, sulphur, chloride and magnesium.  Why B wrong: <100 mg and iron are micro-mineral traits. Why C wrong: zinc is a micro-mineral. Why D wrong: magnesium is macro but requirement is >100 mg not <100 mg."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Micro-minerals (trace elements) have a daily requirement of:","options":["> 100 mg","< 100 mg","> 500 mg","Exactly 100 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Daily requirement of micro-minerals (trace elements) is < 100 mg; examples include iron, zinc, copper, manganese, iodine and selenium.  Why A/C/D wrong: incorrect requirement."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Zinc deficiency can lead to which of the following?","options":["Osteoporosis","Goiter","Delayed wound healing, impaired appetite, and diarrhea","Iron deficiency anaemia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Zinc deficiency can lead to delayed wound healing, impaired appetite, and diarrhea.  Why A wrong: calcium deficiency causes osteoporosis. Why B wrong: iodine deficiency causes goiter. Why D wrong: iron deficiency causes anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Calcium deficiency can lead to which condition?","options":["Goiter","Osteoporosis","Rickets","Anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium deficiency can lead to osteoporosis.  Why A wrong: iodine deficiency causes goiter. Why C wrong: Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets. Why D wrong: iron deficiency causes anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Iodine deficiency can lead to which condition?","options":["Osteoporosis","Goiter","Iron deficiency anaemia","Delayed wound healing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Iodine deficiency can lead to goiter.  Why A wrong: calcium deficiency. Why C wrong: iron deficiency. Why D wrong: zinc deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Which mineral is essential for bone and tooth health?","options":["Iron","Fluoride","Zinc","Copper"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fluoride is an essential mineral for bone and tooth health.  Why A/C/D wrong: iron relates to anaemia, zinc to wound healing, copper is a trace element without this specific role stated."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Phylloquinone (K1) is the plant form of which vitamin?","options":["Vitamin A","Vitamin D","Vitamin E","Vitamin K"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Phylloquinone (K1) is the plant form of vitamin K.  Why A/B/C wrong: these are other fat-soluble vitamins with different chemical names."},{"section":"Batch 014 · PM-JAY, Health & Wellness Centres / Ayushman Arogya Mandir…","question":"Protein is metabolised into amino acids and then into nitrogenic products; which are the end products of protein metabolism?","options":["Glucose and glycogen","Ammonia and urea","Ketone bodies","Fatty acids and glycerol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Protein is metabolised in amino acids then into nitrogenic products like ammonia and urea (end products of protein).  Why A/C/D wrong: these are products of carbohydrate/fat metabolism, not protein."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Menaquinone (K2) is described as which form of vitamin K?","options":["Plant form found in green vegetables","Animal form found in animal-based food and also made by intestinal bacteria","Synthetic water-soluble form","Provitamin converted in the liver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Menaquinone (K2) is the animal form of vitamin K found in animal-based food and also made by intestinal bacteria.  Why A wrong: The plant form (phylloquinone, K1) is found in green leafy vegetables, not K2.  Why C wrong: K2 is not described as a synthetic water-soluble form.  Why D wrong: It is not described as a hepatic provitamin."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Folate is an important B vitamin required for the development of which cells?","options":["Only red blood cells","Only white blood cells","Red and white blood cells","Platelets only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states folate is required in the development of red and white blood cells.  Why A/B wrong: It is required for both, not only one.  Why D wrong: Platelets are not the stated target."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"According to the WHO Key Recommendations on the page, the recommended salt intake per person per day for prevention of cardiovascular disease is:","options":["Less than 5 grams (approximately 2 g sodium)","Less than 10 grams (approximately 4 g sodium)","Less than 2 grams (approximately 1 g sodium)","Less than 8 grams (approximately 3 g sodium)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO recommends a salt intake of <5 grams (approximately 2 g sodium) per person per day.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated <5 g salt / ~2 g sodium value."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per the WHO Key Recommendations, cardiovascular disease is described as:","options":["The leading cause of death globally","A minor cause of death","Only significant in developing nations","The second leading cause of death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states CVD is the leading cause of death globally.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated \"leading cause of death globally.\""},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"According to WHO, to avoid unhealthy weight gain, total fat should not exceed what percentage of total energy intake?","options":["10%","20%","30%","40%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: WHO recommends total fat should not exceed 30% of total energy intake.  Why A wrong: 10% is the limit for saturated fats.  Why B/D wrong: These values are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"As per WHO recommendations on the page, saturated fats and trans-fats should be limited to:","options":["Saturated <10% and trans-fats <1%","Saturated <1% and trans-fats <10%","Saturated <20% and trans-fats <5%","Saturated <5% and trans-fats <2%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO states saturated fats <10% and trans-fats <1% of total energy intake.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the stated limits."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The primary function of Vitamin A (Retinol) is:","options":["Blood clotting","Normal vision and normal cell development in the body","Amino-acid metabolism","Antioxidant only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The vitamin table lists Vitamin A's function as normal vision and normal cell development.  Why A wrong: Blood clotting is vitamin K's function.  Why C wrong: Amino-acid metabolism is vitamin B6's role.  Why D wrong: Antioxidant describes vitamin E/C, not the primary listed function of A."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Deficiency of Vitamin A (Retinol) classically causes:","options":["Scurvy","Beri-Beri","Night blindness and dry eyes/softening of the cornea","Rickets"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists night blindness and dry eyes, softening of the cornea as vitamin A deficiency features.  Why A wrong: Scurvy is vitamin C deficiency.  Why B wrong: Beri-Beri is thiamine (B1) deficiency.  Why D wrong: Rickets is vitamin D deficiency in children."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which vitamin is Thiamin, whose deficiency causes Beri-Beri?","options":["Vitamin B1","Vitamin B2","Vitamin B6","Vitamin B12"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table identifies Thiamin as Vitamin B1, a coenzyme for energy metabolism and nervous system function, with deficiency causing Beri-Beri.  Why B wrong: B2 is Riboflavin.  Why C wrong: B6 is Pyridoxine.  Why D wrong: B12 is Cyanocobalamin."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The animal source specifically listed for Vitamin B1 (Thiamin) in the table is:","options":["Liver","Pork","Milk and milk products","Eggs only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Pork as the animal source of Vitamin B1.  Why A wrong: Liver is listed for folate (B9).  Why C wrong: Milk and milk products are listed for B2 (Riboflavin).  Why D wrong: Eggs alone are not the listed B1 animal source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Deficiency of Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin) is associated with which condition listed in the table?","options":["Ariboflavinosis and Cheilosis","Scurvy","Pernicious anaemia","Rickets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Ariboflavinosis and Cheilosis in the B2/B3 deficiency region (Ariboflavinosis, Cheilosis).  Why B wrong: Scurvy is vitamin C deficiency.  Why C wrong: Pernicious anaemia is B12 deficiency.  Why D wrong: Rickets is vitamin D deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which vitamin is Niacin, whose deficiency is associated with Pellagra?","options":["Vitamin B3","Vitamin B5","Vitamin B7","Vitamin B9"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table identifies Niacin as Vitamin B3, a coenzyme in energy metabolism, with Pellagra listed as its deficiency.  Why B wrong: B5 is Pantothenic acid.  Why C wrong: B7 is Biotin.  Why D wrong: B9 is Folate/folic acid."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic acid) functions as part of a coenzyme used in:","options":["Energy metabolism","Amino-acid metabolism","Synthesis of new cells","Blood clotting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Vitamin B5 as part of a coenzyme used in energy metabolism.  Why B wrong: Amino-acid metabolism is B6's role.  Why C wrong: Synthesis of new cells is B9/B12's role.  Why D wrong: Blood clotting is vitamin K."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) is part of a coenzyme used in:","options":["Amino-acid metabolism","Blood clotting","Collagen formation","Calcium absorption"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Vitamin B6's function as part of a coenzyme used in amino-acid metabolism.  Why B wrong: Blood clotting is vitamin K.  Why C wrong: Collagen formation is vitamin C.  Why D wrong: Calcium regulation is vitamin D."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Deficiency of Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) includes which of the following per the table?","options":["Anemia, nerve damage, seizures, nerve degeneration, peripheral neuritis","Night blindness","Bleeding and haemorrhage","Scurvy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists anemia, nerve damage, seizures, nerve degeneration and peripheral neuritis for B6 deficiency.  Why B wrong: Night blindness is vitamin A.  Why C wrong: Bleeding/haemorrhage is vitamin K.  Why D wrong: Scurvy is vitamin C."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Vitamin B7 in the table is also known as:","options":["Biotin","Folate","Niacin","Cobalamin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Vitamin B7 as Biotin, involved in cell growth and strengthening hair and nails.  Why B wrong: Folate is B9.  Why C wrong: Niacin is B3.  Why D wrong: Cobalamin is B12."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Vitamin B9 (Folate/folic acid) deficiency is associated with which of the following?","options":["NTD in infant, LBW babies, megaloblastic anemia, premature babies, weight loss","Scurvy","Beri-Beri","Rickets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists neural tube defects (NTD) in infant, low-birth-weight babies, megaloblastic anemia, premature babies and weight loss for folate deficiency.  Why B/C/D wrong: Scurvy (C), Beri-Beri (B1) and Rickets (D) are deficiencies of other vitamins."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"According to the table, the vegetarian (Vag.) source of Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) is:","options":["None","Green leafy vegetables","Citrus fruits","Whole grains"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"Vag. Source: None\" for B12, with animal source being meats and egg.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed as B12 vegetarian sources; B12 has no listed vegetarian source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Deficiency of Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) causes which of the following per the table?","options":["Megaloblastic anemia, nerve-cell death, numbness/tingling of extremities, pernicious anaemia","Night blindness","Osteomalacia","Beri-Beri"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists megaloblastic anemia, nerve-cell death, numbness/tingling of extremities and pernicious anaemia for B12 deficiency.  Why B wrong: Night blindness is vitamin A.  Why C wrong: Osteomalacia is vitamin D deficiency in adults.  Why D wrong: Beri-Beri is B1 deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The primary function of Vitamin C (Ascorbic Acid) as listed is:","options":["Antioxidant, collagen formation in the skin, helps immunity","Blood clotting","Energy metabolism coenzyme","Maintains calcium and phosphorus levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists vitamin C as an antioxidant, involved in collagen formation in the skin and helping immunity; deficiency causes scurvy.  Why B wrong: Blood clotting is vitamin K.  Why C wrong: Energy metabolism coenzymes are B vitamins.  Why D wrong: Calcium/phosphorus maintenance is vitamin D."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which is a vegetarian source of Vitamin C listed in the table?","options":["Citrus fruit like lemon, Indian gooseberry (Amla), orange, tomatoes, potatoes","Pork","Meats and egg","Fish"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists citrus fruit (lemon), Indian gooseberry/Amla, orange, tomatoes and potatoes as vitamin C vegetarian sources (animal source: none).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are animal sources of other vitamins, and the animal source for C is listed as None."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Vitamin D (Cholecalciferol) maintains normal levels of which minerals and supports bone growth?","options":["Calcium and phosphorus","Iron and zinc","Sodium and potassium","Iodine and selenium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states vitamin D maintains the normal levels of calcium and phosphorus in the blood, and bone growth and maintenance.  Why B/C/D wrong: These minerals are not the stated targets of vitamin D."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The most common natural source of Vitamin D listed in the table is:","options":["Sunlight","Fortified milk","Fatty fish","Eggs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"Natural Source: Sunlight (most common)\" for vitamin D.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are animal food sources but not the \"most common\" natural source stated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Vitamin D deficiency causes which condition in adults versus children?","options":["Osteomalacia/osteoporosis in adults; Rickets in children","Rickets in adults; Scurvy in children","Night blindness in adults; Beri-Beri in children","Pellagra in adults; Kwashiorkor in children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists osteomalacia and osteoporosis in adults and rickets in children for vitamin D deficiency.  Why B/C/D wrong: These pairings mix in deficiencies of other vitamins/nutrients."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Vitamin K (Phylloquinone) primarily functions in:","options":["Blood clotting","Vision","Antioxidant defense","Amino-acid metabolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists blood clotting as vitamin K's function; deficiency causes bleeding/haemorrhage.  Why B wrong: Vision is vitamin A.  Why C wrong: Antioxidant is vitamin E/C.  Why D wrong: Amino-acid metabolism is vitamin B6."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"According to the table, in addition to green leafy vegetables/cabbage and liver/eggs, vitamin K is also made by the:","options":["Intestine","Liver only","Kidney","Skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states vitamin K is \"Also made by intestine.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The page attributes endogenous vitamin K production to the intestine (intestinal bacteria), not liver, kidney or skin."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The RDA table for Indians shown on the page is based on which report?","options":["ICMR Report 2020","WHO Report 2015","NIN Report 2010","NFHS-5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table title reads \"Summary Of RDA (Recommended Daily Allowances) For Indians, ICMR Report 2020.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the source of this RDA table."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per the ICMR 2020 key points, the daily recommended additional energy requirement for a pregnant woman is:","options":["+350 Kcal/day (2130+350 = 2480 Kcal/day)","+600 Kcal/day","+520 Kcal/day","+200 Kcal/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mission Key Points state +350 Kcal/day for pregnant women (2130+350 = 2480 Kcal/day).  Why B wrong: +600 Kcal/day is for lactation in the first 6 months.  Why C wrong: +520 Kcal/day is for lactation at 7–12 months.  Why D wrong: Not a stated value."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020, the additional daily energy requirement for a lactating woman during the first 6 months is:","options":["+600 Kcal/day (2130+600 = 2730 Kcal/day)","+350 Kcal/day","+520 Kcal/day","+450 Kcal/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points state +600 Kcal/day additionally required during first 6 months (2130+600 = 2730 Kcal/day).  Why B wrong: +350 is for pregnancy.  Why C wrong: +520 is for 7–12 months lactation.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020, the additional daily energy requirement for a lactating woman during 7–12 months is:","options":["+520 Kcal/day (2130+520 = 2650 Kcal/day)","+600 Kcal/day","+350 Kcal/day","+250 Kcal/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points state +520 Kcal/day additionally required during 7–12 months (2130+520 = 2650 Kcal/day).  Why B wrong: +600 is for 0–6 months lactation.  Why C wrong: +350 is for pregnancy.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020 key points, the daily recommended protein for a pregnant woman in the first trimester is:","options":["46 gm/day","45.5 gm/day","58 gm/day","53 gm/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points state first-trimester protein requirement is 46 gm/day.  Why B wrong: 45.5 gm/day (36+9.5) is the 2nd trimester value.  Why C wrong: 58 gm/day (36+22) is the 3rd trimester value.  Why D wrong: 53 gm/day (36+17) is for lactating women in first 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020, the daily recommended protein for a pregnant woman in the 2nd trimester is:","options":["36 + 9.5 = 45.5 gm/day","46 gm/day","36 + 22 = 58 gm/day","36 + 17 = 53 gm/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points give 2nd trimester protein as 36 + 9.5 = 45.5 gm/day.  Why B wrong: 46 gm/day is the 1st trimester value.  Why C wrong: 58 gm/day is the 3rd trimester value.  Why D wrong: 53 gm/day is for lactation first 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020, the daily recommended protein for a pregnant woman in the 3rd trimester is:","options":["36 + 22 = 58 gm/day","45.5 gm/day","46 gm/day","53 gm/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points state 3rd trimester protein as 36 + 22 = 58 gm/day.  Why B wrong: 45.5 gm/day is the 2nd trimester value.  Why C wrong: 46 gm/day is the 1st trimester value.  Why D wrong: 53 gm/day is for lactation first 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020, the daily recommended protein for a lactating woman in the first 6 months is:","options":["36 + 17 = 53 gm/day","58 gm/day","45.5 gm/day","46 gm/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points state 36 + 17 = 53 gm/day for lactating women in first 6 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are pregnancy trimester protein values, not lactation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020 key points, the daily recommended calcium for pregnant and lactating women respectively is:","options":["1000 mg/day (pregnant) and 1200 mg/day (lactating)","1200 mg/day (pregnant) and 1000 mg/day (lactating)","500 mg/day for both","800 mg/day for both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points state calcium 1000 mg/day for pregnant women and 1200 mg/day for lactating women.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These values are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020, the daily recommended Vitamin D for non-pregnant, pregnant, and lactating women is:","options":["600 IU/day","400 IU/day","200 IU/day","800 IU/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points state Vitamin D as 600 IU/day for non-pregnant, pregnant and lactating women.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values are not the stated ICMR 2020 recommendation (note: 200/400 IU appear only in the Old Dietary Guidelines table)."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per ICMR 2020 key points, the daily recommended iron for pregnant and lactating women respectively is:","options":["27 mg (pregnant) and 23 mg (lactating)","23 mg (pregnant) and 27 mg (lactating)","40 mg for both","18 mg for both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key points state iron 27 mg for pregnant women and 23 mg for lactating women.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: 40/18 mg appear in the Old Dietary Guidelines, not the ICMR 2020 key points."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the ICMR 2020 RDA table, the reference body weight and protein RDA for an adult man are:","options":["65 kg and 54.0 g/d","55 kg and 46.0 g/d","60 kg and 60.0 g/d","70 kg and 50.0 g/d"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The RDA table lists Men as 65 kg body weight with 54.0 g/d protein.  Why B wrong: 55 kg and 46.0 g/d are the values for Women.  Why C/D wrong: These values are not in the table."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the Old Dietary Guidelines table, the energy allowance for a non-pregnant woman of reproductive age is:","options":["2,200 kcal","2,500 kcal","2,600 kcal","1,800 kcal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Old Dietary Guidelines list non-pregnant energy as 2,200 kcal.  Why B wrong: 2,500 kcal is for pregnancy (second half).  Why C wrong: 2,600 kcal is for lactation.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the Old Dietary Guidelines, the protein allowance for non-pregnant, pregnancy (second half), and lactation respectively is:","options":["50 g, 60 g, 65 g","40 g, 18 g, 30 g","60 g, 50 g, 65 g","50 g, 65 g, 60 g"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists protein as 50 g (non-pregnant), 60 g (pregnancy second half), 65 g (lactation).  Why B wrong: Those are the iron (mg) values.  Why C/D wrong: The order/values do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Malnutrition is best defined per the page as:","options":["A pathological condition/state which is an absolute deficiency or excess of one or more essential nutrients","Only a deficiency of protein","Only excess intake of calories","A contagious infectious disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The description defines malnutrition as a pathological condition of absolute deficiency or excess of one or more essential nutrients.  Why B/C wrong: It covers both deficiency and excess, not only one.  Why D wrong: Malnutrition is not an infectious disease."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Under-nutrition has how many types per the page?","options":["4 types — wasting, stunting, underweight, and deficiencies in vitamins and minerals","2 types","3 types","5 types"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists 4 types of under-nutrition: wasting, stunting, underweight, and micronutrient (vitamin/mineral) deficiencies.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated count is 4."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Low weight-for-height is known as:","options":["Wasting","Stunting","Underweight","Obesity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states low weight-for-height is wasting, usually indicating recent and severe weight loss such as from diarrhea.  Why B wrong: Stunting is low height-for-age.  Why C wrong: Underweight is low weight-for-age.  Why D wrong: Obesity is over-nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Low height-for-age is known as:","options":["Stunting","Wasting","Underweight","Overweight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states low height-for-age is stunting, the result of chronic/recurrent under-nutrition associated with poor socioeconomic conditions and illness.  Why B wrong: Wasting is low weight-for-height.  Why C wrong: Underweight is low weight-for-age.  Why D wrong: Overweight is over-nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Low weight-for-age is known as:","options":["Underweight","Wasting","Stunting","Obesity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states low weight-for-age is underweight; such a child may be stunted, wasted, or both.  Why B wrong: Wasting is low weight-for-height.  Why C wrong: Stunting is low height-for-age.  Why D wrong: Obesity is over-nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Over-nutrition is described as an imbalance between energy consumed and energy expended, resulting in:","options":["Overweight and obesity","Wasting","Stunting","Marasmus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines over-nutrition as an imbalance (too much energy consumed, too little expended) resulting in overweight and obesity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are forms of under-nutrition, not over-nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which micronutrients are stated to be the most important in global public health terms?","options":["Iodine, vitamin A, and iron","Calcium, vitamin D, and zinc","Sodium, potassium, and magnesium","Folate, vitamin C, and copper"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states iodine, vitamin A, and iron are the most important micronutrients in global public health terms.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the trio stated as most important."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"According to WHO, Protein Energy Malnutrition (PEM) refers to:","options":["An imbalance between the supply of protein and energy and the body's demand for them to ensure optimal growth and function","An excess of protein intake","A deficiency limited to vitamin A","A contagious deficiency disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO definition on the page states PEM is an imbalance between supply of protein and energy and the body's demand to ensure optimal growth and function.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the WHO definition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"PEM is stated to affect mostly which period of life?","options":["First 5 years of life","Adolescence","Old age","First month of life only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states PEM is the most common type of malnutrition and affects mostly the first 5 years of life.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated affected period."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Kwashiorkor is due to which deficiency?","options":["Protein deficiency","Calorie deficiency","Iron deficiency","Iodine deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Kwashiorkor is due to protein deficiency (FAQs).  Why B wrong: Calorie deficiency causes Marasmus.  Why C/D wrong: These are micronutrient deficiencies, not the cause of Kwashiorkor."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The cardinal sign of Kwashiorkor is:","options":["Oedema (swelling of legs and abdomen), thin muscles but fat present, diarrhoea and underweight","Severe subcutaneous fat loss with old-man appearance","Night blindness","Bleeding gums"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists oedema (swelling of legs/abdomen), thin muscles but fat present, diarrhoea and underweight as the cardinal sign of Kwashiorkor.  Why B wrong: That describes Marasmus.  Why C wrong: Night blindness is vitamin A deficiency.  Why D wrong: Bleeding gums relate to vitamin C deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Marasmus is due to which deficiency?","options":["Calorie deficiency","Protein deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency","Iron deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Marasmus is due to calorie deficiency (FAQs).  Why B wrong: Protein deficiency causes Kwashiorkor.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the cause of Marasmus."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The cardinal sign of Marasmus includes:","options":["Severe subcutaneous fat loss, underweight, dry skin, brittle hair, old-man appearance, thin limbs with little muscle","Oedema of legs and abdomen with fat present","Convulsions","Softening of the cornea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists severe subcutaneous fat loss, underweight, dry skin, brittle hair, old-man appearance and thin limbs with little muscle as the cardinal sign of Marasmus.  Why B wrong: Oedema with fat present is Kwashiorkor.  Why C/D wrong: These relate to other deficiencies."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of malnutrition? (Select all that apply)","options":["Poverty","Lack of primary health infrastructure","Lack of awareness and illiteracy","Weak public distribution system in India","Adequate sanitation practices"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists poverty, lack of primary health infrastructure, lack of awareness/illiteracy, weak public distribution system, poor implementation of child development schemes, poor sanitation, and insufficient protein/energy supply as causes.  Why E wrong: The page lists POOR sanitation practices in rural areas as a cause, not adequate sanitation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The best and most reliable technique for identifying malnourished children per the page is to:","options":["Take their height and weight regularly, once a month","Perform MUAC only","Do a blood test monthly","Measure head circumference yearly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states height & weight measured regularly (once a month) is the best and most reliable technique, using the Growth/Road-to-Health chart.  Why B wrong: MUAC is the second choice of method.  Why C wrong: Clinical & laboratory examination is the third method.  Why D wrong: Head circumference monthly measurement is not the stated best technique."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The Growth Chart is also known as:","options":["Road-to-Health chart","Sakhir chart","Quetlet's chart","WHO Z-score card"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the growth chart is also known as the Road-to-Health chart (MP NHM 2020).  Why B wrong: Sakhir tape is used for MUAC.  Why C wrong: Quetlet's is an index (BMI).  Why D wrong: Not the name given."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Growth monitoring of children in India is usually done by Anganwadi workers using which type of growth chart?","options":["Weight-for-age growth charts","Height-for-age growth charts","BMI-for-age growth charts","MUAC-for-age charts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states growth monitoring in India is usually done by Anganwadi workers using weight-for-age growth charts.  Why B wrong: Height-for-age is not the stated chart used.  Why C wrong: BMI-for-age is used for the 5–19 years chart, not the routine Anganwadi under-five monitoring.  Why D wrong: MUAC uses a tape, not a growth chart."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"On the growth chart, pink-border charts and blue-border charts are respectively used for:","options":["Girls (pink) and boys (blue)","Boys (pink) and girls (blue)","Infants (pink) and children (blue)","Under-five (pink) and over-five (blue)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states pink-border growth charts are for girls and blue-border charts are for boys.  Why B wrong: The colors are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The distinction is by sex, not age."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"On growth chart interpretation, when the plotted point is below the third curve, the child is classified as:","options":["Severely underweight","Normal","Moderately underweight","Overweight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states below the third curve = severely underweight (above second curve = normal; between second and third = moderately underweight).  Why B wrong: Normal is above the second curve.  Why C wrong: Moderately underweight is between the second and third curve.  Why D wrong: Overweight is not defined by these curves here."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per the page, growth monitoring during the first year of life should be done:","options":["Every month","Every two months","Every three months","Every six months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states growth monitoring is every month during the first year, every two months during the second year, and every three months up to five/six years.  Why B wrong: Every two months is for the second year.  Why C wrong: Every three months is up to five/six years.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"MUAC (Mid-Upper Arm Circumference) is described as which choice of method for assessing malnutrition in 1–5 year children?","options":["Second choice of method","First and best method","Third method","Not recommended"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states MUAC is the second choice of method for assessing malnutrition in children 1 to 5 years (height & weight being first).  Why B wrong: Height & weight is the best/first method.  Why C wrong: Clinical & laboratory examination is the third method.  Why D wrong: MUAC is recommended."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The normal Mid-Upper Arm Circumference in healthy children older than 1 year is stated to be probably more than:","options":["15–17 cm","11.5 cm","12.5 cm","20 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states normal MUAC in healthy children older than 1 year is probably more than 15–17 cm.  Why B wrong: <11.5 cm indicates under-nutrition/SAM.  Why C wrong: 12.5 cm relates to the normal cutoff on the Sakhir tape interpretation.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"MUAC is conventionally measured over which arm, at a midpoint between which two landmarks?","options":["Left arm, midway between acromion (shoulder) and olecranon (elbow) with arm bent at right angle","Right arm, midway between wrist and elbow","Left arm, at the wrist","Right arm, midway between shoulder and wrist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states MUAC is conventionally measured over the left arm at a point midway between the acromion (shoulder) and olecranon (elbow) with the arm bent at a right angle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated arm/landmarks."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The fiber tape used to measure MUAC is known as:","options":["Sakhir Tape","Shakir/growth tape only","Quetlet tape","Road-to-Health tape"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states MUAC is measured by a fiber tape known as Sakhir Tape (Raj. BPCCHN 2022). [Note: source prints \"Sakhir Tape\"; the correct eponym is \"Shakir's tape\", named after Dr. M. Shakir.]  Why B wrong: The page spells it \"Sakhir Tape\" and option B adds \"growth tape only\", which is not the keyed term.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the tape's name."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"On the Sakhir tape MUAC color coding, a green reading (>12.5 cm) indicates:","options":["Normal","Borderline","Under-nutrition","Severe wasting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The interpretation table lists >12.5 cm = Green = Normal.  Why B wrong: Borderline (yellow) is 11.5–12.5 cm.  Why C wrong: Under-nutrition (red) is <11.5 cm.  Why D wrong: Severe wasting is not this color band."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"On the Sakhir tape, a MUAC of 11.5–12.5 cm is color-coded and interpreted as:","options":["Yellow — Borderline","Green — Normal","Red — Under-nutrition","Blue — Severe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists 11.5–12.5 cm = Yellow = Borderline.  Why B wrong: Green/Normal is >12.5 cm.  Why C wrong: Red/Under-nutrition is <11.5 cm.  Why D wrong: Blue is not a listed band."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"On the Sakhir tape, a MUAC reading of <11.5 cm is color-coded red and interpreted as:","options":["Under-nutrition","Normal","Borderline","Overweight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists <11.5 cm = Red = Under nutrition.  Why B wrong: Normal is >12.5 cm (green).  Why C wrong: Borderline is 11.5–12.5 cm (yellow).  Why D wrong: Not a listed interpretation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"According to the ICMR Classification of malnutrition, \"Normal\" nutrition is defined as weight-for-age of:","options":["80% and above","70–80%","90% and above","60–70%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ICMR Classification lists Normal as 80% and above.  Why B wrong: 70–80% is Grade I.  Why C wrong: >90% normal belongs to the Gomez classification.  Why D wrong: 60–70% is Grade II."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the ICMR Classification, Grade IV malnutrition corresponds to weight-for-age of:","options":["Below 50%","70–80%","60–70%","50–60%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ICMR Classification lists Grade IV as below 50%.  Why B wrong: 70–80% is Grade I.  Why C wrong: 60–70% is Grade II.  Why D wrong: 50–60% is Grade III."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the Gomez Classification, a weight of >90% of standard is graded as:","options":["Normal","1st degree","2nd degree","3rd degree"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Gomez Classification lists >90% as normal; 75–89% = 1st degree; 60–74% = 2nd degree; below 60% = 3rd degree.  Why B wrong: 1st degree is 75–89%.  Why C wrong: 2nd degree is 60–74%.  Why D wrong: 3rd degree is below 60%."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the IAP Classification (1972), Grade-I (mild malnutrition) corresponds to weight-for-age (median)% of:","options":["71–80",">80","61–70","51–60"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IAP 1972 lists Normal >80, Grade-I 71–80 (mild), Grade-II 61–70 (moderate), Grade-III 51–60 (severe), Grade-IV <50 (very severe).  Why B wrong: >80 is Normal.  Why C wrong: 61–70 is Grade-II.  Why D wrong: 51–60 is Grade-III."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the IAP Classification (1972), a weight-for-age (median) of <50% is graded as:","options":["Grade-IV (very severe malnutrition)","Grade-I (mild malnutrition)","Grade-II (moderate malnutrition)","Grade-III (severe malnutrition)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IAP 1972 lists Grade-IV as <50 (very severe malnutrition).  Why B wrong: Grade-I is 71–80.  Why C wrong: Grade-II is 61–70.  Why D wrong: Grade-III is 51–60."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per the WHO/IAP recommended criteria, Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM) includes which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Weight-for-height less than -3 SD","Visible severe wasting","MUAC < 11.5 cm","Oedema of both feet","Weight-for-height ≤ -2 SD to ≤ -3 SD"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: SAM criteria on the page are weight-for-height <-3 SD, visible severe wasting, MUAC <11.5 cm, and/or oedema of both feet.  Why E wrong: Weight-for-height ≤ -2 SD to ≤ -3 SD defines MAM (Moderate Acute Malnutrition), not SAM."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Per WHO/IAP criteria, Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM) is defined by which MUAC range and edema status?","options":["MUAC 11.5–12.5 cm and no edema","MUAC < 11.5 cm and oedema of both feet","MUAC > 12.5 cm and edema","MUAC 10–11 cm and no edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MAM is weight-for-height ≤ -2 SD to ≤ -3 SD, MUAC 11.5–12.5 cm, and no edema.  Why B wrong: MUAC <11.5 cm and bilateral oedema indicate SAM.  Why C/D wrong: These ranges/edema statuses do not match MAM."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The word \"Anthropometry\" is derived from Anthropos and Metron meaning:","options":["Man and Measurement","Body and Weight","Growth and Chart","Nutrition and Study"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Anthropos means Man and Metron means Measurement, so anthropometry is quantitative measurement of the human body.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated word origins."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Anthropometry is described as which kind of technique for assessing body size, proportions, and composition?","options":["The single most probable, universally applicable, inexpensive, and non-invasive technique","An invasive laboratory-only technique","A costly imaging technique","A technique only for over-nutrition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes anthropometry as the single most probable, universally applicable, inexpensive and non-invasive technique, used to evaluate both under- and over-nutrition.  Why B/C wrong: It is non-invasive and inexpensive.  Why D wrong: It evaluates both under- and over-nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which of the following is an AGE-DEPENDENT anthropometric factor? (Select all that apply)","options":["Weight","Height","Head circumference","Chest circumference","Skinfold thickness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists weight, height, head circumference and chest circumference as age-dependent factors.  Why E wrong: Skinfold thickness is listed under age-independent factors."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which of the following is an AGE-INDEPENDENT anthropometric factor per the page? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mid-arm circumference (1 to 5 years)","Weight for height","Body mass index (BMI)","Skinfold thickness","Height"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists mid-arm circumference (1–5 years), weight-for-height, mid-upper-arm-and-height ratio, BMI, skinfold thickness, and indexes (Quetlet's, Ponderal, Dugdale's) as age-independent factors.  Why E wrong: Height is an age-dependent factor."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which indexes are listed as examples of age-independent anthropometric factors?","options":["Quetlet's, Ponderal, Dugdale's","Apgar, Bishop, Wells","Gomez, IAP, ICMR","WHO Z-score only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists Quetlet's, Ponderal and Dugdale's indexes as age-independent factors.  Why B wrong: Apgar/Bishop/Wells are unrelated clinical scores.  Why C wrong: Gomez/IAP/ICMR are malnutrition grading classifications.  Why D wrong: WHO Z-score is not the listed set."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The hardness of water is due to the presence of soluble bicarbonates, chlorides and sulfates of which minerals?","options":["Calcium and magnesium","Sodium and potassium","Iron and copper","Zinc and manganese"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hardness of water is due to soluble bicarbonates, chlorides and sulfates of calcium and magnesium.  Why B/C/D wrong: These minerals are not the stated cause of water hardness."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The hardness of water is determined by the milligrams of which compound per litre and measured in ppm and mg/litre?","options":["Calcium carbonate","Sodium chloride","Magnesium sulfate","Calcium bicarbonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hardness is determined by milligrams of calcium carbonate per litre, measured in parts-per-million (ppm) and mg/litre.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the reference compound for measuring hardness."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Temporary hardness of water is due to the presence of:","options":["Bicarbonate of calcium and magnesium","Sulphate of calcium and magnesium","Nitrate of sodium","Chloride of potassium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states temporary hardness is due to bicarbonate of calcium and magnesium (also called carbonate hardness).  Why B wrong: Sulphate/chloride/nitrate of Ca and Mg cause permanent hardness.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated cause of temporary hardness."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Temporary hardness of water is removed by:","options":["Boiling","Addition of sodium carbonate","Permutit process","Chlorination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states temporary hardness is removed by boiling and is also called carbonate hardness.  Why B/C wrong: Sodium carbonate and permutit process remove permanent hardness.  Why D wrong: Chlorination is disinfection, not hardness removal."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Permanent hardness of water is due to the presence of:","options":["Sulphate, chlorides and nitrate of calcium and magnesium","Bicarbonate of calcium and magnesium","Carbonate of sodium","Soluble oxygen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states permanent hardness is due to sulphate, chlorides and nitrate of calcium and magnesium.  Why B wrong: Bicarbonate of Ca/Mg causes temporary hardness.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated cause of permanent hardness."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Permanent hardness of water is removed by:","options":["Addition of sodium carbonate and Permutit process","Boiling","Sedimentation only","Aeration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states permanent hardness is removed by addition of sodium carbonate and the Permutit process; it is NOT removed by boiling.  Why B wrong: Boiling removes only temporary hardness.  Why C/D wrong: These do not remove permanent hardness per the page."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The three stages of water purification in correct order per the page are:","options":["Storage → Filtration → Disinfection","Filtration → Storage → Disinfection","Disinfection → Storage → Filtration","Storage → Disinfection → Filtration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists First-storage of water, Second-filtration of water, Third-disinfection of water.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sequences do not match the stated order (storage first, then filtration, then disinfection)."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"On the Water Hardness Scale shown, water in the range 0–17 mg/L (ppm) is classified as:","options":["Soft","Slightly hard","Moderately hard","Very hard"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The scale labels 0–17 mg/L as Soft (18–60 slightly hard, 60–120 moderately hard, 121–180 hard, 181–300 very hard, >300 excessive hard).  Why B wrong: Slightly hard is 18–60.  Why C wrong: Moderately hard is 60–120.  Why D wrong: Very hard is 181–300."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"On the Water Hardness Scale, water >300 mg/L (ppm) is classified as:","options":["Excessive hard","Very hard","Hard","Moderately hard"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The scale labels >300 mg/L as Excessive hard.  Why B wrong: Very hard is 181–300.  Why C wrong: Hard is 121–180.  Why D wrong: Moderately hard is 60–120."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Standard precautions are a set of infection control practices used to prevent transmission of diseases acquired by contact with:","options":["Blood, body fluids, non-intact skin (including rashes), and mucous membranes","Only airborne particles","Only contaminated food","Only vector bites"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines standard precautions as practices preventing transmission via blood, body fluids, non-intact skin (including rashes) and mucous membranes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated basis for standard precautions."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which of the following are given as examples of diseases relevant to Standard Precautions?","options":["HIV, HCV, SARS (COVID-19), Hepatitis B","Measles, mumps, rubella","Malaria, dengue, chikungunya","Tuberculosis, chickenpox"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists HIV, HCV, SARS (COVID-19), Hep.B etc. as examples for standard precautions.  Why B wrong: Rubella/mumps are droplet-precaution examples; measles is airborne.  Why C wrong: These are vector-borne diseases.  Why D wrong: TB and chickenpox are airborne-precaution examples."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which of the following is included under Standard Precautions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hand hygiene","Use of personal protective equipment (gloves, masks, eyewear, gown, face shield)","Respiratory hygiene / cough etiquette","Safe injection practices and sterile instruments","Negative pressure isolation room for every patient"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Standard precautions listed include hand hygiene, PPE, respiratory hygiene/cough etiquette, sharps safety, safe injection practices, sterile instruments/devices, and clean/disinfected surfaces.  Why E wrong: Negative pressure isolation rooms are specific to airborne precautions, not routine standard precautions."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Contact Precautions are required for patients infected or colonized with microorganisms transmitted by:","options":["Direct contact or through the patient's secretions or bodily fluids","The airborne route only","Droplets from coughing only","Vector bites"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines contact precautions for microorganisms transmitted by direct contact or through secretions/bodily fluids.  Why B wrong: Airborne route is airborne precautions.  Why C wrong: Droplet spread is droplet precautions.  Why D wrong: Vector transmission is not the basis."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"For Contact Precautions, the recommended PPE and room type are:","options":["Gown and gloves; single patient room (cohort with same infection client)","Surgical mask; negative pressure room","N95 respirator; airborne infection isolation room","No PPE; general ward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists PPE as gown and gloves and room type as single patient room (cohort with same infection client) for contact precautions.  Why B wrong: Surgical mask is droplet precautions.  Why C wrong: N95 + AIIR is airborne precautions.  Why D wrong: PPE is required."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which of the following is a listed EXAMPLE requiring Contact Precautions? (Select all that apply)","options":["MSSA or MRSA","Vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE)","Clostridium difficile infection (CDI)","Scabies, impetigo, pediculosis","Tuberculosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contact-precaution examples on the page include MSSA/MRSA, VRE, CDI, E. coli, rotavirus, salmonella, shigella, RSV, influenza, wound/skin infections (scabies, impetigo, herpes staphylococci and pediculosis).  Why E wrong: Tuberculosis is listed under airborne precautions."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Droplet Precautions are required for patients infected with microorganisms transmitted by droplets, which can be generated by:","options":["Coughing, sneezing, talking, or during the performance of procedures","Direct skin contact only","Contaminated water only","Blood transfusion only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states droplets can be generated by coughing, sneezing, talking or during procedures.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated droplet-generating routes."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"For Droplet Precautions, if a single room is not available, patients should be physically separated by at least:","options":["1 m (3 feet) with privacy curtains drawn","3 m (10 feet)","5 m","No separation required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to physically separate patients by at least 1 m (3 feet) and draw privacy curtains when a single room is not available.  Why B/C wrong: These distances are not stated.  Why D wrong: Separation is required."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"The recommended PPE for Droplet Precautions is:","options":["Surgical mask","N95 respirator","Gown and gloves only","Face shield only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists PPE as surgical mask (room type: single patient room, cohort with same infection client) for droplet precautions.  Why B wrong: N95 is for airborne precautions.  Why C wrong: Gown and gloves is contact precautions.  Why D wrong: Face shield alone is not the stated droplet PPE."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which of the following is a listed EXAMPLE requiring Droplet Precautions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bacterial meningitis (for 24 hours)","Pertussis","Rubella, diphtheria, mumps","Scarlet fever and meningococcal pneumonia","Measles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Droplet-precaution examples on the page include bacterial meningitis (for 24 hours), pertussis, influenza, rubella, diphtheria, mumps, meningococcal pneumonia and scarlet fever.  Why E wrong: Measles is listed under airborne precautions."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"For Airborne Precautions, health workers should wear which respirator before entering the room?","options":["N95, FFP2, or HEPA respirator","Simple cloth mask","Surgical mask only","No mask"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to wear a respirator (e.g., N95, FFP2, HEPA etc.) before entering and remove after exiting, and to perform a respirator seal-check.  Why B/C wrong: Cloth/surgical masks are insufficient for airborne precautions.  Why D wrong: A respirator is required."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"An Airborne Infection Isolation Room (AIIR) should provide negative pressure compared to the corridor and how many air exchanges per hour?","options":["6 to 12 air exchanges per hour","2 to 4 air exchanges per hour","20 to 30 air exchanges per hour","1 to 2 air exchanges per hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the AIIR must have negative pressure compared to the corridor and 6 to 12 air exchanges per hour, direct exhaust to outside, and a door kept closed when not in use.  Why B/C/D wrong: These air-exchange rates do not match the stated 6–12/hour."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"For Airborne Precautions, the recommended PPE and room type are:","options":["N95/FFP2; Negative Pressure Isolation Room with special ventilation","Surgical mask; single room","Gown and gloves; general ward","Face shield; positive pressure room"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists PPE as N95, FFP2 and room type as Negative Pressure Isolation Room with special ventilation for airborne precautions.  Why B wrong: Surgical mask is droplet precautions.  Why C wrong: Gown/gloves is contact precautions.  Why D wrong: A positive pressure room is incorrect; it must be negative pressure."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Which of the following diseases requires Airborne Precautions per the page? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tuberculosis","SARS","Avian Influenza","Chickenpox, herpes zoster, and measles","Pertussis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Airborne-precaution examples on the page are tuberculosis, SARS, avian influenza, chickenpox, herpes zoster and measles.  Why E wrong: Pertussis is listed under droplet precautions."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"Under Contact Precautions, when a single room is not available, health workers should:","options":["Cohort patients with similar symptoms and diagnosis","Place all patients in one open ward regardless of diagnosis","Send patients home","Use only positive pressure rooms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to cohort patients with similar symptoms and diagnosis if a single room is not available.  Why B wrong: Mixing all diagnoses is incorrect; cohorting is by similar symptoms/diagnosis.  Why C wrong: Not stated.  Why D wrong: Positive pressure rooms are not part of contact precautions."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the ICMR 2020 RDA table, the recommended daily iodine intake for a pregnant woman is:","options":["250 µg/d","150 µg/d","280 µg/d","100 µg/d"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The RDA table lists iodine 250 µg/d for a pregnant woman.  Why B wrong: 150 µg/d is the general adult value.  Why C wrong: 280 µg/d is the lactation value.  Why D wrong: 100 µg/d is the 0–6 month infant value."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Vitamins (fat- & water-soluble: functions, sources, deficiencies)…","question":"In the ICMR 2020 RDA table, the calcium recommendation for a lactating woman (0–6 m and 7–12 m) is:","options":["1200 mg/d","1000 mg/d","300 mg/d","500 mg/d"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The RDA table lists calcium 1200 mg/d for lactation (0–6 m and 7–12 m).  Why B wrong: 1000 mg/d is for pregnant women and non-pregnant adults.  Why C wrong: 300 mg/d is the infant value.  Why D wrong: 500 mg/d is a young-children value."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Diseases like AIDS, gonorrhoea, scabies and skin disorders are transmitted mainly by which mode?","options":["Droplet contact","Direct contact","Vehicle borne transmission","Vector borne transmission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Direct contact transmits diseases from person to person; examples include AIDS, gonorrhoea, scabies and skin disorders.  Why A wrong: Droplet contact involves respiratory droplets (TB, measles), not skin contact.  Why C wrong: Vehicle borne involves water/milk/food as the medium.  Why D wrong: Vector borne needs an insect vector."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which is the most common (m/c) mode of spread of diseases?","options":["Vector borne","Vehicle borne","Droplet infection","Contact with soil"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text explicitly states droplet infection is the m/c mode of spread of diseases.  Why A wrong: Vector borne is important but not the most common.  Why B wrong: Vehicle borne is a separate indirect route.  Why D wrong: Contact with soil transmits only limited diseases like tetanus and hookworm."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Droplets of what size become a source of infection when inhaled by a susceptible person?","options":["<5 µm","<10 µm","<15 µm","<20 µm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Droplets less than 5 µm become a source of infection when inhaled.  Why B, C, D wrong: The stated cutoff is <5 µm, not 10/15/20 µm."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Diphtheria, TB, measles, common cold and CSF meningitis are examples of transmission by which route?","options":["Direct contact","Droplet contact","Fomite borne","Trans-placental"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These are listed as examples of droplet contact spread.  Why A wrong: Direct contact examples are AIDS, gonorrhoea, scabies.  Why C wrong: Fomites are contaminated articles like door handles/towels.  Why D wrong: Trans-placental is mother-to-fetus."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Tetanus and hookworm are transmitted mainly by which mode?","options":["Contact with soil","Droplet contact","Vector borne","Fomite borne"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Some diseases are transmitted by contaminated soil; examples given are tetanus and hookworm.  Why B wrong: Droplet examples are TB, measles.  Why C wrong: Vector needs an insect.  Why D wrong: Fomites are contaminated articles."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Rabies and snake bite are examples of transmission by which route?","options":["Inoculation with skin and mucosa","Vehicle borne","Airborne","Contact with soil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inoculation with skin and mucosa examples are rabies and snake bite.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not describe inoculation through skin/mucosa."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"AIDS, rubella, toxoplasmosis and syphilis are examples of which type of transmission?","options":["Droplet contact","Trans-placental (vertical) transmission","Fomite borne","Vehicle borne"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trans-placental/vertical transmission passes the agent in utero from mother to fetus; examples: AIDS, rubella, toxoplasmosis, syphilis.  Why A, C, D wrong: None involve mother-to-fetus transmission."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In vehicle borne transmission, what is the chief vehicle?","options":["Blood","Plasma","Water, milk or food","Air"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The chief vehicle is water, milk or food; others include blood, plasma and biological agents.  Why A, B wrong: Blood and plasma are secondary vehicles, not chief.  Why D wrong: Air is a separate airborne route."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Enteric fever (typhoid), cholera, diarrhoea and Hepatitis B are examples of which transmission?","options":["Vehicle borne","Vector borne","Airborne","Inoculation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These are listed as vehicle borne transmission examples.  Why B, C, D wrong: They do not involve vector, air, or inoculation as the primary route here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which is the most common disease transmitted through contact with blood?","options":["Hepatitis A","Hepatitis B","Cholera","Typhoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B is the most common disease transmitted through contact with blood.  Why A wrong: Hepatitis A is fecal-oral.  Why C, D wrong: Cholera and typhoid are vehicle/water borne."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Malaria, filaria, kala azar, dengue, plague and schistosomiasis are examples of which transmission?","options":["Vehicle borne","Vector borne","Droplet","Fomite borne"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vector borne transmission uses vectors like flies, mosquitoes, cockroaches, lice, ticks, mites; examples: malaria, filaria, kala azar, dengue, plague, schistosomiasis.  Why A, C, D wrong: These routes do not use insect vectors."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Droplet nuclei are extremely small infective particles of what size, generated through coughing and sneezing?","options":["<5 µm","<10 µm","<2 µm","<20 µm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Droplet nuclei are extremely small infective particles <5 µm generated by coughing and sneezing.  Why B, C, D wrong: The stated size is <5 µm."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"TB, measles, influenza and mumps are examples of which transmission?","options":["Fomite borne","Airborne transmission","Vector borne","Contact with soil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These are given as examples of airborne transmission (droplet/droplet nuclei/infected dust).  Why A, C, D wrong: They do not match this list of airborne examples."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Door handles, towels, drinking glasses and toys that convey infection because they are contaminated by pathogens are called?","options":["Vectors","Fomites","Reservoirs","Carriers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fomites are articles that convey infection because they were contaminated by a pathogenic organism; examples: door handles, towels, drinking glasses, toys.  Why A wrong: Vectors are living organisms like mosquitoes.  Why C, D wrong: Reservoirs/carriers are hosts, not inanimate articles."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the most common medium by which pathogenic agents are transmitted to foods from the skin?","options":["Contaminated water","Unclean hands and fingers","Fomites","Air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hands are the most common medium by which pathogenic agents are transmitted to foods from the skin.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are separate transmission media, not the stated most common one for food."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Chlorination prevents the spread of which group of waterborne diseases?","options":["Cholera, dysentery and typhoid","TB, measles and mumps","Malaria, filaria and dengue","Rabies and tetanus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chlorination prevents waterborne diseases such as cholera, dysentery and typhoid.  Why B wrong: These are airborne/droplet.  Why C wrong: These are vector borne.  Why D wrong: These are inoculation/soil transmitted."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Chlorine is a strong oxidant which rapidly kills bacteria, viruses and microbes in water usually after how long?","options":["10 minutes","20 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Chlorine usually kills microbes after 30 minutes in water.  Why A, B, D wrong: The stated contact time is 30 min."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which ions are responsible for the disinfectant action in the chlorination process?","options":["Hydrochloric acid & chloride ion","Hypochlorous acid & hypochlorite ion","Sodium & chloride ions","Nascent oxygen & hydrogen ion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypochlorous acid and hypochlorite ion are responsible for disinfectant action.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated active disinfecting species."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the preferred pH for disinfection with chlorine?","options":["5.5","6.7","7.5","8.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The preferred pH for disinfection with chlorine is 6.7.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated value is 6.7."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The residual chlorine after chlorination of drinking water should be maintained between what range after 1 hour? (Raj. CHO 2020)","options":["0.1–0.3 mg/L","0.5–0.8 mg/L (0.5–0.8 ppm)","1.0–1.5 mg/L","2.0–2.5 mg/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Residual chlorine should be between 0.5–0.8 mg/L (0.5–0.8 ppm) after 1 hour.  Why A, C, D wrong: These fall outside the stated 0.5–0.8 mg/L range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which instrument is used for measuring free residual chlorine?","options":["Chloronome","Chloroscope","Horrock's apparatus","Chlorometer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chloroscope is the instrument used to measure free residual chlorine.  Why A wrong: Chloronome is used for disinfecting with chlorine gas.  Why C wrong: Horrock's apparatus estimates chlorine demand.  Why D wrong: Not mentioned as the measuring instrument."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Horrock's apparatus is used for which purpose?","options":["Measuring free residual chlorine","Disinfecting with chlorine gas","Estimating chlorine demand","Filtration of water"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Horrock's apparatus is used for estimating chlorine demand.  Why A wrong: That is the chloroscope.  Why B wrong: That is the chloronome.  Why D wrong: Not its function."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the correct sequence of steps in large-scale water purification?","options":["Filtration → Storage → Disinfection","Storage → Filtration → Disinfection","Disinfection → Storage → Filtration","Storage → Disinfection → Filtration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The steps are Storage → Filtration → Disinfection.  Why A, C, D wrong: The order is fixed as storage first, then filtration, then disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"According to WHO, any loss or abnormality of psychological, physiological, anatomical structure or function (i.e., sign and symptoms) is defined as?","options":["Disease","Impairment","Disability","Handicap"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO defines impairment as any loss or abnormality of psychological/physiological/anatomical structure or function.  Why A wrong: Disease is any abnormal condition that impairs function.  Why C wrong: Disability is restriction/inability to perform an activity.  Why D wrong: Handicap limits fulfillment of a role."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"According to WHO, any restriction or inability to perform an activity in the range considered normal for a human being is termed?","options":["Impairment","Disability","Handicap","Disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO defines disability as (because of impairment) any restriction or inability to perform an activity in a normal range.  Why A wrong: Impairment is loss/abnormality of structure/function.  Why C wrong: Handicap is the resulting social disadvantage limiting a role.  Why D wrong: Disease impairs function."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Per WHO, the disadvantage resulting from an impairment/disability that limits or prevents fulfillment of a role considered normal for that individual is called?","options":["Impairment","Disability","Handicap","Disease"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Handicap results from impairment/disability and limits fulfillment of a normal role (consequences).  Why A, B, D wrong: Impairment = structural loss; disability = activity restriction; disease = abnormal condition."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the chlorination process, sun light causes chlorine + water to form which two products?","options":["Hydrochloric acid (HCl) + Hypochlorous acid (HOCl)","Sodium hypochlorite + water","Nascent oxygen + hydrogen","Chloride ion + hypochlorite ion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chlorination process diagram shows Cl2 + H2O (with sunlight) → HCl (hydrochloric acid) + HOCl (hydrochlorous acid).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the products shown in the first step."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the chlorination decomposition step, 2HOCl decomposes into 2HCl plus what?","options":["Water","Nascent oxygen [2(O)]","Chlorine gas","Sodium hypochlorite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The decompose step shows 2HOCl → 2HCl + 2(O) nascent oxygen.  Why A, C, D wrong: The end product shown is nascent oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which is described as a natural method of sterilization?","options":["Autoclave","Sun light","Hot air oven","Gamma rays"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sun light is the natural method of sterilization.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are artificial physical methods."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the favourable temperature range for bacterial growth?","options":["20°–40°C","40°–70°C (104°–158°F)","70°–100°C","100°–121°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Favourable temperature for bacterial growth is 40°–70°C (104°–158°F).  Why A, C, D wrong: These fall outside the stated favourable range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the required temperature for the incineration process?","options":["300°–400°C","450°–550°C","600°–650°C (1100°–1200°F)","700°–800°C"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Required temperature for incineration is 600°–650°C (1100°–1200°F).  Why A, B, D wrong: These are not the stated incineration temperature."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which category of medical bio-waste (human anatomical waste, dressing material, soiled waste) is destroyed by incineration?","options":["Red category","Yellow category","Blue category","White category"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Yellow category waste like human anatomical waste, dressing material and soiled waste is incinerated.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incineration is for yellow category waste per the text."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Glassware such as test tubes, flasks and pipettes are sterilised by which method?","options":["Autoclave","Hot air oven","Boiling","Gamma rays"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Glassware (test tubes, flasks, pipettes, glass syringe) is sterilised by hot air oven.  Why A wrong: Autoclave is for dressings, gowns, rubber items.  Why C wrong: Boiling is for metal instruments.  Why D wrong: Gamma rays for sutures, disposables."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which powders are sterilised by the hot air oven method?","options":["Sulfadiazine, starch, zinc oxide and liquid paraffin","Talcum, glucose and salt","Sodium bicarbonate and chalk","Only zinc oxide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Powders sterilised by hot air oven include sulfadiazine, starch, zinc oxide and liquid paraffin.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the listed powders."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In boiling sterilization techniques, instruments are usually boiled at what temperature and duration?","options":["80°C for 5–10 minutes","100°C for 10–30 minutes","121°C for 30 minutes","60°C for 20 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In boiling techniques instruments are boiled at 100°C for 10–30 minutes.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated boiling parameters."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which type of instrument should NOT be sterilized by the boiling method?","options":["Kidney tray","Sharp instruments such as scissors","Artery forceps","Needle holder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sharp instruments such as scissors should not be sterilized by boiling (they lose sharpness).  Why A, C, D wrong: Needle holder, forceps, artery forceps, bowls, basin and kidney tray are sterilised by boiling."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which is the most reliable and specific method of sterilization, most commonly used in the hospital setting?","options":["Hot air oven","Boiling","Autoclave","Gamma rays"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Autoclave is the most reliable and specific method, most commonly used in hospitals.  Why A, B, D wrong: These are less reliable/specific than autoclave."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What are the required temperature, pressure and time for the autoclave?","options":["100°C, 10 lbs, 20 minutes","121°C / 250°F with 15 lbs pressure for 30 minutes","160°C, 15 lbs, 45 minutes","134°C, 30 lbs, 15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autoclave requires 121°C / 250°F with 15 lbs pressure for 30 minutes.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated autoclave parameters."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The autoclave is an example of which type of sterilization?","options":["Dry heat","Moist heat","Radiation","Chemical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autoclave is an example of moist heat sterilization.  Why A wrong: Hot air oven is dry heat.  Why C, D wrong: Autoclave uses steam under pressure, not radiation/chemicals."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The hot air oven is an example of which type of sterilization?","options":["Moist heat","Dry heat","Gas sterilization","Radiation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hot air oven is the example of dry heat sterilization.  Why A wrong: Autoclave is moist heat.  Why C, D wrong: These are separate methods."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Surgical items like sutures, disposable syringes, catheters, cannula, adhesive dressings and prostheses are sterilised by which method?","options":["Boiling","Autoclave","Gamma rays radiation","Hot air oven"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Gamma rays radiation sterilizes surgical items like sutures, disposable syringes, catheters, cannula, adhesive dressings and prostheses.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not sterilize these disposable/heat-sensitive items."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which agent is most widely used in the hospital as an antiseptic and is commonly known as \"spirit\"?","options":["Ethyl alcohol 70%","Formalin 40%","Glutaraldehyde 2%","Savlon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethyl alcohol 70% is most widely used as an antiseptic and is commonly known as spirit.  Why B wrong: Formalin 40% is for fumigation.  Why C wrong: Glutaraldehyde 2% is for endoscopes.  Why D wrong: Savlon is for skin/thermometer/mouth care."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Formalin gas 40% is commonly used for the fumigation of which areas?","options":["General wards","OTs and ICUs","Kitchens","Toilets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Formalin gas 40% is used for fumigation of OTs and ICUs for surface sterilization.  Why A, C, D wrong: The text specifies OTs and ICUs."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Endoscopes and bronchoscopes are usually sterilised by which agent?","options":["Ethyl alcohol 70%","Glutaraldehyde 2% (Cidex)","Sodium hypochlorite","Hydrogen peroxide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endoscopes and bronchoscopes are sterilised by Glutaraldehyde 2% (Cidex).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not used for endoscope sterilization here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"How often should the Cidex (glutaraldehyde 2%) solution be changed?","options":["Every 7 days","Every 14–15 days","Every 30 days","Daily"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cidex solution should be changed every 14–15 days.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated interval is 14–15 days."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Savlon is a combination of which two agents?","options":["Chlorhexidine + Centrimide","Chlorhexidine + alcohol","Chloroxylenol + water","Iodine + alcohol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Savlon is Chlorhexidine + Centrimide, used for skin, thermometer and mouth care disinfection.  Why B wrong: Chlorhexidine + alcohol is a different combination.  Why C wrong: Chloroxylenol is Dettol.  Why D wrong: Iodine + alcohol is tincture of iodine."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Dettol, used for skin and clinical thermometer disinfection, is chemically which agent?","options":["Chlorhexidine","Chloroxylenol","Centrimide","Sodium hypochlorite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dettol is Chloroxylenol, commonly used for skin and clinical thermometer disinfection.  Why A, C wrong: These are components of Savlon.  Why D wrong: Sodium hypochlorite is bleach."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Bleaching powder (CaOCl2), commonly used for water treatment in chlorination, is also known as?","options":["Sodium hypochlorite","Calcium hypochlorite","Potassium permanganate","Chloramine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bleaching powder (CaOCl2) is also known as calcium hypochlorite.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are chemically different compounds."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which disinfectant (and dilution) is used for disinfecting HIV patient articles like linen, blankets and utensils?","options":["Sodium hypochlorite 1% (1:100, 500 ppm)","Sodium hypochlorite 10% (1:10 dilution, 5000 ppm free chlorine)","Ethyl alcohol 70%","Glutaraldehyde 2%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium hypochlorite 10% (1:10 dilution, 5000 ppm free chlorine) is used for HIV patient articles.  Why A wrong: 1% is for COVID-19 surroundings.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated agent for HIV articles."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which disinfectant (and dilution) is used for COVID-19 patient surroundings like floor, side railings and stairs?","options":["Sodium hypochlorite 10% (1:10, 5000 ppm)","Sodium hypochlorite 1% (1:100 dilution, 500 ppm free chlorine)","Hydrogen peroxide","Potassium permanganate 1:5000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium hypochlorite 1% (1:100 dilution, 500 ppm free chlorine) is used for COVID-19 surroundings.  Why A wrong: 10% is for HIV articles.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated agents for COVID surroundings."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which disinfectants are most commonly used world widely for skin disinfection?","options":["Spirit and Iodine (povidone)","Sodium hypochlorite and bleaching powder","Formalin and glutaraldehyde","Hydrogen peroxide and cresol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spirit and Iodine (povidone) are most commonly used world widely for skin disinfection.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated common skin disinfectants."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) is commonly used for what purpose?","options":["Skin disinfection","Large wound dressings for wound cleanliness","Moth care","Water treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrogen peroxide is commonly used for large wound dressings for wound cleanliness.  Why A wrong: Spirit/iodine are for skin.  Why C wrong: KMnO4 is for moth care.  Why D wrong: Bleaching powder is for water."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Potassium permanganate (KMnO4) at what dilution is commonly used for moth care?","options":["1:1000","1:5000","1:100","1:10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: KMnO4 1:5000 is commonly used for moth care.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated dilution is 1:5000."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The process that kills microbes (mainly bacteria) in food and drinks such as milk, juice and canned food is called?","options":["Sterilization","Pasteurization","Fumigation","Chlorination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pasteurization kills microbes (mainly bacteria) in food/drinks like milk, juice and canned food.  Why A wrong: Sterilization destroys all microbes and spores.  Why C wrong: Fumigation is surface sterilization by gas.  Why D wrong: Chlorination is water treatment."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Alcohols are used for disinfecting which type of items?","options":["Large wounds","Small surface items such as thermometers, stethoscopes and rubber stoppers of medication vials","Water supplies","Anatomical waste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alcohols disinfect small surface items like thermometers, stethoscopes, equipment buttons and rubber stoppers of medication vials.  Why A, C, D wrong: These require other methods (peroxide, chlorination, incineration)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"An agent (physical or chemical) that kills infectious microorganisms and is mostly applied on non-living things is a?","options":["Antiseptic","Disinfectant","Deodorant","Bacteriostatic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A disinfectant kills infectious microorganisms and is mostly applied on non-living things.  Why A wrong: Antiseptic is applied on living surfaces/tissues.  Why C wrong: Deodorant suppresses bad odours.  Why D wrong: Bacteriostatic inhibits (not kills) bacteria."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"A chemical agent that destroys or inhibits growth of microorganisms and can be applied on living surfaces or tissues (e.g., alcohol, Dettol) is a?","options":["Disinfectant","Antiseptic","Deodorant","Sterilant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antiseptics destroy/inhibit microorganisms and are applied on living surfaces/tissues like alcohol and Dettol.  Why A wrong: Disinfectant is for non-living things.  Why C wrong: Deodorant suppresses odours.  Why D wrong: Not defined here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"An agent that can suppress bad odours (e.g., lime and bleaching powder) is called a?","options":["Antiseptic","Disinfectant","Deodorant","Fomite"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A deodorant suppresses bad odours; examples are lime and bleaching powder.  Why A, B wrong: These kill/inhibit microbes.  Why D wrong: A fomite is a contaminated object."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The destruction of all microorganisms and their spores is defined as?","options":["Disinfection","Sterilization","Asepsis","Antisepsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterilization is the destruction of all microorganisms and their spores.  Why A wrong: Disinfection kills infectious microbes but not necessarily all spores.  Why C wrong: Asepsis is a non-infectious state.  Why D wrong: Antisepsis applies to living tissue."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"A free-from-infection, non-infectious state is termed as?","options":["Asepsis","Antisepsis","Sterilization","Disinfection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asepsis means free from infection (non-infectious state).  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe processes/agents, not the state itself."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"An infection acquired during the course of treatment (in hospital) is known as?","options":["Iatrogenic infection","Nosocomial infection","Cross infection","Community-acquired infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nosocomial infection is acquired during the course of treatment.  Why A wrong: Iatrogenic is caused by diagnostic/therapeutic procedures (physician-induced).  Why C wrong: Cross infection is spread from one person to another.  Why D wrong: Not defined here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Infection that originates during a diagnostic or therapeutic procedure, also known as physician-induced infection, is called?","options":["Nosocomial infection","Iatrogenic infection","Cross infection","Autoinfection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Iatrogenic infection originates during a diagnostic or therapeutic procedure and is also known as physician-induced infection.  Why A wrong: Nosocomial is acquired during treatment (general hospital-acquired).  Why C wrong: Cross infection is person-to-person spread.  Why D wrong: Not the stated term."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"When one person spreads an infection to another, either directly or indirectly, it is termed?","options":["Nosocomial infection","Cross infection","Iatrogenic infection","Latent infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cross infection occurs when one person spreads infection to another, directly or indirectly.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe other infection types."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"An agent that prohibits the growth and development of bacteria (without killing them) is termed?","options":["Bacteriocidal","Bacteriostatic","Antiseptic","Sterilant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bacteriostatic agents prohibit growth and development of bacteria.  Why A wrong: Bacteriocidal kills bacteria.  Why C, D wrong: Not the defined term here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"An agent that kills bacteria is termed?","options":["Bacteriostatic","Bacteriocidal","Deodorant","Antiseptic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bacteriocidal is an agent that kills bacteria.  Why A wrong: Bacteriostatic only inhibits growth.  Why C, D wrong: Not the defined term."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The PPE wearing process is called?","options":["Doffing","Donning","Draping","Gowning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Donning is the PPE wearing process.  Why A wrong: Doffing is the PPE removing process.  Why C, D wrong: Not the defined terms."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The PPE removing process is called?","options":["Donning","Doffing","Discarding","Undressing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Doffing is the PPE removing process.  Why A wrong: Donning is wearing PPE.  Why C, D wrong: Not the defined terms."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Disinfection applied while the client is still staying in the hospital is called?","options":["Terminal disinfection","Concurrent disinfection","Prophylaxis disinfection","Fumigation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Concurrent disinfection is applied during the client's stay in hospital.  Why A wrong: Terminal disinfection is after discharge/death/removal.  Why C wrong: Prophylaxis disinfection includes boiling, handwashing, milk pasteurization.  Why D wrong: Fumigation is a gas method."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Disinfection applied after discharge, death, or removal of the client from the hospital is called?","options":["Concurrent disinfection","Terminal disinfection","Prophylaxis disinfection","Continuous disinfection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Terminal disinfection is applied after discharge, death, or removal of the client.  Why A wrong: Concurrent is during the stay.  Why C wrong: Prophylaxis includes handwashing/pasteurization.  Why D wrong: Not a defined type."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Boiling, handwashing and pasteurization of milk are examples of which type of disinfection?","options":["Concurrent disinfection","Terminal disinfection","Prophylaxis disinfection","Chemical disinfection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Prophylaxis disinfection examples include boiling, handwashing and milk pasteurization.  Why A, B wrong: These are timing-based hospital disinfection types.  Why D wrong: Not the defined type here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Ethylene oxide (ETO) gas used for sterilization of critical items is best described as?","options":["A non-flammable inert gas","A colourless gas that is flammable and explosive","A liquid disinfectant","A radioactive isotope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ETO is a colourless gas that is flammable and explosive, used for gas sterilization of critical things.  Why A, C, D wrong: These mischaracterize ETO."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is a key advantage of ethylene oxide (ETO) sterilization?","options":["It is very cheap and quick","It can sterilize heat- or moisture-sensitive medical equipment without deleterious effects","It leaves no residue and is non-carcinogenic","It works only on glassware"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ETO's advantage is sterilizing heat/moisture-sensitive equipment without deleterious effects.  Why A wrong: ETO is costly and a long process (disadvantage).  Why C wrong: ETO is a known carcinogen.  Why D wrong: It is used for many plastic/electronic items."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which is a stated disadvantage of ethylene oxide (ETO) sterilization?","options":["Ineffective against spores","Costly, long process and known carcinogen","Damages heat-sensitive equipment","Requires very high temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ETO disadvantages are that it is costly, a long process and a known carcinogen.  Why A wrong: ETO is highly effective (97–100%).  Why C wrong: Its advantage is protecting heat-sensitive equipment.  Why D wrong: ETO works at 37–63°C, not very high temperature."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the effectiveness range of ETO sterilization?","options":["70%–80%","85%–90%","97%–100%","50%–60%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ETO is 97%–100% effective.  Why A, B, D wrong: These are below the stated effectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the operational gas concentration range for ETO sterilization?","options":["100 to 300 mg/l","450 to 1200 mg/l","1500 to 2000 mg/l","50 to 100 mg/l"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ETO operational gas concentration is 450 to 1200 mg/l.  Why A, C, D wrong: These fall outside the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the operational temperature range for ETO sterilization?","options":["20 to 30°C","37 to 63°C","70 to 90°C","100 to 121°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ETO operational temperature is 37 to 63°C.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are outside the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the operational relative humidity range for ETO sterilization?","options":["20 to 40%","40 to 80%","80 to 100%","10 to 30%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ETO relative humidity range is 40 to 80%.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are outside the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the exposure time range for ETO sterilization?","options":["30 minutes to 1 hour","1 to 6 hours","6 to 12 hours","12 to 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ETO exposure time is 1 to 6 hours.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated exposure time."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which of the following are typically sterilized by ethylene oxide (ETO)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ventilators, dialysis machine, heart-lung machine","Cardiac catheters, heart valves, pacemakers, sutures","Multi-lumen tubing like central line","Equipment with integrated electronics","Human anatomical waste"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ETO sterilizes surgical plastic disposables, ventilators/dialysis/heart-lung machines, intra-body items (cardiac catheters, heart valves, pacemakers, sutures), multi-lumen tubing (central line), and equipment with integrated electronics.  Why E wrong: Human anatomical waste is incinerated (yellow category), not ETO sterilized."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Obesity is best defined as?","options":["Increase in muscle mass","Abnormal growth of adipose tissue or an increase in fat cell number","Increase in bone density","Fluid retention in tissues"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obesity is the abnormal growth of adipose tissue or an increase in fat cell number, expressed in terms of BMI.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not describe adipose tissue increase."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the BMI formula in metric units?","options":["Weight (kg) × height (m)","Weight (kg) / height (m)²","Weight (lbs) / height (inch)²","Height (m)² / weight (kg)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BMI (metric) = Weight in kilogram / height (meter)².  Why A wrong: It is a division by height squared, not multiplication.  Why C wrong: That is the English-unit formula (before the 703 factor).  Why D wrong: Inverted formula."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the BMI formula in English units?","options":["Weight (lbs) / height (inch)²","703 × Weight (lbs) / height (inch)²","Weight (kg) / height (m)²","703 × height (inch)² / weight (lbs)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BMI (English) = 703 × Weight in lbs / height (inch)².  Why A wrong: Omits the 703 factor.  Why C wrong: That is the metric formula.  Why D wrong: Inverted."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Body Mass Index is also known as?","options":["Broca's Index","Quetelet's Index","Ponderal Index","Waist-Hip ratio"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BMI is also known as Quetelet's Index.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are different anthropometric indices."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"As per the BMI table, a BMI of <18.5 is classified as?","options":["Underweight","Normal","Overweight","Obesity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BMI <18.5 is underweight.  Why B wrong: Normal is 18.5–24.99.  Why C wrong: Overweight is 25–29.99.  Why D wrong: Obesity is ≥30.0."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"As per the BMI table, the normal BMI range is?","options":["<18.5","18.5–24.99","25–29.99","≥30.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal BMI is 18.5–24.99.  Why A wrong: <18.5 is underweight.  Why C wrong: 25–29.99 is overweight.  Why D wrong: ≥30 is obesity."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"As per the BMI table, the overweight range is?","options":["18.5–24.99","25–29.99","≥30.0","<18.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Overweight BMI is 25–29.99.  Why A wrong: That is normal.  Why C wrong: That is obesity.  Why D wrong: That is underweight."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"As per the BMI table, obesity is defined as a BMI of?","options":["≥25.0","≥30.0","≥35.0","≥40.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obesity is BMI ≥30.0.  Why A wrong: ≥25 is overweight/pre-obese.  Why C, D wrong: These are higher obesity grades."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the classification of underweight based on BMI, which range corresponds to Grade I Underweight?","options":["17.0–18.49","16.0–16.99","<16.0","18.5–24.99"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade I Underweight is BMI 17.0–18.49.  Why B wrong: Grade II is 16.0–16.99.  Why C wrong: Grade III is <16.0.  Why D wrong: That is normal."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the classification of underweight, a BMI of <16.0 corresponds to which grade?","options":["Grade I","Grade II","Grade III","Grade IV"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grade III Underweight is BMI <16.0.  Why A wrong: Grade I is 17.0–18.49.  Why B wrong: Grade II is 16.0–16.99.  Why D wrong: No Grade IV in the table."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the classification of obesity based on BMI, which range corresponds to Obesity Grade I?","options":["25.0–29.99","30.0–34.99","35.0–39.99","≥40.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obesity Grade I is BMI 30.0–34.99.  Why A wrong: 25.0–29.99 is pre-obese (overweight).  Why C wrong: 35.0–39.99 is Obesity Grade II.  Why D wrong: ≥40 is Grade III (morbid obesity)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the obesity classification, morbid obesity (Obesity Grade III) corresponds to which BMI?","options":["30.0–34.99","35.0–39.99","≥40.0","25.0–29.99"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Obesity Grade III (morbid obesity) is BMI ≥40.0.  Why A wrong: That is Grade I.  Why B wrong: That is Grade II.  Why D wrong: That is pre-obese."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the WHO BMI classification for children aged 2–19 years, a BMI at the 95th percentile or above is classified as?","options":["Underweight","Normal weight","Overweight","Obese"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: For children 2–19 years, ≥95th percentile is obese.  Why A wrong: Below 5th percentile is underweight.  Why B wrong: 5th–84.9th percentile is normal weight.  Why C wrong: 85th–94.9th percentile is overweight."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the WHO child BMI classification, the 85th to 94.9th percentile is classified as?","options":["Underweight","Normal weight","Overweight","Obese"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 85th to 94.9th percentile is overweight in children.  Why A wrong: Below 5th is underweight.  Why B wrong: 5th–84.9th is normal.  Why D wrong: ≥95th is obese."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The High Power Committee (1989) was under the chairmanship of?","options":["Smt. Sarojani Vardpan","Madras health minister","Kartar Singh","Bhore"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: High Power Committee-1989 was under chairmanship of Smt. Sarojani Vardpan.  Why B wrong: The Shetty Committee (1954) was under the Madras health minister.  Why C, D wrong: These headed other committees."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The Shetty Committee was constituted in which year?","options":["1947","1954","1989","1949"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Shetty Committee was 1954, under chairmanship of the Madras health minister.  Why A wrong: 1947 was establishment of Health Ministry of India.  Why C wrong: 1989 is the High Power Committee.  Why D wrong: 1949 was India's WHO membership."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In which year was the Health Ministry of India established?","options":["1947","1949","1954","1946"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Establishment of Health Ministry of India was 1947.  Why B wrong: 1949 is India joining WHO.  Why C wrong: 1954 is Shetty Committee.  Why D wrong: 1946 is UNICEF establishment."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In which year did India become a member country of WHO?","options":["1947","1948","1949","1950"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In 1949, India became a member country of WHO.  Why A, B, D wrong: The stated year is 1949."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The RCH-2 programme was started in which year?","options":["2002","2005","1997","2010"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RCH-2 programme was started in 2005.  Why A wrong: 2002 is Swajaldhara.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The Swajaldhara Programme was launched on which date?","options":["2 October 1977","25 December 2002","1 December 2002","24 March 2005"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Swajaldhara Programme was launched on 25 December 2002.  Why A wrong: 2 October 1977 is the Village Health Guide scheme.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated date."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Where is the headquarters of WHO situated?","options":["New York","Geneva (Zeneva)","Paris","Rome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The headquarters of WHO is situated at Geneva (spelled \"Zeneva\" in the source).  Why A, C, D wrong: WHO HQ is in Geneva, Switzerland."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The Red Cross Society was established in which year?","options":["1920","1946","1947","1949"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Red Cross Society was established in 1920.  Why B wrong: 1946 is UNICEF.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"MMR vaccine is which type of vaccine?","options":["Killed vaccine","Live attenuated vaccine","Toxoid","Subunit vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MMR is a live attenuated vaccine.  Why A wrong: Cholera vaccine is a killed vaccine.  Why C, D wrong: MMR is not a toxoid or subunit vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The basic unit of Panchayat Raj is?","options":["Block","Gram Sabha","District Panchayat","Village Health Committee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gram Sabha is the basic unit of Panchayat Raj.  Why A wrong: A block covers 100 villages/population.  Why C, D wrong: Not the basic unit."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"One Block consists of or covers approximately how many villages / what population?","options":["50 villages / 40,000","100 villages / population about 80,000 to 1,20,000","200 villages / 2,00,000","10 villages / 10,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 Block covers 100 villages or population about 80,000 to 1,20,000.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated figures."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The Village Health Guide scheme was launched in India on which date?","options":["2 October 1977","25 December 2002","15 August 1975","1 April 1978"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Village Health Guide scheme was launched 2 October 1977.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated date."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the recommended maximum level of noise (as a general parameter)?","options":["40 db","60 db","85 db","90 db"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The recommended maximum level of noise is 85 db.  Why A wrong: 40 db is the maximum recommended in hospital by WHO.  Why B, D wrong: Not the stated maximum."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The MMR vaccine is given at what age and by which route?","options":["Birth, intradermal","12 to 15 months, SC route","6 weeks, oral","9 months, IM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MMR vaccine is given at 12 to 15 months by SC route and protects against Measles, Mumps and Rubella.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated age/route."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the causative organism of diphtheria?","options":["Bordetella pertussis","Corynebacterium diphtheria","Clostridium tetani","Bacillus cereus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Causative organism of diphtheria is Corynebacterium diphtheria.  Why A wrong: B. pertussis causes whooping cough.  Why C wrong: C. tetani causes tetanus.  Why D wrong: B. cereus causes fried-rice food poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Cholera vaccine is which type of vaccine?","options":["Live attenuated","Killed vaccine","Toxoid","Subunit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cholera vaccine is a killed vaccine.  Why A wrong: MMR/OPV/Measles are live.  Why C, D wrong: Cholera vaccine is killed, not toxoid/subunit."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"For the hot air method, the temperature should be maintained between what range for 4 hours?","options":["100° to 121°C","160° to 180°C","200° to 250°C","60° to 100°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For hot air sterilization, temperature is maintained between 160° to 180°C for 4 hours.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated hot air parameters."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the boiling method, water should be boiled for how long to kill all spores?","options":["10 minutes","20 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Boil water at least 20 minutes to kill organisms and 30 minutes to kill all spores.  Why A, B wrong: 20 minutes kills organisms, not all spores.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes is stated, not 60."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the required steam-under-pressure autoclave setting stated in the Quick Revision Points?","options":["5 to 10 lbs for 15 minutes at 100°C","15 to 20 lbs sq. inch for 30 minutes at 121°C","30 lbs for 15 minutes at 134°C","10 lbs for 45 minutes at 110°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Articles are exposed to steam at 15 to 20 lbs/sq. inch for 30 minutes at 121°C.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated autoclave settings."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the absence of an autoclave, which domestic appliance can be used for moist heat sterilization?","options":["Microwave oven","Domestic pressure cooker","Refrigerator","Electric kettle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the absence of an autoclave, a domestic pressure cooker can be used; both autoclave and pressure cooker are examples of moist heat sterilization.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not stated substitutes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What concentration of crude phenol (phenol + cresol) is used for disinfection of faeces?","options":["5%","10%","2%","20%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 10% crude phenol (phenol + cresol) is used for disinfection of faeces.  Why A wrong: 5–10% cresol is used for faeces, urine and sputum.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated concentration."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What percentage of cresol is used for disinfection of faeces, urine and sputum?","options":["1 to 2%","5 to 10%","20 to 30%","40 to 50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 5 to 10% cresol is used for faeces, urine and sputum.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What concentration of chlorhexidine with alcohol is used for skin disinfection/antiseptic purpose?","options":["5% Chlorhexidine with alcohol","2% Chlorhexidine","10% Chlorhexidine","0.5% Chlorhexidine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 5% Chlorhexidine with alcohol is used for skin disinfection/antiseptic.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated concentration."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"5% Dettol is suitable for sterilization of instruments and plastic articles with how many minutes of contact?","options":["10 minutes","30 minutes","5 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 5% Dettol is suitable for instruments and plastic articles with 30 minutes contact.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated contact time is 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Clinical thermometer is disinfected by savlon and spirit within how many minutes?","options":["1–2 minutes","3–5 minutes","10–15 minutes","20–30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical thermometer is disinfected by savlon and spirit within 3–5 minutes.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated time is 3–5 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which hypochlorite solution concentration is used for HIV patient's bed linen?","options":["1% hypochlorite solution","10% hypochlorite solution","0.5% hypochlorite solution","5% hypochlorite solution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1% hypochlorite solution is used for HIV patient's bed linen (per Quick Revision Points, p.920).  Why B, C, D wrong: The point states 1% for bed linen. [Note: source is internally inconsistent — p.918 lists Sodium hypochlorite 10% (1:10, 5000 ppm) for HIV patient articles like linen/blanket/utensils (see MCQ 49), while p.920 states 1% for HIV bed linen. Both values are preserved as printed; this question keys the p.920 value.]"},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Pure alcohol has what disinfection action, and what solution strength is a good disinfectant?","options":["Pure alcohol is best; 100% is ideal","Pure alcohol has no disinfection action; a 70% solution is a good disinfectant","Pure alcohol works only above 90%","Alcohol has no disinfectant use at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pure alcohol has no disinfection action, but a 70% solution of alcohol is a good disinfectant.  Why A, C wrong: Pure/high-concentration alcohol is ineffective.  Why D wrong: 70% alcohol IS a good disinfectant."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"As a deodorant in public health practice, what concentration of bleaching powder is used?","options":["5% bleaching powder","33% bleaching powder","10% bleaching powder","50% bleaching powder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bleaching powder (CaOCl2) is used as a deodorant at 33% concentration.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated deodorant concentration is 33%."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"After formaldehyde gas sterilization of ICU/OT, the room should be kept closed for how long for better results?","options":["1 to 2 hours","6 to 12 hours","24 hours","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After formaldehyde gas sterilization the room is closed for 6 to 12 hours for better results.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated duration is 6–12 hours."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"0.55% Cidex OPA (Ortho-Phthalaldehyde) disinfects endoscopes/bronchoscopes within what contact time?","options":["Within 5 minutes","Within 20 minutes","Within 30 minutes","Within 60 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 0.55% Cidex OPA (Ortho-Phthalaldehyde) is fast, with contact time within 5 minutes.  Why B wrong: 2% glutaraldehyde needs 20 min.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated OPA time."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The sterilization department in a hospital is known as?","options":["CSSD (Central Supply Sterilization Department)","OPD","ICU","CCU"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sterilization department is known as Central Supply Sterilization Department (CSSD).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated hospital departments."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which is the only immunoglobulin that can cross the placenta?","options":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IgG is the only immunoglobulin that can cross the placenta.  Why A, C, D wrong: IgA, IgM, IgE do not cross the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"IgG constitutes about what percentage of total immunoglobulin?","options":["15%","75%","50%","10%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IgG is about 75% of total immunoglobulin.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated value is 75%."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Immunoglobulins are made from which macromolecule?","options":["Carbohydrate","Lipoprotein","Nucleic acid","Steroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immunoglobulins are made from lipoprotein (per source). [Note: source states \"lipoprotein\"; immunoglobulins are in fact glycoproteins (protein + carbohydrate). \"Lipoprotein\" is the best match among the given options for the source-keyed answer.]  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated composition."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Multi drug therapy is used for which disease?","options":["Tuberculosis","Leprosy","Malaria","Cholera"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Multi drug therapy is used for leprosy disease.  Why A, C, D wrong: These use other treatment regimens."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The National Water Supply and Sanitation Programme was started in which year?","options":["1953","1954","1947","1949"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: National Water Supply and Sanitation Programme was started in 1954.  Why A wrong: 1953 is the National Malaria Control Programme.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the total duration of pregnancy?","options":["260 days","280 days (9 months plus 7 days)","300 days","250 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total duration of pregnancy is 280 days, i.e., 9 months plus 7 days.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated value is 280 days."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the difference between sewage and sullage?","options":["Sewage has no human excreta; sullage has human excreta","Sewage is water with human excreta; sullage is waste water without human excreta","Both contain human excreta","Neither contains water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sewage is water with human excreta, while sullage is only waste water without human excreta.  Why A wrong: It is reversed.  Why C, D wrong: They differ specifically in human excreta content."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The waste product that settles down at the bottom of a container after purification is called?","options":["Sewage","Sullage","Sludge","Effluent"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sludge is a waste product that, after purification, settles at the bottom of the container.  Why A wrong: Sewage contains human excreta.  Why B wrong: Sullage is waste water without excreta.  Why D wrong: Not defined here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which is described as the fastest poisoning, with an incubation period of only 1 to 6 hours?","options":["Bacillus cereus poisoning","Staphylococcal poisoning","Clostridium botulinum","Salmonella poisoning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Staphylococcal poisoning is the fastest poisoning with incubation period of 1 to 6 hours only.  Why A wrong: Bacillus cereus is caused by fried rice.  Why C, D wrong: Not described as fastest here."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Bacillus cereus food poisoning is classically caused by which food?","options":["Canned meat","Fried rice","Milk","Eggs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bacillus cereus poisoning is caused by fried rice.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated food."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Ascariasis is caused by which organism and transmitted by which route?","options":["Entamoeba histolytica; fecal-oral","Ascaris lumbricoides; fecal-oral route","Ancylostoma duodenale; skin penetration","Cyclops; contaminated water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ascariasis is caused by Ascaris lumbricoides and its mode of transmission is fecal-oral route.  Why A wrong: That describes amebiasis.  Why C wrong: That describes hookworm.  Why D wrong: That describes dracunculiasis."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Amebiasis is caused by which organism?","options":["Ascaris lumbricoides","Entamoeba histolytica","Ancylostoma duodenale","Bordetella pertussis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amebiasis is caused by Entamoeba histolytica, transmitted by fecal-oral route.  Why A wrong: Ascaris causes ascariasis.  Why C wrong: Ancylostoma causes hookworm.  Why D wrong: Bordetella causes whooping cough."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Hookworm infection is caused by Ancylostoma duodenale and enters the body by?","options":["Fecal-oral route","Penetrating the skin of the feet","Mosquito bite","Droplet inhalation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hookworm (Ancylostoma duodenale) enters the body by penetrating the skin of the feet.  Why A wrong: That is the route for ascariasis/amebiasis.  Why C, D wrong: Not the hookworm route."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Dracunculiasis (Guinea Worm Disease) is transmitted by drinking water contaminated with which organism?","options":["Cyclops","Anopheles","Cyclops-free snails","Sandfly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dracunculiasis is a vector-borne parasite disease transmitted by contaminated water with cyclops.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated vector."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In which year was India declared Guinea Worm free (eradicated)?","options":["2008","Late 2013","2000","2015"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: India was declared Guinea Worm free (eradicated) in late 2013.  Why A wrong: 2008 was the last case in Nigeria.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated year for India."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In the hearing parameters, normal hearing is defined at what decibel level?","options":["20 db","40 db","60 db","85 db"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal hearing is 20 db.  Why B wrong: 20–40 db is mild hearing loss.  Why C wrong: 40–60 db is moderate loss.  Why D wrong: 85 db is the maximum recommended voice."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which decibel range corresponds to moderate hearing loss?","options":["20 to 40 db","40 to 60 db","60 to 80 db",">90 db"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate hearing loss is 40 to 60 db.  Why A wrong: 20–40 db is mild loss.  Why C wrong: 60–80 db is severe loss.  Why D wrong: >90 db is profound loss."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Profound hearing loss is defined at what decibel level?","options":["40 to 60 db","60 to 80 db",">90 db","20 to 40 db"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Profound hearing loss is >90 db.  Why A wrong: 40–60 db is moderate.  Why B wrong: 60–80 db is severe.  Why D wrong: 20–40 db is mild."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the normal hearing voice frequency range in Hz?","options":["20 to 2,000 Hz","20 to 20,000 Hz","200 to 2,000 Hz","2,000 to 20,000 Hz"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal hearing voice range is 20 to 20,000 Hz.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In hospital, the maximum voice recommended by WHO is?","options":["20 db","40 db","60 db","85 db"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hospital, maximum voice recommended by WHO is 40 db.  Why A, C, D wrong: 40 db is the stated hospital maximum."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"BCG vaccine is given by which route?","options":["Subcutaneous","Intradermal","Intramuscular","Oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG vaccine is given by intradermal route.  Why A, C, D wrong: BCG is intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Anganwadi workers have been appointed under which programme?","options":["ICDS (Integrated Child Development Scheme)","RCH-2","NRHM","Swajaldhara"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anganwadi workers are appointed under the ICDS (Integrated Child Development Scheme) programme.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the stated programme."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"One Anganwadi worker covers an estimated population of?","options":["500","1000","5000","3000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One Anganwadi worker covers an estimated 1000 population.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated coverage is 1000."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Kartar Singh's committee is also known as?","options":["Multipurpose Worker Committee","Health Survey and Development Committee","Health Survey and Planning Committee","High Power Committee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kartar Singh's committee is also known as the Multipurpose Worker Committee.  Why B wrong: That is the Bhore committee.  Why C wrong: That is the Mudaliar committee.  Why D wrong: That was headed by Smt. Sarojani Vardpan."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"UNICEF was established in which year?","options":["1920","1946","1949","1947"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: UNICEF (United Nations International Children Emergency Fund) was established in 1946.  Why A wrong: 1920 is the Red Cross Society.  Why C wrong: 1949 is India joining WHO.  Why D wrong: 1947 is Health Ministry of India."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the main activity of UNICEF?","options":["Disaster relief only","To improve the health of mother and children","Water supply management","Vaccine manufacturing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The main activity of UNICEF is to improve the health of mother and children.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the stated main activity."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The cold chain refers to?","options":["Refrigeration of blood only","The system of storage and transport of vaccine at required low temperature from manufacture to the site of immunization","Freezing of medicines","Storage of food"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cold chain is the system of storage and transport of vaccine at required low temperature from the site of manufacture to the site of immunization.  Why A, C, D wrong: These mischaracterize the cold chain."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Cholera vaccine is given by which route?","options":["Intradermal","Subcutaneously (SC)","Intramuscular","Oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cholera vaccine is given subcutaneously (SC).  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated route is SC."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the dose and route of typhoid vaccine?","options":["0.1 ml, intradermal","0.5 ml, SC route","1 ml, IM","0.5 ml, oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dose of typhoid is 0.5 ml and route is SC.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated dose/route is 0.5 ml SC."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Disinfection of contaminated articles and bodily secretions during the course of disease is known as?","options":["Terminal disinfection","Concurrent disinfection","Prophylaxis disinfection","Fumigation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Concurrent disinfection is disinfection of contaminated articles and bodily secretions during the course of disease.  Why A wrong: Terminal is after discharge/death.  Why C wrong: Prophylaxis is handwashing/pasteurization.  Why D wrong: Fumigation is a gas method."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Bleaching powder is also known as?","options":["Chlorinated lime","Sodium hypochlorite","Slaked lime","Quicklime"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bleaching powder is also known as chlorinated lime.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are chemically different substances."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which is described as the best and most effective skin antiseptic?","options":["Ethyl alcohol","Povidone iodine","Chlorhexidine","Hydrogen peroxide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Povidone iodine is the best and most effective skin antiseptic.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are antiseptics but not stated as the best."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Rubella is also known as?","options":["Rubeola","German measles","Roseola","Fifth disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella is also known as German measles.  Why A wrong: Rubeola is measles.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated synonym."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The Schick test is which type of test?","options":["Subcutaneous test","Intradermal ID test","Patch test","Blood test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Schick test is an intradermal ID test.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated form is intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Whooping cough is a bacterial disease caused by which organism?","options":["Corynebacterium diphtheria","Bordetella pertussis","Clostridium tetani","Ancylostoma duodenale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Whooping cough is caused by Bordetella pertussis.  Why A wrong: That causes diphtheria.  Why C wrong: That causes tetanus.  Why D wrong: That causes hookworm."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Ethambutol is which type of drug?","options":["Bacteriocidal","Bacteriostatic drug","Antiviral","Antifungal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ethambutol is a bacteriostatic drug.  Why A, C, D wrong: It is bacteriostatic, not bactericidal/antiviral/antifungal."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"BCG vaccine is administered by a tuberculin syringe of which gauge?","options":["22 gauge","24 gauge","26 gauge","18 gauge"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: BCG is administered by a tuberculin syringe which is 26 gauge.  Why A, B, D wrong: The stated gauge is 26."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the incubation period of polio?","options":["1 to 6 hours","7 to 14 days","21 to 30 days","2 to 3 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Incubation period of polio is 7 to 14 days.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated period is 7–14 days."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"OPV (Oral Polio Vaccine) was discovered by whom and in which year?","options":["Salk in 1955","Sabin in 1957","Jenner in 1796","Pasteur in 1885"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OPV vaccine was discovered by Sabin in 1957.  Why A wrong: Salk developed the injectable polio vaccine.  Why C, D wrong: Wrong scientists/years."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"One ASHA (Accredited Social Health Activist) covers a population of?","options":["500","1000","3000","5000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One ASHA covers a population of 1000.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated coverage is 1000."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"World AIDS Day is celebrated on which date?","options":["1 December","24 March","7 April","25 December"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World AIDS Day is celebrated on 1 December.  Why B wrong: 24 March is World TB Day.  Why C wrong: 7 April is World Health Day.  Why D wrong: 25 December is Swajaldhara launch."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"World Tuberculosis Day is celebrated on which date every year?","options":["1 December","24 March","7 April","8 March"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: World Tuberculosis Day is celebrated on 24 March every year.  Why A wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day.  Why C wrong: 7 April is World Health Day.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In which year was the measles vaccine discovered?","options":["1921","1957","1960","1953"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Measles vaccine was discovered in 1960.  Why A wrong: 1921 is BCG.  Why B wrong: 1957 is OPV.  Why D wrong: 1953 is National Malaria Control Programme."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"In which year was the BCG vaccine discovered?","options":["1960","1921","1957","1946"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG vaccine was discovered in 1921.  Why A wrong: 1960 is measles vaccine.  Why C wrong: 1957 is OPV.  Why D wrong: 1946 is UNICEF."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Breast feeding and coitus interruptus are examples of which type of contraception?","options":["Barrier method","Natural contraception","Hormonal method","Surgical method"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast feeding and coitus interruptus are examples of natural contraception.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not natural methods."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the full form of BCG?","options":["Bacilli Calmette Guerin","Bacterial Culture Growth","Bovine Cell Guerin","Bacillus Calmette Gram"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Full form of BCG is Bacilli Calmette Guerin.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The National Malaria Control Programme was started in which year?","options":["1953","1954","1949","1947"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: National Malaria Control Programme was started in 1953.  Why B wrong: 1954 is the National Water Supply and Sanitation Programme.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"World Health Day is celebrated every year on which date?","options":["1 December","24 March","7 April","7 May"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: World Health Day is celebrated every year on 7th April.  Why A wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day.  Why B wrong: 24 March is World TB Day.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The number of new cases occurring in a defined population during a specified period of time is known as?","options":["Prevalence","Incidence","Epidemic","Endemic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Incidence is the number of new cases in a defined population during a specified period.  Why A wrong: Prevalence is all cases (new and old).  Why C, D wrong: These describe disease occurrence patterns, not counts."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"All cases new and old of any disease included together is called?","options":["Incidence","Prevalence","Attack rate","Case fatality rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prevalence includes all cases new and old of any disease.  Why A wrong: Incidence counts only new cases.  Why C, D wrong: These are different epidemiological measures."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"The study of distribution and determinants of health-related states is termed?","options":["Demography","Epidemiology","Biostatistics","Sociology"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epidemiology is the study of distribution and determinants of health-related states.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are related but distinct fields."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Passive immunity can be induced by administration of?","options":["Live vaccines","Readymade antibody or immunoglobulins","Toxoids","Killed bacteria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passive immunity is induced by administration of readymade antibody or immunoglobulins.  Why A, C, D wrong: Vaccines/toxoids/killed bacteria induce active immunity."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"DMPA (Depot medroxy progesterone enanthate) and NET-EN (norethisterone acetate) are examples of which contraceptive type?","options":["Injectable hormonal devices / progestin-only hormonal contraceptives","Barrier methods","Intrauterine devices","Natural methods"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DMPA and NET-EN are injectable hormonal devices, both progestin-only hormonal contraceptives.  Why B, C, D wrong: They are injectable hormonal, not barrier/IUD/natural."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Hormonal contraceptive devices inhibit pregnancy mainly by?","options":["Blocking the cervix","Suppressing ovulation","Killing sperm","Preventing implantation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hormonal devices inhibit pregnancy by suppressing ovulation.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are mechanisms of other methods, not the stated hormonal mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Measles is also known as?","options":["Rubella","Rubeola","Roseola","German measles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measles is also known as Rubeola.  Why A, D wrong: Rubella is German measles.  Why C wrong: Roseola is a different illness."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the causative organism of tetanus?","options":["Corynebacterium diphtheria","Clostridium tetani","Bordetella pertussis","Bacillus cereus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clostridium tetani is the causative organism of tetanus.  Why A wrong: Causes diphtheria.  Why C wrong: Causes whooping cough.  Why D wrong: Causes fried-rice food poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Pleomorphism, where macule, papule, vesicle, pustule and crust are seen simultaneously in the same area, is the characteristic feature of?","options":["Measles","Chicken pox","Rubella","Small pox"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pleomorphism (all rash stages simultaneously) is the characteristic feature of chicken pox.  Why A, C, D wrong: This simultaneous multistage rash characterizes chicken pox specifically."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Bhore's committee is also known as?","options":["Health Survey and Development Committee","Health Survey and Planning Committee","Multipurpose Worker Committee","High Power Committee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bhore's committee is also known as the Health Survey and Development Committee.  Why B wrong: That is the Mudaliar committee.  Why C wrong: That is Kartar Singh's committee.  Why D wrong: That was under Smt. Sarojani Vardpan."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Mudaliar's committee is also known as?","options":["Health Survey and Development Committee","Health Survey and Planning Committee","Multipurpose Worker Committee","High Power Committee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mudaliar's committee is also known as the Health Survey and Planning Committee.  Why A wrong: That is Bhore's committee.  Why C wrong: That is Kartar Singh's committee.  Why D wrong: That was under Smt. Sarojani Vardpan."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of measles?","options":["Fecal-oral","Droplet","Vector borne","Blood borne"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mode of transmission of measles is droplet.  Why A, C, D wrong: Measles spreads by droplet, not these routes."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Hospital-acquired infection is also known as?","options":["Iatrogenic infection","Nosocomial infection","Cross infection","Community-acquired infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hospital-acquired infection is also known as nosocomial infection.  Why A wrong: Iatrogenic is procedure/physician-induced.  Why C wrong: Cross infection is person-to-person spread.  Why D wrong: Opposite of hospital-acquired."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Lippes loop is an example of which generation of intrauterine device?","options":["First generation IUD","Second generation IUD","Third generation IUD","Fourth generation IUD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lippes loop is an example of a first generation intra uterine device.  Why B, C, D wrong: Lippes loop belongs to the first generation."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"RTI Act was passed and legalized in India in which year?","options":["2002","2005","2010","1977"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RTI Act was passed and legalized in India in 2005.  Why A wrong: 2002 is Swajaldhara.  Why C, D wrong: Not the stated year."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Disease transmission, Chlorination & residual chlorine…","question":"Which method sterilizes bandages, dressings and catgut, where objects are placed in plastic bags before irradiation?","options":["Boiling","Radiation method","Autoclave","Hot air oven"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The radiation method sterilizes bandages, dressings and catgut, with objects placed in plastic bags before irradiation.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not radiation-based methods."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"At what age and by which route is the measles vaccine administered?","options":["6 weeks, intramuscular","9 months, subcutaneous","Birth, oral","12 months, intradermal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measles vaccine is given at 9 months of age by the subcutaneous route.  Why A wrong: 6 weeks/IM is the schedule for DPT/Penta, not measles.  Why C wrong: Birth/oral describes OPV, not measles.  Why D wrong: 12 months/intradermal is incorrect; BCG is intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"On which date was the World Health Organisation (WHO) constituted?","options":["April 7, 1948","December 1, 1948","May 8, 1980","April 7, 1950"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO was constituted on April 7, 1948 (celebrated as World Health Day).  Why B wrong: December 1 is World AIDS Day.  Why C wrong: May 8, 1980 is when WHO declared smallpox eradicated.  Why D wrong: The year was 1948, not 1950."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which is the cardinal (pathognomonic) sign of measles?","options":["Negri bodies","Koplik's spot","Rice water stool","Reed-Sternberg cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Koplik's spot is the cardinal sign of measles.  Why A wrong: Negri bodies are seen in rabies.  Why C wrong: Rice water stool is a feature of cholera.  Why D wrong: Reed-Sternberg cells are seen in Hodgkin lymphoma."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In April 1977, India achieved which public-health milestone?","options":["Declared polio free","Declared free from smallpox","Launched NACP","Launched RNTCP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In April 1977, India was declared free from smallpox.  Why A wrong: India was declared polio free in March 2014.  Why C wrong: National AIDS Control Programme was started in 1987.  Why D wrong: RNTCP/DOTS was launched in 1997."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year did WHO declare that smallpox had been completely eradicated worldwide?","options":["April 7, 1948","May 8, 1980","March 2014","1977"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On May 8, 1980 WHO declared smallpox completely eradicated from all over the world.  Why A wrong: April 7, 1948 is WHO's constitution date.  Why C wrong: March 2014 relates to polio-free India.  Why D wrong: 1977 was India's smallpox-free declaration, not global eradication."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Injection streptomycin is given only to which category of TB patients?","options":["Category I","Category II (second)","Category III","All categories"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Injection streptomycin is given only for category second TB patients.  Why A/C wrong: It is specifically restricted to the second category.  Why D wrong: It is not given to all categories."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"A packet of OCP pills such as Mala-N and Mala-D contains 28 pills. How many are contraceptive (hormonal) and how many are iron pills?","options":["21 contraceptive + 7 iron","28 contraceptive + 0 iron","24 contraceptive + 4 iron","20 contraceptive + 8 iron"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Out of 28, 21 are contraceptive pills and 7 are iron pills (ferrous fumarate 60 mg).  Why B/C/D wrong: The standard composition is exactly 21 hormonal + 7 iron pills."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Mumps is a viral disease characterized by non-suppurative enlargement and tenderness of which gland(s)?","options":["Thyroid gland","Submandibular gland only","One or both parotid glands","Adrenal gland"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Mumps causes non-suppurative enlargement and tenderness of one or both parotid glands.  Why A/B/D wrong: The parotid glands, not thyroid/submandibular/adrenal, are the classic site."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Diphtheria is transmitted by which route?","options":["Faeco-oral","Droplets","Vector bite","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria is transmitted by droplets.  Why A wrong: Faeco-oral is the route for hepatitis-A.  Why C wrong: Vector-borne applies to malaria/dengue.  Why D wrong: Blood transfusion transmits hepatitis-B/AIDS."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The separation of a person with an infectious disease is called?","options":["Quarantine","Isolation","Disinfection","Surveillance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Separation of a person with an infectious disease is called isolation.  Why A wrong: Quarantine restricts apparently healthy contacts, not the diseased.  Why C wrong: Disinfection destroys pathogens on surfaces.  Why D wrong: Surveillance is continuous monitoring of disease occurrence."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which is described as a natural disinfectant?","options":["Sunlight","Boiling water","Chlorine","Alcohol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sunlight is a natural disinfectant.  Why B/C/D wrong: Boiling, chlorine and alcohol are man-made/physical or chemical disinfectants, not \"natural.\""},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the most common route of transmission of hepatitis-A?","options":["Blood transfusion","Sexual contact","Faeco-oral route","Droplet"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Faeco-oral route is the most common route of transmission of hepatitis-A.  Why A/B wrong: Blood and sexual routes are typical of hepatitis-B (serum hepatitis).  Why D wrong: Droplet spread is not the route for hepatitis-A."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Hepatitis-B is also known as?","options":["Infectious hepatitis","Serum hepatitis","Enteric hepatitis","Epidemic hepatitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis-B is also known as serum hepatitis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These terms are associated with hepatitis-A (infectious/enteric), not B."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Rice water stool\" is an important clinical manifestation of which disease?","options":["Cholera","Typhoid","Amoebiasis","Rotavirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rice water stool is an important clinical manifestation of cholera.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause other stool patterns, not the classic rice-water stool."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Rabies is also known as?","options":["Hydrophobia","Photophobia","Slim disease","Black death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rabies is also known as hydrophobia.  Why B wrong: Photophobia is light sensitivity, not a disease name.  Why C wrong: Slim disease is AIDS.  Why D wrong: Black death is plague."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The most common mode of transmission of AIDS is?","options":["Blood transfusion","Unprotected sex (heterosexual, 87%)","Needle sharing","Mother to child"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most common transmission of AIDS is unprotected sex (heterosexual 87%).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are additional routes but not the most common; heterosexual sex accounts for 87%."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the Multipurpose Worker's Scheme launched?","options":["1974","1977","1958","1952"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The multipurpose worker's scheme was launched in 1974.  Why B wrong: 1977 relates to Rural Health Service / National Family Welfare Programme renaming.  Why C wrong: 1958 is the National Malaria Eradication Programme.  Why D wrong: 1952 is CGHS launch year."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"India was declared a polio-free country in?","options":["March 2014","April 1977","May 1980","2009"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: India has been declared polio free in March 2014.  Why B/C wrong: These relate to smallpox milestones.  Why D wrong: 2009 is when swine flu pandemic was declared."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In 1977 the Government of India renamed the \"National Family Planning Programme\" to?","options":["National Family Welfare Programme","Reproductive and Child Health Programme","Rural Health Service","National Rural Health Mission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 1977 it was renamed the 'National Family Welfare Programme'.  Why B wrong: RCH programme was started in 1997.  Why C wrong: Rural Health Service was a separate 1977 scheme.  Why D wrong: NRHM was launched in 2005."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the permanent method of contraception in females and in males respectively?","options":["Tubectomy (female), Vasectomy (male)","Vasectomy (female), Tubectomy (male)","IUD (female), Condom (male)","OCP (female), Vasectomy (male)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tubectomy is the permanent method in females; Vasectomy is the permanent method in males.  Why B wrong: The terms are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: IUD, condom and OCP are temporary/reversible methods."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the best time for intra-uterine device (IUD) insertion?","options":["3 to 7 days of the menstrual cycle","Mid-cycle","Just before menstruation","Any random day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The best time for IUD insertion is 3 to 7 days of M.C. (menstrual cycle).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the recommended timing; days 3–7 are preferred."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the National Malaria Eradication Programme started?","options":["1958","1974","1948","1997"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: National Malaria Eradication Programme was started in 1958.  Why B/C/D wrong: 1974 (MPW scheme), 1948 (WHO/ESIC), 1997 (RCH/RNTCP) are different milestones."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the symbol of an under-five clinic?","options":["Triangle","Circle","Square","Cross"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Symbol of under-five clinic is a triangle.  Why B/C/D wrong: The recognized symbol is the triangle."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Pertussis is known as?","options":["100-day cough / whooping cough","8th-day disease","5-day fever","Break-bone fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pertussis is known as 100-day cough and whooping cough.  Why B wrong: 8th-day disease is tetanus neonatorum.  Why C wrong: 5-day fever is trench fever.  Why D wrong: Break-bone fever is dengue."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Copper-T belongs to which generation of intra-uterine contraceptive devices?","options":["First generation","Second generation","Third generation","Fourth generation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Copper-T is a second generation intra-uterine contraceptive device.  Why A wrong: First generation IUDs are non-medicated (inert).  Why C wrong: Third generation IUDs are hormonal devices.  Why D wrong: There is no fourth-generation classification here."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Third generation intra-uterine devices consist of?","options":["Inert plastic","Copper","Hormonal devices","Silver"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Third generation IUDs consist of hormonal devices.  Why A/B/D wrong: Inert (1st gen) and copper (2nd gen) are earlier types; silver is not the defining feature."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Vitamin-C is also known as ascorbic acid, and its deficiency may cause?","options":["Rickets","Scurvy","Beri-beri","Pellagra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin-C (ascorbic acid) deficiency may cause scurvy.  Why A wrong: Rickets is vitamin-D deficiency.  Why C wrong: Beri-beri is vitamin-B1 deficiency.  Why D wrong: Pellagra is vitamin-B3/niacin deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the duration of the ANM (Auxiliary Nurse Midwife) course?","options":["1 year","2 years","3 years","4 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Duration of ANM course is 2 years.  Why A/C/D wrong: The ANM course is 2 years in duration."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Pertussis is a bacterial infection caused by?","options":["Bordetella pertussis","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Vibrio cholerae","Bacillus anthracis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pertussis is a bacterial infection caused by Bordetella pertussis.  Why B wrong: Corynebacterium diphtheriae causes diphtheria.  Why C wrong: Vibrio cholerae causes cholera.  Why D wrong: Bacillus anthracis causes anthrax."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which test is used to find out the amount of free residual chlorine present in water?","options":["Ortho-tolidine test","Benedict's test","Schick's test","Scotch tape swab test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ortho-tolidine test is used to find out the amount of free residual chlorine in water.  Why B wrong: Benedict's test detects glucose in urine.  Why C wrong: Schick's test is for diphtheria.  Why D wrong: Scotch tape swab test is for pinworm."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The composition of air is approximately?","options":["78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 0.04% CO2","21% nitrogen, 78% oxygen, 1% CO2","50% nitrogen, 50% oxygen","78% oxygen, 21% nitrogen, 0.04% CO2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Air is 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen and 0.04% CO2.  Why B/C/D wrong: Nitrogen is the predominant gas (78%) and oxygen is 21%."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Incubation period\" is defined as the time interval between?","options":["Entry of the disease agent into the body and occurrence of first clinical manifestations","Exposure and recovery","First symptom and death","Vaccination and immunity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incubation period = interval between entry of the disease-producing agent into the body and occurrence of clinical manifestations for the first time.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe recovery, disease course, or immune response, not incubation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the National AIDS Control Programme (NACP) started in India?","options":["1987","1997","1948","2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: National AIDS Control Programme was started in 1987.  Why B wrong: 1997 is RCH programme.  Why C wrong: 1948 is WHO/ESIC.  Why D wrong: 2005 is NRHM."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The Reproductive and Child Health (RCH) Programme was started in?","options":["1997","1987","1974","2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RCH programme was started in 1997.  Why B/C/D wrong: 1987 (NACP), 1974 (MPW), 2005 (NRHM) are different."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Sharp instruments should NOT be sterilized by which method?","options":["Boiling","Autoclaving","Chemical sterilization","Dry heat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sharp instruments should not be sterilized by boiling (it blunts them).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are acceptable alternatives for sharp instruments."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The Kartar Singh Committee is also known as?","options":["The committee on multipurpose workers under health and family planning","Group on medical education and support manpower","Health Survey and Development Committee","Committee on integration of health services"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kartar Singh committee is known as 'The committee on multipurpose workers under health and family planning'.  Why B wrong: That describes the Shrivastav committee.  Why C/D wrong: These are other committees, not Kartar Singh."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The Shrivastav Committee is also known as?","options":["Group on medical education and support manpower","Committee on multipurpose workers","Bhore Committee","Mudaliar Committee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Shrivastav committee is known as 'Group on medical education and support manpower'.  Why B wrong: That is the Kartar Singh committee.  Why C/D wrong: Bhore and Mudaliar are different committees."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The \"under-five clinic\" was formerly known as?","options":["Well baby clinic","Growth clinic","Immunization clinic","Anganwadi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under-five clinic is formerly known as \"well baby clinic\".  Why B/C/D wrong: These are related concepts but not the former name."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Neonatal mortality is defined as the total number of deaths in children less than how many days of age per 1000 live births in the same year?","options":["Less than 7 days","Less than 28 days","Less than 1 year","Less than 5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neonatal mortality = deaths in children less than 28 days of age per 1000 live births in the same year.  Why A wrong: <7 days is early neonatal mortality.  Why C wrong: <1 year is infant mortality.  Why D wrong: <5 years is under-five mortality."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Vande Mataram Yojana was started on?","options":["9th February 2004","12 April 2005","24 April 1993","1 December 2004"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vande Mataram yojana was started on 9th February 2004.  Why B wrong: 12 April 2005 is NRHM launch.  Why C wrong: 24 April 1993 is the 73rd amendment (village Panchayati Raj).  Why D wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which is described as the most important and universal unit among all social institutions?","options":["Family","Community","Panchayat","School"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Family is the most important and universal unit among all social institutions.  Why B/C/D wrong: Community, panchayat and school are institutions but not the \"most important and universal unit.\""},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The 73rd amendment act (Panchayati Raj at village level) came into effect on?","options":["24 April 1993","1 June 1993","9 February 2004","12 April 2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 73rd amendment act made Panchayati Raj a constitutional right at village level on 24 April 1993.  Why B wrong: 1 June 1993 is the 74th amendment (urban).  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated programme dates."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The 74th amendment act (Panchayati Raj for urban local bodies) came into effect on?","options":["1 June 1993","24 April 1993","1976","1952"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 74th amendment act made it a constitutional right to urban on 1 June 1993.  Why B wrong: 24 April 1993 is the 73rd amendment (village).  Why C/D wrong: Unrelated years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was CGHS (Central Govt Health Scheme) launched in India?","options":["1952","1948","1958","2009"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CGHS was launched in India in 1952 (per source).  Why B wrong: 1948 is ESIC.  Why C wrong: 1958 is National Malaria Eradication Programme.  Why D wrong: 2009 is swine flu pandemic.  [Note: source prints 1952; the widely accepted year CGHS commenced (in Delhi) is 1954. Since 1954 is not an option, answer keyed to source (1952).]"},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The ESIC (Employee State Insurance Corporation) Act was formulated and enforced in which year?","options":["1948","1952","1976","1994"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ESIC act was formulated and enforced in India in 1948.  Why B wrong: 1952 is CGHS.  Why C wrong: 1976 is NPCB / first National Population Policy.  Why D wrong: 1994 is Transplantation of Human Organs Act."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the Swine Flu (H1N1) pandemic declared by WHO?","options":["2009","2014","1948","2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Swine Flu (H1N1) pandemic was declared by WHO in 2009.  Why B wrong: 2014 is Ebola awareness programme / polio-free India.  Why C/D wrong: Unrelated years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year did India start the Ebola Virus awareness programme?","options":["2014","2009","2016","2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ebola Virus awareness programme started in India in 2014.  Why B wrong: 2009 is swine flu pandemic.  Why C wrong: 2016 relates to biomedical waste rules.  Why D wrong: 2005 is NRHM."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the total number of components of communication?","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Total components of communication is five (sender–message–channel(encoding)–receiver(decoding)–feedback).  Why A/B/D wrong: The listed model comprises exactly five components."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Benedict's test is used to detect the presence of what in a urine sample?","options":["Protein","Glucose","Ketones","Blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Benedict's test is used to detect the presence of glucose in a urine sample.  Why A/C/D wrong: Benedict's test is specific for reducing sugars (glucose), not protein/ketones/blood."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"A pregnant woman requires how many additional kilocalories per day?","options":["200 kcal","300 kcal","500 kcal","550 kcal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pregnant women require an additional 300 kcal per day.  Why A/C/D wrong: The recommended additional requirement in pregnancy is 300 kcal/day (550 kcal is for lactation in some texts)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"World Tuberculosis Day is celebrated on?","options":["24 March","1 December","7 April","16 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Tuberculosis Day is celebrated on 24 March.  Why B wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day.  Why C wrong: 7 April is World Health Day.  Why D wrong: 16 May is National Dengue Day."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"World AIDS Day is celebrated on?","options":["1 December","24 March","7 April","17 September"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World AIDS Day is celebrated on 1 December.  Why B wrong: 24 March is World TB Day.  Why C wrong: 7 April is World Health Day.  Why D wrong: 17 September is World Patient Safety Day."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The content of Drug Kit-A supplied at the Sub-Centre includes which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["ORS","Tablet Iron & folic acid","Vitamin A solution","Cotrimoxazole","Paracetamol (PCM)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Drug Kit-A at the Sub-Centre includes ORS, tablet iron & folic acid, vitamin A solution and cotrimoxazole.  Why E wrong: PCM (paracetamol) is NOT supplied in Drug Kit-A at the sub-centre."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"According to the Chadha Committee (1963), one Basic Health Worker was recommended for how much population?","options":["5,000","10,000","20,000","1,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One Basic Health Worker for 10,000 population was recommended by Chadha Committee-1963.  Why A/C/D wrong: The Chadha Committee norm was one worker per 10,000 population."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The Kartar Singh Committee (1973) recommended that ANMs be converted to?","options":["MPW(F) — Multipurpose Worker (Female)","ASHA","Anganwadi worker","LHV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kartar Singh Committee 1973 recommended that ANMs were to be converted to MPW(F).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate cadres, not the Kartar Singh recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Within how many days should a newborn baby be registered in India?","options":["14 days","21 days","28 days","7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The newborn baby should be registered within 21 days in India.  Why A/C/D wrong: The registration window is 21 days."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the drug of choice for Swine Flu, and at what dose?","options":["Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) 75 mg","Tetracycline 500 mg","Streptomycin 1 g","Oseltamivir 150 mg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Drug of choice for Swine Flu is Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) 75 mg.  Why B wrong: Tetracycline is the drug of choice for cholera.  Why C wrong: Streptomycin is for category II TB.  Why D wrong: The stated dose is 75 mg, not 150 mg."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Pleomorphism is a characteristic of rashes seen in which disease?","options":["Chickenpox","Measles","Smallpox","Rubella"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pleomorphism (rashes of different stages together) is a characteristic of chickenpox.  Why B/C/D wrong: Measles, smallpox and rubella do not classically show pleomorphic rash."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which vaccines are given at birth? (Select all that apply)","options":["OPV","BCG","Hep-B","Measles","DPT"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Birth vaccines are OPV, BCG & Hep-B (CRPF 2023).  Why D wrong: Measles is given at 9 months.  Why E wrong: DPT is given at 6 weeks onward, not at birth."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"After birth, the Hepatitis-B vaccine should be given within?","options":["24 hours","48 hours","6 weeks","9 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After birth, Hepatitis-B should be given within 24 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: The birth-dose window is within 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What diluent is used for the BCG vaccine?","options":["Normal saline","Distilled water","Dextrose 5%","Ringer lactate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diluent used for BCG vaccine is normal saline.  Why B wrong: Distilled water is the diluent for measles vaccine.  Why C/D wrong: These are not used as vaccine diluents here."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What diluent is used for the Measles vaccine?","options":["Distilled water","Normal saline","Dextrose","Sterile air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diluent used for measles vaccine is distilled water.  Why B wrong: Normal saline is the BCG diluent.  Why C/D wrong: Not used as measles diluents."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the most common cause of blindness in India?","options":["Cataract (62%)","Glaucoma","Trachoma","Refractive error"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most common cause of blindness in India is cataract (62%).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contribute but cataract at 62% is the leading cause."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the most common side effect of IUDs?","options":["Excessive menstrual bleeding","Amenorrhea","Weight gain","Nausea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Excessive menstrual bleeding is the most common side effect of IUDs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the most common IUD side effect."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The Pearl Index is a measure of what?","options":["Safety of contraceptives","Growth of children","Nutritional status","Water purity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Pearl Index is the measure of the safety (effectiveness/failure rate) of contraceptives.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are measured by other indices/tests."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"At a sub-centre, IUD insertion can be done by?","options":["A trained ANM","Only a gynaecologist","ASHA worker","Anganwadi worker"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At sub centre, IUD's insertion can be done by a trained ANM (Raj. CHO 2023).  Why B/C/D wrong: A trained ANM is authorized at the sub-centre level."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What are the stages of large-scale water purification, in order?","options":["Storage, purification, filtration (last stage)","Filtration, storage, purification","Purification, filtration, storage","Chlorination, boiling, storage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stages of large scale water purification are storage, purification, filtration (filtration being the last stage).  Why B/C/D wrong: The correct order ends with filtration."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Byssinosis is also known as?","options":["Monday Fever","Farmer's lung","Bagassosis","Black lung"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Byssinosis is also known as Monday Fever.  Why B wrong: Farmer's lung is caused by hay dust.  Why C wrong: Bagassosis is caused by sugarcane dust.  Why D wrong: Black lung is coal worker's pneumoconiosis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The floating tip of the iceberg (iceberg concept of disease) represents?","options":["Clinically recognised/diagnosed cases that came into a health care facility","Undiagnosed hidden cases","Deaths","Carriers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The floating tip of the iceberg represents clinically recognised or diagnosed cases (those that came into a health care facility).  Why B wrong: The submerged portion represents undiagnosed/hidden cases.  Why C/D wrong: Deaths and carriers are not the tip."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which is the best measurement of growth in children?","options":["Weight","Height","Mid-arm circumference","Head circumference"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Best measurement of growth is weight (weight is the most sensitive measurement of growth).  Why B/C/D wrong: Height, MUAC and head circumference are useful but weight is the best/most sensitive indicator."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The \"Road to Health\" card (Growth chart) was first designed by?","options":["David Morley","John Snow","Edward Jenner","Louis Pasteur"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Road to health card / growth chart was first designed by David Morley.  Why B/C/D wrong: Snow (epidemiology/cholera), Jenner (smallpox vaccine) and Pasteur (germ theory) did not design the growth chart."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"World Health Day is celebrated every year on?","options":["7 April","24 March","1 December","5 June"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Health Day is celebrated every year on 7 April (WHO's founding date).  Why B wrong: 24 March is World TB Day.  Why C wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day.  Why D wrong: 5 June is World Environment Day."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Mid Arm Circumference (MUAC) is used for assessment of malnutrition in which community age group?","options":["1 to 5 years","0 to 1 year","6 to 12 years","15 to 45 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MUAC is used for assessment of malnutrition in children aged 1 to 5 years (it is constant in this age group).  Why B/C/D wrong: MUAC assessment applies to the 1–5 year group."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"How many curves are there in the growth chart used in India?","options":["1 curve","2 curves","3 curves","4 curves"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: There are 3 curves in the growth chart in India.  Why A/B/D wrong: The Indian growth chart uses 3 curves."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which of the following are principles of Primary Health Care? (Select all that apply)","options":["Intersectoral coordination","Appropriate technology","Equitable distribution","Community participation","Profit maximization"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Principles of PHC include intersectoral coordination, appropriate technology, equitable distribution and community participation.  Why E wrong: Profit maximization is not a PHC principle."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Primary health care is described as?","options":["Health For All","Hospital-based care","Tertiary specialist care","Curative-only care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary health care is \"the Health For All\".  Why B/C/D wrong: PHC emphasizes universal preventive/promotive care, not hospital/tertiary/curative-only care."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"At which level do ASHAs work?","options":["Village level","Block level","District level","State level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASHAs are working at the village level.  Why B/C/D wrong: ASHA is a village-level community health worker."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The focus of secondary prevention is?","options":["Early diagnosis and treatment","Health promotion","Rehabilitation","Immunization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The focus of secondary prevention is early diagnosis and treatment.  Why B/D wrong: Health promotion and immunization are primary prevention.  Why C wrong: Rehabilitation is tertiary prevention."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Immunization is an example of which level of prevention?","options":["Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Primordial prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Immunization is primary prevention.  Why B wrong: Secondary is early diagnosis and treatment.  Why C wrong: Tertiary is rehabilitation.  Why D wrong: Primordial addresses risk factor emergence, not specific immunization."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The submerged portion of the iceberg (iceberg phenomenon) represents?","options":["Undiagnosed or hidden cases","Diagnosed cases","Recovered cases","Immune population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The submerged portion of the iceberg represents undiagnosed or hidden cases.  Why B wrong: Diagnosed cases are the floating tip.  Why C/D wrong: Not represented by the iceberg model."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The constant presence of a disease or infection within a geographic area is called?","options":["Endemic","Epidemic","Pandemic","Sporadic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Constant presence of a disease/infection within a geographic area is called endemic.  Why B wrong: Epidemic is a sudden rise within a community.  Why C wrong: Pandemic is worldwide/country-to-country.  Why D wrong: Sporadic means scattered irregular cases."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"According to the source, the constant presence of a disease or infection within a community area is called?","options":["Epidemic","Endemic","Pandemic","Sporadic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the constant presence within a community area is called epidemic.  Why B wrong: Endemic refers to a geographic area.  Why C wrong: Pandemic is worldwide.  Why D wrong: Sporadic is scattered cases.  [Note: source prints \"constant presence...is called Epidemic\"; the standard epidemiological definition (WHO/Park) of an epidemic is the occurrence of cases clearly in EXCESS of normal expectancy — not \"constant presence\" (which describes endemicity). Answer keyed to source wording.]"},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The constant presence of a disease or infection worldwide (country to country) is called?","options":["Pandemic","Endemic","Epidemic","Sporadic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The constant presence of a disease worldwide or country to country is called pandemic.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe geographic, community, or scattered occurrence respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The number of new cases occurring in a defined population during a specified period is known as?","options":["Incidence","Prevalence","Attack rate","Mortality rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The number of new cases in a defined population during a specified period is incidence.  Why B wrong: Prevalence counts all (old and new) cases.  Why C/D wrong: Attack rate and mortality rate measure different phenomena."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"All cases (old and new cases together) in a population is called?","options":["Prevalence","Incidence","Cumulative incidence","Attack rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All cases (old and new) is called prevalence.  Why B/C/D wrong: These count only new cases or attack proportions."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the commonest cause of maternal mortality in India?","options":["Haemorrhage","Sepsis","Eclampsia","Obstructed labour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The commonest cause of maternal mortality in India is haemorrhage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are causes but haemorrhage is the leading one."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the denominator used in the Crude Birth Rate?","options":["Mid-year population","Total live births","Number of women 15–45 years","Number of households"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Denominator in Crude Birth Rate is mid-year population.  Why B/C/D wrong: The CBR denominator is the mid-year population (per 1000)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the most common cause of infant mortality in India?","options":["Low birth weight (57%)","Respiratory infection (17%)","Congenital malformations (5%)","Diarrhoeal diseases (4%)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most common cause of infant mortality in India is low birth weight (57%).  Why B/C/D wrong: These follow — respiratory infection 17%, congenital malformations 5%, diarrhoeal diseases 4% — but LBW is the leading cause."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"BCG vaccine is what type of vaccine?","options":["Live attenuated vaccine","Killed vaccine","Toxoid","Subunit vaccine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BCG is a live attenuated vaccine.  Why B/C/D wrong: BCG is a live attenuated (not killed/toxoid/subunit) vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"BCG leaves a permanent mark at which site?","options":["Vaccine administration site","Gluteal region","Sole of foot","Anterior chest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BCG leaves a permanent scar at the vaccine administration site (left upper arm).  Why B/C/D wrong: The scar is at the site of administration."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which vaccine is most sensitive to temperature?","options":["Polio vaccine (OPV)","BCG","DPT","Hep-B"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most temperature-sensitive vaccine is the polio vaccine (OPV).  Why B/C/D wrong: OPV is the most heat-sensitive vaccine in the cold chain."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which adjuvants are used in the DPT vaccine? (Select all that apply)","options":["Aluminium phosphate","Aluminium hydroxide","Normal saline","Distilled water"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The adjuvants used in DPT vaccine are aluminium phosphate and aluminium hydroxide.  Why C/D wrong: Normal saline and distilled water are diluents, not adjuvants."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the most common site for IM injection in an infant?","options":["Mediolateral anterior thigh (vastus lateralis muscle)","Deltoid","Gluteal (dorsogluteal)","Ventrogluteal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most common site for IM injection in an infant is the mediolateral anterior thigh in the vastus lateralis muscle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sites are used in older children/adults, not the preferred infant site."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Scabies is best described as?","options":["A contagious disease","A non-communicable disease","A vector-borne disease","A genetic disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Scabies is a contagious disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: Scabies spreads by direct contact; it is contagious."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the maximum size of droplet nuclei that can be inhaled into the alveoli of the lungs?","options":["1–5 microns","10–20 microns","50 microns","100 microns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The maximum size of droplet nuclei that can be inhaled into alveoli is 1–5 microns.  Why B/C/D wrong: Larger particles are filtered in the upper airway; only 1–5 micron nuclei reach alveoli."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Negri bodies are found in the brain in which disease?","options":["Rabies (Hydrophobia)","Tetanus","Meningitis","Polio"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Negri bodies are found in the brain in rabies (hydrophobia).  Why B/C/D wrong: Negri bodies are pathognomonic of rabies, not these diseases."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative organism of rabies?","options":["Lyssavirus type-1","Retrovirus","Flavivirus","Nairovirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The causative organism of rabies is Lyssavirus type-1.  Why B wrong: Retrovirus causes AIDS.  Why C wrong: Flavivirus causes Zika.  Why D wrong: Nairovirus causes Crimean-Congo fever."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative organism of AIDS?","options":["Retrovirus","Lyssavirus","Paramyxovirus","Flavivirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The causative organism of AIDS is a retrovirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause rabies, Nipah/measles, and Zika respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which staining technique is used for the diagnosis of TB?","options":["Ziehl-Neelsen stain technique","Gram stain","Giemsa stain","India ink"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ziehl-Neelsen stain is used for the diagnosis of TB (acid-fast bacilli).  Why B/C/D wrong: Gram, Giemsa and India ink are for other organisms."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Schick's test is used for which disease?","options":["Diphtheria","Tuberculosis","Scarlet fever","Pinworm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schick's test is used for diphtheria.  Why B wrong: TB uses the Mantoux/tuberculin test.  Why C/D wrong: Not diagnosed by Schick's test."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which disease is also known as the \"Barometer of social welfare\"?","options":["Tuberculosis (TB)","Cholera","Leprosy","Plague"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tuberculosis (TB) is known as the Barometer of social welfare.  Why B wrong: Cholera relates to \"Father of Public Health.\"  Why C wrong: Leprosy = Hansen's disease.  Why D wrong: Plague = Black Death."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The complications of chickenpox are remembered by the trick \"RAPE.\" Which of the following are included? (Select all that apply)","options":["Rey's Syndrome","Acute Cerebellar Ataxia","Pneumonia","Encephalitis","Renal failure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: RAPE = Rey's Syndrome, Acute Cerebellar Ataxia, Pneumonia, Encephalitis.  Why E wrong: Renal failure is not part of the RAPE mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the drug of choice for cholera?","options":["Tetracycline","Oseltamivir","Streptomycin","Metronidazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Drug of choice for cholera is tetracycline.  Why B wrong: Oseltamivir is for swine flu.  Why C wrong: Streptomycin is for TB category II.  Why D wrong: Metronidazole treats amoebiasis/giardiasis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which disease is also known as \"Fifth Disease\"?","options":["Erythema infectiosum","Measles","Scarlet fever","Rubella"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fifth Disease is erythema infectiosum.  Why B wrong: Measles = first disease.  Why C wrong: Scarlet fever = second disease.  Why D wrong: German measles (rubella) = third disease."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Haemophilus influenzae is a gram-negative bacterium that is a member of which family?","options":["Pasteurellaceae family","Enterobacteriaceae family","Vibrionaceae family","Micrococcaceae family"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Haemophilus influenzae is a gram negative bacterium, a member of the Pasteurellaceae family.  Why B/C/D wrong: It belongs to Pasteurellaceae, not these families."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"National Dengue Day is celebrated on?","options":["16 May","24 March","1 December","7 April"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: National Dengue Day is celebrated on 16 May.  Why B wrong: 24 March is World TB Day.  Why C wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day.  Why D wrong: 7 April is World Health Day."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which occupational disease is caused by cotton dust?","options":["Byssinosis","Bagassosis","Farmer's lung","Metal fume fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Byssinosis is caused by cotton dust.  Why B wrong: Bagassosis is caused by sugarcane dust.  Why C wrong: Farmer's lung is caused by hay dust.  Why D wrong: Metal fume fever is caused by metal oxides."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which occupational disease is caused by sugarcane dust?","options":["Bagassosis","Byssinosis","Silicosis","Anthracosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bagassosis is caused by sugar cane dust.  Why B wrong: Byssinosis is from cotton dust.  Why C wrong: Silicosis is from silica dust.  Why D wrong: Anthracosis is from coal dust."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Metal Fume Fever, also known as \"Monday Morning Fever,\" is caused by exposure to which chemicals? (Select all that apply)","options":["Zinc oxide","Magnesium oxide","Aluminium oxide","Silica"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Metal Fume Fever is caused by exposure to zinc oxide, magnesium oxide and aluminium oxide.  Why D wrong: Silica causes silicosis, not metal fume fever."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the highest source of Vitamin-A?","options":["Cod Liver Oil","Carrot","Milk","Spinach"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The highest source of Vitamin-A is cod liver oil.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contain vitamin A/carotene but cod liver oil is the richest source."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Match the tinea (ringworm) infection to its site: Tinea Capitis affects the?","options":["Head","Groin","Foot","Beard"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea Capitis is fungal infection of the head.  Why B wrong: Groin is Tinea Cruris.  Why C wrong: Foot is Tinea Pedis.  Why D wrong: Beard is Tinea Barbae."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Tinea Cruris (also called \"Jock itch\") is a fungal infection of which area?","options":["Groin area","Whole body","Hands","Foot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea Cruris is fungal infection of the groin area, also called Jock itch.  Why B wrong: Whole body is Tinea Corporis.  Why C wrong: Hands is Tinea Manuum.  Why D wrong: Foot is Tinea Pedis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Tinea Corporis is a fungal infection of the?","options":["Whole body","Head","Beard","Groin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea Corporis is fungal infection of the whole body.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to capitis (head), barbae (beard) and cruris (groin)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Tinea Pedis (\"Athlete's foot\") is a fungal infection of the?","options":["Foot","Hands","Head","Beard"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea Pedis is fungal infection of the foot, called \"Athlete's foot.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to manuum (hands), capitis (head) and barbae (beard)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Tinea Barbae is a fungal infection of the?","options":["Beard","Foot","Groin","Hands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea Barbae is fungal infection of the beard.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to pedis (foot), cruris (groin) and manuum (hands)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Tinea Manuum is a fungal infection of the?","options":["Hands","Head","Whole body","Foot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea Manuum is fungal infection of the hands.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to capitis (head), corporis (whole body) and pedis (foot)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Farmer's lung disease is caused by hay dust and is also known as?","options":["Extrinsic allergic alveolitis / hypersensitivity alveolitis","Byssinosis","Bagassosis","Silicosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Farmer's lung disease (from hay dust) is also known as extrinsic allergic alveolitis or hypersensitivity alveolitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate occupational lung diseases."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which state was the \"Beti-Bachao Beti-Padao\" programme launched first?","options":["Haryana","Rajasthan","Punjab","Gujarat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Beti-Bachao Beti-Padao\" programme was launched first in Haryana.  Why B/C/D wrong: The programme was first launched in Haryana."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which day is celebrated as World Environment Day?","options":["5 June","21 June","14 November","1 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Environment Day is celebrated on 5 June.  Why B wrong: 21 June is Yoga Day.  Why C wrong: 14 November is Diabetes Day.  Why D wrong: 1 May is Labour Day."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which day is celebrated as International Yoga Day?","options":["21 June","5 June","14 November","1 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Yoga Day is celebrated on 21 June.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are Environment Day, Diabetes Day and Labour Day respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which day is celebrated as World Diabetes Day?","options":["14 November","21 June","5 June","1 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetes Day is celebrated on 14 November.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are Yoga Day, Environment Day and Labour Day respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which day is celebrated as Labour Day?","options":["1 May","14 November","21 June","5 June"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Labour Day is celebrated on 1 May.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are Diabetes Day, Yoga Day and Environment Day respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the main objective of Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY)?","options":["Institutional delivery","Immunization","Family planning","Nutrition supplementation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The main objective of JSY is institutional delivery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are addressed by other programmes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the Baby Friendly Hospital Initiative started in India?","options":["1993","1976","2005","1994"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Baby Friendly Hospital Initiative was started in India in 1993.  Why B/C/D wrong: Unrelated years for this initiative."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"An \"eligible couple\" refers to which age group?","options":["15 to 45 age group (reproductive age group)","18 to 40 age group","20 to 50 age group","15 to 49 age group"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Eligible couple means 15 to 45 age group or reproductive age group (source states wife 15–45 years).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source defines it as 15–45 years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which Indian state has the highest IMR (Infant Mortality Rate)?","options":["Madhya Pradesh (MP)","Kerala","Tamil Nadu","Punjab"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Highest IMR is in Madhya Pradesh (MP).  Why B/C/D wrong: These states have relatively lower IMR."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The \"3 D's\" sign of Pellagra (deficiency of Vitamin B3/Niacin) includes which three features? (Select all that apply)","options":["Diarrhoea","Dermatitis","Dementia","Deafness"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: 3 D's of Pellagra = Diarrhoea, Dermatitis, Dementia (deficiency of Vitamin B3/Niacin).  Why D wrong: Deafness is not part of the 3 D's."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Fresh bleaching powder contains approximately what percentage of chlorine?","options":["33% chlorine","10% chlorine","50% chlorine","5% chlorine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fresh bleaching powder contains 33% chlorine.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated available chlorine is 33%."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the best method for disposal of refuse where land is available?","options":["Controlled tipping","Incineration","Burial","Composting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Best method for disposal of refuse where land is available is controlled tipping.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are alternatives but controlled tipping is best when land is available."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which method of waste disposal is used in small camps?","options":["Burial (to be burn)","Controlled tipping","Composting","Manure pits"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Method of waste disposal in small camps is burial (to be burn).  Why B/C/D wrong: Controlled tipping/composting/manure pits are used in other settings."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The Scotch tape swab test is used for which disease?","options":["Pinworm","Tapeworm","Hookworm","Roundworm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Scotch tape swab test is used for pinworm (Enterobius).  Why B/C/D wrong: The test is specific for pinworm perianal egg detection."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In 1993 the WHO declared TB a global emergency and advised which treatment strategy?","options":["Directly Observed Treatment Short course (DOTS)","Long-course chemotherapy","Intermittent therapy only","Surgical resection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 1993 WHO declared TB a global emergency and advised DOTS (Directly Observed Treatment Short course).  Why B/C/D wrong: WHO recommended DOTS for all countries."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was DOTS officially launched as the RNTCP strategy (Revised National TB Control Programme)?","options":["1997","1993","2005","2017"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOTS was officially launched as the RNTCP strategy in 1997 (Govt of India revitalized NTP as RNTCP in 1993).  Why B wrong: 1993 is when WHO declared TB a global emergency / RNTCP revitalization began.  Why C/D wrong: 2005 (NRHM), 2017 (NSP for TB elimination) are different."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The National Strategic Plan (NSP) for tuberculosis elimination (2017–2025) has which slogan?","options":["Detect, Treat, Prevent, Build","Test, Treat, Track","Reach the Unreached","End TB Now"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NSP for TB elimination (2017–2025) slogan is \"Detect, Treat, Prevent, Build\".  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated NSP slogan."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the Bio-medical Waste Management (amendment) act passed in India?","options":["2016","1998","2009","2022"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bio-medical waste management amendment act was passed in India in 2016.  Why B/C/D wrong: 2016 is the amendment year referenced."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The NAREGA programme was renamed in 2009 as?","options":["MNAREGA (MGNREGA)","NRHM","Swachh Bharat","RCH"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NAREGA programme was renamed in 2009 as MNAREGA (Mahatma Gandhi NREGA).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated programmes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the health ministry established in India?","options":["1947","1948","1952","1950"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Establishment of health ministry in India was in 1947.  Why B/C/D wrong: 1947 is the year of establishment."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which period was the National Surveillance Programme for communicable diseases started?","options":["1997–98","1987","2005–06","1974"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: National Surveillance Programme for communicable diseases was started in 1997-98.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"How often should infant weight be measured during the first year of life?","options":["Once every month","Once every 2 months","Once every 3 months","Once every 6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Infant weight should be measured once every month during the first year.  Why B/C/D wrong: Monthly weighing is recommended in the first year; every 2 months applies to the second year."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The \"Weight for age\" growth chart is also known as?","options":["Mother and Child Protection Card","Road to Health card","Immunization card","ANC card"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Weight for age\" growth chart is also known as the \"Mother and Child Protection Card\".  Why B/C/D wrong: These are related but the weight-for-age chart is the MCP card."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"During the second year of life, how frequently should a child be weighed?","options":["Every 2 months","Every month","Every 3 months","Every 6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The child should be weighed every 2 months during the second year (and every 3 months thereafter up to age 5–6 years).  Why B wrong: Monthly weighing is for the first year.  Why C wrong: Every 3 months is used from year 3 up to 5–6 years.  Why D wrong: Every 6 months is not stated for the second year."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Village-level health workers include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dai / TBA (Traditional Birth Attendant)","ASHA","Village health guides","District Medical Officer"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Village level health workers include Dai, TBA (Traditional Birth Attendant), ASHA and village health guides.  Why D wrong: The District Medical Officer is not a village-level worker."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Gram Sabha is the basic unit of which system?","options":["Panchayati Raj","ICDS","NRHM","ESIC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gram Sabha is the basic unit of Panchayati Raj.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate systems/schemes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"According to WHO, what is the nurse–population ratio in India?","options":["2.1 nurses per 1000 population","0.62 per 1000 population","0.5 per 1000 population","1 per 1000 population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: According to WHO, nurse population ratio in India is 2.1 nurses per 1000 population; India needs 20.4 lakh registered nurses.  Why B wrong: 0.62 per 1000 is the doctor–population ratio.  Why C wrong: 0.5 per 1000 is the bed–population ratio.  Why D wrong: Not the stated ratio."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"According to WHO, what is the doctor–population ratio in India?","options":["0.62 per 1000 population","2.1 per 1000 population","0.5 per 1000 population","9.26 per 1000 population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: According to WHO, doctor–population ratio is 0.62 per 1000 population; India needs 9.26 lakh doctors.  Why B wrong: 2.1 per 1000 is the nurse ratio.  Why C wrong: 0.5 per 1000 is the bed ratio.  Why D wrong: 9.26 lakh is the shortfall number, not a ratio."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"According to WHO, what is the bed–population ratio in India?","options":["0.5 bed per 1000 population","2.1 beds per 1000 population","0.62 bed per 1000 population","1 bed per 1000 population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: According to WHO, in India the bed–population ratio is 0.5 bed per 1000 population.  Why B wrong: 2.1 is nurse ratio.  Why C wrong: 0.62 is doctor ratio.  Why D wrong: Not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which six Indian states have more doctors than the WHO guideline? (Select all that apply)","options":["Delhi","Kerala","Tamil Nadu","Punjab and Karnataka","Bihar"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The states with more doctors than the WHO guideline are Delhi, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Punjab and Karnataka.  Why E wrong: Bihar is not listed among these states.  [Note: source text says \"Six state of India\" but names only five (Delhi, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Punjab, Karnataka); the sixth is not specified in the source. Answer keyed to the five named states.]"},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"ASHA (Accredited Social Health Activist) works under the NRHM programme, which was launched in India on?","options":["12 April 2005","9 February 2004","2 October 2014","1952"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASHA works under NRHM, launched in India on 12 April 2005.  Why B wrong: 9 February 2004 is Vande Mataram yojana.  Why C wrong: 2 October 2014 relates to Swachh Bharat.  Why D wrong: 1952 is CGHS."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What does the abbreviation HALE stand for?","options":["Health Adjusted Life Expectancy","Health Assessment Life Estimate","Healthy Ageing Life Expectancy","Health Assurance Life Enrollment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HALE = Health Adjusted Life Expectancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What does the abbreviation DALE stand for?","options":["Disability Adjusted Life Expectancy","Disease Adjusted Life Estimate","Disability Assessment Life Enrollment","Death Adjusted Life Expectancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DALE = Disability Adjusted Life Expectancy (MP CHO 2021).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Under the ESI scheme, what is the salary limit for ESIC deduction per month?","options":["Rs. 21,000 per month","Rs. 15,000 per month","Rs. 25,000 per month","Rs. 10,000 per month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The salary limit for ESIC deduction is 21,000 per month.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated wage ceiling is Rs. 21,000/month."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Under the ESI scheme, the employer contribution and employee contribution respectively are?","options":["Employer 3.25%, Employee 0.75%","Employer 0.75%, Employee 3.25%","Employer 4%, Employee 4%","Employer 1%, Employee 3%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Employer contribution is 3.25% and employee contribution is 0.75% (total 4%).  Why B wrong: The figures are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated split (though total is 4%)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Under the ESI scheme, the state and central government share medical expenditure in what ratio?","options":["1:7","7:1","1:1","3:1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: State and central govt share medical expenditure in the ratio of 1:7.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated ratio is 1:7."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Under the ESI scheme, what percentage are the medical benefits, and for how many days are sickness benefits payable?","options":["Medical 100%; sickness benefit 70% of average daily wages, payable for 91 days","Medical 70%; sickness benefit 100%, payable for 60 days","Medical 50%; sickness benefit 50%, payable for 90 days","Medical 100%; sickness benefit 50%, payable for 180 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Medical benefits are 100%; sickness benefits are 70% of average daily wages, payable for 91 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the ESI benefit figures."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Discarded medicines should be disposed of in which colour bag (new guidelines-2016)?","options":["Yellow bag","Red bag","Blue bag","Black bag"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Discarded medicines should be disposed in a yellow bag (new guidelines-2016).  Why B/C/D wrong: The correct colour under 2016 guidelines is yellow."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year and month was \"PM TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyan\" launched in India?","options":["9 September 2022","12 April 2005","24 March 2018","1 December 2020"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In India, PM TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyan was launched on 9 September 2022.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated dates."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Expired cytotoxic medicines should be disposed of in which colour bag (new guidelines-2016)?","options":["Yellow bag","Red bag","Blue bag","White bag"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Expired cytotoxic medicines should be disposed in a yellow bag (new guidelines-2016).  Why B/C/D wrong: The correct colour is yellow."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"How should placenta be disposed of at a PHC?","options":["Treat with bleaching powder and burial","Incineration","Deep freezing","Autoclaving only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Disposal of placenta at PHC is to treat with bleaching powder and burial.  Why B/C/D wrong: The recommended method is bleaching powder treatment plus burial."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the disposal mechanism for expired medicines?","options":["Incineration","Burial","Composting","Recycling"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Disposal mechanism for expired medicines is incineration.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incineration is the specified method."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Human and animal anatomical wastes should be disposed of in which colour bag?","options":["Yellow colour bag","Red colour bag","Blue colour bag","Black colour bag"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Human & animal anatomical wastes should be disposed in a yellow colour bag.  Why B/C/D wrong: Anatomical waste goes into the yellow category."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the legal cutoff age for employment in India according to the Factory Act-1948?","options":["14 years","15 years","18 years","12 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Legal cutoff age for employment in India is 14 years according to the Factory Act-1948 (MP CHO Dec 2020).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated minimum is 14 years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The 3-Tier Panchayati Raj system was proposed by which committee?","options":["Balwant Rai Mehta committee-1957","Kartar Singh committee","Chadha committee","Shrivastav committee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 3-Tier Panchayati Raj system was proposed by Balwant Rai Mehta committee-1957.  Why B/C/D wrong: These committees dealt with health workers/manpower, not the Panchayati Raj tiers."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Where is the Eligible Couple register maintained?","options":["Sub-Centre","PHC","District hospital","Anganwadi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Eligible couple register is maintained at Sub-Centres (MPNHM 2019).  Why B/C/D wrong: The register is maintained at the sub-centre."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment (NPCB&VI) launched?","options":["1976","1983","1994","1952"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NPCB&VI was launched in the year 1976.  Why B wrong: 1983 is First National Health Policy.  Why C wrong: 1994 is Transplantation of Human Organs Act.  Why D wrong: 1952 is CGHS."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) has its headquarters where and was established in which year?","options":["Headquarter New York, USA; established 1965","Headquarter Geneva; established 1948","Headquarter New Delhi; established 1976","Headquarter Paris; established 1965"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: UNDP headquarter is New York, USA, established in 1965.  Why B/C/D wrong: The HQ is New York and it was established in 1965."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The WHO Growth Chart-2006 is used for which age group?","options":["Infant and toddler up to 2 years","2–19 years","5–10 years","Birth to 5 years only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO growth chart-2006 is used for infant and toddler age up to 2 years, reflecting an international standard developed in 2006.  Why B wrong: 2–19 years uses the CDC growth chart-2000.  Why C/D wrong: These ranges are incorrect for the WHO chart."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The CDC Growth Chart-2000 is used for which age group?","options":["2–19 years old","Up to 2 years","Birth to 1 year","5–10 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CDC Growth chart-2000 is used if the child is 2-19 years old (developed by CDC in 2000).  Why B wrong: Up to 2 years uses the WHO chart-2006.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect ranges."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the Transplantation of Human Organs Act passed?","options":["1994","1976","1948","2012"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Transplantation of Human Organs Act was passed in 1994.  Why B/C/D wrong: Unrelated years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the full form of NOTTO, and where is its headquarters?","options":["National Organ and Tissue Transplant Organisation; HQ New Delhi","National Organ Transplant Trust Office; HQ Mumbai","National Organ and Tissue Testing Organisation; HQ Chennai","National Oncology and Tissue Transplant Organisation; HQ Kolkata"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NOTTO = National Organ and Tissue Transplant Organisation; HQ New Delhi.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions/locations."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Nirmal Bharat Abhiyan (launched 2012) was earlier known as Total Sanitation Campaign (TSC) and is also known as?","options":["Swachh Bharat Abhiyan","Clean India Movement Act","Swajaldhara","NRHM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nirmal Bharat Abhiyan (2012, earlier Total Sanitation Campaign) is also known as Swachh Bharat Abhiyan.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different programmes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"When was the Epidemic Disease Act enforced in India?","options":["1897","1948","1954","2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Epidemic Disease Act was enforced in India in 1897.  Why B/C/D wrong: 1897 is the year it was enforced."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In India, the Epidemic Disease Act came into force due to which pandemic disease?","options":["Black death (Bubonic Plague)","Cholera","Smallpox","Influenza"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In India, the Epidemic disease act came into force due to Black death (Bubonic Plague).  Why B/C/D wrong: The trigger was the bubonic plague."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"World Patient Safety Day is celebrated every year on?","options":["17 September","7 April","16 May","1 December"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Patient Safety Day is celebrated on 17 September.  Why B wrong: 7 April is World Health Day.  Why C wrong: 16 May is National Dengue Day.  Why D wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Who is considered the \"Father of Medicine\" in India?","options":["Acharya Charak (Charaka Samhita)","Sushruta","Hippocrates","David Morley"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Father of medicine in India is Acharya Charak (Charaka Samhita).  Why B wrong: Sushruta is father of surgery in India.  Why C wrong: Hippocrates is the father of (Western) medicine.  Why D wrong: David Morley designed the growth chart."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Who is considered the \"Father of Surgery\" in India?","options":["Sushruta (Sushruta Samhita)","Acharya Charak","Hippocrates","Edward Jenner"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Father of surgery in India is Sushruta (Sushruta Samhita).  Why B wrong: Acharya Charak is father of medicine in India.  Why C/D wrong: Not the father of surgery in India."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The SAANS initiative (Social Awareness and Actions to Neutralize Pneumonia Successfully) was implemented since which year, for reduction of childhood morbidity and mortality due to what?","options":["2019; Pneumonia","2005; Diarrhoea","2014; Tuberculosis","2012; Measles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SAANS was implemented since 2019 for reduction of childhood morbidity and mortality due to pneumonia.  Why B/C/D wrong: SAANS targets pneumonia, launched 2019."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"A man comes for checkup after his father had a cerebrovascular accident due to hypertension. What type of prevention is this?","options":["Secondary prevention","Primary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Primordial prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Screening/checkup because of a family risk (early detection) is secondary prevention.  Why B wrong: Primary prevention prevents disease onset (e.g., immunization).  Why C wrong: Tertiary is rehabilitation after established disease.  Why D wrong: Primordial prevents risk-factor development in populations."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"On which date is the mid-year population taken?","options":["01 July","01 January","31 December","15 August"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mid year population is taken on 01 July.  Why B/C/D wrong: The mid-year point of the calendar year is 1 July."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the WHO definition of blindness (vision in the better eye)?","options":["<3/60 in the better eye","<6/60 in the better eye","<6/18 in the better eye","<1/60 in the better eye"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO definition of blindness is <3/60 in the better eye.  Why B/C/D wrong: The WHO cut-off cited is <3/60."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"A child reported to a PHC with runny nose and fever for 3 days, and a rash that started on the face then covered the trunk. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Measles","Chickenpox","Rubella","Scabies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fever, coryza and a rash spreading from face to trunk is most likely measles.  Why B wrong: Chickenpox rash is centripetal and pleomorphic.  Why C wrong: Rubella is milder; measles fits the classic craniocaudal spread.  Why D wrong: Scabies causes itchy burrows, not a febrile facial rash."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"A country suspects a severe disease. Within what time should it report to WHO?","options":["24 hours","48 hours","7 days","12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A probable severe disease should be reported to WHO within 24 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated reporting window is 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which food has the least iron content?","options":["Milk","Green leafy vegetables","Liver","Jaggery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Least iron content is present in milk.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are relatively richer iron sources."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"A woman who is lactating comes to OPD 6 weeks after delivery for contraceptive advice. Which of the following is NOT advised?","options":["Combined OCP","Progesterone-only pill","IUD","Barrier method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Combined OCP (with estrogen) is not advised in lactating women as it reduces milk supply.  Why B/C/D wrong: Progesterone-only pills, IUDs and barrier methods are suitable during lactation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"A 22-year-old low-socioeconomic female worker presents within 8 hours of sexual assault on the 13th day of her cycle and asks for emergency contraception. What is the advice?","options":["LNG 1.5 mg (Levonorgestrel)","Combined OCP daily","IUD immediately only","No action needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Emergency contraception advice is LNG 1.5 mg (levonorgestrel).  Why B wrong: Daily combined OCP is not emergency contraception.  Why C wrong: IUD is an option but the source answer is LNG 1.5 mg.  Why D wrong: On the 13th day (fertile period) action is required."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the best high-level disinfectant?","options":["Glutaraldehyde","Alcohol","Chlorine","Sunlight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Best high-level disinfectant is glutaraldehyde.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are lower-level disinfectants."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which diseases are included in the NPCDCS program? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cardiovascular disease (CVD)","Cancer","Diabetes Mellitus (DM)","Stroke","Tuberculosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: NPCDCS covers CVD, Cancer, Diabetes and Stroke.  Why E wrong: Tuberculosis is a communicable disease under RNTCP/NTEP, not NPCDCS."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Health check-up in a village school is the responsibility of which facility?","options":["PHC (Primary Health Centre)","Sub-centre","District hospital","Medical college"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Health check-up in village school is the responsibility of the PHC.  Why B/C/D wrong: The PHC is responsible for school health check-ups."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Under the Ayushman Bharat Scheme, how much money can a family get per year as insurance?","options":["Rs. 5 lakh","Rs. 1 lakh","Rs. 10 lakh","Rs. 2 lakh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under Ayushman Bharat Scheme, a family can get Rs. 5 lakh per year as insurance.  Why B/C/D wrong: The insurance cover is Rs. 5 lakh per family per year."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the most cost-effective method for screening of cervical cancer?","options":["Visual inspection with 5% acetic acid (VIA)","Pap smear","HPV DNA test","Colposcopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most cost-effective method for cervical cancer screening is Visual Inspection with 5% acetic acid (VIA).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are effective but not the most cost-effective for community screening."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"A Health Worker (female) goes out in public once every 15 days for malarial surveillance. This is which type of surveillance?","options":["Active surveillance","Passive surveillance","Sentinel surveillance","Sero-surveillance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Going out to actively seek/collect cases is active surveillance.  Why B wrong: Passive surveillance relies on reported cases coming in.  Why C/D wrong: Sentinel and sero-surveillance are other specific types."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The average number of children born to a woman over her reproductive lifespan refers to?","options":["Total fertility rate (TFR)","Crude birth rate","General fertility rate","Gross reproduction rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This defines the Total Fertility Rate (TFR).  Why B/C/D wrong: CBR uses mid-year population; GFR/GRR are different measures."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which vitamin increases iron absorption?","options":["Vitamin C","Vitamin A","Vitamin D","Vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin C increases iron absorption.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not enhance iron absorption like vitamin C does."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the older name of Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY)?","options":["National Maternity Benefit Scheme","Vande Mataram Yojana","Reproductive and Child Health Programme","Balwadi Nutrition Programme"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Older name of Janani Suraksha Yojana is National Maternity Benefit Scheme.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate programmes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In IMNCI colour coding, the pink colour indicates?","options":["Immediate referral","Home-based management","Treatment at health facility","No action"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pink colour in IMNCI indicates immediate referral.  Why B wrong: Green indicates home-based management.  Why C/D wrong: Not the meaning of pink coding."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In IMNCI colour coding, the green colour represents?","options":["Home-based management","Immediate referral","Outpatient antibiotic treatment","Emergency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Green colour in IMNCI represents home-based management (patient treated at home).  Why B wrong: Pink indicates immediate referral.  Why C/D wrong: Not the meaning of green coding."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"According to IMNCI, fast breathing in a 5-month-old child is defined as?","options":[">50 per minute",">60 per minute",">40 per minute",">30 per minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: According to IMNCI, fast breathing in a child aged 2–12 months (e.g., 5 months) is >50 per minute.  Why B wrong: >60/min applies to infants <2 months.  Why C wrong: >40/min applies to children 12 months–5 years.  Why D wrong: Not an IMNCI threshold here."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"How is an \"eligible couple\" defined (per source page 925)?","options":["Currently married couple, wife 15–45 years age","Any couple 18–40 years","Married couple, husband 15–45 years","Cohabiting couple 20–50 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Eligible couple is defined as a currently married couple, wife 15–45 years age.  Why B/C/D wrong: The definition specifies wife's age 15–45 years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The Midday Meal Programme provides what proportion of calories and proteins?","options":["1/3 calories and 1/2 proteins","1/2 calories and 1/3 proteins","Full calories and proteins","1/4 calories and 1/4 proteins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Midday meal program provides 1/3 calories and 1/2 proteins.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated proportions are 1/3 calories and 1/2 proteins."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the First National Population Policy formulated?","options":["1976","1983","2000","1952"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: First National Population Policy was formulated in 1976.  Why B wrong: 1983 is First National Health Policy.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year was the First National Health Policy formulated?","options":["1983","1976","2002","1948"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: First National Health Policy was formulated in 1983.  Why B wrong: 1976 is First National Population Policy.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Exclusive breastfeeding is recommended till what age?","options":["6 months","3 months","1 year","2 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Exclusive breast feeding is recommended till 6 months age.  Why B/C/D wrong: The recommended duration of exclusive breastfeeding is 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the most abundant immunoglobulin (Ig) in breast milk?","options":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgE"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most abundant Ig in breast milk is IgA (secretory IgA).  Why B/C/D wrong: IgA predominates in breast milk."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"When is World Breastfeeding Week celebrated?","options":["1–7 August","1–7 April","1–7 December","16–22 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World breastfeeding week is celebrated 1–7 August.  Why B/C/D wrong: The correct dates are 1–7 August."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Low birth weight is defined as birth weight of?","options":["Less than 2.5 kg","Less than 2.0 kg","Less than 1.5 kg","Less than 1.0 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low Birth Weight is birth weight less than 2.5 kg.  Why B/C wrong: <1.5 kg is very low birth weight.  Why D wrong: <1.0 kg is extremely low birth weight."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the best parameter for assessment of chronic malnutrition?","options":["Height for age","Weight for height","Weight for age","Mid-arm circumference"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Best parameter for assessment of chronic malnutrition is height for age (stunting).  Why B wrong: Weight for height assesses acute malnutrition.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the best chronic-malnutrition indicators."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the best parameter for assessment of acute malnutrition?","options":["Weight for height","Height for age","Weight for age","Head circumference"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Best parameter for acute malnutrition is weight for height (wasting).  Why B wrong: Height for age assesses chronic malnutrition.  Why C/D wrong: Not the best acute-malnutrition indicators."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Extremely low birth weight is defined as?","options":["<1000 gram","<1500 gram","<2500 gram","<2000 gram"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Extremely low birth weight is <1000 gram.  Why B wrong: <1500 g is very low birth weight.  Why C wrong: <2500 g is low birth weight.  Why D wrong: Not a standard cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"One community development block equals approximately how many villages and population?","options":["100 villages (100,000 population)","50 villages (50,000 population)","200 villages (200,000 population)","10 villages (10,000 population)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One community development block equals 100 villages (100,000 population).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated norm is 100 villages / 1 lakh population."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Up to what age are children covered under ICDS?","options":["Up to 6 years age","Up to 5 years age","Up to 3 years age","Up to 2 years age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Children under ICDS are covered up to 6 years age.  Why B/C/D wrong: ICDS covers children up to 6 years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is described as the \"heart\" of the ICDS system?","options":["Anganwadi","Sub-centre","PHC","Gram Sabha"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The heart of the ICDS system is the Anganwadi.  Why B/C/D wrong: The Anganwadi (and Anganwadi worker) is the focal delivery point of ICDS."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the recommended frequency of school health examinations?","options":["Once every 6 months","Once a year","Once every 3 months","Once every month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Recommended frequency of school health examination is once every 6 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: The recommended interval is 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In which year were the Special Nutrition Programme and Balwadi Nutrition Programme launched?","options":["1970","1975","1985","1952"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Special nutrition program and Balwadi nutrition program launch year is 1970.  Why B/C/D wrong: Both were launched in 1970."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Nipah Virus disease was first recognised in a large outbreak in which countries and year?","options":["Malaysia & Singapore, 1998–99","India, 2018","West Africa, 2014","Congo, 1976"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nipah Virus was first recognised in a large outbreak in Malaysia & Singapore in 1998-99.  Why B wrong: 2018 is when India first reported cases.  Why C/D wrong: These relate to Ebola outbreaks."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In India, the first cases of Nipah virus were reported in 2018 from which locations? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nadia, West Bengal","Kozhikode, Kerala","Nagpur, Maharashtra","Pune, Maharashtra"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: In India the first cases were reported in Nadia (West Bengal) and Kozhikode (Kerala) in 2018.  Why C/D wrong: These locations were not the first reporting sites."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Fruit bats of which genus are the natural reservoir of Nipah virus?","options":["Pteropus genus","Aedes genus","Anopheles genus","Culex genus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fruit bats of the Pteropus genus are the natural reservoir of Nipah virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are mosquito genera, not bat reservoirs."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Nipah virus belongs to which virus family/type?","options":["Paramyxovirus","Retrovirus","Flavivirus","Nairovirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nipah is a Paramyxovirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are the causative agents of AIDS, Zika, and Crimean-Congo fever respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Mode of transmission of Nipah virus occurs through contact with? (Select all that apply)","options":["Infected bats","Pigs","Human beings","Mosquitoes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Nipah virus transmission occurs with infected bats, pigs or human beings.  Why D wrong: Mosquitoes are not a mode of Nipah transmission."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the incubation period of Nipah virus?","options":["4 to 21 days","1 to 3 days","5 to 60 days","14 to 16 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of Nipah virus (source text) is 4 to 21 days.  Why B/C wrong: These correspond to other diseases.  Why D wrong: 14–16 days is the source table value; the source text lists 4–21 days — see note.  [Note: source is internally inconsistent — the Viral Diseases text states 4–21 days while the table states 14–16 days. Per WHO, the Nipah incubation period is typically 4–14 days, ranging up to 21 days. Option A (4–21 days) is keyed as it aligns with the WHO-accepted range; the table's 14–16 days is not consistent with WHO.]"},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative agent of Ebola Virus Disease?","options":["Ebola virus","Nairovirus","Zika virus","Bacillus anthracis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The causative agent of Ebola Virus Disease is the Ebola virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause Crimean-Congo fever, Zika and anthrax respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the incubation period of Ebola Virus Disease?","options":["2 to 21 days","1 to 4 days","14 to 16 days","5 to 60 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of Ebola is 2 to 21 days.  Why B wrong: 1–4 days is swine flu.  Why C wrong: 14–16 days is Nipah (table).  Why D wrong: 5–60 days is brucellosis."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"The route of transmission of Ebola virus is through body fluids including? (Select all that apply)","options":["Semen","Breast milk","Mosquito bite","Water"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Ebola transmission is via body fluids including semen and breast milk.  Why C/D wrong: Ebola is not transmitted by mosquito bite or through water."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the reservoir of Ebola virus?","options":["Bats","Pigs","Mosquitoes","Cattle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The reservoir of Ebola virus is bats.  Why B/C/D wrong: Bats are the natural reservoir."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative agent of Zika Virus Disease?","options":["Zika virus (Flavivirus)","Ebola virus","Retrovirus","Paramyxovirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The causative agent of Zika Virus Disease is the Zika virus (a Flavivirus).  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause Ebola, AIDS and Nipah respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the incubation period of Zika Virus Disease?","options":["3 to 14 days","1 to 4 days","2 to 21 days","14 to 16 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubation period of Zika Virus Disease is 3 to 14 days.  Why B wrong: 1–4 days is swine flu.  Why C wrong: 2–21 days is Ebola.  Why D wrong: 14–16 days is Nipah (table)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"Which of the following are routes of transmission of Zika virus? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mosquito bite by an infected Aedes species mosquito","Blood transfusion","Sexual contact","Vertical transmission (mother to child at time of delivery, not via breastfeeding)","Airborne droplet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Zika spreads via infected Aedes mosquito bite, blood transfusion, sexual contact and vertical transmission (mother to child at delivery, not via breastfeeding).  Why E wrong: Airborne droplet is not a Zika transmission route."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In Zika virus disease, the complication in newborns from vertical transmission is?","options":["Microcephaly","GBS (Guillain-Barré Syndrome)","Cataract","Hydrocephalus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In newborns, vertical transmission of Zika causes microcephaly.  Why B wrong: GBS is the complication in adults.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated newborn complication."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In Zika virus disease, the complication in adults is?","options":["GBS (Guillain-Barré Syndrome)","Microcephaly","Encephalitis","Blindness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adults, Zika complication is GBS (Guillain-Barré Syndrome).  Why B wrong: Microcephaly is the newborn complication.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated adult complication."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative organism and incubation period of Swine Flu?","options":["H1N1 Type A Influenza; 1–4 days","Nairovirus; 1–3 days","Ebola virus; 2–21 days","Zika virus; 3–14 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Swine flu is caused by H1N1 Type A Influenza with incubation 1–4 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe Crimean-Congo fever, Ebola and Zika respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative organism and incubation period of Crimean-Congo Fever?","options":["Nairovirus; 1–3 days","H1N1 Influenza; 1–4 days","Bacillus anthracis; 1–7 days","Bacillus melitensis; 5–60 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Crimean-Congo Fever is caused by Nairovirus with incubation 1–3 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe swine flu, anthrax and brucellosis respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"According to the table, the causative organism of NIPAH virus is listed as?","options":["Hendra / Henipa virus (incubation 14–16 days)","Ebola virus","H7N9 Type A Influenza","Bacillus anthracis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists NIPAH virus organism as Hendra/Henipa virus with incubation 14–16 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are Ebola, avian influenza and anthrax organisms."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative organism and incubation period of Anthrax?","options":["Bacillus anthracis; 1–7 days","Bacillus melitensis; 5–60 days","Nairovirus; 1–3 days","Zika virus; 3–14 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anthrax is caused by Bacillus anthracis with incubation 1–7 days.  Why B wrong: Bacillus melitensis (5–60 days) causes brucellosis.  Why C/D wrong: These are Crimean-Congo fever and Zika."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative organism and incubation period of Brucellosis?","options":["Bacillus melitensis; 5–60 days","Bacillus anthracis; 1–7 days","Ebola virus; 2–21 days","H1N1 Influenza; 1–4 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Brucellosis is caused by Bacillus melitensis with incubation 5–60 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe anthrax, Ebola and swine flu respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"What is the causative organism and incubation period of H5N1 (Avian Influenza) per the table?","options":["H7N9 Type A Influenza; 1–10 days","H1N1 Type A Influenza; 1–4 days","Nairovirus; 1–3 days","Zika virus; 3–14 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists H5N1 (Avian Influenza) organism as H7N9 Type A Influenza with incubation 1–10 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to swine flu, Crimean-Congo fever and Zika.  [Note: source table is factually inconsistent — it labels the disease \"H5N1, Avian Influenza\" but lists the organism as \"H7N9 Type A Influenza.\" H5N1 and H7N9 are distinct avian influenza A subtypes; H5N1 disease is caused by the H5N1 virus. Answer keyed to source table wording as the question asks \"per the table.\"]"},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In the \"Quick Reviews about Public Health Diseases,\" who/what is called the \"Father of Public Health\"?","options":["Cholera","Tuberculosis","Plague","Leprosy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Father of Public Health is cholera.  Why B wrong: TB is the Barometer of social welfare.  Why C wrong: Plague is Black Death.  Why D wrong: Leprosy is Hansen's disease."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Black Sickness\" (Black fever) refers to which disease?","options":["Kala Azar (Leishmaniasis)","Plague","Dengue","Tuberculosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Black Sickness is Kala Azar (Leishmaniasis).  Why B wrong: Plague is Black Death.  Why C wrong: Dengue is break-bone fever.  Why D wrong: TB is barometer of social welfare / Koch's phenomenon."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Black Death\" refers to which disease?","options":["Plague","Kala Azar","AIDS","Cholera"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Black Death is plague.  Why B wrong: Kala Azar is Black Sickness.  Why C wrong: AIDS is Slim disease.  Why D wrong: Cholera is Father of Public Health."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Cerebrospinal fever\" refers to which disease?","options":["Meningococcal meningitis","Tetanus","Dengue","Leprosy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cerebrospinal fever is meningococcal meningitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are 8th-day disease, break-bone fever and Hansen's disease respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Break-bone fever\" refers to which disease?","options":["Dengue","Chikungunya","Trench fever","Kala Azar"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Break-bone fever is dengue.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not called break-bone fever in the source."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Monkey fever\" (Monkey disease) refers to which disease?","options":["KFD (Kyasanur Forest Disease)","Dengue","Plague","Trench fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monkey fever/disease is KFD (Kyasanur Forest Disease).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are break-bone fever, Black Death and 5-day fever respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"5-day fever\" refers to which disease?","options":["Trench fever","Dengue","Tetanus","Plague"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 5-day fever is trench fever.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are break-bone fever, 8th-day disease and Black Death respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"8th-day disease\" refers to which condition?","options":["Tetanus (Tetanus neonatorum)","Trench fever","Dengue","Leprosy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 8th-day disease is tetanus (tetanus neonatorum).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are 5-day fever, break-bone fever and Hansen's disease respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Koch's Phenomenon\" is associated with which disease?","options":["Tuberculosis","Cholera","Leprosy","Plague"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Koch's phenomenon is associated with tuberculosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are the Father of Public Health, Hansen's disease and Black Death respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Rubeola\" refers to which disease?","options":["Measles","German measles","Scarlet fever","Erythema infectiosum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubeola is measles.  Why B wrong: German measles is Rubella.  Why C wrong: Scarlet fever is the second disease.  Why D wrong: Erythema infectiosum is the fifth disease."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Rubella\" refers to which disease?","options":["German measles","Measles","Scarlet fever","Plague"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella is German measles (third disease).  Why B wrong: Measles is Rubeola.  Why C/D wrong: These are scarlet fever and plague."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Hansen's disease\" refers to which disease?","options":["Leprosy","Tuberculosis","Plague","Dengue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hansen's disease is leprosy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are TB, plague and dengue respectively."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"Slim disease\" refers to which disease?","options":["AIDS","Tuberculosis","Kala Azar","Leprosy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Slim disease is AIDS (HIV/AIDS).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are Barometer of social welfare, Black Sickness and Hansen's disease."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"In the \"first disease / second disease / third disease\" classification, which is correct?","options":["First disease/Rubeola = Measles; Second disease = Scarlet fever; Third disease/German Measles = Rubella","First disease = Rubella; Second = Measles; Third = Scarlet fever","First disease = Scarlet fever; Second = Rubella; Third = Measles","First disease = Chickenpox; Second = Measles; Third = Rubella"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: First disease/Rubeola is measles; Second disease is scarlet fever; Third disease/German measles is rubella.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mis-order the classical exanthem numbering."},{"section":"Batch 017 · immunization & vaccine facts, WHO milestones & smallpox/polio eradication…","question":"\"River Blindness\" refers to which disease?","options":["Onchocerciasis","Trachoma","Cataract","Kala Azar"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: River Blindness is onchocerciasis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other eye/disease conditions, not river blindness."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Who was the chairman of the Bhore Committee?","options":["Sir Joseph Bhore","Dr. A. L. Mudaliar","Dr. M. S. Chadha","Kartar Singh"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sir Joseph Bhore was the chairman of the Bhore Committee (1946).  Why B wrong: Dr. Mudaliar chaired the Mudaliar Committee (1962).  Why C wrong: Chadha is linked to the Chadha Committee (1963).  Why D wrong: Kartar Singh chaired the Kartar Singh Committee (1972-73)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Bhore Committee was appointed/established in which year and submitted its report in which year?","options":["Appointed 1943, report submitted 1946","Appointed 1946, report submitted 1948","Appointed 1940, report submitted 1943","Appointed 1943, report submitted 1950"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Bhore Committee was appointed in 1943 and submitted its report in 1946.  Why B/C/D wrong: These years do not match the recorded dates."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"By which other name is the Bhore Committee also known?","options":["Health Survey and Development Committee","Health Survey and Planning Committee","Committee on Integration of Health Services","Committee on Multipurpose Workers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Bhore Committee is also known as the Health Survey and Development Committee.  Why B wrong: That name refers to the Mudaliar Committee.  Why C wrong: That is the Jungalwalla Committee.  Why D wrong: That is the Kartar Singh Committee."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What was the short-term recommendation of the Bhore Committee regarding a Primary Health Centre (PHC)?","options":["1 PHC per 40,000 population, 30 beds, 3 subcentres and 2 medical officers","1 PHC per 50,000 population, 6 beds and 1 medical officer","1 PHC per 30,000 population, 15 beds and 3 medical officers","1 PHC per 20,000 population, 10 beds and 2 medical officers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bhore's short-term recommendation was 1 PHC/40,000 population with 30 beds, 3 subcentres and 2 medical officers.  Why B wrong: That is the Kartar Singh norm (1 PHC/50,000).  Why C/D wrong: These numbers were not recommended by Bhore."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"In the Bhore Committee's long-term recommendation, primary health units were linked to which bed and population figures?","options":["75-bedded hospital / 10-20k population","30-bedded hospital / 40k population","100-bedded hospital / 50k population","50-bedded hospital / 30k population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Long-term recommendation was primary health units of 75-bedded hospital for 10-20k population, secondary health units 650-bedded hospital, and regional health units with 2500 beds.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the long-term recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Bhore Committee suggested how many months of training for doctors in Preventive & Social Medicine (PSM)?","options":["3 months","6 months","1 month","12 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bhore suggested 3 months training for doctors in Preventive & Social Medicine.  Why B/C/D wrong: Only 3 months is recorded."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Which committee recommended the concept of the \"3 million plan & social physician\"?","options":["Bhore Committee","Mudaliar Committee","Chadha Committee","Mukherjee Committee"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Bhore Committee recommended the '3 million plan & social physician' and comprehensive health care concepts.  Why B/C/D wrong: These committees made other recommendations."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"By which other name is the Mudaliar Committee (1962) also known?","options":["Health Survey and Planning Committee","Health Survey and Development Committee","Group on Medical Education and Support Manpower","Committee on Integration of Health Services"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mudaliar Committee is also known as the Health Survey and Planning Committee.  Why B wrong: That is the Bhore Committee.  Why C wrong: That is the Shrivastav Committee.  Why D wrong: That is the Jungalwalla Committee."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What PHC-to-population norm did the Mudaliar Committee (1962) recommend as a maximum?","options":["1 PHC per 40,000 population maximum","1 PHC per 50,000 population maximum","1 PHC per 20,000 population maximum","1 PHC per 1,00,000 population maximum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mudaliar Committee recommended 1 PHC/40,000 population maximum.  Why B wrong: 50,000 is the Kartar Singh norm.  Why C/D wrong: Not recommended by Mudaliar."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Chadha Committee (1963) recommended the appointment of which officer for malaria vigilance?","options":["Malaria Vigilance Officer","Malaria Inspector","Malaria Surveillance Worker","Village Health Guide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Chadha Committee recommended a Malaria Vigilance Officer.  Why B wrong: 1 malaria inspector per 1000 population was a Mukherjee (1965) recommendation.  Why C/D wrong: These were suggested by other committees."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"According to the Chadha Committee, one basic health worker was recommended per how many population for malaria vigilance, family planning and collection of vital statistics?","options":["1000 population","5000 population","3000 population","40000 population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chadha recommended 1 basic health worker per 1000 population for malaria vigilance, family planning and vital statistics collection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures are not the Chadha norm."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"As per the Chadha Committee, family planning health assistants were to supervise how many basic health workers?","options":["34","10","4","15"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Family planning health assistants were to supervise 34 of these basic health workers.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the number stated by Chadha."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Mukherjee Committee (1965) recommended which of the following?","options":["Bifurcating/separating malaria activities from family planning with separate staff","Integration of preventive and curative services at all levels","Equal pay for equal work","Establishing a Medical & Health Education Commission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mukherjee Committee (1965) recommended separating malaria activities from family planning and separate staff for the family planning program.  Why B wrong: That was a Bhore recommendation.  Why C wrong: That was Jungalwalla.  Why D wrong: That was Shrivastav."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Mukherjee Committee (1966) recommended that basic health services should be provided at which level?","options":["Block level","District level","State level","Village level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mukherjee Committee-1966 recommended that basic health services should be provided at the block level.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated level was block level."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"By which other name is the Jungalwalla Committee (1967) known?","options":["Committee on Integration of Health Services","Committee on Multipurpose Workers","Health Survey and Development Committee","Group on Medical Education and Support Manpower"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Jungalwalla Committee is also known as the Committee on Integration of Health Services.  Why B wrong: That is the Kartar Singh Committee.  Why C wrong: That is the Bhore Committee.  Why D wrong: That is the Shrivastav Committee."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Which of the following was a recommendation of the Jungalwalla Committee (1967)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Equal pay for equal work","Additional pay for specialised works","Elimination of private practice by government doctors","Integration of health services from top to bottom","1 PHC for 50000 population"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Jungalwalla recommended equal pay for equal work, additional pay for specialised works, elimination of private practice by government doctors, and integration of health services from top to bottom with similar seniority and a unified cadre.  Why E wrong: 1 PHC for 50000 population was the Kartar Singh recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"By which other name is the Kartar Singh Committee (1972-73) known?","options":["Committee on Multipurpose Workers under Health & Family Planning","Committee on Integration of Health Services","Health Survey and Planning Committee","Group on Medical Education and Support Manpower"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Kartar Singh Committee is also known as the Committee on Multipurpose Workers under Health & Family Planning.  Why B wrong: That is Jungalwalla.  Why C wrong: That is Mudaliar.  Why D wrong: That is Shrivastav."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Kartar Singh Committee was appointed and submitted its report in which years respectively?","options":["Appointed 1972, report 1973","Appointed 1970, report 1972","Appointed 1974, report 1975","Appointed 1965, report 1966"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Kartar Singh Committee was appointed in 1972 and submitted its report in 1973.  Why B/D wrong: Incorrect years.  Why C wrong: Those are the Shrivastav Committee years."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"As per the Kartar Singh Committee, ANMs were to be replaced by which category of workers?","options":["Female Health Workers","Male Health Workers","Village Health Guides","Malaria Vigilance Officers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kartar Singh recommended ANMs be replaced by Female Health Workers (and family planning health assistants by Male Health Workers).  Why B wrong: Male Health Workers replaced family planning health assistants.  Why C/D wrong: Not the replacement stated for ANMs."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What PHC-to-population norm and subcentre coverage did the Kartar Singh Committee recommend?","options":["1 PHC for 50000 population and 15-16 subcentres for 3000-3500 population","1 PHC for 40000 population and 3 subcentres","1 PHC for 30000 population and 10 subcentres","1 PHC for 20000 population and 5 subcentres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kartar Singh recommended 1 PHC for 50000 population and 15-16 sub-centres for 3000-3500 population, with 1 male and 1 female health worker appointed.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the Kartar Singh norm."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"By which other name is the Shrivastav Committee (1974-75) known?","options":["Group on Medical Education and Support Manpower","Committee on Integration of Health Services","Health Survey and Development Committee","Committee on Multipurpose Workers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Shrivastav Committee is also known as the Group on Medical Education and Support Manpower.  Why B wrong: That is Jungalwalla.  Why C wrong: That is Bhore.  Why D wrong: That is Kartar Singh."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The three-tier health system (SC, PHC, CHC) for rural areas recommended by the Shrivastav Committee was adopted at which conference?","options":["Alma-Ata Conference 1978","Ottawa Conference 1986","Bhore Conference 1946","Geneva Conference 1948"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three-tier health system (SC, PHC, CHC) was adopted at the Alma-Ata Conference 1978.  Why B/C/D wrong: These conferences are not linked to this adoption in the source."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"According to the Shrivastav Committee, CHCs serving as referral centres from PHC to higher level are known as:","options":["FRUs (First Referral Units)","VHGs (Village Health Guides)","Subcentres","Regional Health Units"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CHCs providing referral services from PHC to higher level are known as FRUs.  Why B wrong: VHG stands for Village Health Guides, a separate recommendation.  Why C/D wrong: These are different facility types."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The ROME scheme recommended by the Shrivastav Committee stands for:","options":["Reorientation of Medical Education","Rural Oriented Medical Establishment","Rehabilitation of Medical Education","Reform of Medical Enrolment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ROME Scheme stands for Reorientation of Medical Education.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"In which year was the Village Health Guide scheme launched?","options":["1977","1975","1978","1972"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Village Health Guide scheme was launched in 1977.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect launch years."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Rural Health Scheme launched on 2 October 1977 was based on which guiding principle?","options":["\"Placing people's health in people's hand\"","\"Health for all by 2000 AD\"","\"Oral Rehydration, No More Dehydration\"","\"Placing doctors in every village\""],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The principle of the Rural Health Scheme was 'Placing people's health in people's hand'.  Why B wrong: That refers to Health for All by 2000.  Why C wrong: That is an ORS Week slogan.  Why D wrong: Not the stated principle."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The main aim of the Rural Health Scheme (1977) was to train which category of workers?","options":["Community health workers","Female health workers","Malaria inspectors","Village health guides only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The main aim of the Rural Health Scheme was to train community health workers, involving medical colleges for ROME schemes.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the primary aim stated."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Krishnan Committee (1983) recommended providing health services to slum areas through which scheme?","options":["Urban Revamping Scheme","Rural Health Scheme","ROME Scheme","Village Health Guide Scheme"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Krishnan Committee recommended providing health services to slum areas through the Urban Revamping Scheme.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the Krishnan Committee's slum recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The \"Health for all by 2000\" goal was identified in which year to be outlined within the sixth five-year plan?","options":["1981","1983","1977","1986"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 1981, the goal to identify Health for All by 2000 AD and outline programs for the sixth five-year plan was set.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect years for this milestone."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"In which year was the National Family Planning Program (NFPP) launched in India?","options":["1952","1958","1962","1977"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The National Family Planning Program (NFPP) was launched in 1952.  Why B wrong: 1958 was NMEP.  Why C wrong: 1962 was NTCP (tuberculosis).  Why D wrong: 1977 was the renaming to National Family Welfare Program."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Integrated Child Development Scheme (ICDS) was launched in which year?","options":["1975","1985","1992","1997"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICDS was launched in 1975.  Why B wrong: 1985 was UIP.  Why C wrong: 1992 was CSSM.  Why D wrong: 1997 was RCH/RNTCP."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National AIDS Control Program (NACP) was launched in which year?","options":["1987","1985","1992","1999"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NACP was launched in 1987.  Why B wrong: 1985 was UIP.  Why C wrong: 1992 was CSSM.  Why D wrong: 1999 was NAMP."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) was launched in which year?","options":["2005","2013","2003","2014"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NRHM was launched in 2005.  Why B wrong: 2013 was NHM (NRHM + NUHM) and NUHM.  Why C wrong: 2003 was PMSSY.  Why D wrong: 2014 was PMJDY / Swachh Bharat."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) was launched in India in which year?","options":["1985","1978","1992","1975"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: UIP was launched in 1985.  Why B wrong: 1978 was the Expanded Programme on Immunization (EPI).  Why C wrong: 1992 was CSSM.  Why D wrong: 1975 was ICDS."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Pradhanmantri Jan Aarogya Yojana (PMJAY) / Ayushman Bharat Yojana was launched in which year?","options":["2018","2014","2016","2020"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMJAY / Ayushman Bharat Yojana was launched in 2018.  Why B wrong: 2014 was PMJDY.  Why C wrong: 2016 was PM Ujjawala.  Why D wrong: 2020 was Aatm Nirbhar Yojana / National TB Elimination program."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) and Janani Shishu Suraksha Yojana (JSSY) were launched in which years respectively?","options":["JSY 2005, JSSY 2011","JSY 2011, JSSY 2005","JSY 2009, JSSY 2013","JSY 2005, JSSY 2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: JSY was launched in 2005 and JSSY in 2011.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the recorded launch years."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"In which year was the National Family Planning Program renamed the National Family Welfare Program?","options":["1977","1952","1962","2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The National Family Planning Program was renamed National Family Welfare Program in 1977.  Why B wrong: 1952 was its original launch.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect years."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Pradhan Mantri Poshan Shakti Nirman (PM-POSHAN) was launched in which year?","options":["2021","2018","2022","2017"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PM-POSHAN was launched in 2021.  Why B wrong: 2018 was Poshan Abhiyaan / Anaemia Mukt Bharat.  Why C wrong: 2022 was Saksham Anganwadi and Poshan 2.0 / Ni-Kshay 2.0.  Why D wrong: 2017 was Mission Parivar Vikas."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Baby Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFHI) was launched by WHO and in India in which years respectively?","options":["WHO 1991, India 1993","WHO 1993, India 1991","WHO 1985, India 1991","WHO 1991, India 1997"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BFHI was launched by WHO in 1991 and in India in 1993.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the recorded years."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Under the Non-Communicable Disease Control Programmes, in which year was the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke (NPCDCS) launched?","options":["2010","1982","2007-08","2012"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NPCDCS was launched in 2010.  Why B wrong: 1982 was NMHP.  Why C wrong: 2007-08 was NTCP (tobacco).  Why D wrong: 2012 was NPPC (palliative care)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Programme for Control of Blindness & Visual Impairment (NPCBVI) was launched in which year?","options":["1976","1982","2007","2010"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NPCBVI was launched in 1976.  Why B wrong: 1982 was NMHP.  Why C wrong: 2007 was NPPCD.  Why D wrong: 2010 was NPCDCS."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Mental Health Programme (NMHP) was launched in which year?","options":["1982","1976","2010","2012"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NMHP was launched in 1982.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect launch years."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Tobacco Control Programme (NTCP) was launched in which year?","options":["2007-08","2010","1982","2014-15"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The National Tobacco Control Programme was launched in 2007-08.  Why B wrong: 2010 was NPCDCS.  Why C wrong: 1982 was NMHP.  Why D wrong: 2014-15 was NOHP (oral health)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Oral Health Programme (NOHP) was launched in which year?","options":["2014-15","2007-08","2010","2012"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NOHP was launched in 2014-15.  Why B wrong: 2007-08 was NTCP.  Why C wrong: 2010 was NPCDCS / NPPMBI.  Why D wrong: 2012 was NPPC."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Program for Palliative Care (NPPC) was launched in which year?","options":["2012","2010","2007","2014"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NPPC was launched in 2012.  Why B wrong: 2010 was NPPMBI / NPCDCS.  Why C wrong: 2007 was NPPCD.  Why D wrong: Not the recorded year."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Organ Tissue and Transplant Organization (NOTTO) was established in which year?","options":["2014","2007-08","1986","2010"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NOTTO was established in 2014.  Why B wrong: 2007-08 was NPPCF (fluorosis).  Why C wrong: 1986 was NIDDCP (iodine deficiency).  Why D wrong: Not the recorded year."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP) was established in which year?","options":["1986","2014","2007-08","1976"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NIDDCP was established in 1986.  Why B wrong: 2014 was NOTTO.  Why C wrong: 2007-08 was NPPCF.  Why D wrong: 1976 was NPCBVI."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"In which year was the Employees State Insurance (ESI) Act passed in India?","options":["1948","1961","1954","1969"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ESI Act was passed in 1948 (as was the Factories Act).  Why B wrong: 1961 was the Maternity Benefit Act.  Why C wrong: 1954 was the PFA Act.  Why D wrong: 1969 was the Registration of Births and Deaths Act."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Prevention of Food Adulteration (PFA) Act was passed in which year (and enforced when)?","options":["1954 (enforced 1955)","1948 (enforced 1950)","1956 (enforced 1958)","1971 (enforced 1972)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The PFA Act was passed in 1954 and enforced in 1955.  Why B/C wrong: Incorrect years.  Why D wrong: Those are the MTP Act years."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP) Act was passed in which year?","options":["1971 (enforced 1972, amended 1975 and 2020)","1994","1985","1961"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MTP Act was passed in 1971, enforced 1972, and amended in 1975 and 2020.  Why B wrong: 1994 was the PC-PNDT / OT Act.  Why C wrong: 1985 was the NDPS Act.  Why D wrong: 1961 was the Maternity Benefit Act."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"In which year was the Pre-conception and Pre-natal Diagnostic Techniques (Prohibition of Sex Selection) [PC-PNDT] Act passed?","options":["1994","1971","1986","2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The PC-PNDT Act was passed in 1994 (same year as the Transplantation of Human Organs / OT Act).  Why B wrong: 1971 was the MTP Act.  Why C wrong: 1986 was COPRA / EPA.  Why D wrong: 2005 was NREGA / Disaster Management / RTI."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Consumer Protection Act (COPRA) and Environmental Protection Act (EPA) were both passed in which year?","options":["1986","1985","1987","1994"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both COPRA and EPA were passed in 1986.  Why B wrong: 1985 was the NDPS Act.  Why C wrong: 1987 was the National Mental Health Act.  Why D wrong: 1994 was PC-PNDT / OT Act."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Right to Information (RTI) Act was passed in India in which year?","options":["2005","2009","2012","2017"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The RTI Act was passed in 2005.  Why B wrong: 2009 was the RTE Act.  Why C wrong: 2012 was POCSO.  Why D wrong: 2017 was HIV/AIDS Act / Mental Health Care Act."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act was enacted in which year?","options":["2012","2015","2009","2017"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The POCSO Act was enacted in 2012.  Why B wrong: 2015 was the Juvenile Justice Act.  Why C wrong: 2009 was RTE.  Why D wrong: 2017 was the HIV/AIDS Act."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Mental Health Care Act and the HIV/AIDS (Prevention & Control) Act were both passed in which year?","options":["2017","1987","2012","2005"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both the Mental Health Care Act and HIV/AIDS Act were passed in 2017.  Why B wrong: 1987 was the (earlier) National Mental Health Act.  Why C wrong: 2012 was POCSO.  Why D wrong: 2005 was RTI / NREGA."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Where are the headquarters of the World Health Organisation (WHO), which was established on 7 April 1948?","options":["Geneva, Switzerland","New York, USA","Rome, Italy","New Delhi, India"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO's headquarters are in Geneva, Switzerland; it was established 7 April 1948.  Why B wrong: New York houses UNICEF and UNO.  Why C wrong: Rome houses FAO.  Why D wrong: New Delhi houses INC and IRC."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"UNICEF, established on 11 December 1946, is headquartered in which city?","options":["New York, USA","Geneva, Switzerland","Rome, Italy","Washington D.C."],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: UNICEF is headquartered in New York, USA, and was established 11 Dec 1946.  Why B wrong: Geneva houses WHO, ICN, ILO.  Why C wrong: Rome houses FAO.  Why D wrong: Washington D.C. houses USAID."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO), established in 1945, is headquartered in:","options":["Rome, Italy","Geneva, Switzerland","New York, USA","Washington D.C."],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FAO is headquartered in Rome, Italy (established 1945).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are headquarters of other organisations."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The International Council of Nurses (ICN) was established in which year?","options":["1899","1919","1947","1948"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICN was established in 1899 and is headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland.  Why B wrong: 1919 was ILO.  Why C wrong: 1947 was INC (Indian Nursing Council).  Why D wrong: 1948 was WHO."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Indian Nursing Council (INC), established in 1947, is headquartered in which city?","options":["New Delhi","Geneva","Mumbai","Chennai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Indian Nursing Council is headquartered in New Delhi (established 1947).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the INC headquarters."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The International Labour Organisation (ILO) was established in which year?","options":["1919","1899","1945","1948"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ILO was established in 1919 and is headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland.  Why B wrong: 1899 was ICN.  Why C wrong: 1945 was FAO / UNO.  Why D wrong: 1948 was WHO."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The International Red Cross Society (IRCS) was established in which year?","options":["1863","1920","1945","1919"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The International Red Cross Society was established in 1863 (Geneva, Switzerland).  Why B wrong: 1920 was the Indian Red Cross Society.  Why C wrong: 1945 was CARE / FAO.  Why D wrong: 1919 was ILO."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What does the abbreviation CARE stand for among international health organisations?","options":["Corporation for Assistance and Relief Everywhere","Committee for Aid and Rehabilitation of Everyone","Council for Assistance and Relief Everywhere","Care and Assistance for Rural Establishments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, CARE stands for Corporation for Assistance and Relief Everywhere (established 1945). [Note: source prints \"Corporation\"; the organisation's actual full form is \"Cooperative for Assistance and Relief Everywhere\".]  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"USAID (United States Agency for International Development), established in 1961, is headquartered in:","options":["Washington D.C.","New York","Geneva","Rome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: USAID is headquartered in Washington D.C. (established 1961).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not USAID's headquarters."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Cancer Day is observed on which date?","options":["4 February","24 March","7 April","8 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Cancer Day is observed on 4 February.  Why B wrong: 24 March is World Tuberculosis Day.  Why C wrong: 7 April is World Health Day.  Why D wrong: 8 May is World Thalassemia Day / Red Cross Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"International Women's Day is observed on which date?","options":["8 March","9 March","15 March","24 March"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: International Women's Day is observed on 8 March.  Why B wrong: 9 March is Anganwadi Worker's Day.  Why C wrong: 15 March is World Consumer Right Day.  Why D wrong: 24 March is World Tuberculosis Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Tuberculosis Day is observed on which date?","options":["24 March","16 March","8 March","25 April"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Tuberculosis Day is observed on 24 March.  Why B wrong: 16 March is National Immunization/Vaccination Day and World Measles Day.  Why C wrong: 8 March is International Women's Day.  Why D wrong: 25 April is World Malaria Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Health Day is observed on which date?","options":["7 April","11 April","22 April","25 April"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Health Day is observed on 7 April.  Why B wrong: 11 April is National Safe Motherhood Day.  Why C wrong: 22 April is World Earth Day.  Why D wrong: 25 April is World Malaria Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Malaria Day is observed on which date?","options":["25 April","22 April","24 to 30 April","7 April"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Malaria Day is observed on 25 April.  Why B wrong: 22 April is World Earth Day.  Why C wrong: 24 to 30 April is World Immunization Week.  Why D wrong: 7 April is World Health Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"International Nurses Day is observed on which date?","options":["12 May","5 May","8 May","1 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: International Nurses Day is observed on 12 May.  Why B wrong: 5 May is International Midwives' Day.  Why C wrong: 8 May is World Thalassemia Day / World Red Cross Day.  Why D wrong: 1 May is World Labour Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"International Midwives' Day is observed on which date?","options":["5 May","12 May","8 May","28 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: International Midwives' Day is observed on 5 May.  Why B wrong: 12 May is International Nurses Day.  Why C wrong: 8 May is World Thalassemia/Red Cross Day.  Why D wrong: 28 May is Menstrual Hygiene Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World No Tobacco Day is observed on which date?","options":["31 May","28 May","16 May","1 May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World No Tobacco Day is observed on 31 May.  Why B wrong: 28 May is Menstrual Hygiene Day.  Why C wrong: 16 May is National Dengue Day.  Why D wrong: 1 May is World Labour Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"International Yoga Day is observed on which date?","options":["21 June","5 June","14 June","26 June"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: International Yoga Day is observed on 21 June.  Why B wrong: 5 June is World Environment Day.  Why C wrong: 14 June is World Blood Donor Day.  Why D wrong: 26 June is International Day Against Drug Abuse."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Blood Donor Day is observed on which date?","options":["14 June","5 June","21 June","1 July"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Blood Donor Day is observed on 14 June.  Why B wrong: 5 June is World Environment Day.  Why C wrong: 21 June is International Yoga Day.  Why D wrong: 1 July is Doctors Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Population Day is observed on which date?","options":["11 July","1 July","28 July","29 July"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Population Day is observed on 11 July.  Why B wrong: 1 July is Doctors Day.  Why C wrong: 28 July is World Hepatitis Day.  Why D wrong: 29 July is World ORS Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Hepatitis Day and World ORS Day are observed on which dates respectively?","options":["28 July and 29 July","29 July and 28 July","1 July and 11 July","11 July and 28 July"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Hepatitis Day is 28 July and World ORS Day is 29 July.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the recorded dates."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Breast Feeding Day / start of World Breast Feeding Week falls on which date?","options":["1 August","10 August","12 August","28 September"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Breast Feeding Day is 1 August and World Breast Feeding Week is 01-07 August.  Why B wrong: 10 August is National Deworming Day.  Why C wrong: 12 August is International Youth Day.  Why D wrong: 28 September is World Rabies Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"National Nutrition Week is observed during which period?","options":["01-07 September","7-11 September","01-07 August","24-30 April"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: National Nutrition Week is observed 01-07 September.  Why B wrong: 7-11 September is Malaria Week.  Why C wrong: 01-07 August is Breast Feeding Week.  Why D wrong: 24-30 April is World Immunization Week."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Rabies Day and World Heart Day are observed on which dates respectively?","options":["28 September and 29 September","29 September and 28 September","16 September and 25 September","25 September and 16 September"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Rabies Day is 28 September and World Heart Day is 29 September.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dates do not match."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Mental Health Day is observed on which date?","options":["10 October","15 October","16 October","24 October"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Mental Health Day is observed on 10 October.  Why B wrong: 15 October is Global Hand Washing Day.  Why C wrong: 16 October is World Anaesthesia Day.  Why D wrong: 24 October is World Polio Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Polio Day is observed on which date?","options":["24 October","10 October","15 October","16 October"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World Polio Day is observed on 24 October.  Why B wrong: 10 October is World Mental Health Day.  Why C wrong: 15 October is Global Hand Washing Day.  Why D wrong: 16 October is World Anaesthesia Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World Diabetes Day and National Children's Day are both observed on which date?","options":["14 November","10 November","12 November","17 November"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 14 November is both National Children's Day and World Diabetes Day.  Why B wrong: 10 November is World Immunization Day.  Why C wrong: 12 November is World Pneumonia Day.  Why D wrong: 17 November is National Epilepsy Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"World AIDS Day is observed on which date?","options":["1 December","10 December","12 December","14 November"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: World AIDS Day is observed on 1 December.  Why B wrong: 10 December is Human Rights Day.  Why C wrong: 12 December is Universal Health Coverage Day.  Why D wrong: 14 November is World Diabetes Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"Universal Health Coverage Day is observed on which date?","options":["12 December","10 December","1 December","14 November"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Universal Health Coverage Day is observed on 12 December.  Why B wrong: 10 December is Human Rights Day.  Why C wrong: 1 December is World AIDS Day.  Why D wrong: 14 November is World Diabetes Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What was the 2024 theme of World Health Day?","options":["My health, My Right","Healing Hands, Caring Hearts","Our Land, Our Future","Humanly Possible"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2024 theme of World Health Day (7 April) was \"My health, My Right\".  Why B wrong: That was National Doctor's Day theme.  Why C wrong: That was World Environment Day theme.  Why D wrong: That was World Immunisation Week theme."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What was the 2024 theme of International Nurses Day?","options":["Our Nurses. Our Future. The economic power of care","My health, My Right","Vaccines Work for All","Diabetes and Well-being"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2024 theme of International Nurses Day (12 May) was \"Our Nurses. Our Future. The economic power of care\".  Why B wrong: That was World Health Day.  Why C wrong: That was National Vaccination Day.  Why D wrong: That was World Diabetes Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What was the 2024 theme of World Population Day?","options":["Leave no one behind, count everyone","Asthma Education Empowers","Yoga for Women Empowerment","Our Land, Our Future"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 2024 theme of World Population Day (11 July) was \"Leave no one behind, count everyone\".  Why B wrong: That was World Asthma Day.  Why C wrong: That was International Yoga Day.  Why D wrong: That was World Environment Day."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What does the ORS abbreviation stand for according to the World ORS Day note (O, R, S)?","options":["O – Only, R – Rational, S – Solution for diarrhoea","O – Oral, R – Rehydration, S – Salt","O – Only, R – Rehydration, S – Solution","O – Oral, R – Rational, S – Salt"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source expands ORS as O-Only, R-Rational, S-Solution for diarrhoea.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's expansion."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"According to the source, ORS Week is observed during which period?","options":["25 July – 31 July","1-7 August","01-07 September","24-30 April"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ORS Week is 25 July-31 July with the 2023 slogan \"Oral Rehydration, No More Dehydration\".  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other weeks (Breast Feeding, Nutrition, Immunization)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"In which city is the National Institute of Virology (NIV) located?","options":["Pune","New Delhi","Bangalore","Lucknow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The National Institute of Virology (NIV) is located in Pune.  Why B wrong: New Delhi houses NCDC / ICMR.  Why C wrong: Bangalore houses NIMHANS.  Why D wrong: Lucknow houses CDRI / SGPGI."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Institute of Mental Health & Neuro-Sciences (NIMHANS) is located in which city?","options":["Bangalore","Pune","New Delhi","Chennai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NIMHANS is located in Bangalore.  Why B wrong: Pune houses NIV / NARI.  Why C wrong: New Delhi houses NCDC / ICMR / NIMR / NIP.  Why D wrong: Chennai houses NIRT / NIE."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Centre for Disease Control (NCDC) and the Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR) are both located in which city?","options":["New Delhi","Pune","Mumbai","Chennai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both NCDC and ICMR are located in New Delhi.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cities house other institutes."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Institute of Nutrition (NIN) is located in which city?","options":["Hyderabad","Chennai","New Delhi","Mumbai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The National Institute of Nutrition (NIN) is located in Hyderabad (as is the Institute of Preventive Medicine).  Why B wrong: Chennai houses NIRT / NIE.  Why C wrong: New Delhi houses NCDC / ICMR.  Why D wrong: Mumbai houses ERC / NIRRH."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Institute for Research in Tuberculosis (NIRT) is located in which city?","options":["Chennai","Hyderabad","Agra","New Delhi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NIRT is located in Chennai (as is NIE).  Why B wrong: Hyderabad houses NIN.  Why C wrong: Agra houses NJILOMD (JALMA - leprosy).  Why D wrong: New Delhi houses NIMR."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Institute of Malaria Research (NIMR) is located in which city?","options":["New Delhi","Noida","Pune","Mumbai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NIMR is located in New Delhi.  Why B wrong: Noida houses NICPR (cancer prevention).  Why C wrong: Pune houses NARI (AIDS research).  Why D wrong: Mumbai houses NIRRH / NIIH."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National AIDS Research Institute (NARI) is located in which city?","options":["Pune","New Delhi","Mumbai","Ahmedabad"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NARI is located in Pune.  Why B wrong: New Delhi houses NIMR / NIP.  Why C wrong: Mumbai houses NIRRH.  Why D wrong: Ahmedabad houses NIOH."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National JALMA Institute for Leprosy & Other Mycobacterial Diseases (NJILOMD) is located in which city?","options":["Agra","Bhopal","Belgav","Mumbai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NJILOMD (JALMA - leprosy) is located in Agra.  Why B wrong: Bhopal houses NIREH (environmental health).  Why C wrong: Belgav houses NITM (traditional medicine).  Why D wrong: Mumbai houses NIIH / NIRRH / ERC."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Institute of Occupational Health (NIOH) is located in which city?","options":["Ahmedabad","Pune","New Delhi","Jodhpur"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NIOH is located in Ahmedabad.  Why B wrong: Pune houses NARI / NIV.  Why C wrong: New Delhi houses NIP / NIMR.  Why D wrong: Jodhpur houses NIIRNCD."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Vector Control Research Centre (VCRC) is located in which city?","options":["Puducherry","New Delhi","Bhopal","Kolkata"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VCRC is located in Puducherry.  Why B wrong: New Delhi houses NIP.  Why C wrong: Bhopal houses NIREH.  Why D wrong: Kolkata houses ICMR-NICED / IRM."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The ICMR-National Institute of Cholera & Enteric Diseases (NICED) is located in which city?","options":["Kolkata","Chennai","Pune","Mumbai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ICMR-National Institute of Cholera & Enteric Diseases is located in Kolkata (as is the Institute of Reproductive Medicine).  Why B/C/D wrong: These cities house other institutes."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Institute for Implementation Research on Non-Communicable Diseases (NIIRNCD) is located in which city?","options":["Jodhpur","Agra","Bhopal","Noida"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NIIRNCD is located in Jodhpur.  Why B wrong: Agra houses NJILOMD.  Why C wrong: Bhopal houses NIREH.  Why D wrong: Noida houses NICPR."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of measles?","options":["4 days before to 5 days after appearance of rashes on body","7 days before to 7 days after appearance of rashes on body","1-2 days before to 4-5 days after appearance of rashes on body","14-28 days from disease onset"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measles is communicable 4 days before to 5 days after appearance of rashes.  Why B wrong: That is rubella.  Why C wrong: That is chickenpox.  Why D wrong: That is diphtheria."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of rubella?","options":["7 days before symptoms to 7 days after appearance of rashes on body","4 days before to 5 days after rashes","1-2 days before to 4-5 days after rashes","4-6 days before symptoms to 7 days thereafter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella is communicable 7 days before symptoms to 7 days after appearance of rashes.  Why B wrong: That is measles.  Why C wrong: That is chickenpox.  Why D wrong: That is mumps."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of chickenpox?","options":["1-2 days before to 4-5 days after appearance of rashes on body","4 days before to 5 days after rashes","7 days before to 7 days after rashes","14-28 days from disease onset"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chickenpox is communicable 1-2 days before to 4-5 days after appearance of rashes.  Why B wrong: That is measles.  Why C wrong: That is rubella.  Why D wrong: That is diphtheria."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of mumps?","options":["4-6 days before symptoms to 7 days thereafter","4 days before to 5 days after rashes","14-28 days from disease onset","7-10 days before and after onset of symptoms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mumps is communicable 4-6 days before symptoms to 7 days thereafter.  Why B wrong: That is measles.  Why C wrong: That is diphtheria.  Why D wrong: That is poliomyelitis."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of diphtheria?","options":["14-28 days from disease onset","4-6 days before symptoms to 7 days thereafter","7-10 days before and after onset","1-2 days before to 1-2 days after onset"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria is communicable 14-28 days from disease onset.  Why B wrong: That is mumps.  Why C wrong: That is poliomyelitis.  Why D wrong: That is influenza."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of influenza?","options":["1-2 days before to 1-2 days after onset of symptoms","7 days after exposure to 3 weeks after paroxysmal stage","14-28 days from disease onset","7-10 days before and after onset"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Influenza is communicable 1-2 days before to 1-2 days after onset of symptoms.  Why B wrong: That is pertussis.  Why C wrong: That is diphtheria.  Why D wrong: That is poliomyelitis."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of pertussis (whooping cough)?","options":["7 days after exposure to 3 weeks after the paroxysmal stage of disease","1-2 days before to 1-2 days after onset","14-28 days from disease onset","2 weeks before to 1 week after onset of jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pertussis is communicable 7 days after exposure to 3 weeks after the paroxysmal stage of disease.  Why B wrong: That is influenza.  Why C wrong: That is diphtheria.  Why D wrong: That is hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of tuberculosis?","options":["As long as viable bacilli are being discharged from the sputum","7-10 days before and after onset of symptoms","14-28 days from disease onset","2 weeks before to 1 week after onset of jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tuberculosis is communicable as long as viable bacilli are being discharged from the sputum.  Why B wrong: That is poliomyelitis.  Why C wrong: That is diphtheria.  Why D wrong: That is hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of poliomyelitis?","options":["7-10 days before and after onset of symptoms","1-2 days before to 1-2 days after onset","14-28 days from disease onset","2 weeks before to 1 week after onset of jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Poliomyelitis is communicable 7-10 days before and after onset of symptoms.  Why B wrong: That is influenza.  Why C wrong: That is diphtheria.  Why D wrong: That is hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of hepatitis A?","options":["2 weeks before to 1 week after onset of jaundice","Till disappearance of HBsAg & appearance of anti-HBs","7-10 days before and after onset","14-28 days from disease onset"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis A is communicable 2 weeks before to 1 week after onset of jaundice.  Why B wrong: That describes hepatitis B.  Why C wrong: That is poliomyelitis.  Why D wrong: That is diphtheria."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"What is the period of communicability of hepatitis B?","options":["Till disappearance of HBsAg & appearance of anti-HBs","2 weeks before to 1 week after onset of jaundice","7-10 days before and after onset","As long as viable bacilli are discharged in sputum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B is communicable till disappearance of HBsAg and appearance of anti-HBs.  Why B wrong: That is hepatitis A.  Why C wrong: That is poliomyelitis.  Why D wrong: That is tuberculosis."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Baby Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFHI) was launched by which organisation globally in 1991?","options":["WHO","UNICEF","FAO","ICN"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BFHI was launched by WHO in 1991 (and in India in 1993).  Why B/C/D wrong: These organisations are not credited with the 1991 launch in the source."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP) was launched in which year?","options":["2003-04","1953","1958","1985"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NVBDCP was launched in 2003-04.  Why B wrong: 1953 was NMCP.  Why C wrong: 1958 was NMEP.  Why D wrong: 1985 was UIP."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP) was launched in which year?","options":["2004","2009","2013","2018"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IDSP was launched in 2004.  Why B wrong: 2009 was NSSK / ICPS.  Why C wrong: 2013 was RBSK / RMNCH+A.  Why D wrong: 2018 was Mission Parivar Vikas era / Anaemia Mukt Bharat."},{"section":"Batch 018 · History of health planning committees in India, Rural Health Scheme…","question":"The Expected Programme for Immunization (EPI) was introduced in which year?","options":["1978","1985","1975","1952"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: EPI (Expected/Expanded Programme for Immunization) was introduced in 1978; UIP followed in 1985.  Why B wrong: 1985 was UIP.  Why C wrong: 1975 was ICDS.  Why D wrong: 1952 was NFPP."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The abbreviation RNTCP used in Indian public health stands for:","options":["Revised National Tuberculosis Control Program","Rural National Tuberculosis Care Program","Regional National TB Coverage Plan","Revised National Typhoid Control Program"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RNTCP = Revised National Tuberculosis Control Program.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are fabricated expansions not matching the standard full form."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In family planning services, PPIUCD stands for:","options":["Post-Partum Intra-Uterine Contraceptive Device","Pre-Pregnancy Intra-Uterine Contraceptive Device","Post-Partum Injectable Uterine Contraceptive Drug","Permanent Post-Uterine Insertion Contraceptive Device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PPIUCD = Post-Partum Intra-Uterine Contraceptive Device.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions of the acronym."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"IUCD is expanded as:","options":["Intra Uterine Contraceptive Device","Internal Uterine Control Device","Injectable Uterine Contraceptive Drug","Intra Umbilical Cord Device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IUCD = Intra Uterine Contraceptive Device.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the recognized expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"RBSK (a child health screening program) stands for:","options":["Rashtriya Bal Suraksha Karyakaram","Rashtriya Balak Sanrakshan Karyakaram","Rural Bal Swasthya Karyakaram","Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakaram"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: RBSK = Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (National Child Health Programme, 0–18 yrs, screening for the 4 Ds). [Note: source prints \"Rashtriya Bal Suraksha Karyakaram\"; the correct official name per MoHFW/NHM uses \"Swasthya\" (health), not \"Suraksha\" — hence option D is keyed.]  Why A/B/C wrong: A is the source's misprint (Suraksha); B and C are fabricated/partial expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"RKSK stands for:","options":["Rashtriya Kishori Suraksha Karyakaram","Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakaram","Rural Kishori Suraksha Kendra","Rashtriya Kanya Suraksha Karyakaram"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RKSK = Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (National Adolescent Health Programme, 2014). [Note: source prints \"Rashtriya Kishori Suraksha Karyakaram\"; the correct official name per MoHFW uses \"Kishor Swasthya\" (adolescent health), not \"Kishori Suraksha\" — hence option B is keyed.]  Why A/C/D wrong: A is the source's misprint (Suraksha); C and D are fabricated expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"RMNCH+A, a strategy under the National Health Mission, stands for:","options":["Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, Child and Adolescent Health/Program","Rural Maternal Newborn Child Health Alliance","Reproductive Mother Newborn Care Health Assistance","Regional Maternal Neonatal Child Health Approach"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RMNCH+A = Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, Child and Adolescent Health/Program.  Why B/C/D wrong: Fabricated expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"NPCDCS is the National Programme for Prevention and Control of:","options":["Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke","Communicable Diseases and Chronic Sickness","Cholera, Dengue, Chikungunya and Scrub typhus","Cancer, Dengue, Cardiac and Skin diseases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NPCDCS = National Programme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect disease sets."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"COPD stands for:","options":["Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disorder","Chronic Obstructive Pancreatic Disease","Congenital Obstructive Pulmonary Defect","Chronic Obtrusive Pleural Disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: COPD = Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disorder.  Why B/C/D wrong: Wrong expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In tuberculosis treatment, DOTS stands for:","options":["Direct Observed Treatment Short-Course","Daily Oral Tuberculosis Schedule","Directly Ordered Therapy Service","Drug Observation and Treatment System"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOTS = Direct Observed Treatment Short-Course.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the standard expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"PMSMA, a maternal health initiative, stands for:","options":["Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan","Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Matritva Abhiyan","Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Mahila Abhiyan","Pradhan Mantri Sampoorna Matritva Abhiyan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMSMA = Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In the cold chain, VVM stands for:","options":["Vaccine Vial Monitor","Vaccine Volume Meter","Viral Vial Marker","Vaccine Validity Monitor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VVM = Vaccine Vial Monitor, a label that indicates heat exposure of a vaccine vial.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct term."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"BMWM stands for:","options":["Bio-Medical Waste Management","Basic Medical Waste Method","Bio-Medical Water Monitoring","Body Mass Weight Measurement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BMWM = Bio-Medical Waste Management.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In the vaccine cold chain, ILR stands for:","options":["Ice Linked Refrigerator","Internal Line Refrigerator","Insulated Low-temperature Reservoir","Ice Layered Rack"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ILR = Ice Linked Refrigerator, used to store vaccines at +2 to +8°C.  Why B/C/D wrong: Fabricated expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"NACO stands for:","options":["National AIDS Control Organization","National Anti-Cancer Organization","National Adolescent Care Office","National Ayush Coordination Organization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NACO = National AIDS Control Organization.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"ELISA, a common serological test, stands for:","options":["Enzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay","Enzyme Level Immuno Serum Assay","Electro Linked Immuno Sorbent Analysis","Enzyme Linked Immunoglobulin Screening Antibody"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ELISA = Enzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"HIV stands for:","options":["Human Immune-deficiency Virus","Human Infectious Viral disease","Hepatic Immuno Virus","Highly Infective Virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HIV = Human Immune-deficiency Virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"AIDS stands for:","options":["Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome","Auto Immune Disease Syndrome","Acquired Infectious Disease Syndrome","Acute Immune Deficiency Sickness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AIDS = Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The injectable contraceptive DMPA stands for:","options":["Depot Medroxyprogesterone Acetate","Daily Medroxyprogesterone Antagonist","Depot Methylprogesterone Acetate","Depot Medroxyprogesterone Antenatal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DMPA = Depot Medroxyprogesterone Acetate.  Why B/C/D wrong: Wrong drug names."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"PCPNDT Act stands for:","options":["Pre-Conception & Pre-Natal Diagnostic Technique Act","Prevention of Child and Prenatal Death Technique Act","Pre-Conception & Post-Natal Diagnostic Test Act","Public Care Prenatal Diagnostic Test Act"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PCPNDT = Pre-Conception & Pre-Natal Diagnostic Technique Act (bans sex determination).  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"MTP Act stands for:","options":["Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act","Maternal Treatment Program Act","Medical Treatment of Pregnancy Act","Maternity Termination Protection Act"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MTP = Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the standard expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"ASHA, the community health worker, stands for:","options":["Accredited Social Health Activist","Auxiliary Social Health Assistant","Accredited Statewide Health Aide","Approved Social Health Advocate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASHA = Accredited Social Health Activist.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"ICDS stands for:","options":["Integrated Child Development Services","Indian Child Disease Surveillance","Integrated Community Development Scheme","Infant Care and Development Service"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICDS = Integrated Child Development Services.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"NRHM stands for:","options":["National Rural Health Mission","National Reproductive Health Movement","National Rural Hygiene Mission","National Regional Health Ministry"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NRHM = National Rural Health Mission.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"IMNCI stands for:","options":["Integrated Management of Neonatal & Childhood Illness","Integrated Maternal & Newborn Care Initiative","Immunization Management of Neonatal & Child Infection","Integrated Management of Nutrition in Children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IMNCI = Integrated Management of Neonatal & Childhood Illness.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"BFHI stands for:","options":["Baby Friendly Hospital Initiative","Breast Feeding Health Initiative","Basic Family Health Improvement","Baby Feeding Health Institute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BFHI = Baby Friendly Hospital Initiative (promotes breastfeeding).  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"WIFS stands for:","options":["Weekly Iron & Folic acid Supplementation Program","Weekly Infant Feeding Scheme","Women's Iron and Folate Screening","Weekly Iodine and Folic acid Supplementation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WIFS = Weekly Iron & Folic acid Supplementation Program.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"CSSM stands for:","options":["Child Survival & Safe Motherhood Program","Community Support for Safe Motherhood","Child Support and Social Motherhood","Child Safety and School Medicine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSSM = Child Survival & Safe Motherhood Program.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"NVBDCP stands for:","options":["National Vector Borne Diseases Control Program","National Viral & Bacterial Disease Control Program","National Vaccine & Blood Disease Control Program","National Vector Biology and Disease Care Program"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NVBDCP = National Vector Borne Diseases Control Program.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"PMSSY stands for:","options":["Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana","Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Swasthya Yojana","Pradhan Mantri Samajik Suraksha Yojana","Pradhan Mantri Shishu Swasthya Yojana"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMSSY = Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"PMJDY, a financial inclusion scheme, stands for:","options":["Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana","Pradhan Mantri Jeevan Daya Yojana","Pradhan Mantri Janani Dhan Yojana","Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yatra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PMJDY = Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"UNICEF stands for:","options":["United Nations International Children Emergency Fund","United Nations Infant Care and Education Fund","Universal Nations Children Emergency Foundation","United Nations Integrated Child Education Fund"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: UNICEF = United Nations International Children Emergency Fund.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"UNESCO stands for:","options":["United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization","United Nations Economic, Social and Cultural Organization","Universal Nations Education and Science Council","United Nations Environmental Science Coordination Organization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: UNESCO = United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The tuberculosis vaccine abbreviation BCG stands for:","options":["Bacille Calmette Guerin","Bacterial Culture Guerin","Bacille Calmette Gram","Basic Cellular Guerin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BCG = Bacille Calmette Guerin, the vaccine against tuberculosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"WHO stands for:","options":["World Health Organization","World Hygiene Office","World Healthcare Organization","Worldwide Health Order"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO = World Health Organization.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In nutritional assessment, MUAC stands for:","options":["Mid-Upper Arm Circumference","Maximum Upper Arm Cutoff","Mid-Upper Abdominal Circumference","Muscle and Upper Arm Content"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MUAC = Mid-Upper Arm Circumference, used to screen for malnutrition.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"AYUSH stands for:","options":["Ayurveda, Yoga and Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homeopathy","Ayurveda, Yoga, Unani, Surgery and Homeopathy","Allopathy, Yoga, Unani, Siddha and Homeopathy","Ayurveda, Yoga, Naturopathy, Unani and Herbal medicine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AYUSH = Ayurveda, Yoga and Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homeopathy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These substitute or omit one of the correct systems."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"As printed in the source, COVID-19 is expanded as:","options":["Co-Corona, Vi-Virus, D-Diseases-2019","Common Viral Disease-2019","Corona Viral Infectious Disease-2019","Contagious Virus Disease-2019"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book expands COVID-19 as Co-Corona, Vi-Virus, D-Diseases-2019 (matching COronaVIrus Disease 2019).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the expansion given in the table."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In documentation, POMR stands for:","options":["Problem Oriented Medical Record","Patient Outcome Monitoring Record","Primary Oriented Medical Report","Progress Oriented Medical Record"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: POMR = Problem Oriented Medical Record.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The diagnostic investigation EEG stands for:","options":["Electro Encephalo Gram","Electro Enteric Gram","Echo Encephalo Graph","Electric Encephalitis Gram"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: EEG = Electro Encephalo Gram (records brain electrical activity).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"CPR stands for:","options":["Cardio-Pulmonary Resuscitation","Cardiac Pressure Recovery","Continuous Pulse Rate","Cardio-Pulmonary Recovery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CPR = Cardio-Pulmonary Resuscitation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"DAMA, a term seen in hospital records, stands for:","options":["Discharge Against Medical Advice","Death After Medical Admission","Discharge After Medical Assessment","Discharge And Medication Advice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DAMA = Discharge Against Medical Advice.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"LAMA stands for:","options":["Leaving Against Medical Advice","Long Acting Medical Agent","Leaving After Medical Assessment","Late Arrival Medical Alert"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: LAMA = Leaving Against Medical Advice.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"VDRL, a test for syphilis, stands for:","options":["Venereal Disease Research Laboratory Test","Viral Disease Rapid Laboratory Test","Venereal Detection and Research Lab","Virus Detection Research Laboratory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VDRL = Venereal Disease Research Laboratory Test.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"TPN stands for:","options":["Total Parenteral Nutrition","Total Protein Nitrogen","Trans-Peritoneal Nutrition","Timed Parenteral Nourishment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TPN = Total Parenteral Nutrition.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"STD stands for:","options":["Sexually Transmitted Diseases","Skin Transmitted Diseases","Systemic Transmissible Disorders","Serum Test for Disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: STD = Sexually Transmitted Diseases.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"RNA stands for:","options":["Ribonucleic Acid","Ribose Nitrogen Acid","Reduced Nucleic Acid","Ribonucleotide Amino acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RNA = Ribonucleic Acid.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"DNA stands for:","options":["Deoxyribonucleic Acid","Dinucleic Acid","Deoxyribose Nitrogen Acid","Double Nucleic Acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DNA = Deoxyribonucleic Acid.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In contraception, OCP stands for:","options":["Oral Contraceptive Pill","Once-daily Contraceptive Patch","Outer Cervical Protection","Optional Contraceptive Program"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: OCP = Oral Contraceptive Pill.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In psychiatric care, ECT stands for:","options":["Electro-Convulsive Therapy","Electro-Cardiac Therapy","Emergency Care Treatment","Electro-Chemical Therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ECT = Electro-Convulsive Therapy.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"ESR stands for:","options":["Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate","Erythrocyte Serum Ratio","Enzyme Sedimentation Reaction","Extra Sensory Response"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ESR = Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The US public-health agency CDC stands for:","options":["Centers For Disease Control and Prevention","Central Disease Control Committee","Community Disease Care Center","Centre for Diagnosis and Cure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CDC = Centers For Disease Control and Prevention.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"CABG, a cardiac surgical procedure, stands for:","options":["Coronary Artery Bypass Graft","Cardiac Arterial Balloon Graft","Coronary Angioplasty and Bypass Graft","Common Artery Bypass Grafting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CABG = Coronary Artery Bypass Graft.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"ICTC, part of HIV/AIDS services, stands for:","options":["Integrated Counselling and Testing Centre","Immunization and Child Treatment Centre","Infectious Care and Treatment Centre","Integrated Community Testing Camp"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICTC = Integrated Counselling and Testing Centre.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The DPT vaccine protects against:","options":["Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus","Diphtheria, Polio, Typhoid","Diphtheria, Pertussis, Typhoid","Dengue, Pertussis, Tetanus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DPT = Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect disease combinations."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The MMR vaccine protects against:","options":["Measles, Mumps, Rubella","Measles, Malaria, Rubella","Mumps, Measles, Rabies","Measles, Mumps, Rotavirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MMR = Measles, Mumps, Rubella.  Why B/C/D wrong: Wrong disease sets."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"BMI stands for:","options":["Body Mass Index","Basal Metabolic Index","Body Muscle Indicator","Body Measurement Index"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BMI = Body Mass Index.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In antenatal care, EDD stands for:","options":["Estimated Date of Delivery","Expected Date of Discharge","Exact Delivery Date","Estimated Day of Development"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: EDD = Estimated Date of Delivery.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"FP-LMIS stands for:","options":["Family Planning Logistics Management Information System","Family Planning Local Medical Information Service","Fertility Program Logistics Monitoring Information System","Family Planning Learning and Monitoring Information System"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FP-LMIS = Family Planning Logistics Management Information System.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the correct expansion."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The causative organism of small pox is:","options":["Variola virus","Varicella zoster virus","Measles virus","Vibrio cholerae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Small pox is caused by the Variola virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Varicella zoster causes chickenpox; measles virus causes measles; Vibrio cholerae causes cholera."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of small pox is:","options":["11–14 days","1–4 days","21–28 days","2–5 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table lists small pox incubation as 11–14 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incubation periods of other diseases (swine flu, none, diphtheria)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The characteristic rash pattern in small pox is:","options":["Centrifugal","Centripetal","Symmetrical only on trunk","No fixed pattern"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Small pox rash follows a centrifugal pattern (more on extremities/face).  Why B/C/D wrong: Centripetal is the chickenpox pattern; the others are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The causative organism of chickenpox is:","options":["Varicella Zoster Virus","Variola virus","Herpes simplex virus","Rubella virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chickenpox is caused by Varicella Zoster Virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Variola causes small pox; the others are unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of chickenpox is:","options":["14–16 days","11–14 days","1–4 days","45–180 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives chickenpox incubation as 14–16 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to small pox, swine flu, and hepatitis B respectively."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The rash of chickenpox characteristically follows which distribution pattern?","options":["Centripetal","Centrifugal","Only on palms and soles","Linear along dermatomes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chickenpox rash is centripetal (more on the trunk than extremities).  Why B/C/D wrong: Centrifugal is small pox; the others are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Small pox, chickenpox, mumps and diphtheria all share which mode of transmission per the table?","options":["Droplet","Feco-oral route","Mosquito bite","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists droplet transmission for small pox, chickenpox, mumps, diphtheria, pertussis and meningococcal meningitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Feco-oral is for polio/cholera/hepatitis A/typhoid; mosquito for dengue/malaria/JE; blood for hepatitis B."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The causative organism of measles (Rubeola) is:","options":["Measles virus (Paramyxovirus)","Rubella virus","Mumps virus","Variola virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measles is caused by the Measles virus, a Paramyxovirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Those cause rubella, mumps, and small pox respectively."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of measles is:","options":["10–14 days","14–21 days","1–4 days","45–180 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives measles incubation as 10–14 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are periods for rubella/mumps, swine flu, and hepatitis B."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The mode of transmission of measles is:","options":["Air borne and droplet","Feco-oral route","Mosquito bite","Sexual contact"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measles spreads via air borne and droplet routes (also true of swine flu in the table).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the routes listed for measles."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Koplik's spots are a diagnostic feature seen in:","options":["Measles","Mumps","Rubella","Chickenpox"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table notes Koplik's spots are seen in measles.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not associated with Koplik's spots."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Rubella (German measles) is caused by:","options":["An RNA virus of the toga virus family","A DNA poxvirus","Mumps virus","Corynebacterium diphtheriae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rubella is caused by an RNA virus of the toga (Togavirus) family.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect organisms for rubella."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of rubella (German measles) is:","options":["14–21 days","10–14 days","3–10 days","1–4 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives rubella incubation as 14–21 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are periods for measles/typhoid, meningococcal meningitis, and swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"According to the table, congenital rubella syndrome develops in the baby if maternal infection occurs during:","options":["The first week of pregnancy (first trimester)","The last week of pregnancy","Labour","Lactation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark states congenital rubella syndrome occurs if infection happens during the first week/trimester of pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These timings are not associated with congenital rubella syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Mumps is caused by:","options":["Mumps virus (Paramyxovirus)","Rubella virus","Measles virus","Neisseria meningitidis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mumps is caused by the Mumps virus, a Paramyxovirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect organisms."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of mumps is:","options":["14–21 days","3–10 days","1–4 days","2–5 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives mumps incubation as 14–21 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to other diseases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In mumps, pain and swelling characteristically occur in which glands?","options":["Parotid glands","Thyroid gland","Submandibular lymph nodes only","Adrenal glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mumps causes pain and swelling of the parotid glands.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the glands involved in mumps."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The most common complication of mumps is:","options":["Orchitis","Encephalitis","Deafness","Pancreatitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table states the most common complication of mumps is orchitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These can occur but the table names orchitis as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Swine flu is caused by:","options":["H1N1 virus","H5N1 virus","Corona virus","Lyssa virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Swine flu is caused by the H1N1 virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause other diseases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of swine flu is:","options":["1–4 days","11–14 days","14–21 days","15–45 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives swine flu incubation as 1–4 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to small pox, mumps/rubella/polio, and hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"According to the table, the first case of H1N1 (swine flu) was reported in India in:","options":["May 2009","2015","2019","2014"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark states the first case of H1N1 in India was reported in May 2009.  Why B/C/D wrong: 2015 was the outbreak year; 2019/2014 refer to other diseases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"As per the table, a major H1N1 (swine flu) outbreak occurred in India in 2015, primarily in which states?","options":["Gujarat & Rajasthan","Kerala & Tamil Nadu","Bihar & Jharkhand","Punjab & Haryana"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark notes the 2015 H1N1 outbreak in India was in Gujarat & Rajasthan.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the states mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Diphtheria is caused by:","options":["Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Bordetella pertussis","Clostridium tetani","Salmonella typhi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria is caused by Corynebacterium diphtheriae.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause pertussis, tetanus, and typhoid respectively."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of diphtheria is:","options":["2–5 days","7–14 days","14–16 days","45–180 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives diphtheria incubation as 2–5 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to pertussis, chickenpox, and hepatitis B."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Which test is used for the diagnosis of diphtheria?","options":["Schick test","Widal test","Mantoux test","VDRL test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Schick test is used to diagnose diphtheria (susceptibility).  Why B/C/D wrong: Widal is for typhoid; Mantoux for TB; VDRL for syphilis."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"A pseudo membrane characteristically occurs in which disease?","options":["Diphtheria","Pertussis","Cholera","Malaria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table notes a pseudo membrane occurs in diphtheria.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not associated with a pseudo membrane."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Pertussis (whooping cough) is caused by:","options":["Bordetella pertussis","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Vibrio cholerae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pertussis is caused by Bordetella pertussis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause diphtheria, TB, and cholera."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of pertussis is:","options":["7–14 days","2–5 days","14–21 days","1–4 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives pertussis incubation as 7–14 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to diphtheria, mumps/rubella, and swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Pertussis is also commonly known as:","options":["100 day cough / whooping cough","Break bone fever","Serum hepatitis","Hydrophobia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark gives pertussis the other name \"100 day cough, whooping cough.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other names for dengue, hepatitis B, and rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Tuberculosis is caused by:","options":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Bordetella pertussis","Neisseria meningitidis","Clostridium tetani"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause pertussis, meningococcal meningitis, and tetanus."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of tuberculosis is described in the table as:","options":["Weeks to months","1–4 days","2–5 days","Months to years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table lists TB incubation as \"weeks to months.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: \"Months to years\" is rabies; the others are shorter fixed periods."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The mode of transmission of tuberculosis is:","options":["Airborne","Feco-oral route","Mosquito bite","Through wounds and soil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB is transmitted by the airborne route.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are routes for other diseases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"According to the table, the cardinal signs of tuberculosis are:","options":["Low grade fever, cough, weight loss","Rice water stools","Break bone joint pain","Hydrophobia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB cardinal signs are low grade fever, cough, and weight loss.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe cholera, dengue, and rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Meningococcal meningitis is caused by:","options":["Neisseria meningitidis","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Vibrio cholerae","Salmonella typhi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Meningococcal meningitis is caused by Neisseria meningitidis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause TB, cholera, and typhoid."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of meningococcal meningitis is:","options":["3–10 days","14–21 days","45–180 days","1–4 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives meningococcal meningitis incubation as 3–10 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to other diseases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"According to the table, the most common age for bacterial meningitis is:","options":["Less than 5 years","5–15 years","Above 60 years","20–40 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark states the most common age for bacterial meningitis is <5 years.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the age group stated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Poliomyelitis is caused by:","options":["Polio virus","Lyssa virus","Variola virus","Group-B arbovirus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Poliomyelitis is caused by the Polio virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause rabies, small pox, and Japanese encephalitis."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of poliomyelitis is:","options":["14–21 days","3–10 days","1–4 days","Months to years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives polio incubation as 14–21 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to meningococcal meningitis, swine flu, and rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The mode of transmission of poliomyelitis is:","options":["Feco-oral route","Droplet","Airborne","Mosquito bite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Polio spreads via the feco-oral route (as do cholera, hepatitis A, and typhoid in the table).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the route for polio."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"According to the table, poliomyelitis was declared worldwide eradicated on:","options":["27 March 2014","25 April 2014","1 January 2011","8 May 2009"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table's remark gives the date as 27 March 2014. [Note: source says \"worldwide eradicated\"; factually polio is NOT globally eradicated (only smallpox is) — 27 March 2014 is the date WHO certified the South-East Asia Region, including India, polio-free.]  Why B/C/D wrong: 25 April is World Malaria Day; other dates are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Cholera is caused by:","options":["Vibrio cholerae","Salmonella typhi","Polio virus","RNA Hep-A virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cholera is caused by Vibrio cholerae.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause typhoid, polio, and hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of cholera as given in the table is:","options":["Few hours to days","15–45 days","14–21 days","3–21 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table lists cholera incubation as few hours to days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to hepatitis A, polio, and tetanus."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The cardinal sign of cholera is:","options":["Rice water stool","Koplik's spots","Whooping cough","Hydrophobia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of cholera is rice water stool.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to measles, pertussis, and rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Hepatitis A is caused by:","options":["RNA Hep-A virus","DNA Hep-B virus","Salmonella typhi","Vibrio cholerae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis A is caused by an RNA Hep-A virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: DNA virus causes hepatitis B; the others cause typhoid and cholera."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of hepatitis A is:","options":["15–45 days","45–180 days","10–14 days","1–4 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives hepatitis A incubation as 15–45 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: 45–180 days is hepatitis B; 10–14 days is typhoid; 1–4 days is swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Hepatitis A is also known as:","options":["Infectious hepatitis","Serum hepatitis","Enteric fever","Break bone fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The other name for hepatitis A is infectious hepatitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Serum hepatitis = hepatitis B; enteric fever = typhoid; break bone fever = dengue."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Hepatitis B is caused by:","options":["DNA Hep-B virus","RNA Hep-A virus","Vibrio cholerae","Salmonella typhi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B is caused by a DNA Hep-B virus.  Why B/C/D wrong: RNA virus causes hepatitis A; the others cause cholera and typhoid."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of hepatitis B is:","options":["45–180 days","15–45 days","10–14 days","5–6 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives hepatitis B incubation as 45–180 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: 15–45 days is hepatitis A; 10–14 days is typhoid; 5–6 days is dengue."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The mode of transmission of hepatitis B includes:","options":["Sexual, blood and perinatal","Feco-oral route","Mosquito bite","Airborne droplet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis B spreads through sexual contact, blood, and perinatally.  Why B/C/D wrong: Feco-oral is hepatitis A; the others are unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Hepatitis B is also known as:","options":["Serum hepatitis","Infectious hepatitis","Enteric fever","Hydrophobia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The other name for hepatitis B is serum hepatitis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Infectious hepatitis = hepatitis A; enteric fever = typhoid; hydrophobia = rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Typhoid fever is caused by:","options":["Salmonella typhi","Vibrio cholerae","Shigella dysenteriae","RNA Hep-A virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Typhoid fever is caused by Salmonella typhi.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause cholera, dysentery, and hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of typhoid fever is:","options":["10–14 days","45–180 days","5–6 days","3–21 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives typhoid incubation as 10–14 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to hepatitis B, dengue, and tetanus."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Typhoid fever is also known as, and diagnosed by, respectively:","options":["Enteric fever; Widal test","Serum hepatitis; VDRL test","Break bone fever; Schick test","Infectious hepatitis; Mantoux test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Typhoid's other name is enteric fever, and it is diagnosed by the Widal test.  Why B/C/D wrong: These pair names/tests of other diseases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Dengue fever is caused by:","options":["Dengue virus (arbovirus)","Plasmodium parasite","Group-B arbovirus","H1N1 virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dengue is caused by the dengue virus, an arbovirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause malaria, Japanese encephalitis, and swine flu."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of dengue fever is:","options":["5–6 days","4–14 days","10–14 days","15–45 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives dengue incubation as 5–6 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: 4–14 days is JE; 10–14 days is typhoid; 15–45 days is hepatitis A."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Dengue fever is transmitted by:","options":["Aedes aegypti mosquito","Anopheles (female) mosquito","Culex mosquito","Sandfly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dengue is transmitted by the Aedes aegypti mosquito.  Why B/C/D wrong: Anopheles transmits malaria; Culex transmits JE; sandfly transmits kala-azar."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Dengue fever is also known as:","options":["Break bone fever","Enteric fever","Serum hepatitis","Hydrophobia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dengue's other name is break bone fever.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other names for typhoid, hepatitis B, and rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Malaria is caused by:","options":["Plasmodium parasite","Dengue virus","Group-B arbovirus","Vibrio cholerae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Malaria is caused by the Plasmodium parasite.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause dengue, JE, and cholera."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of malaria, per the table, is best described as:","options":["Depends on parasite","Fixed at 5–6 days","Fixed at 10–14 days","Months to years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states malaria incubation \"depends on parasite\" (species-dependent).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are fixed values for other diseases."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Malaria is transmitted by which vector?","options":["Anopheles (female) mosquito","Aedes aegypti mosquito","Culex mosquito","Housefly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Malaria is transmitted by the female Anopheles mosquito.  Why B/C/D wrong: Aedes transmits dengue; Culex transmits JE."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"World Malaria Day is observed on:","options":["25th April","27th March","1st December","24th March"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark states World Malaria Day is observed on 25th April.  Why B/C/D wrong: 27 March relates to polio; 1 Dec is World AIDS Day; 24 March is World TB Day."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Japanese encephalitis is caused by:","options":["Group-B arbovirus","Plasmodium parasite","Dengue virus","Lyssa virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Japanese encephalitis is caused by a Group-B arbovirus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause malaria, dengue, and rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of Japanese encephalitis is:","options":["4–14 days","5–6 days","1–4 days","3–21 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives JE incubation as 4–14 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: 5–6 days is dengue; 1–4 days is swine flu; 3–21 days is tetanus."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Japanese encephalitis is transmitted by which mosquito?","options":["Culex mosquito","Aedes aegypti mosquito","Anopheles mosquito","Mansonia mosquito"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: JE is transmitted by the Culex mosquito.  Why B/C/D wrong: Aedes transmits dengue; Anopheles transmits malaria."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In Japanese encephalitis, the incidental \"dead end\" host is:","options":["Man","Pigs","Wading birds","Mosquito"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark identifies Man as the incidental \"dead end\" host in JE.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pigs are the (most common) amplifier host and wading birds also amplify; the mosquito is the vector."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In Japanese encephalitis, the most common amplifier host is:","options":["Pigs","Man","Dogs","Cattle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark states pigs are the most common amplifier host (wading birds also amplify).  Why B/C/D wrong: Man is the dead-end host; dogs/cattle are not the amplifier host in JE."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Tetanus is caused by:","options":["Clostridium tetani","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Bordetella pertussis","Lyssa virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tetanus is caused by Clostridium tetani.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause diphtheria, pertussis, and rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of tetanus is:","options":["3–21 days","5–6 days","10–14 days","Months to years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives tetanus incubation as 3–21 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: 5–6 days is dengue; 10–14 days is typhoid; months to years is rabies."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The mode of transmission of tetanus is:","options":["Through wounds, cuts and soil","Mosquito bite","Feco-oral route","Droplet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tetanus is transmitted through wounds, cuts, and soil.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the routes for tetanus."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"In tetanus, the body system primarily affected is the:","options":["Nervous system","Respiratory system","Renal system","Gastrointestinal system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark states the nervous system is primarily affected in tetanus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the primarily affected system."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Rabies is caused by:","options":["Lyssa virus type-1","Group-B arbovirus","Clostridium tetani","Polio virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rabies is caused by Lyssa virus type-1.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cause JE, tetanus, and polio."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"The incubation period of rabies is:","options":["Months to years","3–21 days","14–21 days","5–6 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Table gives rabies incubation as months to years.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are periods for tetanus, polio, and dengue."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Rabies is transmitted through infected animals, most commonly:","options":["Dogs (90%)","Cats only","Rodents","Cattle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states rabies spreads through infected animals like dogs (90%), monkeys and others.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dogs account for ~90% of cases; the others are minor sources."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Which statements about rabies are correct per the table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Its other name is hydrophobia","It is a 100% fatal disease","It is caused by Lyssa virus type-1","It is transmitted by the Culex mosquito","Its incubation period is fixed at 5–6 days"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists rabies' other name as hydrophobia, notes it is 100% fatal, and gives Lyssa virus type-1 as the cause.  Why D/E wrong: Rabies spreads through infected animal bites (not Culex); its incubation is months to years (not 5–6 days)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Which of the following diseases are transmitted by the feco-oral route according to the table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Poliomyelitis","Cholera","Hepatitis A","Typhoid fever","Hepatitis B"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Polio, cholera, hepatitis A, and typhoid are all listed with feco-oral transmission.  Why E wrong: Hepatitis B is transmitted sexually, via blood, and perinatally — not feco-oral."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Full forms of important CHN abbreviations/terms…","question":"Which vector–disease pairings are correct per the table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Aedes aegypti — Dengue fever","Anopheles (female) — Malaria","Culex — Japanese encephalitis","Aedes aegypti — Malaria","Anopheles — Japanese encephalitis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Dengue is spread by Aedes aegypti, malaria by female Anopheles, and JE by Culex.  Why D/E wrong: Malaria is Anopheles (not Aedes); JE is Culex (not Anopheles)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A researcher wants to study the cause of a rare disease (prevalence <1 in 10,000) with a long latency. Which study design is MOST appropriate as the first-line choice?","options":["Prospective cohort study","Case-control study","Cross-sectional survey","Randomized controlled trial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Case-control studies start with the outcome and look back for exposure, so they are efficient for rare diseases and long-latency conditions — you can assemble enough cases without following a huge population for years.  Why A wrong: A prospective cohort for a rare disease would require an impractically enormous sample and very long follow-up to accrue enough cases.  Why C wrong: A cross-sectional survey measures exposure and outcome at one point in time (prevalence) and cannot efficiently capture a rare disease or establish temporality.  Why D wrong: An RCT tests interventions, not etiology of a naturally occurring rare disease, and would be unethical/impractical here.  High-Yield Takeaway: Rare DISEASE -> case-control; rare EXPOSURE -> cohort. This reciprocal pairing is a classic NORCET trap."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"In a cohort study, 100 smokers and 100 non-smokers are followed. Lung disease develops in 40 smokers and 10 non-smokers. What is the relative risk?","options":["2.0","3.0","4.0","6.0"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Incidence in exposed = 40/100 = 0.40; incidence in unexposed = 10/100 = 0.10. RR = 0.40 / 0.10 = 4.0.  Why A wrong: 2.0 comes from wrongly subtracting-then-halving or misreading the counts.  Why B wrong: 3.0 is the attributable-risk ratio confusion (0.30/0.10 = 3), which is not RR.  Why D wrong: 6.0 is not obtainable from these figures.  High-Yield Takeaway: RR = incidence in exposed / incidence in unexposed, and RR is only calculable from a cohort study (which gives true incidence), never directly from a case-control study."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"In the same cohort (smoker incidence 0.40, non-smoker incidence 0.10), what is the attributable risk percent (etiologic fraction in the exposed)?","options":["30%","50%","75%","90%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: AR% = (RR - 1)/RR x 100 = (4 - 1)/4 x 100 = 75%. Equivalently (0.40 - 0.10)/0.40 = 0.75. It means 75% of disease in smokers is attributable to smoking.  Why A wrong: 30% (0.40 - 0.10 = 0.30) is the absolute attributable risk, not the percent.  Why B wrong: 50% is not derivable from these values.  Why D wrong: 90% overstates the exposed fraction attributable to smoking.  High-Yield Takeaway: Attributable risk (absolute) = Ie - Iu; Attributable risk % = (RR-1)/RR. Population attributable risk uses total-population incidence instead."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A case-control study of oral cancer finds: among 100 cases, 80 chewed tobacco; among 100 controls, 40 chewed tobacco. What is the odds ratio?","options":["2.0","4.0","6.0","8.0"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Table: cases exposed a=80, cases unexposed c=20, controls exposed b=40, controls unexposed d=60. OR = ad/bc = (80 x 60)/(40 x 20) = 4800/800 = 6.  Why A wrong: 2.0 is the crude ratio of exposed proportions (80/40), not the odds ratio.  Why B wrong: 4.0 ignores the unexposed cells.  Why D wrong: 8.0 results from a cell-transposition error.  High-Yield Takeaway: In case-control studies you compute the ODDS RATIO (ad/bc), not RR, because incidence cannot be measured. OR approximates RR only when the disease is rare (rare-disease assumption)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A screening test for a disease with 10% prevalence is applied to 1,000 people. The 2x2 result is: True positives 90, False positives 90, False negatives 10, True negatives 810. What is the positive predictive value (PPV)?","options":["50%","90%","98.8%","44.4%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PPV = TP/(TP+FP) = 90/(90+90) = 90/180 = 50%.  Why B wrong: 90% is the sensitivity [TP/(TP+FN) = 90/100], not PPV.  Why C wrong: 98.8% is the NPV [TN/(TN+FN) = 810/820].  Why D wrong: 44.4% is a distractor from a different table.  High-Yield Takeaway: PPV depends on prevalence; sensitivity/specificity do not. Read the question stem carefully — examiners deliberately mix the four cells."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"The same test (sensitivity 90%, specificity 90%) is now used in a population where prevalence is only 1%. What happens to the PPV compared to the 10%-prevalence setting?","options":["PPV rises above 50%","PPV stays at 50% because sensitivity/specificity are unchanged","PPV falls to roughly 8-9%","PPV becomes 100% because the test is highly specific"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: At 1% prevalence in 1,000 people: 10 diseased (TP=9, FN=1), 990 healthy (FP=99, TN=891). PPV = 9/(9+99) = 9/108 = 8.3%. Lower prevalence means far more false positives relative to true positives.  Why A wrong: PPV moves in the SAME direction as prevalence — it falls, not rises.  Why B wrong: PPV is prevalence-dependent even though sensitivity/specificity are intrinsic and fixed.  Why D wrong: 90% specificity still generates many false positives in a large healthy population.  High-Yield Takeaway: The classic screening trap — applying a good test to a low-prevalence population yields a poor PPV (most positives are false). This is why we screen high-risk groups."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"For a screening test, if the cut-off level defining a \"positive\" result is raised (made more stringent), what happens?","options":["Sensitivity increases, specificity decreases","Sensitivity decreases, specificity increases","Both sensitivity and specificity increase","Both remain unchanged because they are fixed test properties"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Raising the cut-off classifies fewer people as positive, so more true cases are missed (sensitivity falls) but fewer healthy people are wrongly flagged (specificity rises). There is an inherent sensitivity-specificity trade-off.  Why A wrong: This is the reverse — it describes LOWERING the cut-off.  Why C wrong: You cannot increase both by moving a single cut-off; one is traded for the other.  Why D wrong: Sensitivity and specificity change with the chosen cut-off point on the same test.  High-Yield Takeaway: Lower cut-off -> catches more cases (high sensitivity, good for ruling OUT / initial screening); higher cut-off -> high specificity (good for confirmation). The ROC curve maps this trade-off."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"When two screening tests are used in SEQUENCE (serial testing, test B only on those positive on test A), what is the net effect?","options":["Net sensitivity increases, net specificity decreases","Net specificity increases, net sensitivity decreases","Both sensitivity and specificity are unaffected","Only PPV falls, specificity is unchanged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Serial (sequential) testing requires BOTH tests positive to be called positive, which raises specificity and PPV but lowers overall sensitivity (a person missed by either test is called negative).  Why A wrong: That describes PARALLEL testing (either test positive = positive), which raises sensitivity and lowers specificity.  Why C wrong: Combining tests definitely alters the net operating characteristics.  Why D wrong: Specificity does change (it increases) with serial testing.  High-Yield Takeaway: Serial testing -> up specificity (confirm disease, minimize false positives); Parallel testing -> up sensitivity (rule out disease in emergencies, minimize missed cases)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A new screening program appears to improve 5-year survival for a cancer, but the age-specific mortality rate in the population does not change. This apparent benefit is BEST explained by:","options":["Length-time bias","Lead-time bias","Selection bias","Recall bias"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lead-time bias is the spurious increase in survival time that occurs merely because screening detects disease earlier — the clock starts sooner, but death is not postponed, so mortality is unchanged.  Why A wrong: Length-time bias is over-detection of slowly-progressive (indolent) cases by screening, a different mechanism.  Why C wrong: Selection bias arises from non-representative sampling into study groups.  Why D wrong: Recall bias is differential accuracy of past-exposure recall, typical of case-control studies.  High-Yield Takeaway: If screening improves SURVIVAL but not MORTALITY, suspect lead-time bias. Mortality (not survival) is the valid endpoint for judging a screening program."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A screening program preferentially detects slow-growing, indolent tumours that would never have caused harm, making the program look beneficial. This is:","options":["Lead-time bias","Length-time bias","Confounding","Berksonian bias"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Length-time bias occurs because slowly progressive disease has a longer detectable pre-clinical phase and is thus more likely to be caught by periodic screening; these good-prognosis cases inflate apparent survival. Overdiagnosis is its extreme form.  Why A wrong: Lead-time bias is about earlier detection advancing the diagnosis date, not selective detection of indolent disease.  Why C wrong: Confounding is a third-variable distortion of an exposure-outcome association.  Why D wrong: Berksonian bias is a selection bias specific to hospital-based case-control studies.  High-Yield Takeaway: Length-time bias -> indolent/slow cases over-represented in screen-detected group; its most extreme manifestation is overdiagnosis."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A test gives consistent, reproducible results on repeat measurement but the values are systematically far from the true value. The test has:","options":["High validity, low reliability","High reliability, low validity","High validity and high reliability","Low reliability and low validity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reliability (reproducibility/precision) is high because results are consistent; validity (accuracy) is low because the values are systematically off from truth (systematic error/bias).  Why A wrong: Reversed — consistency is reliability, closeness to truth is validity.  Why C wrong: A consistent but wrong result is not valid.  Why D wrong: The results ARE consistent, so reliability is high.  High-Yield Takeaway: Reliability = precision/consistency; Validity = accuracy/truth. A test can be reliably wrong (precise but biased). Kappa statistic quantifies reliability/agreement beyond chance."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"In a village, prevalence of a chronic disease is rising over years even though the incidence (new cases per year) is stable. The MOST likely explanation is:","options":["An acute increase in exposure","Improved treatment prolonging survival (longer disease duration)","A fall in the case-fatality rate to zero","A rise in the crude birth rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prevalence = Incidence x Duration (P = I x D). With incidence constant, rising prevalence must reflect longer average duration — typically because better treatment keeps patients alive longer with the disease (not cured).  Why A wrong: Increased exposure would raise incidence, but incidence is stated to be stable.  Why C wrong: Case-fatality reaching zero is an extreme; the point is prolonged survival, and this option is not the standard framing.  Why D wrong: Birth rate affects the denominator population, not this chronic-disease prevalence dynamic in the described way.  High-Yield Takeaway: P = I x D. Prevalence rises if people live longer with disease OR incidence rises; it falls with cure or rapid death. A better therapy that prolongs life without curing INCREASES prevalence."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"After a community feast, 50 of 200 attendees who ate the chicken salad developed gastroenteritis. What is the food-specific attack rate for chicken salad?","options":["10%","25%","40%","50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Attack rate = (number ill among exposed / total exposed) x 100 = (50/200) x 100 = 25%.  Why A wrong: 10% misreads the denominator.  Why C wrong: 40% is not obtainable from 50/200.  Why D wrong: 50 is the number ill, not the rate.  High-Yield Takeaway: Attack rate is a special cumulative incidence used in outbreaks over a short defined period. In food-poisoning investigations, the food item with the highest attack rate (and the largest difference between ate-vs-did-not-eat rates) is the likely vehicle."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"An epidemic curve shows a single sharp peak with all cases occurring within one incubation period of each other. This pattern indicates:","options":["A propagated (person-to-person) epidemic","A point-source (common-source, single-exposure) epidemic","A continuous common-source epidemic","An endemic disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A point-source outbreak arises from a single common exposure at one point in time; all cases appear within the range of one incubation period, producing one sharp peak (e.g., a contaminated meal at a wedding).  Why A wrong: A propagated epidemic shows a series of successively taller peaks separated by roughly one incubation period, reflecting person-to-person spread.  Why C wrong: A continuous common-source epidemic has a prolonged plateau because exposure persists over time.  Why D wrong: Endemic refers to the constant baseline presence of disease, not an outbreak curve.  High-Yield Takeaway: Point source = single sharp peak within one incubation period; Propagated = multiple progressively rising peaks about one incubation period apart. The width of a point-source curve can be used to estimate the incubation period."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"According to Park/standard epidemiology, which is the FIRST step in the investigation of an epidemic?","options":["Formulate and test the hypothesis","Verification of the diagnosis","Institute control measures","Write the final report"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The investigation begins with verification of the diagnosis (confirming cases are truly the suspected disease, using clinical + lab criteria), followed by confirming the existence of an epidemic.  Why A wrong: Hypothesis formulation and testing comes after defining and counting cases and doing descriptive epidemiology.  Why C wrong: Control measures follow once the source/mode is identified (though control and investigation often proceed in parallel), not as the first analytic step.  Why D wrong: Report writing is the final step.  High-Yield Takeaway: Sequence (Park): verify diagnosis -> confirm epidemic exists -> define & count cases (case definition) -> describe by time/place/person -> formulate & test hypothesis -> control measures -> report. Verification of diagnosis is step one."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"In a randomized trial, a drug reduces the risk of stroke from 10% (control) to 5% (treated). What is the number needed to treat (NNT)?","options":["5","10","20","50"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Absolute risk reduction (ARR) = 10% - 5% = 5% = 0.05. NNT = 1/ARR = 1/0.05 = 20. You must treat 20 patients to prevent one stroke.  Why A wrong: 5 confuses the ARR percentage value with NNT.  Why B wrong: 10 is the control event rate, not NNT.  Why D wrong: 50 comes from using 1/0.02 or another miscalculation.  High-Yield Takeaway: NNT = 1/ARR (use the ABSOLUTE, not relative, risk reduction). A relative risk reduction of 50% sounds impressive but here yields an NNT of 20 — always ask for the absolute difference."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A study reports that people who carry matches are more likely to develop lung cancer. Matches do not cause cancer; the association is driven by smoking (matches co-occur with smoking, which causes cancer). Smoking here is a:","options":["Effect modifier","Confounder","Mediator on the causal pathway","Source of information bias"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A confounder is associated with the exposure (match-carrying) AND is an independent risk factor for the outcome (lung cancer) AND is not on the causal pathway between them — smoking fits all three criteria.  Why A wrong: An effect modifier changes the strength of a true exposure-outcome effect across strata; here there is no true match-cancer effect to modify.  Why C wrong: A mediator lies ON the causal pathway; matches do not cause smoking, so smoking is not a mediator of a match effect.  Why D wrong: This is confounding, not measurement/information bias.  High-Yield Takeaway: Confounder criteria: (1) associated with exposure, (2) independent risk factor for outcome, (3) NOT an intermediate step in the causal chain. Control it by randomization, restriction, matching (design) or stratification/multivariable adjustment (analysis)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"Which of the Bradford Hill criteria for causation is considered the ONLY absolutely essential (necessary) one?","options":["Strength of association","Biological gradient (dose-response)","Temporality","Specificity"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Temporality — the cause must precede the effect — is the only criterion that is absolutely essential; without it causation is impossible.  Why A wrong: Strong associations support causation but strength is not mandatory (weak causal effects exist).  Why B wrong: A dose-response relationship strengthens the argument but is not always present or required.  Why D wrong: Specificity (one exposure -> one disease) is the weakest criterion and often absent for multifactorial diseases.  High-Yield Takeaway: Of Hill's nine criteria (strength, consistency, specificity, temporality, biological gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, analogy), TEMPORALITY is the single indispensable one."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A cross-sectional survey finds that people with depression have lower vitamin D levels. The KEY limitation preventing a causal conclusion is that a cross-sectional study:","options":["Cannot calculate prevalence","Cannot establish temporality (which came first)","Is only suitable for rare diseases","Always requires a control group followed over time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cross-sectional studies measure exposure and outcome simultaneously, so they cannot tell whether low vitamin D preceded depression or resulted from it — temporality is unknowable, precluding causal inference.  Why A wrong: Cross-sectional studies are in fact the standard design for measuring PREVALENCE.  Why C wrong: They are best for common conditions, not rare diseases.  Why D wrong: Following subjects over time describes a cohort study, not cross-sectional.  High-Yield Takeaway: Cross-sectional = \"snapshot\" -> good for prevalence and hypothesis-generation, but the chicken-or-egg (reverse causality) problem means it cannot prove cause and effect."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"To compare mortality between two towns with very different age structures, an epidemiologist applies the age-specific death rates of each town to a single standard population. This method is:","options":["Indirect standardization","Direct standardization","Calculation of the standardized mortality ratio (SMR)","Proportional mortality"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Direct standardization applies the study population's age-specific rates to a chosen standard population, yielding an age-adjusted rate that removes the confounding effect of differing age structures.  Why A wrong: Indirect standardization does the reverse — it applies standard-population age-specific rates to the study population's age structure, used when age-specific rates in the study group are unstable or unavailable.  Why C wrong: The SMR is the OUTPUT of indirect standardization (observed/expected deaths), not this method.  Why D wrong: Proportional mortality is the fraction of deaths due to a specific cause, unrelated to age adjustment.  High-Yield Takeaway: Direct standardization needs age-specific rates of the study group; indirect standardization is used when those are unavailable and produces the SMR (observed deaths / expected deaths)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A hospital-based case-control study of a disease uses hospitalized patients as both cases and controls, distorting the exposure-disease association because hospitalization rates differ by exposure. This is:","options":["Recall bias","Berksonian (Berkson's) bias","Lead-time bias","Neyman (prevalence-incidence) bias"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Berkson's bias is a selection bias unique to hospital-based studies: the probability of hospitalization differs for cases, controls, and by exposure, creating a spurious association not present in the general population.  Why A wrong: Recall bias is differential accuracy of recalling past exposures between cases and controls.  Why C wrong: Lead-time bias is a screening phenomenon.  Why D wrong: Neyman bias arises when prevalent (survivor) cases rather than incident cases are studied, over-representing mild/long-surviving cases.  High-Yield Takeaway: Hospital controls -> think Berkson's bias. Prefer population-based controls where feasible. Neyman bias = studying survivors (prevalent cases) instead of new (incident) cases."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"In a cohort study measuring disease per unit of follow-up time, 20 new cases occur over a total of 400 person-years of observation. The incidence density (rate) is:","options":["5 per 100 person-years","20 per 100 person-years","5 per 100 persons","50 per 1,000 population"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incidence density = new cases / total person-time = 20 / 400 person-years = 0.05 = 5 per 100 person-years.  Why B wrong: 20 per 100 misuses the case count as the numerator rate.  Why C wrong: \"per 100 persons\" describes cumulative incidence (a proportion), whereas person-time gives a rate.  Why D wrong: 50 per 1,000 (5%) mislabels the measure and drops the time dimension.  High-Yield Takeaway: Incidence density (person-time rate) has time in the denominator and handles varying follow-up/loss; cumulative incidence (risk) is cases divided by population-at-risk over a fixed period (a dimensionless proportion 0-1)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"Which vital statistic is defined as the number of deaths of infants under 28 days of age per 1,000 live births in a year?","options":["Infant mortality rate","Neonatal mortality rate","Perinatal mortality rate","Post-neonatal mortality rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neonatal mortality rate (NMR) = deaths under 28 days / 1,000 live births.  Why A wrong: IMR counts deaths under 1 year (365 days) per 1,000 live births.  Why C wrong: Perinatal mortality = late fetal deaths (stillbirths >=28 weeks) + early neonatal deaths (first 7 days) per 1,000 TOTAL births.  Why D wrong: Post-neonatal mortality covers 28 days to under 1 year per 1,000 live births.  High-Yield Takeaway: NMR (<28 days) is subdivided into early neonatal (<7 days) and late neonatal (7-<28 days). A high NMR share of IMR points to endogenous causes (prematurity, birth asphyxia, congenital anomalies) needing better perinatal/newborn care."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"In a district the IMR has fallen but the neonatal mortality rate (NMR) now forms a LARGER proportion of infant deaths. This shift most strongly indicates a need to strengthen:","options":["Immunization and sanitation programs","Perinatal and essential newborn care (facility deliveries, resuscitation, SNCU)","Nutritional rehabilitation for toddlers","Deworming and ORS programs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A rising NMR share reflects predominance of endogenous causes (prematurity, birth asphyxia, sepsis, congenital anomalies) that require improved intrapartum and newborn care — skilled birth attendance, resuscitation, and Special Newborn Care Units.  Why A wrong: Immunization/sanitation mainly reduce post-neonatal and exogenous (infectious/environmental) deaths, not early neonatal deaths.  Why C wrong: Nutrition for toddlers targets the 1-5 year (under-5) group, not neonates.  Why D wrong: Deworming/ORS address older infants and children with diarrhoeal/parasitic disease, not the neonatal period.  High-Yield Takeaway: Post-neonatal deaths are mostly EXOGENOUS (infection, malnutrition, environment — preventable by public-health measures); neonatal deaths are mostly ENDOGENOUS (require clinical perinatal care). The NMR is now the hardest component to reduce."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the maternal mortality RATIO from the maternal mortality RATE?","options":["The ratio uses women of reproductive age as denominator; the rate uses live births","The ratio uses live births as denominator; the rate uses women of reproductive age (15-49 years)","Both use total population as denominator","The ratio and rate are identical terms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal mortality RATIO = maternal deaths per 100,000 LIVE BIRTHS (the commonly reported MMR, reflecting obstetric risk per pregnancy). Maternal mortality RATE = maternal deaths per 100,000 WOMEN of reproductive age (15-49 years), reflecting both fertility and obstetric risk.  Why A wrong: This reverses the two denominators.  Why C wrong: Neither uses the total population as denominator.  Why D wrong: They are distinct measures with different denominators.  High-Yield Takeaway: The headline \"MMR\" reported by SRS/WHO is the RATIO (per 100,000 live births). India's SRS 2018-20 MMR is 97 per 100,000 live births (down from 130 in 2014-16); the national target (SDG) is <70."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"As per SRS 2020, India's infant mortality rate (IMR) is closest to:","options":["8 per 1,000 live births","28 per 1,000 live births","45 per 1,000 live births","60 per 1,000 live births"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SRS 2020 reports India's IMR at 28 per 1,000 live births (continuing a steady decline; SRS 2019 was 30).  Why A wrong: ~8 reflects developed-country levels, not India.  Why C wrong: ~45-50 reflects India's IMR from roughly two decades earlier, now outdated.  Why D wrong: 60+ corresponds to the 1990s figures.  High-Yield Takeaway: Anchor SRS 2020 India figures: IMR 28, NMR 20, U5MR 32 per 1,000 live births; CBR ~19.5 and CDR ~6.0 per 1,000; TFR ~2.0. The National Health Policy 2017 IMR target is 28 by 2019 and SDG target is <=25 by 2030."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"The under-5 mortality rate (U5MR) is expressed as the probability of dying before age 5 per 1,000:","options":["Total population","Live births","Women of reproductive age","Children aged 1-4 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: U5MR is the number (probability) of deaths of children under 5 years per 1,000 LIVE BIRTHS in a given year.  Why A wrong: Total population is the denominator for crude rates (birth/death rate), not U5MR.  Why C wrong: Women of reproductive age is the denominator for the maternal mortality RATE.  Why D wrong: The 1-4 year child death rate is a separate indicator (deaths 1-4 yrs per 1,000 children aged 1-4).  High-Yield Takeaway: U5MR (per 1,000 live births) is a key SDG child-survival indicator; India SRS 2020 U5MR is 32. Do not confuse it with the child mortality rate (age 1-4) whose denominator is children 1-4 years."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"In a case-control study, cases (who have the disease) tend to remember and report past exposures more thoroughly than controls. This threatens validity through:","options":["Selection bias","Recall bias","Confounding","Lead-time bias"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Recall bias is a form of information (measurement) bias in which cases, motivated by their illness, recall exposures differently (usually more completely) than controls — a hallmark limitation of retrospective case-control studies.  Why A wrong: Selection bias comes from how subjects are chosen into the study, not how they report exposure.  Why C wrong: Confounding is distortion by a third variable, not differential reporting.  Why D wrong: Lead-time bias is a screening/survival artefact.  High-Yield Takeaway: Case-control studies are especially prone to RECALL bias and selection bias. Using objective records or non-diseased comparison groups with similar recall (e.g., other-disease controls) can reduce it."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"Given a 2x2 screening table — Disease+: Test+ =80, Test- =20; Disease-: Test+ =100, Test- =800 — what is the specificity?","options":["80%","88.9%","44.4%","97.6%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Specificity = TN/(TN+FP) = 800/(800+100) = 800/900 = 88.9%. It is the ability to correctly identify those WITHOUT disease.  Why A wrong: 80% (80/100) is the SENSITIVITY [TP/(TP+FN)].  Why C wrong: 44.4% (80/180) is the PPV.  Why D wrong: 97.6% (800/820) is the NPV.  High-Yield Takeaway: Specificity uses the disease-NEGATIVE column (TN/(TN+FP)); sensitivity uses the disease-POSITIVE column (TP/(TP+FN)). SnNout/SpPin: a highly Sensitive test with a Negative result rules OUT; a highly Specific test with a Positive result rules IN."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A cohort study is the preferred design in which of the following situations?","options":["The disease is extremely rare","The exposure is rare and you want to measure incidence and relative risk","You need results quickly and cheaply","You cannot follow participants over time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cohort studies start with exposure, follow groups forward, and directly measure incidence and relative risk; they are ideal when the EXPOSURE is rare (you can deliberately sample exposed persons) and when temporality/multiple outcomes matter.  Why A wrong: For a rare DISEASE, case-control is preferred; cohorts would need enormous samples.  Why C wrong: Cohorts (especially prospective) are typically slow and expensive; cross-sectional studies are quick and cheap.  Why D wrong: Cohort designs require follow-up over time by definition.  High-Yield Takeaway: Cohort advantages: measures incidence, RR, temporality, multiple outcomes, good for rare exposures. Disadvantages: costly, long, loss to follow-up, inefficient for rare diseases."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"In a case-control study the odds ratio can be taken as a good approximation of the relative risk ONLY when:","options":["The exposure is very common","The disease is rare in the population (rare-disease assumption)","The sample size is very large","Cases and controls are matched on age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the disease is rare, the number of cases is small relative to the population, so the odds of exposure approximate the risks, and OR approximates RR (the rare-disease assumption).  Why A wrong: Exposure frequency does not govern the OR-to-RR approximation.  Why C wrong: A large sample improves precision but does not make OR equal RR when the disease is common.  Why D wrong: Matching controls confounding but does not, by itself, make the OR approximate the RR.  High-Yield Takeaway: OR ~ RR only when disease is rare. For common outcomes the OR overestimates (exaggerates) the RR — a frequently tested pitfall in interpreting case-control results."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 1: Epidemiology, Biostatistics & Screening · study designs, RR vs OR from 2x2 tables, attributable risk & NNT…","question":"A disease has 500 new cases and 50 deaths from it in a year among a population where 2,000 people have the disease. The case-fatality rate is:","options":["2.5%","10%","25%","100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Case-fatality rate (CFR) = (deaths from the disease / number of diagnosed cases of that disease) x 100. Using the diagnosed/affected cases (500 new cases): 50/500 = 10%. CFR measures the killing power (severity/virulence) of a disease among those who have it.  Why A wrong: 2.5% (50/2000) uses total prevalent cases loosely but the standard CFR numerator/denominator here yields 10% for the incident cohort; 2.5% understates severity.  Why C wrong: 25% is not obtainable from these figures.  Why D wrong: 100% would mean every case died.  High-Yield Takeaway: CFR = deaths / cases of the disease (a measure of virulence/severity), distinct from the mortality rate, which uses the TOTAL population as denominator. CFR reflects how deadly a disease is once contracted."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A health worker at a session site is using a multi-dose vial of OPV that was opened 6 days ago at a previous session. The VVM is in usable stage, the vial has been kept at 2–8°C, the septum is not submerged in water, and the expiry date is intact. Under the Open Vial Policy, the MOST appropriate action is:","options":["Discard the vial — any vial opened more than 4 hours ago must be thrown away","Continue to use the vial as OPV is covered under the Open Vial Policy for up to 4 weeks","Use it only after re-testing potency by the shake test","Discard because OPV cannot be stored once opened"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OPV is one of the eligible liquid vaccines under the Open Vial Policy and may be used at subsequent sessions for up to 4 weeks provided all four conditions are met — VVM usable, expiry not passed, stored at 2–8°C, and the septum was never submerged in water/contaminated. All conditions here are satisfied.  Why A wrong: The 4-hour discard rule applies to RECONSTITUTED lyophilized vaccines (BCG, measles, MR, JE), not to eligible liquid multi-dose vaccines like OPV.  Why C wrong: The shake test is for freeze-damage assessment of adsorbed vaccines, not a routine potency re-check for OPV.  Why D wrong: OPV is specifically an Open Vial Policy vaccine.  High-Yield Takeaway: Open Vial Policy vaccines = OPV, DPT, TT/Td, Hepatitis B, Pentavalent (liquid), IPV, liquid PCV, fIPV — usable up to 4 weeks; the exceptions (never under OVP) are the reconstituted BCG, Measles, MR and JE."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"During a fixed immunization session, BCG was reconstituted at 9:00 AM. At 1:30 PM a mother arrives with an eligible infant. The nurse's correct action is:","options":["Give the dose — reconstituted BCG is good for the whole day","Discard the reconstituted vial and reconstitute a fresh vial if beneficiaries remain","Give the dose after keeping the vial at room temperature to check colour","Give a double dose to compensate for possible potency loss"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reconstituted BCG (and measles/MR/JE) must be discarded 4 hours after reconstitution OR at the end of the session, whichever is earlier. 9:00 AM + 4 hours = 1:00 PM, so at 1:30 PM the vial is expired and must be discarded; reconstitute fresh if beneficiaries remain.  Why A wrong: Reconstituted lyophilized vaccines are NOT covered by the \"whole day/4 weeks\" open vial rule.  Why C wrong: There is no room-temperature colour check for BCG; the time limit is absolute.  Why D wrong: Double dosing is never a compensation strategy and risks harm.  High-Yield Takeaway: Reconstituted vaccine = 4-hour clock (or end of session). This is the single most tested Open Vial Policy trap."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A nurse inspects a vaccine vial. The inner square of the VVM is LIGHTER than the outer circle. The correct interpretation and action is:","options":["Heat exposure exceeded — discard the vial","The vaccine is still usable — the inner square is lighter than the outer ring","VVM has reached discard point — send for potency testing","The vaccine has been frozen — perform the shake test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A VVM is usable while the inner square remains LIGHTER than the outer circle. As cumulative heat exposure increases the inner square darkens; the vaccine must be discarded once the inner square matches (equals) or is darker than the outer circle.  Why A wrong: Discard applies only when the inner square is equal to or darker than the outer ring.  Why C wrong: There is no \"send for testing\" step — VVM is the field decision tool itself.  Why D wrong: VVM monitors cumulative HEAT, not freezing; freeze damage is assessed by the shake test, not the VVM.  High-Yield Takeaway: VVM: inner square lighter than outer circle = USE; equal or darker = DISCARD. VVM detects heat only, never freezing."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A cold-chain handler finds that the ILR power failed overnight and a box of DPT, Td and Hepatitis B vials was accidentally moved into the deep freezer and is now frozen solid. The MOST appropriate FIRST action is:","options":["Thaw them to 2–8°C and use immediately as potency is unaffected","Perform the shake test on a frozen and an unfrozen (control) vial before any use","Discard all vials immediately without testing","Send all vials for laboratory potency testing before use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DPT, Td, Hepatitis B (and IPV, PCV, DT, pentavalent) are freeze-SENSITIVE adsorbed vaccines. When freezing is suspected the shake test is done: shake the suspect vial alongside a known unfrozen control of the same vaccine. If the suspect sediments faster and shows flakes/granules (frozen, damaged), discard it.  Why A wrong: Freezing irreversibly damages adsorbed vaccines — potency IS lost and they cannot simply be thawed and used.  Why C wrong: Blanket discard without a shake test wastes potentially good vials; the test guides the decision.  Why D wrong: Lab testing is not the field protocol; the shake test is the recommended point-of-use test.  High-Yield Takeaway: Freeze-sensitive vaccines fail the SHAKE TEST — a damaged vial shows rapid sedimentation and flakes vs a smooth uniform control. Frozen adsorbed vaccine = discard."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"At the PHC, a new nurse is loading the Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR). Which vaccine should NOT be placed touching the frozen sides/bottom or near the evaporator where it may freeze?","options":["OPV","Reconstituted BCG diluent that is separate","Pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib)","Measles powder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pentavalent is a freeze-sensitive adsorbed vaccine and must be kept in the ILR basket away from the freezing zone; freezing destroys it.  Why A wrong: OPV is freeze-STABLE (actually stored in the deep freezer/coldest part) and is not freeze-damaged.  Why B wrong: Diluents are not damaged by cold in the same way; the concern is freeze damage to adsorbed antigens.  Why D wrong: Lyophilized measles powder is not freeze-sensitive in the way adsorbed liquids are.  High-Yield Takeaway: In the ILR, keep freeze-sensitive vaccines (DPT, Td, Hep B, Penta, IPV, PCV) in baskets away from the bottom/sides; freeze-stable ones (OPV, and lyophilized BCG/measles) tolerate the coldest zones."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"At a District Vaccine Store, which items are correctly stored in the DEEP FREEZER (–15°C to –25°C) rather than the ILR (+2°C to +8°C)?","options":["DPT and Td vials","OPV stock and ice packs for freezing","Hepatitis B and IPV","Reconstituted measles vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The deep freezer holds OPV (freeze-stable, kept at the coldest temperature for longer shelf life) and is used to freeze the ice packs used in vaccine carriers/cold boxes.  Why A wrong: DPT and Td are freeze-sensitive and belong in the ILR at 2–8°C.  Why C wrong: Hepatitis B and IPV are freeze-sensitive and must be in the ILR, never the deep freezer.  Why D wrong: Reconstituted measles is used within 4 hours at 2–8°C, never frozen.  High-Yield Takeaway: Deep freezer = OPV + ice-pack freezing. ILR = everything freeze-sensitive at 2–8°C. Putting Hep B/DPT/IPV/Penta in a deep freezer destroys them."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A store keeper receives a new consignment of vaccines. Applying stock-rotation principles for vaccine issue, the correct rule is:","options":["Last-In-First-Out regardless of expiry","Issue by earliest expiry / VVM status first (EEFO), and among equal expiry, First-Expiry-First-Out overriding First-In-First-Out","Issue only the newest stock to guarantee freshness","Issue randomly since all vials are within date"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vaccine issue follows Earliest-Expiry-First-Out and the VVM stage — vials with more advanced VVM or nearer expiry go first even if received later. FEFO takes precedence over simple FIFO.  Why A wrong: Last-In-First-Out risks older stock expiring on the shelf.  Why C wrong: Issuing only newest stock strands older stock to expiry — wasteful.  Why D wrong: Random issue ignores expiry/VVM and causes wastage.  High-Yield Takeaway: Vaccines follow \"Early Expiry First Out\" plus VVM status — a later-received vial with a more advanced VVM is used before an earlier one with a good VVM."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"Two minutes after receiving a vaccine, a 5-year-old develops stridor, generalized urticaria, wheeze and hypotension. The immunization clinic nurse's FIRST priority action is to administer:","options":["Intravenous hydrocortisone","Intramuscular adrenaline (1:1000) into the anterolateral thigh","Oral antihistamine and observe","Subcutaneous adrenaline into the deltoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anaphylaxis first-line treatment is adrenaline 1:1000 given INTRAMUSCULARLY into the anterolateral (mid-outer) thigh, given immediately — it reverses airway oedema, bronchospasm and hypotension. IM thigh gives the fastest reliable absorption.  Why A wrong: Steroids act slowly (hours) and are adjuncts, never the first/priority drug.  Why C wrong: Antihistamines treat only urticaria/itch and do not reverse airway/circulatory collapse.  Why D wrong: The IM route (not subcutaneous) and the thigh (not deltoid) are preferred; SC absorption is slow and unreliable in shock.  High-Yield Takeaway: Anaphylaxis = IM adrenaline 1:1000, anterolateral thigh, FIRST. Everything else (oxygen, fluids, antihistamine, steroid) is secondary."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"For the child in the previous scenario, the appropriate single dose of adrenaline (1:1000) and repeat interval is:","options":["0.01 mg/kg IM, up to a maximum of 0.5 mg, repeated every 5–15 minutes if needed","0.1 mg/kg IV bolus, repeated every 2 minutes","1 mg IM as a fixed adult dose for all ages","0.5 mg/kg IM, single dose only, no repeat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The standard adrenaline dose is 0.01 mg/kg of 1:1000 solution IM (i.e., 0.01 mL/kg), to a maximum of 0.5 mg (0.3 mg in younger children per some guidelines), repeated every 5–15 minutes if there is inadequate response.  Why B wrong: The IV route with 1:1000 is dangerous (arrhythmia/hypertensive crisis); IV adrenaline is diluted and reserved for refractory shock by trained teams.  Why C wrong: A fixed 1 mg dose can overdose a child; dosing is weight-based to a ceiling.  Why D wrong: Doses are repeated every 5–15 minutes as required; it is not a single-dose-only drug.  High-Yield Takeaway: Remember \"0.01 mg/kg IM, max 0.5 mg, repeat q5–15 min.\" Never give 1:1000 adrenaline IV in the field clinic."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A cluster of children develops fainting, dizziness and hyperventilation immediately after an injection during a school MR campaign; no urticaria or hypotension is seen and all recover on reassurance and rest. Under the WHO (revised, 2019) AEFI causality classification, this is BEST categorized as:","options":["Vaccine product-related reaction","Vaccine quality defect-related reaction","Immunization anxiety-related reaction (immunization stress-related response)","Coincidental event"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Anxiety/fear about the injection — not the vaccine's contents — is the cause. WHO classifies this as an immunization anxiety-related reaction (now often termed Immunization Stress-Related Response, ISRR); clustering of fainting/hyperventilation at campaigns is classic.  Why A wrong: A product-related reaction is caused by the vaccine's inherent properties (e.g., a genuine allergic reaction to a component).  Why B wrong: A quality defect reaction stems from a manufacturing/quality flaw in the product.  Why D wrong: A coincidental event is one that would have occurred regardless of vaccination (e.g., intercurrent illness).  High-Yield Takeaway: WHO's 5 AEFI cause categories: (1) vaccine product-related, (2) vaccine quality defect-related, (3) immunization ERROR-related (programmatic), (4) immunization anxiety/stress-related, (5) coincidental. Mass fainting at a campaign = anxiety-related."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"Several children develop abscesses at the injection site after a session where a reconstituted vaccine was used beyond 4 hours and with a contaminated needle. This AEFI is classified as:","options":["Immunization error-related (programmatic) reaction","Coincidental event","Vaccine product-related reaction","Immunization anxiety-related reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The harm resulted from improper handling/technique (use of expired reconstituted vaccine, contamination) — this is an immunization error-related reaction (previously \"programme error\"), which is preventable through correct practice.  Why B wrong: A coincidental event has no causal link to the immunization process; here the error is the direct cause.  Why C wrong: The vaccine's inherent properties are not the cause — the handling error is.  Why D wrong: Injection-site abscesses from contamination are not an anxiety/stress response.  High-Yield Takeaway: Cluster of abscesses/sepsis traced to reuse of reconstituted vials or non-sterile technique = immunization ERROR-related AEFI, and it is preventable — the most examinable \"programmatic error\" example."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"Regarding AEFI surveillance in India, which statement is CORRECT?","options":["Only deaths need to be reported; minor reactions are ignored","All serious AEFIs (hospitalization, disability, cluster, or death) must be reported and investigated; reporting is passive-plus-active and line-listed","AEFI reporting is optional and left to the treating physician's discretion","AEFIs are reported only after the annual programme review"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Serious AEFIs — those causing death, hospitalization, disability, clustering, or public concern — must be notified promptly and investigated by the district AEFI committee, with line-listing and causality assessment.  Why A wrong: Serious non-fatal events (hospitalization, disability, clusters) are equally reportable.  Why C wrong: Reporting of serious AEFIs is mandatory, not discretionary.  Why D wrong: Serious AEFIs are reported immediately, not deferred to an annual review.  High-Yield Takeaway: \"Serious AEFI\" = death, life-threatening event, hospitalization/prolongation, persistent disability, congenital anomaly, or a cluster — all trigger prompt reporting and investigation."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A 9-month-old received Penta-1 and OPV-1 at 6 weeks but never returned. The mother brings the child now. The correct action regarding the interrupted primary schedule is:","options":["Restart the entire primary series from dose 1","Resume from where the schedule was left off — give Penta-2 now; there is no need to restart","Give all remaining doses today together to save time","Consider the child fully immunized as one dose was received"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"no need to restart\" principle: an interrupted schedule is resumed from where it stopped — you do not repeat earlier doses. Give the next due dose (Penta-2/OPV-2) respecting minimum intervals.  Why A wrong: Restarting is never required and wastes doses; immunological memory persists.  Why C wrong: Doses of the same vaccine must respect the minimum interval (e.g., 4 weeks between Penta doses); they cannot be crammed together on one day.  Why D wrong: A single dose does not confer full protection; the series must be completed.  High-Yield Takeaway: \"The lapsed schedule is never restarted — resume and complete, honoring minimum intervals.\" A universally tested UIP rule."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A mother wants two live vaccines that were NOT given together. If MR (measles-rubella) was given today and a second injectable/oral live vaccine could not be co-administered, the minimum interval before giving the other live vaccine is:","options":["No interval needed — give any time","At least 4 weeks (28 days)","At least 6 months","At least 2 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If two live (especially injectable live) vaccines are not administered on the SAME day, they should be separated by a minimum of 4 weeks (28 days) to avoid interference with the immune response.  Why A wrong: Live vaccines given on different days need spacing; giving them a few days apart can blunt the response to the second.  Why C wrong: 6 months is not the required interval.  Why D wrong: The rule is 4 weeks, not 2.  High-Yield Takeaway: Live injectable vaccines: give SAME day OR space by ≥4 weeks. (OPV, being oral, is generally exempt and can be given at any interval with other vaccines.)"},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"Which of the following is a TRUE contraindication (not a false one) to a routine vaccine?","options":["A child with a mild upper respiratory infection and low-grade fever","A breastfeeding mother","Severe (anaphylactic) allergic reaction to a previous dose of the same vaccine or its component","A child on oral antibiotics for a minor infection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A documented anaphylactic/severe allergic reaction to a prior dose or to a vaccine component is a genuine contraindication to further doses of that vaccine.  Why A wrong: Mild illness with low-grade fever is a FALSE contraindication — vaccinate as usual.  Why B wrong: Breastfeeding is not a contraindication for either mother or infant (except very few specific live vaccines in special situations).  Why D wrong: Being on antibiotics for a minor illness does not contraindicate routine immunization.  High-Yield Takeaway: FALSE contraindications = mild illness, low-grade fever, mild diarrhoea, malnutrition, breastfeeding, antibiotic use, prematurity, family history of AEFI. TRUE = anaphylaxis to prior dose/component; live vaccines in severe immunodeficiency/pregnancy."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A febrile, HIV-positive severely immunocompromised infant is due for vaccines. Which vaccine is generally CONTRAINDICATED in symptomatic severe immunodeficiency?","options":["Hepatitis B (inactivated)","Pentavalent (DPT-HepB-Hib)","BCG (live)","IPV (inactivated polio)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: BCG is a LIVE bacterial vaccine and is contraindicated in symptomatic HIV/severe cellular immunodeficiency because of the risk of disseminated BCG disease (BCGosis).  Why A wrong: Hepatitis B is inactivated and safe in immunodeficiency.  Why B wrong: Pentavalent contains inactivated/subunit components and is not contraindicated on immunodeficiency grounds.  Why D wrong: IPV is inactivated and is actually PREFERRED over OPV in immunocompromised individuals.  High-Yield Takeaway: LIVE vaccines (BCG, OPV, MR, JE live) are the ones restricted in severe immunodeficiency; inactivated vaccines are safe. Prefer IPV over OPV in immunocompromised children."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A nurse administering BCG to a neonate should use which technique and dose, and which finding is a NORMAL expected reaction?","options":["Deep intramuscular into the thigh; immediate large blister is normal","Intradermal into the left upper arm (deltoid insertion), 0.05 mL for newborns; a small papule that ulcerates and heals into a scar over 6–12 weeks is normal","Subcutaneous over the scapula; instant scarring is normal","Intradermal 0.5 mL into the forearm; no local reaction is expected"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG is given INTRADERMALLY over the left deltoid insertion; dose is 0.05 mL for infants under 1 month and 0.1 mL thereafter. The expected course: a papule/wheal, later a small ulcer, healing into a scar over ~6–12 weeks — this is normal, not an AEFI.  Why A wrong: BCG is intradermal, never deep IM; an immediate large blister is abnormal.  Why C wrong: The site is the left upper arm intradermally, not subcutaneous over the scapula.  Why D wrong: The dose is 0.05–0.1 mL (not 0.5 mL) and a local reaction (papule→ulcer→scar) IS expected.  High-Yield Takeaway: BCG = intradermal, left deltoid, 0.05 mL (<1 mo) / 0.1 mL; the delayed ulcer-and-scar is normal. A too-large/abscess/regional lymphadenitis suggests wrong (subcutaneous) technique or overdose."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"Six weeks after BCG, an infant develops a large fluctuant axillary lymph node that suppurates. The MOST likely cause and correct classification is:","options":["Coincidental TB infection unrelated to vaccination","Suppurative BCG lymphadenitis — commonly from faulty (subcutaneous instead of intradermal) technique or overdose; an immunization error/vaccine-related AEFI","Normal expected BCG scar formation, no action","Anaphylaxis to BCG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suppurative regional (axillary) lymphadenitis after BCG is a recognized complication, most often from injecting too deep (subcutaneous) or giving too high a dose — an immunization technique/error-related AEFI.  Why A wrong: The temporal link and typical presentation point to BCG, not incidental TB.  Why C wrong: A large suppurating node is a complication, not the normal small healing scar.  Why D wrong: Anaphylaxis is immediate and systemic, not a delayed local suppurative node.  High-Yield Takeaway: BCG adenitis / abscess ⇒ suspect subcutaneous (too deep) injection or overdose. Correct depth = intradermal."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A CHN student asks why IPV was introduced into UIP alongside OPV and why the global switch from trivalent to bivalent OPV occurred. The BEST explanation is:","options":["IPV causes VAPP whereas OPV does not","OPV (live) can rarely cause vaccine-associated paralytic polio (VAPP) and circulating vaccine-derived poliovirus (cVDPV, mostly type 2); tOPV was switched to bOPV (removing type 2) and IPV added to maintain type-2 immunity","IPV was introduced only because it is cheaper than OPV","OPV was withdrawn completely and only IPV is now used in India"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OPV, being live-attenuated, can rarely cause VAPP and can seed cVDPV (type 2 was the main culprit). The globally synchronized \"switch\" (April 2016) removed type 2 from OPV (trivalent→bivalent) and IPV was added to sustain type-2 immunity.  Why A wrong: It is OPV (live), not IPV (inactivated), that can cause VAPP.  Why C wrong: The rationale is safety/eradication endgame, not merely cost — IPV is not cheaper.  Why D wrong: OPV was NOT fully withdrawn in India; bOPV continues along with IPV.  High-Yield Takeaway: VAPP/cVDPV are OPV risks (live). The switch = tOPV→bOPV (drop type 2) + introduce IPV. IPV is inactivated and cannot cause VAPP."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"Under India's current NIS, birth doses given within the first 24 hours (or at first contact soon after birth) include:","options":["Measles and Vitamin A","BCG, OPV-0 (zero dose), and Hepatitis B birth dose","Pentavalent-1 and Rotavirus-1","DPT booster and Td"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At birth the NIS gives BCG, the zero dose of OPV (OPV-0), and the Hepatitis B birth dose (ideally within 24 hours).  Why A wrong: Measles-Rubella is given at 9–12 months, not at birth; Vitamin A starts at 9 months with MR-1.  Why C wrong: Pentavalent and Rotavirus begin at 6 weeks, not at birth.  Why D wrong: DPT booster is at 16–24 months; Td is given at 10 and 16 years and in pregnancy.  High-Yield Takeaway: Birth doses = BCG + OPV-0 + Hep B (within 24 h). Hep B birth dose within 24 hours is critical for preventing perinatal transmission."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"Regarding the Rotavirus vaccine (Rotavac) age restriction in UIP, which statement is CORRECT and why does the age cap exist?","options":["There is no upper age limit for the first dose","The first dose should be given by 6 weeks (with the series completed early); catch-up beyond the recommended upper age is avoided due to a small association of the vaccine with intussusception at older ages","It can be started up to 2 years of age","It is given only after 1 year of age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotavirus vaccine is scheduled with Pentavalent/OPV at 6, 10 and 14 weeks; initiation is time-limited because the theoretical risk of intussusception rises when the vaccine is first given at older ages, so late initiation/catch-up is restricted.  Why A wrong: There IS an age restriction on initiation and completion precisely because of the intussusception concern.  Why C wrong: Starting at up to 2 years is not recommended — initiation is confined to early infancy.  Why D wrong: It is an infant vaccine given from 6 weeks, not after 1 year.  High-Yield Takeaway: Rotavirus vaccine has AGE CAPS on initiation because of the intussusception signal — start early (6 weeks) and don't do late catch-up. Intussusception is the classic rotavirus vaccine trap."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"In 2018, MoHFW recommended replacing plain Tetanus Toxoid (TT) with Td (tetanus + reduced-dose diphtheria) across the programme. The MAIN rationale for this change is:","options":["Td is cheaper than TT","To maintain diphtheria immunity into adolescence/adulthood in response to diphtheria outbreaks, since TT alone left older age groups susceptible to diphtheria","Td provides pertussis protection that TT lacks","TT was found to be ineffective against tetanus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria outbreaks among older children/adults revealed waning diphtheria immunity. Replacing TT with Td (which adds a reduced dose of diphtheria toxoid) sustains diphtheria protection into adolescence and adulthood while still protecting against tetanus.  Why A wrong: The driver is disease epidemiology (diphtheria resurgence), not cost.  Why C wrong: Td contains NO pertussis component (that is Tdap); Td = tetanus + diphtheria only.  Why D wrong: TT remained effective against tetanus; the gap was diphtheria protection.  High-Yield Takeaway: TT→Td (2018) to close the diphtheria immunity gap in older ages. Td = Tetanus + low-dose diphtheria (no pertussis). Given at 10 yr, 16 yr, and to pregnant women."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A previously unimmunized/unknown-status pregnant woman books in the second trimester. The correct Td (formerly TT) schedule is:","options":["A single dose is sufficient in all cases","Two doses of Td 4 weeks apart (Td-1 as early as possible, Td-2 four weeks later); if she was immunized in a pregnancy within the last 3 years, only one booster Td is needed","Three doses at monthly intervals","No Td is needed if she received childhood DPT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an unprotected pregnant woman, give Td-1 early and Td-2 at least 4 weeks later (and at least 2–4 weeks before delivery). If she received two doses in a pregnancy within the previous 3 years, a single booster dose suffices.  Why A wrong: A single dose suffices only as a booster for the recently/previously immunized, not for the unimmunized.  Why C wrong: The primary maternal schedule is two doses, not three monthly doses.  Why D wrong: Childhood DPT does not remove the need for maternal Td to prevent neonatal tetanus; a booster is still indicated.  High-Yield Takeaway: Maternal Td: 2 doses ≥4 weeks apart if unprotected; 1 booster if 2 doses given within the last 3 years. Aim to complete before delivery to prevent neonatal tetanus."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A child receiving Pentavalent, OPV, Rotavirus, IPV and PCV is due for several vaccines the same day. Regarding simultaneous administration, the correct principle is:","options":["Only one injectable vaccine may be given per visit; defer the rest","Multiple vaccines (live and inactivated) can be given on the SAME day at different sites; there is no need to space them","Live and inactivated vaccines can never be given together","All injections must be given in the same limb at the same site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most vaccines can be co-administered safely at the same visit using separate sites/syringes; simultaneous administration does not reduce efficacy or increase serious reactions and improves coverage.  Why A wrong: There is no rule limiting to one injection per visit; multiple injectables are given at different sites.  Why C wrong: Live and inactivated vaccines can be given together on the same day.  Why D wrong: Different vaccines should use different sites (and if the same limb, well separated), never mixed in one syringe unless licensed as a combination.  High-Yield Takeaway: Give simultaneously at separate sites; the only spacing rule that matters is: two LIVE injectable vaccines not given the same day must be ≥4 weeks apart."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"During the Measles-Rubella (MR) campaign, the target age group vaccinated (regardless of prior measles/MR/MMR vaccination status) is:","options":["Only children under 1 year","9 months to 15 years","Only pregnant women","All persons up to 45 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The MR campaign targeted all children aged 9 months to <15 years with a single dose, irrespective of prior vaccination, to rapidly build population immunity toward measles elimination and rubella/CRS control; MR was then integrated into routine UIP at 9–12 months and 16–24 months.  Why A wrong: The campaign reached up to 15 years, not just infants.  Why C wrong: Pregnant women are NOT given MR (it is a live vaccine — rubella component is contraindicated in pregnancy).  Why D wrong: The upper age was <15 years, not 45.  High-Yield Takeaway: MR campaign = 9 months–15 years, one dose for all regardless of history. MR/MMR is contraindicated in pregnancy (live rubella → CRS risk); advise avoiding pregnancy for 1 month after."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"With respect to the HPV vaccine and current WHO recommendation for the primary target group, which statement is CORRECT?","options":["HPV is recommended only for boys","The primary target is girls aged 9–14 years; WHO now endorses a single-dose (or two-dose) schedule as providing comparable protection for this age group","HPV must be given as four doses in all cases","HPV is a live vaccine contraindicated in adolescents"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The priority target is girls 9–14 years (before sexual debut). WHO's updated position accepts a single-dose schedule as comparably effective to two doses for the 9–14 age group; immunocompromised individuals still need multiple doses.  Why A wrong: The primary target is girls (some programmes add boys), not \"only boys.\"  Why C wrong: Four doses are not required; 1 or 2 doses is now recommended for the target age.  Why D wrong: HPV is a recombinant (non-live) vaccine and is recommended for adolescents.  High-Yield Takeaway: HPV: primary target girls 9–14 yr; WHO supports single-dose (or 2-dose) schedules. Best given before sexual debut for maximum protection against cervical cancer."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A colleague states that pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV) and rotavirus vaccine (Rotavac) are \"not part of the national programme.\" The BEST correction is:","options":["She is correct — both remain private-only vaccines","Both PCV and Rotavirus vaccine (Rotavac) have been rolled out nationally under UIP; PCV is given at 6 weeks, 14 weeks and a booster at 9 months","Only PCV is in UIP; rotavirus is not","Both are given as birth doses under UIP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Both PCV (against pneumococcal pneumonia/meningitis) and Rotavirus vaccine (Rotavac) have been expanded to national coverage under UIP. PCV in India uses a 2+1 schedule: primary doses at 6 and 14 weeks with a booster at 9 months.  Why A wrong: Both are now UIP vaccines, not private-only.  Why C wrong: Rotavirus vaccine is also part of UIP nationally.  Why D wrong: Neither is a birth dose; both start at 6 weeks.  High-Yield Takeaway: Recent UIP additions to know: PCV (6 wk, 14 wk, 9 mo booster), Rotavirus (6/10/14 wk), IPV (introduced with the polio endgame), MR, and JE in endemic districts."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A vaccine carrier is being packed for an outreach session. The correct practice for conditioning ice packs before loading freeze-sensitive vaccines is:","options":["Load ice packs straight from the deep freezer against the vaccines","\"Condition\" the frozen ice packs (leave at room temperature until water droplets appear and they are no longer at freezing) before placing them, to prevent freezing of freeze-sensitive vaccines","Use warm water packs to keep vaccines at 2–8°C","Omit ice packs and rely on the carrier insulation alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Frozen ice packs are conditioned — kept at room temperature until sweating/water droplets form and the ice starts to melt (edge no longer at 0°C) — so they cool to 2–8°C without freezing the freeze-sensitive vaccines placed against them.  Why A wrong: Unconditioned packs straight from the freezer can freeze and destroy DPT/Td/Hep B/Penta/IPV/PCV.  Why C wrong: Warm packs cannot maintain 2–8°C and would overheat vaccines.  Why D wrong: Insulation alone cannot maintain cold-chain temperatures for the session.  High-Yield Takeaway: Always CONDITION ice packs (until droplets form) before packing carriers — an unconditioned frozen pack is a common cause of in-field freeze damage."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A nurse must transport vaccines from the PHC to an outreach site 3 hours away. Which cold-chain equipment is designed for THIS purpose, and how long does it typically maintain temperature compared with a cold box?","options":["Deep freezer; indefinite","Vaccine carrier — for day-use outreach carrying a small number of vials, holding temperature for a shorter period (about a day); a cold box holds larger quantities for longer (several days) and is used for bulk transport/power-cut backup","ILR; used for transport between sessions","Domestic refrigerator; preferred for transport"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The vaccine carrier is the small day-use device for outreach immunization sessions (holds temperature roughly a day with conditioned ice packs). The cold box is larger, holds temperature for several days, and is used for bulk transport and as backup during power failures.  Why A wrong: A deep freezer is fixed store equipment, not a transport device to the field.  Why C wrong: The ILR is stationary storage, not for transport.  Why D wrong: Domestic refrigerators are not recommended cold-chain equipment (poor temperature stability and no ice-lining).  High-Yield Takeaway: Carrier = short outreach (small load, ~1 day); Cold box = bulk/longer transport & power-cut backup (several days). ILR/deep freezer = fixed storage only."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A DPT booster is due at 16–24 months but the child arrives at 3 years having missed it. The correct action is:","options":["Do not give — the child is now too old for DPT","Give the DPT booster now (catch-up), since delayed doses are given as soon as possible without restarting the series","Give the adult Td instead of DPT booster","Wait until 5 years and give the second booster only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A delayed/missed dose is administered at first contact (catch-up); the DPT booster can be given late without restarting the primary series.  Why A wrong: A modest delay does not disqualify the child from the booster; catch-up is standard.  Why C wrong: The 16–24 month booster uses DPT, not Td; Td is used at the 10- and 16-year boosters. (For much older catch-up, formulation is chosen per age guidelines, but at ~3 years DPT is appropriate.)  Why D wrong: Skipping to the 5-year booster leaves an immunity gap; give the missed dose now.  High-Yield Takeaway: \"Give it at first contact\" — delayed doses are caught up, never abandoned or restarted. Age-appropriate formulation still applies (DPT in early childhood, DT/Td later)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"The correct minimum interval between doses of Pentavalent vaccine in the primary series, and the reason it must not be shortened, is:","options":["2 weeks; shorter intervals waste vaccine","4 weeks (28 days); giving doses closer than the minimum interval blunts the immune response, so such a dose is invalid and should be repeated at the correct interval","8 weeks; anything shorter causes anaphylaxis","No minimum interval is required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The minimum interval between primary Pentavalent doses is 4 weeks. A dose given earlier than the minimum interval may not evoke an adequate response and is considered invalid, requiring repetition at the proper interval.  Why A wrong: The minimum is 4 weeks, not 2, and the concern is immunogenicity, not merely wastage.  Why C wrong: The routine minimum is 4 weeks; over-shortening does not cause anaphylaxis.  Why D wrong: A minimum interval definitely applies to multi-dose primary series.  High-Yield Takeaway: Minimum interval between primary doses = 4 weeks; doses given too early are invalid and must be repeated. Intervals can be longer than recommended (no restart) but never shorter than the minimum."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 2: Immunization, Cold Chain & Vaccines · Open Vial Policy & 4-hour reconstituted-vaccine rule, VVM interpretation…","question":"A supervisor reviews cold-chain monitoring. Which practice correctly ensures freeze-sensitive vaccine integrity during storage?","options":["Twice-daily temperature recording of the ILR (morning and evening) and using a freeze-indicator; investigate any reading outside 2–8°C","Recording ILR temperature once a week","Relying only on the VVM and not recording temperatures","Storing food and vaccines together to save space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ILR/deep-freezer temperatures must be recorded TWICE daily (morning and evening, including holidays) on a temperature chart, with freeze indicators and prompt investigation of out-of-range readings — this catches breaches early.  Why B wrong: Weekly recording is far too infrequent to detect breaches.  Why C wrong: The VVM (heat only) does not replace twice-daily temperature logging and freeze monitoring.  Why D wrong: Food must never be stored with vaccines — it causes frequent door opening, contamination and temperature fluctuation.  High-Yield Takeaway: Twice-daily temp recording (AM & PM, every day) + freeze indicator; never store food/drinks with vaccines. VVM = heat; freeze indicator/shake test = cold damage."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A 72-year-old man from a high-income household approaches a CSC to enrol under Ayushman Bharat, saying his son earns well and the family is not on the SECC deprivation list. Under the scheme change notified in 2024, which is correct?","options":["He is ineligible because his family income exceeds the poverty threshold","He is eligible for a dedicated additional Rs 5 lakh cover for senior citizens aged 70+, regardless of income, via the Ayushman Vay Vandana card","He can enrol only if he surrenders any private health insurance he holds","He gets the standard family-shared Rs 5 lakh cover only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 2024, PM-JAY was expanded to cover ALL citizens aged 70 years and above irrespective of socio-economic status; they receive a distinct additional top-up cover of Rs 5 lakh per year (not shared with the rest of the family) issued through the new \"Ayushman Vay Vandana\" card.  Why A wrong: Income is explicitly NOT a bar for the 70+ category — that is the whole point of the expansion.  Why C wrong: Beneficiaries with private/other insurance (or CGHS etc., with the option to choose) remain eligible for this senior cover.  Why D wrong: Seniors 70+ get a SEPARATE Rs 5 lakh that is not pooled with other family members.  High-Yield Takeaway: 70+ expansion = own Rs 5 lakh, income-independent, via Ayushman Vay Vandana card. Old books stop at \"5 lakh for poor families\" — flag the 2024 update."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A TB patient enrolled on the Ni-kshay portal in December 2024 asks how much nutritional support he will now receive each month by DBT. The correct current figure is:","options":["Rs 500 per month","Rs 750 per month","Rs 1000 per month","Rs 2000 per month"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Under Nikshay Poshan Yojana, the monthly nutritional support was doubled from Rs 500 to Rs 1000 per month with effect from November 2024, credited via DBT for the full duration of treatment.  Why A wrong: Rs 500 was the amount from 2018 until the November 2024 revision — the classic outdated-book trap.  Why B/D wrong: Rs 750 and Rs 2000 are distractor figures never used by NPY.  High-Yield Takeaway: NPY = Rs 1000/month (since Nov 2024), up from Rs 500. Target High / P.R. Yadav editions still print Rs 500 — this is the single most likely NORCET trap here."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A pregnant woman delivers by C-section in a government facility. She is charged nothing for the surgery, drugs, diagnostics, blood, diet during stay, and transport home. Which scheme guarantees this \"zero out-of-pocket, cashless\" entitlement?","options":["Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY)","Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK)","Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY)","Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: JSSK entitles every pregnant woman to FREE cashless services — normal delivery and C-section, drugs, diagnostics, diet, blood, and free transport (home→facility, referral, and drop-back) in public facilities; same free package extended to sick neonates/infants.  Why A wrong: JSY is a CASH incentive (conditional cash transfer) to promote institutional delivery, not a free-services guarantee.  Why C wrong: PMMVY is a maternity CASH benefit (wage compensation), not delivery-cost coverage.  Why D wrong: PMSMA provides free assured ANTENATAL check-ups on the 9th of each month, not delivery/C-section cost coverage.  High-Yield Takeaway: JSSK = free SERVICE (cashless), JSY = CASH incentive. \"Cashless C-section + free transport + free diet\" → always JSSK."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"On the 9th of every month, a specialist provides free, assured, quality antenatal care including one ultrasound and identification of high-risk pregnancies. This describes:","options":["SUMAN","PMSMA (Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan)","JSSK","LaQshya"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PMSMA guarantees a fixed-day (9th of every month), free, assured comprehensive ANC by an OBG specialist/MO to all pregnant women in their 2nd/3rd trimester, with emphasis on detecting and following up high-risk pregnancies.  Why A wrong: SUMAN (Surakshit Matritva Aashwasan) is the umbrella assurance of zero-cost, respectful maternity care with guaranteed services up to 6 months post-delivery — not the fixed 9th-of-month ANC campaign.  Why C wrong: JSSK covers free delivery-related services and transport, not fixed-day specialist ANC.  Why D wrong: LaQshya targets quality of care in the LABOUR ROOM and maternity OT, not outreach ANC.  High-Yield Takeaway: \"9th of every month + free ANC by specialist\" = PMSMA. SUMAN = umbrella assurance of dignified zero-cost care up to 6 months postpartum."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A woman is pregnant with her SECOND child, which is a girl. Under PMMVY 2.0, what maternity cash benefit is she entitled to for this second child?","options":["Nothing — PMMVY is only for the first living child","Rs 5000 in two installments, same as first child","Rs 6000 in a single installment for a second child if it is a girl","Rs 11000 total"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PMMVY 2.0 extended the benefit to the SECOND child ONLY IF the second child is a GIRL — Rs 6000 in a single installment (paid after birth registration and completion of due immunization), to discourage sex-selective practices.  Why A wrong: The \"first-child-only\" rule was the ORIGINAL PMMVY design; 2.0 added the second-girl-child provision.  Why B wrong: The first child benefit is Rs 5000 in installments; the second-girl-child benefit is a separate Rs 6000 single tranche.  Why D wrong: The two are not summed into a single Rs 11000 entitlement per event; they are distinct benefits tied to different children.  High-Yield Takeaway: PMMVY 2.0 → 1st child Rs 5000 (installments); 2nd child Rs 6000 (single, ONLY if girl). Older material says \"first child only, Rs 5000\" — flag the 2.0 girl-child addition."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"During a community visit, an ASHA sees the local sub-centre now displays a signboard reading \"Ayushman Arogya Mandir.\" She asks what it was formerly called and what its tagline is. The correct answer is:","options":["Formerly Primary Health Centre; tagline \"Health for All\"","Formerly Ayushman Bharat Health and Wellness Centre (AB-HWC); tagline \"Arogyam Parmam Dhanam\"","Formerly Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre; tagline \"Swasth Bharat\"","Formerly Village Health Sanitation Committee; tagline \"Sabka Swasthya\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In late 2023, Ayushman Bharat – Health and Wellness Centres (AB-HWCs) were officially renamed \"Ayushman Arogya Mandir\" with the tagline \"Arogyam Parmam Dhanam\" (health is the supreme wealth).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated facilities/committees and slogans; AAM is specifically the rebranded HWC delivering Comprehensive Primary Health Care.  High-Yield Takeaway: AB-HWC → Ayushman Arogya Mandir (2023), tagline \"Arogyam Parmam Dhanam.\" Books printed before 2023 only say \"HWC\" — recognize the new name."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"Ayushman Arogya Mandirs are mandated to deliver Comprehensive Primary Health Care. How many packages of services are expanded from the original selective primary care approach?","options":["6 packages","8 packages","12 packages","15 packages"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Comprehensive Primary Health Care at HWCs/Ayushman Arogya Mandirs is delivered through an expanded set of 12 service packages (from care in pregnancy & childbirth, neonatal/infant, childhood/adolescent, family planning, communicable diseases, NCDs, mental health, ENT, ophthalmic, dental/oral, geriatric & palliative, to emergency/trauma).  Why A wrong: 6 relates to the classic selective/limited primary care (RMNCH+A style), not the expanded HWC package count.  Why B/D wrong: 8 and 15 are distractor figures.  High-Yield Takeaway: HWC/Ayushman Arogya Mandir = 12 CPHC service packages (expanded from selective PHC). Commonly examined exact number."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A Community Health Officer (CHO) is being posted at a Sub Health Centre-Health and Wellness Centre. Which qualification/cadre most accurately describes the CHO / Mid-Level Health Provider (MLHP)?","options":["An MBBS doctor posted at the sub-centre","A BSc/GNM nurse or Ayurveda practitioner with a Certificate in Community Health / bridge programme, leading the SHC-HWC team","A pharmacist managing drug supply only","An ASHA promoted after 5 years of service"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CHO/MLHP is a mid-level provider — a B.Sc./GNM Nurse (with a certificate/bridge course in Community Health) or a BAMS practitioner — who leads the Sub Health Centre-HWC team (with ANM/MPW and ASHAs) and delivers comprehensive primary care.  Why A wrong: MBBS medical officers head PHCs, not the SHC-HWC as CHO.  Why C wrong: Pharmacists have a supportive role, not the CHO leadership role.  Why D wrong: ASHAs are accredited activists/volunteers; the CHO is a qualified nurse/AYUSH professional, not a promoted ASHA.  High-Yield Takeaway: CHO = MLHP = nurse/AYUSH + community-health bridge course, team leader at SHC-HWC. Distinguish clearly from ASHA and PHC Medical Officer."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"Under NP-NCD, population-based screening for common non-communicable diseases (hypertension, diabetes, and the three common cancers) is targeted at individuals of which age?","options":["All adults aged ≥18 years","All individuals aged ≥30 years","Only those aged ≥45 years","Only those aged ≥60 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Population-based screening under NP-NCD (via HWCs) targets everyone aged 30 years and above for hypertension, diabetes, and the three common cancers (oral, breast, cervical).  Why A wrong: 18 is not the screening threshold for the population-based NCD programme.  Why C/D wrong: 45 and 60 are not the entry ages; 30+ is the standard.  High-Yield Takeaway: NP-NCD population-based screening = ≥30 years; three common cancers = oral, breast, cervical. Also note the programme was RENAMED NPCDCS → NP-NCD (2023)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"During a health-and-wellness day, a CHO records tobacco/alcohol use, physical activity, diet, and family history on a specific one-page tool to estimate NCD risk before screening. This tool is the:","options":["WHO CVD risk chart only","Community-Based Assessment Checklist (CBAC) form","Mother and Child Protection (MCP) card","HMIS reporting format"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CBAC (Community-Based Assessment Checklist) form is filled (by ASHA/CHO) for individuals ≥30 years to assess NCD risk factors; a score ≥4 flags higher risk warranting screening/referral.  Why A wrong: The WHO/CVD risk chart is used later for cardiovascular risk stratification, not the initial community risk-factor checklist.  Why C wrong: The MCP card is for maternal-child health tracking.  Why D wrong: HMIS is an aggregate reporting system, not an individual risk checklist.  High-Yield Takeaway: CBAC form → NCD risk screening at community level (≥30 yrs); score ≥4 = increased risk. Under NP-NCD (renamed from NPCDCS 2023)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"Anemia Mukt Bharat is built on a \"6x6x6\" strategy. The three \"6\"s stand for:","options":["6 states, 6 districts, 6 blocks","6 target beneficiary groups, 6 interventions, 6 institutional mechanisms","6 months of IFA, 6 doses of deworming, 6 checkups","6 age groups, 6 lab tests, 6 diets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AMB's 6x6x6 = 6 target/beneficiary groups (across the life cycle), 6 interventions (prophylactic IFA, deworming, behaviour change/appropriate feeding, testing & treatment, mandatory fortification, addressing non-nutritional causes like malaria/fluorosis), and 6 institutional mechanisms.  Why A/C/D wrong: These misattribute the framework to geography, dosing schedules, or lab/diet items.  High-Yield Takeaway: 6x6x6 = 6 beneficiary groups × 6 interventions × 6 institutional mechanisms. Frequently confused — memorize the three axes."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"Under the Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS) programme, an adolescent (10–19 yrs) should receive iron and folic acid at what frequency, along with which co-intervention?","options":["Daily IFA + monthly deworming","Weekly IFA (blue tablet: 100 mg elemental iron + 500 µg folic acid) + biannual albendazole deworming","Monthly IFA injection + weekly vitamin A","Weekly IFA only, no deworming"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WIFS provides ONE supervised WEEKLY IFA tablet (adolescent \"blue\" tablet = 100 mg elemental iron + 500 µg folic acid) plus biannual (6-monthly) albendazole 400 mg deworming, along with nutrition/health education.  Why A wrong: WIFS is WEEKLY, not daily; deworming is biannual, not monthly.  Why C wrong: WIFS uses oral tablets, not monthly injections; vitamin A is a separate programme.  Why D wrong: Biannual deworming IS a component of WIFS.  High-Yield Takeaway: WIFS = weekly IFA (blue tablet 100 mg iron + 500 µg FA) + biannual deworming for adolescents. Note: therapeutic dosing differs from prophylactic; know both."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"RBSK (Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram) screens children 0–18 years for the \"4 Ds.\" These four Ds are:","options":["Disease, Death, Disability, Deformity","Defects at birth, Diseases, Deficiencies, Developmental delays including disability","Diarrhoea, Diphtheria, Dengue, Diabetes","Diet, Deworming, Diagnosis, Delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RBSK's 4 Ds = Defects at birth, Diseases, Deficiencies, and Developmental delays (including disabilities); it screens for a defined set of 30 conditions across these categories, with free treatment/surgery at tertiary centres (including DEIC referral).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions unrelated to RBSK's framework.  High-Yield Takeaway: RBSK 4 Ds = Defects at birth, Diseases, Deficiencies, Developmental delays; 30 identified conditions; 0–18 yrs; Mobile Health Teams + DEIC."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A programme provides adolescents (10–19 yrs) with peer educators (Saathiya), Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics, and addresses nutrition, sexual/reproductive health, mental health, substance misuse, injuries/violence, and NCDs. This is:","options":["RBSK","RKSK (Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram)","SABLA","WIFS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RKSK targets adolescents 10–19 yrs with a peer-educator (Saathiya) model, Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics (AFHCs), and covers 6 priority areas: nutrition, sexual & reproductive health, mental health, substance misuse, injuries & violence (including gender-based violence), and non-communicable diseases.  Why A wrong: RBSK screens 0–18 yrs for the 4 Ds/30 conditions, not the adolescent peer-educator/AFHC model.  Why C wrong: SABLA (RGSEAG) is a girl-focused empowerment/nutrition scheme, not the RKSK health programme.  Why D wrong: WIFS is one iron-supplementation intervention, not the whole adolescent health strategy.  High-Yield Takeaway: RKSK = adolescents 10–19, Saathiya peer educators + AFHC, 6 priority areas. Distinguish sharply from RBSK (0–18, 4Ds)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"The RMNCH+A strategy added the \"+A\" (Adolescent) component. What is the key conceptual reason for including adolescents and adopting a \"continuum of care\" approach?","options":["Adolescents have the highest mortality rate","To link interventions across life stages and through levels of care (community→facility), recognizing adolescence as the entry point to the reproductive cycle","Because adolescents cannot access any other programme","To replace the earlier Child Survival and Safe Motherhood programme"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RMNCH+A operationalizes a \"continuum of care\" — linking Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, Child, and Adolescent health interventions across life stages AND across service-delivery levels (home/community → sub-centre → PHC/CHC → district hospital); adolescence is recognized as the critical entry point into the reproductive life cycle.  Why A wrong: Adolescents do not have the highest mortality; the rationale is life-cycle linkage, not mortality ranking.  Why C wrong: Adolescents access several programmes; exclusivity is not the rationale.  Why D wrong: CSSM was an earlier initiative; RMNCH+A is not merely its rename.  High-Yield Takeaway: RMNCH+A = continuum of care across life stages AND levels of care; adolescence = entry point to reproductive cycle."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"Under IPHS 2022, the population norm for establishing one Primary Health Centre (PHC) in a plain area is approximately:","options":["5,000 population","10,000 population","30,000 population","1,20,000 population"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PHC norm ≈ 30,000 population in plain areas (20,000 in hilly/tribal/difficult areas). This is the primary institutional tier headed by a Medical Officer.  Why A wrong: 5,000 (3,000 in hilly) is the Sub-Centre norm.  Why B wrong: ~10,000 relates to sub-centre-HWC coverage discussions, not the PHC norm.  Why D wrong: CHC serves ~1,20,000 (80,000 in hilly areas), not the PHC.  High-Yield Takeaway: SC 5,000 / PHC 30,000 / CHC 1,20,000 (plains); reduce to 3,000 / 20,000 / 80,000 in hilly/tribal areas. IPHS 2022 revised infrastructure/HR norms."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"In the three-tier rural health system, a First Referral Unit (FRU) providing emergency obstetric care, newborn care, and blood storage is expected at which level?","options":["Sub-Centre","Primary Health Centre","Community Health Centre / FRU with specialists","Village Health Committee"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The CHC (30-bed, with specialists — surgeon, physician, OBG, paediatrician) functions as the First Referral Unit, expected to provide comprehensive emergency obstetric care (CEmONC), newborn care, and blood storage facility.  Why A wrong: The Sub-Centre is the most peripheral contact point (ANM/MPW), no specialist services.  Why B wrong: The PHC provides basic curative/preventive care with a Medical Officer but is not the specialist FRU.  Why D wrong: The Village Health Committee is a community body, not a clinical referral unit.  High-Yield Takeaway: FRU = CHC (24x7 CEmONC + blood storage + specialists). SC→PHC→CHC referral chain; know which tier does what."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A patient in a remote village consults a Medical Officer at the district hospital through a video link from the Ayushman Arogya Mandir, assisted by the CHO. The national platform enabling this is:","options":["U-WIN","eSanjeevani","Ni-kshay","RCH portal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: eSanjeevani is the national telemedicine platform — including the HWC/AAM \"hub-and-spoke\" (eSanjeevani AB-HWC) model where the CHO at the spoke connects patients to MOs/specialists at the hub — plus OPD (patient-to-doctor) services.  Why A wrong: U-WIN is the digital immunization registry platform (digitizing UIP, like a \"CoWIN for routine immunization\").  Why C wrong: Ni-kshay is the TB notification/case-management portal (NTEP).  Why D wrong: The RCH portal tracks reproductive-child-health service delivery, not live teleconsultation.  High-Yield Takeaway: eSanjeevani = teleconsultation (HWC hub-and-spoke + OPD); U-WIN = immunization registry; Ni-kshay = TB. Don't mix the portals."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A mother wants a permanent digital record so any hospital in India can retrieve her child's health data with consent. Under ABDM, the unique 14-digit health ID she should generate is called:","options":["Aadhaar Health Number","ABHA (Ayushman Bharat Health Account) number","PM-JAY family ID","Ni-kshay ID"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under the Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM), the ABHA (Ayushman Bharat Health Account) is a unique 14-digit health ID that links and stores personal health records accessible across facilities with the individual's consent.  Why A wrong: Aadhaar is the general identity number; it may be used to create ABHA but is not the health account itself.  Why C wrong: The PM-JAY card/ID is the insurance beneficiary card, not the longitudinal digital health record.  Why D wrong: Ni-kshay ID is TB-programme-specific.  High-Yield Takeaway: ABDM's health ID = ABHA (14-digit). PM-JAY = insurance; ABHA = digital health record. Both fall under \"Ayushman Bharat\" but serve different pillars."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"India's TB elimination target under NTEP, as reflected in the National Strategic Plan, was set as \"End TB\" by:","options":["2025 (five years ahead of the global SDG target of 2030)","2030, aligned exactly with the SDG target","2035","2022"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: India committed to ending TB by 2025 — five years ahead of the global SDG/End TB Strategy milestone of 2030 — under NTEP (renamed from RNTCP in 2020).  Why B wrong: 2030 is the global SDG target; India's own target is the more ambitious 2025.  Why C wrong: 2035 is the WHO End TB Strategy's final milestone for reduction, not India's elimination target.  Why D wrong: 2022 was not the TB target.  High-Yield Takeaway: India \"End TB by 2025\" (ahead of global 2030). Also: RNTCP → NTEP (2020). Know both the rename and the ambitious date."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A donor \"adopts\" TB patients on the Ni-kshay portal and provides them nutritional/vocational/diagnostic support. This community-support (Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan) contribution model is called:","options":["Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana","Ni-kshay Mitra","U-DST","BPaLM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ni-kshay Mitra are donors (individuals, corporates, NGOs, elected representatives) who adopt TB patients/blocks/districts under the Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan and provide additional nutritional, diagnostic, and vocational support.  Why A wrong: Nikshay Poshan Yojana is the GOVERNMENT DBT nutrition benefit (Rs 1000/month), not the donor-adoption scheme.  Why C wrong: U-DST (Universal Drug Susceptibility Testing) is a diagnostic strategy to test drug resistance in all diagnosed TB patients.  Why D wrong: BPaLM is a treatment regimen for drug-resistant TB.  High-Yield Takeaway: Ni-kshay Mitra = donor adopts patients (PM TB Mukt Bharat). NPY = govt nutrition cash. Don't confuse the two \"Ni-kshay\" terms."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"NTEP has adopted a shorter, all-oral regimen for eligible drug-resistant TB patients that replaces the longer injectable-containing regimens. This regimen is abbreviated:","options":["DOTS-Plus","BPaLM (Bedaquiline, Pretomanid, Linezolid, Moxifloxacin)","Category II with streptomycin","Standard RIPE for 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BPaLM (Bedaquiline + Pretomanid + Linezolid + Moxifloxacin) is the newer, shorter (~6-month), all-oral regimen adopted by NTEP for eligible MDR/RR-TB patients, replacing longer, more toxic injectable regimens.  Why A wrong: DOTS-Plus is the older programmatic term for MDR-TB management, not this specific regimen.  Why C wrong: Category II (with injectable streptomycin) is a retired/old regimen classification.  Why D wrong: RIPE for 6 months is standard DRUG-SENSITIVE TB treatment, not the DR-TB regimen.  High-Yield Takeaway: BPaLM = shorter all-oral DR-TB regimen (Bdq/Pa/Lzd/Mfx). Also know U-DST = drug-susceptibility testing for ALL TB patients. Newer than most CHN books."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"POSHAN Abhiyaan (National Nutrition Mission) has been restructured. Its current umbrella form, integrating supplementary nutrition, ICDS/Anganwadi, and POSHAN Abhiyaan, is known as:","options":["Mid-Day Meal Scheme","Saksham Anganwadi and Poshan 2.0","Integrated Child Protection Scheme","National Food Security Mission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: POSHAN Abhiyaan was subsumed under \"Saksham Anganwadi and Poshan 2.0,\" the integrated nutrition support umbrella (merging the erstwhile supplementary nutrition programme, POSHAN Abhiyaan, and scheme for adolescent girls) with a focus on nutrition content, delivery, outreach and Saksham (upgraded) Anganwadis.  Why A wrong: Mid-Day Meal (now PM POSHAN) is the school-meal scheme, separate from Poshan 2.0.  Why C wrong: ICPS is a child-protection (not nutrition) scheme.  Why D wrong: NFSM is an agriculture-sector food-production mission.  High-Yield Takeaway: POSHAN Abhiyaan → Poshan 2.0 (Saksham Anganwadi). PM POSHAN = school mid-day meal. Note the rebranding."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A worker in the unorganized sector, not eligible for PM-JAY, gets treated free of cost under a STATE government health scheme with a higher sum insured. This illustrates which principle of PM-JAY implementation?","options":["PM-JAY forbids any state scheme","States may implement PM-JAY through their own state health-insurance schemes and can top-up/expand beneficiary base and cover","PM-JAY automatically covers all unorganized-sector workers regardless of SECC status","State schemes and PM-JAY cannot coexist for the same population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PM-JAY is implemented in a federal manner — many states run it convergently through their own state schemes (e.g., with expanded beneficiary lists or higher cover), topping up the central entitlement; states have flexibility in the implementation model (trust/insurance/mixed).  Why A wrong: State schemes are permitted and widely used for convergence, not forbidden.  Why C wrong: PM-JAY's base eligibility is SECC deprivation-based (plus the new 70+ universal category); it does not automatically cover ALL unorganized workers.  Why D wrong: They routinely coexist/converge for the same beneficiaries.  High-Yield Takeaway: PM-JAY allows state schemes to converge and expand cover/beneficiaries — a state may offer MORE than Rs 5 lakh or a broader list. Understand federal flexibility."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"Per NFHS-5 (2019–21), which statement about India's key indicators is CORRECT?","options":["Total Fertility Rate (TFR) fell to 2.0, below replacement level (2.1)","TFR rose above 3.0","The sex ratio at birth remained unchanged from NFHS-4","Institutional births declined compared to NFHS-4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NFHS-5 recorded a national TFR of 2.0 — for the first time BELOW the replacement level of 2.1, a landmark demographic finding.  Why B wrong: TFR fell, it did not rise above 3.0.  Why C wrong: The overall sex ratio (females per 1000 males) improved to 1020 in NFHS-5 (population), and institutional/health indicators generally improved — not unchanged.  Why D wrong: Institutional births ROSE substantially (to ~89%) in NFHS-5 vs NFHS-4.  High-Yield Takeaway: NFHS-5 headline: TFR 2.0 (below replacement 2.1); institutional births ~89%; population sex ratio 1020. Frequently examined \"first time below replacement\" fact."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A nurse must correctly attribute cash vs service benefits. Which pairing is CORRECT?","options":["JSY — free cashless C-section services","PMMVY — conditional maternity cash benefit (wage compensation)","JSSK — Rs 6000 cash to the mother","PMSMA — cash incentive for institutional delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PMMVY is a maternity-benefit CASH transfer (partial wage compensation for the first live birth, Rs 5000 in installments; second child Rs 6000 if girl under 2.0), conditional on ANC/registration/immunization.  Why A wrong: JSY gives CASH (institutional delivery incentive), not cashless C-section services; free C-section is JSSK.  Why C wrong: JSSK provides FREE services (not Rs 6000 cash).  Why D wrong: PMSMA is free fixed-day ANC, not a delivery cash incentive; the cash incentive for institutional delivery is JSY.  High-Yield Takeaway: CASH: JSY (delivery incentive), PMMVY (wage compensation). SERVICE (free): JSSK, PMSMA, SUMAN. This cash-vs-service split is a classic NORCET differentiation trap."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"An ASHA delivers a JSY cash incentive to a woman for institutional delivery. In a LOW Performing State (LPS), the JSY cash entitlement to a rural mother (and its rationale) is designed to be:","options":["Lower than in High Performing States, to save funds","Higher than in High Performing States, because LPS have poorer institutional delivery and need stronger incentives","Identical everywhere regardless of state category","Given only to urban mothers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: JSY differentiates cash assistance by state category — Low Performing States (LPS, e.g., EAG states) receive HIGHER incentives (and the incentive is given to all mothers irrespective of age/parity/poverty line in LPS) to more strongly promote institutional delivery where uptake is low.  Why A wrong: The logic is the opposite — LPS get MORE, not less.  Why C wrong: Amounts and eligibility rules differ between LPS and HPS.  Why D wrong: JSY heavily targets RURAL mothers (higher rural than urban package), not urban-only.  High-Yield Takeaway: JSY LPS > HPS incentive; in LPS all mothers eligible regardless of age/parity/BPL. Understand the LPS/HPS logic, not just the amount."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"SUMAN (Surakshit Matritva Aashwasan) primarily assures which of the following?","options":["Only free antenatal ultrasound","Assured, dignified, respectful and zero-cost (no out-of-pocket) quality maternity and newborn care, including free services up to 6 months post-delivery and zero tolerance for denial of services","A one-time cash grant of Rs 5000","Free surgery for all gynaecological cancers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SUMAN guarantees assured, dignified, respectful, and ZERO-cost quality healthcare for every pregnant woman, mother (up to 6 months post-delivery), and sick newborn, with zero tolerance for denial of services — an umbrella \"assurance\" building on JSSK/PMSMA entitlements.  Why A wrong: It is far broader than a single ultrasound.  Why C wrong: SUMAN is a service ASSURANCE, not a cash grant (that is PMMVY/JSY).  Why D wrong: Gynae-cancer surgery is not the focus; SUMAN is maternal-newborn care assurance.  High-Yield Takeaway: SUMAN = zero-cost, respectful, assured maternity + newborn care up to 6 months postpartum, zero denial. Umbrella over JSSK/PMSMA."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"Under the Universal Immunization Programme, the newer digital platform that replaces paper-based tracking and provides an electronic registry with a digital vaccination certificate for every beneficiary is:","options":["CoWIN (retained as-is for routine immunization)","U-WIN","RCH register","Ni-kshay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: U-WIN is the digital platform (modeled on CoWIN) that digitizes routine immunization under UIP — maintaining an electronic registry of pregnant women and children, generating digital immunization records/certificates, and enabling due-list tracking and anywhere-vaccination.  Why A wrong: CoWIN was built for the COVID-19 vaccination drive; U-WIN is the distinct routine-immunization platform (leveraging CoWIN's tech).  Why C wrong: The RCH register is paper/legacy tracking that U-WIN is intended to digitize.  Why D wrong: Ni-kshay is the TB portal.  High-Yield Takeaway: U-WIN = digital UIP/routine immunization registry (CoWIN-like). Newer than most CHN texts — recognize it."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 3: National Health Programs & Schemes (Latest Updates) · Ayushman Bharat…","question":"A CHO must distinguish overlapping-sounding programmes. Which pairing of \"old name → current/renamed name\" is CORRECT?","options":["NPCDCS → NP-NCD","NP-NCD → NPCDCS","NTEP → RNTCP","Ayushman Arogya Mandir → AB-HWC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The National Programme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke (NPCDCS) was renamed \"National Programme for Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD)\" in 2023, reflecting an expanded NCD scope.  Why B wrong: The direction is reversed — the OLD name is NPCDCS, the NEW is NP-NCD.  Why C wrong: Reversed — RNTCP is the old name; NTEP (2020) is the current name.  Why D wrong: Reversed — AB-HWC is the old name; Ayushman Arogya Mandir (2023) is the new name.  High-Yield Takeaway: Track the renames: RNTCP→NTEP (2020), NPCDCS→NP-NCD (2023), AB-HWC→Ayushman Arogya Mandir (2023). Reversed-direction options are the trap."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A community health nurse is informed of 3 suspected measles cases in an anganwadi within 48 hours. After confirming and notifying, what is the MOST appropriate priority action to interrupt transmission among the exposed children?","options":["Start all sick children on oral antibiotics","Administer measles vaccine to all susceptible contacts within 72 hours of exposure","Isolate only the children who have developed rash","Advise parents to keep all children home for 21 days and take no other action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measles vaccine given to susceptible contacts within 72 hours of exposure can prevent or attenuate disease — this is the outbreak control priority alongside isolation of cases and vitamin A.  Why A wrong: Measles is viral; antibiotics treat only secondary bacterial complications, not the outbreak.  Why C wrong: Cases are infectious from 4 days before rash onset, so isolating only rash-positive children misses the infectious prodromal contacts.  Why D wrong: Passive quarantine alone wastes the 72-hour post-exposure vaccination window that actively protects contacts.  High-Yield Takeaway: Measles outbreak = vaccinate susceptible contacts within 72 hrs; measles is infectious 4 days before to 4 days after rash."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A 30-year-old man is bitten by a stray dog with a single deep transdermal bite on the forearm that is bleeding. As per WHO, what is the SINGLE most important immediate first-aid action the nurse must ensure at the point of care?","options":["Immediate suturing of the wound to stop bleeding","Application of turmeric and a tight bandage","Thorough washing of the wound with soap and running water for at least 15 minutes","Immediate intramuscular deltoid injection of anti-rabies vaccine before any wound care"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Immediate, thorough washing/flushing with soap and running water for ≥15 minutes physically removes and inactivates virus at the wound and is the most effective single first-aid measure to reduce viral load.  Why A wrong: Suturing should be avoided/delayed; if unavoidable, do it after RIG infiltration, as suturing can inoculate virus deeper.  Why B wrong: Applying irritants/traditional substances is contraindicated and delays effective washing.  Why D wrong: Vaccine is essential but wound washing precedes it; washing is the first-aid priority.  High-Yield Takeaway: Rabies exposure first action = wash with soap + running water for 15 minutes; never suture immediately, never apply irritants."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A child sustains a dog-bite injury with a single bleeding scratch over the face. According to WHO exposure categories, this classifies as which category and mandates what?","options":["Category I — no treatment needed","Category II — wound washing and anti-rabies vaccine only","Category III — wound washing, anti-rabies vaccine AND rabies immunoglobulin (RIG)","Category II — vaccine only, because it is a scratch and not a bite"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Any wound over the face, head, neck, hands or genitalia — and any bleeding transdermal wound — is Category III, requiring wound washing + vaccine + RIG. Highly innervated/richly vascular sites are automatically Category III.  Why A wrong: Category I is only touching/feeding animals or licks on intact skin — not a bleeding scratch.  Why B/D wrong: A bleeding wound (bites/scratches that draw blood) is Category III, not Category II; RIG is mandatory.  High-Yield Takeaway: Wounds on head/neck/face/hands/genitalia = Category III regardless of severity → RIG is indicated."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A 26-year-old woman who is 20 weeks pregnant reports a Category III dog bite. Regarding post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP), the nurse should advise:","options":["Delay all PEP until after delivery to protect the fetus","Give vaccine only; RIG is contraindicated in pregnancy","Give full PEP including RIG; pregnancy is NOT a contraindication","Give a reduced half-dose regimen because of pregnancy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Rabies is almost 100% fatal; PEP (wound care + vaccine + RIG for Category III) is fully indicated in pregnancy. There is no contraindication and doses are not reduced.  Why A wrong: Delaying PEP risks a fatal outcome; rabies has no effective treatment once symptomatic.  Why B wrong: RIG is safe and indicated in pregnancy for Category III exposure.  Why D wrong: Standard full dosing applies; there is no half-dose pregnancy regimen.  High-Yield Takeaway: Pregnancy, lactation and infancy are NEVER contraindications to rabies PEP."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A patient with a Category III bite is receiving anti-rabies management. Regarding rabies immunoglobulin (RIG), which statement is correct?","options":["RIG should be given intramuscularly in the same deltoid as the vaccine","As much RIG as feasible should be infiltrated into and around the wound","RIG must be given on day 7 of the vaccine schedule","Equine RIG is dosed at 20 IU/kg and human RIG at 40 IU/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RIG provides immediate passive immunity; as much of the calculated dose as anatomically feasible is infiltrated in and around the wound(s), with any remainder given IM at a site distant from the vaccine.  Why A wrong: RIG must never be injected into the same site/syringe as vaccine — it can neutralize the vaccine antigen; use a distant site for any remainder.  Why C wrong: RIG should be given as early as possible, ideally on day 0 with the first vaccine dose (or within 7 days of starting vaccine), not scheduled for day 7.  Why D wrong: Doses are reversed — human RIG (HRIG) is 20 IU/kg, equine RIG (ERIG) is 40 IU/kg.  High-Yield Takeaway: HRIG 20 IU/kg, ERIG 40 IU/kg; infiltrate into wound, remainder IM at a distant site — never mix with vaccine."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"At a Health & Wellness Centre, a patient presents with 3 weeks of cough, evening fever and weight loss. While awaiting sputum results, what infection-control precaution is the FIRST priority for the nurse?","options":["Contact precautions with gloves and gown","Droplet precautions with a surgical mask on the nurse","Airborne precautions — place patient in a well-ventilated area and have staff use an N95 respirator","No precautions until diagnosis is confirmed"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary TB spreads by airborne droplet nuclei; presumptive TB requires airborne precautions — well-ventilated separate area, patient wearing a surgical mask, and staff using a fit-tested N95 respirator — implemented before confirmation.  Why A wrong: TB is not spread by contact; gloves/gown are not the key barrier.  Why B wrong: A surgical mask on the nurse is inadequate for airborne nuclei; the nurse needs an N95, while the patient wears the surgical mask.  Why D wrong: Precautions must begin on suspicion; waiting for confirmation exposes staff and other patients.  High-Yield Takeaway: TB = airborne → N95 for staff + surgical mask for patient + ventilation; start on suspicion, not confirmation."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A TB patient on the intensive phase remains sputum smear-positive at the end of month 2 and is a household contact of a known MDR-TB case. The MOST appropriate nursing action is to:","options":["Extend the same first-line regimen without further testing","Refer for drug-susceptibility testing (CBNAAT/NAAT) to rule out drug-resistant TB","Stop treatment as it has failed","Double the dose of the existing drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Persistent smear positivity and contact with an MDR case are red flags for drug-resistant TB; upfront rapid molecular testing (CBNAAT/NAAT) to detect rifampicin resistance is indicated before continuing.  Why A wrong: Continuing a possibly ineffective regimen amplifies resistance.  Why C wrong: Treatment is not simply stopped; the regimen is revised based on DST results.  Why D wrong: Dose-doubling is not a recognized response and risks toxicity without addressing resistance.  High-Yield Takeaway: MDR contact + non-conversion at 2 months → suspect DR-TB → do CBNAAT/rapid DST for rifampicin resistance."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"Under the DOT (Directly Observed Treatment) component of the National TB Elimination Programme, the essential feature the nurse must ensure is:","options":["The patient collects a month's medicines and self-administers at home","A treatment supporter directly observes the patient swallowing each dose","Injections are given by a doctor only","Medicines are given only when the patient is symptomatic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DOT means a trained treatment supporter directly watches the patient swallow every dose, ensuring adherence and preventing resistance from irregular intake.  Why A wrong: Unsupervised self-administration of a month's supply defeats the purpose of DOT.  Why C wrong: TB treatment is now largely oral daily fixed-dose combinations; the defining feature is observation, not who gives injections.  Why D wrong: TB drugs must be taken continuously for the full course, not only when symptomatic.  High-Yield Takeaway: DOT = observed swallowing of every dose by a treatment supporter; ensures adherence and curbs resistance."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A 2-year-old presents with acute watery diarrhoea. On IMNCI assessment he is lethargic, has sunken eyes, is drinking poorly, and the skin pinch goes back very slowly (>2 seconds). The nurse should classify and manage this as:","options":["No dehydration — Plan A, home ORS","Some dehydration — Plan B, supervised ORS in facility","Severe dehydration — Plan C, immediate IV fluids","Severe dehydration but manage with ORS only as the child can still be spoon-fed"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Two or more of — lethargy/unconsciousness, sunken eyes, unable to drink/drinking poorly, very slow skin pinch — defines SEVERE dehydration, requiring Plan C with immediate IV fluids (e.g., Ringer's lactate).  Why A wrong: These are danger signs, not a \"no dehydration\" picture.  Why B wrong: Plan B (ORS) is for \"some dehydration\" (restless/irritable, thirsty, drinks eagerly) — not for a lethargic child drinking poorly.  Why D wrong: A lethargic child who drinks poorly cannot be reliably rehydrated orally and needs IV; delaying IV is unsafe.  High-Yield Takeaway: Lethargic + sunken eyes + drinking poorly + very slow skin pinch = severe dehydration = Plan C (IV), not ORS."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"Regarding the WHO/UNICEF-recommended low-osmolarity ORS used for childhood diarrhoea, which composition is correct?","options":["Total osmolarity 311 mOsm/L with sodium 90 mmol/L","Total osmolarity 245 mOsm/L with sodium 75 mmol/L and glucose 75 mmol/L","Total osmolarity 200 mOsm/L with sodium 45 mmol/L","Total osmolarity 245 mOsm/L with sodium 130 mmol/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Low-osmolarity ORS has total osmolarity 245 mOsm/L, sodium 75, glucose 75, potassium 20, chloride 65 and citrate 10 mmol/L — it reduces stool output, vomiting and need for IV fluids compared with the old formula.  Why A wrong: 311 mOsm/L with Na 90 is the OLD (standard) higher-osmolarity ORS, now replaced.  Why C wrong: Sodium 45 is too low and is not the standard low-osmolarity value.  Why D wrong: Sodium 130 mmol/L is a plasma/IV-fluid figure, not ORS composition.  High-Yield Takeaway: Low-osmolarity ORS = 245 mOsm/L, Na 75, glucose 75 (old ORS was 311 mOsm/L, Na 90)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"For a 14-month-old child with acute diarrhoea, the nurse counsels on zinc supplementation. The correct regimen is:","options":["10 mg once, single dose only","20 mg/day for 14 days","40 mg/day for 3 days","Zinc is contraindicated in diarrhoea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO/MoHFW recommend zinc 20 mg/day for 14 days for children ≥6 months (10 mg/day for infants <6 months) with diarrhoea; it reduces duration, severity and recurrence over the following 2–3 months.  Why A wrong: A single dose is inadequate; the benefit requires the full 14-day course.  Why C wrong: 40 mg/day for 3 days is not the recommended dose or duration.  Why D wrong: Zinc is specifically recommended, not contraindicated, in childhood diarrhoea.  High-Yield Takeaway: Zinc in diarrhoea = 20 mg/day × 14 days (10 mg/day if <6 months), given with ORS."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A nurse is preparing a ward assignment and must match each disease with the correct transmission-based precaution. Which pairing is CORRECT?","options":["Pertussis — Airborne precautions","Measles — Droplet precautions","Pulmonary tuberculosis — Airborne precautions","Meningococcal meningitis — Contact precautions"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary TB spreads via droplet nuclei that remain suspended in air → Airborne precautions (N95, negative-pressure/ventilated room).  Why A wrong: Pertussis (whooping cough) spreads by larger respiratory droplets → Droplet precautions (surgical mask), not airborne.  Why B wrong: Measles is one of the classic AIRBORNE diseases (with TB and varicella), not droplet.  Why D wrong: Meningococcal disease requires Droplet precautions, not contact.  High-Yield Takeaway: Airborne = \"My TV\" (Measles, TB, Varicella); Droplet = pertussis, diphtheria, meningococcus, mumps, rubella, influenza."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"The difference between isolation and quarantine is BEST described as:","options":["Isolation is for well exposed persons; quarantine is for the sick","Isolation separates known/confirmed ill persons; quarantine restricts apparently well persons who were exposed","Both terms mean the same and are used interchangeably","Quarantine applies only to animals, isolation only to humans"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isolation = separation of persons known to be infected/ill during their period of communicability. Quarantine = restriction of movement of apparently healthy persons who have been exposed, for the maximum incubation period, to watch for disease.  Why A wrong: The definitions are reversed.  Why C wrong: They are distinct concepts applied to different groups.  Why D wrong: Both apply to humans; the distinction is ill vs exposed-but-well.  High-Yield Takeaway: Isolation = the SICK (confirmed cases); Quarantine = the EXPOSED-but-well, for the incubation period."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"During a suspected diphtheria case in the community, in addition to isolation and notification, the MOST time-critical treatment priority is:","options":["Wait for culture confirmation before any treatment","Early administration of diphtheria antitoxin (DAT) without waiting for culture","Antipyretics and rest only","Tracheostomy for all cases at presentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diphtheria antitoxin neutralizes circulating toxin only before it binds tissue, so DAT must be given early on clinical suspicion without waiting for culture; antibiotics (e.g., penicillin/erythromycin) are added to eliminate the organism.  Why A wrong: Delaying antitoxin for culture allows irreversible toxin binding to heart/nerves.  Why C wrong: Symptomatic care alone does not address the toxin-mediated disease.  Why D wrong: Airway intervention is only for obstruction, not a universal first step.  High-Yield Takeaway: Diphtheria = give antitoxin EARLY on clinical suspicion; DAT neutralizes only unbound toxin."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"Under the International Health Regulations (2005), which set of diseases is notifiable to WHO as diseases that may constitute a public health emergency of international concern regardless of context?","options":["Diabetes, hypertension, and asthma","Smallpox, poliomyelitis due to wild-type poliovirus, human influenza caused by a new subtype, and SARS","Dengue, malaria, and typhoid","Leprosy, filariasis, and trachoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under IHR (2005), smallpox, wild-type poliomyelitis, human influenza caused by a new subtype, and SARS must always be notified to WHO as they are inherently capable of causing a PHEIC.  Why A wrong: These are non-communicable diseases, not internationally notifiable events.  Why C wrong: Dengue/malaria/typhoid are notifiable domestically but are not on the IHR \"always notify\" list.  Why D wrong: These are targeted elimination programmes, not IHR emergency-notifiable diseases.  High-Yield Takeaway: IHR \"always notify\" four = smallpox, wild polio, new-subtype human influenza, SARS."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A cholera outbreak is confirmed in a flood-affected village. The single MOST important intervention to reduce case-fatality is:","options":["Mass prophylactic antibiotics to the entire village","Prompt fluid and electrolyte replacement (ORS/IV) to correct dehydration","Isolation of every household for 30 days","Delaying rehydration until stool culture confirms Vibrio cholerae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Death in cholera is due to rapid dehydration and shock; aggressive rehydration (ORS for mild/moderate, IV for severe) is the life-saving priority and reduces case-fatality to <1%.  Why A wrong: Antibiotics are adjuncts for severe cases (reduce stool volume/duration), not a substitute for rehydration or for mass prophylaxis.  Why C wrong: Prolonged blanket household isolation is impractical and not the fatality-reducing measure; safe water/sanitation and rehydration are.  Why D wrong: Rehydration must be immediate on clinical grounds; waiting for culture is dangerous.  High-Yield Takeaway: Cholera kills by dehydration — rehydration is the priority; antibiotics are only adjuncts."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A 37-year-old woman who smokes 20 cigarettes/day requests combined oral contraceptive pills (COCs). Applying WHO Medical Eligibility Criteria (MEC), the nurse should:","options":["Prescribe COCs freely as she is under 40","Recognize this as MEC Category 4 (unacceptable risk) and offer a non-estrogen method","Prescribe COCs at half dose","Give COCs only after she quits for one week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Age ≥35 years AND smoking ≥15 cigarettes/day is WHO MEC Category 4 for COCs (unacceptable health risk due to arterial/thrombotic events) — estrogen-containing methods should not be used; offer progestin-only or non-hormonal methods.  Why A wrong: Age alone is not the issue; the age-plus-heavy-smoking combination is a Category 4 contraindication.  Why C wrong: Dose reduction does not remove the thrombotic risk of estrogen in this group.  Why D wrong: A one-week gap does not resolve the risk profile of a heavy smoker ≥35.  High-Yield Takeaway: Smoker ≥35 yrs + ≥15 cigs/day = WHO MEC Category 4 for COC → avoid estrogen; use progestin-only/non-hormonal."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A 28-year-old woman with a history of migraine WITH aura wants to start contraception. According to WHO MEC, combined oral contraceptives are:","options":["Category 1 — no restriction","Category 2 — advantages outweigh risks","Category 4 — should not be used","The method of choice for her headaches"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Migraine WITH aura (at any age) is WHO MEC Category 4 for COCs because estrogen substantially increases ischaemic stroke risk in these women — COCs must be avoided.  Why A/B wrong: The presence of aura elevates it to Category 4, not 1 or 2 (migraine WITHOUT aura is generally Category 2 for initiation).  Why D wrong: Estrogen can worsen risk and is not a treatment for migraine.  High-Yield Takeaway: Migraine WITH aura = MEC 4 for COC (stroke risk); migraine WITHOUT aura is much less restrictive."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A woman comes to the clinic requesting a copper IUCD (Cu-T). Which finding is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to insertion at this visit?","options":["History of a normal vaginal delivery 2 years ago","Current active pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)","Age of 30 years","Mild iron-deficiency anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Current/active PID (and current purulent cervicitis, known pregnancy, unexplained vaginal bleeding, distorted uterine cavity, genital malignancy) contraindicates IUCD insertion as it can worsen the infection.  Why A wrong: Previous normal delivery is not a contraindication; parous women are good candidates.  Why C wrong: Age 30 is not a contraindication.  Why D wrong: Mild anaemia is a relative caution for copper IUCD (which may increase menstrual loss) but not an absolute bar; active PID is.  High-Yield Takeaway: IUCD absolute contraindications include active PID, known/suspected pregnancy, unexplained bleeding, distorted cavity, genital cancer."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"Regarding the optimal timing of copper IUCD insertion, which is INCORRECT?","options":["Within 12 days of the onset of menstruation","Immediately postpartum within 48 hours, or after 4 weeks","Post-abortion if no infection is present","Only during the mid-luteal (secretory) phase of the cycle"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct (as the incorrect statement): There is no rule restricting insertion to the mid-luteal phase; insertion is preferred during/just after menses (within ~12 days) partly to exclude pregnancy and because the os is slightly open.  Why A wrong (i.e., it is correct): Insertion within 12 days of menstrual onset is a recommended window.  Why B wrong (correct): Immediate postpartum (within 48 hrs) or after 4 weeks are accepted windows; the 48 hr–4 week gap is avoided due to expulsion/perforation risk.  Why C wrong (correct): Post-abortion insertion is appropriate if there is no infection.  High-Yield Takeaway: IUCD is best inserted within 12 days of menses, postpartum <48 hrs or >4 weeks, or post-abortion (no sepsis) — not limited to the luteal phase."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A woman reports unprotected intercourse 3 days ago and wants to avoid pregnancy. Which is the MOST effective emergency contraceptive option and its window?","options":["Levonorgestrel pill, effective only within 24 hours","Combined pills are the most effective and can be used up to 10 days","Copper IUCD insertion, effective up to 5 days (120 hours) after unprotected sex and the most effective method","Emergency contraception is no longer possible after 48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The copper IUCD inserted within 5 days (120 hours) of unprotected intercourse is the MOST effective emergency contraceptive (>99%) and also provides ongoing contraception.  Why A wrong: Levonorgestrel EC works best within 72 hours (up to 120 hrs with reduced efficacy), not just 24 hours; it is effective but less so than copper IUCD.  Why B wrong: The Yuzpe (combined pill) method is less effective and has more side effects; the window is not 10 days.  Why D wrong: EC options remain available up to 120 hours (copper IUCD/ulipristal), so 48 hours is not the cut-off.  High-Yield Takeaway: Copper IUCD within 120 hrs = most effective EC; oral levonorgestrel is best within 72 hrs."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A postnatal mother asks about the Lactational Amenorrhoea Method (LAM). LAM provides reliable (~98%) contraception ONLY when ALL of the following are met:","options":["Exclusive breastfeeding, amenorrhoea, and infant younger than 6 months","Partial breastfeeding, any menstrual status, and infant under 1 year","Exclusive breastfeeding alone, regardless of menses or infant age","Amenorrhoea alone up to 12 months postpartum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: LAM is ~98% effective only when all three criteria hold — the mother is fully/nearly fully breastfeeding, remains amenorrhoeic, and the infant is under 6 months. Loss of any criterion requires a backup method.  Why B wrong: Partial breastfeeding and infant >6 months break the LAM criteria and reduce effectiveness.  Why C wrong: Breastfeeding alone is not enough — amenorrhoea and <6 months are also required.  Why D wrong: Amenorrhoea alone, especially beyond 6 months, does not fulfil LAM.  High-Yield Takeaway: LAM = ALL three: exclusive breastfeeding + amenorrhoea + infant <6 months."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"Two contraceptive methods are compared: Method X has a Pearl Index of 0.5 and Method Y has a Pearl Index of 8. The correct interpretation is:","options":["Method Y is more effective because it has a higher number","Method X is more effective — a lower Pearl Index means fewer pregnancies per 100 woman-years","Pearl Index measures cost, not effectiveness","Both methods are equally effective"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Pearl Index = number of unintended pregnancies per 100 woman-years of use; a LOWER value indicates a MORE effective method, so Method X (0.5) is more effective than Method Y (8).  Why A wrong: A higher Pearl Index means MORE failures, i.e., less effective.  Why C wrong: The Pearl Index measures failure/effectiveness, not cost.  Why D wrong: A 16-fold difference in index means they are not equivalent.  High-Yield Takeaway: Pearl Index = pregnancies per 100 woman-years; lower = better."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"At an anganwadi, a 30-month-old child is assessed for acute malnutrition. Which finding classifies the child as having SEVERE acute malnutrition (SAM)?","options":["MUAC of 12.0 cm","MUAC less than 11.5 cm (115 mm)","Weight-for-height between −2 and −3 SD","Mild pallor without edema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For children 6–59 months, SAM is defined by MUAC <11.5 cm, and/or weight-for-height <−3 SD, and/or bilateral pitting edema.  Why A wrong: MUAC 11.5–12.5 cm indicates MODERATE acute malnutrition (MAM), not SAM.  Why C wrong: WFH between −2 and −3 SD is MAM; SAM requires <−3 SD.  Why D wrong: Pallor is anaemia; only bilateral pitting edema (not pallor) is a SAM criterion.  High-Yield Takeaway: SAM = MUAC <11.5 cm OR WFH <−3 SD OR bilateral pitting edema; MAM = MUAC 11.5–12.5 cm / WFH −2 to −3 SD."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A child with SAM has bilateral pitting edema and fails the appetite test at the outpatient level. The MOST appropriate nursing action is:","options":["Continue outpatient community-based management with RUTF at home","Refer to a Nutrition Rehabilitation Centre (NRC) for inpatient care","Give only iron and folic acid and review in one month","Start high-protein feeds immediately at home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SAM with any complication — bilateral edema, failed appetite test, medical complications, or age <6 months — requires inpatient management at an NRC with therapeutic feeding and monitoring.  Why A wrong: Community/outpatient management is only for SAM WITHOUT complications and with a passed appetite test.  Why C wrong: Iron alone is inadequate; iron is actually withheld in the early stabilization phase of SAM.  Why D wrong: Abrupt high-protein/high-energy feeding risks refeeding syndrome; stabilization uses controlled F-75 first, in a facility.  High-Yield Takeaway: SAM + complication/edema/failed appetite test/<6 months → refer to NRC for inpatient care."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"In facility-based (NRC) management of a child with complicated SAM, the correct sequencing of therapeutic diets is:","options":["F-100 during stabilization, then F-75 during rehabilitation","F-75 during stabilization, then F-100 during rehabilitation (catch-up growth)","F-100 throughout, as it has more calories","Only ready-to-use therapeutic food during stabilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: F-75 (75 kcal/100 ml, lower protein/sodium) is used in the STABILIZATION phase to avoid overwhelming the metabolism and prevent refeeding syndrome; F-100 (100 kcal/100 ml) is used in the REHABILITATION phase for catch-up growth.  Why A wrong: The order is reversed — using calorie-dense F-100 first risks fluid overload/refeeding complications.  Why C wrong: F-100 throughout is unsafe during initial stabilization.  Why D wrong: RUTF is generally for the rehabilitation/community phase, not initial stabilization of a complicated case.  High-Yield Takeaway: \"Stabilize with 75, Grow with 100\" — F-75 first, then F-100 for catch-up."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A malnourished child presents with generalized bilateral pitting edema, a \"moon face,\" irritability, sparse depigmented hair and skin flaking, but only moderate weight loss. This clinical picture is MOST consistent with:","options":["Marasmus","Kwashiorkor","Marasmic-kwashiorkor with no edema","Simple stunting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kwashiorkor (predominant protein deficiency) is characterized by bilateral pitting edema, moon face, apathy/irritability, hair/skin changes and hepatomegaly, with weight partially masked by edema.  Why A wrong: Marasmus shows severe wasting with an \"old man's face,\" no edema, and marked weight loss due to overall energy deficiency.  Why C wrong: Marasmic-kwashiorkor by definition includes edema; \"no edema\" contradicts it.  Why D wrong: Stunting is chronic low height-for-age without the acute edema/skin features described.  High-Yield Takeaway: Edema + moon face + hair/skin changes = Kwashiorkor (protein deficit); severe wasting + old-man face, no edema = Marasmus (energy deficit)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"As per the MoHFW National Vitamin A Prophylaxis Programme, the schedule for a child is:","options":["First dose 2 lakh IU at birth, then monthly","First dose 1 lakh IU (1,00,000 IU) at 9 months with measles vaccine, then 2 lakh IU (2,00,000 IU) every 6 months up to 5 years","A single 1 lakh IU dose at 5 years only","2 lakh IU at 6 weeks with pentavalent vaccine only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first dose of 1,00,000 IU is given at 9 months along with measles-containing vaccine; thereafter 2,00,000 IU is given every 6 months up to 5 years — 9 doses in total.  Why A wrong: Vitamin A is not started at birth at 2 lakh IU monthly; that would risk toxicity.  Why C wrong: A single dose at 5 years does not match the biannual schedule.  Why D wrong: The programme dose is not linked to the 6-week pentavalent visit; the first dose is at 9 months with measles vaccine.  High-Yield Takeaway: Vitamin A: 1 lakh IU at 9 months (with measles), then 2 lakh IU every 6 months up to 5 years = 9 doses."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A mother of a 5-month-old exclusively breastfed infant asks when to start other foods. As per IYCF guidelines, the correct advice is:","options":["Start water and diluted cow's milk now","Continue exclusive breastfeeding until 6 months, then begin complementary feeding while continuing breastfeeding up to 2 years","Stop breastfeeding at 6 months and switch fully to solids","Begin complementary foods now at 5 months as the baby is big"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IYCF recommends exclusive breastfeeding (no water/other fluids) for the first 6 months, introduction of complementary foods at 6 months, and continued breastfeeding up to 2 years or beyond.  Why A wrong: Nothing other than breast milk (not even water) is advised before 6 months.  Why C wrong: Breastfeeding continues alongside complementary foods, not stopped at 6 months.  Why D wrong: Complementary foods should begin at 6 completed months, not at 5.  High-Yield Takeaway: Exclusive BF for 6 months → complementary feeding AT 6 months → continue BF up to 2 years."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"A community nurse is testing drinking water at a village hand-pump after chlorination. To be considered microbiologically safe, the minimum free residual chlorine after the recommended contact period is:","options":["0.05 mg/L after 10 minutes","0.5 mg/L after a contact period of 1 hour","5 mg/L immediately after adding chlorine","2.0 mg/L after 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A minimum FREE residual chlorine of 0.5 mg/L (0.5 ppm) after a contact period of at least 1 hour indicates adequate disinfection and a margin of safety against recontamination.  Why A wrong: 0.05 mg/L is far below the protective level and the contact time is too short.  Why C wrong: The value must be measured after the 1-hour contact period, not immediately; 5 mg/L is not the standard.  Why D wrong: 2.0 mg/L at 24 hours is not the defined safety criterion.  High-Yield Takeaway: Safe water = free residual chlorine ≥0.5 mg/L after 1-hour contact time."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"As per the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016 (CPCB), used needles, syringes with fixed needles, scalpels and blades must be discarded in which colour-coded container?","options":["Yellow bin","Red bin","White (translucent) puncture-proof, leak-proof container","Blue bin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Waste sharps (needles, syringes with fixed needles, scalpels, blades — including contaminated/discarded metal sharps) go into WHITE translucent, puncture-proof, leak-proof, tamper-proof containers.  Why A wrong: Yellow is for human/animal anatomical waste, soiled waste, expired medicines and chemical waste (incineration/deep burial).  Why B wrong: Red is for contaminated recyclable plastic waste — tubing, IV sets, catheters, urine bags, syringes WITHOUT needles and gloves (autoclaved then recycled).  Why D wrong: Blue is for glassware (broken/discarded/contaminated glass, vials, ampoules) and metallic body implants.  High-Yield Takeaway: BMW 2016 — Yellow (anatomical/soiled/expired meds), Red (recyclable plastics), White (sharps), Blue (glass + metal implants)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"As per BMW 2016, a blood-soaked cotton dressing and expired tablets should be discarded into which colour bin?","options":["Yellow bin","Red bin","White container","Blue bin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Soiled waste (items contaminated with blood/body fluids such as dressings, cotton) and expired/discarded medicines belong to the YELLOW category, destined for incineration/deep burial.  Why B wrong: Red is only for contaminated recyclable plastics (tubing, catheters, non-needle syringes, gloves), not blood-soaked cotton or medicines.  Why C wrong: White is exclusively for sharps.  Why D wrong: Blue is for glass and metallic implants.  High-Yield Takeaway: Soiled dressings + expired medicines = YELLOW bin (incineration)."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"An HIV-positive adult is newly diagnosed with active pulmonary TB. Regarding the sequence of therapy, the correct principle (NACO/NTEP) is:","options":["Start ART first, then delay anti-TB treatment for 6 months","Start anti-TB treatment first, then initiate ART early (usually within 2–8 weeks) irrespective of CD4 count","Treat only HIV; TB will resolve with ART alone","Give ART and defer TB treatment until CD4 rises"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In TB-HIV co-infection, anti-TB treatment is started first and ART is initiated early (typically within 2–8 weeks of starting ATT, sooner if CD4 is very low) for all TB-HIV patients regardless of CD4, balancing pill burden, IRIS and mortality benefit.  Why A wrong: ART is not delayed for 6 months; early ART reduces mortality.  Why C wrong: ART alone does not treat active TB; full ATT is required.  Why D wrong: TB treatment must not be deferred — untreated TB is a leading killer in HIV.  High-Yield Takeaway: TB-HIV → start ATT first, then ART early (within 2–8 weeks) regardless of CD4 count."},{"section":"Advanced NORCET High-Yield MCQs — Part 4: Communicable Disease, Family Planning, Nutrition & Environmental Health · Outbreak priority actions & notification…","question":"During a measles outbreak investigation, the nurse is asked about \"ring vaccination.\" This strategy is BEST described as:","options":["Vaccinating the entire national population simultaneously","Vaccinating the contacts, and contacts of contacts, around confirmed cases to contain spread","Vaccinating only health workers","Giving repeated doses to a single index case"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ring vaccination targets and immunizes the contacts (and their contacts) surrounding confirmed cases, creating a protective \"ring\" that contains transmission — famously used to eradicate smallpox and applied in outbreak containment.  Why A wrong: That describes mass vaccination, not ring vaccination.  Why C wrong: Vaccinating only staff is not the definition; the ring is built around cases in the community.  Why D wrong: Ring vaccination protects susceptible contacts, not repeat dosing of the case.  High-Yield Takeaway: Ring vaccination = immunize contacts and contacts-of-contacts around a case to contain spread (smallpox eradication strategy)."}];
